window.EXAM_QUESTIONS = [{"id": 961, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Wilson can lift his right arm to table height, extend his wrist against gravity and grasp a cup but cannot release objects smoothly. He wants to hold a fishing line again. Which training approach is best supported for improving his arm use?", "options": {"A": "Graded repetitive practice of reach, grasp and release in tasks that resemble fishing", "B": "Passive range of motion for the wrist and fingers daily, delaying active tasks until strength returns", "C": "Neurodevelopmental handling to normalise tone first, with functional tasks introduced only once tone is normal", "D": "Strength training with free weights for the shoulder girdle alone, with hand function addressed later"}, "cluster_id": "CHS1", "cluster_text": "Raymond Wilson, 62, Aboriginal man, retired mechanic and keen fisher, lives in a regional town two hours from the clinic. Left hemisphere ischaemic stroke ten weeks ago with right arm weakness; right hand dominant. Discharged home and seen by community physiotherapy twice weekly, with his sister attending. He can lift his right arm to table height, extend the wrist against gravity and grasp a cup but releases objects poorly. Sensation intact. Mild inferior shoulder subluxation, and his family often lift him by the affected arm during transfers. Mood low but engaged. He missed two sessions for Sorry Business. An Aboriginal Health Worker from the local Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service is available to assist.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Graded repetitive practice of reach, grasp and release in tasks that resemble fishing): Task specific, repetitive and graded practice of meaningful activities is the best supported way to improve arm function after stroke. Passive movement and tone normalisation first do not carry over to function, and shoulder strengthening alone neglects the hand skills he actually wants back."}, {"id": 962, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "During overhead reaching Mr Wilson reports sharp right shoulder pain, and a finger breadth gap is palpable below the acromion. His family often lift him by the affected arm during transfers. What is the most appropriate change to his programme?", "options": {"A": "Continue the programme unchanged, since mild subluxation usually settles with ongoing active reaching practice", "B": "Use overhead pulley exercises to stretch the painful shoulder and strengthen the arm against the discomfort", "C": "Limit reach to below shoulder height with support and teach family safe transfer handling", "D": "Stop arm training until the pain settles and use a sling for daytime protection"}, "cluster_id": "CHS1", "cluster_text": "Raymond Wilson, 62, Aboriginal man, retired mechanic and keen fisher, lives in a regional town two hours from the clinic. Left hemisphere ischaemic stroke ten weeks ago with right arm weakness; right hand dominant. Discharged home and seen by community physiotherapy twice weekly, with his sister attending. He can lift his right arm to table height, extend the wrist against gravity and grasp a cup but releases objects poorly. Sensation intact. Mild inferior shoulder subluxation, and his family often lift him by the affected arm during transfers. Mood low but engaged. He missed two sessions for Sorry Business. An Aboriginal Health Worker from the local Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service is available to assist.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Limit reach to below shoulder height with support and teach family safe transfer handling): Pain with subluxation is often worsened by overhead loading and pulling on the arm, so reach should stay below shoulder height with support while the family learn safe handling. Pulleys and forceful overhead stretching irritate the joint. Stopping all training or relying on a sling slows recovery, and ignoring pain overlooks the likely cause."}, {"id": 963, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Wilson missed two community sessions because of Sorry Business, and the service policy suggests discharge after two missed appointments. His sister says he still wants therapy. What is the most culturally safe and appropriate response by the treating physiotherapist?", "options": {"A": "Apply the discharge policy consistently and offer him a new referral when he is ready", "B": "Keep his place open but ask him to provide written evidence of the cultural event", "C": "Honour the obligation, keep him on the caseload, and reschedule flexibly with family", "D": "Transfer his care to a different clinician who is able to attend at fixed weekly times"}, "cluster_id": "CHS1", "cluster_text": "Raymond Wilson, 62, Aboriginal man, retired mechanic and keen fisher, lives in a regional town two hours from the clinic. Left hemisphere ischaemic stroke ten weeks ago with right arm weakness; right hand dominant. Discharged home and seen by community physiotherapy twice weekly, with his sister attending. He can lift his right arm to table height, extend the wrist against gravity and grasp a cup but releases objects poorly. Sensation intact. Mild inferior shoulder subluxation, and his family often lift him by the affected arm during transfers. Mood low but engaged. He missed two sessions for Sorry Business. An Aboriginal Health Worker from the local Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service is available to assist.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Honour the obligation, keep him on the caseload, and reschedule flexibly with family): Cultural obligations such as Sorry Business take priority for many Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, and culturally safe practice adapts the service to them rather than penalising absence. Flexible rescheduling with his family, with the Aboriginal Health Worker involved, maintains trust and continuity. Discharge, demanding evidence or changing clinician damages the relationship."}, {"id": 964, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Wilson's grasp and release have improved with sessions twice weekly, but he practises little between visits and lives two hours from the clinic. How should the physiotherapist increase the amount of task practice he can safely achieve each week?", "options": {"A": "Give a standard printed sheet of generic arm exercises and review it next visit", "B": "Agree a home programme of fishing related tasks with local health worker support", "C": "Increase clinic sessions to daily visits and advise him not to use the arm at home", "D": "Arrange an allied health assistant to supervise his arm exercises at home each week"}, "cluster_id": "CHS1", "cluster_text": "Raymond Wilson, 62, Aboriginal man, retired mechanic and keen fisher, lives in a regional town two hours from the clinic. Left hemisphere ischaemic stroke ten weeks ago with right arm weakness; right hand dominant. Discharged home and seen by community physiotherapy twice weekly, with his sister attending. He can lift his right arm to table height, extend the wrist against gravity and grasp a cup but releases objects poorly. Sensation intact. Mild inferior shoulder subluxation, and his family often lift him by the affected arm during transfers. Mood low but engaged. He missed two sessions for Sorry Business. An Aboriginal Health Worker from the local Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service is available to assist.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Agree a home programme of fishing related tasks with local health worker support): Recovery depends on the amount of meaningful practice, so a home programme built on tasks he values, with support close to home, increases dose in a way he can sustain. Daily clinic visits are impractical given the distance, and generic sheets lack personal meaning. Weekly assistant supervision alone gives far too little practice."}, {"id": 965, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Samaha has reduced lateral foot sensation, weak plantarflexion with difficulty toe walking, and a reduced Achilles reflex on the right. His straight leg raise also reproduces his leg pain at 40 degrees. Which nerve root is most likely involved?", "options": {"A": "The fifth lumbar root, which supplies great toe extension and sensation over the dorsal foot", "B": "The fourth lumbar root, which supplies the knee reflex and sensation over the medial leg", "C": "The first sacral root, which supplies the Achilles reflex and lateral foot sensation", "D": "The second lumbar root, which supplies hip flexion and sensation over the anterior thigh"}, "cluster_id": "CHS2", "cluster_text": "Karim Samaha is a 45 year old courier driver attending your private practice. For three weeks he has had right buttock and posterior leg pain extending to the lateral foot, worse with prolonged driving and coughing. Low back pain is 3/10 and leg pain 7/10. Examination: right straight leg raise reproduces leg pain at 40 degrees, reduced sensation along the lateral border of the right foot, right ankle plantarflexion strength 4/5 with a reduced Achilles reflex, heel walk normal, toe walk difficult on the right. No bladder or bowel change, no saddle numbness. Your afternoon list is full: three more patients are booked, a medicolegal report is overdue, and you have ten minutes before the next patient.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The first sacral root, which supplies the Achilles reflex and lateral foot sensation): The Achilles reflex, plantarflexion power and lateral foot sensation map to S1, and a positive straight leg raise supports irritation of the lower lumbar and sacral roots. L5 would weaken great toe extension, while L4 and L2 involvement would alter the knee reflex or the front of the thigh."}, {"id": 966, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Samaha's leg pain is worst after 30 minutes of driving and eases when he stands and walks. He is neurologically stable at this visit and needs to keep working as a courier. Which initial management plan is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Education, easing positions, neural sliders and graded walking, with driving break advice", "B": "Firm end range neural tensioning and spinal manipulation, aiming to reduce the leg pain at once", "C": "Bed rest for several days, then lumbar flexion stretching and a lumbar support belt for driving", "D": "Strict avoidance of driving for six weeks, with isometric trunk strengthening in the meantime"}, "cluster_id": "CHS2", "cluster_text": "Karim Samaha is a 45 year old courier driver attending your private practice. For three weeks he has had right buttock and posterior leg pain extending to the lateral foot, worse with prolonged driving and coughing. Low back pain is 3/10 and leg pain 7/10. Examination: right straight leg raise reproduces leg pain at 40 degrees, reduced sensation along the lateral border of the right foot, right ankle plantarflexion strength 4/5 with a reduced Achilles reflex, heel walk normal, toe walk difficult on the right. No bladder or bowel change, no saddle numbness. Your afternoon list is full: three more patients are booked, a medicolegal report is overdue, and you have ten minutes before the next patient.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Education, easing positions, neural sliders and graded walking, with driving break advice): Staying active, using positions that ease the leg pain, gentle neural sliders and planned breaks respect his work and suit a stable radiculopathy. Bed rest slows recovery, forceful tensioning or manipulation can aggravate an irritable root, and long driving bans are unnecessary when symptoms ease with movement."}, {"id": 967, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "At review in one week, Mr Samaha's right ankle plantarflexion has dropped from 4/5 to 3/5, he can no longer toe walk, and numbness has spread along the foot. His leg pain is slightly better. What is the most appropriate decision?", "options": {"A": "Same day contact with his GP for urgent specialist opinion on progressive weakness", "B": "Reduce the intensity of neural sliders and review again in two weeks", "C": "Continue the current program and review in one week, because his leg pain is clearly improving", "D": "Add strengthening for the calf muscles and reassess strength at the next session"}, "cluster_id": "CHS2", "cluster_text": "Karim Samaha is a 45 year old courier driver attending your private practice. For three weeks he has had right buttock and posterior leg pain extending to the lateral foot, worse with prolonged driving and coughing. Low back pain is 3/10 and leg pain 7/10. Examination: right straight leg raise reproduces leg pain at 40 degrees, reduced sensation along the lateral border of the right foot, right ankle plantarflexion strength 4/5 with a reduced Achilles reflex, heel walk normal, toe walk difficult on the right. No bladder or bowel change, no saddle numbness. Your afternoon list is full: three more patients are booked, a medicolegal report is overdue, and you have ten minutes before the next patient.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Same day contact with his GP for urgent specialist opinion on progressive weakness): Worsening motor weakness and spreading sensory loss indicate a progressing neurological deficit that needs urgent medical assessment, whatever the pain level. Reduced pain can accompany worsening nerve damage, so it should not reassure. Waiting, strengthening or adjusting exercises delays a possible surgical opinion."}, {"id": 968, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Samaha has left. You have ten minutes before your next patient. Pending are a phone message from a patient with a recent disc injury reporting new saddle numbness and urinary hesitancy, an overdue medicolegal report, and today's treatment notes. Which should you do first?", "options": {"A": "Complete today's treatment notes while details of each consultation remain fresh in your memory", "B": "Phone the patient straight away, advise emergency department attendance and document the contact", "C": "Finish the overdue medicolegal report, as its deadline has passed and carries legal consequences", "D": "Book the caller for the earliest appointment tomorrow and then continue with the overdue report"}, "cluster_id": "CHS2", "cluster_text": "Karim Samaha is a 45 year old courier driver attending your private practice. For three weeks he has had right buttock and posterior leg pain extending to the lateral foot, worse with prolonged driving and coughing. Low back pain is 3/10 and leg pain 7/10. Examination: right straight leg raise reproduces leg pain at 40 degrees, reduced sensation along the lateral border of the right foot, right ankle plantarflexion strength 4/5 with a reduced Achilles reflex, heel walk normal, toe walk difficult on the right. No bladder or bowel change, no saddle numbness. Your afternoon list is full: three more patients are booked, a medicolegal report is overdue, and you have ten minutes before the next patient.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Phone the patient straight away, advise emergency department attendance and document the contact): New saddle numbness and urinary hesitancy after a disc injury may signal cauda equina syndrome, a time critical risk to the patient that outranks administrative deadlines. Reports and notes can follow once the patient is safely directed to emergency care, and a booking for tomorrow is too late."}, {"id": 969, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "During handling Tahlia's parents ask what changes they should report promptly because of her shunt. You explain the warning signs of raised intracranial pressure. Which set of changes most strongly suggests shunt malfunction in a baby of her age and needs urgent medical review?", "options": {"A": "New weakness at the hips, changed leg posture and increased tightness in both legs", "B": "Vomiting, irritability or drowsiness, a bulging fontanelle and rapidly increasing head size", "C": "Fever, redness and discharge from the closure site, with a tender swollen back", "D": "Noisy breathing, episodes of apnoea, a weak cry and difficulty swallowing during feeds and sleep"}, "cluster_id": "CHS3", "cluster_text": "Tahlia Nakamura, 6 weeks old, was born with a lumbar myelomeningocele closed surgically on day 2. A ventriculoperitoneal shunt was inserted at 2 weeks for hydrocephalus. She remains on the paediatric surgical ward. The wound is clean and dry, the fontanelle is soft and head circumference is tracking its centile. She has active hip flexion, weak knee extension and no active ankle movement, with a reduced response to pinch below the knees. Both feet rest in mild flexible equinus. Hip range is full. Her parents are anxious about handling her. HR 144, RR 44, SpO2 98%. A newly rostered allied health assistant (AHA) works on the ward, and your senior asks you to plan which parts of her care could be delegated.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Vomiting, irritability or drowsiness, a bulging fontanelle and rapidly increasing head size): Shunt blockage causes raised intracranial pressure, which shows as vomiting, lethargy or irritability, a tense fontanelle and rapid head growth. Wound infection, brainstem signs such as stridor or apnoea, and new leg changes suggesting cord tethering are also important in spina bifida but point to different problems."}, {"id": 970, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Tahlia's feet rest in mild flexible equinus with reduced sensation below the knees. You teach her parents a passive stretching programme for her ankles. Her parents have never stretched an infant before and ask how firmly to push. Which technique is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Warm the tissues with a heat pack first, then hold the stretch for several minutes", "B": "Use brisk repeated dorsiflexion pushes, ten times each foot, to build tissue tolerance gradually", "C": "Press through the ball of the foot, using her facial expression to judge comfort and stop", "D": "Use gentle sustained dorsiflexion with the heel held, stopping at first resistance and checking skin"}, "cluster_id": "CHS3", "cluster_text": "Tahlia Nakamura, 6 weeks old, was born with a lumbar myelomeningocele closed surgically on day 2. A ventriculoperitoneal shunt was inserted at 2 weeks for hydrocephalus. She remains on the paediatric surgical ward. The wound is clean and dry, the fontanelle is soft and head circumference is tracking its centile. She has active hip flexion, weak knee extension and no active ankle movement, with a reduced response to pinch below the knees. Both feet rest in mild flexible equinus. Hip range is full. Her parents are anxious about handling her. HR 144, RR 44, SpO2 98%. A newly rostered allied health assistant (AHA) works on the ward, and your senior asks you to plan which parts of her care could be delegated.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Use gentle sustained dorsiflexion with the heel held, stopping at first resistance and checking skin): With reduced sensation she cannot signal pain, so stretching must be gentle, slow and stopped at first resistance, with the heel held so the force acts at the ankle, and the skin checked afterwards. Pressing through the forefoot can distort the foot, and heat on insensate skin risks burns. Fast repeated pushes risk tissue damage."}, {"id": 971, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "You want the allied health assistant to help with Tahlia's care. She is competent in infant handling basics but has not worked with a baby with a shunt or insensate legs. Which task is most appropriate to delegate to her, with supervision?", "options": {"A": "Reassessing her active leg movements and updating the plan, as the assistant sees her more often", "B": "Teaching the parents how to recognise shunt malfunction, using the written handout you prepared for them", "C": "Deciding independently when to progress the ankle stretching programme if her feet appear more flexible", "D": "Repeating the positioning and play routine you have taught and watched her perform safely"}, "cluster_id": "CHS3", "cluster_text": "Tahlia Nakamura, 6 weeks old, was born with a lumbar myelomeningocele closed surgically on day 2. A ventriculoperitoneal shunt was inserted at 2 weeks for hydrocephalus. She remains on the paediatric surgical ward. The wound is clean and dry, the fontanelle is soft and head circumference is tracking its centile. She has active hip flexion, weak knee extension and no active ankle movement, with a reduced response to pinch below the knees. Both feet rest in mild flexible equinus. Hip range is full. Her parents are anxious about handling her. HR 144, RR 44, SpO2 98%. A newly rostered allied health assistant (AHA) works on the ward, and your senior asks you to plan which parts of her care could be delegated.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Repeating the positioning and play routine you have taught and watched her perform safely): Delegation suits a routine, predictable task for which the assistant has been trained and observed, with the physiotherapist retaining responsibility and supervising. Assessment, plan changes and clinical decisions about progression need professional judgement and are not delegated. Education about a shunt complication also requires clinical reasoning and should stay with the physiotherapist or nursing team."}, {"id": 972, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "At the next visit Tahlia's mother brings a soft rubbery play mat and toys bought online, and the packaging does not state the material. Tahlia has had several surgeries and procedures since birth, with frequent contact with hospital equipment. Her mother asks if she can use them for tummy time. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Use only items confirmed latex free, and discuss her allergy risk with the medical team", "B": "Allow supervised use for short periods only, as brief contact carries little risk for young infants", "C": "Allow the mat after washing it thoroughly because washing removes the proteins that cause allergy", "D": "Suggest a skin test on her thigh first, stopping use if a rash appears"}, "cluster_id": "CHS3", "cluster_text": "Tahlia Nakamura, 6 weeks old, was born with a lumbar myelomeningocele closed surgically on day 2. A ventriculoperitoneal shunt was inserted at 2 weeks for hydrocephalus. She remains on the paediatric surgical ward. The wound is clean and dry, the fontanelle is soft and head circumference is tracking its centile. She has active hip flexion, weak knee extension and no active ankle movement, with a reduced response to pinch below the knees. Both feet rest in mild flexible equinus. Hip range is full. Her parents are anxious about handling her. HR 144, RR 44, SpO2 98%. A newly rostered allied health assistant (AHA) works on the ward, and your senior asks you to plan which parts of her care could be delegated.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use only items confirmed latex free, and discuss her allergy risk with the medical team): Babies with spina bifida have repeated early exposure through surgery and procedures, which raises the risk of latex sensitisation, so only items confirmed latex free should be used. Washing does not reliably remove allergenic proteins, and limiting time does not remove the risk. Home skin testing could provoke a reaction and is not a safe screening method."}, {"id": 973, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Baraka has no bony tenderness at the malleoli, navicular or fifth metatarsal base, and he took four steps both at the time of injury and in the department. Applying the Ottawa ankle rules to these findings, what is the most appropriate conclusion about radiography?", "options": {"A": "Radiography is required because laxity on anterior drawer suggests an associated avulsion fracture may be present", "B": "A radiograph is not required, because none of the Ottawa criteria for fracture are met", "C": "Radiography is required because he limps and has swelling and bruising around the lateral ankle", "D": "Radiography is required because the injury happened at work and compensation claims may need imaging"}, "cluster_id": "CHS4", "cluster_text": "Baraka Nkosi, 29, warehouse worker, rolled his right ankle into inversion three hours ago when stepping off a pallet. He walked a few steps immediately afterwards and has come to the emergency department. No previous ankle injuries, no medications. Pain is 5/10 at rest. Findings: swelling and early bruising over the anterolateral ankle, tenderness over the anterior talofibular ligament, no tenderness over the posterior edge or tip of either malleolus, and no tenderness over the base of the fifth metatarsal or the navicular. He took four steps in the department with a limp. Anterior drawer test is painful with mild laxity, talar tilt is painful, the calf is soft and non-tender, foot pulses are present and sensation is normal. His job involves ten hours of standing and lifting up to 20 kg, and he hopes to return in three days.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A radiograph is not required, because none of the Ottawa criteria for fracture are met): The Ottawa ankle rules call for imaging only with bone tenderness in defined sites or inability to take four steps. Neither applies here. Swelling, bruising and laxity point to ligament injury and do not trigger imaging, and workplace context does not change the clinical rule."}, {"id": 974, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Baraka has a probable lateral ligament sprain, no fracture criteria, and a soft, non-tender calf. He is keen to recover quickly and asks what to do over the coming days. The physiotherapist plans his management for the first week. Which plan is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Protected weight bearing, early pain-guided movement, then progressive balance and strengthening exercises for the ankle", "B": "Rigid immobilisation in a walking boot for two weeks, then start movement and weight bearing", "C": "Non weight bearing on crutches until swelling resolves, then begin loading once the ankle looks normal", "D": "Passive calf stretching and joint mobilisation into dorsiflexion, to restore range within the first few days"}, "cluster_id": "CHS4", "cluster_text": "Baraka Nkosi, 29, warehouse worker, rolled his right ankle into inversion three hours ago when stepping off a pallet. He walked a few steps immediately afterwards and has come to the emergency department. No previous ankle injuries, no medications. Pain is 5/10 at rest. Findings: swelling and early bruising over the anterolateral ankle, tenderness over the anterior talofibular ligament, no tenderness over the posterior edge or tip of either malleolus, and no tenderness over the base of the fifth metatarsal or the navicular. He took four steps in the department with a limp. Anterior drawer test is painful with mild laxity, talar tilt is painful, the calf is soft and non-tender, foot pulses are present and sensation is normal. His job involves ten hours of standing and lifting up to 20 kg, and he hopes to return in three days.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Protected weight bearing, early pain-guided movement, then progressive balance and strengthening exercises for the ankle): Evidence supports short-term protection with early, pain-guided movement and progressive loading, including balance training, because it speeds recovery and reduces recurrence. Prolonged immobilisation or avoiding weight bearing delays function, and aggressive passive stretching would irritate the injured ligament."}, {"id": 975, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Baraka asks about returning to a job that involves long periods of standing and lifting up to 20 kg. His ankle is still swollen and painful on loading, and he reports he cannot afford a long absence. What advice is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Stay off work until he can hop pain free and shows full strength on formal testing", "B": "Return on modified duties with limited lifting and seated breaks, progressed as function allows", "C": "Take sick leave until an ultrasound confirms that the ligament has healed and swelling has gone", "D": "Return to full duties after three days once walking is comfortable, wearing a lace-up brace"}, "cluster_id": "CHS4", "cluster_text": "Baraka Nkosi, 29, warehouse worker, rolled his right ankle into inversion three hours ago when stepping off a pallet. He walked a few steps immediately afterwards and has come to the emergency department. No previous ankle injuries, no medications. Pain is 5/10 at rest. Findings: swelling and early bruising over the anterolateral ankle, tenderness over the anterior talofibular ligament, no tenderness over the posterior edge or tip of either malleolus, and no tenderness over the base of the fifth metatarsal or the navicular. He took four steps in the department with a limp. Anterior drawer test is painful with mild laxity, talar tilt is painful, the calf is soft and non-tender, foot pulses are present and sensation is normal. His job involves ten hours of standing and lifting up to 20 kg, and he hopes to return in three days.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Return on modified duties with limited lifting and seated breaks, progressed as function allows): A graded return with modified duties keeps him active while protecting a healing ankle and is consistent with best practice for work-related injuries. Full duties too soon risks reinjury, and waiting for hopping or imaging benchmarks delays recovery unnecessarily."}, {"id": 976, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "A colleague wants to change the department ankle sprain protocol after reading one small unblinded trial of a new taping method that reported better short-term pain scores. The proposal goes to the team meeting tomorrow for a decision. What should the physiotherapist recommend first?", "options": {"A": "Keep the current protocol because research published in the last year is unsuitable for guidelines", "B": "Adopt the taping method now, because positive results in a published trial justify team use", "C": "Ask the manufacturer for a clinical summary of the product, then decide with the senior physiotherapist", "D": "Appraise the trial's design, size and risk of bias, then look for guideline or review evidence"}, "cluster_id": "CHS4", "cluster_text": "Baraka Nkosi, 29, warehouse worker, rolled his right ankle into inversion three hours ago when stepping off a pallet. He walked a few steps immediately afterwards and has come to the emergency department. No previous ankle injuries, no medications. Pain is 5/10 at rest. Findings: swelling and early bruising over the anterolateral ankle, tenderness over the anterior talofibular ligament, no tenderness over the posterior edge or tip of either malleolus, and no tenderness over the base of the fifth metatarsal or the navicular. He took four steps in the department with a limp. Anterior drawer test is painful with mild laxity, talar tilt is painful, the calf is soft and non-tender, foot pulses are present and sensation is normal. His job involves ten hours of standing and lifting up to 20 kg, and he hopes to return in three days.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Appraise the trial's design, size and risk of bias, then look for guideline or review evidence): A single small unblinded trial is weak evidence and must be critically appraised, then placed in context with systematic reviews and guidelines before practice changes. Accepting it uncritically or relying on commercial summaries risks bias, while rejecting all recent research ignores that some of it may be sound."}, {"id": 977, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "The ward doctor asks which features of Anastasia's presentation support delirium rather than established dementia. Her confusion began suddenly over a day, she was fully independent before admission, and her alertness fluctuates across the day. Which feature is the most characteristic of delirium?", "options": {"A": "Disorientation and memory impairment, which are prominent in delirium and help tell it from dementia", "B": "Night agitation with wandering and disturbed sleep, which is specific to delirium and absent in dementia", "C": "Fluctuating cognition with visual hallucinations over months, with preserved attention, suggesting dementia with Lewy bodies", "D": "Acute onset with fluctuating attention and alertness, typically driven by an underlying medical illness"}, "cluster_id": "CHS5", "cluster_text": "Mrs Anastasia Galanis, 82, is on day three of a medical ward admission for a urinary tract infection and dehydration. She is widowed, lives alone in a two-storey home and was independent with a walking stick before admission. Since last night she has been intermittently confused, mistook a nurse for her daughter, and fluctuates between agitation and drowsiness. The nursing delirium screen is positive. Medications: paracetamol and an antihypertensive; no benzodiazepines. Observations: temperature 37.4, HR 92 regular, BP 128/72 sitting, RR 18, SpO2 95% on room air. Her glasses and hearing aids are in her locker. The medical team is treating the infection. Her daughter wants to know how to prevent future falls and readmission.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Acute onset with fluctuating attention and alertness, typically driven by an underlying medical illness): Delirium develops over hours to days, fluctuates, impairs attention and arousal, and usually has an acute medical trigger such as infection or dehydration. Disorientation and memory loss occur in both delirium and dementia, so they do not separate the two. Night agitation and wandering also occur in dementia, and fluctuating cognition with hallucinations that develops over months points to a gradual dementia such as Lewy body disease rather than an acute change."}, {"id": 978, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Once the delirium has resolved, Anastasia's daughter asks how she can help her mother reduce falls and avoid readmission after discharge. Anastasia lives alone and was independent before admission. What is the most appropriate health promotion advice for the physiotherapist to give?", "options": {"A": "Advise limiting activity at home for several weeks to reduce falls risk while her strength recovers", "B": "Encourage daily walking, a balance exercise programme and hydration, and arrange a home safety review", "C": "Give a written strength exercise sheet and ask her GP to review it routinely", "D": "Suggest she move in with family, as living alone is the main cause of readmission"}, "cluster_id": "CHS5", "cluster_text": "Mrs Anastasia Galanis, 82, is on day three of a medical ward admission for a urinary tract infection and dehydration. She is widowed, lives alone in a two-storey home and was independent with a walking stick before admission. Since last night she has been intermittently confused, mistook a nurse for her daughter, and fluctuates between agitation and drowsiness. The nursing delirium screen is positive. Medications: paracetamol and an antihypertensive; no benzodiazepines. Observations: temperature 37.4, HR 92 regular, BP 128/72 sitting, RR 18, SpO2 95% on room air. Her glasses and hearing aids are in her locker. The medical team is treating the infection. Her daughter wants to know how to prevent future falls and readmission.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Encourage daily walking, a balance exercise programme and hydration, and arrange a home safety review): Falls prevention in older adults relies on regular balance and strength activity, adequate fluid intake and a safe home, with Anastasia and her daughter supported to take part in the plan. Reducing activity leads to deconditioning and raises falls risk. Relocating her ignores her independence and preferences, and a sheet alone gives no environmental assessment."}, {"id": 979, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "This morning Anastasia is calm, follows simple one-step commands and is sitting in the chair with her glasses and hearing aids on. Her observations are stable. The physiotherapist wants to promote mobility while the delirium resolves. Which approach is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Short, frequent supervised walks with her stick, using simple instructions and orientating cues", "B": "A single afternoon strengthening session in the gym, with walking deferred until her strength has improved", "C": "Mobilise with a wheeled frame instead of her stick when family attends, to reduce confusion", "D": "Defer mobilisation until a formal cognitive screen shows full orientation, encouraging bed rest in the meantime"}, "cluster_id": "CHS5", "cluster_text": "Mrs Anastasia Galanis, 82, is on day three of a medical ward admission for a urinary tract infection and dehydration. She is widowed, lives alone in a two-storey home and was independent with a walking stick before admission. Since last night she has been intermittently confused, mistook a nurse for her daughter, and fluctuates between agitation and drowsiness. The nursing delirium screen is positive. Medications: paracetamol and an antihypertensive; no benzodiazepines. Observations: temperature 37.4, HR 92 regular, BP 128/72 sitting, RR 18, SpO2 95% on room air. Her glasses and hearing aids are in her locker. The medical team is treating the infection. Her daughter wants to know how to prevent future falls and readmission.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Short, frequent supervised walks with her stick, using simple instructions and orientating cues): Early, frequent, supervised mobilisation supports recovery from delirium and prevents deconditioning, with her usual aid and sensory aids in place and clear cues. Waiting for full orientation promotes bed rest and prolongs delirium. Changing her walking aid and limiting sessions to family attendance reduce practice and add unfamiliarity, and one long gym session is tiring and poorly suited to fluctuating attention."}, {"id": 980, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Later that day, during a treatment session, Anastasia becomes agitated, tries to climb out of her chair and insists she must go home to feed her cat. She does not recognise the physiotherapist. Her observations are unchanged. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Continue the planned exercises, since activity should tire her and settle the agitation by this evening", "B": "Calmly reorientate her, acknowledge her worry about the cat, and ask nursing staff about supervision", "C": "Fit a lap table to keep her seated until she settles, observing from the nurses' station", "D": "Explain firmly that she is in hospital and cannot leave, repeating this until she accepts it"}, "cluster_id": "CHS5", "cluster_text": "Mrs Anastasia Galanis, 82, is on day three of a medical ward admission for a urinary tract infection and dehydration. She is widowed, lives alone in a two-storey home and was independent with a walking stick before admission. Since last night she has been intermittently confused, mistook a nurse for her daughter, and fluctuates between agitation and drowsiness. The nursing delirium screen is positive. Medications: paracetamol and an antihypertensive; no benzodiazepines. Observations: temperature 37.4, HR 92 regular, BP 128/72 sitting, RR 18, SpO2 95% on room air. Her glasses and hearing aids are in her locker. The medical team is treating the infection. Her daughter wants to know how to prevent future falls and readmission.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Calmly reorientate her, acknowledge her worry about the cat, and ask nursing staff about supervision): Calm reorientation with validation of her concern is the least distressing way to manage agitation in delirium, and nursing staff can arrange supervision and look at triggers such as pain or needing the toilet. Repeated confrontation raises distress. Physical restraint with a lap table is a restrictive practice, and pushing exercise on an agitated, confused patient risks a fall."}, {"id": 981, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "During a later supervised session Mr Okafor reports his stomach sensation, then stops responding, stares and makes lip-smacking movements. He is standing near equipment. He is not falling but is wandering slowly toward the weights area. Which first aid action is correct for this type of seizure?", "options": {"A": "Leave him in a quiet room to recover alone and check on him again shortly", "B": "Lay him on his side and place a towel between his teeth to protect his tongue", "C": "Stay with him, guide him from hazards without restraint, and speak calmly until he recovers", "D": "Hold him firmly in place to stop wandering, and ask him questions to bring him round"}, "cluster_id": "CHS6", "cluster_text": "Mr Thomas Okafor, 38, IT support officer, has epilepsy (focal impaired awareness seizures, occasionally progressing to tonic-clonic) since a traumatic brain injury three years ago. His last seizure was four months ago. He takes antiseizure medication twice daily. He describes an aura of a rising stomach sensation and a feeling of deja vu. Known triggers: poor sleep and heavy alcohol use. He has mild memory difficulty but manages his own affairs. Neurological examination is normal. BMI 31, resting heart rate 78, blood pressure 124/78 mmHg. Goals: return to gym training and lap swimming at a local aquatic centre. Setting: hospital outpatient exercise clinic. He has agreed verbally that the clinic may write a short letter to the gym about seizure first aid. The letter has not yet been sent.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stay with him, guide him from hazards without restraint, and speak calmly until he recovers): During a focal impaired awareness seizure the person cannot respond, so the aim is to keep them safe by guiding them away from danger and staying until awareness returns. Restraint can cause injury or aggression, and nothing should be placed in the mouth. Leaving him alone removes the observation needed if the seizure spreads."}, {"id": 982, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Okafor has been seizure free for four months and wants to start lap swimming twice weekly alongside gym sessions. His memory difficulty means he sometimes forgets advice. He asks whether exercise itself is safe. Which exercise and safety advice is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Swim alone during quieter periods in a shallow lane, since fewer swimmers means less collision risk", "B": "Swim with a friend in open water, as four months seizure free shows good control now", "C": "Advise avoiding swimming and strenuous exercise, since raised heart rate and fatigue lower the seizure threshold", "D": "Encourage regular exercise, with swimming only in a supervised pool where staff know his condition"}, "cluster_id": "CHS6", "cluster_text": "Mr Thomas Okafor, 38, IT support officer, has epilepsy (focal impaired awareness seizures, occasionally progressing to tonic-clonic) since a traumatic brain injury three years ago. His last seizure was four months ago. He takes antiseizure medication twice daily. He describes an aura of a rising stomach sensation and a feeling of deja vu. Known triggers: poor sleep and heavy alcohol use. He has mild memory difficulty but manages his own affairs. Neurological examination is normal. BMI 31, resting heart rate 78, blood pressure 124/78 mmHg. Goals: return to gym training and lap swimming at a local aquatic centre. Setting: hospital outpatient exercise clinic. He has agreed verbally that the clinic may write a short letter to the gym about seizure first aid. The letter has not yet been sent.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Encourage regular exercise, with swimming only in a supervised pool where staff know his condition): Regular exercise is generally safe and beneficial in epilepsy, but a seizure in water can be fatal, so swimming needs reliable supervision from people who know what to do. Blanket avoidance of exercise is outdated. A recent seizure-free period does not remove the risk, and swimming alone or in open water removes the protection he needs."}, {"id": 983, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Twenty minutes after the seizure Mr Okafor is drowsy and repeats the same questions. He insists he is fine, refuses a medical review and says he will drive home. The physiotherapist considers his decision-making capacity. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Ask him to sign a form acknowledging the driving risks, then let him leave with advice", "B": "Stay with him until orientated, advise against driving, and revisit the decision once he recovers", "C": "Ask his emergency contact to consent on his behalf to medical review, since he cannot decide", "D": "Accept his decision as an autonomous adult and let him leave once he can stand steadily"}, "cluster_id": "CHS6", "cluster_text": "Mr Thomas Okafor, 38, IT support officer, has epilepsy (focal impaired awareness seizures, occasionally progressing to tonic-clonic) since a traumatic brain injury three years ago. His last seizure was four months ago. He takes antiseizure medication twice daily. He describes an aura of a rising stomach sensation and a feeling of deja vu. Known triggers: poor sleep and heavy alcohol use. He has mild memory difficulty but manages his own affairs. Neurological examination is normal. BMI 31, resting heart rate 78, blood pressure 124/78 mmHg. Goals: return to gym training and lap swimming at a local aquatic centre. Setting: hospital outpatient exercise clinic. He has agreed verbally that the clinic may write a short letter to the gym about seizure first aid. The letter has not yet been sent.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stay with him until orientated, advise against driving, and revisit the decision once he recovers): Post-ictal confusion temporarily impairs capacity, so a refusal made now does not reflect a capable choice. The safe course is to stay with him, prevent him driving and ask again when he is orientated. Signing a risk form is not valid consent while confused, and a relative cannot simply consent for him when his capacity is expected to return."}, {"id": 984, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "The gym manager telephones the clinic asking for Mr Okafor's diagnosis, cause of his epilepsy, medications and triggers so the staff can 'manage risk'. Mr Okafor previously agreed only to a short letter about seizure first aid. How should the physiotherapist respond?", "options": {"A": "Share safety-relevant details now and ask Mr Okafor to sign consent retrospectively at his next visit", "B": "Give a summary of his diagnosis and triggers, since the gym has a duty of care", "C": "Pass the details only to the gym's first aid officer, as few staff will see them", "D": "Share only what he agreed to, and record the call and disclosure in his notes"}, "cluster_id": "CHS6", "cluster_text": "Mr Thomas Okafor, 38, IT support officer, has epilepsy (focal impaired awareness seizures, occasionally progressing to tonic-clonic) since a traumatic brain injury three years ago. His last seizure was four months ago. He takes antiseizure medication twice daily. He describes an aura of a rising stomach sensation and a feeling of deja vu. Known triggers: poor sleep and heavy alcohol use. He has mild memory difficulty but manages his own affairs. Neurological examination is normal. BMI 31, resting heart rate 78, blood pressure 124/78 mmHg. Goals: return to gym training and lap swimming at a local aquatic centre. Setting: hospital outpatient exercise clinic. He has agreed verbally that the clinic may write a short letter to the gym about seizure first aid. The letter has not yet been sent.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Share only what he agreed to, and record the call and disclosure in his notes): Disclosure of health information needs the patient's consent for the specific information and recipient, and he agreed only to first aid guidance. Wider sharing, even to a limited audience or with consent sought afterwards, breaches that consent. Documenting the call keeps a record of what was and was not shared, and he can decide whether to release more."}, {"id": 985, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Margaret's pain eases when she lies down and her neurological screen is normal, but she is anxious and has stopped walking. You are planning the first exercise programme in the outpatient clinic to protect the healing T12 fracture while addressing her kyphosis and poor balance. Which approach is most appropriate at this stage?", "options": {"A": "Bed rest in a rigid brace until imaging confirms union, then begin active exercise", "B": "Gentle extensor and postural strengthening with balance practice, avoiding loaded flexion and twisting", "C": "Abdominal curl-ups and trunk flexion stretches to ease pain, with balance work added later", "D": "Impact loading such as jumping and heavy resisted lifting, graded weekly, to stimulate bone density"}, "cluster_id": "CHS7", "cluster_text": "Margaret Hallett, 74, retired primary school teacher, lives alone in a two-storey townhouse. Three weeks ago she felt sudden mid-back pain lifting a laundry basket. X-ray showed an acute T12 vertebral compression fracture with about 25 percent height loss. Femoral neck DEXA T-score was minus 3.1 and her GP has started osteoporosis treatment. She takes no anticoagulants. Pain is 6/10 on standing and 2/10 lying down. Observation: marked thoracic kyphosis and 3 cm height loss over ten years. Neurological screen is normal: lower limb power 5/5, sensation intact, reflexes normal. Timed Up and Go 14 seconds, single-leg stance 3 seconds on each side. She had one fall last year. She is anxious about moving and has stopped her weekly walk. She attends the outpatient physiotherapy clinic hoping to walk to the local shops and return to gardening.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Gentle extensor and postural strengthening with balance practice, avoiding loaded flexion and twisting): Extensor and postural work with balance training suits a recent osteoporotic fracture because it builds support without compressing the anterior vertebral body. Flexion loading and twisting raise the risk of further wedging, which is why curl-ups fail here. Prolonged bed rest and a rigid brace accelerate deconditioning and bone loss, and heavy impact is too aggressive this early."}, {"id": 986, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Her Timed Up and Go is 14 seconds, single-leg stance is 3 seconds on each side, and she had a fall last year. She lives alone in a two-storey townhouse and walks little now. Which plan best addresses her risk of a further fall, and therefore a further fracture?", "options": {"A": "Progressive balance and leg strengthening, plus a home hazard review via occupational therapy referral", "B": "Vitamin D and calcium counselling with a dietitian referral, since nutrition is the main modifiable factor", "C": "Hip protectors and an alert pendant as the main strategy, deferring exercise until healing", "D": "Advice to stay on one floor and avoid stairs, with exercise limited to gentle walking"}, "cluster_id": "CHS7", "cluster_text": "Margaret Hallett, 74, retired primary school teacher, lives alone in a two-storey townhouse. Three weeks ago she felt sudden mid-back pain lifting a laundry basket. X-ray showed an acute T12 vertebral compression fracture with about 25 percent height loss. Femoral neck DEXA T-score was minus 3.1 and her GP has started osteoporosis treatment. She takes no anticoagulants. Pain is 6/10 on standing and 2/10 lying down. Observation: marked thoracic kyphosis and 3 cm height loss over ten years. Neurological screen is normal: lower limb power 5/5, sensation intact, reflexes normal. Timed Up and Go 14 seconds, single-leg stance 3 seconds on each side. She had one fall last year. She is anxious about moving and has stopped her weekly walk. She attends the outpatient physiotherapy clinic hoping to walk to the local shops and return to gardening.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Progressive balance and leg strengthening, plus a home hazard review via occupational therapy referral): Falls prevention in an older adult with poor balance is best achieved with progressive balance and strength training combined with a home environment review. Passive protective measures and restricting activity reduce independence and let strength and balance decline further. Nutrition matters for bone health but does not directly correct her measured balance deficits."}, {"id": 987, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Two weeks later, after a coughing fit, Margaret reports new heaviness in both legs and difficulty starting to pass urine. Her back pain has not changed. She wants to keep exercising because she feels the programme is helping. What should the physiotherapist do now?", "options": {"A": "Stop exercise and send her to the emergency department urgently, as neural compromise is possible", "B": "Stop exercise, repeat a neurological screen in clinic and refer on only if power is reduced", "C": "Stop exercise and book a GP appointment today for assessment of her bladder and leg symptoms", "D": "Stop exercise, advise rest and avoid coughing strain, and arrange urgent outpatient imaging through the GP"}, "cluster_id": "CHS7", "cluster_text": "Margaret Hallett, 74, retired primary school teacher, lives alone in a two-storey townhouse. Three weeks ago she felt sudden mid-back pain lifting a laundry basket. X-ray showed an acute T12 vertebral compression fracture with about 25 percent height loss. Femoral neck DEXA T-score was minus 3.1 and her GP has started osteoporosis treatment. She takes no anticoagulants. Pain is 6/10 on standing and 2/10 lying down. Observation: marked thoracic kyphosis and 3 cm height loss over ten years. Neurological screen is normal: lower limb power 5/5, sensation intact, reflexes normal. Timed Up and Go 14 seconds, single-leg stance 3 seconds on each side. She had one fall last year. She is anxious about moving and has stopped her weekly walk. She attends the outpatient physiotherapy clinic hoping to walk to the local shops and return to gardening.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stop exercise and send her to the emergency department urgently, as neural compromise is possible): New bilateral leg symptoms with bladder disturbance after a vertebral fracture may signal spinal cord or cauda equina compromise, which needs emergency assessment because delay risks permanent deficit. Repeating a clinic screen and waiting for a power change, or relying on a GP appointment or outpatient scan, all add delay without changing the need for emergency imaging and specialist review. Stopping exercise alone does not address the red flags."}, {"id": 988, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "During a standing balance task with close guarding, Margaret catches her foot on the edge of an exercise mat and slides to the floor. She reports a sharp new pain at the fracture site. Medical review is arranged. What is the most appropriate next step regarding the incident?", "options": {"A": "Tell the manager verbally and apologise to her, skipping written records that imply clinician fault", "B": "Wait for the imaging result before deciding whether the event needs a report or disclosure", "C": "Record it in her notes only, as incident reports are reserved for injuries confirmed on imaging", "D": "Lodge an incident report, record the event factually, and openly discuss it with Margaret"}, "cluster_id": "CHS7", "cluster_text": "Margaret Hallett, 74, retired primary school teacher, lives alone in a two-storey townhouse. Three weeks ago she felt sudden mid-back pain lifting a laundry basket. X-ray showed an acute T12 vertebral compression fracture with about 25 percent height loss. Femoral neck DEXA T-score was minus 3.1 and her GP has started osteoporosis treatment. She takes no anticoagulants. Pain is 6/10 on standing and 2/10 lying down. Observation: marked thoracic kyphosis and 3 cm height loss over ten years. Neurological screen is normal: lower limb power 5/5, sensation intact, reflexes normal. Timed Up and Go 14 seconds, single-leg stance 3 seconds on each side. She had one fall last year. She is anxious about moving and has stopped her weekly walk. She attends the outpatient physiotherapy clinic hoping to walk to the local shops and return to gardening.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Lodge an incident report, record the event factually, and openly discuss it with Margaret): Incidents are reported when they occur, not only when harm is confirmed, so the service can learn from them and manage risk. Factual documentation and open disclosure to the patient are expected professional practice. Delaying until imaging or avoiding written records undermines transparency and the quality improvement process."}, {"id": 989, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Brandon has had two weeks of morning headaches, vomiting on two mornings, and new drowsiness and irritability, and he says his head hurts now in clinic. He has no fever. What should the physiotherapist do first in response to these findings?", "options": {"A": "Stop treatment and advise Nan to see the GP same day for headache and vomiting", "B": "Complete a full neurological exam and arrange medical review at the next session if signs persist", "C": "Stop treatment and arrange urgent medical assessment for possible shunt malfunction, telling the family why", "D": "Record a Glasgow Coma Scale, phone the neurosurgery clinic for advice and review him tomorrow"}, "cluster_id": "CHS8", "cluster_text": "Brandon Dunn, 9 years, is a Wiradjuri boy with L4 level myelomeningocele and a ventriculoperitoneal shunt. He walks at home with ankle foot orthoses and forearm crutches and uses a wheelchair at school for long distances. His Nan, who is his legal guardian, attends with an Aboriginal health worker from the local Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service. Nan reports two weeks of morning headaches, vomiting on two mornings, and unusual irritability and sleepiness at school. Findings: knee extension 4/5, ankle dorsiflexion 1/5, sensation reduced below L5. He has no fever. He is drowsy but rousable and says his head hurts. Nan asks that the health worker stay for the session and that the family talk together before agreeing to any changes to Brandon's plan.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop treatment and arrange urgent medical assessment for possible shunt malfunction, telling the family why): Headache, vomiting and drowsiness in a child with a shunt suggest raised intracranial pressure from possible shunt malfunction, which is an emergency needing same day medical assessment. Sending the family to a GP may not reach the shunt service quickly, and further examination with review at the next session or tomorrow delays care. A baseline score and a phone call without arranging assessment do not protect him."}, {"id": 990, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Two weeks later, after medical care, Brandon returns with a 2 cm red area over his right heel that stays red when pressed. Sensation there is absent, and his ankle foot orthosis was worn all day yesterday. What is the most appropriate management?", "options": {"A": "Apply a firm dressing and keep wearing the orthosis so his walking goals continue", "B": "Massage the area gently each day to improve circulation so the skin tolerates the orthosis again", "C": "Pad the orthosis heel, keep wearing it at school, and recheck the heel next visit", "D": "Stop orthosis use on that foot, offload the heel, and arrange an orthotist review"}, "cluster_id": "CHS8", "cluster_text": "Brandon Dunn, 9 years, is a Wiradjuri boy with L4 level myelomeningocele and a ventriculoperitoneal shunt. He walks at home with ankle foot orthoses and forearm crutches and uses a wheelchair at school for long distances. His Nan, who is his legal guardian, attends with an Aboriginal health worker from the local Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service. Nan reports two weeks of morning headaches, vomiting on two mornings, and unusual irritability and sleepiness at school. Findings: knee extension 4/5, ankle dorsiflexion 1/5, sensation reduced below L5. He has no fever. He is drowsy but rousable and says his head hurts. Nan asks that the health worker stay for the session and that the family talk together before agreeing to any changes to Brandon's plan.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stop orthosis use on that foot, offload the heel, and arrange an orthotist review): A non-blanching red area over an insensate heel is early pressure injury, and further loading under the orthosis will worsen it. Removing pressure and involving the orthotist for fit addresses the cause. Massage can damage compromised tissue, and padding or dressings under continued use keep the pressure in place."}, {"id": 991, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Nan asks what walking Brandon can realistically expect in the longer term given his L4 motor level, because the family is planning for high school. He currently uses forearm crutches at home. Which statement best reflects the typical outlook for this level of lesion?", "options": {"A": "Independent community walking with ankle foot orthoses alone, as quadriceps and hip strength are well preserved", "B": "Household walking only with knee ankle foot orthoses and crutches, using a wheelchair in the community", "C": "Household and some community walking with orthoses and crutches, using a wheelchair for longer distances", "D": "Therapeutic standing and short exercise walking only, with a wheelchair as main mobility for school"}, "cluster_id": "CHS8", "cluster_text": "Brandon Dunn, 9 years, is a Wiradjuri boy with L4 level myelomeningocele and a ventriculoperitoneal shunt. He walks at home with ankle foot orthoses and forearm crutches and uses a wheelchair at school for long distances. His Nan, who is his legal guardian, attends with an Aboriginal health worker from the local Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service. Nan reports two weeks of morning headaches, vomiting on two mornings, and unusual irritability and sleepiness at school. Findings: knee extension 4/5, ankle dorsiflexion 1/5, sensation reduced below L5. He has no fever. He is drowsy but rousable and says his head hurts. Nan asks that the health worker stay for the session and that the family talk together before agreeing to any changes to Brandon's plan.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Household and some community walking with orthoses and crutches, using a wheelchair for longer distances): With an L4 level, hip flexors and quadriceps are present but ankle control is limited, so ankle foot orthoses and crutches usually allow household and some community walking, with a wheelchair for distance. Walking independently with orthoses alone fits a lower lesion with stronger ankle muscles. Knee ankle foot orthoses and wheelchair use in the community describe a higher lesion, and exercise walking only suits a much higher lesion with little leg function."}, {"id": 992, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "During the session Nan asks that the Aboriginal health worker stay, and that the family discuss the plan together before agreeing to it. Nan is Brandon's legal guardian. The clinic usually uses a short written plan. What is the most culturally safe and appropriate response by the physiotherapist?", "options": {"A": "Welcome the health worker, explain the plan, and take Nan's consent before she leaves today", "B": "Welcome the health worker, explain the plan plainly, and give the family time to discuss", "C": "Welcome the health worker, explain the plan, and let the worker confirm which goals to keep", "D": "Welcome the health worker, but complete the usual written plan first and invite family discussion afterwards"}, "cluster_id": "CHS8", "cluster_text": "Brandon Dunn, 9 years, is a Wiradjuri boy with L4 level myelomeningocele and a ventriculoperitoneal shunt. He walks at home with ankle foot orthoses and forearm crutches and uses a wheelchair at school for long distances. His Nan, who is his legal guardian, attends with an Aboriginal health worker from the local Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service. Nan reports two weeks of morning headaches, vomiting on two mornings, and unusual irritability and sleepiness at school. Findings: knee extension 4/5, ankle dorsiflexion 1/5, sensation reduced below L5. He has no fever. He is drowsy but rousable and says his head hurts. Nan asks that the health worker stay for the session and that the family talk together before agreeing to any changes to Brandon's plan.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Welcome the health worker, explain the plan plainly, and give the family time to discuss): Culturally safe practice welcomes the family's chosen supports and respects collective decision making, while consent stays with the legal guardian once she has had the time she needs. Taking consent before she leaves, or finishing the written plan first and discussing afterwards, ignores her request to talk together before agreeing. Letting the worker decide which goals to keep misplaces responsibility, which stays with Nan as guardian."}, {"id": 993, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "This is Ms Chopra's first video consultation and you cannot palpate her back. She has a laptop camera, a clear space to stand and has been told how video assessment differs from a face to face visit. Which approach to the physical assessment is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Observe movement on camera, then repeat the assessment face to face before giving any advice", "B": "Ask her to self palpate her spine and report tenderness, using her findings to guide diagnosis", "C": "Observe active lumbar movements and sit to stand on camera, recording her symptom response", "D": "Use a validated questionnaire and pain diary, adding movement testing only if symptoms worsen"}, "cluster_id": "CHS9", "cluster_text": "Anjali Chopra is a 38 year old accountant working from home who attends by video from her kitchen. Three weeks ago she developed central low back pain after lifting boxes during a house move. Pain is 5/10, worse after sitting for 30 minutes and easing with walking. She has no leg symptoms, night pain, bladder or bowel change, saddle numbness, fever, weight loss or history of cancer. She sleeps well and is otherwise healthy. A screening tool suggests medium risk of persistent disability. Her GP has asked for a brief update, and her employer's rehabilitation provider has requested a workplace report. She wants to know whether she needs an MRI, as a friend with back pain had one.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Observe active lumbar movements and sit to stand on camera, recording her symptom response): Observed active movement and functional tasks with her reported symptom response provide useful, safe information by video. Questionnaires and diaries add context but leave out the movement assessment she can safely do, and waiting for a face to face visit before advice wastes the session. Self palpation is unreliable and does not replace observing movement, so it does not guide her management."}, {"id": 994, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Ms Chopra asks whether she needs an MRI because a friend with similar pain had one. She has no red flags, she has been walking daily, and her pain is improving slowly over the last week. How should you respond to her question in this session?", "options": {"A": "Agree to request an MRI through her GP so structural causes are ruled out early", "B": "Explain imaging is not needed without red flags and offer reassurance with an active plan", "C": "Recommend a plain X-ray first, as it shows disc and joint changes behind her pain", "D": "Suggest waiting six weeks and requesting an MRI if the pain has not resolved by then"}, "cluster_id": "CHS9", "cluster_text": "Anjali Chopra is a 38 year old accountant working from home who attends by video from her kitchen. Three weeks ago she developed central low back pain after lifting boxes during a house move. Pain is 5/10, worse after sitting for 30 minutes and easing with walking. She has no leg symptoms, night pain, bladder or bowel change, saddle numbness, fever, weight loss or history of cancer. She sleeps well and is otherwise healthy. A screening tool suggests medium risk of persistent disability. Her GP has asked for a brief update, and her employer's rehabilitation provider has requested a workplace report. She wants to know whether she needs an MRI, as a friend with back pain had one.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Explain imaging is not needed without red flags and offer reassurance with an active plan): In non specific low back pain without red flags, imaging seldom changes management and can increase worry and unnecessary treatment. Clear explanation with an active plan meets her concern. Delayed or substitute imaging still exposes her to findings that are common in people without pain."}, {"id": 995, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "In a follow up video session, Ms Chopra reports that since yesterday she has had numbness around her inner thighs and buttocks, and difficulty starting to urinate. Her back pain is otherwise unchanged from last week. What should you do now?", "options": {"A": "Teach positions of comfort and review again by video in two days if symptoms persist", "B": "Message her GP urgently with the findings and ask for an appointment this week", "C": "Advise immediate attendance at the nearest emergency department and notify them of your concerns", "D": "Arrange a face to face physiotherapy review tomorrow to test lower limb reflexes and strength"}, "cluster_id": "CHS9", "cluster_text": "Anjali Chopra is a 38 year old accountant working from home who attends by video from her kitchen. Three weeks ago she developed central low back pain after lifting boxes during a house move. Pain is 5/10, worse after sitting for 30 minutes and easing with walking. She has no leg symptoms, night pain, bladder or bowel change, saddle numbness, fever, weight loss or history of cancer. She sleeps well and is otherwise healthy. A screening tool suggests medium risk of persistent disability. Her GP has asked for a brief update, and her employer's rehabilitation provider has requested a workplace report. She wants to know whether she needs an MRI, as a friend with back pain had one.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Advise immediate attendance at the nearest emergency department and notify them of your concerns): Saddle numbness with urinary difficulty suggests possible cauda equina compression, which needs emergency assessment because delay risks permanent loss of function. Waiting for a physiotherapy review or a GP appointment is too slow, and monitoring by video is unsafe with these new symptoms."}, {"id": 996, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Her GP has asked for a brief update after the first video session, and Ms Chopra has consented to sharing her information. You will send a written note to the practice today, before her next appointment. Which content makes the most appropriate handover?", "options": {"A": "Key findings and working diagnosis, with a request that the GP arrange imaging first", "B": "Key findings, red flag screening result, working diagnosis, management plan and review timeframe", "C": "A summary of the plan and expected recovery, with examination findings kept in the clinic record", "D": "The full session notes and scores, with a request that the GP summarise what matters"}, "cluster_id": "CHS9", "cluster_text": "Anjali Chopra is a 38 year old accountant working from home who attends by video from her kitchen. Three weeks ago she developed central low back pain after lifting boxes during a house move. Pain is 5/10, worse after sitting for 30 minutes and easing with walking. She has no leg symptoms, night pain, bladder or bowel change, saddle numbness, fever, weight loss or history of cancer. She sleeps well and is otherwise healthy. A screening tool suggests medium risk of persistent disability. Her GP has asked for a brief update, and her employer's rehabilitation provider has requested a workplace report. She wants to know whether she needs an MRI, as a friend with back pain had one.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Key findings, red flag screening result, working diagnosis, management plan and review timeframe): A useful handover is concise and structured, giving the receiver what they need to act: findings, whether red flags were screened, your reasoning, the plan and when you will reassess. Asking for imaging first is not supported when there are no red flags. A plan summary without the findings and screening result leaves the GP unable to judge the reasoning, and raw notes bury the message and shift the work to the receiver."}, {"id": 997, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "The nurse has flagged a swollen, tender left calf on day 3 after knee replacement. Mr Tran has hypertension, type 2 diabetes and a smoking history. Which feature of his history most strongly increases his risk of deep vein thrombosis at this stage?", "options": {"A": "His age of 74, which raises thrombosis risk more than surgery and reduced mobility do", "B": "Type 2 diabetes, which increases clotting tendency and is the main cause of postoperative leg thrombosis", "C": "Recent major lower limb joint surgery, with reduced mobility in the early postoperative days", "D": "Past smoking, which continues to raise clotting risk strongly for many years after quitting"}, "cluster_id": "CHS10", "cluster_text": "Mr Minh Tran, 74, retired tailor, is day 3 after a right total knee replacement. History: hypertension, type 2 diabetes, previous smoker. Prophylactic anticoagulant is charted. This morning the nurse noted a swollen, tender left calf and wrote 'hold mobilisation, awaiting medical review' on the whiteboard. Findings: left calf circumference larger than the right, skin warm, pitting oedema to the ankle, tenderness on palpation of the calf. Observations: HR 96, BP 134/82, RR 18, SpO2 96% on room air, temperature 37.4. The right knee wound is clean and flexion is 70 degrees. He is anxious and asks to get up to the chair. The nurse also says an allied health assistant walked him to the bathroom this morning despite the whiteboard note, because the note was not visible from the doorway. No harm is apparent.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Recent major lower limb joint surgery, with reduced mobility in the early postoperative days): Major lower limb orthopaedic surgery with reduced mobility is one of the strongest recognised risk factors for venous thrombosis, and it is the feature most specific to the first days after joint replacement. Age, diabetes and past smoking each add some risk but do not outweigh recent surgery and immobility, and the smoking risk falls after quitting."}, {"id": 998, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Tran asks to get up to the chair. His left calf is swollen and tender and the medical review has not yet taken place. What is the most appropriate action regarding his mobilisation at this point, given the swollen calf and the pending review?", "options": {"A": "Perform a calf squeeze test and Homans sign to confirm the diagnosis before deciding on mobilisation", "B": "Keep him in bed and ask the medical team to review him before further mobilisation", "C": "Mobilise him a short distance with the frame, watching for chest symptoms throughout", "D": "Fit a compression stocking on the left leg and transfer him to the chair with assistance"}, "cluster_id": "CHS10", "cluster_text": "Mr Minh Tran, 74, retired tailor, is day 3 after a right total knee replacement. History: hypertension, type 2 diabetes, previous smoker. Prophylactic anticoagulant is charted. This morning the nurse noted a swollen, tender left calf and wrote 'hold mobilisation, awaiting medical review' on the whiteboard. Findings: left calf circumference larger than the right, skin warm, pitting oedema to the ankle, tenderness on palpation of the calf. Observations: HR 96, BP 134/82, RR 18, SpO2 96% on room air, temperature 37.4. The right knee wound is clean and flexion is 70 degrees. He is anxious and asks to get up to the chair. The nurse also says an allied health assistant walked him to the bathroom this morning despite the whiteboard note, because the note was not visible from the doorway. No harm is apparent.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Keep him in bed and ask the medical team to review him before further mobilisation): With a clinically suspected deep vein thrombosis, mobilisation should wait until a doctor has assessed him and imaging or treatment decisions are made, because activity may dislodge a clot. Calf squeezing and Homans sign are unreliable, outdated, and may disturb a clot. A stocking and a short walk both load the limb before it has been cleared."}, {"id": 999, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Later that day, before medical review, Mr Tran reports sudden sharp chest pain on breathing in and feels short of breath. HR is 118, RR 28 and SpO2 89% on room air. He is sitting on the edge of the bed. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Help him walk slowly around the bed to improve his oxygenation, with a nurse supporting him", "B": "Lay him flat with his legs raised and ask the nurse about pain relief first", "C": "Coach slow diaphragmatic breathing and reassess saturation after a few minutes before involving the team", "D": "Keep him still, sit him upright, and activate the ward emergency response"}, "cluster_id": "CHS10", "cluster_text": "Mr Minh Tran, 74, retired tailor, is day 3 after a right total knee replacement. History: hypertension, type 2 diabetes, previous smoker. Prophylactic anticoagulant is charted. This morning the nurse noted a swollen, tender left calf and wrote 'hold mobilisation, awaiting medical review' on the whiteboard. Findings: left calf circumference larger than the right, skin warm, pitting oedema to the ankle, tenderness on palpation of the calf. Observations: HR 96, BP 134/82, RR 18, SpO2 96% on room air, temperature 37.4. The right knee wound is clean and flexion is 70 degrees. He is anxious and asks to get up to the chair. The nurse also says an allied health assistant walked him to the bathroom this morning despite the whiteboard note, because the note was not visible from the doorway. No harm is apparent.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Keep him still, sit him upright, and activate the ward emergency response): Sudden pleuritic pain, breathlessness, tachycardia and desaturation in a patient with a suspected clot raise the possibility of pulmonary embolism, which is an emergency. He should stay still and upright, and help must be called immediately. Breathing exercises delay escalation, walking increases risk, and lying flat worsens breathlessness while pain relief is not the priority."}, {"id": 1000, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "The assistant walked Mr Tran to the bathroom this morning despite the hold note, because the note was not visible from the doorway. Once he has been transferred for urgent medical care, what is the most appropriate professional response to that event?", "options": {"A": "Speak privately with the assistant about the lapse and record the conversation in the patient's notes", "B": "Enter it in the incident reporting system and inform the nurse in charge about the gap", "C": "Record the walk in the clinical notes only, as no harm occurred to the patient", "D": "Wait for the medical team to decide whether the walk contributed to his deterioration before reporting"}, "cluster_id": "CHS10", "cluster_text": "Mr Minh Tran, 74, retired tailor, is day 3 after a right total knee replacement. History: hypertension, type 2 diabetes, previous smoker. Prophylactic anticoagulant is charted. This morning the nurse noted a swollen, tender left calf and wrote 'hold mobilisation, awaiting medical review' on the whiteboard. Findings: left calf circumference larger than the right, skin warm, pitting oedema to the ankle, tenderness on palpation of the calf. Observations: HR 96, BP 134/82, RR 18, SpO2 96% on room air, temperature 37.4. The right knee wound is clean and flexion is 70 degrees. He is anxious and asks to get up to the chair. The nurse also says an allied health assistant walked him to the bathroom this morning despite the whiteboard note, because the note was not visible from the doorway. No harm is apparent.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Enter it in the incident reporting system and inform the nurse in charge about the gap): A mobility hold that was not communicated effectively is a system issue and a reportable event whether or not harm followed, and reporting allows the ward to fix how holds are displayed. A private conversation or a note alone does not trigger review. Waiting for a causal decision delays reporting, which is not conditional on harm."}, {"id": 1001, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Colin's resting SpO2 is normal but falls to 84% during the walk test, with crackles and clubbing. His spirometry, reviewed by the physician, shows a restrictive pattern with no significant airflow limitation. Which mechanism best explains this exertional desaturation in interstitial lung disease?", "options": {"A": "Thickened alveolar walls impair diffusion, and faster capillary transit during exercise shortens oxygen transfer time", "B": "A small pleural effusion compresses the bases, causing shunt that worsens as cardiac output rises", "C": "Dynamic airway collapse in expiration traps air, raising dead space and lowering alveolar ventilation during exertion", "D": "Weak respiratory muscles cause hypoventilation, and rising carbon dioxide displaces alveolar oxygen during sustained exertion"}, "cluster_id": "CHS11", "cluster_text": "Colin Yarran, 61, is a Noongar man who retired from fencing work. He has idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis diagnosed 18 months ago and is referred by his respiratory physician to a hospital outpatient pulmonary rehabilitation programme. Ex-smoker. Observations at rest: SpO2 95% on room air, HR 84, RR 18, BP 128/76. Fine bilateral basal crackles, finger clubbing, no wheeze. Six minute walk test: 310 m, SpO2 fell to 84% by minute 4, peak HR 118, Borg dyspnoea 6/10 and leg fatigue 3/10 at the end. He travelled 90 minutes with his niece, who waits in the car park. He says he would rather exercise at home and is quiet when asked about the group class. The hospital employs an Aboriginal Health Worker. His wife cannot attend sessions because of her own heart condition.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Thickened alveolar walls impair diffusion, and faster capillary transit during exercise shortens oxygen transfer time): In fibrotic disease the thickened alveolar-capillary membrane slows oxygen diffusion, and exercise shortens red cell transit time so equilibration fails. Spirometry shows restriction rather than airflow limitation, so air trapping does not explain the fall. Respiratory muscle weakness would not usually cause desaturation alongside a normal resting saturation."}, {"id": 1002, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Colin's desaturation to 84% occurs at a moderate walking pace. Medical review is booked for next week and he has no home oxygen. In the first few supervised sessions, with continuous SpO2 monitoring available, what is the most appropriate exercise approach?", "options": {"A": "Continuous walking to tolerance, because training intensity drives gains and saturation is not a limiting factor", "B": "Low-intensity stretching and breathing exercises only, avoiding walking until the physician prescribes exertional oxygen", "C": "Interval exercise at moderate dyspnoea with rests, monitoring SpO2 and seeking medical advice on exertional oxygen", "D": "Long slow walks below symptom threshold, checking saturation only at the end of the session"}, "cluster_id": "CHS11", "cluster_text": "Colin Yarran, 61, is a Noongar man who retired from fencing work. He has idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis diagnosed 18 months ago and is referred by his respiratory physician to a hospital outpatient pulmonary rehabilitation programme. Ex-smoker. Observations at rest: SpO2 95% on room air, HR 84, RR 18, BP 128/76. Fine bilateral basal crackles, finger clubbing, no wheeze. Six minute walk test: 310 m, SpO2 fell to 84% by minute 4, peak HR 118, Borg dyspnoea 6/10 and leg fatigue 3/10 at the end. He travelled 90 minutes with his niece, who waits in the car park. He says he would rather exercise at home and is quiet when asked about the group class. The hospital employs an Aboriginal Health Worker. His wife cannot attend sessions because of her own heart condition.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Interval exercise at moderate dyspnoea with rests, monitoring SpO2 and seeking medical advice on exertional oxygen): Interval training lets him accumulate exercise time with less dyspnoea and less desaturation than continuous work, and monitoring shows how far saturation falls. Exertional oxygen needs a medical decision, so the physiotherapist raises it with the physician rather than ignoring it or withholding exercise. Continuous work to tolerance risks prolonged low saturation."}, {"id": 1003, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Colin is quiet about the group class and says he would rather exercise at home. His wife cannot attend because of her own health. The programme coordinator suggests he simply be booked into the Tuesday group. What is the most culturally safe next step?", "options": {"A": "Ask Colin how he wants to participate and offer Aboriginal Health Worker support and flexible options", "B": "Ask the Aboriginal Health Worker to decide his programme format and arrange attendance on his behalf", "C": "Give a written home programme and discharge him, documenting that he declined the group class offered", "D": "Book him into the group because peer support improves outcomes, and review attendance after several sessions"}, "cluster_id": "CHS11", "cluster_text": "Colin Yarran, 61, is a Noongar man who retired from fencing work. He has idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis diagnosed 18 months ago and is referred by his respiratory physician to a hospital outpatient pulmonary rehabilitation programme. Ex-smoker. Observations at rest: SpO2 95% on room air, HR 84, RR 18, BP 128/76. Fine bilateral basal crackles, finger clubbing, no wheeze. Six minute walk test: 310 m, SpO2 fell to 84% by minute 4, peak HR 118, Borg dyspnoea 6/10 and leg fatigue 3/10 at the end. He travelled 90 minutes with his niece, who waits in the car park. He says he would rather exercise at home and is quiet when asked about the group class. The hospital employs an Aboriginal Health Worker. His wife cannot attend sessions because of her own heart condition.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ask Colin how he wants to participate and offer Aboriginal Health Worker support and flexible options): Culturally safe practice starts with asking the person what he wants and offering supports he chooses, such as the Aboriginal Health Worker and flexible delivery. Booking the group without his input, or deciding for him, removes his choice. Discharging after a first hesitation labels him non-adherent when the barrier may simply be the format."}, {"id": 1004, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "In session two, mid-interval Colin reports chest tightness and looks pale. His SpO2 reads 81%, HR 125, and dyspnoea is 7/10. He can speak only in short phrases and is still on his feet beside the treadmill. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Slow the treadmill to a gentle cool down pace and recheck saturation while he continues walking", "B": "Let him finish the interval while breathing slowly, then stop and document the saturation reading", "C": "Stop, seat him leaning forward with support, coach pursed-lip breathing and recheck saturation until it recovers", "D": "Lie him flat, apply oxygen by mask and then phone the respiratory physician for further advice"}, "cluster_id": "CHS11", "cluster_text": "Colin Yarran, 61, is a Noongar man who retired from fencing work. He has idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis diagnosed 18 months ago and is referred by his respiratory physician to a hospital outpatient pulmonary rehabilitation programme. Ex-smoker. Observations at rest: SpO2 95% on room air, HR 84, RR 18, BP 128/76. Fine bilateral basal crackles, finger clubbing, no wheeze. Six minute walk test: 310 m, SpO2 fell to 84% by minute 4, peak HR 118, Borg dyspnoea 6/10 and leg fatigue 3/10 at the end. He travelled 90 minutes with his niece, who waits in the car park. He says he would rather exercise at home and is quiet when asked about the group class. The hospital employs an Aboriginal Health Worker. His wife cannot attend sessions because of her own heart condition.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop, seat him leaning forward with support, coach pursed-lip breathing and recheck saturation until it recovers): A fall to 81% with chest tightness, pallor and a rising heart rate is a signal to stop. Sitting forward with pursed-lip breathing reduces the work of breathing while saturation recovers, and ongoing monitoring tells you whether medical help is needed. Cooling down on his feet or finishing the interval keeps him working while desaturated, and lying flat tends to worsen breathlessness."}, {"id": 1005, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "During a class game, Mai has a generalised tonic-clonic seizure. She falls to the floor and convulses while classmates gather. The physiotherapist is standing nearby, and the school action plan lists standard first aid for this type of seizure. What is the most appropriate immediate response?", "options": {"A": "Note the time, clear hard objects, cushion her head, and call an ambulance immediately", "B": "Note the time, clear hard objects, cushion her head, and offer water when she wakes", "C": "Note the time, move classmates away, cushion her head, and keep her flat on her back", "D": "Note the time, clear hard objects, cushion her head, and turn her onto her side afterwards"}, "cluster_id": "CHS12", "cluster_text": "Mai Ngo, 9 years, attends her local primary school. She has generalised tonic-clonic epilepsy controlled on antiseizure medication, with her last seizure three months ago during sleep. Her paediatrician has asked for advice on physical activity. Mai feels tired after school and avoids sport because she fears having a seizure in front of classmates. Observations: HR 88 bpm, BP 100/62 mmHg, SpO2 99% on room air. Gross motor screen is typical for age, with mildly reduced endurance (she chose to stop a shuttle walk at level 4, without symptoms). The school holds a written seizure action plan from her neurologist. Mai's mother, Linh, asks how the family can pay for regular physiotherapy and a support worker at swimming, as household income is limited. Mai has no NDIS plan and no GP management plan.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Note the time, clear hard objects, cushion her head, and turn her onto her side afterwards): Protecting her from injury, timing the seizure and positioning her on her side once the convulsing stops keeps her airway clear and gives staff the information the action plan relies on. An ambulance is not needed for a typical seizure unless the plan or its duration calls for it. Offering water before she is fully awake risks choking, and keeping her flat on her back leaves her airway at risk."}, {"id": 1006, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mai's seizures are currently controlled and she wants to join the school swimming carnival. The teacher asks the physiotherapist for water safety advice. Mai's parents are happy for her to take part with sensible precautions. Which recommendation best fits Mai's epilepsy?", "options": {"A": "Limit her to shallow water where she can stand, as a seizure could occur anywhere", "B": "Allow swimming after a rest day, with a buoyancy vest replacing the need for close supervision", "C": "Allow swimming in a normal lane while a pool lifeguard watches everyone from the deck", "D": "Allow swimming with a trained adult close enough to reach and support her quickly"}, "cluster_id": "CHS12", "cluster_text": "Mai Ngo, 9 years, attends her local primary school. She has generalised tonic-clonic epilepsy controlled on antiseizure medication, with her last seizure three months ago during sleep. Her paediatrician has asked for advice on physical activity. Mai feels tired after school and avoids sport because she fears having a seizure in front of classmates. Observations: HR 88 bpm, BP 100/62 mmHg, SpO2 99% on room air. Gross motor screen is typical for age, with mildly reduced endurance (she chose to stop a shuttle walk at level 4, without symptoms). The school holds a written seizure action plan from her neurologist. Mai's mother, Linh, asks how the family can pay for regular physiotherapy and a support worker at swimming, as household income is limited. Mai has no NDIS plan and no GP management plan.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Allow swimming with a trained adult close enough to reach and support her quickly): A seizure in water can lead to drowning within moments, so a trained adult must be within reach and able to support her face above the water. A lifeguard watching the whole pool or a buoyancy vest does not replace individual supervision, and keeping to shallow water does not remove the risk. Controlled seizures do not rule out taking part."}, {"id": 1007, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "At the next session, during warm-up, Mai smells burning, stares blankly and fiddles with her shirt for about a minute without responding. She then recovers slowly and is drowsy. The action plan lists an ambulance only for seizures over five minutes or injury. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Let her drink water, then restart at lower intensity while watching for further staring episodes", "B": "Stay with her, guide her from hazards, stop exercise, record the event and tell her parents", "C": "Resume the warm-up once she responds, because the event was brief and has settled by itself", "D": "Call an ambulance, as a change in seizure type needs urgent review, and keep her down"}, "cluster_id": "CHS12", "cluster_text": "Mai Ngo, 9 years, attends her local primary school. She has generalised tonic-clonic epilepsy controlled on antiseizure medication, with her last seizure three months ago during sleep. Her paediatrician has asked for advice on physical activity. Mai feels tired after school and avoids sport because she fears having a seizure in front of classmates. Observations: HR 88 bpm, BP 100/62 mmHg, SpO2 99% on room air. Gross motor screen is typical for age, with mildly reduced endurance (she chose to stop a shuttle walk at level 4, without symptoms). The school holds a written seizure action plan from her neurologist. Mai's mother, Linh, asks how the family can pay for regular physiotherapy and a support worker at swimming, as household income is limited. Mai has no NDIS plan and no GP management plan.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stay with her, guide her from hazards, stop exercise, record the event and tell her parents): This looks like a focal seizure with impaired awareness, so she needs supervision until fully orientated, no restraint and no exercise while she recovers. It does not meet the plan's ambulance criteria, but a change in her seizure pattern should be passed to her parents and neurologist. Returning to exercise while drowsy risks a fall or a further seizure."}, {"id": 1008, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Linh asks how the family can afford regular physiotherapy and a support worker at swimming. Mai has no NDIS plan and no GP management plan, and the family has limited income. What advice should the physiotherapist give at this stage now?", "options": {"A": "Advise lodging an NDIS application now, as an epilepsy diagnosis gives automatic eligibility for funded supports", "B": "Tell Linh Medicare covers private physiotherapy for children with epilepsy once a paediatrician refers", "C": "Ask the principal to fund sessions from the school budget, as swimming is a school activity", "D": "Suggest a GP chronic condition management plan, and explain NDIS eligibility depends on lasting functional impact"}, "cluster_id": "CHS12", "cluster_text": "Mai Ngo, 9 years, attends her local primary school. She has generalised tonic-clonic epilepsy controlled on antiseizure medication, with her last seizure three months ago during sleep. Her paediatrician has asked for advice on physical activity. Mai feels tired after school and avoids sport because she fears having a seizure in front of classmates. Observations: HR 88 bpm, BP 100/62 mmHg, SpO2 99% on room air. Gross motor screen is typical for age, with mildly reduced endurance (she chose to stop a shuttle walk at level 4, without symptoms). The school holds a written seizure action plan from her neurologist. Mai's mother, Linh, asks how the family can pay for regular physiotherapy and a support worker at swimming, as household income is limited. Mai has no NDIS plan and no GP management plan.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Suggest a GP chronic condition management plan, and explain NDIS eligibility depends on lasting functional impact): A GP can consider a chronic condition management plan, which allows Medicare rebates for a limited number of allied health visits. NDIS access depends on a lasting disability with significant functional impact, so it cannot be assumed from a diagnosis alone, and the physiotherapist can supply functional evidence if the family applies. A referral does not make private physiotherapy automatically Medicare funded."}, {"id": 1009, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "On examination Jacob's passive ankle dorsiflexion measures 0 degrees with the knee extended but 10 degrees with the knee flexed, on both sides. His toe walking has been increasing over the past term. What does this difference in range most likely indicate about the structures limiting movement?", "options": {"A": "Anterior tibialis weakness, which limits the range available during passive testing of the joint", "B": "Posterior ankle capsule restriction, which would limit dorsiflexion similarly at both knee positions", "C": "Soleus shortening, because this muscle limits dorsiflexion most when the knee is held in extension", "D": "Tight gastrocnemius, a two-joint muscle that is placed on stretch when the knee is extended"}, "cluster_id": "CHS13", "cluster_text": "Jacob Nguyen, 14, year 8 student with bilateral spastic cerebral palsy, GMFCS level II. You are the physiotherapist visiting his secondary school fortnightly under a funded school program. He walks without aids on level ground but uses a manual wheelchair for school excursions and long distances. Observation: bilateral toe walking with intermittent knee flexion in stance, reduced stride length and trunk lean. Passive ankle dorsiflexion: 0 degrees with knee straight, 10 degrees with knee bent. Popliteal angle 55 degrees. Calf pain 3/10 after sport. His mother has given written consent to share functional information with the school. The year coordinator emails you asking for his full diagnosis, scan results and your treatment notes for his school file. He wants to run at the athletics carnival.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Tight gastrocnemius, a two-joint muscle that is placed on stretch when the knee is extended): Gastrocnemius crosses the knee, so extending the knee lengthens it across the ankle and reduces dorsiflexion, while bending the knee slackens it and range improves. Soleus and the posterior capsule would restrict range at both knee positions. Weakness of tibialis anterior does not limit passive range."}, {"id": 1010, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Jacob is keen to run in the school athletics carnival in six weeks and reports calf soreness of 3/10 after sport. He tires on uneven ground, as expected for his GMFCS level. Which advice about his participation and preparation is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Support participation with graded strengthening, stretching and aerobic training, monitoring soreness and fatigue", "B": "Allow participation without extra preparation, since his soreness is mild and adolescents recover quickly from sport", "C": "Limit him to stretching only until the carnival, so that his calf muscles are not overloaded", "D": "Advise withdrawal from running events, as high-intensity activity increases spasticity and risks muscle damage"}, "cluster_id": "CHS13", "cluster_text": "Jacob Nguyen, 14, year 8 student with bilateral spastic cerebral palsy, GMFCS level II. You are the physiotherapist visiting his secondary school fortnightly under a funded school program. He walks without aids on level ground but uses a manual wheelchair for school excursions and long distances. Observation: bilateral toe walking with intermittent knee flexion in stance, reduced stride length and trunk lean. Passive ankle dorsiflexion: 0 degrees with knee straight, 10 degrees with knee bent. Popliteal angle 55 degrees. Calf pain 3/10 after sport. His mother has given written consent to share functional information with the school. The year coordinator emails you asking for his full diagnosis, scan results and your treatment notes for his school file. He wants to run at the athletics carnival.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Support participation with graded strengthening, stretching and aerobic training, monitoring soreness and fatigue): Young people with cerebral palsy benefit from physical activity, and graded strengthening and aerobic training improve capacity while stretching maintains muscle length. Exercise does not increase spasticity, so withdrawal removes a valuable activity. Stretching alone or no preparation would not build the strength and endurance needed."}, {"id": 1011, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "The year coordinator emails asking for Jacob's full diagnosis, scan reports and your treatment notes for his school file. His mother's written consent covers functional information relevant to school participation only. The email arrived from a school address. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Share a functional summary of his school abilities and needs, within the limits of the consent", "B": "Share the diagnosis and scan results, as these are more useful to school than your notes", "C": "Send the full file, because the school has a duty of care and needs complete information", "D": "Refuse to share anything, because health information is protected and cannot be released to schools"}, "cluster_id": "CHS13", "cluster_text": "Jacob Nguyen, 14, year 8 student with bilateral spastic cerebral palsy, GMFCS level II. You are the physiotherapist visiting his secondary school fortnightly under a funded school program. He walks without aids on level ground but uses a manual wheelchair for school excursions and long distances. Observation: bilateral toe walking with intermittent knee flexion in stance, reduced stride length and trunk lean. Passive ankle dorsiflexion: 0 degrees with knee straight, 10 degrees with knee bent. Popliteal angle 55 degrees. Calf pain 3/10 after sport. His mother has given written consent to share functional information with the school. The year coordinator emails you asking for his full diagnosis, scan results and your treatment notes for his school file. He wants to run at the athletics carnival.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Share a functional summary of his school abilities and needs, within the limits of the consent): Information may be disclosed only within the scope of the consent given, so a summary of functional abilities and support needs meets the school's purpose without releasing diagnoses, imaging or notes. Sending the complete record exceeds the consent. Refusing everything ignores the consent that was provided and may disadvantage Jacob at school."}, {"id": 1012, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "At the next visit Jacob reports leg weakness that has worsened over three weeks, new difficulty controlling his bladder and a change in his walking that his mother has noticed. His spasticity seems unchanged. He denies any recent fall. What is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Increase stretching and strengthening for his legs, since progression in weakness suggests he has lost conditioning", "B": "Reassess at the next fortnightly visit, as changes in function are common during adolescent growth spurts", "C": "Arrange urgent medical review, as new neurological change is not explained by cerebral palsy", "D": "Refer for orthotic review and botulinum toxin assessment, since worsening gait most often reflects increased tone"}, "cluster_id": "CHS13", "cluster_text": "Jacob Nguyen, 14, year 8 student with bilateral spastic cerebral palsy, GMFCS level II. You are the physiotherapist visiting his secondary school fortnightly under a funded school program. He walks without aids on level ground but uses a manual wheelchair for school excursions and long distances. Observation: bilateral toe walking with intermittent knee flexion in stance, reduced stride length and trunk lean. Passive ankle dorsiflexion: 0 degrees with knee straight, 10 degrees with knee bent. Popliteal angle 55 degrees. Calf pain 3/10 after sport. His mother has given written consent to share functional information with the school. The year coordinator emails you asking for his full diagnosis, scan results and your treatment notes for his school file. He wants to run at the athletics carnival.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Arrange urgent medical review, as new neurological change is not explained by cerebral palsy): Cerebral palsy is a non-progressive condition, so worsening weakness with new bladder change points to possible spinal cord or other neurological pathology that needs prompt medical assessment. Exercise, waiting for a growth spurt or reviewing orthoses would delay diagnosis, especially when tone is reported to be unchanged."}, {"id": 1013, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "On confrontation testing Gerard sees a finger on either side when it is presented alone, but misses the left finger when both sides are presented together, and he omits left sided items on cancellation. Which interpretation best fits these findings?", "options": {"A": "Longstanding cognitive decline causing general inattention that would affect both sides of space equally", "B": "Left unilateral spatial neglect with sensory extinction, rather than a primary visual field loss", "C": "Reduced effort and low motivation affecting his participation in therapy, which should be addressed with encouragement", "D": "Left homonymous hemianopia from damage to the visual pathway, which prevents him seeing the left side"}, "cluster_id": "CHS14", "cluster_text": "Gerard Fitzpatrick, 64, a retired electrician, is on day 4 after a right middle cerebral artery ischaemic stroke on an acute stroke ward. He is medically stable: BP 142/86, HR 76, SpO2 97 percent on room air. He lies with his head and eyes turned to the right. Left arm strength is 1/5 and left leg 3/5. He sits at the edge of the bed with moderate assistance and leans slightly to the left. He eats food only from the right side of his plate and bumped his left shoulder on the door frame during a wheelchair transfer. He says there is nothing wrong with his arm. On a line bisection task he marks to the right of centre, and he omits items on the left on a cancellation task. A nurse mentioned that he is not trying with his left side, and a student nurse has been telling him off when he ignores it.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Left unilateral spatial neglect with sensory extinction, rather than a primary visual field loss): Seeing a stimulus when it is presented alone but missing it when competing stimuli appear is extinction, and omitting one side on cancellation and line bisection is the hallmark of spatial neglect, an attention disorder rather than a loss of vision. A hemianopia would cause a miss of the left finger even when presented alone. Low effort does not produce this consistent side specific pattern."}, {"id": 1014, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Staff currently approach Gerard and speak to him only from his right, and his meal tray is set on his right. The physiotherapist advises the team on strategies to encourage attention to the left during daily care while keeping him safe. Which strategy is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Patch the right eye for long periods during each therapy session to force attention leftward", "B": "Approach and speak from the left, and cue him to scan toward a visible left anchor", "C": "Move him to a bright, busy bay so many stimuli on the left capture his attention", "D": "Keep contact and items on his right to avoid frustration, and let left side recover naturally"}, "cluster_id": "CHS14", "cluster_text": "Gerard Fitzpatrick, 64, a retired electrician, is on day 4 after a right middle cerebral artery ischaemic stroke on an acute stroke ward. He is medically stable: BP 142/86, HR 76, SpO2 97 percent on room air. He lies with his head and eyes turned to the right. Left arm strength is 1/5 and left leg 3/5. He sits at the edge of the bed with moderate assistance and leans slightly to the left. He eats food only from the right side of his plate and bumped his left shoulder on the door frame during a wheelchair transfer. He says there is nothing wrong with his arm. On a line bisection task he marks to the right of centre, and he omits items on the left on a cancellation task. A nurse mentioned that he is not trying with his left side, and a student nurse has been telling him off when he ignores it.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Approach and speak from the left, and cue him to scan toward a visible left anchor): Approaching, speaking and placing objects on the neglected side, with cues toward a visible anchor, encourages exploration of left space, and the cues are faded as he improves while safety items stay reachable. Keeping everything on the right reinforces the neglect, and busy cluttered surroundings worsen attention problems. Eye patching is not a routine bedside strategy."}, {"id": 1015, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Gerard asks to walk to the bathroom. He has left leg strength 3/5, sits with moderate assistance, is impulsive, and ignores his left side. His falls risk is high. Which plan is the safest way to mobilise him to the bathroom?", "options": {"A": "Supervise him from his right side, where he can see staff, while he moves unaided", "B": "Keep him on bed rest using a bedpan until neglect resolves, to remove the fall risk", "C": "Allow independent toileting with the call bell on his right, since he is alert and motivated", "D": "Staff assist from his left, cue him to look left, and follow a falls prevention plan"}, "cluster_id": "CHS14", "cluster_text": "Gerard Fitzpatrick, 64, a retired electrician, is on day 4 after a right middle cerebral artery ischaemic stroke on an acute stroke ward. He is medically stable: BP 142/86, HR 76, SpO2 97 percent on room air. He lies with his head and eyes turned to the right. Left arm strength is 1/5 and left leg 3/5. He sits at the edge of the bed with moderate assistance and leans slightly to the left. He eats food only from the right side of his plate and bumped his left shoulder on the door frame during a wheelchair transfer. He says there is nothing wrong with his arm. On a line bisection task he marks to the right of centre, and he omits items on the left on a cancellation task. A nurse mentioned that he is not trying with his left side, and a student nurse has been telling him off when he ignores it.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Staff assist from his left, cue him to look left, and follow a falls prevention plan): Standing and walking with physical help on the weak side and verbal cues to scan left reduces the chance of collisions and falls, and a falls plan covers the times staff are absent. Independent toileting is unsafe with impulsivity and neglect, bed rest causes deconditioning and delays recovery, and supervising only from the right keeps him from attending to the side that needs cueing."}, {"id": 1016, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "A nurse tells the physiotherapist that Gerard is not trying with his left arm and ignores his lunch. At the afternoon handover, the physiotherapist wants the whole team to respond consistently. What is the most effective way to communicate about his neglect?", "options": {"A": "Explain neglect as a perceptual problem at handover, then ask staff to use their own judgement", "B": "Raise neglect at the weekly multidisciplinary meeting, so the full team can agree one approach", "C": "Record neglect clearly in the progress notes, which nursing and medical staff read each shift", "D": "Give a structured handover covering neglect, strategies and falls precautions, plus a bedside sign"}, "cluster_id": "CHS14", "cluster_text": "Gerard Fitzpatrick, 64, a retired electrician, is on day 4 after a right middle cerebral artery ischaemic stroke on an acute stroke ward. He is medically stable: BP 142/86, HR 76, SpO2 97 percent on room air. He lies with his head and eyes turned to the right. Left arm strength is 1/5 and left leg 3/5. He sits at the edge of the bed with moderate assistance and leans slightly to the left. He eats food only from the right side of his plate and bumped his left shoulder on the door frame during a wheelchair transfer. He says there is nothing wrong with his arm. On a line bisection task he marks to the right of centre, and he omits items on the left on a cancellation task. A nurse mentioned that he is not trying with his left side, and a student nurse has been telling him off when he ignores it.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Give a structured handover covering neglect, strategies and falls precautions, plus a bedside sign): A structured handover that names the deficit as a perceptual attention problem, not poor effort, and lists the agreed strategies, falls precautions and a bedside reminder gives every staff member the same message today. Explaining the problem without agreed strategies leaves each person to improvise, so responses stay inconsistent. A weekly meeting delays change to daily care that is happening now. A note in the record is passive and is often unread or misinterpreted."}, {"id": 1017, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Hieu has coarse crackles in both lower zones and a wet cough with thick sputum that he finds hard to clear. He is alert, mobile and cooperative. Which airway clearance approach is most appropriate to establish during this admission for a seven year old child?", "options": {"A": "Positive expiratory pressure with huffing, adapted for his age and combined with active play", "B": "Repeated forced huffs from high lung volumes, continued until no further sputum can be cleared", "C": "Mechanical insufflation-exsufflation through a mask, because thick secretions are hard for him to clear independently", "D": "Manual percussion and vibration by his mother in head-down tilt positions, repeated after every meal"}, "cluster_id": "CHS15", "cluster_text": "Hieu Do, 7 years old, is admitted to a rural hospital 600 km from the nearest cystic fibrosis centre with a pulmonary exacerbation of cystic fibrosis and known bronchiectasis. He has pancreatic insufficiency and takes enzymes with meals. Yesterday he developed more wet cough with thick green sputum and a reduced appetite. Observations: RR 28, HR 112, SpO2 94 percent on room air, temperature 37.6. Coarse crackles over both lower zones, no wheeze. He is alert and walks the ward corridor without distress. His mother speaks little English and the family speaks Vietnamese. His 14 year old sister has been translating at the bedside, and the mother answers yes to most questions. The physiotherapist wants to teach the mother a home airway clearance routine before discharge to a community where a visiting physiotherapist attends fortnightly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Positive expiratory pressure with huffing, adapted for his age and combined with active play): Positive expiratory pressure with huffing suits a school age child, can be shaped into play and is easy to sustain at home. Percussion in head down tilt is largely outdated and can provoke reflux. Prolonged forced huffing tires a child and can collapse small airways, and mechanical insufflation is for a weak cough, not an alert mobile child."}, {"id": 1018, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "The next morning Hieu has a temperature of 38.6, respiratory rate 34, SpO2 91 percent on room air, a new grunt and markedly reduced air entry at the right base. He is more tired and less interested in play. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Pause treatment, support with oxygen per local protocol, and escalate urgently to the medical officer", "B": "Give nebulised hypertonic saline before airway clearance, as usual in cystic fibrosis, then reassess response", "C": "Extend airway clearance in a right side up position to mobilise secretions, then report findings afterwards", "D": "Encourage corridor walking to improve ventilation and clearance, then recheck saturation after ten minutes of activity"}, "cluster_id": "CHS15", "cluster_text": "Hieu Do, 7 years old, is admitted to a rural hospital 600 km from the nearest cystic fibrosis centre with a pulmonary exacerbation of cystic fibrosis and known bronchiectasis. He has pancreatic insufficiency and takes enzymes with meals. Yesterday he developed more wet cough with thick green sputum and a reduced appetite. Observations: RR 28, HR 112, SpO2 94 percent on room air, temperature 37.6. Coarse crackles over both lower zones, no wheeze. He is alert and walks the ward corridor without distress. His mother speaks little English and the family speaks Vietnamese. His 14 year old sister has been translating at the bedside, and the mother answers yes to most questions. The physiotherapist wants to teach the mother a home airway clearance routine before discharge to a community where a visiting physiotherapist attends fortnightly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Pause treatment, support with oxygen per local protocol, and escalate urgently to the medical officer): A new fever, tachypnoea, falling saturation, grunting and reduced air entry point to a deteriorating lower respiratory infection, possibly consolidation or collapse. The physiotherapist should pause, support oxygenation within local protocol and escalate urgently. Extending clearance or walking adds work for an unstable child, and hypertonic saline needs a medical order."}, {"id": 1019, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Hieu's mother asks why he is encouraged to exercise daily as well as perform airway clearance, since he already takes enzymes with meals and has no breathlessness at rest. Which explanation best reflects the role of exercise in children with cystic fibrosis?", "options": {"A": "Exercise replaces formal airway clearance when the child is well, so sessions can stop between exacerbations", "B": "Exercise should stay gentle because vigorous activity accelerates lung function decline in children with cystic fibrosis", "C": "Regular exercise supports airway clearance, maintains lung function and fitness, and improves quality of life", "D": "Exercise mainly helps appetite and weight gain, which offsets the malabsorption caused by pancreatic insufficiency"}, "cluster_id": "CHS15", "cluster_text": "Hieu Do, 7 years old, is admitted to a rural hospital 600 km from the nearest cystic fibrosis centre with a pulmonary exacerbation of cystic fibrosis and known bronchiectasis. He has pancreatic insufficiency and takes enzymes with meals. Yesterday he developed more wet cough with thick green sputum and a reduced appetite. Observations: RR 28, HR 112, SpO2 94 percent on room air, temperature 37.6. Coarse crackles over both lower zones, no wheeze. He is alert and walks the ward corridor without distress. His mother speaks little English and the family speaks Vietnamese. His 14 year old sister has been translating at the bedside, and the mother answers yes to most questions. The physiotherapist wants to teach the mother a home airway clearance routine before discharge to a community where a visiting physiotherapist attends fortnightly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Regular exercise supports airway clearance, maintains lung function and fitness, and improves quality of life): Exercise assists mucociliary clearance, helps maintain lung function and fitness, and improves wellbeing, so it complements airway clearance rather than replacing it. Weight gain is not its main purpose. Vigorous activity is encouraged, not restricted, when a child is clinically stable."}, {"id": 1020, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Before discharge, the physiotherapist must teach Hieu's mother the home airway clearance routine and confirm she understands the signs of a worsening chest. She nods and says yes to every question, and his sister offers to interpret again. What is the best approach?", "options": {"A": "Use a qualified Vietnamese telephone interpreter and ask the mother to demonstrate the routine back", "B": "Keep using the sister as interpreter for convenience and add a written handout translated into Vietnamese", "C": "Defer the teaching to the visiting physiotherapist in the community, where family may feel more comfortable", "D": "Teach in simple English using demonstration and short sentences, as she seems to follow visual steps"}, "cluster_id": "CHS15", "cluster_text": "Hieu Do, 7 years old, is admitted to a rural hospital 600 km from the nearest cystic fibrosis centre with a pulmonary exacerbation of cystic fibrosis and known bronchiectasis. He has pancreatic insufficiency and takes enzymes with meals. Yesterday he developed more wet cough with thick green sputum and a reduced appetite. Observations: RR 28, HR 112, SpO2 94 percent on room air, temperature 37.6. Coarse crackles over both lower zones, no wheeze. He is alert and walks the ward corridor without distress. His mother speaks little English and the family speaks Vietnamese. His 14 year old sister has been translating at the bedside, and the mother answers yes to most questions. The physiotherapist wants to teach the mother a home airway clearance routine before discharge to a community where a visiting physiotherapist attends fortnightly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use a qualified Vietnamese telephone interpreter and ask the mother to demonstrate the routine back): Children should not be relied on as interpreters for clinical teaching because accuracy and privacy cannot be assured. A qualified interpreter plus asking the mother to demonstrate the routine checks real understanding, which repeated agreement does not. A translated handout supports teaching but cannot confirm comprehension, and deferring leaves the family without a safe plan at discharge."}, {"id": 1021, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Giuseppe has neck pain and altered hand sensation, which could suggest nerve root irritation alone. The physiotherapist reviews his examination for evidence of spinal cord involvement. Which combination of findings on this examination most strongly points to cervical myelopathy rather than isolated radiculopathy?", "options": {"A": "Reduced biceps and triceps reflexes with dermatomal sensory loss and myotomal weakness in one arm", "B": "Positive Spurling test, arm pain eased by shoulder abduction, and a negative upper limb tension test", "C": "Painful, limited cervical extension with patchy hand sensory loss and reduced grip strength on both sides", "D": "Bilateral Hoffmann signs, lower limb hyperreflexia with clonus, impaired tandem walking and clumsy hands"}, "cluster_id": "CHS16", "cluster_text": "Giuseppe Ferraro, 71, a retired builder, is referred by his GP for neck stiffness and clumsy hands. Over six months he has had difficulty with buttons, worsening handwriting and unsteadiness walking on uneven ground. There was no neck trauma and he denies bladder symptoms. Examination: grip strength reduced bilaterally, finger abduction 4/5, bilateral positive Hoffmann sign, brisk lower limb reflexes with three beats of clonus at the right ankle, hyperreflexic biceps, impaired tandem walking, Romberg negative. Cervical extension is painful and limited. Light touch is patchily reduced in both hands. The clinic is introducing a routine outcome measure for all neck patients and the physiotherapist has been asked to help audit current practice.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Bilateral Hoffmann signs, lower limb hyperreflexia with clonus, impaired tandem walking and clumsy hands): Upper motor neuron signs in the limbs, such as Hoffmann sign, clonus, hyperreflexia and gait disturbance with clumsy hands, indicate cord compression. Pain, restricted extension and sensory change are non-specific and occur with radiculopathy as well. Reduced reflexes in a dermatomal pattern and a positive Spurling test describe a lower motor neuron root problem."}, {"id": 1022, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Giuseppe's progressive bilateral hand clumsiness, gait unsteadiness and upper motor neuron signs raise concern for degenerative cervical myelopathy. He is neurologically stable at this visit with no bladder change. What is the most appropriate next step for the physiotherapist to take today?", "options": {"A": "Trial gentle cervical traction with deep neck flexor training, reassessing neurological signs after two sessions", "B": "Start supervised neck and upper limb strengthening and monitor, referring if bladder symptoms develop", "C": "Withhold cervical manual therapy and refer promptly for specialist medical review and imaging", "D": "Treat as cervical radiculopathy using neural mobilisation and thoracic mobilisation, and review progress at four weeks"}, "cluster_id": "CHS16", "cluster_text": "Giuseppe Ferraro, 71, a retired builder, is referred by his GP for neck stiffness and clumsy hands. Over six months he has had difficulty with buttons, worsening handwriting and unsteadiness walking on uneven ground. There was no neck trauma and he denies bladder symptoms. Examination: grip strength reduced bilaterally, finger abduction 4/5, bilateral positive Hoffmann sign, brisk lower limb reflexes with three beats of clonus at the right ankle, hyperreflexic biceps, impaired tandem walking, Romberg negative. Cervical extension is painful and limited. Light touch is patchily reduced in both hands. The clinic is introducing a routine outcome measure for all neck patients and the physiotherapist has been asked to help audit current practice.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Withhold cervical manual therapy and refer promptly for specialist medical review and imaging): Suspected cord compression needs medical assessment and imaging to guide decisions about surgery, and cervical manual techniques and traction may harm a compressed cord. Waiting for bladder symptoms means acting after significant deterioration. Treating it as a root problem misreads the upper motor neuron signs."}, {"id": 1023, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Several validated tools exist with different purposes. The clinic manager asks the physiotherapist to choose a routine outcome measure for all neck patients, including those like Giuseppe, and to use the results to improve the service. Which approach is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Let each physiotherapist pick a preferred measure per patient and review individual cases at annual appraisal", "B": "Use a clinician-rated global impression of change and report the proportion improved each year", "C": "Record patient satisfaction scores at discharge, since they directly reflect the quality of treatment delivered", "D": "Use a validated patient-reported neck disability measure at baseline and discharge, then audit aggregated results"}, "cluster_id": "CHS16", "cluster_text": "Giuseppe Ferraro, 71, a retired builder, is referred by his GP for neck stiffness and clumsy hands. Over six months he has had difficulty with buttons, worsening handwriting and unsteadiness walking on uneven ground. There was no neck trauma and he denies bladder symptoms. Examination: grip strength reduced bilaterally, finger abduction 4/5, bilateral positive Hoffmann sign, brisk lower limb reflexes with three beats of clonus at the right ankle, hyperreflexic biceps, impaired tandem walking, Romberg negative. Cervical extension is painful and limited. Light touch is patchily reduced in both hands. The clinic is introducing a routine outcome measure for all neck patients and the physiotherapist has been asked to help audit current practice.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Use a validated patient-reported neck disability measure at baseline and discharge, then audit aggregated results): A single validated patient-reported measure collected at the start and end of care allows change to be compared across patients and over time, which supports service quality improvement. Satisfaction reflects experience rather than functional change. Different measures per therapist cannot be pooled, and a global clinician rating is subjective."}, {"id": 1024, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "While waiting for specialist review, Giuseppe phones the clinic reporting that his legs gave way twice this week and he has had one episode of urinary incontinence. He asks whether to keep his appointment next week. What advice is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Contact his GP tomorrow to request an earlier specialist appointment and advise him to rest meanwhile", "B": "Advise him to attend the emergency department today because his neurological signs are worsening", "C": "Book an earlier physiotherapy session so his neurological signs can be rechecked and exercises adjusted", "D": "Keep next week's appointment and wear a soft collar in the meantime to protect his neck"}, "cluster_id": "CHS16", "cluster_text": "Giuseppe Ferraro, 71, a retired builder, is referred by his GP for neck stiffness and clumsy hands. Over six months he has had difficulty with buttons, worsening handwriting and unsteadiness walking on uneven ground. There was no neck trauma and he denies bladder symptoms. Examination: grip strength reduced bilaterally, finger abduction 4/5, bilateral positive Hoffmann sign, brisk lower limb reflexes with three beats of clonus at the right ankle, hyperreflexic biceps, impaired tandem walking, Romberg negative. Cervical extension is painful and limited. Light touch is patchily reduced in both hands. The clinic is introducing a routine outcome measure for all neck patients and the physiotherapist has been asked to help audit current practice.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Advise him to attend the emergency department today because his neurological signs are worsening): New leg give-way and bladder incontinence in suspected myelopathy indicate rapidly progressing cord compromise and need same-day emergency assessment. A collar does not protect against progression. Waiting for a physiotherapy recheck or an appointment tomorrow adds delay that risks permanent loss of function."}, {"id": 1025, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Warren reports five days of back pain after long driving and now has several new findings. The physiotherapist must decide which combination most strongly indicates a surgical emergency rather than a mechanical back problem. Which combination carries the greatest weight for this decision?", "options": {"A": "Difficulty starting urination, reduced perianal sensation and bilateral leg weakness appearing together", "B": "Bilateral reduced ankle reflexes with limited straight leg raise and pain that began after prolonged sitting", "C": "Great toe extension weakness with dorsiflexion weakness suggesting an L5 root problem needing routine review", "D": "Pain after prolonged driving with bilateral limited straight leg raise and blood pressure of 138/86"}, "cluster_id": "CHS17", "cluster_text": "Warren Holland, 52, is a Wiradjuri man from a regional town who drives long haul trucks. He is visiting Sydney and attends a private practice with his sister, Donna. He reports five days of low back pain that began after a long drive. Since last night he has numbness around his buttocks and inner thighs, and this morning had difficulty starting urination. Both legs feel weak. Examination: ankle dorsiflexion 4/5 bilaterally, great toe extension 4/5 bilaterally, ankle reflexes reduced on both sides, and reduced sensation to light touch reported over the perianal area. Straight leg raise is limited by pain on both sides. HR 78, BP 138/86, afebrile. He says hospitals have treated his family poorly in the past and he would prefer to go home and see his own doctor next week. He is quiet and keeps looking at his sister.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Difficulty starting urination, reduced perianal sensation and bilateral leg weakness appearing together): New bladder difficulty with reduced saddle sensation and bilateral leg involvement is the classic cluster for cauda equina syndrome, which can cause permanent bladder, bowel and sexual dysfunction without prompt decompression. Root level signs and driving related pain occur in common mechanical and radicular problems and do not by themselves require emergency surgery."}, {"id": 1026, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "The physiotherapist suspects cauda equina syndrome. Warren's neurological findings are stable during the session and he is alert with normal vital signs and moderate pain. He is hesitant to go to hospital today. What is the most appropriate next action?", "options": {"A": "Book an urgent GP appointment within the next few days and provide advice on warning signs", "B": "Respect his preference, advise rest, and rebook within forty eight hours to repeat the neurological examination", "C": "Explain the urgency and arrange same day emergency department assessment, phoning ahead with the findings", "D": "Apply lumbar traction and neural mobilisation, then reassess bladder and sensory symptoms before deciding on referral"}, "cluster_id": "CHS17", "cluster_text": "Warren Holland, 52, is a Wiradjuri man from a regional town who drives long haul trucks. He is visiting Sydney and attends a private practice with his sister, Donna. He reports five days of low back pain that began after a long drive. Since last night he has numbness around his buttocks and inner thighs, and this morning had difficulty starting urination. Both legs feel weak. Examination: ankle dorsiflexion 4/5 bilaterally, great toe extension 4/5 bilaterally, ankle reflexes reduced on both sides, and reduced sensation to light touch reported over the perianal area. Straight leg raise is limited by pain on both sides. HR 78, BP 138/86, afebrile. He says hospitals have treated his family poorly in the past and he would prefer to go home and see his own doctor next week. He is quiet and keeps looking at his sister.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Explain the urgency and arrange same day emergency department assessment, phoning ahead with the findings): Suspected cauda equina syndrome is a time critical emergency needing urgent imaging and surgical review, so the physiotherapist arranges same day emergency assessment and communicates the findings to the receiving team. Delaying for a GP visit or a repeat examination risks irreversible bladder and bowel loss. Traction or neural mobilisation could aggravate compression."}, {"id": 1027, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Warren has bilateral weakness of ankle dorsiflexion, and the physiotherapist wants to quickly document the root levels involved before the transfer. Which simple additional resisted test most specifically examines the L5 myotome and should be recorded for the receiving team?", "options": {"A": "Resisted knee extension in sitting, with the examiner applying force above the ankle and comparing sides", "B": "Resisted ankle plantarflexion, assessed by repeated single leg heel raises, graded against body weight", "C": "Resisted great toe extension, compared with the other side, held for a few seconds", "D": "Resisted hip flexion in sitting, with the examiner pressing down on the distal thigh"}, "cluster_id": "CHS17", "cluster_text": "Warren Holland, 52, is a Wiradjuri man from a regional town who drives long haul trucks. He is visiting Sydney and attends a private practice with his sister, Donna. He reports five days of low back pain that began after a long drive. Since last night he has numbness around his buttocks and inner thighs, and this morning had difficulty starting urination. Both legs feel weak. Examination: ankle dorsiflexion 4/5 bilaterally, great toe extension 4/5 bilaterally, ankle reflexes reduced on both sides, and reduced sensation to light touch reported over the perianal area. Straight leg raise is limited by pain on both sides. HR 78, BP 138/86, afebrile. He says hospitals have treated his family poorly in the past and he would prefer to go home and see his own doctor next week. He is quiet and keeps looking at his sister.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Resisted great toe extension, compared with the other side, held for a few seconds): Great toe extension, produced by extensor hallucis longus, is the key indicator muscle for the L5 root. Plantarflexion mainly reflects S1, knee extension mainly L3 and L4, and hip flexion mainly L2 and L3, so these tests would not help localise L5 involvement."}, {"id": 1028, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Warren, a Wiradjuri man, keeps glancing at his sister and says hospitals have treated his family poorly. He has capacity and is alert. The physiotherapist needs his agreement to transfer today. Which approach is the most culturally safe way to proceed?", "options": {"A": "Speak with Warren alone, asking Donna to wait outside so he can decide without family influence", "B": "Listen to his concerns, explain the urgency, invite Donna to stay, and offer Aboriginal liaison support", "C": "Ask Donna, as his closest family member, to give consent for the transfer on his behalf", "D": "Call an ambulance and begin transfer, then explain the reasons and options once he reaches hospital"}, "cluster_id": "CHS17", "cluster_text": "Warren Holland, 52, is a Wiradjuri man from a regional town who drives long haul trucks. He is visiting Sydney and attends a private practice with his sister, Donna. He reports five days of low back pain that began after a long drive. Since last night he has numbness around his buttocks and inner thighs, and this morning had difficulty starting urination. Both legs feel weak. Examination: ankle dorsiflexion 4/5 bilaterally, great toe extension 4/5 bilaterally, ankle reflexes reduced on both sides, and reduced sensation to light touch reported over the perianal area. Straight leg raise is limited by pain on both sides. HR 78, BP 138/86, afebrile. He says hospitals have treated his family poorly in the past and he would prefer to go home and see his own doctor next week. He is quiet and keeps looking at his sister.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Listen to his concerns, explain the urgency, invite Donna to stay, and offer Aboriginal liaison support): Culturally safe care involves listening, explaining clearly why the transfer is urgent, respecting his wish to have family present and offering access to an Aboriginal liaison officer or Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service support. Because Warren has capacity, consent must come from him, not his sister, and removing family or proceeding without explanation can repeat past negative experiences."}, {"id": 1029, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Kaur's examination shows several abnormal signs, and a junior colleague asks which combination best indicates cervical cord compression rather than a peripheral nerve or root problem alone, given her bilateral hand clumsiness and gait change. Which set of findings from today's assessment best supports this?", "options": {"A": "Reduced grip, clumsy fine finger movement and numb hands on both sides", "B": "Bilateral hand numbness, positive Spurling test and neck pain easing with rest", "C": "Weakness in one root distribution, reduced reflex at that level and unilateral arm pain", "D": "Positive Hoffmann sign, hyperreflexia with clonus, and a wide-based unsteady gait"}, "cluster_id": "CHS18", "cluster_text": "Gurpreet Kaur, 71, a retired seamstress, was admitted to the neurosurgical ward two days ago with six months of clumsy hands and unsteady walking, worse over the past fortnight. MRI shows multilevel cervical spondylosis with canal stenosis and cord signal change at C5 to C6. Decompressive surgery is planned later this week. History: hypertension and knee osteoarthritis. Assessment today: Hoffmann sign positive bilaterally, brisk knee reflexes, three beats of clonus at the right ankle, grip strength grade 4 minus bilaterally, and difficulty with fine finger movement (she cannot button her gown). Gait is wide-based and she cannot complete tandem walking. Light touch is reduced over both hands. No bladder symptoms. Neck pain is 3 out of 10, and observations are stable. You have five referrals this morning: Mrs Kaur, a day 1 hip replacement, a patient with pneumonia and a rising oxygen requirement, a discharge assessment, and a family meeting request.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Positive Hoffmann sign, hyperreflexia with clonus, and a wide-based unsteady gait): Upper motor neurone signs such as a positive Hoffmann sign, hyperreflexia with clonus and a wide-based unsteady gait point to cord compromise, and they fit her cord signal change at C5 to C6. Bilateral hand clumsiness, grip weakness and numbness can also arise from peripheral nerve or root problems, so they are less specific. A Spurling test and neck pain suggest root irritation. A single root pattern with a reduced reflex describes a lower motor neurone lesion, the opposite of cord compression."}, {"id": 1030, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "You have about three hours before a team meeting. One patient has pneumonia with a rising oxygen requirement, one is day 1 after hip replacement, one is Mrs Kaur, one awaits a discharge assessment, and a family has requested a meeting. Which patient should the physiotherapist see first?", "options": {"A": "The hip replacement patient, because early mobilisation on day 1 reduces complications and supports planned discharge", "B": "The patient with pneumonia, because deteriorating respiratory status is the most time-critical need today", "C": "The patient awaiting discharge, because bed availability depends on a timely safe assessment from the team", "D": "Mrs Kaur, because progressive cord compression carries the highest risk of lasting neurological harm"}, "cluster_id": "CHS18", "cluster_text": "Gurpreet Kaur, 71, a retired seamstress, was admitted to the neurosurgical ward two days ago with six months of clumsy hands and unsteady walking, worse over the past fortnight. MRI shows multilevel cervical spondylosis with canal stenosis and cord signal change at C5 to C6. Decompressive surgery is planned later this week. History: hypertension and knee osteoarthritis. Assessment today: Hoffmann sign positive bilaterally, brisk knee reflexes, three beats of clonus at the right ankle, grip strength grade 4 minus bilaterally, and difficulty with fine finger movement (she cannot button her gown). Gait is wide-based and she cannot complete tandem walking. Light touch is reduced over both hands. No bladder symptoms. Neck pain is 3 out of 10, and observations are stable. You have five referrals this morning: Mrs Kaur, a day 1 hip replacement, a patient with pneumonia and a rising oxygen requirement, a discharge assessment, and a family meeting request.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The patient with pneumonia, because deteriorating respiratory status is the most time-critical need today): Prioritisation follows clinical urgency and risk. A patient with pneumonia and a rising oxygen requirement may deteriorate quickly and benefits most from prompt respiratory assessment and treatment, with medical escalation if needed. Mrs Kaur is stable and awaiting planned surgery, so her review can follow soon, and discharge and post-operative mobilisation are less time-critical. Family meetings are arranged around clinical priorities."}, {"id": 1031, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Before surgery, Mrs Kaur asks what she can do on the ward to stay mobile. She is steady only with a frame and close supervision. She reports tingling in both hands when she looks up. Which approach to her physiotherapy programme is most appropriate while she awaits decompression?", "options": {"A": "Supervised walking with a frame and balance practice, avoiding sustained or end-range neck extension", "B": "Rest in bed with a soft collar until surgery, with daily hand and ankle exercises", "C": "Seated upper limb and hand dexterity training only, to avoid the falls risk of walking practice", "D": "Gentle cervical mobilisation within pain limits, with supervised walking, to maintain neck range before surgery"}, "cluster_id": "CHS18", "cluster_text": "Gurpreet Kaur, 71, a retired seamstress, was admitted to the neurosurgical ward two days ago with six months of clumsy hands and unsteady walking, worse over the past fortnight. MRI shows multilevel cervical spondylosis with canal stenosis and cord signal change at C5 to C6. Decompressive surgery is planned later this week. History: hypertension and knee osteoarthritis. Assessment today: Hoffmann sign positive bilaterally, brisk knee reflexes, three beats of clonus at the right ankle, grip strength grade 4 minus bilaterally, and difficulty with fine finger movement (she cannot button her gown). Gait is wide-based and she cannot complete tandem walking. Light touch is reduced over both hands. No bladder symptoms. Neck pain is 3 out of 10, and observations are stable. You have five referrals this morning: Mrs Kaur, a day 1 hip replacement, a patient with pneumonia and a rising oxygen requirement, a discharge assessment, and a family meeting request.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Supervised walking with a frame and balance practice, avoiding sustained or end-range neck extension): Pre-operative care aims to keep safe mobility and balance while avoiding movements that load a compressed cord. Neck extension can narrow the canal further and provoke her tingling, so it is avoided, while supervised gait work with the frame limits falls risk. Bed rest in a collar adds deconditioning without protecting the cord. Seated hand work alone neglects the gait and balance deficit that places her at risk. Cervical mobilisation can aggravate cord compression, so it is not appropriate before decompression."}, {"id": 1032, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "On review the next morning, Mrs Kaur says her legs feel heavier and she leaked urine without noticing. Hip flexor power is now grade 3 bilaterally, down from grade 4 yesterday. The ward is busy and the surgeon rounds at noon. What should the physiotherapist do now?", "options": {"A": "Reassure her that symptoms vary before surgery and record the findings for the noon round", "B": "Hold mobilisation, keep her resting and urgently inform the neurosurgical team of the change", "C": "Repeat the neurological examination after her walk, then report if the weakness persists with activity", "D": "Request a continence review and a falls risk plan, then continue walking with extra supervision"}, "cluster_id": "CHS18", "cluster_text": "Gurpreet Kaur, 71, a retired seamstress, was admitted to the neurosurgical ward two days ago with six months of clumsy hands and unsteady walking, worse over the past fortnight. MRI shows multilevel cervical spondylosis with canal stenosis and cord signal change at C5 to C6. Decompressive surgery is planned later this week. History: hypertension and knee osteoarthritis. Assessment today: Hoffmann sign positive bilaterally, brisk knee reflexes, three beats of clonus at the right ankle, grip strength grade 4 minus bilaterally, and difficulty with fine finger movement (she cannot button her gown). Gait is wide-based and she cannot complete tandem walking. Light touch is reduced over both hands. No bladder symptoms. Neck pain is 3 out of 10, and observations are stable. You have five referrals this morning: Mrs Kaur, a day 1 hip replacement, a patient with pneumonia and a rising oxygen requirement, a discharge assessment, and a family meeting request.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hold mobilisation, keep her resting and urgently inform the neurosurgical team of the change): New bladder change with worsening leg power suggests progressive cord compromise, which is a surgical emergency where delay risks lasting loss of function. The physiotherapist stops activity and escalates directly to the surgical team rather than waiting for scheduled rounds. Reassurance, repeat testing during activity or routine continence planning would all delay time-critical decompression."}, {"id": 1033, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Tendai is alert and oriented. He says he understands that exercise helps his recovery but is too exhausted today and wants his session cancelled. His father insists he continue and offers to sign consent. What is the best way for the physiotherapist to proceed with the session?", "options": {"A": "Continue a reduced session as his father requests, since illness and fatigue may cloud his judgement", "B": "Cancel the session but ask the medical team to formally assess capacity before accepting further refusals", "C": "Respect his decision, explore his concerns, and offer a modified session or rest with later review", "D": "Accept his father's signed consent so therapy proceeds, and document that Tendai was reluctant today"}, "cluster_id": "CHS19", "cluster_text": "Tendai Moyo, 29, a warehouse worker, was diagnosed with Guillain-Barre syndrome five weeks ago, treated with intravenous immunoglobulin, and ventilated for ten days in intensive care. He is now in inpatient rehabilitation and his weakness plateaued last week. MRC grades: hip flexion 3, knee extension 3, ankle dorsiflexion 2, shoulder abduction 4, grip 4. Reflexes are absent. He has stocking distribution numbness and burning foot pain rated 6 out of 10. Sitting on the edge of the bed causes dizziness: BP falls from 125/80 to 95/60 and HR rises to 105. He fatigues after about ten minutes of exercise. Barthel Index is 40. He is alert and oriented. His father visits daily and wants the team to push harder, exercising Tendai twice daily. Tendai says he is exhausted.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Respect his decision, explore his concerns, and offer a modified session or rest with later review): Tendai is alert, understands the information and can weigh it, so he has capacity and consent is his alone, and family cannot consent for a competent adult. Fatigue and illness alone do not remove capacity, so a conversation about options respects his autonomy while keeping engagement."}, {"id": 1034, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Tendai becomes dizzy on sitting at the edge of the bed, with blood pressure falling from 125/80 to 95/60 and heart rate rising to 105 beats per minute. He is otherwise medically stable. What is the most appropriate management for his next mobilisation session?", "options": {"A": "Grade upright exposure gradually using a tilt table or reclined chair, with compression and monitoring", "B": "Hoist him quickly to standing to build tolerance, since orthostatic changes settle with repeated exposure alone", "C": "Keep him lying flat until his blood pressure normalises, avoiding upright positions during rehabilitation sessions", "D": "Proceed with seated balance exercises and stop only if his blood pressure falls further"}, "cluster_id": "CHS19", "cluster_text": "Tendai Moyo, 29, a warehouse worker, was diagnosed with Guillain-Barre syndrome five weeks ago, treated with intravenous immunoglobulin, and ventilated for ten days in intensive care. He is now in inpatient rehabilitation and his weakness plateaued last week. MRC grades: hip flexion 3, knee extension 3, ankle dorsiflexion 2, shoulder abduction 4, grip 4. Reflexes are absent. He has stocking distribution numbness and burning foot pain rated 6 out of 10. Sitting on the edge of the bed causes dizziness: BP falls from 125/80 to 95/60 and HR rises to 105. He fatigues after about ten minutes of exercise. Barthel Index is 40. He is alert and oriented. His father visits daily and wants the team to push harder, exercising Tendai twice daily. Tendai says he is exhausted.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Grade upright exposure gradually using a tilt table or reclined chair, with compression and monitoring): Autonomic involvement in Guillain-Barre syndrome causes orthostatic hypotension, which is managed with graded, monitored exposure to upright positions plus support such as compression. Avoiding upright positions delays recovery, while forced standing or ignoring the current drop risks syncope."}, {"id": 1035, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Tendai's weakness has plateaued, he becomes fatigued after about ten minutes of exercise, and he is anxious to regain his work capacity. His team asks about exercise prescription. Which exercise approach is most appropriate at this stage of his recovery?", "options": {"A": "Passive range of motion alone until strength returns, as active exercise can damage regenerating nerves", "B": "Daily exercise to maximal effort until fatigued, since overwork weakness is no concern here", "C": "Submaximal, short bouts with rests between, avoiding fatigue that carries into the following day", "D": "High resistance strengthening three times daily to maximise muscle recovery while his nerves regenerate"}, "cluster_id": "CHS19", "cluster_text": "Tendai Moyo, 29, a warehouse worker, was diagnosed with Guillain-Barre syndrome five weeks ago, treated with intravenous immunoglobulin, and ventilated for ten days in intensive care. He is now in inpatient rehabilitation and his weakness plateaued last week. MRC grades: hip flexion 3, knee extension 3, ankle dorsiflexion 2, shoulder abduction 4, grip 4. Reflexes are absent. He has stocking distribution numbness and burning foot pain rated 6 out of 10. Sitting on the edge of the bed causes dizziness: BP falls from 125/80 to 95/60 and HR rises to 105. He fatigues after about ten minutes of exercise. Barthel Index is 40. He is alert and oriented. His father visits daily and wants the team to push harder, exercising Tendai twice daily. Tendai says he is exhausted.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Submaximal, short bouts with rests between, avoiding fatigue that carries into the following day): After Guillain-Barre syndrome, low to moderate intensity exercise with rest breaks builds strength safely, while overwork can cause prolonged fatigue and setbacks. Passive movement alone is too conservative for a patient with active muscle, and high intensity daily loading ignores his documented fatigue."}, {"id": 1036, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "During a mobilisation session the physiotherapist wants to recognise features of autonomic instability in Tendai, given his Guillain-Barre syndrome and recent blood pressure drops, that would need prompt medical review before exercise continues. Which finding most clearly suggests this problem?", "options": {"A": "Burning foot pain that rises to 8 out of 10 during stretching and lingers afterwards", "B": "Shortness of breath with a fall in oxygen saturation and weaker cough during exercise", "C": "Sudden slowing of heart rate or large blood pressure swings with sweating or pallor", "D": "Tiredness after ten minutes with muscle aching that settles after a rest and fluids"}, "cluster_id": "CHS19", "cluster_text": "Tendai Moyo, 29, a warehouse worker, was diagnosed with Guillain-Barre syndrome five weeks ago, treated with intravenous immunoglobulin, and ventilated for ten days in intensive care. He is now in inpatient rehabilitation and his weakness plateaued last week. MRC grades: hip flexion 3, knee extension 3, ankle dorsiflexion 2, shoulder abduction 4, grip 4. Reflexes are absent. He has stocking distribution numbness and burning foot pain rated 6 out of 10. Sitting on the edge of the bed causes dizziness: BP falls from 125/80 to 95/60 and HR rises to 105. He fatigues after about ten minutes of exercise. Barthel Index is 40. He is alert and oriented. His father visits daily and wants the team to push harder, exercising Tendai twice daily. Tendai says he is exhausted.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Sudden slowing of heart rate or large blood pressure swings with sweating or pallor): Abrupt bradycardia, labile blood pressure and accompanying sweating or pallor are typical of autonomic dysfunction and can be dangerous during exercise, so they need prompt medical review. A flare of neuropathic pain needs management but does not signal autonomic instability. Breathlessness with falling saturation suggests respiratory muscle weakness and also needs a response, but it is a different problem from the one asked about. Fatigue that settles with rest is expected in this condition."}, {"id": 1037, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Rekha asks what she should do if she develops chest tightness while exercising at home. Her cardiologist has prescribed GTN spray. According to standard Australian guidance for people with known coronary disease, which action sequence should the physiotherapist teach her as her written plan?", "options": {"A": "Stop and sit, use GTN spray, and call 000 if pain is not relieved", "B": "Rest briefly, then restart at a lower intensity and ring the cardiologist's rooms the following morning", "C": "Stop and sit, take three sprays in a row for fast effect, then drive to hospital", "D": "Slow down and keep walking gently, using GTN spray only if the tightness lasts past recovery"}, "cluster_id": "CHS20", "cluster_text": "Rekha Banerjee, 52, is a school principal attending a private cardiac rehabilitation clinic. Three weeks ago she had a drug-eluting stent placed in her left anterior descending artery via the right radial artery after a non-ST elevation myocardial infarction. Echo showed a mildly reduced ejection fraction. Medications: aspirin, ticagrelor, bisoprolol, rosuvastatin, GTN spray. Resting HR 62 bpm, BP 118/72 mmHg, SpO2 98 percent. No chest pain since discharge. Right wrist puncture site is healed, radial pulse present. She reports mild fatigue by late afternoon. She wants to lift weights and return to tennis soon. She is a friend of your sister and has invited you to dinner and to coach her privately at her home.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stop and sit, use GTN spray, and call 000 if pain is not relieved): The standard action plan for people with coronary disease is to stop activity, sit, use GTN and call an ambulance if pain is not relieved, rather than waiting or self-transporting. Continuing to walk or leaving contact until the next day leaves possible ischaemia unattended. Taking several sprays at once and driving to hospital add avoidable risk."}, {"id": 1038, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Rekha repeats her invitation, saying private sessions at her home would be more relaxed and she would pay in cash. She is progressing well. Both of you are aware of the family connection. How should the physiotherapist best respond to maintain professional boundaries and duty of care?", "options": {"A": "Accept occasional home sessions for convenience, provided records are completed and her GP is informed", "B": "Attend the dinner as a friend while continuing treatment, since rapport improves her adherence to exercise", "C": "Politely decline home sessions, keep treating her in clinic, and document the conversation", "D": "Transfer her care to a colleague straight away, since a social link makes continued treatment inappropriate"}, "cluster_id": "CHS20", "cluster_text": "Rekha Banerjee, 52, is a school principal attending a private cardiac rehabilitation clinic. Three weeks ago she had a drug-eluting stent placed in her left anterior descending artery via the right radial artery after a non-ST elevation myocardial infarction. Echo showed a mildly reduced ejection fraction. Medications: aspirin, ticagrelor, bisoprolol, rosuvastatin, GTN spray. Resting HR 62 bpm, BP 118/72 mmHg, SpO2 98 percent. No chest pain since discharge. Right wrist puncture site is healed, radial pulse present. She reports mild fatigue by late afternoon. She wants to lift weights and return to tennis soon. She is a friend of your sister and has invited you to dinner and to coach her privately at her home.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Politely decline home sessions, keep treating her in clinic, and document the conversation): A treating physiotherapist keeps a clear professional relationship, so social and home arrangements outside the clinic are declined politely while care continues. Documenting the conversation protects both parties and shows the boundary was addressed. Transferring care is reserved for situations where objectivity or safety is genuinely compromised, not triggered by a social link alone."}, {"id": 1039, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Three weeks after stenting with a mildly reduced ejection fraction, Rekha wants to begin lifting weights. Her right radial access site has healed and she has no symptoms. She is keen to start with heavy gym lifts. What is the most appropriate way to introduce resistance training?", "options": {"A": "Begin with light loads, many repetitions, and breathing throughout, monitoring exertion and symptoms", "B": "Delay all resistance work until her ejection fraction has normalised, and continue walking only meanwhile", "C": "Use a moderate load for about eight repetitions, with a brief breath hold for stability", "D": "Progress to heavy lifts in the first week because her stent protects the artery from strain"}, "cluster_id": "CHS20", "cluster_text": "Rekha Banerjee, 52, is a school principal attending a private cardiac rehabilitation clinic. Three weeks ago she had a drug-eluting stent placed in her left anterior descending artery via the right radial artery after a non-ST elevation myocardial infarction. Echo showed a mildly reduced ejection fraction. Medications: aspirin, ticagrelor, bisoprolol, rosuvastatin, GTN spray. Resting HR 62 bpm, BP 118/72 mmHg, SpO2 98 percent. No chest pain since discharge. Right wrist puncture site is healed, radial pulse present. She reports mild fatigue by late afternoon. She wants to lift weights and return to tennis soon. She is a friend of your sister and has invited you to dinner and to coach her privately at her home.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Begin with light loads, many repetitions, and breathing throughout, monitoring exertion and symptoms): Early resistance training after a coronary event starts with light loads, higher repetitions and continuous breathing, since breath holding raises blood pressure sharply. A mildly reduced ejection fraction without symptoms does not require delaying strengthening altogether. A stent restores flow but does not make heavy lifting safe."}, {"id": 1040, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "At review, Rekha reports throbbing at her right wrist for two days. Examination shows a tender, pulsatile lump near the old puncture site with mild forearm swelling. Distal pulses and sensation are intact. Her plan today includes grip exercises. What is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Apply firm compression to the lump, modify grip work, and recheck at the next session", "B": "Continue with upper limb exercise below pain threshold and ask her to report any enlargement", "C": "Apply heat and gentle massage to the forearm as the findings suggest a haematoma needing mobilisation", "D": "Withhold grip exercise, avoid pressure on the lump, and arrange urgent same-day medical assessment"}, "cluster_id": "CHS20", "cluster_text": "Rekha Banerjee, 52, is a school principal attending a private cardiac rehabilitation clinic. Three weeks ago she had a drug-eluting stent placed in her left anterior descending artery via the right radial artery after a non-ST elevation myocardial infarction. Echo showed a mildly reduced ejection fraction. Medications: aspirin, ticagrelor, bisoprolol, rosuvastatin, GTN spray. Resting HR 62 bpm, BP 118/72 mmHg, SpO2 98 percent. No chest pain since discharge. Right wrist puncture site is healed, radial pulse present. She reports mild fatigue by late afternoon. She wants to lift weights and return to tennis soon. She is a friend of your sister and has invited you to dinner and to coach her privately at her home.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Withhold grip exercise, avoid pressure on the lump, and arrange urgent same-day medical assessment): A tender pulsatile lump at a recent arterial access site suggests a pseudoaneurysm, which can enlarge or rupture and needs prompt medical assessment, particularly on dual antiplatelet therapy. Loading the limb with grip work could worsen it. Compression, heat or massage by the physiotherapist are not safe for a suspected arterial injury."}, {"id": 1041, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Hoa has bony nodes at the distal finger joints and also swelling at the base joints of her fingers. The physiotherapist is reasoning about which findings point to an inflammatory process rather than osteoarthritis alone. Which finding most strongly suggests inflammatory arthritis?", "options": {"A": "Thumb base pain on gripping with reduced grip strength and crepitus on carpometacarpal movement", "B": "Reduced wrist flexion with pain at end range and symptoms linked to repeated scissor use", "C": "Bony nodes at several distal interphalangeal joints with pain that worsens late in the day", "D": "Warm swollen MCP joints with prolonged morning stiffness and a positive squeeze test"}, "cluster_id": "CHS21", "cluster_text": "Hoa Le, 54, a hairdresser, attends a private practice with pain and stiffness in both hands. Over six months she has developed swollen, warm metacarpophalangeal (MCP) joints, morning stiffness of about 90 minutes, and fatigue. Her GP has requested bloods and a rheumatology referral is pending. She also has bony nodes at several distal interphalangeal joints and thumb base pain when gripping. Examination: grip strength reduced on both sides, right MCP joints 2 and 3 swollen and tender, MCP squeeze test positive, wrist flexion limited, pain 5 out of 10. Her work involves repeated scissor use and she worries about losing hours. Her sister Mai came with her and is sitting in the room. At the end of the session Mai asks you to explain Hoa's examination findings and what the treatment plan will be.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Warm swollen MCP joints with prolonged morning stiffness and a positive squeeze test): Warmth, soft tissue swelling of the MCP joints, morning stiffness lasting well over 30 minutes and a positive MCP squeeze test are typical of synovitis from inflammatory arthritis. Bony nodes, thumb base pain with crepitus and activity-related pain fit osteoarthritis and mechanical overload. These can coexist, but only the first group points to inflammation."}, {"id": 1042, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Her right MCP joints are currently swollen, warm and tender, and she wants to begin a hand programme at home before the rheumatology review next month. Which approach to exercise and joint protection is most appropriate while the synovitis is active?", "options": {"A": "Gentle active movement within pain limits, joint protection strategies, and avoiding forceful resisted gripping", "B": "Vigorous passive stretching of the MCP joints into end range to prevent contracture formation", "C": "Complete rest of both hands with a rigid splint worn day and night until swelling resolves", "D": "Progressive resisted grip strengthening with putty each day, since stronger muscles protect inflamed joints from damage"}, "cluster_id": "CHS21", "cluster_text": "Hoa Le, 54, a hairdresser, attends a private practice with pain and stiffness in both hands. Over six months she has developed swollen, warm metacarpophalangeal (MCP) joints, morning stiffness of about 90 minutes, and fatigue. Her GP has requested bloods and a rheumatology referral is pending. She also has bony nodes at several distal interphalangeal joints and thumb base pain when gripping. Examination: grip strength reduced on both sides, right MCP joints 2 and 3 swollen and tender, MCP squeeze test positive, wrist flexion limited, pain 5 out of 10. Her work involves repeated scissor use and she worries about losing hours. Her sister Mai came with her and is sitting in the room. At the end of the session Mai asks you to explain Hoa's examination findings and what the treatment plan will be.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Gentle active movement within pain limits, joint protection strategies, and avoiding forceful resisted gripping): During active synovitis the aim is to keep joints moving and muscles active without stressing inflamed structures, so gentle active range and joint protection are appropriate. Heavy resisted gripping and forceful stretching load inflamed capsules and can aggravate damage. Total immobilisation encourages stiffness and weakness."}, {"id": 1043, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Hoa worries about losing hours because she cuts hair all day, six days a week, using a tight pinch grip on standard scissors with her wrist deviated. Which advice best reduces load on her inflamed finger joints while allowing her to keep working?", "options": {"A": "Trial ergonomic scissors with larger handles, take short scheduled breaks, and keep the wrist neutral", "B": "Reduce shifts to part time until rheumatology review, since repeated scissor use accelerates joint damage", "C": "Wear compression gloves and a rigid wrist splint throughout the working day to limit joint movement", "D": "Use a tight pinch with thumb and index finger only, which spares the weaker fingers"}, "cluster_id": "CHS21", "cluster_text": "Hoa Le, 54, a hairdresser, attends a private practice with pain and stiffness in both hands. Over six months she has developed swollen, warm metacarpophalangeal (MCP) joints, morning stiffness of about 90 minutes, and fatigue. Her GP has requested bloods and a rheumatology referral is pending. She also has bony nodes at several distal interphalangeal joints and thumb base pain when gripping. Examination: grip strength reduced on both sides, right MCP joints 2 and 3 swollen and tender, MCP squeeze test positive, wrist flexion limited, pain 5 out of 10. Her work involves repeated scissor use and she worries about losing hours. Her sister Mai came with her and is sitting in the room. At the end of the session Mai asks you to explain Hoa's examination findings and what the treatment plan will be.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Trial ergonomic scissors with larger handles, take short scheduled breaks, and keep the wrist neutral): Equipment with larger handles, pacing with brief breaks, and a neutral wrist reduce force and deviation at the MCP joints while keeping her at work. Concentrating the load on two digits shifts stress to the thumb base and index MCP. Immobilising all day impairs function, and stopping work early is not needed when the task can be modified."}, {"id": 1044, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Hoa's sister Mai asks you to explain the examination findings and the plan while Hoa is in the room. Hoa has capacity but has not said whether she wants Mai to hear her health information. What is the best action by the physiotherapist?", "options": {"A": "Share only the diagnosis and treatment plan, withholding the examination results as these are less sensitive", "B": "Ask Hoa what she wants shared, share only that, and record her decision in the notes", "C": "Share a general summary, since the sister attended the appointment and is clearly supporting Hoa", "D": "Decline to discuss anything and ask the sister to leave, because privacy law prevents sharing"}, "cluster_id": "CHS21", "cluster_text": "Hoa Le, 54, a hairdresser, attends a private practice with pain and stiffness in both hands. Over six months she has developed swollen, warm metacarpophalangeal (MCP) joints, morning stiffness of about 90 minutes, and fatigue. Her GP has requested bloods and a rheumatology referral is pending. She also has bony nodes at several distal interphalangeal joints and thumb base pain when gripping. Examination: grip strength reduced on both sides, right MCP joints 2 and 3 swollen and tender, MCP squeeze test positive, wrist flexion limited, pain 5 out of 10. Her work involves repeated scissor use and she worries about losing hours. Her sister Mai came with her and is sitting in the room. At the end of the session Mai asks you to explain Hoa's examination findings and what the treatment plan will be.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ask Hoa what she wants shared, share only that, and record her decision in the notes): A patient with capacity controls who receives her health information, so the physiotherapist should ask Hoa directly, disclose only what she agrees to, and document the consent. Assuming consent because Mai is present breaches confidentiality. Refusing to speak to family at all is not required when the patient agrees, and sorting information by perceived sensitivity ignores her wishes."}, {"id": 1045, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Cooper understands that early loading may displace the joint surface, yet states he will not use crutches because he risks losing his contract. He is alert, oriented and explains the risks accurately in his own words. How should the physiotherapist respond?", "options": {"A": "Withhold treatment until he agrees to use crutches, as continuing would make you liable for harm", "B": "Explain the risks again firmly and have him sign a waiver releasing you from responsibility", "C": "Respect his informed choice, document the discussion and update the surgeon with his agreement", "D": "Request a formal capacity assessment, since declining medical advice suggests he may lack decision-making capacity"}, "cluster_id": "CHS22", "cluster_text": "Mr Jarrah Cooper, 38, a Noongar man and fly-in fly-out electrician, attends outpatient physiotherapy 7 weeks after fixation of a left lateral tibial plateau fracture (split depression). The surgeon's protocol allowed partial weight bearing up to 20 kg for 6 weeks in a hinged brace, then full weight bearing only after X-ray review and written clearance. The 6 week X-ray review was missed and no clearance has arrived. He has stopped using crutches at home and walks with a mild limp. Pain is 2/10. Knee range is 5 to 105 degrees, quadriceps lag is 10 degrees, there is a small effusion, the calf is soft and non-tender, and the wound is clean. He must return to site in 3 weeks (uneven ground, ladders) or lose the contract. He says he understands the risk and does not want to use crutches again.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Respect his informed choice, document the discussion and update the surgeon with his agreement): A person with capacity may decline advice after the risks are explained, and his accurate account shows he understands them. The physiotherapist should respect the decision, document the discussion and update the surgeon with his agreement. Disagreement alone does not raise doubt about capacity, and withholding care or relying on a waiver does not meet the duty of care."}, {"id": 1046, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "The protocol limited loading to 20 kg for six weeks after fixation of Mr Cooper's split-depression lateral tibial plateau fracture. What is the main reason for restricting weight bearing in this injury, and what is most at risk if loading is too early?", "options": {"A": "Limit knee effusion, since joint loading increases swelling that delays bone healing at the fracture", "B": "Protect the reduced joint surface, since early loading can cause articular depression and malalignment", "C": "Avoid quadriceps inhibition, because loading the limb early reduces muscle activation and delays strength recovery", "D": "Protect the plate from fatigue failure, because implants cannot carry bodyweight before the fracture has united"}, "cluster_id": "CHS22", "cluster_text": "Mr Jarrah Cooper, 38, a Noongar man and fly-in fly-out electrician, attends outpatient physiotherapy 7 weeks after fixation of a left lateral tibial plateau fracture (split depression). The surgeon's protocol allowed partial weight bearing up to 20 kg for 6 weeks in a hinged brace, then full weight bearing only after X-ray review and written clearance. The 6 week X-ray review was missed and no clearance has arrived. He has stopped using crutches at home and walks with a mild limp. Pain is 2/10. Knee range is 5 to 105 degrees, quadriceps lag is 10 degrees, there is a small effusion, the calf is soft and non-tender, and the wound is clean. He must return to site in 3 weeks (uneven ground, ladders) or lose the contract. He says he understands the risk and does not want to use crutches again.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Protect the reduced joint surface, since early loading can cause articular depression and malalignment): In a split-depression fracture the elevated articular fragment is held by healing bone and fixation, and early load can let it sink or shift, damaging the joint surface. Implant fatigue is a lesser concern here, and swelling or quadriceps inhibition are not the main reason for the restriction."}, {"id": 1047, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Cooper's knee lacks 5 degrees of extension, shows a 10 degree quadriceps lag and a small effusion, and he remains restricted from full weight bearing. Which exercise is most appropriate to safely improve quadriceps control within the surgeon's restrictions?", "options": {"A": "Closed chain mini squats to sixty degrees with bodyweight, to retrain quadriceps in functional positions", "B": "Seated open chain knee extension with an ankle weight, progressing resistance weekly to build strength", "C": "Stationary cycling against moderate resistance for twenty minutes to restore range and quadriceps endurance", "D": "Quadriceps setting and brace-supported straight leg raises, adding electrical stimulation if lag persists"}, "cluster_id": "CHS22", "cluster_text": "Mr Jarrah Cooper, 38, a Noongar man and fly-in fly-out electrician, attends outpatient physiotherapy 7 weeks after fixation of a left lateral tibial plateau fracture (split depression). The surgeon's protocol allowed partial weight bearing up to 20 kg for 6 weeks in a hinged brace, then full weight bearing only after X-ray review and written clearance. The 6 week X-ray review was missed and no clearance has arrived. He has stopped using crutches at home and walks with a mild limp. Pain is 2/10. Knee range is 5 to 105 degrees, quadriceps lag is 10 degrees, there is a small effusion, the calf is soft and non-tender, and the wound is clean. He must return to site in 3 weeks (uneven ground, ladders) or lose the contract. He says he understands the risk and does not want to use crutches again.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Quadriceps setting and brace-supported straight leg raises, adding electrical stimulation if lag persists): Quadriceps setting and brace-supported straight leg raises work the lag without loading the joint surface, and electrical stimulation can help voluntary activation. Closed chain squats and cycling against resistance load the knee, and loaded open chain extension creates a long lever across the healing plateau."}, {"id": 1048, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Cooper asks whether he may now walk without crutches because he feels fine. The surgeon's written clearance has not arrived and the six week X-ray was missed. What is the most appropriate action for the physiotherapist to take today?", "options": {"A": "Allow full weight bearing as six weeks have passed and his symptoms have settled well", "B": "Allow half bodyweight bearing as a compromise until the next review, recording that clearance is outstanding", "C": "Request an X-ray through his GP and allow full weight bearing once the report shows union", "D": "Contact the surgeon's rooms today for imaging and clearance, keeping protected loading until then"}, "cluster_id": "CHS22", "cluster_text": "Mr Jarrah Cooper, 38, a Noongar man and fly-in fly-out electrician, attends outpatient physiotherapy 7 weeks after fixation of a left lateral tibial plateau fracture (split depression). The surgeon's protocol allowed partial weight bearing up to 20 kg for 6 weeks in a hinged brace, then full weight bearing only after X-ray review and written clearance. The 6 week X-ray review was missed and no clearance has arrived. He has stopped using crutches at home and walks with a mild limp. Pain is 2/10. Knee range is 5 to 105 degrees, quadriceps lag is 10 degrees, there is a small effusion, the calf is soft and non-tender, and the wound is clean. He must return to site in 3 weeks (uneven ground, ladders) or lose the contract. He says he understands the risk and does not want to use crutches again.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Contact the surgeon's rooms today for imaging and clearance, keeping protected loading until then): Full weight bearing needs written surgical clearance after X-ray review, and the physiotherapist cannot grant it on the basis of time or symptoms. Contacting the surgeon's rooms promptly closes the gap while protected loading continues. Half bodyweight is an unauthorised compromise, and a GP-requested report does not replace the surgeon's clearance."}, {"id": 1049, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "During the ulnar neurodynamic test Tomasi's tingling increases when his wrist is extended and eases when it returns to neutral, and it does not change with neck side flexion away. What does this response most strongly suggest about the likely source of his symptoms?", "options": {"A": "A peripheral source along the ulnar nerve, since a distal movement changes the symptoms", "B": "A cervical radiculopathy, since symptoms are reproduced by loading the nerve root through arm positioning", "C": "A muscular restriction in forearm flexors, since wrist movement alone changes the stretch sensation felt", "D": "A central sensitisation pattern, since the response varies with position rather than following a nerve pathway"}, "cluster_id": "CHS23", "cluster_text": "Tomasi Liufau, 16, a Year 11 student and club rower, attends a community physiotherapy clinic with his mother. He reports six weeks of intermittent tingling in the ring and little fingers of his left hand, worse after long gaming sessions and when his phone is held to his ear with the elbow bent. Rowing training is five mornings a week. There is no neck pain, no trauma and no night waking. Examination: cervical spine movement is full and pain free, and the Spurling test is negative. A 60 second elbow flexion test reproduces tingling in the little finger, and Tinel sign over the cubital tunnel is positive. A neurodynamic test for the ulnar nerve reproduces his tingling. Grip and finger abduction strength are 5 out of 5, light touch is intact, and there is no intrinsic muscle wasting. He sleeps with his arms bent under his pillow. He asks what he can do himself and whether he must stop rowing.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A peripheral source along the ulnar nerve, since a distal movement changes the symptoms): In neurodynamic testing, a response that changes when a movement is added or removed at a site distant from the symptoms (structural differentiation) points to a neural, peripheral origin. Together with the positive Tinel sign and elbow flexion test, this supports ulnar nerve irritation at the elbow. A cervical source would more likely change with neck position, and muscle tightness would not follow the nerve."}, {"id": 1050, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Tomasi's neurological examination is normal apart from provoked tingling, and he has a regional rowing event in six weeks. He asks whether to stop training altogether. Which advice best balances nerve protection with his sporting goals at this stage of his recovery?", "options": {"A": "Stop all rowing until symptoms resolve, then return gradually using a structured graded programme", "B": "Continue full training and add firm nerve stretching until tingling is provoked, to desensitise the nerve", "C": "Continue rowing at modified load, limit prolonged elbow flexion outside training, and monitor symptoms", "D": "Switch to gym-based strengthening only, since rowing itself is the sole cause of his nerve irritation"}, "cluster_id": "CHS23", "cluster_text": "Tomasi Liufau, 16, a Year 11 student and club rower, attends a community physiotherapy clinic with his mother. He reports six weeks of intermittent tingling in the ring and little fingers of his left hand, worse after long gaming sessions and when his phone is held to his ear with the elbow bent. Rowing training is five mornings a week. There is no neck pain, no trauma and no night waking. Examination: cervical spine movement is full and pain free, and the Spurling test is negative. A 60 second elbow flexion test reproduces tingling in the little finger, and Tinel sign over the cubital tunnel is positive. A neurodynamic test for the ulnar nerve reproduces his tingling. Grip and finger abduction strength are 5 out of 5, light touch is intact, and there is no intrinsic muscle wasting. He sleeps with his arms bent under his pillow. He asks what he can do himself and whether he must stop rowing.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Continue rowing at modified load, limit prolonged elbow flexion outside training, and monitor symptoms): With normal strength and sensation and only provoked symptoms, the nerve irritation is mild, so activity can usually continue with load modification while contributing postures are reduced. Complete rest is not required and delays conditioning, while forcing a stretch into symptoms can aggravate an irritable nerve. Rowing is one of several contributors, along with gaming and sleeping postures."}, {"id": 1051, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Tomasi and his mother want practical self-management steps. He sleeps with his elbows bent under his pillow and games for hours on end. Which strategy would best support his self-management and help prevent a return of his nerve symptoms between appointments?", "options": {"A": "Co-develop a simple plan with regular gaming breaks, a towel wrap at night, and symptom tracking", "B": "Provide a printed exercise handout and ask him to follow it daily until symptoms stop", "C": "Instruct him to keep the elbow straight during every daytime activity, including gaming and rowing training", "D": "Tell his mother to supervise his device use and enforce screen limits until his symptoms resolve"}, "cluster_id": "CHS23", "cluster_text": "Tomasi Liufau, 16, a Year 11 student and club rower, attends a community physiotherapy clinic with his mother. He reports six weeks of intermittent tingling in the ring and little fingers of his left hand, worse after long gaming sessions and when his phone is held to his ear with the elbow bent. Rowing training is five mornings a week. There is no neck pain, no trauma and no night waking. Examination: cervical spine movement is full and pain free, and the Spurling test is negative. A 60 second elbow flexion test reproduces tingling in the little finger, and Tinel sign over the cubital tunnel is positive. A neurodynamic test for the ulnar nerve reproduces his tingling. Grip and finger abduction strength are 5 out of 5, light touch is intact, and there is no intrinsic muscle wasting. He sleeps with his arms bent under his pillow. He asks what he can do himself and whether he must stop rowing.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Co-develop a simple plan with regular gaming breaks, a towel wrap at night, and symptom tracking): Self-management in adolescents works best when the young person helps set the plan, so changes fit his gaming and sleep habits and he can monitor his own response. Keeping the elbow only partly flexed during sleep and breaking up sustained flexion reduces cubital tunnel load. A handout alone, blanket restriction or parent-enforced limits give less ownership and are less likely to be sustained."}, {"id": 1052, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Three weeks later Tomasi reports numbness in the little finger that now persists all day, and he has noticed clumsiness when spreading his fingers. Finger abduction is now grade 4 and there is slight wasting of the first dorsal interosseous. What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Add ulnar nerve gliding exercises and review in two weeks to check if strength improves", "B": "Refer him to his GP for further assessment, such as nerve conduction studies and specialist opinion", "C": "Continue the current programme for another month, since mild motor changes usually recover with conservative care", "D": "Apply a night splint and ask him to record symptoms for a month before any referral"}, "cluster_id": "CHS23", "cluster_text": "Tomasi Liufau, 16, a Year 11 student and club rower, attends a community physiotherapy clinic with his mother. He reports six weeks of intermittent tingling in the ring and little fingers of his left hand, worse after long gaming sessions and when his phone is held to his ear with the elbow bent. Rowing training is five mornings a week. There is no neck pain, no trauma and no night waking. Examination: cervical spine movement is full and pain free, and the Spurling test is negative. A 60 second elbow flexion test reproduces tingling in the little finger, and Tinel sign over the cubital tunnel is positive. A neurodynamic test for the ulnar nerve reproduces his tingling. Grip and finger abduction strength are 5 out of 5, light touch is intact, and there is no intrinsic muscle wasting. He sleeps with his arms bent under his pillow. He asks what he can do himself and whether he must stop rowing.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Refer him to his GP for further assessment, such as nerve conduction studies and specialist opinion): New persistent sensory loss with weakness and wasting of an ulnar-innervated muscle signals progressive nerve involvement, which goes beyond a mild irritation managed conservatively. Prompt referral for medical assessment and nerve conduction testing is needed because delay can risk lasting motor loss. Continuing the same plan, adding glides or using a splint alone would defer a decision that is now time-sensitive."}, {"id": 1053, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Kim is 20 months old, cruises along furniture and takes no independent steps. Her reflexes are normal, movement is symmetrical, hip range is full, and cognition and communication are typical for her age. Which interpretation should the physiotherapist give her parents about today's findings?", "options": {"A": "Within normal variation for age, so reassure the family and discharge with open access", "B": "Delay caused by W-sitting, so stop that posture and expect walking to begin within weeks", "C": "Mild gross motor delay without red flags, so monitor with a home programme and planned review", "D": "Hypotonia suggesting a neuromuscular disorder, so request urgent specialist testing before starting any programme"}, "cluster_id": "CHS24", "cluster_text": "Kim Vo, 20 months, was referred by her GP for delayed walking. She was born at 37 weeks after an uncomplicated pregnancy and has had no hospital admissions. Her parents report she pulls to stand, cruises along furniture and bottom shuffles, but has never taken independent steps. She walks well when holding two hands and prefers to W-sit on the floor. Her trunk tone is mildly low, limb reflexes are normal, there is no clonus, and hip range is full and symmetrical. Growth tracks along her percentile line, and vision, hearing, babbling and following of simple instructions are typical. Kim's mother attends with her grandmother. Her childcare educator phones the clinic during the session asking what was found and what the centre should practise.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Mild gross motor delay without red flags, so monitor with a home programme and planned review): Walking is usually established by about 18 months, so Kim is delayed, but symmetrical movement, normal reflexes and typical cognition mean there are no red flags for a specific pathology. Monitoring with a home programme and review is proportionate. Discharging her as normal would miss a delay, and urgent specialist testing is not justified by mildly low trunk tone alone."}, {"id": 1054, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Kim's parents ask what to practise at home between clinic visits. She pulls to stand and cruises along furniture but does not yet release her support to take steps. Which single home activity is most appropriate to progress her towards independent walking?", "options": {"A": "Extended periods in a supportive standing frame to strengthen her legs before any stepping", "B": "Holding both her hands and walking her around the house for long periods each day", "C": "Cruising along a sofa with gaps gradually widened, using toys held just out of reach", "D": "Daily time in a wheeled baby walker so she practises stepping with her weight supported"}, "cluster_id": "CHS24", "cluster_text": "Kim Vo, 20 months, was referred by her GP for delayed walking. She was born at 37 weeks after an uncomplicated pregnancy and has had no hospital admissions. Her parents report she pulls to stand, cruises along furniture and bottom shuffles, but has never taken independent steps. She walks well when holding two hands and prefers to W-sit on the floor. Her trunk tone is mildly low, limb reflexes are normal, there is no clonus, and hip range is full and symmetrical. Growth tracks along her percentile line, and vision, hearing, babbling and following of simple instructions are typical. Kim's mother attends with her grandmother. Her childcare educator phones the clinic during the session asking what was found and what the centre should practise.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Cruising along a sofa with gaps gradually widened, using toys held just out of reach): Graded cruising with widening gaps challenges balance and weight shift in a task she is already close to achieving, and a motivating toy encourages her to let go. Baby walkers do not develop independent balance and carry injury risk, and prolonged hand holding or frame standing encourages dependence on external support."}, {"id": 1055, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "At review four months later, Kim is 24 months old and still does not walk. Her parents now report she uses her left hand less and reaches asymmetrically, and the physiotherapist finds increased tone in her left leg. What is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Continue the home programme and review again in three months before contacting any other clinician", "B": "Refer promptly to a paediatrician for neurological assessment and update the GP about the asymmetry", "C": "Ask the parents to film her movements at home and wait for the next planned review", "D": "Add left sided strengthening exercises and reassess after four further treatment sessions with her family"}, "cluster_id": "CHS24", "cluster_text": "Kim Vo, 20 months, was referred by her GP for delayed walking. She was born at 37 weeks after an uncomplicated pregnancy and has had no hospital admissions. Her parents report she pulls to stand, cruises along furniture and bottom shuffles, but has never taken independent steps. She walks well when holding two hands and prefers to W-sit on the floor. Her trunk tone is mildly low, limb reflexes are normal, there is no clonus, and hip range is full and symmetrical. Growth tracks along her percentile line, and vision, hearing, babbling and following of simple instructions are typical. Kim's mother attends with her grandmother. Her childcare educator phones the clinic during the session asking what was found and what the centre should practise.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Refer promptly to a paediatrician for neurological assessment and update the GP about the asymmetry): New asymmetry and increased tone indicate a possible upper motor neuron lesion, which is a change from the earlier picture and needs prompt medical investigation. Continuing therapy or waiting delays diagnosis and appropriate management. Strengthening alone does not address a suspected central cause."}, {"id": 1056, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "During the session Kim's childcare educator phones the clinic, asking what was found and what the centre should practise. Kim's parents have not given consent to share any information with the centre. How should the physiotherapist respond to the educator's phone call now?", "options": {"A": "Send a written report to the centre director, who holds responsibility for the educators' records", "B": "Share only general advice about walking activities, since no diagnosis or clinical findings are being disclosed", "C": "Give a brief verbal summary of findings because the educator shares responsibility for Kim's daily care", "D": "Explain that sharing needs parental consent, offer to ask the parents, and document the call"}, "cluster_id": "CHS24", "cluster_text": "Kim Vo, 20 months, was referred by her GP for delayed walking. She was born at 37 weeks after an uncomplicated pregnancy and has had no hospital admissions. Her parents report she pulls to stand, cruises along furniture and bottom shuffles, but has never taken independent steps. She walks well when holding two hands and prefers to W-sit on the floor. Her trunk tone is mildly low, limb reflexes are normal, there is no clonus, and hip range is full and symmetrical. Growth tracks along her percentile line, and vision, hearing, babbling and following of simple instructions are typical. Kim's mother attends with her grandmother. Her childcare educator phones the clinic during the session asking what was found and what the centre should practise.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain that sharing needs parental consent, offer to ask the parents, and document the call): A child's health information is confidential and can be disclosed to a third party only with consent from a person with parental responsibility. Offering to seek consent keeps the centre involved appropriately, and documenting the call records the contact. Shared care of the child does not itself authorise disclosure."}, {"id": 1057, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Marino freezes when approaching the kitchen doorway and when turning. He has good cognition and no postural hypotension. Maria reports he gets stuck more when hurrying. Which strategy should the physiotherapist teach first to help him start moving again when a freeze occurs?", "options": {"A": "Lean the trunk forward and push off sharply to generate momentum through the stuck feet", "B": "Use an external cue such as stepping over a floor line or a rhythmic count", "C": "Concentrate on lifting each foot high while looking down at the feet through the doorway", "D": "Pause until the freeze passes, then restart with small quick steps to keep up momentum"}, "cluster_id": "CHS25", "cluster_text": "Salvatore Marino, 74, retired builder, lives with his wife Maria in a single-storey home in Wollongong. He has had Parkinson disease for nine years and takes levodopa with carbidopa four times daily. His GP referred him after two falls in three months, both when stopping at the kitchen doorway and turning. He describes his feet 'sticking to the floor', worse when rushing or when his medication is wearing off. Findings: stooped posture, reduced arm swing, short shuffling steps, freezing at doorways and on turning, Timed Up and Go 19 seconds without an aid, no postural hypotension on lying and standing blood pressure. Cognition is intact. He wants regular physiotherapy but lives on a pension and has no private health insurance. His GP has not yet written a care plan. Maria asks what help is available once the initial sessions finish.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use an external cue such as stepping over a floor line or a rhythmic count): External visual or auditory cues bypass the impaired automatic movement control in Parkinson disease and are the first-line approach for freezing. Leaning and pushing off shifts his centre of mass forward and raises fall risk, and small quick steps tend to feed the freeze rather than release it."}, {"id": 1058, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Two weeks later Mr Marino reports another near fall in the kitchen at home late in the afternoon while carrying a cup of tea and talking to Maria. He froze at the doorway. What advice about managing combined tasks like this is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Walk faster through doorways so that less time is spent in the area where freezing occurs", "B": "Simplify tasks near doorways, then add dual tasking gradually once cued stepping is reliable", "C": "Stop carrying items and ask Maria to manage drinks and meals to remove the risk", "D": "Practise dual tasking daily at full difficulty so the brain adapts and freezing reduces"}, "cluster_id": "CHS25", "cluster_text": "Salvatore Marino, 74, retired builder, lives with his wife Maria in a single-storey home in Wollongong. He has had Parkinson disease for nine years and takes levodopa with carbidopa four times daily. His GP referred him after two falls in three months, both when stopping at the kitchen doorway and turning. He describes his feet 'sticking to the floor', worse when rushing or when his medication is wearing off. Findings: stooped posture, reduced arm swing, short shuffling steps, freezing at doorways and on turning, Timed Up and Go 19 seconds without an aid, no postural hypotension on lying and standing blood pressure. Cognition is intact. He wants regular physiotherapy but lives on a pension and has no private health insurance. His GP has not yet written a care plan. Maria asks what help is available once the initial sessions finish.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Simplify tasks near doorways, then add dual tasking gradually once cued stepping is reliable): Dual tasking overloads the attention-based compensation people with Parkinson disease rely on, so tasks are separated at high-risk points and combined only as cueing becomes dependable. Removing all carrying takes away independence and meaningful roles, while hurrying is a known trigger for freezing."}, {"id": 1059, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "To monitor change in his freezing over the coming months, the physiotherapist wants a patient-reported measure specific to freezing of gait. Mr Marino's current Timed Up and Go time already provides a baseline for mobility. Which measure is most suitable to add?", "options": {"A": "The Freezing of Gait Questionnaire, which records how often and how severely he freezes", "B": "The Hoehn and Yahr scale, which stages disease progression using bilateral involvement and postural instability", "C": "The 6 minute walk test, which measures walking endurance and records any pauses during the walk", "D": "The Berg Balance Scale, which scores static and dynamic balance tasks for fall risk estimation"}, "cluster_id": "CHS25", "cluster_text": "Salvatore Marino, 74, retired builder, lives with his wife Maria in a single-storey home in Wollongong. He has had Parkinson disease for nine years and takes levodopa with carbidopa four times daily. His GP referred him after two falls in three months, both when stopping at the kitchen doorway and turning. He describes his feet 'sticking to the floor', worse when rushing or when his medication is wearing off. Findings: stooped posture, reduced arm swing, short shuffling steps, freezing at doorways and on turning, Timed Up and Go 19 seconds without an aid, no postural hypotension on lying and standing blood pressure. Cognition is intact. He wants regular physiotherapy but lives on a pension and has no private health insurance. His GP has not yet written a care plan. Maria asks what help is available once the initial sessions finish.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The Freezing of Gait Questionnaire, which records how often and how severely he freezes): The Freezing of Gait Questionnaire is designed to capture the frequency and severity of freezing from the patient's own report. The other tools measure balance, endurance or disease stage, and none quantifies freezing episodes directly."}, {"id": 1060, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Maria asks how Mr Marino can keep getting therapy and home support after the first sessions, given their limited income and no private health cover. He is 74 and has not yet had any government assessment. What is the best advice for the physiotherapist to give?", "options": {"A": "Continue weekly private sessions on a payment plan and review his progress each month", "B": "Discharge him with a written home programme, since subsidised services are limited to younger adults", "C": "Ask his GP about a chronic disease management plan and contact My Aged Care for assessment", "D": "Apply for NDIS access, since his Parkinson disease is a permanent condition affecting daily activities"}, "cluster_id": "CHS25", "cluster_text": "Salvatore Marino, 74, retired builder, lives with his wife Maria in a single-storey home in Wollongong. He has had Parkinson disease for nine years and takes levodopa with carbidopa four times daily. His GP referred him after two falls in three months, both when stopping at the kitchen doorway and turning. He describes his feet 'sticking to the floor', worse when rushing or when his medication is wearing off. Findings: stooped posture, reduced arm swing, short shuffling steps, freezing at doorways and on turning, Timed Up and Go 19 seconds without an aid, no postural hypotension on lying and standing blood pressure. Cognition is intact. He wants regular physiotherapy but lives on a pension and has no private health insurance. His GP has not yet written a care plan. Maria asks what help is available once the initial sessions finish.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ask his GP about a chronic disease management plan and contact My Aged Care for assessment): A GP plan can open rebated allied health visits under Medicare, and My Aged Care assesses older Australians for ongoing subsidised support such as the Commonwealth Home Support Programme. The NDIS is generally not available to people first applying after 65, and discharging him ignores existing pathways for older adults."}, {"id": 1061, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Darren says he has thought about stopping but is not ready to quit today. In a brief conversation between exercise stations, which approach best fits his readiness and current evidence for smoking cessation support in cardiac rehabilitation after his infarct?", "options": {"A": "Explore his ambivalence respectfully, advise quitting clearly and keep the offer of support open", "B": "Present graphic data on repeat infarction risk from smoking so that fear prompts him to quit", "C": "Set a quit date this week and write a detailed plan including a nicotine replacement schedule", "D": "Defer smoking until the final session, because early discussion may reduce his attendance at exercise"}, "cluster_id": "CHS26", "cluster_text": "Darren Walsh, 58, is a plumber attending a Phase II outpatient cardiac rehabilitation program three weeks after a non ST elevation myocardial infarction treated with a drug eluting stent. He smokes about 15 cigarettes a day and enjoys them with his crew at smoko. He says he has thought about stopping but is not ready to quit. Medications: aspirin, ticagrelor, atorvastatin, ramipril and metoprolol. Resting HR 62, BP 134/84, SpO2 97 percent, BMI 31. Six minute walk test 390 m, finishing at Borg 13 with no chest pain. The program runs eight sessions over four weeks and includes a hospital group education class. Today the physiotherapist is the only clinician on the gym floor with eight participants exercising.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Explore his ambivalence respectfully, advise quitting clearly and keep the offer of support open): A motivational approach respects that he is thinking about change but is not ready, and brief advice plus an open offer of help keeps support available. Pushing a quit date before readiness usually meets resistance. Fear based messaging and delaying the conversation both waste the chance to build motivation while his cardiac event is recent."}, {"id": 1062, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Darren takes metoprolol, his resting heart rate is 62, and his six minute walk ended at Borg 13 without symptoms. The physiotherapist is setting initial aerobic exercise intensity for the program. Which method is most appropriate for prescribing and monitoring intensity?", "options": {"A": "Keep intensity low enough to sing comfortably, using the talk test without any extra symptom monitoring", "B": "Heart rate between 70 and 85 percent of predicted maximum, using 220 minus his age", "C": "Moderate perceived exertion with symptom and blood pressure checks, as beta blockers blunt heart rate", "D": "Match his walk test pace and increase speed each session until he reaches Borg 17 effort"}, "cluster_id": "CHS26", "cluster_text": "Darren Walsh, 58, is a plumber attending a Phase II outpatient cardiac rehabilitation program three weeks after a non ST elevation myocardial infarction treated with a drug eluting stent. He smokes about 15 cigarettes a day and enjoys them with his crew at smoko. He says he has thought about stopping but is not ready to quit. Medications: aspirin, ticagrelor, atorvastatin, ramipril and metoprolol. Resting HR 62, BP 134/84, SpO2 97 percent, BMI 31. Six minute walk test 390 m, finishing at Borg 13 with no chest pain. The program runs eight sessions over four weeks and includes a hospital group education class. Today the physiotherapist is the only clinician on the gym floor with eight participants exercising.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Moderate perceived exertion with symptom and blood pressure checks, as beta blockers blunt heart rate): Beta blockade blunts the heart rate response, so predicted heart rate zones are unreliable, and 220 minus age is only an estimate. Perceived exertion, along with symptom and blood pressure monitoring, guides safe moderate intensity. Pushing to a very high effort rating is not appropriate early after infarction, and the talk test alone gives too little safety information."}, {"id": 1063, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Two weeks later Darren says he is ready to quit and asks whether nicotine patches are safe after a heart attack and whether the physiotherapist can supply some. What is the most appropriate response within physiotherapy scope of practice that supports his attempt?", "options": {"A": "Advise him to avoid nicotine replacement because it raises cardiac risk, and to stop abruptly instead", "B": "Tell him patches are safe after an infarction and give a starter pack from ward stock", "C": "Affirm his decision, link him to his GP or Quitline for medication advice, and follow up", "D": "Explain that cessation advice belongs to the cardiologist, so he should wait for his next appointment"}, "cluster_id": "CHS26", "cluster_text": "Darren Walsh, 58, is a plumber attending a Phase II outpatient cardiac rehabilitation program three weeks after a non ST elevation myocardial infarction treated with a drug eluting stent. He smokes about 15 cigarettes a day and enjoys them with his crew at smoko. He says he has thought about stopping but is not ready to quit. Medications: aspirin, ticagrelor, atorvastatin, ramipril and metoprolol. Resting HR 62, BP 134/84, SpO2 97 percent, BMI 31. Six minute walk test 390 m, finishing at Borg 13 with no chest pain. The program runs eight sessions over four weeks and includes a hospital group education class. Today the physiotherapist is the only clinician on the gym floor with eight participants exercising.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Affirm his decision, link him to his GP or Quitline for medication advice, and follow up): Physiotherapists support quitting through brief advice, motivation and referral, but advice on and supply of nicotine replacement belong to the GP, pharmacist or Quitline. Affirming his readiness and linking him to those services supports the attempt safely. Handing out products or discouraging replacement therapy goes beyond scope, and waiting wastes a window of readiness."}, {"id": 1064, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "During a busy session, a participant on the bike reports new central chest tightness and looks pale. At the same moment Darren asks for help with his exercise diary, a new referral calls the desk, and the next group is arriving. As the only clinician present, what should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Lower the bike intensity and recheck symptoms in two minutes, continuing only if the tightness settles", "B": "Stop the exercise, seat the participant, check symptoms and observations, and activate the emergency plan", "C": "Ask a participant to watch the rider, finish Darren's diary help, then reassess the rider", "D": "Stop the bike, seat the participant, check their pulse, and resume once the tightness eases"}, "cluster_id": "CHS26", "cluster_text": "Darren Walsh, 58, is a plumber attending a Phase II outpatient cardiac rehabilitation program three weeks after a non ST elevation myocardial infarction treated with a drug eluting stent. He smokes about 15 cigarettes a day and enjoys them with his crew at smoko. He says he has thought about stopping but is not ready to quit. Medications: aspirin, ticagrelor, atorvastatin, ramipril and metoprolol. Resting HR 62, BP 134/84, SpO2 97 percent, BMI 31. Six minute walk test 390 m, finishing at Borg 13 with no chest pain. The program runs eight sessions over four weeks and includes a hospital group education class. Today the physiotherapist is the only clinician on the gym floor with eight participants exercising.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stop the exercise, seat the participant, check symptoms and observations, and activate the emergency plan): New central chest tightness with pallor during exercise is a potential cardiac emergency, so exercise stops, the person is seated, symptoms and observations are checked and the emergency plan is activated. Lowering intensity and waiting delays recognition of a possible acute coronary event. Asking another participant to watch the rider leaves a symptomatic person without a clinician while a routine task is finished. Stopping and checking the pulse is correct as far as it goes, but resuming once it eases skips escalation."}, {"id": 1065, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mia is on day 4 after PCI and walks along the ward corridor with stable observations, feeling well. Her heart rate rose modestly from 74 to 96 yesterday without symptoms. Which approach to monitoring exercise intensity is most appropriate for this early inpatient phase?", "options": {"A": "Use the talk test alone, stopping only if she becomes unable to speak in sentences", "B": "Progress distance daily until she reports breathlessness at a hard rating, then rest and repeat tomorrow", "C": "Keep effort light to somewhat hard on the Borg scale, monitoring symptoms and heart rate response", "D": "Aim for eighty percent of age predicted maximum heart rate, stopping once that target is reached"}, "cluster_id": "CHS27", "cluster_text": "Mia Tran, 52, is a trans woman and hairdresser, admitted four days ago with an inferior ST elevation myocardial infarction treated by primary PCI to the right coronary artery via the right radial artery. Echocardiogram shows an ejection fraction of 50 percent. History: smoker (20 per day), hypertension, and gender affirming oestrogen therapy for six years. Observations at rest: HR 74, BP 118/72, RR 16, SpO2 97 percent on room air. Yesterday she walked 50 metres on the ward with HR rising to 96 and no symptoms. Today she is keen to walk further. The radial site is clean with no swelling. She sleeps poorly and says she has avoided health services since staff repeatedly used her former name. Her chart still lists that name and male sex, and she asks to be called Mia, using she and her. She says she dreads group exercise classes.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Keep effort light to somewhat hard on the Borg scale, monitoring symptoms and heart rate response): In the early phase after an acute coronary event, intensity is guided by a low to moderate perceived exertion rating together with symptoms, heart rate and blood pressure responses. A fixed percentage of predicted maximum heart rate is a poor guide this soon after infarction, and pushing to a hard rating exceeds the cautious progression expected on the ward. The talk test on its own ignores the haemodynamic response that must also be watched."}, {"id": 1066, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "During the corridor walk, after about sixty metres, Mia reports central chest tightness rated four out of ten and looks pale. HR is 98 and SpO2 96 percent on room air. She asks to rest and continue shortly. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Allow a seated rest, then resume more slowly if the tightness settles within a few minutes", "B": "Return her to bed, give sublingual glyceryl trinitrate from ward stock, and record the response afterwards", "C": "Stop activity, sit her down, monitor observations and notify the nurse in charge immediately", "D": "Finish the planned walk, then rate the tightness again and report it if it persists"}, "cluster_id": "CHS27", "cluster_text": "Mia Tran, 52, is a trans woman and hairdresser, admitted four days ago with an inferior ST elevation myocardial infarction treated by primary PCI to the right coronary artery via the right radial artery. Echocardiogram shows an ejection fraction of 50 percent. History: smoker (20 per day), hypertension, and gender affirming oestrogen therapy for six years. Observations at rest: HR 74, BP 118/72, RR 16, SpO2 97 percent on room air. Yesterday she walked 50 metres on the ward with HR rising to 96 and no symptoms. Today she is keen to walk further. The radial site is clean with no swelling. She sleeps poorly and says she has avoided health services since staff repeatedly used her former name. Her chart still lists that name and male sex, and she asks to be called Mia, using she and her. She says she dreads group exercise classes.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop activity, sit her down, monitor observations and notify the nurse in charge immediately): New chest tightness with pallor after a recent infarction is a stop criterion, so exercise ceases and she is seated while observations are checked and the nursing and medical team are told straight away. Resuming after a short rest delays assessment of possible ischaemia. Administering medication is outside physiotherapy scope and is a decision for the treating team."}, {"id": 1067, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mia says she will not attend group cardiac rehabilitation because staff have misgendered her before, though her cardiologist advised a programme soon after discharge. She still smokes and wants to stay active. Which approach to secondary prevention best supports her self-management?", "options": {"A": "Provide a written home walking programme and cessation leaflet, then close the physiotherapy referral at discharge", "B": "Explain group sessions give the best outcomes, and encourage her to attend one before deciding", "C": "Refer her to a gender health service, and defer rehabilitation until her concerns have been resolved", "D": "Offer individual or telehealth rehabilitation, ensure staff use her name, and arrange cessation support"}, "cluster_id": "CHS27", "cluster_text": "Mia Tran, 52, is a trans woman and hairdresser, admitted four days ago with an inferior ST elevation myocardial infarction treated by primary PCI to the right coronary artery via the right radial artery. Echocardiogram shows an ejection fraction of 50 percent. History: smoker (20 per day), hypertension, and gender affirming oestrogen therapy for six years. Observations at rest: HR 74, BP 118/72, RR 16, SpO2 97 percent on room air. Yesterday she walked 50 metres on the ward with HR rising to 96 and no symptoms. Today she is keen to walk further. The radial site is clean with no swelling. She sleeps poorly and says she has avoided health services since staff repeatedly used her former name. Her chart still lists that name and male sex, and she asks to be called Mia, using she and her. She says she dreads group exercise classes.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Offer individual or telehealth rehabilitation, ensure staff use her name, and arrange cessation support): Rehabilitation delivered in a format she will accept, by a team that uses her correct name and pronouns, keeps her engaged and addresses smoking, a major modifiable risk. Pressing group attendance ignores the barrier she has named. Discharging with only a leaflet leaves a high risk patient without support, and delaying rehabilitation until a referral resolves her concerns loses the early window for secondary prevention."}, {"id": 1068, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "The next morning Mia reports left calf pain and swelling after sitting for most of yesterday. HR is 80, SpO2 97 percent, and foot pulses are palpable. She is eager to walk with the physiotherapist. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Perform Homans sign to confirm or exclude thrombosis before deciding whether to notify the medical team", "B": "Encourage a short walk to promote circulation, then reassess the calf afterwards for any change", "C": "Apply compression stockings and elevate the leg, then review the calf at her next session", "D": "Withhold walking, avoid massaging the calf, and report the findings to the medical team for assessment"}, "cluster_id": "CHS27", "cluster_text": "Mia Tran, 52, is a trans woman and hairdresser, admitted four days ago with an inferior ST elevation myocardial infarction treated by primary PCI to the right coronary artery via the right radial artery. Echocardiogram shows an ejection fraction of 50 percent. History: smoker (20 per day), hypertension, and gender affirming oestrogen therapy for six years. Observations at rest: HR 74, BP 118/72, RR 16, SpO2 97 percent on room air. Yesterday she walked 50 metres on the ward with HR rising to 96 and no symptoms. Today she is keen to walk further. The radial site is clean with no swelling. She sleeps poorly and says she has avoided health services since staff repeatedly used her former name. Her chart still lists that name and male sex, and she asks to be called Mia, using she and her. She says she dreads group exercise classes.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Withhold walking, avoid massaging the calf, and report the findings to the medical team for assessment): Unilateral calf pain and swelling in a smoker taking oestrogen after reduced mobility raises concern for deep vein thrombosis, so mobilisation and massage are withheld until medical assessment. Walking or stockings before diagnosis risk dislodging a clot or delaying treatment. Homans sign is unreliable and should not delay escalation."}, {"id": 1069, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Rekha takes a beta blocker, and her smartwatch heart rate rises little even when she feels she is working hard. The physiotherapist is prescribing aerobic intensity for home walking. Which method of setting and monitoring intensity is most appropriate for her?", "options": {"A": "Set a target as a percentage of age-predicted maximum heart rate, raised weekly as she improves", "B": "Base intensity on resting blood pressure before each session, increasing walking time when readings stay low", "C": "Use a rating of perceived exertion in the moderate range, supported by the talk test", "D": "Use the heart rate reached at the end of her discharge walk test, increased each week"}, "cluster_id": "CHS28", "cluster_text": "Rekha Joshi, 63, a retired schoolteacher, lives in a regional town two hours from the hospital. She is six weeks after coronary artery bypass grafting (three grafts) and has type 2 diabetes managed with insulin and tablets. She joins phase two cardiac rehabilitation by videoconference with her husband present. Her sternal wound has healed and there is no sternal click. Resting HR 72, BP 124/76, SpO2 97 percent; she takes a beta blocker. Her discharge six minute walk distance was 330 metres. Her smartwatch records heart rate. She walks 20 minutes daily at a comfortable pace and wants to be able to lift her grandchildren. The service is reviewing its telehealth programme and asks every clinician to record the same outcomes at entry and exit. No on-site emergency support is available at her home.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use a rating of perceived exertion in the moderate range, supported by the talk test): Beta blockade blunts the heart rate response, so heart rate targets from formulas or from a test taken at a different time misrepresent effort. Perceived exertion with the talk test remains valid and is practical over video. Resting blood pressure does not indicate exercise intensity."}, {"id": 1070, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Twelve minutes into a brisk walk during a video session, Rekha reports new light-headedness, sweating and a clammy feeling, though she has no chest pain. Her husband is present and a glucose meter is nearby. What is the most appropriate immediate response?", "options": {"A": "Stop exercise, sit her down, and arrange a GP review within a day if she recovers", "B": "Stop exercise, sit her down, give fast-acting sugar immediately, and resume walking once she feels better", "C": "Have her sit and check pulse with the watch, then call for help if worse", "D": "Stop exercise, sit her down, check blood glucose, and use the emergency plan if symptoms persist"}, "cluster_id": "CHS28", "cluster_text": "Rekha Joshi, 63, a retired schoolteacher, lives in a regional town two hours from the hospital. She is six weeks after coronary artery bypass grafting (three grafts) and has type 2 diabetes managed with insulin and tablets. She joins phase two cardiac rehabilitation by videoconference with her husband present. Her sternal wound has healed and there is no sternal click. Resting HR 72, BP 124/76, SpO2 97 percent; she takes a beta blocker. Her discharge six minute walk distance was 330 metres. Her smartwatch records heart rate. She walks 20 minutes daily at a comfortable pace and wants to be able to lift her grandchildren. The service is reviewing its telehealth programme and asks every clinician to record the same outcomes at entry and exit. No on-site emergency support is available at her home.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stop exercise, sit her down, check blood glucose, and use the emergency plan if symptoms persist): Sweating and light-headedness in an insulin-treated cardiac patient could be hypoglycaemia or a cardiac event, so exercise stops, she sits, glucose is measured with the meter at hand, and the emergency plan is used if she does not recover. Giving sugar without measuring assumes the cause and resuming walking ignores a possible cardiac event. A pulse from a watch does not distinguish the two causes and waits for deterioration. Deferring review to the next day is unsafe with no on-site support."}, {"id": 1071, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Eight weeks after surgery Rekha reports her sternum feels solid and she wishes to start strengthening for lifting her grandchildren. The surgeon's guidance allows gradual upper limb loading. Which resistance training prescription is most appropriate to begin with in her home programme?", "options": {"A": "Light loads with higher repetitions and steady breathing, avoiding breath holding, progressed as technique allows", "B": "Heavy pushing and lifting exercises with maximal efforts, since the sternum is healed by eight weeks", "C": "Delay resistance work until three months after surgery, as early loading risks sternal separation", "D": "Test a one repetition maximum for each exercise and prescribe loads from that result"}, "cluster_id": "CHS28", "cluster_text": "Rekha Joshi, 63, a retired schoolteacher, lives in a regional town two hours from the hospital. She is six weeks after coronary artery bypass grafting (three grafts) and has type 2 diabetes managed with insulin and tablets. She joins phase two cardiac rehabilitation by videoconference with her husband present. Her sternal wound has healed and there is no sternal click. Resting HR 72, BP 124/76, SpO2 97 percent; she takes a beta blocker. Her discharge six minute walk distance was 330 metres. Her smartwatch records heart rate. She walks 20 minutes daily at a comfortable pace and wants to be able to lift her grandchildren. The service is reviewing its telehealth programme and asks every clinician to record the same outcomes at entry and exit. No on-site emergency support is available at her home.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Light loads with higher repetitions and steady breathing, avoiding breath holding, progressed as technique allows): Cardiac rehabilitation resistance work starts with low loads and higher repetitions, with breathing maintained to avoid pressure surges, and is progressed once technique and symptoms allow. Heavy maximal work is premature and maximal testing is not appropriate at this stage. Complete delay is outdated when the surgeon has already cleared graded loading."}, {"id": 1072, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "The service is auditing its telehealth cardiac rehabilitation programme. The physiotherapist is asked to contribute to quality improvement. Which approach to outcome measurement would best show whether the programme improves function and is safe for participants like Rekha over time?", "options": {"A": "Compare exit scores with published clinic averages, which saves the clinician time on repeated assessments", "B": "Collect satisfaction ratings at exit only, as these show how much each participant's capacity has changed", "C": "Count sessions attended and completed, since attendance is the best indicator that function has improved", "D": "Record the same validated function and patient-reported measures at entry and exit, with adverse events"}, "cluster_id": "CHS28", "cluster_text": "Rekha Joshi, 63, a retired schoolteacher, lives in a regional town two hours from the hospital. She is six weeks after coronary artery bypass grafting (three grafts) and has type 2 diabetes managed with insulin and tablets. She joins phase two cardiac rehabilitation by videoconference with her husband present. Her sternal wound has healed and there is no sternal click. Resting HR 72, BP 124/76, SpO2 97 percent; she takes a beta blocker. Her discharge six minute walk distance was 330 metres. Her smartwatch records heart rate. She walks 20 minutes daily at a comfortable pace and wants to be able to lift her grandchildren. The service is reviewing its telehealth programme and asks every clinician to record the same outcomes at entry and exit. No on-site emergency support is available at her home.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Record the same validated function and patient-reported measures at entry and exit, with adverse events): Evaluating a programme needs the same validated measures taken before and after, so change can be attributed to each participant, plus safety data on adverse events. Attendance and satisfaction describe engagement, not functional change. Comparing to external averages without baselines cannot show whether this programme made a difference."}, {"id": 1073, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "You are about to enter Mr Tesfaye's negative pressure room to teach sputum clearance, which will provoke coughing. He is on airborne precautions for confirmed pulmonary tuberculosis. Which personal protective equipment is most appropriate for the physiotherapist to put on before entering?", "options": {"A": "A fit-checked P2 respirator with gown and gloves, and eye protection when coughing is likely", "B": "A fit-checked P2 respirator alone, as gown and gloves are required only under contact precautions", "C": "A surgical mask, gown and gloves, as the patient wearing a mask provides adequate airborne protection", "D": "A P2 respirator and gloves, since fit checking is required only at annual fit testing"}, "cluster_id": "CHS29", "cluster_text": "Eyob Tesfaye, 22, a student from Eritrea, arrived in Australia eight months ago on a humanitarian visa. He has six weeks of cough, night sweats, 6 kg weight loss and recent haemoptysis. Sputum is smear positive for acid-fast bacilli and a chest x-ray shows right upper lobe cavitation. Pulmonary tuberculosis is confirmed, and anti-tuberculous therapy started two days ago. He is in a negative pressure single room under airborne precautions. Observations: HR 92, BP 112/68, RR 18, SpO2 96 percent on room air, afebrile. He has a productive cough with thick sputum and generalised weakness. He speaks Tigrinya and limited English. The team requests sputum clearance and a mobility assessment. A cousin who is visiting offers to interpret, and no professional interpreter has been booked for the ward.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A fit-checked P2 respirator with gown and gloves, and eye protection when coughing is likely): Airborne precautions require a fit-checked P2 respirator, and a cough-provoking intervention also calls for gown, gloves and eye protection because of droplet and surface contamination. A surgical mask does not filter airborne particles reliably. Skipping the seal check before each use removes the safeguard the respirator depends on."}, {"id": 1074, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Tesfaye has a weak cough, thick sputum and generalised weakness after weight loss. He remains infectious, and the room has a closed door with negative pressure. Which approach to sputum clearance and activity is most appropriate at this early stage?", "options": {"A": "Teach self-directed breathing and huffing techniques inside the room, with limited hands-on time and bedside exercises", "B": "Use hands-on assisted coughing and vibration inside the room, as he cannot clear secretions himself", "C": "Treat him inside the room with the door closed, using manual percussion and suctioning", "D": "Postpone all chest physiotherapy until he is non-infectious, offering reassurance and written advice only"}, "cluster_id": "CHS29", "cluster_text": "Eyob Tesfaye, 22, a student from Eritrea, arrived in Australia eight months ago on a humanitarian visa. He has six weeks of cough, night sweats, 6 kg weight loss and recent haemoptysis. Sputum is smear positive for acid-fast bacilli and a chest x-ray shows right upper lobe cavitation. Pulmonary tuberculosis is confirmed, and anti-tuberculous therapy started two days ago. He is in a negative pressure single room under airborne precautions. Observations: HR 92, BP 112/68, RR 18, SpO2 96 percent on room air, afebrile. He has a productive cough with thick sputum and generalised weakness. He speaks Tigrinya and limited English. The team requests sputum clearance and a mobility assessment. A cousin who is visiting offers to interpret, and no professional interpreter has been booked for the ward.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Teach self-directed breathing and huffing techniques inside the room, with limited hands-on time and bedside exercises): While he is infectious, treatment stays inside the negative pressure room, using techniques he can perform himself to limit staff exposure, plus bedside exercises for his weakness. Hands-on assisted coughing is effective for a weak cough but brings staff into close contact with aerosols and can provoke bleeding from a cavity. Manual percussion over a cavitating lesion carries the same risks. Postponing everything leaves his secretion clearance and weakness untreated when he can be helped safely."}, {"id": 1075, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "On day four Mr Tesfaye reports sudden sharp right chest pain and worsening breathlessness while sitting. RR is 28, SpO2 is 89 percent, air entry is reduced on the right and percussion is hyperresonant there. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Stop treatment, lie him flat, and ask the nurse to review him at routine observations", "B": "Reposition him with the right side up to drain secretions, and recheck in ten minutes", "C": "Stop treatment, keep him upright, and urgently alert the nurse and doctors to a possible pneumothorax", "D": "Increase his oxygen, coach slow diaphragmatic breathing, and reassess his saturation after the session ends"}, "cluster_id": "CHS29", "cluster_text": "Eyob Tesfaye, 22, a student from Eritrea, arrived in Australia eight months ago on a humanitarian visa. He has six weeks of cough, night sweats, 6 kg weight loss and recent haemoptysis. Sputum is smear positive for acid-fast bacilli and a chest x-ray shows right upper lobe cavitation. Pulmonary tuberculosis is confirmed, and anti-tuberculous therapy started two days ago. He is in a negative pressure single room under airborne precautions. Observations: HR 92, BP 112/68, RR 18, SpO2 96 percent on room air, afebrile. He has a productive cough with thick sputum and generalised weakness. He speaks Tigrinya and limited English. The team requests sputum clearance and a mobility assessment. A cousin who is visiting offers to interpret, and no professional interpreter has been booked for the ward.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop treatment, keep him upright, and urgently alert the nurse and doctors to a possible pneumothorax): Sudden pleuritic pain, falling saturation, reduced air entry and hyperresonance on one side suggest a pneumothorax, a recognised complication of cavitary tuberculosis. Treatment stops and the team is alerted at once while he stays upright and comfortable. Lying him flat and waiting for routine observations delays urgent care. Drainage positioning treats retained secretions, which do not explain hyperresonance. Oxygen and breathing exercises with later reassessment also delay medical review of a deteriorating patient."}, {"id": 1076, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Tesfaye's cousin offers to interpret during the assessment, and Mr Tesfaye nods. No professional interpreter has been booked. His tuberculosis diagnosis has not been shared with his cousin. The session will explain infection control and seek his consent for treatment. What is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Book a professional interpreter, by telephone if needed, and explain confidentiality and accuracy to the cousin", "B": "Use pictures and gestures with the cousin present to confirm understanding, since demonstration conveys instructions accurately", "C": "Ask the cousin to write key instructions in Tigrinya, then check he agrees with the plan", "D": "Accept the cousin's help for consent and instructions, using a professional interpreter if he looks confused"}, "cluster_id": "CHS29", "cluster_text": "Eyob Tesfaye, 22, a student from Eritrea, arrived in Australia eight months ago on a humanitarian visa. He has six weeks of cough, night sweats, 6 kg weight loss and recent haemoptysis. Sputum is smear positive for acid-fast bacilli and a chest x-ray shows right upper lobe cavitation. Pulmonary tuberculosis is confirmed, and anti-tuberculous therapy started two days ago. He is in a negative pressure single room under airborne precautions. Observations: HR 92, BP 112/68, RR 18, SpO2 96 percent on room air, afebrile. He has a productive cough with thick sputum and generalised weakness. He speaks Tigrinya and limited English. The team requests sputum clearance and a mobility assessment. A cousin who is visiting offers to interpret, and no professional interpreter has been booked for the ward.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Book a professional interpreter, by telephone if needed, and explain confidentiality and accuracy to the cousin): Consent and infection control teaching require accurate, impartial interpreting and protection of his health information, which a professional interpreter, including by telephone, can provide. A family member may omit or soften information and would be told his diagnosis without his agreement. Gestures and written notes from a relative do not confirm genuine understanding."}, {"id": 1077, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Ms Ioane has had 18 months of back and hip pain with no red flags. She has right hip abductor weakness, a positive Trendelenburg sign, and reduced activity because of fear of harassment. Which interpretation best summarises her priority problems for planning your treatment goals?", "options": {"A": "Persistent pain with hip abductor weakness and activity avoidance, needing graded loading and pain education", "B": "Lumbar facet joint pain from standing shifts, needing manual therapy and avoidance of aggravating work postures", "C": "Hormone therapy related musculoskeletal pain, needing referral back to her endocrinologist before any exercise begins", "D": "Hip joint pathology with irritable range, needing imaging and activity restriction until a diagnosis is confirmed"}, "cluster_id": "CHS30", "cluster_text": "Ms Kasey Ioane, 34, a trans woman and barista of Samoan background, attends with 18 months of right low back and hip pain. She uses she/her pronouns, which you have recorded with her permission. She takes oestrogen therapy prescribed by her GP and endocrinologist. Pain is 5/10, worse after standing shifts, with poor sleep. No fever, weight loss, night pain, bladder change or leg neurology. Examination: lumbar flexion limited by pain at 60 percent of range, right hip abductors 4 minus out of 5 with a positive right Trendelenburg sign, hip range full and non-irritable. She avoids gyms after harassment and has stopped walking for exercise. Patient-Specific Functional Scale average 4 out of 10. She feels her hormone dose may be worsening her pain and asks what dose she should be on.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Persistent pain with hip abductor weakness and activity avoidance, needing graded loading and pain education): With no red flags, a long duration, hip abductor weakness, a positive Trendelenburg sign and fear-driven avoidance, the priorities are persistent pain with a loading deficit and reduced activity, which graded loading and education address. Facet pain and passive treatment would not explain the abductor deficit. Her hormone dose is for her prescribers to manage, and exercise need not wait. Her hip range is full and non-irritable, so hip joint pathology needing imaging and restriction is not supported."}, {"id": 1078, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Ms Ioane avoids gyms because of past harassment and has stopped exercising. She says she feels safe in private spaces. Her hip abductor weakness needs progressive loading. Which approach to delivering her exercise programme is most appropriate to start this week?", "options": {"A": "A gym-based programme with group classes, to build confidence through exposure and improve long term adherence", "B": "A graded strengthening programme in clinic and at home, adding community options only if she chooses", "C": "Passive manual therapy and electrotherapy as the core treatment, until her pain eases enough for exercise", "D": "A printed handout of general hip exercises for self-directed use, with a review in three months"}, "cluster_id": "CHS30", "cluster_text": "Ms Kasey Ioane, 34, a trans woman and barista of Samoan background, attends with 18 months of right low back and hip pain. She uses she/her pronouns, which you have recorded with her permission. She takes oestrogen therapy prescribed by her GP and endocrinologist. Pain is 5/10, worse after standing shifts, with poor sleep. No fever, weight loss, night pain, bladder change or leg neurology. Examination: lumbar flexion limited by pain at 60 percent of range, right hip abductors 4 minus out of 5 with a positive right Trendelenburg sign, hip range full and non-irritable. She avoids gyms after harassment and has stopped walking for exercise. Patient-Specific Functional Scale average 4 out of 10. She feels her hormone dose may be worsening her pain and asks what dose she should be on.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A graded strengthening programme in clinic and at home, adding community options only if she chooses): Safety shapes whether she will exercise at all, so starting with a clinic and home programme and letting her decide on community options respects her experience and supports adherence. Group gym exposure ignores her stated barrier, and passive care or a generic handout fails to load the weak hip muscles progressively."}, {"id": 1079, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Ms Ioane asks why her pain has lasted so long when scans show no major damage. She sleeps poorly, is under stress, and worries that exercise will make it worse. Which explanation is most appropriate to give her at this stage?", "options": {"A": "Her pain will settle once the hip weakness is corrected, so pain science education is unnecessary", "B": "Her pain is mainly psychological, so mood treatment should replace physical exercise for the time being", "C": "Pain can persist after healing as the nervous system grows more sensitive, and gradual activity helps", "D": "Her pain reflects ongoing tissue damage from hormones, so activity should be limited until it settles"}, "cluster_id": "CHS30", "cluster_text": "Ms Kasey Ioane, 34, a trans woman and barista of Samoan background, attends with 18 months of right low back and hip pain. She uses she/her pronouns, which you have recorded with her permission. She takes oestrogen therapy prescribed by her GP and endocrinologist. Pain is 5/10, worse after standing shifts, with poor sleep. No fever, weight loss, night pain, bladder change or leg neurology. Examination: lumbar flexion limited by pain at 60 percent of range, right hip abductors 4 minus out of 5 with a positive right Trendelenburg sign, hip range full and non-irritable. She avoids gyms after harassment and has stopped walking for exercise. Patient-Specific Functional Scale average 4 out of 10. She feels her hormone dose may be worsening her pain and asks what dose she should be on.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Pain can persist after healing as the nervous system grows more sensitive, and gradual activity helps): Persistent pain commonly reflects a sensitised nervous system rather than ongoing damage, and explaining this reduces fear while supporting graded activity. Attributing pain to tissue damage or to psychology alone, or skipping education, either feeds fear avoidance or dismisses her experience."}, {"id": 1080, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Ms Ioane says her pain began after her hormone dose was increased and asks you what dose she should take instead. Her GP and endocrinologist manage her prescription, and she says she feels unheard by them. What is the most appropriate response from you?", "options": {"A": "Explain that dosing is outside your scope and help her raise the concern with her prescribers", "B": "Share doses other clients found helpful for pain so she can raise options with her doctor", "C": "Suggest a small dose reduction trial while tracking her pain, as other clients report improvement", "D": "Advise pausing hormone therapy briefly to see whether pain changes, as it can be restarted afterwards"}, "cluster_id": "CHS30", "cluster_text": "Ms Kasey Ioane, 34, a trans woman and barista of Samoan background, attends with 18 months of right low back and hip pain. She uses she/her pronouns, which you have recorded with her permission. She takes oestrogen therapy prescribed by her GP and endocrinologist. Pain is 5/10, worse after standing shifts, with poor sleep. No fever, weight loss, night pain, bladder change or leg neurology. Examination: lumbar flexion limited by pain at 60 percent of range, right hip abductors 4 minus out of 5 with a positive right Trendelenburg sign, hip range full and non-irritable. She avoids gyms after harassment and has stopped walking for exercise. Patient-Specific Functional Scale average 4 out of 10. She feels her hormone dose may be worsening her pain and asks what dose she should be on.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Explain that dosing is outside your scope and help her raise the concern with her prescribers): Medication dosing belongs to the prescribers, and advising changes is outside physiotherapy scope and could harm her. Supporting her to raise the pain concern, and offering to communicate with her doctors, respects her autonomy and addresses feeling unheard. Quoting other clients' doses or suggesting a pause is still giving prescribing advice."}, {"id": 1081, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Colleen's main goals are lifting and playing with her grandchildren and attending community gatherings. She completed 8 sit-to-stands in 30 seconds at baseline and rates her pain 5/10. Which measure would best track change in her function during the program that follows?", "options": {"A": "Single leg stance time on the right leg recorded at every second session", "B": "Goniometer measurement of passive hip flexion at each visit as the only main outcome", "C": "Repeat hip radiographs at the end of the program to show any change in joint space", "D": "The 30 second sit to stand count, recorded alongside her pain rating"}, "cluster_id": "CHS31", "cluster_text": "Aunty Colleen Dodds, 67, a Wiradjuri elder, attends an Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service in regional New South Wales, with the Aboriginal health worker present at her request. She has had right groin pain for six months, worse on walking and stairs, with morning stiffness that eases within half an hour. She cares for three grandchildren most days and wants to keep going to community yarning circles. Pain is 5/10, BMI 33, hip internal rotation is limited and painful, flexion 95 degrees, and she completed 8 sit-to-stands in 30 seconds. A recent X-ray shows moderate joint space narrowing. She does not want surgery. A colleague has also suggested a new manual traction technique, citing a small case series of ten people who reported marked improvement.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The 30 second sit to stand count, recorded alongside her pain rating): A performance measure that reflects daily tasks such as rising and lifting and is easy to repeat suits her goals and tracks real change. Joint space on imaging does not change with exercise and correlates poorly with symptoms. Range alone and single leg stance relate less closely to what she wants to do."}, {"id": 1082, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Colleen is ready to start exercise but worries that pain during exercise means she is damaging her hip. Her pain is 5/10, her BMI is 33 and she does not want surgery. Which program is the most appropriate way for her to begin treatment?", "options": {"A": "Strengthen hip and thigh muscles and add walking, accepting mild temporary pain that settles soon after", "B": "Avoid any exercise that provokes pain, and use pain-free swimming only until symptoms have fully settled", "C": "Advise rest and less walking to protect cartilage, with exercise only if she has surgery", "D": "Begin with passive manual therapy and hip stretching for four weeks, then add strengthening afterwards"}, "cluster_id": "CHS31", "cluster_text": "Aunty Colleen Dodds, 67, a Wiradjuri elder, attends an Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service in regional New South Wales, with the Aboriginal health worker present at her request. She has had right groin pain for six months, worse on walking and stairs, with morning stiffness that eases within half an hour. She cares for three grandchildren most days and wants to keep going to community yarning circles. Pain is 5/10, BMI 33, hip internal rotation is limited and painful, flexion 95 degrees, and she completed 8 sit-to-stands in 30 seconds. A recent X-ray shows moderate joint space narrowing. She does not want surgery. A colleague has also suggested a new manual traction technique, citing a small case series of ten people who reported marked improvement.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Strengthen hip and thigh muscles and add walking, accepting mild temporary pain that settles soon after): Land-based strengthening and aerobic activity are core management for hip osteoarthritis, and mild temporary pain increases during exercise are expected and not harmful. Complete pain avoidance, passive-only treatment and rest all reduce activity and do not address muscle weakness."}, {"id": 1083, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Colleen mentions that her X-ray worries her and that she has not felt heard in past appointments. She cares for her grandchildren daily and attends with the Aboriginal health worker. How should the physiotherapist best deliver education and plan her care in this setting?", "options": {"A": "Yarn with her and the health worker, link advice to her grandchildren, and offer group exercise", "B": "Give a written leaflet and a video link, with a short review so she progresses alone", "C": "Prescribe an individual clinic program, and avoid discussing her family circumstances to protect her privacy", "D": "Explain the X-ray findings in detail so she understands the joint damage and needs adherence"}, "cluster_id": "CHS31", "cluster_text": "Aunty Colleen Dodds, 67, a Wiradjuri elder, attends an Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service in regional New South Wales, with the Aboriginal health worker present at her request. She has had right groin pain for six months, worse on walking and stairs, with morning stiffness that eases within half an hour. She cares for three grandchildren most days and wants to keep going to community yarning circles. Pain is 5/10, BMI 33, hip internal rotation is limited and painful, flexion 95 degrees, and she completed 8 sit-to-stands in 30 seconds. A recent X-ray shows moderate joint space narrowing. She does not want surgery. A colleague has also suggested a new manual traction technique, citing a small case series of ten people who reported marked improvement.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Yarn with her and the health worker, link advice to her grandchildren, and offer group exercise): Culturally safe care involves listening, working with the Aboriginal health worker, and tying advice to her roles and community, with group options that suit her. Emphasising structural damage can heighten fear and avoidance. Leaflets alone and avoiding her family context miss what matters to her."}, {"id": 1084, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "The colleague wants to use the new manual traction technique on Colleen, citing a case series of ten people who improved after treatment. How should the physiotherapist appraise this evidence before deciding whether to change Colleen's current care plan at the clinic?", "options": {"A": "Reject any new technique until a systematic review of controlled trials has been published on it", "B": "Adopt the technique as an add-on only, because the case series reported large improvements in pain", "C": "Adopt the technique because it was published in a peer reviewed journal and seems low risk", "D": "Recognise a small case series as weak evidence, and check guidelines before changing care"}, "cluster_id": "CHS31", "cluster_text": "Aunty Colleen Dodds, 67, a Wiradjuri elder, attends an Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service in regional New South Wales, with the Aboriginal health worker present at her request. She has had right groin pain for six months, worse on walking and stairs, with morning stiffness that eases within half an hour. She cares for three grandchildren most days and wants to keep going to community yarning circles. Pain is 5/10, BMI 33, hip internal rotation is limited and painful, flexion 95 degrees, and she completed 8 sit-to-stands in 30 seconds. A recent X-ray shows moderate joint space narrowing. She does not want surgery. A colleague has also suggested a new manual traction technique, citing a small case series of ten people who reported marked improvement.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Recognise a small case series as weak evidence, and check guidelines before changing care): A small uncontrolled case series cannot show that the technique caused improvement, so it carries little weight compared with guideline-supported exercise and education. Peer review and low risk do not make it effective. Waiting for a systematic review before considering anything new is too rigid, because the question is whether the evidence supports changing her care now."}, {"id": 1085, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Jordan is a university student with limited income who has a GP Management Plan but few subsidised visits available, and is unsure how many sessions can be afforded. How should the physiotherapist best address funding and access for ongoing care?", "options": {"A": "Advise Jordan to use all remaining subsidised visits early, then pause care until next year's allocation", "B": "Refer Jordan to a public outpatient service and discharge from private care, to avoid out-of-pocket costs", "C": "Clarify the subsidised visits left, plan focused sessions with a home programme, and explore supports", "D": "Advise Jordan to apply for NDIS funded therapy, as a neurological diagnosis qualifies for individual support"}, "cluster_id": "CHS32", "cluster_text": "Jordan Whitehorse, 24, a Wiradjuri university student, is referred by a neurologist with a diagnosis of functional neurological disorder (functional left leg weakness and tremor) that began after a viral illness four months ago. The left leg gives way and drags, with an intermittent left arm tremor. The neurologist found a positive Hoover's sign and a tremor that entrains with a tapping task and settles with distraction. Reflexes are normal, there is no Babinski response, and MRI was normal. Symptoms worsen with fatigue and stress. Jordan walks with a left drag but can walk backwards and jog briefly when distracted. Jordan fears it is all in the head. Jordan has limited income, no private health cover and a GP Management Plan with a limited number of subsidised allied health visits per year.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Clarify the subsidised visits left, plan focused sessions with a home programme, and explore supports): Being open about the limited subsidised visits, planning fewer targeted sessions with a self-directed programme, and exploring other supports makes care achievable and lets Jordan choose. Using all visits early and then pausing risks a gap in care at the time input matters most. Moving to a public service and discharging decides for Jordan, may involve long waits and loses continuity. NDIS eligibility depends on permanent and significant disability, not on a diagnosis alone."}, {"id": 1086, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "While Jordan lies supine, the neurologist notes that the weak left hip extension becomes strong when the right hip is flexed against the examiner's resistance, in the absence of any structural lesion. Which clinical sign does this describe, and what does it indicate?", "options": {"A": "Babinski response, indicating an upper motor neuron lesion with brisk reflex changes in the affected leg", "B": "Hip abductor sign, a functional sign where abduction strength returns when the other leg abducts", "C": "Hoover's sign, a positive sign of functional weakness where strength returns during automatic movement", "D": "Give-way weakness, indicating pain inhibition, where strength breaks suddenly under resistance testing"}, "cluster_id": "CHS32", "cluster_text": "Jordan Whitehorse, 24, a Wiradjuri university student, is referred by a neurologist with a diagnosis of functional neurological disorder (functional left leg weakness and tremor) that began after a viral illness four months ago. The left leg gives way and drags, with an intermittent left arm tremor. The neurologist found a positive Hoover's sign and a tremor that entrains with a tapping task and settles with distraction. Reflexes are normal, there is no Babinski response, and MRI was normal. Symptoms worsen with fatigue and stress. Jordan walks with a left drag but can walk backwards and jog briefly when distracted. Jordan fears it is all in the head. Jordan has limited income, no private health cover and a GP Management Plan with a limited number of subsidised allied health visits per year.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hoover's sign, a positive sign of functional weakness where strength returns during automatic movement): Hoover's sign shows involuntary hip extension strength in the weak leg when the other hip flexes against resistance, a positive clinical sign of functional leg weakness that supports the diagnosis. The hip abductor sign is a related functional test but examines abduction, not extension. A Babinski response is a plantar reflex change tied to upper motor neurone lesions. Give-way weakness reflects pain inhibition under resistance and does not involve strength returning during automatic movement."}, {"id": 1087, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Jordan's left leg drags when attention is directed to walking but is smoother when distracted, and Jordan asks what physiotherapy can do for a condition without structural damage. The physiotherapist plans treatment based on current best practice for functional movement disorders. Which approach is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Apply graded motor imagery and mirror therapy, targeting altered cortical representation of the left leg", "B": "Provide a wheelchair for community mobility to conserve energy while symptoms settle on their own", "C": "Strengthen the left leg with progressive resistance exercise, focusing attention on each muscle's contraction", "D": "Explain the diagnosis, then retrain automatic movement using distraction and external focus of attention"}, "cluster_id": "CHS32", "cluster_text": "Jordan Whitehorse, 24, a Wiradjuri university student, is referred by a neurologist with a diagnosis of functional neurological disorder (functional left leg weakness and tremor) that began after a viral illness four months ago. The left leg gives way and drags, with an intermittent left arm tremor. The neurologist found a positive Hoover's sign and a tremor that entrains with a tapping task and settles with distraction. Reflexes are normal, there is no Babinski response, and MRI was normal. Symptoms worsen with fatigue and stress. Jordan walks with a left drag but can walk backwards and jog briefly when distracted. Jordan fears it is all in the head. Jordan has limited income, no private health cover and a GP Management Plan with a limited number of subsidised allied health visits per year.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain the diagnosis, then retrain automatic movement using distraction and external focus of attention): Physiotherapy for functional movement disorders begins with a clear explanation of the diagnosis and then practises normal, automatic movement while drawing attention away from the body. Focusing on the leg tends to worsen the dragging. Mobility aids can reinforce disability, and imagery based approaches are not first-line treatment here."}, {"id": 1088, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Ten minutes into walking practice at the second session, Jordan's leg becomes noticeably weaker, the arm tremor increases and Jordan looks visibly distressed, saying the symptoms prove something is structurally wrong. Vital signs are normal. What is the best immediate response?", "options": {"A": "End the session and arrange urgent neurological review because worsening symptoms may signal new structural pathology", "B": "Pause briefly, acknowledge the symptoms are real and involuntary, then redirect to a distracting task", "C": "End walking practice for today and teach relaxation breathing, resuming only once symptoms have settled", "D": "Persist with repeated attempts at the same task to show Jordan the leg can work"}, "cluster_id": "CHS32", "cluster_text": "Jordan Whitehorse, 24, a Wiradjuri university student, is referred by a neurologist with a diagnosis of functional neurological disorder (functional left leg weakness and tremor) that began after a viral illness four months ago. The left leg gives way and drags, with an intermittent left arm tremor. The neurologist found a positive Hoover's sign and a tremor that entrains with a tapping task and settles with distraction. Reflexes are normal, there is no Babinski response, and MRI was normal. Symptoms worsen with fatigue and stress. Jordan walks with a left drag but can walk backwards and jog briefly when distracted. Jordan fears it is all in the head. Jordan has limited income, no private health cover and a GP Management Plan with a limited number of subsidised allied health visits per year.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Pause briefly, acknowledge the symptoms are real and involuntary, then redirect to a distracting task): Symptoms of functional neurological disorder fluctuate with fatigue and anxiety, and validating them as genuine while redirecting attention keeps Jordan engaged and builds confidence. Urgent review is not needed when the picture is unchanged and observations are normal. Forcing repeated attempts increases distress, and stopping until symptoms vanish reinforces avoidance."}, {"id": 1089, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Nguyen's son offers to interpret for the entire consultation and says his mother would rather not have a stranger involved. The clinic can access a qualified Vietnamese interpreter by phone. What is the most appropriate way for the physiotherapist to proceed with this initial assessment and consent discussion?", "options": {"A": "Use simple English with gestures and demonstrations, as the assessment tests are mostly physical and visual", "B": "Let her son interpret today as she prefers, and book an interpreter if problems arise", "C": "Give a translated written summary prepared by online software, then confirm her understanding by phone later", "D": "Use a qualified interpreter, speak to Mrs Nguyen directly, and invite her son to stay"}, "cluster_id": "CHS33", "cluster_text": "Mrs Mai Truong Nguyen, 58, a retired seamstress, attends a private physiotherapy clinic with her adult son. She speaks Vietnamese and limited English. Six weeks ago she had a sudden severe spinning sensation with vomiting and was diagnosed with right vestibular neuritis in the emergency department. The vertigo settled after days, but she still feels unsteady and dizzy with quick head turns, and avoids walking outside at dusk. No hearing loss, headache or double vision. Medical history: hypertension, well controlled. Findings: no spontaneous nystagmus. Head impulse test: corrective saccade when the head is turned quickly to the right, none to the left. Dix Hallpike test negative on both sides. Smooth pursuit normal. Dynamic visual acuity reduced when the head is moving. Standing on foam with eyes closed she sways and needs a step to recover. Dizziness Handicap Inventory score in the moderate range. Her son has offered to interpret.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Use a qualified interpreter, speak to Mrs Nguyen directly, and invite her son to stay): Informed consent and accurate history need a qualified interpreter, who is bound by confidentiality and neutrality, while the son can still offer support if she agrees. Family interpreters may filter or alter information. Gestures and machine translation cannot reliably convey consent, symptoms or safety advice."}, {"id": 1090, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "During assessment, quickly turning Mrs Nguyen's head to the right produces a visible corrective saccade, while turning left does not. Her Dix Hallpike test is negative and her vision blurs with head movement. What does the head impulse finding most likely indicate?", "options": {"A": "Free otoconia in the right posterior semicircular canal causing brief positional vertigo on lying back", "B": "Reduced vestibulo-ocular reflex gain on the right, consistent with peripheral vestibular hypofunction", "C": "A central cerebellar lesion, which would typically impair smooth pursuit and produce gaze-evoked nystagmus", "D": "Cervical proprioceptive dysfunction from neck stiffness, which would be expected to change with trunk rotation"}, "cluster_id": "CHS33", "cluster_text": "Mrs Mai Truong Nguyen, 58, a retired seamstress, attends a private physiotherapy clinic with her adult son. She speaks Vietnamese and limited English. Six weeks ago she had a sudden severe spinning sensation with vomiting and was diagnosed with right vestibular neuritis in the emergency department. The vertigo settled after days, but she still feels unsteady and dizzy with quick head turns, and avoids walking outside at dusk. No hearing loss, headache or double vision. Medical history: hypertension, well controlled. Findings: no spontaneous nystagmus. Head impulse test: corrective saccade when the head is turned quickly to the right, none to the left. Dix Hallpike test negative on both sides. Smooth pursuit normal. Dynamic visual acuity reduced when the head is moving. Standing on foam with eyes closed she sways and needs a step to recover. Dizziness Handicap Inventory score in the moderate range. Her son has offered to interpret.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reduced vestibulo-ocular reflex gain on the right, consistent with peripheral vestibular hypofunction): A catch-up saccade after a rapid head turn shows the eyes failed to hold gaze, meaning the right vestibulo-ocular reflex is under-acting. Her negative Dix Hallpike test argues against canal debris, and normal pursuit with no spontaneous nystagmus makes a central lesion less likely."}, {"id": 1091, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Nguyen is ready to begin a home program for her unilateral vestibular hypofunction. She is medically stable and no longer taking vestibular suppressant medication. Her main complaints are blurred vision and dizziness with head turns. Which exercise component is most appropriate to prescribe first to promote vestibular compensation?", "options": {"A": "Canalith repositioning manoeuvres for the right posterior canal, repeated until positional vertigo no longer occurs", "B": "Avoiding provocative head movements and resting until the dizziness settles on its own before any exercise", "C": "Static balance practice on firm ground with eyes open, keeping the head still to avoid symptoms", "D": "Gaze stabilisation: head turns while fixating a target, progressed by speed and background complexity"}, "cluster_id": "CHS33", "cluster_text": "Mrs Mai Truong Nguyen, 58, a retired seamstress, attends a private physiotherapy clinic with her adult son. She speaks Vietnamese and limited English. Six weeks ago she had a sudden severe spinning sensation with vomiting and was diagnosed with right vestibular neuritis in the emergency department. The vertigo settled after days, but she still feels unsteady and dizzy with quick head turns, and avoids walking outside at dusk. No hearing loss, headache or double vision. Medical history: hypertension, well controlled. Findings: no spontaneous nystagmus. Head impulse test: corrective saccade when the head is turned quickly to the right, none to the left. Dix Hallpike test negative on both sides. Smooth pursuit normal. Dynamic visual acuity reduced when the head is moving. Standing on foam with eyes closed she sways and needs a step to recover. Dizziness Handicap Inventory score in the moderate range. Her son has offered to interpret.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Gaze stabilisation: head turns while fixating a target, progressed by speed and background complexity): Gaze stabilisation drives adaptation of the vestibulo-ocular reflex and targets her blurred vision with head movement. Repositioning treats canal debris, which her negative Dix Hallpike test does not support. Avoiding movement and keeping the head still slow compensation."}, {"id": 1092, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "After one week, Mrs Nguyen reports that her gaze exercises provoke moderate dizziness that settles within a few minutes after each set. She asks through the interpreter whether she should stop. What advice about this symptom response is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Stop each set at first dizziness, since provoked symptoms mean the exercise is causing harm", "B": "Pause exercises until she has several symptom free days, then restart gently at a lower level", "C": "Take an anti-dizziness tablet before each set so the exercises cause no symptoms, then continue", "D": "Mild to moderate dizziness settling within minutes is expected, so continue and progress as tolerated"}, "cluster_id": "CHS33", "cluster_text": "Mrs Mai Truong Nguyen, 58, a retired seamstress, attends a private physiotherapy clinic with her adult son. She speaks Vietnamese and limited English. Six weeks ago she had a sudden severe spinning sensation with vomiting and was diagnosed with right vestibular neuritis in the emergency department. The vertigo settled after days, but she still feels unsteady and dizzy with quick head turns, and avoids walking outside at dusk. No hearing loss, headache or double vision. Medical history: hypertension, well controlled. Findings: no spontaneous nystagmus. Head impulse test: corrective saccade when the head is turned quickly to the right, none to the left. Dix Hallpike test negative on both sides. Smooth pursuit normal. Dynamic visual acuity reduced when the head is moving. Standing on foam with eyes closed she sways and needs a step to recover. Dizziness Handicap Inventory score in the moderate range. Her son has offered to interpret.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Mild to moderate dizziness settling within minutes is expected, so continue and progress as tolerated): Controlled symptom provocation that settles quickly is the stimulus for central compensation, so the dose is appropriate and can be progressed. Stopping at first symptoms or waiting for symptom free days removes the stimulus. Suppressant medication is outside scope and would blunt compensation."}, {"id": 1093, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Harold takes metoprolol and his resting heart rate is 58 bpm. The physiotherapist is setting exercise intensity for his group sessions. He wants a simple target to use at home. Which method is the most reliable way to prescribe and monitor his exercise intensity?", "options": {"A": "Set a target heart rate zone from age-predicted maximum heart rate and keep him inside it", "B": "Use the talk test alone, since it replaces blood pressure and symptom checks in this group", "C": "Prescribe by a moderate perceived exertion rating, with symptom and blood pressure monitoring", "D": "Match his effort to the walking speed reached at the baseline six minute walk test"}, "cluster_id": "CHS34", "cluster_text": "Harold Dempsey, 74, retired bus driver, attends a community phase two cardiac rehabilitation group. He had coronary artery bypass grafting eight weeks ago; the sternotomy has healed with no clicking. Medications: metoprolol, aspirin, atorvastatin, ramipril. Resting HR 58 bpm, BP 128/76 mmHg, SpO2 97 percent. Baseline 6 minute walk test: 340 m, peak Borg RPE 14 (6 to 20 scale), no symptoms. Hospital discharge summary lists a left ventricular ejection fraction in the normal range. He walks 15 minutes daily at home. The program leader wants every participant's outcomes recorded in the same way at entry and exit so the service can review its results. He reports no chest pain and wants to return to lawn bowls.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Prescribe by a moderate perceived exertion rating, with symptom and blood pressure monitoring): Beta blockade lowers and blunts the heart rate response, so zones built from age-predicted maximum give a misleading target. A moderate perceived exertion rating, checked against symptoms and blood pressure, stays valid and can be taught for home use. The talk test is a useful adjunct but does not replace monitoring of symptoms and blood pressure."}, {"id": 1094, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "After six weeks Harold reports his usual session feels easy at an exertion rating of 11. He has had no chest pain, dizziness or abnormal blood pressure responses. His resting measures are stable. What is the most appropriate way to progress his aerobic training?", "options": {"A": "Increase duration or workload gradually, then reassess his perceived exertion and symptoms next session", "B": "Keep the current prescription until discharge, as phase two aims to confirm safety, not raise fitness", "C": "Increase duration, intensity and frequency together this week, then reassess his exertion rating and symptoms", "D": "Progress to interval training with high intensity bursts, since his ejection fraction is normal"}, "cluster_id": "CHS34", "cluster_text": "Harold Dempsey, 74, retired bus driver, attends a community phase two cardiac rehabilitation group. He had coronary artery bypass grafting eight weeks ago; the sternotomy has healed with no clicking. Medications: metoprolol, aspirin, atorvastatin, ramipril. Resting HR 58 bpm, BP 128/76 mmHg, SpO2 97 percent. Baseline 6 minute walk test: 340 m, peak Borg RPE 14 (6 to 20 scale), no symptoms. Hospital discharge summary lists a left ventricular ejection fraction in the normal range. He walks 15 minutes daily at home. The program leader wants every participant's outcomes recorded in the same way at entry and exit so the service can review its results. He reports no chest pain and wants to return to lawn bowls.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Increase duration or workload gradually, then reassess his perceived exertion and symptoms next session): When responses are normal and the session feels easy, progression is made by changing one training variable gradually and reassessing exertion rating and symptoms. Holding the same load wastes the adaptation still available and ignores that the program aims to build fitness. Changing duration, intensity and frequency together makes it hard to see which change caused any problem. Moving to high intensity intervals is a large step for eight weeks after surgery with an exertion rating of 11, and a normal ejection fraction does not remove that risk."}, {"id": 1095, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "During a session Harold stops cycling and says he feels light-headed and unusually tired. His rating is 12, but his systolic pressure has fallen 15 mmHg below his resting value while the workload stayed the same. Pulse is regular. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Send him to the emergency department by ambulance immediately, because any pressure fall signals graft failure", "B": "Stop exercise, seat him safely, and recheck symptoms and blood pressure until they settle", "C": "Complete the cool-down walk at a slow pace to prevent blood pooling, then take his pressure", "D": "Lower the workload to warm-up level, encourage fluids, and continue while rechecking pressure after five minutes"}, "cluster_id": "CHS34", "cluster_text": "Harold Dempsey, 74, retired bus driver, attends a community phase two cardiac rehabilitation group. He had coronary artery bypass grafting eight weeks ago; the sternotomy has healed with no clicking. Medications: metoprolol, aspirin, atorvastatin, ramipril. Resting HR 58 bpm, BP 128/76 mmHg, SpO2 97 percent. Baseline 6 minute walk test: 340 m, peak Borg RPE 14 (6 to 20 scale), no symptoms. Hospital discharge summary lists a left ventricular ejection fraction in the normal range. He walks 15 minutes daily at home. The program leader wants every participant's outcomes recorded in the same way at entry and exit so the service can review its results. He reports no chest pain and wants to return to lawn bowls.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stop exercise, seat him safely, and recheck symptoms and blood pressure until they settle): An exertional fall in systolic pressure with symptoms is an abnormal response and a standard reason to stop exercise. Seating him and monitoring until values settle is the safe first step, with escalation only if they do not. Continuing at a lower load or walking through a cool-down keeps him moving while the problem is unresolved."}, {"id": 1096, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "The service wants to audit whether its program improves function. Harold completes the six minute walk test at entry and exit. Participants vary in age and baseline fitness. Which approach to using this outcome measure best supports a valid service review across all participants?", "options": {"A": "Report only the group average exit distance, because individual baselines differ too much to be compared", "B": "Let each therapist choose a walking course and encouragement style, so tests suit each person's preferences", "C": "Collect satisfaction ratings at exit, since patient opinion reflects functional improvement more directly than walking tests", "D": "Standardise the protocol, then report each person's change against a recognised meaningful difference"}, "cluster_id": "CHS34", "cluster_text": "Harold Dempsey, 74, retired bus driver, attends a community phase two cardiac rehabilitation group. He had coronary artery bypass grafting eight weeks ago; the sternotomy has healed with no clicking. Medications: metoprolol, aspirin, atorvastatin, ramipril. Resting HR 58 bpm, BP 128/76 mmHg, SpO2 97 percent. Baseline 6 minute walk test: 340 m, peak Borg RPE 14 (6 to 20 scale), no symptoms. Hospital discharge summary lists a left ventricular ejection fraction in the normal range. He walks 15 minutes daily at home. The program leader wants every participant's outcomes recorded in the same way at entry and exit so the service can review its results. He reports no chest pain and wants to return to lawn bowls.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Standardise the protocol, then report each person's change against a recognised meaningful difference): A valid before and after comparison depends on the same protocol each time, including course, instructions and encouragement. Interpreting change against a recognised meaningful difference shows whether the gain matters to the individual, not only whether the number moved. Group averages alone hide people who did not improve."}, {"id": 1097, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Three weeks after right vestibular neuritis, Dennis has constant unsteadiness and blurred vision on quick head turns, with no spinning when rolling in bed. Hearing is unchanged. Which finding best supports ongoing peripheral vestibular hypofunction rather than another cause of his dizziness?", "options": {"A": "Brief spinning episodes lasting under a minute when he rolls in bed toward either side", "B": "Headaches with visual aura before dizziness that lasts for hours and sensitivity to bright light", "C": "Fluctuating hearing loss with ear fullness and tinnitus accompanying episodes of spinning that last for hours", "D": "Blurred vision on head turns with persisting imbalance, and no brief spinning when changing position"}, "cluster_id": "CHS35", "cluster_text": "Dennis Walker, 52, is a Yamatji man living on a remote station in Western Australia. He joins a telehealth session from home, with his daughter nearby and an Aboriginal health worker from the local clinic available by phone. Three weeks ago he had sudden vertigo with vomiting, diagnosed by the GP as right vestibular neuritis. The spinning has settled, but he now feels unsteady, especially in dim light and when turning his head, and his vision blurs on quick head turns. Rolling in bed does not provoke spinning. Hearing is unchanged and there is no headache or tinnitus. He is a stockman and wants to ride again. He is quiet at the start of the session and looks away from the camera.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Blurred vision on head turns with persisting imbalance, and no brief spinning when changing position): Blurred vision on head movement reflects reduced vestibulo ocular reflex gain, and constant imbalance without positional triggers fits unilateral hypofunction after neuritis. Brief positional spinning suggests benign paroxysmal positional vertigo, hearing symptoms suggest Meniere disease, and aura with headache suggests vestibular migraine."}, {"id": 1098, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Dennis will begin gaze stabilisation exercises at home to improve his blurred vision on head turns. The physiotherapist explains the technique over video, with his daughter watching and the health worker listening by phone. Which exercise is most appropriate to start?", "options": {"A": "Keep his eyes on a fixed target while turning his head side to side", "B": "Perform the Epley manoeuvre each day on the right side until the unsteadiness has resolved", "C": "Avoid head movement and rest until the dizziness settles, restarting activity once symptoms have fully gone", "D": "Keep his head still while following a moving target with his eyes, lengthening the time weekly"}, "cluster_id": "CHS35", "cluster_text": "Dennis Walker, 52, is a Yamatji man living on a remote station in Western Australia. He joins a telehealth session from home, with his daughter nearby and an Aboriginal health worker from the local clinic available by phone. Three weeks ago he had sudden vertigo with vomiting, diagnosed by the GP as right vestibular neuritis. The spinning has settled, but he now feels unsteady, especially in dim light and when turning his head, and his vision blurs on quick head turns. Rolling in bed does not provoke spinning. Hearing is unchanged and there is no headache or tinnitus. He is a stockman and wants to ride again. He is quiet at the start of the session and looks away from the camera.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Keep his eyes on a fixed target while turning his head side to side): Holding gaze on a fixed target during head movement trains the vestibulo ocular reflex and promotes compensation, with mild symptoms accepted during practice. Following a moving target with a still head trains smooth pursuit instead. Rest delays recovery, and the Epley manoeuvre treats a different condition."}, {"id": 1099, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Dennis will practise standing balance tasks at home between sessions, with mild dizziness expected during practice. He lives on a station with a large yard, and his daughter works nearby most days. Which set up is safest for these tasks?", "options": {"A": "Practise alone so he can concentrate, then phone the clinic afterwards if he falls", "B": "Start with his eyes closed on a foam cushion to challenge him, since symptoms are mild", "C": "Practise in the open yard on soft grass to cushion any fall, camera angled toward him", "D": "Practise beside a firm bench with his daughter close by, stopping if dizziness becomes severe"}, "cluster_id": "CHS35", "cluster_text": "Dennis Walker, 52, is a Yamatji man living on a remote station in Western Australia. He joins a telehealth session from home, with his daughter nearby and an Aboriginal health worker from the local clinic available by phone. Three weeks ago he had sudden vertigo with vomiting, diagnosed by the GP as right vestibular neuritis. The spinning has settled, but he now feels unsteady, especially in dim light and when turning his head, and his vision blurs on quick head turns. Rolling in bed does not provoke spinning. Hearing is unchanged and there is no headache or tinnitus. He is a stockman and wants to ride again. He is quiet at the start of the session and looks away from the camera.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Practise beside a firm bench with his daughter close by, stopping if dizziness becomes severe): A firm support to grab and a person nearby allow safe progression when mild dizziness is expected. Soft or uneven surfaces and removing vision add challenge too early and raise fall risk. Practising alone leaves no help available if he falls, which matters on a remote property."}, {"id": 1100, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "At the start of the telehealth session Dennis says little and looks away from the camera, and the physiotherapist feels pressed for time because another client is booked soon. How should the physiotherapist best provide culturally safe care at this point?", "options": {"A": "Slow down, ask how he wants family or the health worker involved, and follow his lead", "B": "Ask whether he is comfortable on camera, then invite his daughter to answer on his behalf", "C": "Keep to a brief structured assessment to save time, and offer a longer review next week", "D": "Hand the assessment to the Aboriginal health worker and limit your role to advice afterwards"}, "cluster_id": "CHS35", "cluster_text": "Dennis Walker, 52, is a Yamatji man living on a remote station in Western Australia. He joins a telehealth session from home, with his daughter nearby and an Aboriginal health worker from the local clinic available by phone. Three weeks ago he had sudden vertigo with vomiting, diagnosed by the GP as right vestibular neuritis. The spinning has settled, but he now feels unsteady, especially in dim light and when turning his head, and his vision blurs on quick head turns. Rolling in bed does not provoke spinning. Hearing is unchanged and there is no headache or tinnitus. He is a stockman and wants to ride again. He is quiet at the start of the session and looks away from the camera.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Slow down, ask how he wants family or the health worker involved, and follow his lead): Avoiding direct eye contact can be a sign of respect and should not be read as disengagement. Culturally safe practice takes time to build trust, asks him how he wants family and the health worker involved, and shares control of the pace. Inviting his daughter to answer for him removes his voice and assumes his preference. A rushed structured assessment prioritises the clinician's schedule over trust. Handing the assessment to the health worker shifts the physiotherapist's responsibility rather than working alongside that support."}, {"id": 1101, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "The telehealth consult is the first physiotherapy session, and Mr Pillai asks how his back pain differs from the mechanical pain his colleagues describe. Based on his history, which feature most strongly points to an inflammatory rather than mechanical cause of back pain?", "options": {"A": "Pain aggravated by sustained sitting and relieved by lying on his back with the knees bent", "B": "Pain that builds through the working day and eases with rest after repeated lifting at work", "C": "Morning stiffness that eases with movement, and pain that wakes him in the early hours", "D": "Pain that reproduces on repeated lumbar extension and refers into the back of the thigh"}, "cluster_id": "CHS36", "cluster_text": "Mr Arjun Pillai, 29, is a software developer who lives in a regional town and joins a telehealth video appointment from home. He has had 18 months of gradual low back and buttock pain, with morning stiffness lasting over an hour that eases with walking. Pain wakes him in the early hours, and sitting at his desk is not a trigger. A rheumatologist diagnosed ankylosing spondylitis last month and he takes an anti-inflammatory as directed. He smokes about ten cigarettes a day and rarely exercises. His BASDAI score is 6 out of 10. On video, he stands with a flattened lumbar lordosis, forward flexion reaches mid-thigh, and neck rotation looks mildly limited. He has a yoga mat and a chair at home. He asks what he can do himself between appointments. No neurological symptoms, eye symptoms or bowel changes are reported today.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Morning stiffness that eases with movement, and pain that wakes him in the early hours): Inflammatory back pain typically starts gradually in a younger person, causes prolonged morning stiffness, improves with activity and wakes the person in the second half of the night. The other patterns describe pain driven by posture, load or movement direction, which is how mechanical problems behave."}, {"id": 1102, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Pillai agrees to start exercise. Video observation shows reduced spinal extension and a flattened lumbar lordosis, and he is not currently in a flare. He has a yoga mat and a chair at home. Which exercise programme is most appropriate to prescribe through telehealth?", "options": {"A": "Daily spinal mobility and extension exercises, postural stretches and aerobic exercise, adjusted to symptoms", "B": "Rest during any increase in pain, with a lumbar brace worn until inflammatory markers normalise", "C": "Isolated deep abdominal core exercises as the main programme, progressed weekly for lumbar stability", "D": "Heavy resisted spinal flexion strengthening, done first thing in the morning when he is stiffest"}, "cluster_id": "CHS36", "cluster_text": "Mr Arjun Pillai, 29, is a software developer who lives in a regional town and joins a telehealth video appointment from home. He has had 18 months of gradual low back and buttock pain, with morning stiffness lasting over an hour that eases with walking. Pain wakes him in the early hours, and sitting at his desk is not a trigger. A rheumatologist diagnosed ankylosing spondylitis last month and he takes an anti-inflammatory as directed. He smokes about ten cigarettes a day and rarely exercises. His BASDAI score is 6 out of 10. On video, he stands with a flattened lumbar lordosis, forward flexion reaches mid-thigh, and neck rotation looks mildly limited. He has a yoga mat and a chair at home. He asks what he can do himself between appointments. No neurological symptoms, eye symptoms or bowel changes are reported today.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Daily spinal mobility and extension exercises, postural stretches and aerobic exercise, adjusted to symptoms): Regular mobility, extension, postural and aerobic exercise helps maintain spinal movement and function in axial spondyloarthritis and can be done safely at home with remote guidance. Prolonged rest or bracing encourages stiffness, and loaded flexion or isolated core training does not target the loss of extension."}, {"id": 1103, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Two weeks later, Mr Pillai reports a painful red left eye with light sensitivity that began two days ago and is worsening. His back is stable and he asks to carry on with the session as planned. What should the physiotherapist advise?", "options": {"A": "Advise review by his GP within the next few days, and inform his rheumatologist", "B": "Stop the session, advise rest, and arrange a telehealth review of the eye in two days", "C": "Same-day review by a GP, eye specialist or emergency department, and inform his rheumatologist", "D": "Continue the session in dim light, and ask his rheumatologist to review the eye this week"}, "cluster_id": "CHS36", "cluster_text": "Mr Arjun Pillai, 29, is a software developer who lives in a regional town and joins a telehealth video appointment from home. He has had 18 months of gradual low back and buttock pain, with morning stiffness lasting over an hour that eases with walking. Pain wakes him in the early hours, and sitting at his desk is not a trigger. A rheumatologist diagnosed ankylosing spondylitis last month and he takes an anti-inflammatory as directed. He smokes about ten cigarettes a day and rarely exercises. His BASDAI score is 6 out of 10. On video, he stands with a flattened lumbar lordosis, forward flexion reaches mid-thigh, and neck rotation looks mildly limited. He has a yoga mat and a chair at home. He asks what he can do himself between appointments. No neurological symptoms, eye symptoms or bowel changes are reported today.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Same-day review by a GP, eye specialist or emergency department, and inform his rheumatologist): A painful red eye with photophobia in a person with ankylosing spondylitis suggests anterior uveitis, which needs same-day specialist assessment to protect vision, with his rheumatologist informed. Review within days is too slow for a sight-threatening condition. Stopping and rechecking by video in two days relies on symptoms improving by themselves. Continuing exercise in dim light and leaving the eye to a rheumatologist visit later in the week does not provide the urgent eye examination he needs."}, {"id": 1104, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "At the end of the session, Mr Pillai says he feels overwhelmed by information and wants to take charge of his condition. He smokes and sits long hours at work. He is willing to try changes in small steps. Which approach best supports his health promotion and self-management?", "options": {"A": "Set a fixed weekly exercise timetable and discourage changes, so his adherence stays consistent between sessions", "B": "Focus on pacing and desk ergonomics, and leave lifestyle matters such as smoking to his GP", "C": "Agree goals, a flare action plan and smoking cessation support, reviewed at each session", "D": "Provide printed exercise and fact sheets, then refer him back to his rheumatologist for education"}, "cluster_id": "CHS36", "cluster_text": "Mr Arjun Pillai, 29, is a software developer who lives in a regional town and joins a telehealth video appointment from home. He has had 18 months of gradual low back and buttock pain, with morning stiffness lasting over an hour that eases with walking. Pain wakes him in the early hours, and sitting at his desk is not a trigger. A rheumatologist diagnosed ankylosing spondylitis last month and he takes an anti-inflammatory as directed. He smokes about ten cigarettes a day and rarely exercises. His BASDAI score is 6 out of 10. On video, he stands with a flattened lumbar lordosis, forward flexion reaches mid-thigh, and neck rotation looks mildly limited. He has a yoga mat and a chair at home. He asks what he can do himself between appointments. No neurological symptoms, eye symptoms or bowel changes are reported today.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Agree goals, a flare action plan and smoking cessation support, reviewed at each session): Self-management works best when the person sets collaborative goals, knows what to do in a flare and receives support for modifiable risks such as smoking, which affects disease progression. Handing over sheets, imposing rigid timetables or ignoring smoking overlooks his stated readiness for small, self-directed changes."}, {"id": 1105, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Before the session, the bedside nurse reports Dorothy's noradrenaline dose has doubled in the last hour and her MAP is now 62. She remains alert on the same ventilator settings. Which finding most strongly indicates that mobilisation should be deferred today?", "options": {"A": "Arterial and central lines in situ, which restrict the range of positions available during mobilisation", "B": "Lower limb strength of about grade 3, which is too weak to attempt weight bearing activity", "C": "Presence of an endotracheal tube with ongoing pressure support ventilation at a PEEP of 8 cmH2O", "D": "A rising vasopressor requirement with a falling MAP, which suggests worsening cardiovascular stability"}, "cluster_id": "CHS37", "cluster_text": "Dorothy Kowalski, 74, is on day 5 in ICU after septic shock from pneumonia. History: type 2 diabetes and hypertension, previously independent and walking without an aid. She is intubated via an endotracheal tube on pressure support ventilation, PEEP 8 cmH2O, FiO2 0.40. RASS 0, follows commands, mild weakness (about grade 4 in the arms and 3 in the legs). HR 92, MAP 72 on a low dose noradrenaline infusion that has been stable for 12 hours. SpO2 96%, RR 22. Arterial line, central line and urinary catheter in place. Sedation was ceased 2 days ago. The ICU consultant has asked for early mobilisation. You also have a post-operative patient in bay 3 with a rising oxygen requirement and sputum retention, a routine ward review request, and 90 minutes before your next meeting.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A rising vasopressor requirement with a falling MAP, which suggests worsening cardiovascular stability): A doubling vasopressor requirement with a falling MAP signals haemodynamic instability, a recognised reason to hold mobilisation until the team has reviewed her. Ventilation with a modest PEEP, lines in situ and moderate limb weakness are not contraindications and are managed routinely with teamwork and planning. Mobilising an unstable patient risks further hypotension."}, {"id": 1106, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Two hours later Dorothy's noradrenaline is stable again and the consultant approves mobilisation. She is awake, on the same ventilator settings. The intensive care nurse and an allied health assistant are available to help. Which is the best approach to the first session?", "options": {"A": "Stepwise progression from bed exercises to edge sitting, ventilator connected, with team roles assigned", "B": "Passive movements in bed only until she is extubated, because active exercise is unsafe while intubated", "C": "Stand her at the bedside with a frame straight away, using a manual bag for ventilation", "D": "Hoist her straight to a chair for a prolonged sit, omitting bed exercises as low value"}, "cluster_id": "CHS37", "cluster_text": "Dorothy Kowalski, 74, is on day 5 in ICU after septic shock from pneumonia. History: type 2 diabetes and hypertension, previously independent and walking without an aid. She is intubated via an endotracheal tube on pressure support ventilation, PEEP 8 cmH2O, FiO2 0.40. RASS 0, follows commands, mild weakness (about grade 4 in the arms and 3 in the legs). HR 92, MAP 72 on a low dose noradrenaline infusion that has been stable for 12 hours. SpO2 96%, RR 22. Arterial line, central line and urinary catheter in place. Sedation was ceased 2 days ago. The ICU consultant has asked for early mobilisation. You also have a post-operative patient in bay 3 with a rising oxygen requirement and sputum retention, a routine ward review request, and 90 minutes before your next meeting.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stepwise progression from bed exercises to edge sitting, ventilator connected, with team roles assigned): Early mobilisation in ventilated patients is progressive: active bed exercises, edge sitting, then standing or transferring as she tolerates, with the ventilator and lines managed by assigned team members. Passive work alone wastes the window of being alert and cooperative. Skipping steps or disconnecting her from the ventilator increases risk without benefit."}, {"id": 1107, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "While sitting on the edge of the bed, Dorothy's HR rises from 92 to 128, RR from 22 to 34, and SpO2 falls from 96% to 90%. She is anxious and using accessory muscles. Her BP is unchanged. What should the physiotherapist do next?", "options": {"A": "Return her to a supported resting position, reassure her, and reassess observations until they recover", "B": "Lie her flat in bed for a few minutes, then try standing again once she settles", "C": "Keep her seated, ask the nurse to increase the ventilator pressure support, and continue the session", "D": "Keep her seated for two more minutes while coaching slow breathing, then stop if unchanged"}, "cluster_id": "CHS37", "cluster_text": "Dorothy Kowalski, 74, is on day 5 in ICU after septic shock from pneumonia. History: type 2 diabetes and hypertension, previously independent and walking without an aid. She is intubated via an endotracheal tube on pressure support ventilation, PEEP 8 cmH2O, FiO2 0.40. RASS 0, follows commands, mild weakness (about grade 4 in the arms and 3 in the legs). HR 92, MAP 72 on a low dose noradrenaline infusion that has been stable for 12 hours. SpO2 96%, RR 22. Arterial line, central line and urinary catheter in place. Sedation was ceased 2 days ago. The ICU consultant has asked for early mobilisation. You also have a post-operative patient in bay 3 with a rising oxygen requirement and sputum retention, a routine ward review request, and 90 minutes before your next meeting.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Return her to a supported resting position, reassure her, and reassess observations until they recover): A marked rise in heart rate and respiratory rate with falling saturation and accessory muscle use shows she has exceeded her physiological reserve, so activity stops, she is supported at rest and observations are monitored until they recover. Lying flat can worsen breathing effort and trying to stand soon after adds load again. Asking for higher ventilator support and continuing treats the numbers while the demand stays the same. Waiting two more minutes under the same load delays stopping a clearly poor response."}, {"id": 1108, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "You have 40 minutes before a meeting. The post-operative patient in bay 3 now has SpO2 falling to 89%, audible secretions and a weak cough. Dorothy is stable at rest, and the ward review is routine. Which is the most appropriate prioritisation?", "options": {"A": "Split the time evenly, treating each ICU patient for 20 minutes to share physiotherapy input fairly", "B": "Complete Dorothy's mobilisation as planned because the consultant requested it, then assess the bay 3 patient", "C": "See the post-operative patient first, then Dorothy, and inform her nurse of the changed plan", "D": "Do the routine ward review first as it was booked earlier, then see both ICU patients"}, "cluster_id": "CHS37", "cluster_text": "Dorothy Kowalski, 74, is on day 5 in ICU after septic shock from pneumonia. History: type 2 diabetes and hypertension, previously independent and walking without an aid. She is intubated via an endotracheal tube on pressure support ventilation, PEEP 8 cmH2O, FiO2 0.40. RASS 0, follows commands, mild weakness (about grade 4 in the arms and 3 in the legs). HR 92, MAP 72 on a low dose noradrenaline infusion that has been stable for 12 hours. SpO2 96%, RR 22. Arterial line, central line and urinary catheter in place. Sedation was ceased 2 days ago. The ICU consultant has asked for early mobilisation. You also have a post-operative patient in bay 3 with a rising oxygen requirement and sputum retention, a routine ward review request, and 90 minutes before your next meeting.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (See the post-operative patient first, then Dorothy, and inform her nurse of the changed plan): Urgent clinical need guides priority: a patient with falling saturation and secretion retention can deteriorate quickly and is likely to benefit from timely treatment, while Dorothy is stable and her session can be rescheduled. Communicating the change keeps the team informed and avoids unmet expectations. Booking order and a consultant request do not outweigh acute need."}, {"id": 1109, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Dabrowski can reach towards a cup on the table with his left arm but cannot extend his fingers to release it, and he currently uses his right hand for all daily tasks. Which approach is most appropriate to improve his left upper limb function?", "options": {"A": "Constraint induced movement therapy with the right hand restrained for most waking hours", "B": "Passive stretching and positioning of the left hand until active finger extension has clearly returned", "C": "Repetitive, graded task specific practice of reaching and releasing real functional objects daily", "D": "Neuromuscular electrical stimulation to wrist extensors as the main treatment, with the arm supported at rest"}, "cluster_id": "CHS38", "cluster_text": "Mr Jakub Dabrowski, 58, electrician, right middle cerebral artery ischaemic stroke 5 weeks ago, now in subacute rehabilitation. History of hypertension and type 2 diabetes. Left upper limb: shoulder flexion 3/5, elbow extension 3/5, wrist extension 2/5, finger extension 1/5, grip 2/5. Modified Ashworth Scale 1+ at the elbow flexors. He can reach a cup on the table but cannot open his hand to release it. Shoulder is pain free with no palpable subluxation. Cognition is intact and he is highly motivated to return to work. Walks independently with a stick. Nursing staff assist him with dressing and showering, and the occupational therapist runs two hand sessions a week. On the ward he uses his right hand for everything and keeps his left in his lap.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Repetitive, graded task specific practice of reaching and releasing real functional objects daily): Intensive, repetitive, graded practice of meaningful tasks is the best supported approach for arm recovery after stroke. Constraint induced therapy needs a minimum of active wrist and finger extension, which he does not yet have, so it would be frustrating and unsuitable. Passive work or stimulation alone does not train the reach and release skill he needs."}, {"id": 1110, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "During reaching practice Mr Dabrowski hikes his left shoulder and leans his trunk forward to bring the hand to the cup, and the wrist drops into flexion. He is frustrated. What is the best way to modify the task so that practice stays productive?", "options": {"A": "Constrain his right hand so he must use the left, keeping the same distance and speed", "B": "Move the cup closer and support the forearm to reduce trunk and shoulder compensation", "C": "Cue him to keep his shoulder down and trunk upright while reaching the same distance", "D": "Switch to isolated shoulder flexion strengthening with a light weight, then return to reaching when stronger"}, "cluster_id": "CHS38", "cluster_text": "Mr Jakub Dabrowski, 58, electrician, right middle cerebral artery ischaemic stroke 5 weeks ago, now in subacute rehabilitation. History of hypertension and type 2 diabetes. Left upper limb: shoulder flexion 3/5, elbow extension 3/5, wrist extension 2/5, finger extension 1/5, grip 2/5. Modified Ashworth Scale 1+ at the elbow flexors. He can reach a cup on the table but cannot open his hand to release it. Shoulder is pain free with no palpable subluxation. Cognition is intact and he is highly motivated to return to work. Walks independently with a stick. Nursing staff assist him with dressing and showering, and the occupational therapist runs two hand sessions a week. On the ward he uses his right hand for everything and keeps his left in his lap.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Move the cup closer and support the forearm to reduce trunk and shoulder compensation): Grading the task down by moving the cup closer and supporting the forearm lets him succeed with better movement quality, which builds useful motor patterns and keeps him motivated. Restricting the right hand without changing the task difficulty forces more compensation, and he lacks the finger extension usually needed for that approach. Verbal cues alone cannot overcome the weakness that is driving the compensation at this distance. Isolated strengthening removes the task practice he needs."}, {"id": 1111, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Dabrowski's elbow flexors score 1+ on the Modified Ashworth Scale during passive elbow extension, and the arm moves freely otherwise. A student asks what this grade means in terms of resistance felt by the examiner. Which description of the finding is the most accurate?", "options": {"A": "A considerable increase in tone, with passive movement difficult through the whole range", "B": "A catch and release, or minimal resistance, only at the very end of the range", "C": "A catch followed by minimal resistance for less than half of the range", "D": "A more marked increase in tone through most of the range, with the part moved"}, "cluster_id": "CHS38", "cluster_text": "Mr Jakub Dabrowski, 58, electrician, right middle cerebral artery ischaemic stroke 5 weeks ago, now in subacute rehabilitation. History of hypertension and type 2 diabetes. Left upper limb: shoulder flexion 3/5, elbow extension 3/5, wrist extension 2/5, finger extension 1/5, grip 2/5. Modified Ashworth Scale 1+ at the elbow flexors. He can reach a cup on the table but cannot open his hand to release it. Shoulder is pain free with no palpable subluxation. Cognition is intact and he is highly motivated to return to work. Walks independently with a stick. Nursing staff assist him with dressing and showering, and the occupational therapist runs two hand sessions a week. On the ward he uses his right hand for everything and keeps his left in his lap.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A catch followed by minimal resistance for less than half of the range): On the Modified Ashworth Scale, 1+ is a catch followed by minimal resistance through less than half of the range. Grade 1 is limited to the end of range, grade 2 is a more marked increase through most of the range, and grade 3 makes passive movement difficult. His tone is mild and should not stop active task practice."}, {"id": 1112, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Nursing staff dress Mr Dabrowski and he currently keeps his left hand in his lap on the ward. At the team meeting the physiotherapist wants him to use the arm more in daily activities. What is the most appropriate and practical handover action?", "options": {"A": "Ask nursing staff to withdraw dressing help until the left arm shows clear functional recovery", "B": "Leave daily arm use to the occupational therapist and review progress at the fortnightly team meeting", "C": "Give nursing staff a written sheet of left arm exercises to complete with him every morning", "D": "Agree with nursing and the occupational therapist on specific daily tasks for his left hand"}, "cluster_id": "CHS38", "cluster_text": "Mr Jakub Dabrowski, 58, electrician, right middle cerebral artery ischaemic stroke 5 weeks ago, now in subacute rehabilitation. History of hypertension and type 2 diabetes. Left upper limb: shoulder flexion 3/5, elbow extension 3/5, wrist extension 2/5, finger extension 1/5, grip 2/5. Modified Ashworth Scale 1+ at the elbow flexors. He can reach a cup on the table but cannot open his hand to release it. Shoulder is pain free with no palpable subluxation. Cognition is intact and he is highly motivated to return to work. Walks independently with a stick. Nursing staff assist him with dressing and showering, and the occupational therapist runs two hand sessions a week. On the ward he uses his right hand for everything and keeps his left in his lap.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Agree with nursing and the occupational therapist on specific daily tasks for his left hand): Practice outside therapy sessions is more likely to carry over when the team agrees on named tasks and everyone prompts the same way. Withdrawing help risks unsafe or demoralising attempts without a plan. Delegating the whole issue to one profession or handing over an exercise sheet leaves nursing without clear roles in his everyday routines."}, {"id": 1113, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Paramedics started oxygen before any blood gas was available, and Walter's saturation is now 94 percent on a Hudson mask at 6 litres per minute, with PaCO2 62 mmHg and drowsiness. What oxygen recommendation to the treating team is now most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Titrate controlled oxygen by Venturi mask to 88 to 92 percent, then repeat the blood gas", "B": "Change to high flow nasal oxygen at maximum flow to wash out carbon dioxide", "C": "Switch to a non rebreather mask to maximise oxygen delivery until the blood gas shows improvement", "D": "Continue the Hudson mask at slightly reduced flow, keeping saturation above 94 percent for brain protection"}, "cluster_id": "CHS39", "cluster_text": "Walter Nguyen, 71, retired bus driver, arrives by ambulance with a three day history of worsening breathlessness and thick sputum. He has known severe COPD with previous episodes of hypercapnic respiratory failure. Paramedics started oxygen at 6 L/min by Hudson mask. On arrival: RR 28, HR 112, BP 138/80, temperature 37.6, SpO2 94 percent on the mask, drowsy but rousable to voice. Auscultation: widespread wheeze with reduced air entry at both bases. Initial arterial blood gas on oxygen: pH 7.31, PaCO2 62 mmHg, PaO2 78 mmHg. Sputum is thick and he cannot clear it. Chest X-ray shows hyperinflation without consolidation. The emergency department physiotherapy team is taking part in a departmental audit of respiratory call outs, and the physiotherapist has been asked to help record outcomes.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Titrate controlled oxygen by Venturi mask to 88 to 92 percent, then repeat the blood gas): In a patient at risk of hypercapnia, oxygen is titrated with a controlled device to an 88 to 92 percent target and the blood gas is repeated to check the response. Aiming above 94 percent is too high and can worsen carbon dioxide retention, as his gas already suggests. A non rebreather mask delivers uncontrolled high concentrations, and maximal high flow oxygen is not a way to correct retention."}, {"id": 1114, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Walter's sputum is thick and he struggles to clear it. He is likely to need oxygen therapy for several days, and the team asks at the bedside whether humidification should be added to his circuit. Which statement about humidifying oxygen therapy is accurate?", "options": {"A": "Dry gas thickens secretions, so heated humidification helps with high flows or prolonged oxygen use", "B": "Bubble humidifiers add useful moisture at low flows, so they suit most Venturi mask settings", "C": "Warmed humidified gas alone clears retained secretions from smaller airways, so nebulised saline is unnecessary", "D": "Heated humidification is needed only for intubated patients, because the upper airway conditions inspired oxygen sufficiently"}, "cluster_id": "CHS39", "cluster_text": "Walter Nguyen, 71, retired bus driver, arrives by ambulance with a three day history of worsening breathlessness and thick sputum. He has known severe COPD with previous episodes of hypercapnic respiratory failure. Paramedics started oxygen at 6 L/min by Hudson mask. On arrival: RR 28, HR 112, BP 138/80, temperature 37.6, SpO2 94 percent on the mask, drowsy but rousable to voice. Auscultation: widespread wheeze with reduced air entry at both bases. Initial arterial blood gas on oxygen: pH 7.31, PaCO2 62 mmHg, PaO2 78 mmHg. Sputum is thick and he cannot clear it. Chest X-ray shows hyperinflation without consolidation. The emergency department physiotherapy team is taking part in a departmental audit of respiratory call outs, and the physiotherapist has been asked to help record outcomes.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Dry gas thickens secretions, so heated humidification helps with high flows or prolonged oxygen use): Medical gases are dry, and prolonged or high flow delivery dries the airway and thickens secretions, so heated humidification is valuable in that situation. Bubble humidifiers add little moisture at low flows and can interfere with entrainment on a Venturi device. Humidified gas supports mucociliary clearance but does not replace other treatment, and it is not limited to intubated patients."}, {"id": 1115, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Forty minutes later Walter is more drowsy. A repeat blood gas on controlled oxygen with SpO2 90 percent shows pH 7.27 and PaCO2 72 mmHg despite nebulised bronchodilators. RR is 30 with accessory muscle use. Medical staff are busy with another resuscitation. What should the physiotherapist do next?", "options": {"A": "Position him upright in supported forward lean and observe for twenty minutes before escalating", "B": "Escalate to the medical team now so non-invasive ventilation can be considered for hypercapnic acidosis", "C": "Increase the Venturi oxygen concentration to lift saturation, then reassess drowsiness in thirty minutes", "D": "Start the active cycle of breathing technique with huffing, then repeat the blood gas after treatment"}, "cluster_id": "CHS39", "cluster_text": "Walter Nguyen, 71, retired bus driver, arrives by ambulance with a three day history of worsening breathlessness and thick sputum. He has known severe COPD with previous episodes of hypercapnic respiratory failure. Paramedics started oxygen at 6 L/min by Hudson mask. On arrival: RR 28, HR 112, BP 138/80, temperature 37.6, SpO2 94 percent on the mask, drowsy but rousable to voice. Auscultation: widespread wheeze with reduced air entry at both bases. Initial arterial blood gas on oxygen: pH 7.31, PaCO2 62 mmHg, PaO2 78 mmHg. Sputum is thick and he cannot clear it. Chest X-ray shows hyperinflation without consolidation. The emergency department physiotherapy team is taking part in a departmental audit of respiratory call outs, and the physiotherapist has been asked to help record outcomes.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Escalate to the medical team now so non-invasive ventilation can be considered for hypercapnic acidosis): Worsening acidosis and carbon dioxide retention despite controlled oxygen and bronchodilators is the situation in which non-invasive ventilation is considered, so prompt escalation is the priority even when staff are busy. Airway clearance in a drowsy, tiring patient adds work of breathing and delays the definitive step. Raising the oxygen concentration risks further retention, and positioning alone does not justify waiting."}, {"id": 1116, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "The audit lead wants evidence of whether emergency department physiotherapy reviews improve patient outcomes across the service. Walter's respiratory rate and SpO2 were recorded before and after the review. Which approach to data collection will give the most meaningful quality improvement information?", "options": {"A": "Collect patient satisfaction ratings after each review, because they are easier to gather than physiological values", "B": "Record the same measures for every patient, but only at discharge from the department", "C": "Record the same measures at consistent times for all patients and compare them with agreed targets", "D": "Let each physiotherapist choose which measures to record, so data reflect their own clinical reasoning"}, "cluster_id": "CHS39", "cluster_text": "Walter Nguyen, 71, retired bus driver, arrives by ambulance with a three day history of worsening breathlessness and thick sputum. He has known severe COPD with previous episodes of hypercapnic respiratory failure. Paramedics started oxygen at 6 L/min by Hudson mask. On arrival: RR 28, HR 112, BP 138/80, temperature 37.6, SpO2 94 percent on the mask, drowsy but rousable to voice. Auscultation: widespread wheeze with reduced air entry at both bases. Initial arterial blood gas on oxygen: pH 7.31, PaCO2 62 mmHg, PaO2 78 mmHg. Sputum is thick and he cannot clear it. Chest X-ray shows hyperinflation without consolidation. The emergency department physiotherapy team is taking part in a departmental audit of respiratory call outs, and the physiotherapist has been asked to help record outcomes.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Record the same measures at consistent times for all patients and compare them with agreed targets): Meaningful audit data come from standardised measures collected the same way at the same points for every patient, judged against agreed targets so the service can see where to improve. Satisfaction ratings add useful information but cannot show whether physiological status changed. Measuring only at discharge removes the before and after comparison. Letting each clinician choose measures makes the results impossible to compare across the service."}, {"id": 1117, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Khoa develops a pounding headache with flushing and sweating above the injury level, and his blood pressure has risen well above his usual resting value. He is sitting in his wheelchair. What should the physiotherapist do immediately in this situation?", "options": {"A": "Transfer him back to bed promptly and recheck his blood pressure at the next session", "B": "Lay him flat with legs raised to restore circulation, then recheck his blood pressure afterwards", "C": "Keep him upright, loosen tight clothing, and urgently alert nursing staff about his bladder", "D": "Continue the session at lower intensity and monitor his blood pressure every few minutes"}, "cluster_id": "CHS40", "cluster_text": "Khoa Huynh, 34, an electrician, is five weeks after a fall from a ladder that caused a T6 spinal cord injury (ASIA impairment scale A). He is in a spinal rehabilitation unit, uses intermittent catheterisation every four to six hours and a daily bowel program, and transfers with a sliding board and one assistant. Resting supine BP is 100/60 mmHg and HR 62 bpm. A nurse noted a non-blanching red area over his sacrum yesterday. During a morning session in his wheelchair he suddenly reports a pounding headache. He is flushed and sweating above the nipple line, with pale cool legs. BP is now 168/104 mmHg and HR 54 bpm. His catheter is due and the drainage bag is empty. Afterwards Khoa says he is tired and does not want the skin check or a pressure relief session today. He is alert, oriented and asks sensible questions about the risks.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Keep him upright, loosen tight clothing, and urgently alert nursing staff about his bladder): Autonomic dysreflexia is a medical emergency in injuries at T6 or above. The first steps are to sit the person upright so blood pressure falls, remove tight clothing and look for the trigger, most often a full bladder. Lying flat would push pressure higher, and nursing staff must be alerted urgently because catheter drainage or a bowel check is usually needed."}, {"id": 1118, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Khoa's symptoms settled after his bladder was drained and his blood pressure returned to baseline. The team explains the episode to him. Which statement best describes why a noxious stimulus below the injury, such as a full bladder, causes this response at the T6 level?", "options": {"A": "Reflex sympathetic vasoconstriction below the lesion raises blood pressure because descending inhibition is blocked", "B": "Leg spasticity compresses veins, increasing venous return and driving blood pressure up above the lesion", "C": "Pain signals reaching the cortex trigger a stress response with adrenaline release that raises blood pressure", "D": "Vagal overactivity dilates the leg vessels, pooling blood and triggering a compensatory rise in blood pressure"}, "cluster_id": "CHS40", "cluster_text": "Khoa Huynh, 34, an electrician, is five weeks after a fall from a ladder that caused a T6 spinal cord injury (ASIA impairment scale A). He is in a spinal rehabilitation unit, uses intermittent catheterisation every four to six hours and a daily bowel program, and transfers with a sliding board and one assistant. Resting supine BP is 100/60 mmHg and HR 62 bpm. A nurse noted a non-blanching red area over his sacrum yesterday. During a morning session in his wheelchair he suddenly reports a pounding headache. He is flushed and sweating above the nipple line, with pale cool legs. BP is now 168/104 mmHg and HR 54 bpm. His catheter is due and the drainage bag is empty. Afterwards Khoa says he is tired and does not want the skin check or a pressure relief session today. He is alert, oriented and asks sensible questions about the risks.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reflex sympathetic vasoconstriction below the lesion raises blood pressure because descending inhibition is blocked): Below the lesion, a noxious stimulus sets off a reflex sympathetic surge and vasoconstriction, and the brain's descending inhibition cannot pass the injury, so pressure stays high. The slow heart rate comes from baroreceptor driven vagal activity above the injury. In a complete injury pain signals do not reach the cortex, so a cortical stress response does not explain the episode."}, {"id": 1119, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Khoa's sacral area is red and non-blanching. He is keen to resume much longer wheelchair sitting for a planned family event this weekend. Which advice about sitting and pressure care is most appropriate for the physiotherapist to give at this stage?", "options": {"A": "Massage the red area to improve circulation, then allow normal sitting once the redness fades", "B": "Keep the current sitting schedule, add a cushion, and teach pressure relief lifts every thirty minutes", "C": "Offload the area, reduce sitting time, and ask the team to review it before progressing", "D": "Apply barrier cream to the area and allow sitting as tolerated, as he cannot feel discomfort"}, "cluster_id": "CHS40", "cluster_text": "Khoa Huynh, 34, an electrician, is five weeks after a fall from a ladder that caused a T6 spinal cord injury (ASIA impairment scale A). He is in a spinal rehabilitation unit, uses intermittent catheterisation every four to six hours and a daily bowel program, and transfers with a sliding board and one assistant. Resting supine BP is 100/60 mmHg and HR 62 bpm. A nurse noted a non-blanching red area over his sacrum yesterday. During a morning session in his wheelchair he suddenly reports a pounding headache. He is flushed and sweating above the nipple line, with pale cool legs. BP is now 168/104 mmHg and HR 54 bpm. His catheter is due and the drainage bag is empty. Afterwards Khoa says he is tired and does not want the skin check or a pressure relief session today. He is alert, oriented and asks sensible questions about the risks.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Offload the area, reduce sitting time, and ask the team to review it before progressing): A non-blanching red area signals early pressure injury, so loading must be removed and sitting time cut back until the team has reviewed the skin. A cushion and relief lifts alone keep loading damaged tissue. Massage harms already compromised tissue and is outdated advice, and loss of sensation makes pain an unreliable guide."}, {"id": 1120, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Khoa is alert and oriented, understands that a skin check helps detect pressure injury, and can explain the risks back accurately. He says he is tired and declines the check today. How should the physiotherapist best respond to his decision today?", "options": {"A": "Accept the refusal and take no further action, because adult patients decide about their own care", "B": "Respect his refusal, document it with the advice given, and offer another time to check", "C": "Proceed with a brief check anyway, since early detection of skin damage outweighs his tiredness today", "D": "Request a formal capacity assessment before accepting the refusal, then record the result in his notes"}, "cluster_id": "CHS40", "cluster_text": "Khoa Huynh, 34, an electrician, is five weeks after a fall from a ladder that caused a T6 spinal cord injury (ASIA impairment scale A). He is in a spinal rehabilitation unit, uses intermittent catheterisation every four to six hours and a daily bowel program, and transfers with a sliding board and one assistant. Resting supine BP is 100/60 mmHg and HR 62 bpm. A nurse noted a non-blanching red area over his sacrum yesterday. During a morning session in his wheelchair he suddenly reports a pounding headache. He is flushed and sweating above the nipple line, with pale cool legs. BP is now 168/104 mmHg and HR 54 bpm. His catheter is due and the drainage bag is empty. Afterwards Khoa says he is tired and does not want the skin check or a pressure relief session today. He is alert, oriented and asks sensible questions about the risks.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Respect his refusal, document it with the advice given, and offer another time to check): Khoa can understand, retain and weigh the information, so he has capacity and may refuse. The physiotherapist respects the decision, documents the information given and his refusal, and offers another time. A capacity assessment is not needed without a reason to doubt capacity, while doing nothing further would leave the risk unaddressed and proceeding would breach consent."}, {"id": 1121, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Before Hieu leaves, you must check for nerve injury, a recognised complication after anterior dislocation. Sensation over the lateral shoulder region has not yet been recorded by the emergency team. Which combination of findings best tests the axillary nerve at this stage?", "options": {"A": "Radial pulse and capillary refill at the fingertips, which confirm that the axillary artery is intact", "B": "Light touch over the lateral upper arm and a palpable isometric deltoid contraction", "C": "Resisted elbow flexion and light touch over the lateral forearm, testing the musculocutaneous nerve", "D": "Resisted thumb opposition and light touch over the index fingertip, testing the median nerve"}, "cluster_id": "CHS41", "cluster_text": "Hieu Ly, 24, a Vietnamese-speaking international student with limited English, is seen by the emergency department physiotherapy service after an anterior dislocation of the right shoulder playing futsal. It is his first dislocation. The emergency doctor reduced it under sedation 3 hours ago, and the post-reduction X-ray shows a congruent joint with no fracture. He is in a sling and reports pain of 4/10. Radial pulse is present and the hand is warm. Elbow, wrist and finger movement is normal. Sensation over the lateral upper arm has not yet been recorded. He is right-hand dominant, studies engineering and wants to return to futsal. His 16-year-old sister, who came with him, has offered to interpret. No staff member in the department speaks Vietnamese. You are asked to assess and give advice before discharge.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Light touch over the lateral upper arm and a palpable isometric deltoid contraction): The axillary nerve supplies the deltoid and the skin over the lateral upper arm, so sensation there and a palpable isometric deltoid contraction, with the arm supported, are the practical tests after a dislocation. A pulse and capillary refill check the circulation, which matters but does not exclude nerve injury. The musculocutaneous and median nerves can also be injured in this setting, yet testing them does not examine the axillary nerve."}, {"id": 1122, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Hieu's neurovascular status is normal and he asks what to do over the next two weeks. He is worried about stiffness. He is right-hand dominant and, as a student, writes and types a great deal. What early advice is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Continuous sling use in internal rotation for six weeks, removing it only for washing and dressing", "B": "Passive external rotation stretches early, because stiffness in external rotation is the main risk", "C": "Active overhead reaching and light resistance work from day one, to prevent stiffness and keep strength", "D": "Comfort-based sling use, gentle elbow, wrist and hand movement, and avoiding abduction with external rotation"}, "cluster_id": "CHS41", "cluster_text": "Hieu Ly, 24, a Vietnamese-speaking international student with limited English, is seen by the emergency department physiotherapy service after an anterior dislocation of the right shoulder playing futsal. It is his first dislocation. The emergency doctor reduced it under sedation 3 hours ago, and the post-reduction X-ray shows a congruent joint with no fracture. He is in a sling and reports pain of 4/10. Radial pulse is present and the hand is warm. Elbow, wrist and finger movement is normal. Sensation over the lateral upper arm has not yet been recorded. He is right-hand dominant, studies engineering and wants to return to futsal. His 16-year-old sister, who came with him, has offered to interpret. No staff member in the department speaks Vietnamese. You are asked to assess and give advice before discharge.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Comfort-based sling use, gentle elbow, wrist and hand movement, and avoiding abduction with external rotation): Early care aims to settle pain, keep the distal joints moving and protect the anterior capsule by avoiding the combined abduction and external rotation position that provokes redislocation. Prolonged strict immobilisation encourages stiffness and weakness. Early overhead loading or passive external rotation stretching risks stressing the injured anterior structures."}, {"id": 1123, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Four weeks later Hieu has near-full range and rotator cuff strength of 4+/5, but a positive apprehension test at 90 degrees of abduction and external rotation. He asks to play in a futsal match in two weeks. What is the most appropriate plan?", "options": {"A": "Refer straight for surgical stabilisation without further rehabilitation, because recurrence is highly likely at his age", "B": "Clear him for the match with taping, as range and strength are near normal", "C": "Progress cuff and scapular strengthening, delay contact play until criteria are met, and discuss orthopaedic review", "D": "Rest the shoulder and stop strengthening until apprehension resolves, then restart gradual rehabilitation"}, "cluster_id": "CHS41", "cluster_text": "Hieu Ly, 24, a Vietnamese-speaking international student with limited English, is seen by the emergency department physiotherapy service after an anterior dislocation of the right shoulder playing futsal. It is his first dislocation. The emergency doctor reduced it under sedation 3 hours ago, and the post-reduction X-ray shows a congruent joint with no fracture. He is in a sling and reports pain of 4/10. Radial pulse is present and the hand is warm. Elbow, wrist and finger movement is normal. Sensation over the lateral upper arm has not yet been recorded. He is right-hand dominant, studies engineering and wants to return to futsal. His 16-year-old sister, who came with him, has offered to interpret. No staff member in the department speaks Vietnamese. You are asked to assess and give advice before discharge.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Progress cuff and scapular strengthening, delay contact play until criteria are met, and discuss orthopaedic review): Persisting apprehension indicates ongoing anterior instability, so contact sport should wait until strength, control and apprehension criteria are met. Continued dynamic stabiliser rehabilitation is appropriate, and an orthopaedic opinion is reasonable given his age and sport. Taping does not make up for the deficit, and stopping rehabilitation or skipping it altogether is not supported."}, {"id": 1124, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Hieu's sister offers to interpret the discharge advice and says she speaks English well. Hieu nods but seems unsure. You also need his informed consent to begin treatment, and no Vietnamese-speaking physiotherapist is available today. How should the physiotherapist proceed?", "options": {"A": "Give translated exercise sheets and a demonstration, which standardise information and avoid relying on family", "B": "Use his sister and check understanding by asking Hieu to repeat the advice back to you", "C": "Book an accredited interpreter through the hospital, and use them for consent and teaching", "D": "Ask a Vietnamese-speaking colleague from another department to interpret, noting the arrangement in his record"}, "cluster_id": "CHS41", "cluster_text": "Hieu Ly, 24, a Vietnamese-speaking international student with limited English, is seen by the emergency department physiotherapy service after an anterior dislocation of the right shoulder playing futsal. It is his first dislocation. The emergency doctor reduced it under sedation 3 hours ago, and the post-reduction X-ray shows a congruent joint with no fracture. He is in a sling and reports pain of 4/10. Radial pulse is present and the hand is warm. Elbow, wrist and finger movement is normal. Sensation over the lateral upper arm has not yet been recorded. He is right-hand dominant, studies engineering and wants to return to futsal. His 16-year-old sister, who came with him, has offered to interpret. No staff member in the department speaks Vietnamese. You are asked to assess and give advice before discharge.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Book an accredited interpreter through the hospital, and use them for consent and teaching): Accurate communication and valid consent require a qualified interpreter, who is trained in confidentiality and accurate rendering of clinical information. A young family member may omit or alter content and should not carry the responsibility. Written sheets and an untrained colleague do not allow checking of his understanding or questions."}, {"id": 1125, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Sione fractured his femur in a minor fall and has had three months of night pain in the same thigh. What does this history most strongly suggest about the cause of the fracture, and how should the limb be managed until surgery?", "options": {"A": "A stress fracture from rugby training, so relative rest and graded loading suit the limb", "B": "A pathological fracture that heals normally once stabilised, so standard early mobilisation applies after surgery", "C": "A growth plate injury typical of adolescence, so early gentle weight bearing promotes healing", "D": "Bone weakened by tumour, so protect the limb and avoid stress through the fracture site"}, "cluster_id": "CHS42", "cluster_text": "Sione Matagi, 16, a school rugby player, is on the orthopaedic ward after a minor fall at training caused a left thigh fracture. He reports three months of left knee and thigh pain that wakes him at night. Imaging shows a destructive lesion in the left distal femur with a fracture through it; biopsy results are pending and a bone tumour is suspected. Orthopaedic oncology will review him in two days and has ordered no weight bearing on the left leg. Pain is 6/10 at rest, the knee is swollen, and foot pulses, sensation and warmth are normal. Platelets are low and bleeding precautions are in place. Your morning list also includes Mr Alan Price, day 1 after laparotomy, coughing weakly with falling SpO2 and audible secretions, and Mrs Pham, awaiting a routine discharge equipment trial.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Bone weakened by tumour, so protect the limb and avoid stress through the fracture site): Night pain for three months and a fracture after minimal force point to bone weakened by a lesion, which fits the destructive lesion seen on imaging. A fracture through a tumour will not unite or tolerate load normally, so the limb is protected and stress is avoided, in line with the no weight bearing order. A stress fracture does not cause a destructive lesion. A pathological fracture does not heal normally while the tumour is present. Loading a growth plate injury early would not suit this lesion and risks displacement."}, {"id": 1126, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Orthopaedic oncology has ordered no weight bearing on the left leg until review. Platelets are low with bleeding precautions in place. The surgeon asks for a first physiotherapy session today. Which plan is most appropriate and safe for this first session?", "options": {"A": "Teach non-weight-bearing transfers and frame use, with active exercise of the other limbs only", "B": "Bed rest without mobilisation or exercise of any limb until the oncology review is completed", "C": "Partial weight bearing with crutches to keep the bone loaded, plus gentle thigh stretching", "D": "Gentle passive knee bending to prevent stiffness, and light manual resisted quadriceps work in bed"}, "cluster_id": "CHS42", "cluster_text": "Sione Matagi, 16, a school rugby player, is on the orthopaedic ward after a minor fall at training caused a left thigh fracture. He reports three months of left knee and thigh pain that wakes him at night. Imaging shows a destructive lesion in the left distal femur with a fracture through it; biopsy results are pending and a bone tumour is suspected. Orthopaedic oncology will review him in two days and has ordered no weight bearing on the left leg. Pain is 6/10 at rest, the knee is swollen, and foot pulses, sensation and warmth are normal. Platelets are low and bleeding precautions are in place. Your morning list also includes Mr Alan Price, day 1 after laparotomy, coughing weakly with falling SpO2 and audible secretions, and Mrs Pham, awaiting a routine discharge equipment trial.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Teach non-weight-bearing transfers and frame use, with active exercise of the other limbs only): A fracture through a lesion calls for protected mobility within the non-weight-bearing order, with active work for the other limbs. With low platelets and a weakened femur, passive stretch, manual resistance or loading near the thigh risk bleeding and displacement. Partial weight bearing contradicts the order, and complete bed rest causes avoidable deconditioning."}, {"id": 1127, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "During a bed exercise session Sione cries out with sudden severe thigh pain. The thigh looks newly deformed and more swollen, and the foot feels cooler and slower to refill than the other. He looks pale. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Apply ice to the thigh, elevate the leg and reassess his pain and foot colour soon", "B": "Gently realign the limb with light traction to restore circulation, then notify the nurse in charge", "C": "Stop, support the limb where it lies, and urgently call for medical review", "D": "Continue the session at lower intensity, since pain with exercise is expected in this condition"}, "cluster_id": "CHS42", "cluster_text": "Sione Matagi, 16, a school rugby player, is on the orthopaedic ward after a minor fall at training caused a left thigh fracture. He reports three months of left knee and thigh pain that wakes him at night. Imaging shows a destructive lesion in the left distal femur with a fracture through it; biopsy results are pending and a bone tumour is suspected. Orthopaedic oncology will review him in two days and has ordered no weight bearing on the left leg. Pain is 6/10 at rest, the knee is swollen, and foot pulses, sensation and warmth are normal. Platelets are low and bleeding precautions are in place. Your morning list also includes Mr Alan Price, day 1 after laparotomy, coughing weakly with falling SpO2 and audible secretions, and Mrs Pham, awaiting a routine discharge equipment trial.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop, support the limb where it lies, and urgently call for medical review): Sudden pain, new deformity and a cooler foot with slow refill suggest fracture displacement with possible vascular compromise, which is time critical. Keeping the limb supported and calling for urgent medical help is right. Realigning with traction is outside safe practice here, and ice or continued exercise delays care."}, {"id": 1128, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "It is 0900 and you have time for two sessions before ward round. Mr Price is coughing weakly with falling SpO2 and audible secretions, Sione needs his first mobility session, and Mrs Pham awaits a routine equipment trial. Which prioritisation is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "See Mr Price first for airway clearance, then Sione, and defer the equipment trial", "B": "See Mrs Pham first to free her bed, then Mr Price, and leave Sione until review", "C": "Ask the ward nurse to do Mr Price's chest care, and see Sione and Mrs Pham", "D": "See Sione first because his fracture carries the greatest risk, then Mr Price, then Mrs Pham"}, "cluster_id": "CHS42", "cluster_text": "Sione Matagi, 16, a school rugby player, is on the orthopaedic ward after a minor fall at training caused a left thigh fracture. He reports three months of left knee and thigh pain that wakes him at night. Imaging shows a destructive lesion in the left distal femur with a fracture through it; biopsy results are pending and a bone tumour is suspected. Orthopaedic oncology will review him in two days and has ordered no weight bearing on the left leg. Pain is 6/10 at rest, the knee is swollen, and foot pulses, sensation and warmth are normal. Platelets are low and bleeding precautions are in place. Your morning list also includes Mr Alan Price, day 1 after laparotomy, coughing weakly with falling SpO2 and audible secretions, and Mrs Pham, awaiting a routine discharge equipment trial.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (See Mr Price first for airway clearance, then Sione, and defer the equipment trial): Mr Price has a deteriorating respiratory problem that is time critical, so he is seen first. Sione is stable and non-weight-bearing, so his session comes second, which still meets the surgeon's request. The routine equipment trial can wait. Seeing Sione first, though his condition is serious, would delay care for a deteriorating patient, and chest physiotherapy is not interchangeable with nursing care."}, {"id": 1129, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Nguyen asks why her scans are normal when the pain is so severe, and whether her muscles are being damaged. She is worried that activity will make her worse. Which explanation of her pain is most accurate and helpful to give her now?", "options": {"A": "Eighteen months of inactivity weakened her muscles, so pain will ease once strength is rebuilt", "B": "The nervous system amplifies pain signals without tissue damage, so gradual activity is safe", "C": "Low grade joint inflammation too small for scans to show is causing the pain and stiffness", "D": "Small tears in her muscle fibres cause the pain, so rest allows healing before activity restarts"}, "cluster_id": "CHS43", "cluster_text": "Mai Huynh Nguyen, 47, is a night-shift cleaner who attends the outpatient pain clinic with fibromyalgia diagnosed by her GP three years ago. She speaks Vietnamese and limited English. Her husband interpreted at earlier visits. She reports widespread aching pain (7/10), fatigue, unrefreshing sleep and morning stiffness. Blood tests and imaging were normal. She stopped her daily walks eighteen months ago and now manages about five minutes before pain builds, then rests for the afternoon. She fears that exercise will damage her muscles. Examination shows widespread tenderness to light pressure, full joint range, normal neurological findings and a Borg rating of 13 after one minute of brisk marching on the spot. A telephone interpreter service is available through the clinic.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The nervous system amplifies pain signals without tissue damage, so gradual activity is safe): Fibromyalgia is understood as altered pain processing, so normal scans fit the picture and ongoing tissue damage is not occurring. This explanation reduces fear and supports graded activity. Describing muscle tears, hidden inflammation or weakness as the cause would reinforce her belief that movement harms her and prompt more rest."}, {"id": 1130, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Nguyen agrees to try walking again. She can walk about five minutes before pain increases and then needs the afternoon to recover. With an interpreter present, how should the physiotherapist set the starting dose of her daily walking programme safely?", "options": {"A": "Set a target of thirty minutes daily and push through flares to retrain her pain response", "B": "Start below her flare threshold, such as three minutes daily, and increase gradually each week", "C": "Start at ten minutes of brisk walking so her body adapts, accepting some pain during sessions", "D": "Wait until pain falls below three out of ten, then begin fifteen minutes on alternate days"}, "cluster_id": "CHS43", "cluster_text": "Mai Huynh Nguyen, 47, is a night-shift cleaner who attends the outpatient pain clinic with fibromyalgia diagnosed by her GP three years ago. She speaks Vietnamese and limited English. Her husband interpreted at earlier visits. She reports widespread aching pain (7/10), fatigue, unrefreshing sleep and morning stiffness. Blood tests and imaging were normal. She stopped her daily walks eighteen months ago and now manages about five minutes before pain builds, then rests for the afternoon. She fears that exercise will damage her muscles. Examination shows widespread tenderness to light pressure, full joint range, normal neurological findings and a Borg rating of 13 after one minute of brisk marching on the spot. A telephone interpreter service is available through the clinic.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Start below her flare threshold, such as three minutes daily, and increase gradually each week): Starting below the level that triggers a flare lets her succeed and builds confidence, then time increases in small steps. Starting at brisk ten minutes or thirty minutes repeats the cycle that ended her walking. Waiting for pain to fall below a set score is unrealistic in persistent pain and keeps her inactive."}, {"id": 1131, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Nguyen walked ten minutes one morning because she felt well, though three minutes was agreed. Pain then rose to 9/10 for two days and she stayed in bed. She asks whether exercise is harming her. What is the most appropriate response and adjustment to her programme?", "options": {"A": "Explain the flare followed overdoing, restart at a steady baseline, and pace activity on good days", "B": "Replace walking with passive heat and massage until the flare resolves and her sleep improves", "C": "Continue ten minutes daily, since repeated exposure will desensitise her nervous system over time", "D": "Stop walking until pain returns to its usual level, then restart at the same ten minutes"}, "cluster_id": "CHS43", "cluster_text": "Mai Huynh Nguyen, 47, is a night-shift cleaner who attends the outpatient pain clinic with fibromyalgia diagnosed by her GP three years ago. She speaks Vietnamese and limited English. Her husband interpreted at earlier visits. She reports widespread aching pain (7/10), fatigue, unrefreshing sleep and morning stiffness. Blood tests and imaging were normal. She stopped her daily walks eighteen months ago and now manages about five minutes before pain builds, then rests for the afternoon. She fears that exercise will damage her muscles. Examination shows widespread tenderness to light pressure, full joint range, normal neurological findings and a Borg rating of 13 after one minute of brisk marching on the spot. A telephone interpreter service is available through the clinic.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Explain the flare followed overdoing, restart at a steady baseline, and pace activity on good days): Her flare followed doing far more than agreed on a good day, a typical boom and bust pattern. Reassuring her, restarting at the steady baseline and pacing on good days keeps the programme going. Repeating ten minutes would provoke another flare, and swapping to passive care removes the active work that benefits fibromyalgia."}, {"id": 1132, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "At the third visit the booked telephone interpreter is unavailable. Mr Nguyen offers to interpret, and the receptionist, who speaks some Vietnamese, offers to help. Mrs Nguyen is about to discuss her mood and stress at home. What should the physiotherapist do?", "options": {"A": "Use Mr Nguyen to interpret, since he knows her history well and avoids a long delay", "B": "Proceed in simple English with pictures and gestures, then confirm understanding by asking her to nod", "C": "Get another trained interpreter or reschedule, as family and staff risk errors and limit disclosure", "D": "Ask the receptionist to interpret briefly, then record the main points in her notes afterwards"}, "cluster_id": "CHS43", "cluster_text": "Mai Huynh Nguyen, 47, is a night-shift cleaner who attends the outpatient pain clinic with fibromyalgia diagnosed by her GP three years ago. She speaks Vietnamese and limited English. Her husband interpreted at earlier visits. She reports widespread aching pain (7/10), fatigue, unrefreshing sleep and morning stiffness. Blood tests and imaging were normal. She stopped her daily walks eighteen months ago and now manages about five minutes before pain builds, then rests for the afternoon. She fears that exercise will damage her muscles. Examination shows widespread tenderness to light pressure, full joint range, normal neurological findings and a Borg rating of 13 after one minute of brisk marching on the spot. A telephone interpreter service is available through the clinic.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Get another trained interpreter or reschedule, as family and staff risk errors and limit disclosure): A professional interpreter keeps the meaning accurate and lets her speak openly about mood and stress, which a husband or staff member may inhibit. Relatives and untrained staff can omit, add or soften information. Gestures and nodding cannot support informed discussion of sensitive topics."}, {"id": 1133, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "On the six minute walk test Koby's SpO2 fell from 94 to 82 percent while his heart rate peaked at 118, and it recovered to 92 percent after five minutes of seated rest. What is the most likely main mechanism of this exercise desaturation in idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis?", "options": {"A": "Airway collapse during forced expiration traps air and reduces effective alveolar ventilation as exercise intensity rises", "B": "Impaired gas diffusion across a thickened membrane worsens as exercise shortens capillary transit time", "C": "Reduced cardiac output from right ventricular strain is the usual main cause of the fall", "D": "Poor peripheral muscle conditioning causes early lactate build up and increases oxygen extraction from the blood"}, "cluster_id": "CHS44", "cluster_text": "Koby Foster, 68, a Wiradjuri man and retired shearer, has travelled 90 minutes from a regional town to start outpatient pulmonary rehabilitation. He has had idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis for four years, is not on oxygen, and has a resting SpO2 of 94 percent. Examination: bibasal fine end-inspiratory crackles and digital clubbing. Six minute walk test: 310 metres, SpO2 fell from 94 to 82 percent by minute three, peak HR 118, Borg dyspnoea 5 and leg fatigue 3; SpO2 recovered to 92 percent after five minutes seated. He attends alone because his niece, who usually travels with him, could not come. He speaks quietly, avoids direct eye contact, says little about his goals, and mentions he would rather talk about his breathing with the Aboriginal Health Worker at his local clinic. Sessions run in a mixed gym with a group of eight.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Impaired gas diffusion across a thickened membrane worsens as exercise shortens capillary transit time): Fibrosis thickens the gas exchange membrane, and during exercise blood moves through the capillaries too quickly for oxygen to equilibrate, so saturation falls. Dynamic airway collapse describes obstructive disease such as COPD. Right heart strain and deconditioning can contribute but do not explain the main fall."}, {"id": 1134, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Koby desaturated to 82 percent on the walk test and recovered after seated rest. He is about to begin the aerobic and strength training component of pulmonary rehabilitation in a group gym. What is the most appropriate way to start his exercise training?", "options": {"A": "Use high intensity intervals to maximise gains, checking saturation only after each block", "B": "Use low intensity intervals, monitor SpO2 and dyspnoea, and ask the doctor about exertional oxygen", "C": "Delay exercise until the desaturation resolves, as exercise is unsafe in fibrotic lung disease", "D": "Begin moderate intensity training and titrate oxygen to keep SpO2 above 94 percent during each walk"}, "cluster_id": "CHS44", "cluster_text": "Koby Foster, 68, a Wiradjuri man and retired shearer, has travelled 90 minutes from a regional town to start outpatient pulmonary rehabilitation. He has had idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis for four years, is not on oxygen, and has a resting SpO2 of 94 percent. Examination: bibasal fine end-inspiratory crackles and digital clubbing. Six minute walk test: 310 metres, SpO2 fell from 94 to 82 percent by minute three, peak HR 118, Borg dyspnoea 5 and leg fatigue 3; SpO2 recovered to 92 percent after five minutes seated. He attends alone because his niece, who usually travels with him, could not come. He speaks quietly, avoids direct eye contact, says little about his goals, and mentions he would rather talk about his breathing with the Aboriginal Health Worker at his local clinic. Sessions run in a mixed gym with a group of eight.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use low intensity intervals, monitor SpO2 and dyspnoea, and ask the doctor about exertional oxygen): Exercise training benefits people with fibrotic lung disease, but exertional desaturation calls for gentle intervals with continuous monitoring and medical input on oxygen, which the physiotherapist cannot prescribe. Withholding exercise removes a proven benefit. High intensity work with delayed checks, or self-directed oxygen titration, is unsafe."}, {"id": 1135, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Koby speaks quietly, avoids direct eye contact and says little when asked about his goals. He mentions he would rather talk with the Aboriginal Health Worker at his local clinic about his breathing. How should the physiotherapist best respond to provide culturally safe care?", "options": {"A": "Set clear goals for him from the standard program and explain the plan using written handouts", "B": "Treat his quiet manner as a cultural norm for Aboriginal men and keep sessions brief", "C": "Defer goal setting until his niece can attend, then plan the program mainly with her", "D": "Ask how Koby prefers to work and offer to involve his Aboriginal Health Worker"}, "cluster_id": "CHS44", "cluster_text": "Koby Foster, 68, a Wiradjuri man and retired shearer, has travelled 90 minutes from a regional town to start outpatient pulmonary rehabilitation. He has had idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis for four years, is not on oxygen, and has a resting SpO2 of 94 percent. Examination: bibasal fine end-inspiratory crackles and digital clubbing. Six minute walk test: 310 metres, SpO2 fell from 94 to 82 percent by minute three, peak HR 118, Borg dyspnoea 5 and leg fatigue 3; SpO2 recovered to 92 percent after five minutes seated. He attends alone because his niece, who usually travels with him, could not come. He speaks quietly, avoids direct eye contact, says little about his goals, and mentions he would rather talk about his breathing with the Aboriginal Health Worker at his local clinic. Sessions run in a mixed gym with a group of eight.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ask how Koby prefers to work and offer to involve his Aboriginal Health Worker): Culturally safe practice means asking the person how he wants care delivered and working with trusted supports he names, such as his Aboriginal Health Worker. Behaviour should not be read as a stereotype of a whole group. Setting goals for him or planning mainly through a relative removes his voice in his own care."}, {"id": 1136, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "During week two, Koby walks on the treadmill using the exertional oxygen flow his respiratory physician prescribed. He reports dyspnoea of 7 out of 10 and light-headedness, and his SpO2 reads 85 percent, with a heart rate well below his target. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Encourage him to continue at the same pace for two minutes to see whether saturation settles", "B": "Increase the oxygen flow on the portable unit until saturation returns above 90 percent", "C": "Stop the treadmill, seat him, monitor recovery, and report the response to the physician", "D": "Reduce the treadmill speed slightly and continue, as his heart rate is still below target"}, "cluster_id": "CHS44", "cluster_text": "Koby Foster, 68, a Wiradjuri man and retired shearer, has travelled 90 minutes from a regional town to start outpatient pulmonary rehabilitation. He has had idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis for four years, is not on oxygen, and has a resting SpO2 of 94 percent. Examination: bibasal fine end-inspiratory crackles and digital clubbing. Six minute walk test: 310 metres, SpO2 fell from 94 to 82 percent by minute three, peak HR 118, Borg dyspnoea 5 and leg fatigue 3; SpO2 recovered to 92 percent after five minutes seated. He attends alone because his niece, who usually travels with him, could not come. He speaks quietly, avoids direct eye contact, says little about his goals, and mentions he would rather talk about his breathing with the Aboriginal Health Worker at his local clinic. Sessions run in a mixed gym with a group of eight.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop the treadmill, seat him, monitor recovery, and report the response to the physician): Severe breathlessness, light-headedness and a low saturation despite prescribed oxygen are stop signs, whatever the heart rate shows. He should be seated and monitored until he recovers and the physician told, as his oxygen prescription may need review. Changing the flow without a prescription or carrying on would add risk."}, {"id": 1137, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Rosa's symptoms resolved after twenty minutes yesterday morning, and her neurological screen today is normal. She asks whether she can simply mention it to her GP at her appointment next week. What should the physiotherapist advise her to do about this symptom?", "options": {"A": "Arrange a GP visit with an ECG request, since the irregular pulse is the main concern", "B": "Keep next week's GP appointment, note the event, and reassure her resolved symptoms mean no damage", "C": "Advise this may have been a TIA and arrange urgent same day medical assessment", "D": "Complete today's falls session and ask her to ring the GP if symptoms recur before then"}, "cluster_id": "CHS45", "cluster_text": "Rosa Moretti, 78, is a retired seamstress receiving community physiotherapy at home for a falls prevention program after a fall last month. Medical history: hypertension, and an irregular pulse noted by her GP but not yet investigated. She is not on blood thinning medication. At today's visit she mentions that yesterday morning she had 20 minutes of right arm weakness and slurred speech, which settled completely. She did not tell anyone. Today she feels well. Neurological screen: face symmetrical, power 5/5 in all limbs, speech clear, sensation intact, gait steady with her stick. HR 96 irregular, BP 168/94, SpO2 97 percent. You also have four more home visits booked this afternoon: a routine review for a client with stable chronic knee osteoarthritis, a client discharged from hospital yesterday after a fall, and two NDIS progress reviews.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Advise this may have been a TIA and arrange urgent same day medical assessment): A brief episode of focal weakness and slurred speech that resolves is a transient ischaemic attack until proven otherwise, and the risk of a disabling stroke is highest in the first hours and days. With an irregular pulse suggesting atrial fibrillation, she needs same day medical assessment rather than a delayed appointment. Normal findings today do not remove the risk."}, {"id": 1138, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Rosa is reluctant to attend an emergency department because her symptoms have gone. The physiotherapist wants to explain the reason for urgency in plain language. Which statement about transient ischaemic attack (TIA) is most accurate and appropriate to share with her?", "options": {"A": "Symptoms that settle in an hour carry little risk, so a GP visit next week suffices", "B": "The risk of stroke is highest soon after a TIA, so quick assessment can prevent one", "C": "Her risk is mainly from raised blood pressure, so home monitoring and medication review come first", "D": "Assessment is needed only if symptoms return, since a TIA that resolves rarely recurs soon"}, "cluster_id": "CHS45", "cluster_text": "Rosa Moretti, 78, is a retired seamstress receiving community physiotherapy at home for a falls prevention program after a fall last month. Medical history: hypertension, and an irregular pulse noted by her GP but not yet investigated. She is not on blood thinning medication. At today's visit she mentions that yesterday morning she had 20 minutes of right arm weakness and slurred speech, which settled completely. She did not tell anyone. Today she feels well. Neurological screen: face symmetrical, power 5/5 in all limbs, speech clear, sensation intact, gait steady with her stick. HR 96 irregular, BP 168/94, SpO2 97 percent. You also have four more home visits booked this afternoon: a routine review for a client with stable chronic knee osteoarthritis, a client discharged from hospital yesterday after a fall, and two NDIS progress reviews.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The risk of stroke is highest soon after a TIA, so quick assessment can prevent one): The stroke risk after a TIA is greatest in the first days, and prompt assessment allows imaging, cardiac rhythm checks and preventive treatment to start, which is the reason for urgency. A TIA that has settled still signals an underlying cause, so waiting for a visit next week leaves her exposed. Her blood pressure does matter, but home monitoring alone does not assess her irregular pulse or start prevention. Waiting for symptoms to return risks the stroke the assessment is meant to prevent."}, {"id": 1139, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Two weeks later Rosa has been assessed, started on preventive medication and her BP is 142/86. Her medical team has cleared her for physical activity and she has had no further episodes. She asks about exercise. Which exercise advice is most appropriate to include in her home program?", "options": {"A": "Short supervised walks only, avoiding balance training until further imaging excludes new vascular changes", "B": "Moderate aerobic activity on most days with balance work, and report symptoms at her next visit", "C": "Gradual moderate aerobic activity on most days with balance work, and call 000 if symptoms return", "D": "Strength training with controlled breathing twice weekly, deferring aerobic and balance work until BP normalises"}, "cluster_id": "CHS45", "cluster_text": "Rosa Moretti, 78, is a retired seamstress receiving community physiotherapy at home for a falls prevention program after a fall last month. Medical history: hypertension, and an irregular pulse noted by her GP but not yet investigated. She is not on blood thinning medication. At today's visit she mentions that yesterday morning she had 20 minutes of right arm weakness and slurred speech, which settled completely. She did not tell anyone. Today she feels well. Neurological screen: face symmetrical, power 5/5 in all limbs, speech clear, sensation intact, gait steady with her stick. HR 96 irregular, BP 168/94, SpO2 97 percent. You also have four more home visits booked this afternoon: a routine review for a client with stable chronic knee osteoarthritis, a client discharged from hospital yesterday after a fall, and two NDIS progress reviews.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Gradual moderate aerobic activity on most days with balance work, and call 000 if symptoms return): Regular graded aerobic activity with balance training lowers vascular risk and falls risk, and a clear plan for recurrent symptoms keeps her safe. Walking alone leaves her falls risk unaddressed, and delaying balance work until imaging is repeated is unnecessary once she has medical clearance. Leaving symptom reporting to the next visit delays emergency care, and strength work without aerobic and balance training neglects her main needs."}, {"id": 1140, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "After arranging urgent transfer for Rosa you are 50 minutes behind with four visits left: a stable knee osteoarthritis review, a client discharged yesterday after a fall, and two NDIS progress reviews. Which approach to the rest of the afternoon is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Keep the original order to treat clients fairly, and apologise at each door for the delay", "B": "Cancel the remaining visits and phone each client tomorrow, since the afternoon is already compromised", "C": "See the recently discharged client first, then ask the coordinator to rebook and inform others", "D": "Ask an allied health assistant to assess the discharged client alone, so the schedule is kept"}, "cluster_id": "CHS45", "cluster_text": "Rosa Moretti, 78, is a retired seamstress receiving community physiotherapy at home for a falls prevention program after a fall last month. Medical history: hypertension, and an irregular pulse noted by her GP but not yet investigated. She is not on blood thinning medication. At today's visit she mentions that yesterday morning she had 20 minutes of right arm weakness and slurred speech, which settled completely. She did not tell anyone. Today she feels well. Neurological screen: face symmetrical, power 5/5 in all limbs, speech clear, sensation intact, gait steady with her stick. HR 96 irregular, BP 168/94, SpO2 97 percent. You also have four more home visits booked this afternoon: a routine review for a client with stable chronic knee osteoarthritis, a client discharged from hospital yesterday after a fall, and two NDIS progress reviews.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (See the recently discharged client first, then ask the coordinator to rebook and inform others): Prioritising by clinical risk puts the client recently home after a fall first, because early follow up is when problems appear, while stable and routine reviews can be safely rebooked. Telling clients about delays respects their needs, and the coordinator can reorganise the list. Cancelling everything or delegating a new assessment to an assistant is neither safe nor within delegation limits."}, {"id": 1141, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Esposito's pain is 5/10 on movement, his lying BP is 108/62 mmHg and his Hb is 92 g/L the day after surgery. Before the first mobilisation attempt with a frame and one assistant, which approach is most appropriate for the physiotherapist to take now?", "options": {"A": "Assess resisted hip abduction in supine first to confirm strength of the operated limb before sitting", "B": "Stand him straight from lying with the frame, as early weight bearing prevents blood pressure falls", "C": "Delay all mobilising until his haemoglobin rises above the normal range, because anaemia prevents weight bearing", "D": "Check analgesia timing, then progress from lying to sitting and monitor BP, HR and symptoms"}, "cluster_id": "CHS46", "cluster_text": "Paolo Esposito, 83, a retired mechanic, had a cemented hemiarthroplasty (posterior approach) yesterday for a displaced right intracapsular neck of femur fracture after a fall at home. Surgeon's orders: weight bear as tolerated and follow posterior hip precautions. History: type 2 diabetes, hypertension (taking an ACE inhibitor) and mild cognitive impairment noted by his GP. He lives with his wife in a house with three entry steps. Today he is alert, oriented to person and place but not to date. Pain is 5/10 on movement and 2/10 at rest on regular paracetamol and as needed oxycodone. HR 96, BP lying 108/62 mmHg, SpO2 95 percent on room air, Hb 92 g/L. The wound dressing is dry and the calves are soft and non-tender. He grimaces and says he is tired and wants to be left alone. His daughter, at the bedside, wants him up straight away.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Check analgesia timing, then progress from lying to sitting and monitor BP, HR and symptoms): A low normal lying BP and post-operative anaemia raise the risk of postural hypotension, so mobilisation should be staged with well timed analgesia and observation of BP, pulse and symptoms. Standing straight from lying skips that safety step. Anaemia at this level calls for monitoring, not a ban on mobilising, and early mobilisation is a key aim after hip fracture surgery. Testing resisted abduction adds nothing safe before sitting."}, {"id": 1142, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Esposito's daughter asks why staff want him standing so soon after surgery. The physiotherapist explains the standard of care. According to Australian clinical care standards for hip fracture, when should an older person who is clinically stable first be mobilised after surgery?", "options": {"A": "Once his pain, haemoglobin and blood pressure have stabilised, usually several days after surgery", "B": "Out of bed on the day after surgery, standing and walking if able", "C": "Once his confusion has cleared and he can follow instructions reliably, to protect the new hip", "D": "After day two, once the surgical team has reviewed the wound and formally cleared him"}, "cluster_id": "CHS46", "cluster_text": "Paolo Esposito, 83, a retired mechanic, had a cemented hemiarthroplasty (posterior approach) yesterday for a displaced right intracapsular neck of femur fracture after a fall at home. Surgeon's orders: weight bear as tolerated and follow posterior hip precautions. History: type 2 diabetes, hypertension (taking an ACE inhibitor) and mild cognitive impairment noted by his GP. He lives with his wife in a house with three entry steps. Today he is alert, oriented to person and place but not to date. Pain is 5/10 on movement and 2/10 at rest on regular paracetamol and as needed oxycodone. HR 96, BP lying 108/62 mmHg, SpO2 95 percent on room air, Hb 92 g/L. The wound dressing is dry and the calves are soft and non-tender. He grimaces and says he is tired and wants to be left alone. His daughter, at the bedside, wants him up straight away.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Out of bed on the day after surgery, standing and walking if able): The standard supports mobilising stable patients out of bed the day after surgery, because early activity lowers the risk of pneumonia, pressure injury, delirium and functional decline, and his orders already allow weight bearing. Waiting for confusion to clear or for several days of stability prolongs bed rest and adds the very complications mobilisation prevents. Waiting for a later formal clearance adds avoidable delay when orders are already in place."}, {"id": 1143, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Esposito tells the physiotherapist he does not want to get out of bed today, saying he is tired and sore. He follows the conversation, but cannot recall his surgery. His daughter insists he must get up and says she will sign for him. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Mobilise him gently with two staff, as the risks of delayed mobilisation outweigh his current wishes", "B": "Proceed with mobilising, since his daughter as next of kin can consent for him", "C": "Record that memory impairment means he lacks capacity, then mobilise him in his best interests", "D": "Explain benefits and risks simply, check he understands this decision, and respect his choice"}, "cluster_id": "CHS46", "cluster_text": "Paolo Esposito, 83, a retired mechanic, had a cemented hemiarthroplasty (posterior approach) yesterday for a displaced right intracapsular neck of femur fracture after a fall at home. Surgeon's orders: weight bear as tolerated and follow posterior hip precautions. History: type 2 diabetes, hypertension (taking an ACE inhibitor) and mild cognitive impairment noted by his GP. He lives with his wife in a house with three entry steps. Today he is alert, oriented to person and place but not to date. Pain is 5/10 on movement and 2/10 at rest on regular paracetamol and as needed oxycodone. HR 96, BP lying 108/62 mmHg, SpO2 95 percent on room air, Hb 92 g/L. The wound dressing is dry and the calves are soft and non-tender. He grimaces and says he is tired and wants to be left alone. His daughter, at the bedside, wants him up straight away.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain benefits and risks simply, check he understands this decision, and respect his choice): Capacity is presumed and is specific to each decision, so a memory problem does not remove it. The physiotherapist should give information he can follow, check he understands and can weigh it, and respect an informed refusal while offering to try later. A relative cannot override a capable adult's decision, and the benefits of early mobilisation do not justify proceeding against his wishes."}, {"id": 1144, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Esposito agrees to sit at the edge of the bed with a staff member supporting him. After two minutes he becomes pale and sweaty and reports dizziness. Seated BP is 84/50 mmHg and HR 112. What should the physiotherapist do next?", "options": {"A": "Stand him with the frame to activate the calf muscle pump and raise his blood pressure", "B": "Keep him sitting and encourage slow deep breaths, as postural symptoms usually settle if he persists", "C": "Offer sips of water and recheck his blood pressure after ten minutes while he remains seated", "D": "Return him to lying, monitor observations and inform the nursing and medical team"}, "cluster_id": "CHS46", "cluster_text": "Paolo Esposito, 83, a retired mechanic, had a cemented hemiarthroplasty (posterior approach) yesterday for a displaced right intracapsular neck of femur fracture after a fall at home. Surgeon's orders: weight bear as tolerated and follow posterior hip precautions. History: type 2 diabetes, hypertension (taking an ACE inhibitor) and mild cognitive impairment noted by his GP. He lives with his wife in a house with three entry steps. Today he is alert, oriented to person and place but not to date. Pain is 5/10 on movement and 2/10 at rest on regular paracetamol and as needed oxycodone. HR 96, BP lying 108/62 mmHg, SpO2 95 percent on room air, Hb 92 g/L. The wound dressing is dry and the calves are soft and non-tender. He grimaces and says he is tired and wants to be left alone. His daughter, at the bedside, wants him up straight away.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Return him to lying, monitor observations and inform the nursing and medical team): Symptomatic hypotension with tachycardia after hip surgery and anaemia may reflect blood loss, dehydration or medication effects, so activity must stop and he should lie flat while observations are repeated and the team is told. Persisting upright or standing risks a faint and a fall onto the new hip. Waiting seated for ten minutes delays treatment of a possibly serious cause."}, {"id": 1145, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "George has a 4 cm bright red area over the sacrum that stays red when pressed, with skin intact and no blister. A new graduate asks how this finding is classified using the standard pressure injury staging system. What is the correct stage?", "options": {"A": "Unstageable pressure injury: full thickness tissue loss with the wound base obscured by slough or eschar", "B": "Deep tissue pressure injury: persistent non-blanchable deep red, maroon or purple discolouration of intact skin", "C": "Stage 1 pressure injury: intact skin with non-blanchable erythema over a bony prominence", "D": "Stage 2 pressure injury: partial thickness skin loss with an exposed shallow open wound bed"}, "cluster_id": "CHS47", "cluster_text": "George Papadopoulos, 83, lives in a residential aged care facility. He sustained an incomplete T10 spinal cord injury in a fall two years ago and uses a wheelchair, transferring with one assistant. He has a neurogenic bladder managed with intermittent catheterisation by nursing staff, and constipation managed with a regular bowel routine. Sensation is reduced below T10, so he cannot feel when he needs to shift his weight. Sitting blood pressure is 118/70. History: type 2 diabetes and mild cognitive impairment. His son visits daily. In the gym today you notice a 4 cm bright red area over his left sacrum that does not blanch when pressed, with the skin intact, as he is helped onto the plinth. He says it does not hurt and asks you not to tell the nursing staff because he fears being sent to hospital. Staff report he sits in the wheelchair for long periods each day on a standard foam cushion.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stage 1 pressure injury: intact skin with non-blanchable erythema over a bony prominence): Non-blanchable erythema of intact skin over a bony prominence defines a stage 1 pressure injury, and it is a warning that tissue is already under stress. Deep tissue injury shows maroon or purple discolouration rather than bright red, and partial or full thickness loss requires visible skin breakdown, which is not present."}, {"id": 1146, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "George's redness is over the sacrum and his wheelchair cushion is a standard foam type with long daily sitting. He cannot feel pressure below T10. Which intervention is most appropriate to implement for his skin protection and pressure relief at this session?", "options": {"A": "Apply a protective dressing, then continue usual sitting with scheduled forward leans for pressure relief", "B": "Offload the sacrum, limit sitting on the area, and arrange a pressure-redistributing cushion review", "C": "Switch him to a slightly firmer cushion and monitor the area at the next scheduled session", "D": "Report the area to nursing staff and continue his standard programme, since the skin is intact"}, "cluster_id": "CHS47", "cluster_text": "George Papadopoulos, 83, lives in a residential aged care facility. He sustained an incomplete T10 spinal cord injury in a fall two years ago and uses a wheelchair, transferring with one assistant. He has a neurogenic bladder managed with intermittent catheterisation by nursing staff, and constipation managed with a regular bowel routine. Sensation is reduced below T10, so he cannot feel when he needs to shift his weight. Sitting blood pressure is 118/70. History: type 2 diabetes and mild cognitive impairment. His son visits daily. In the gym today you notice a 4 cm bright red area over his left sacrum that does not blanch when pressed, with the skin intact, as he is helped onto the plinth. He says it does not hurt and asks you not to tell the nursing staff because he fears being sent to hospital. Staff report he sits in the wheelchair for long periods each day on a standard foam cushion.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Offload the sacrum, limit sitting on the area, and arrange a pressure-redistributing cushion review): A stage 1 injury is reversible if pressure is removed promptly, so the sacrum is offloaded and his seating reviewed, because a standard foam cushion redistributes pressure poorly for someone without sensation. A dressing with continued sitting keeps load on the area, a firmer cushion gives less redistribution, and reporting alone without offloading leaves the pressure in place."}, {"id": 1147, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "George's bowel routine has been unsuccessful on the bed, where he is positioned flat on his back. The nurse asks the physiotherapist about position and support. Which approach best supports effective bowel emptying while protecting his skin and sacral area during the routine?", "options": {"A": "Flat supine in bed with a pillow under the knees to relax the pelvic floor", "B": "Upright sitting on a commode with feet supported, accepting sacral pressure for a short time", "C": "Prone lying over a pillow so gravity assists emptying, with the head turned to the side", "D": "Left side lying, hips and knees flexed, pillows placed to keep load off the sacrum"}, "cluster_id": "CHS47", "cluster_text": "George Papadopoulos, 83, lives in a residential aged care facility. He sustained an incomplete T10 spinal cord injury in a fall two years ago and uses a wheelchair, transferring with one assistant. He has a neurogenic bladder managed with intermittent catheterisation by nursing staff, and constipation managed with a regular bowel routine. Sensation is reduced below T10, so he cannot feel when he needs to shift his weight. Sitting blood pressure is 118/70. History: type 2 diabetes and mild cognitive impairment. His son visits daily. In the gym today you notice a 4 cm bright red area over his left sacrum that does not blanch when pressed, with the skin intact, as he is helped onto the plinth. He says it does not hurt and asks you not to tell the nursing staff because he fears being sent to hospital. Staff report he sits in the wheelchair for long periods each day on a standard foam cushion.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Left side lying, hips and knees flexed, pillows placed to keep load off the sacrum): Left side lying with hips and knees flexed follows the direction of the colon and is a standard alternative to sitting, and with pillows it keeps pressure off the sacral area where he already has early skin damage. Sitting on a commode would load the sacrum and ischia, while supine and prone positions do not suit colonic anatomy or are poorly tolerated."}, {"id": 1148, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "George asks you again not to tell nursing staff about the red area, saying he trusts you and fears being sent to hospital. He has mild cognitive impairment but understands your explanation. What should the physiotherapist do about George's request for secrecy?", "options": {"A": "Record it in the physiotherapy notes only and manage it yourself by offloading the area", "B": "Explain gently that his care team must know, and involve him in how it is shared", "C": "Respect his wishes, keep it confidential and recheck the area next session before deciding", "D": "Ask his son, who visits daily, whether the nursing staff should be told about it"}, "cluster_id": "CHS47", "cluster_text": "George Papadopoulos, 83, lives in a residential aged care facility. He sustained an incomplete T10 spinal cord injury in a fall two years ago and uses a wheelchair, transferring with one assistant. He has a neurogenic bladder managed with intermittent catheterisation by nursing staff, and constipation managed with a regular bowel routine. Sensation is reduced below T10, so he cannot feel when he needs to shift his weight. Sitting blood pressure is 118/70. History: type 2 diabetes and mild cognitive impairment. His son visits daily. In the gym today you notice a 4 cm bright red area over his left sacrum that does not blanch when pressed, with the skin intact, as he is helped onto the plinth. He says it does not hurt and asks you not to tell the nursing staff because he fears being sent to hospital. Staff report he sits in the wheelchair for long periods each day on a standard foam cushion.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Explain gently that his care team must know, and involve him in how it is shared): Sharing relevant clinical findings with the treating team is part of duty of care and is consistent with consent when the patient is told why, because a pressure injury needs coordinated nursing and seating action. Agreeing to secrecy or managing it alone leaves the problem unmonitored and undermines team responsibility. Involving him supports his autonomy and fears, and relatives do not decide on disclosure for a patient who understands."}, {"id": 1149, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Kylie's Beighton score is 7/9, and she has recurrent shoulder and ankle instability with end of day pain. The GP has not recorded any diagnosis of a connective tissue or hypermobility disorder. How should the physiotherapist interpret and use this score when planning her care?", "options": {"A": "It is a screening count of laxity only and has no value for planning treatment here", "B": "It shows generalised laxity to inform a symptom-led plan, but diagnosis belongs to medical colleagues", "C": "It predicts she will need surgery, so exercise should wait until a surgeon has reviewed her", "D": "It confirms a connective tissue disorder, so the physiotherapist should record that diagnosis and treat accordingly"}, "cluster_id": "CHS48", "cluster_text": "Kylie Watego, 29, an Aboriginal woman and early childhood educator, attends your private practice with a GP referral for recurrent shoulder and ankle problems. Both shoulders have subluxated several times, and she has sprained each ankle repeatedly. Pain reaches 5/10 by the end of a work day. Beighton score is 7/9. Sulcus sign and anterior apprehension are positive on both sides, scapular control is poor in elevation, and single leg stance with eyes closed lasts 8 seconds. Strength is 4+/5 throughout. She mentions occasional lightheadedness and a racing heart when standing quickly. She travelled from her community with her aunty, who sits in the waiting room. Kylie answers questions briefly and looks at the floor. No connective tissue disorder has been diagnosed.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It shows generalised laxity to inform a symptom-led plan, but diagnosis belongs to medical colleagues): The Beighton score screens for generalised laxity and does not diagnose a disorder, so her symptoms and function drive the plan. Diagnosis of a hypermobility spectrum or connective tissue disorder rests with medical practitioners, and recording one without that assessment would overstep. Waiting for surgery is wrong because instability of this kind is first managed with exercise."}, {"id": 1150, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Kylie's shoulders sublux with overhead elevation and her ankles roll on uneven ground. She lifts children and stands for long periods at work, and wants to start exercise this week. Which initial exercise approach is most appropriate to improve joint control and reduce the risk of further injury?", "options": {"A": "End-range stretching of the lax joints first, to improve flexibility before any strengthening begins", "B": "Heavy resistance training through full range to build muscle bulk quickly around the unstable joints", "C": "Low load control and balance work in mid-range positions, progressing gradually to strengthening", "D": "Rest from loading until pain settles, using supports for the shoulders and ankles during daily tasks"}, "cluster_id": "CHS48", "cluster_text": "Kylie Watego, 29, an Aboriginal woman and early childhood educator, attends your private practice with a GP referral for recurrent shoulder and ankle problems. Both shoulders have subluxated several times, and she has sprained each ankle repeatedly. Pain reaches 5/10 by the end of a work day. Beighton score is 7/9. Sulcus sign and anterior apprehension are positive on both sides, scapular control is poor in elevation, and single leg stance with eyes closed lasts 8 seconds. Strength is 4+/5 throughout. She mentions occasional lightheadedness and a racing heart when standing quickly. She travelled from her community with her aunty, who sits in the waiting room. Kylie answers questions briefly and looks at the floor. No connective tissue disorder has been diagnosed.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Low load control and balance work in mid-range positions, progressing gradually to strengthening): Hypermobile joints need dynamic stability, so low load control and proprioceptive work in mid-range, then graded strengthening, suits her shoulder and ankle instability. Stretching lax joints to end range adds length she already has, and heavy loading too early risks subluxation. Resting from loading leaves the deficits in control that cause the instability."}, {"id": 1151, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Kylie reports lightheadedness and a pounding heartbeat when she stands up quickly, which occurred twice last week, once while carrying a child. She has no chest pain or fainting. Before progressing standing balance work, what is the most appropriate step for the physiotherapist?", "options": {"A": "Limit sessions to seated shoulder work until the dizziness stops, then restart standing exercise", "B": "Stop all exercise and refer her directly to a cardiologist for investigation of a possible arrhythmia", "C": "Continue the balance programme and advise her to drink more fluids and rise slowly", "D": "Record lying and standing heart rate and blood pressure today, then report findings to her GP"}, "cluster_id": "CHS48", "cluster_text": "Kylie Watego, 29, an Aboriginal woman and early childhood educator, attends your private practice with a GP referral for recurrent shoulder and ankle problems. Both shoulders have subluxated several times, and she has sprained each ankle repeatedly. Pain reaches 5/10 by the end of a work day. Beighton score is 7/9. Sulcus sign and anterior apprehension are positive on both sides, scapular control is poor in elevation, and single leg stance with eyes closed lasts 8 seconds. Strength is 4+/5 throughout. She mentions occasional lightheadedness and a racing heart when standing quickly. She travelled from her community with her aunty, who sits in the waiting room. Kylie answers questions briefly and looks at the floor. No connective tissue disorder has been diagnosed.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Record lying and standing heart rate and blood pressure today, then report findings to her GP): Lightheadedness and palpitations on standing in a person with joint hypermobility raise the possibility of orthostatic intolerance, which is simple to screen with lying and standing observations. Sharing the result with the GP lets medical staff decide on investigation. Stopping exercise or referring straight to a specialist skips that simple step, and seated shoulder work does not address the cause."}, {"id": 1152, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Kylie answers questions briefly and avoids eye contact. Her aunty is in the waiting room. You want the consultation to be culturally safe for an Aboriginal woman. What is the most appropriate way to proceed with the assessment and planning discussion?", "options": {"A": "Arrange a referral to an Aboriginal liaison officer first and delay the assessment until they attend", "B": "Explain the plan plainly, invite her to choose who attends, and let her set the pace", "C": "Ask her aunty to join and answer for her, as family usually decide health matters together", "D": "Interview her alone to protect privacy, and note her reluctance to speak as low motivation"}, "cluster_id": "CHS48", "cluster_text": "Kylie Watego, 29, an Aboriginal woman and early childhood educator, attends your private practice with a GP referral for recurrent shoulder and ankle problems. Both shoulders have subluxated several times, and she has sprained each ankle repeatedly. Pain reaches 5/10 by the end of a work day. Beighton score is 7/9. Sulcus sign and anterior apprehension are positive on both sides, scapular control is poor in elevation, and single leg stance with eyes closed lasts 8 seconds. Strength is 4+/5 throughout. She mentions occasional lightheadedness and a racing heart when standing quickly. She travelled from her community with her aunty, who sits in the waiting room. Kylie answers questions briefly and looks at the floor. No connective tissue disorder has been diagnosed.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Explain the plan plainly, invite her to choose who attends, and let her set the pace): Cultural safety means the patient decides how involved family and others will be, and the physiotherapist builds trust by explaining plainly and slowing the pace. Assuming family should speak for her, or reading limited eye contact as disinterest, applies a stereotype rather than asking her. A liaison officer is valuable when she wants one, but delaying care without asking removes her say in the matter."}, {"id": 1153, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Kaleopa repeats his invitation to the family barbecue and asks to connect on your personal social media, explaining that he likes you and wants to thank you. He remains your patient. How should you respond, considering professional boundaries and duty of care?", "options": {"A": "Accept the social media request on a restricted privacy setting and politely decline the barbecue invitation", "B": "Decline both kindly, explain the need for a professional relationship and continue his care", "C": "Transfer his care to a colleague straight away, explaining that the friendship has compromised the relationship", "D": "Attend the barbecue briefly with another colleague, decline the social media request and tell your supervisor"}, "cluster_id": "CHS49", "cluster_text": "Mr Sam Kaleopa, 52, a concrete finisher of Samoan background, attends a private practice with four months of night waking from tingling in his right thumb, index and middle fingers, eased by shaking the hand. He drops tools and grip feels weak. He has type 2 diabetes (recent HbA1c 8.2 percent) managed with metformin. Tinel's and Phalen's tests are positive on the right. Thumb palmar abduction is 4/5 with mild thenar wasting, and two-point discrimination is reduced in the median distribution. Sensation over the thenar eminence is normal. Cervical examination is normal. He is warm and talkative, has invited you to his family barbecue and asked whether he can follow your personal social media accounts.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Decline both kindly, explain the need for a professional relationship and continue his care): A kind, clear refusal maintains the professional relationship and protects the patient's trust while care continues. Attending social events or accepting personal social media blurs boundaries even with precautions, and transferring care is not needed when the boundary can be set and kept."}, {"id": 1154, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "On examination of his right hand Mr Kaleopa has normal sensation over the thenar eminence despite numbness in the right thumb, index and middle fingers. Why is this sparing consistent with carpal tunnel syndrome rather than a more proximal median nerve lesion?", "options": {"A": "The recurrent motor branch carries the thenar sensation, and it is compressed distal to the tunnel", "B": "Diabetic peripheral neuropathy spares proximal skin territories, so sparing indicates a metabolic cause not entrapment", "C": "The palmar cutaneous branch arises proximal to the tunnel and passes superficial to it", "D": "The thenar skin is ulnar nerve territory, and that nerve passes through Guyon's canal instead"}, "cluster_id": "CHS49", "cluster_text": "Mr Sam Kaleopa, 52, a concrete finisher of Samoan background, attends a private practice with four months of night waking from tingling in his right thumb, index and middle fingers, eased by shaking the hand. He drops tools and grip feels weak. He has type 2 diabetes (recent HbA1c 8.2 percent) managed with metformin. Tinel's and Phalen's tests are positive on the right. Thumb palmar abduction is 4/5 with mild thenar wasting, and two-point discrimination is reduced in the median distribution. Sensation over the thenar eminence is normal. Cervical examination is normal. He is warm and talkative, has invited you to his family barbecue and asked whether he can follow your personal social media accounts.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The palmar cutaneous branch arises proximal to the tunnel and passes superficial to it): The palmar cutaneous branch leaves the median nerve above the carpal tunnel and crosses over it, so skin of the thenar eminence is spared in carpal tunnel syndrome. A proximal lesion would usually affect that skin too. The thenar skin is not ulnar territory, and diabetic neuropathy does not explain a sparing pattern."}, {"id": 1155, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Kaleopa has positive Tinel's and Phalen's tests, mild thenar wasting, 4/5 thumb palmar abduction and night symptoms of four months. He wants to keep working full time as a concrete finisher, and his diabetes is only moderately controlled. Which management plan is most appropriate to begin?", "options": {"A": "Wrist extension splint worn through the day and night, with grip strengthening to counter thenar weakness", "B": "Neutral night splint and task modification, with referral via his GP for a surgical opinion", "C": "Neutral night splint, tendon and nerve gliding exercises and a six-week trial before considering any referral", "D": "Ultrasound and carpal bone mobilisation twice weekly, then reassess in four weeks before deciding on referral"}, "cluster_id": "CHS49", "cluster_text": "Mr Sam Kaleopa, 52, a concrete finisher of Samoan background, attends a private practice with four months of night waking from tingling in his right thumb, index and middle fingers, eased by shaking the hand. He drops tools and grip feels weak. He has type 2 diabetes (recent HbA1c 8.2 percent) managed with metformin. Tinel's and Phalen's tests are positive on the right. Thumb palmar abduction is 4/5 with mild thenar wasting, and two-point discrimination is reduced in the median distribution. Sensation over the thenar eminence is normal. Cervical examination is normal. He is warm and talkative, has invited you to his family barbecue and asked whether he can follow your personal social media accounts.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Neutral night splint and task modification, with referral via his GP for a surgical opinion): Night splinting and task modification are reasonable, but thenar wasting and reduced abduction suggest axonal involvement and warrant a surgical opinion through the GP. A prolonged conservative trial without referral risks irreversible motor loss, and a day-and-night extension splint, strengthening or ultrasound does not appropriately decompress the nerve."}, {"id": 1156, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Kaleopa returns after a six-week trial: night symptoms are unchanged, thumb palmar abduction has fallen to 3/5 and thenar wasting is more obvious. A nerve conduction study requested by his GP shows moderate to severe median neuropathy at the wrist. What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Refer him to a diabetes educator for glycaemic control, then reassess the hand once HbA1c improves", "B": "Extend the splint trial another six weeks with nerve sliding exercises before reviewing referral", "C": "Communicate with the GP to expedite surgical referral, as conservative care has failed", "D": "Start progressive thenar strengthening with putty and grip training to reverse the wasting before any referral"}, "cluster_id": "CHS49", "cluster_text": "Mr Sam Kaleopa, 52, a concrete finisher of Samoan background, attends a private practice with four months of night waking from tingling in his right thumb, index and middle fingers, eased by shaking the hand. He drops tools and grip feels weak. He has type 2 diabetes (recent HbA1c 8.2 percent) managed with metformin. Tinel's and Phalen's tests are positive on the right. Thumb palmar abduction is 4/5 with mild thenar wasting, and two-point discrimination is reduced in the median distribution. Sensation over the thenar eminence is normal. Cervical examination is normal. He is warm and talkative, has invited you to his family barbecue and asked whether he can follow your personal social media accounts.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Communicate with the GP to expedite surgical referral, as conservative care has failed): Worsening abduction, more wasting and moderate to severe conduction changes after a splint trial show conservative care has failed, so prompt surgical referral through the GP is needed. Extending the trial, strengthening or waiting for better glycaemic control delays decompression and risks permanent motor loss."}, {"id": 1157, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Ms Do is wheezy, speaking in short phrases, and has a PEF of 60 percent of her personal best. Which additional finding would most clearly signal a life-threatening deterioration requiring immediate emergency ambulance activation during your assessment in the clinic room?", "options": {"A": "Drowsiness or confusion with a silent chest and oxygen saturation that keeps falling", "B": "Use of the neck muscles on inspiration together with a productive cough of white mucoid sputum", "C": "Tachycardia at rest with mild anxiety and a persistent urge to keep exercising during the session", "D": "Wheeze that is louder than before the reliever was taken, with a persistent dry cough"}, "cluster_id": "CHS50", "cluster_text": "Huong Do, 29, barista, attends a community health centre for general reconditioning after a viral illness. Asthma since childhood. She has a low-dose inhaled corticosteroid preventer but admits she often misses doses. Over two days she has had more cough and wheeze, woken twice overnight, and used her blue reliever every three hours with short-lived relief. Today: RR 26, HR 110, SpO2 93 percent on room air, speaking in short phrases, diffuse expiratory wheeze, light accessory neck muscle use. Peak expiratory flow is 60 percent of her personal best. She says she wants to 'push through' the session. Her sister has given her a printout about a breathing technique said to replace preventer medication. She carries her reliever and a spacer.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Drowsiness or confusion with a silent chest and oxygen saturation that keeps falling): Reduced consciousness, a silent chest and falling saturation indicate exhaustion and near-complete airflow obstruction, so ambulance activation cannot wait. Louder wheeze after a reliever often means air is moving better, and tachycardia, anxiety, mild accessory muscle use and sputum all fit a moderate exacerbation that she already has."}, {"id": 1158, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Her speech is limited to short phrases. Her PEF is 60 percent of personal best, with SpO2 93 percent on room air, and she carries a reliever inhaler and spacer. You stop the exercise session. What is the most appropriate immediate action?", "options": {"A": "Sit her upright, help her take reliever puffs through her spacer, and reassess after four minutes", "B": "Call an ambulance and stay with her, leaving her reliever for the paramedics to give", "C": "Guide diaphragmatic breathing in sitting for ten minutes and give the reliever only if wheeze persists", "D": "Have her lie back with her head supported and sip warm water until the wheeze settles"}, "cluster_id": "CHS50", "cluster_text": "Huong Do, 29, barista, attends a community health centre for general reconditioning after a viral illness. Asthma since childhood. She has a low-dose inhaled corticosteroid preventer but admits she often misses doses. Over two days she has had more cough and wheeze, woken twice overnight, and used her blue reliever every three hours with short-lived relief. Today: RR 26, HR 110, SpO2 93 percent on room air, speaking in short phrases, diffuse expiratory wheeze, light accessory neck muscle use. Peak expiratory flow is 60 percent of her personal best. She says she wants to 'push through' the session. Her sister has given her a printout about a breathing technique said to replace preventer medication. She carries her reliever and a spacer.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Sit her upright, help her take reliever puffs through her spacer, and reassess after four minutes): Asthma first aid starts with sitting upright and giving reliever doses through a spacer, then checking the response after a short wait. Lying back worsens breathing effort, delaying the reliever to try breathing exercises wastes treatment time, and holding back her own medication for paramedics withholds an effective, immediate treatment."}, {"id": 1159, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Ms Do has had four puffs, waited four minutes, then taken four more puffs. She still speaks in short phrases, her wheeze is unchanged and her PEF has not improved. SpO2 remains 93 percent. What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the four puffs once more, and call triple zero only if she then worsens", "B": "Move her to a quiet room, coach slow diaphragmatic breathing, and reassess in ten minutes", "C": "Contact her GP for a same-day appointment and let her go home with her reliever", "D": "Call triple zero and keep giving four reliever puffs every four minutes until help arrives"}, "cluster_id": "CHS50", "cluster_text": "Huong Do, 29, barista, attends a community health centre for general reconditioning after a viral illness. Asthma since childhood. She has a low-dose inhaled corticosteroid preventer but admits she often misses doses. Over two days she has had more cough and wheeze, woken twice overnight, and used her blue reliever every three hours with short-lived relief. Today: RR 26, HR 110, SpO2 93 percent on room air, speaking in short phrases, diffuse expiratory wheeze, light accessory neck muscle use. Peak expiratory flow is 60 percent of her personal best. She says she wants to 'push through' the session. Her sister has given her a printout about a breathing technique said to replace preventer medication. She carries her reliever and a spacer.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Call triple zero and keep giving four reliever puffs every four minutes until help arrives): Failure to improve after repeated reliever doses means the exacerbation is not responding, so emergency help is called now while reliever dosing continues. Repeating another round before calling, calming breathing work, and a routine GP visit all delay care for a patient who is already failing first-line treatment."}, {"id": 1160, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Once she has recovered, Ms Do shows you her sister's printout claiming a breathing technique replaces preventer medication, citing a small unblinded study of 20 people. She asks if she can stop her preventer. How should the physiotherapist best use evidence and guidelines?", "options": {"A": "Review the study's results and support a trial reduction of her preventer, monitored by peak flow", "B": "Appraise the study's quality, compare it with current guidelines, and direct medication decisions to her GP", "C": "Appraise the study critically, then adjust her preventer dose yourself if its findings match current guidelines", "D": "Compare it with current guidelines, advise her to continue the preventer, and set breathing techniques aside"}, "cluster_id": "CHS50", "cluster_text": "Huong Do, 29, barista, attends a community health centre for general reconditioning after a viral illness. Asthma since childhood. She has a low-dose inhaled corticosteroid preventer but admits she often misses doses. Over two days she has had more cough and wheeze, woken twice overnight, and used her blue reliever every three hours with short-lived relief. Today: RR 26, HR 110, SpO2 93 percent on room air, speaking in short phrases, diffuse expiratory wheeze, light accessory neck muscle use. Peak expiratory flow is 60 percent of her personal best. She says she wants to 'push through' the session. Her sister has given her a printout about a breathing technique said to replace preventer medication. She carries her reliever and a spacer.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Appraise the study's quality, compare it with current guidelines, and direct medication decisions to her GP): A small unblinded study carries a high risk of bias, so the physiotherapist appraises it against current Australian asthma guidance, recognises breathing retraining as a possible adjunct, and leaves medication changes to the prescriber. Supporting or making dose changes falls outside physiotherapy scope and risks harm from reduced preventer use, and setting breathing work aside ignores its value as an adjunct."}, {"id": 1161, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Sione has a barking cough, hoarse voice and stridor, and air entry is equal with transmitted upper airway noise. The physiotherapist explains in plain language to his parents. Which is the most accurate description of what is causing his noisy breathing?", "options": {"A": "Bronchospasm in the medium airways is causing wheeze and noisy breathing, as in an asthma attack", "B": "A bacterial infection of the epiglottis, above the vocal cords, is partly closing his airway", "C": "Thick secretions are blocking his small lower airways, so the noise is worse when coughing", "D": "Viral swelling of the airway lining below the vocal cords is narrowing his breathing passage"}, "cluster_id": "CHS51", "cluster_text": "Sione Tuilagi, 2 years 4 months, is brought by his parents at 2 am with a barking cough, hoarse voice and noisy breathing that began tonight after two days of runny nose. He is fully immunised with no previous airway problems. Triage: temperature 37.9, HR 150, RR 38, SpO2 96 percent on room air. He is alert, sitting on his father's lap, drinking sips of water, with no drooling. There is audible stridor when crying and mild stridor at rest, mild suprasternal and subcostal recession, a harsh barking cough, and equal air entry with transmitted upper airway noise. The emergency physician has prescribed oral dexamethasone and asked physiotherapy to see him for chest physio because of the cough. His parents are worried and ask what the physiotherapist can do.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Viral swelling of the airway lining below the vocal cords is narrowing his breathing passage): Croup is a viral infection causing swelling below the vocal cords, which produces the barking cough, hoarseness and stridor. Equal air entry with transmitted noise points to the upper airway rather than retained secretions or bronchospasm. Epiglottitis is a different, bacterial illness that typically presents with drooling and a toxic child."}, {"id": 1162, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Sione's parents ask for chest physiotherapy to help him cough up phlegm. He is calm on his father's lap with mild stridor at rest, a dry barking cough and no thick sputum. What is the most appropriate physiotherapy management at this point?", "options": {"A": "Give nebulised saline with gentle nasal suction to clear secretions and ease his noisy breathing", "B": "Give brief gentle percussion and vibration in supported sitting to loosen secretions, keeping the session short", "C": "Offer humidified steam in a closed room with his parents to loosen the cough", "D": "Keep him calm on his father's lap, avoid handling, and monitor his work of breathing"}, "cluster_id": "CHS51", "cluster_text": "Sione Tuilagi, 2 years 4 months, is brought by his parents at 2 am with a barking cough, hoarse voice and noisy breathing that began tonight after two days of runny nose. He is fully immunised with no previous airway problems. Triage: temperature 37.9, HR 150, RR 38, SpO2 96 percent on room air. He is alert, sitting on his father's lap, drinking sips of water, with no drooling. There is audible stridor when crying and mild stridor at rest, mild suprasternal and subcostal recession, a harsh barking cough, and equal air entry with transmitted upper airway noise. The emergency physician has prescribed oral dexamethasone and asked physiotherapy to see him for chest physio because of the cough. His parents are worried and ask what the physiotherapist can do.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Keep him calm on his father's lap, avoid handling, and monitor his work of breathing): Croup involves upper airway swelling with no lower airway secretions to clear, and handling or distress increases turbulent flow and worsens the obstruction. The useful physiotherapy role is keeping him settled and watching for fatigue or deterioration. Nebulised saline with suction, percussion and steam either agitate him or target secretions that are not the problem."}, {"id": 1163, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "While the physiotherapist is still present, Sione becomes agitated, with loud stridor at rest, marked recession, a respiratory rate of 52 and SpO2 91 percent on room air. The nurse is at the other end of the department. What is the most appropriate immediate action?", "options": {"A": "Send his father to fetch the nurse while you stay with him and keep him calm", "B": "Leave briefly to find the nurse in charge and request a medical review of the deterioration", "C": "Activate the emergency call and keep him calm with his parents until help arrives", "D": "Give oxygen by blow-by, then call for help only if saturation stays low"}, "cluster_id": "CHS51", "cluster_text": "Sione Tuilagi, 2 years 4 months, is brought by his parents at 2 am with a barking cough, hoarse voice and noisy breathing that began tonight after two days of runny nose. He is fully immunised with no previous airway problems. Triage: temperature 37.9, HR 150, RR 38, SpO2 96 percent on room air. He is alert, sitting on his father's lap, drinking sips of water, with no drooling. There is audible stridor when crying and mild stridor at rest, mild suprasternal and subcostal recession, a harsh barking cough, and equal air entry with transmitted upper airway noise. The emergency physician has prescribed oral dexamethasone and asked physiotherapy to see him for chest physio because of the cough. His parents are worried and ask what the physiotherapist can do.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Activate the emergency call and keep him calm with his parents until help arrives): Loud stridor at rest, marked recession, tachypnoea and falling saturation indicate severe airway obstruction needing immediate medical attention, so the emergency call is activated while a calm, parent-supported child is kept at ease. Sending someone else or leaving to find the nurse delays and fragments the response, and calling only if saturation stays low waits for further deterioration in a child already in severe distress."}, {"id": 1164, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "After the review Sione is admitted for observation. The physiotherapist must hand over her assessment to the ward nurse before leaving. She observed that his stridor at rest eased after the doctor's treatment but returned when he cried. Which handover content and structure is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Give a verbal summary of his diagnosis and treatment, then add findings to the notes", "B": "Use ISBAR, covering his work of breathing, the change seen and escalation triggers", "C": "Hand over with his parents present, using their observations of his night as the main source", "D": "Give a verbal handover in SOAP format, covering history, measures, assessment and plan for physiotherapy"}, "cluster_id": "CHS51", "cluster_text": "Sione Tuilagi, 2 years 4 months, is brought by his parents at 2 am with a barking cough, hoarse voice and noisy breathing that began tonight after two days of runny nose. He is fully immunised with no previous airway problems. Triage: temperature 37.9, HR 150, RR 38, SpO2 96 percent on room air. He is alert, sitting on his father's lap, drinking sips of water, with no drooling. There is audible stridor when crying and mild stridor at rest, mild suprasternal and subcostal recession, a harsh barking cough, and equal air entry with transmitted upper airway noise. The emergency physician has prescribed oral dexamethasone and asked physiotherapy to see him for chest physio because of the cough. His parents are worried and ask what the physiotherapist can do.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use ISBAR, covering his work of breathing, the change seen and escalation triggers): A structured clinical handover such as ISBAR passes on current status, what has changed and what should prompt a call for help, so the receiving nurse can act safely. A diagnosis-led summary or a SOAP style account covers history and physiotherapy plans but misses the monitoring and escalation triggers. Parents' observations are a useful addition, not a replacement for a professional handover."}, {"id": 1165, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Rohan's parents ask what is happening in his knee. He has a prominent, tender tibial tuberosity, pain with resisted knee extension, and recent rapid growth. Which explanation about the structure affected and the cause of his pain should the physiotherapist give?", "options": {"A": "Repetitive quadriceps traction irritates the growing tibial apophysis at the patellar tendon insertion", "B": "Irritation of the infrapatellar fat pad, which is compressed against the femoral condyles in kneeling", "C": "Inflammation of the patellofemoral joint cartilage from poor patellar tracking during rapid bone lengthening", "D": "Stress reaction in the proximal tibial growth plate from repeated impact loading during cross country running"}, "cluster_id": "CHS52", "cluster_text": "Rohan Singh, 12, plays club football four times a week and runs cross country at school. Over six months he has grown about 7 cm. He reports right anterior knee pain and left heel pain, both worse after training and eased by rest, pain 4/10, with no night pain, swelling, locking or fever. Findings: right tibial tuberosity prominent and tender, pain with resisted knee extension and full kneeling, reduced quadriceps flexibility (heel to buttock gap 12 cm), no effusion, ligaments stable. Left calcaneus: tender on medial and lateral squeeze, pain on single leg heel raise, ankle dorsiflexion 5 degrees with the knee straight. Single leg hopping reproduces both pains. The clinic's allied health assistant (AHA), a Certificate IV graduate who has worked there for two years, is available to supervise gym sessions and the practice owner wants her to run Rohan's programme.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Repetitive quadriceps traction irritates the growing tibial apophysis at the patellar tendon insertion): Osgood-Schlatter disease is a traction apophysitis in which the quadriceps pulls through the patellar tendon on the still developing tibial tuberosity during growth spurts. That explains the local tenderness and the pain on resisted extension and kneeling. Patellofemoral pain is more diffuse and is not centred on the tuberosity."}, {"id": 1166, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Rohan's left heel is tender on medial and lateral calcaneal squeeze, painful on single leg heel raise, and ankle dorsiflexion with the knee straight is limited to 5 degrees. Which initial management is most appropriate for his heel pain today?", "options": {"A": "Rest from running and jumping for three months, then return to full training at once", "B": "Rigid ankle immobilisation in a walking boot for six weeks to unload the calcaneal growth plate", "C": "Activity modification with a heel lift, calf stretching and gradual strengthening within pain limits", "D": "Refer for a corticosteroid injection at the Achilles insertion to settle the apophyseal inflammation quickly"}, "cluster_id": "CHS52", "cluster_text": "Rohan Singh, 12, plays club football four times a week and runs cross country at school. Over six months he has grown about 7 cm. He reports right anterior knee pain and left heel pain, both worse after training and eased by rest, pain 4/10, with no night pain, swelling, locking or fever. Findings: right tibial tuberosity prominent and tender, pain with resisted knee extension and full kneeling, reduced quadriceps flexibility (heel to buttock gap 12 cm), no effusion, ligaments stable. Left calcaneus: tender on medial and lateral squeeze, pain on single leg heel raise, ankle dorsiflexion 5 degrees with the knee straight. Single leg hopping reproduces both pains. The clinic's allied health assistant (AHA), a Certificate IV graduate who has worked there for two years, is available to supervise gym sessions and the practice owner wants her to run Rohan's programme.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Activity modification with a heel lift, calf stretching and gradual strengthening within pain limits): Sever disease is a self-limiting traction irritation of the calcaneal apophysis, and first line care is load management with a heel lift, calf flexibility and graded strengthening within symptoms. Prolonged rest or immobilisation causes deconditioning and is rarely needed. An injection has no place in a growth plate overuse problem."}, {"id": 1167, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Rohan has a tournament in ten days. His pain peaks at 4/10 after training, settles within an hour, and he has no rest or night pain, no swelling and a normal gait. He and his father ask whether he can play. What is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Withdraw from the tournament and stop running until the tuberosity is pain free on palpation", "B": "Play at the usual volume, as this self-limiting condition is not harmed by activity", "C": "Play with an infrapatellar strap and take pain relief beforehand so pain does not limit him", "D": "Play with reduced load and agreed pain limits, with review if pain lingers or gait changes"}, "cluster_id": "CHS52", "cluster_text": "Rohan Singh, 12, plays club football four times a week and runs cross country at school. Over six months he has grown about 7 cm. He reports right anterior knee pain and left heel pain, both worse after training and eased by rest, pain 4/10, with no night pain, swelling, locking or fever. Findings: right tibial tuberosity prominent and tender, pain with resisted knee extension and full kneeling, reduced quadriceps flexibility (heel to buttock gap 12 cm), no effusion, ligaments stable. Left calcaneus: tender on medial and lateral squeeze, pain on single leg heel raise, ankle dorsiflexion 5 degrees with the knee straight. Single leg hopping reproduces both pains. The clinic's allied health assistant (AHA), a Certificate IV graduate who has worked there for two years, is available to supervise gym sessions and the practice owner wants her to run Rohan's programme.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Play with reduced load and agreed pain limits, with review if pain lingers or gait changes): Osgood-Schlatter and Sever disease are self-limiting, and sport can usually continue when symptoms are tolerable, settle promptly and do not alter gait, provided load is adjusted and monitored. Full withdrawal is not needed at this level of symptoms, whereas unchanged loading ignores the vulnerability of the growth spurt. Analgesia before play masks the pain signal that guides load."}, {"id": 1168, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "The clinic wants the AHA, who has two years' experience, to run Rohan's gym stretching and strengthening programme. Rohan's heel pain has not yet settled and his programme is still being progressed. Which approach to delegation best meets the physiotherapist's professional responsibilities?", "options": {"A": "Delegate set exercises with written limits and stop rules, but reassess and progress him personally", "B": "Retain supervision personally, as an AHA should not work with a child who has active pain", "C": "Delegate the whole programme including progression to the AHA, given her experience, with a monthly review", "D": "Delegate once the AHA observes one session, with the physiotherapist reachable by phone if concerns arise"}, "cluster_id": "CHS52", "cluster_text": "Rohan Singh, 12, plays club football four times a week and runs cross country at school. Over six months he has grown about 7 cm. He reports right anterior knee pain and left heel pain, both worse after training and eased by rest, pain 4/10, with no night pain, swelling, locking or fever. Findings: right tibial tuberosity prominent and tender, pain with resisted knee extension and full kneeling, reduced quadriceps flexibility (heel to buttock gap 12 cm), no effusion, ligaments stable. Left calcaneus: tender on medial and lateral squeeze, pain on single leg heel raise, ankle dorsiflexion 5 degrees with the knee straight. Single leg hopping reproduces both pains. The clinic's allied health assistant (AHA), a Certificate IV graduate who has worked there for two years, is available to supervise gym sessions and the practice owner wants her to run Rohan's programme.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Delegate set exercises with written limits and stop rules, but reassess and progress him personally): Delegation depends on the task, the complexity of the patient and the assistant's competence, and the physiotherapist remains accountable for assessment, programme design and progression. Defined exercises with clear parameters and stop rules can be delegated while the physiotherapist reassesses regularly. Handing over progression, or refusing to delegate anything, does not fit accepted delegation practice."}, {"id": 1169, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Rekha has weak ankle dorsiflexion and eversion, tight calves and clawed toes, and she loves swimming and wants to play netball. The physiotherapist is choosing an exercise programme for her to follow over the next term. Which approach is most appropriate for her?", "options": {"A": "Aerobic exercise only, avoiding resistance work because it is thought to speed nerve degeneration over time", "B": "Stretching alone, because strengthening cannot improve function in a progressive peripheral neuropathy such as hers", "C": "Submaximal strengthening with rest between sets, combined with regular calf and plantar fascia stretching", "D": "High load resistance training to fatigue three times weekly to maximise gains in weakened muscles"}, "cluster_id": "CHS53", "cluster_text": "Rekha Gupta, 9, has genetically confirmed Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease type 1A and attends an outpatient clinic with her father. She trips often, has had three ankle sprains this year, and tires when walking at school. Examination shows bilateral pes cavus, clawed toes and wasting of the intrinsic foot muscles. Ankle dorsiflexion and eversion are 3/5, hip and knee strength is 5/5, ankle reflexes are absent, and light touch is reduced over both feet. She walks with a high stepping gait and mild toe drag. She loves swimming and wants to play netball. The physiotherapist plans a stretching and strengthening programme and is considering asking an allied health assistant to run it in a weekly group session.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Submaximal strengthening with rest between sets, combined with regular calf and plantar fascia stretching): Moderate, submaximal strengthening is considered safe and helpful in slowly progressive neuropathy, while stretching addresses tightening in the calves and feet. Overworking weak muscles risks fatigue and overuse injury. Avoiding resistance work or relying on stretching alone leaves her weakness untreated."}, {"id": 1170, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Rekha has had three ankle sprains this year, with pes cavus, weak ankle evertors and dorsiflexors, and reduced foot sensation. Her father asks why the sprains keep happening even though she is careful at school and in games. Which explanation is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Weak evertors and dorsiflexors, a high arched foot and reduced sensation make her ankles unstable", "B": "Tight calf muscles and a short Achilles tendon pull the heel inwards, rolling her ankles over", "C": "Poor footwear and fatigue from school sport overload the ankles, which is common in active children", "D": "Foot drop from weak dorsiflexors causes toe catch and tripping, which explains the sprains"}, "cluster_id": "CHS53", "cluster_text": "Rekha Gupta, 9, has genetically confirmed Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease type 1A and attends an outpatient clinic with her father. She trips often, has had three ankle sprains this year, and tires when walking at school. Examination shows bilateral pes cavus, clawed toes and wasting of the intrinsic foot muscles. Ankle dorsiflexion and eversion are 3/5, hip and knee strength is 5/5, ankle reflexes are absent, and light touch is reduced over both feet. She walks with a high stepping gait and mild toe drag. She loves swimming and wants to play netball. The physiotherapist plans a stretching and strengthening programme and is considering asking an allied health assistant to run it in a weekly group session.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Weak evertors and dorsiflexors, a high arched foot and reduced sensation make her ankles unstable): Distal weakness of the evertors and dorsiflexors, a cavus foot and impaired sensation combine to reduce ankle stability in this condition. Foot drop explains tripping but not the sprains, which also reflect weak eversion and poor position sense. Her calf tone is not described as tight and there is no spasticity because the lesion is peripheral, and footwear or fatigue does not account for her examination findings."}, {"id": 1171, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Rekha trips on stairs and drags her toes when tired, and her sprains continue despite stretching. The physiotherapist wants to improve toe clearance and ankle stability for school and netball. What is the most appropriate next step in her management?", "options": {"A": "Provide night splints to hold the ankles in neutral and review again in three months", "B": "Refer to an orthotist to assess for a lightweight ankle foot orthosis and supportive footwear", "C": "Intensify strengthening and balance training first, and consider an orthosis only if falls increase", "D": "Use sports strapping tape on both ankles for school and netball instead of an orthosis"}, "cluster_id": "CHS53", "cluster_text": "Rekha Gupta, 9, has genetically confirmed Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease type 1A and attends an outpatient clinic with her father. She trips often, has had three ankle sprains this year, and tires when walking at school. Examination shows bilateral pes cavus, clawed toes and wasting of the intrinsic foot muscles. Ankle dorsiflexion and eversion are 3/5, hip and knee strength is 5/5, ankle reflexes are absent, and light touch is reduced over both feet. She walks with a high stepping gait and mild toe drag. She loves swimming and wants to play netball. The physiotherapist plans a stretching and strengthening programme and is considering asking an allied health assistant to run it in a weekly group session.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Refer to an orthotist to assess for a lightweight ankle foot orthosis and supportive footwear): A lightweight orthosis with suitable footwear improves toe clearance and ankle stability without limiting activity, and an orthotist is the specialist who assesses and fits it. Strengthening matters, but delaying support leaves her tripping and spraining in the meantime. Taping is a short-term aid that does not control foot drop, and night splints address stretch rather than daytime gait."}, {"id": 1172, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "The physiotherapist wants an allied health assistant to run Rekha's stretching and strengthening in a weekly group session at the clinic. The assistant is experienced with children and keen to help. Which approach to delegation is most appropriate for this plan?", "options": {"A": "Delegate only after the assistant completes a first aid certificate, then review notes monthly", "B": "Demonstrate the programme, confirm the assistant's competence, and set clear review and escalation points", "C": "Keep every exercise personally and use the assistant only for room set up and tidying", "D": "Delegate the programme including progression decisions, since the assistant has years of experience with children"}, "cluster_id": "CHS53", "cluster_text": "Rekha Gupta, 9, has genetically confirmed Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease type 1A and attends an outpatient clinic with her father. She trips often, has had three ankle sprains this year, and tires when walking at school. Examination shows bilateral pes cavus, clawed toes and wasting of the intrinsic foot muscles. Ankle dorsiflexion and eversion are 3/5, hip and knee strength is 5/5, ankle reflexes are absent, and light touch is reduced over both feet. She walks with a high stepping gait and mild toe drag. She loves swimming and wants to play netball. The physiotherapist plans a stretching and strengthening programme and is considering asking an allied health assistant to run it in a weekly group session.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Demonstrate the programme, confirm the assistant's competence, and set clear review and escalation points): The physiotherapist remains accountable for the plan, so delegation requires clear instructions, confirmed competence and defined supervision and escalation points. Progression decisions are clinical judgements that stay with the physiotherapist. Delegating to children is permitted when these conditions are met, so refusing outright is not required."}, {"id": 1173, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Greco asks why he must hug a pillow when coughing and avoid pushing up through his arms from the bed. What is the best explanation of the main purpose of these sternal precautions at this early stage of healing?", "options": {"A": "They discourage deep breaths so the sternotomy incision is not stretched during the first week", "B": "They prevent the new valve from shifting out of position while the surrounding tissue settles", "C": "They keep blood pressure low enough to protect the suture line on the new valve", "D": "They limit stress across the divided sternum so the bone edges can knit together"}, "cluster_id": "CHS54", "cluster_text": "Marco Greco, 81, retired builder, day 3 after bioprosthetic aortic valve replacement through a median sternotomy for severe aortic stenosis. History: hypertension, type 2 diabetes, mild chronic kidney disease. Post-operative atrial fibrillation settled yesterday with medication. Nursing notes record brief evening confusion on night 2. Today he is alert and oriented to person, place and time. HR 78 regular, BP 118/68, RR 18, SpO2 95 percent on room air, pain 3/10 at the sternum when coughing. Chest sounds clear after huffing. Sternum stable, wound dry. He sits unsupported at the bedside but says he is tired and wants to stay in bed. His daughter, Maria, asks you to walk him regardless. The team plans discharge home in about a week.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (They limit stress across the divided sternum so the bone edges can knit together): Healing of the sternal bone edges depends on keeping shearing and distracting forces low, which is why splinting and arm loading are limited. The valve is secured by sutures and tissue ingrowth, not by these precautions, so protecting it is not their purpose. Deep breathing is still encouraged with splinting, so discouraging it would worsen lung complications."}, {"id": 1174, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Greco is clinically stable with clear chest sounds and a regular pulse. He wants to stay in bed because he feels tired, but he can sit unsupported. What is the most appropriate mobility plan for this session, given his early post-operative status?", "options": {"A": "Walk him the length of the ward corridor twice to build endurance before his planned discharge", "B": "Assist a short walk with monitoring of heart rate, symptoms and saturation, then rest", "C": "Practise stair climbing with a handrail first, because he will need stairs when he returns home", "D": "Keep him sitting out of bed in a chair only until his tiredness fully resolves"}, "cluster_id": "CHS54", "cluster_text": "Marco Greco, 81, retired builder, day 3 after bioprosthetic aortic valve replacement through a median sternotomy for severe aortic stenosis. History: hypertension, type 2 diabetes, mild chronic kidney disease. Post-operative atrial fibrillation settled yesterday with medication. Nursing notes record brief evening confusion on night 2. Today he is alert and oriented to person, place and time. HR 78 regular, BP 118/68, RR 18, SpO2 95 percent on room air, pain 3/10 at the sternum when coughing. Chest sounds clear after huffing. Sternum stable, wound dry. He sits unsupported at the bedside but says he is tired and wants to stay in bed. His daughter, Maria, asks you to walk him regardless. The team plans discharge home in about a week.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Assist a short walk with monitoring of heart rate, symptoms and saturation, then rest): Early, graded and monitored walking is the standard after cardiac surgery and reduces pulmonary and deconditioning complications when he is stable. Chair sitting alone misses the benefit of upright walking. Long corridor walks or stairs exceed what is appropriate on day 3 and are progressed later as tolerance and monitoring allow."}, {"id": 1175, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Greco declines to walk again this afternoon. His daughter Maria says he is simply stubborn and asks you to proceed. He is alert and repeats back the risks of staying in bed in his own words. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Respect his informed refusal, explore his reasons, document the discussion and offer to try again later", "B": "Mobilise him with encouragement and explain afterwards that walking is part of his recovery plan", "C": "Proceed with Maria's consent as next of kin because she knows his usual wishes best", "D": "Ask the medical team to formally assess his capacity first, because declining treatment suggests impaired judgement"}, "cluster_id": "CHS54", "cluster_text": "Marco Greco, 81, retired builder, day 3 after bioprosthetic aortic valve replacement through a median sternotomy for severe aortic stenosis. History: hypertension, type 2 diabetes, mild chronic kidney disease. Post-operative atrial fibrillation settled yesterday with medication. Nursing notes record brief evening confusion on night 2. Today he is alert and oriented to person, place and time. HR 78 regular, BP 118/68, RR 18, SpO2 95 percent on room air, pain 3/10 at the sternum when coughing. Chest sounds clear after huffing. Sternum stable, wound dry. He sits unsupported at the bedside but says he is tired and wants to stay in bed. His daughter, Maria, asks you to walk him regardless. The team plans discharge home in about a week.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Respect his informed refusal, explore his reasons, document the discussion and offer to try again later): An adult who understands the information and can retell the risks has capacity to make the decision, and a family member cannot consent on his behalf. Declining treatment alone is not evidence of impaired capacity, so a formal assessment is not triggered. Exploring his reasons, such as fatigue, may let you renegotiate timing."}, {"id": 1176, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "During a corridor walk the next day Mr Greco reports palpitations and light-headedness. His pulse is now irregular at about 128 beats per minute and BP is 96/58 mmHg, compared with 118/68 yesterday morning. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Stop walking, sit him down and recheck his pulse again in ten minutes before reporting", "B": "Stop, sit him down with saturation monitored, and alert the nurse and medical team", "C": "Lie him flat with legs raised and record observations, reporting at the end of the session", "D": "Sit him down, coach slow breathing, and alert the nurse only if symptoms worsen"}, "cluster_id": "CHS54", "cluster_text": "Marco Greco, 81, retired builder, day 3 after bioprosthetic aortic valve replacement through a median sternotomy for severe aortic stenosis. History: hypertension, type 2 diabetes, mild chronic kidney disease. Post-operative atrial fibrillation settled yesterday with medication. Nursing notes record brief evening confusion on night 2. Today he is alert and oriented to person, place and time. HR 78 regular, BP 118/68, RR 18, SpO2 95 percent on room air, pain 3/10 at the sternum when coughing. Chest sounds clear after huffing. Sternum stable, wound dry. He sits unsupported at the bedside but says he is tired and wants to stay in bed. His daughter, Maria, asks you to walk him regardless. The team plans discharge home in about a week.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stop, sit him down with saturation monitored, and alert the nurse and medical team): A fast irregular pulse with falling pressure and symptoms suggests recurrent atrial fibrillation with haemodynamic effect, so exercise stops, he is seated with monitoring, and the team is alerted immediately. Waiting ten minutes, reporting only at the end of the session, or reporting only if he worsens delays review of a possibly unstable rhythm, and lying flat without prompt escalation does not address the cause."}, {"id": 1177, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Arjun brings his hands to midline inconsistently, lifts his head only briefly in prone, shows marked head lag on pull to sit, and has low symmetrical tone with normal visual tracking. Which interpretation best explains this combination of findings at 7 months of age?", "options": {"A": "Normal variation in development for his age, needing reassurance and a review in some months", "B": "Generalised low tone with delayed antigravity control, worsened by limited exposure to prone play", "C": "Early cerebral palsy with central hypotonia, needing urgent neurology referral before starting a handling programme", "D": "A primary neuromuscular disorder, since marked head lag and low tone suggest peripheral weakness"}, "cluster_id": "CHS55", "cluster_text": "Arjun Singh is a 7 month old boy, born at term, referred by the child health nurse to a community health physiotherapy clinic because he is not rolling and holds his head poorly. His parents attend. He feeds and sleeps well, smiles and vocalises. His parents rarely place him on his tummy because he cries and they worry he is distressed. Findings: in supine he brings his hands to midline only occasionally and does not reach for toys. In prone he lifts his head briefly to about 45 degrees and does not bear weight through his forearms. Pull to sit shows marked head lag. Muscle tone is low and symmetrical, reflexes are present, and he tracks a face and a toy well. The service manager proposes that an allied health assistant provide his weekly home visits.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Generalised low tone with delayed antigravity control, worsened by limited exposure to prone play): Symmetrical low tone with head lag and limited prone extension, in a baby rarely placed on his tummy, points to generalised hypotonia with delayed antigravity control that prone play can improve. His reflexes are present and tracking is good, which argues against a primary neuromuscular disorder, and cerebral palsy cannot be diagnosed from these findings alone. Marked head lag at this age is not normal variation."}, {"id": 1178, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Arjun's parents say he cries within seconds of being placed on his tummy and ask what they should do about tummy time. They want a simple plan they can use at home every day. What advice is most appropriate to give them?", "options": {"A": "Wait until he rolls, using propped sitting with cushions meanwhile to strengthen his trunk muscles", "B": "Hold him in prone for longer blocks until the crying settles, so he adapts", "C": "Use a bouncer seat for daily upright practice, building neck and trunk strength with less distress", "D": "Offer short, frequent periods of supervised prone play, such as on a parent's chest"}, "cluster_id": "CHS55", "cluster_text": "Arjun Singh is a 7 month old boy, born at term, referred by the child health nurse to a community health physiotherapy clinic because he is not rolling and holds his head poorly. His parents attend. He feeds and sleeps well, smiles and vocalises. His parents rarely place him on his tummy because he cries and they worry he is distressed. Findings: in supine he brings his hands to midline only occasionally and does not reach for toys. In prone he lifts his head briefly to about 45 degrees and does not bear weight through his forearms. Pull to sit shows marked head lag. Muscle tone is low and symmetrical, reflexes are present, and he tracks a face and a toy well. The service manager proposes that an allied health assistant provide his weekly home visits.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Offer short, frequent periods of supervised prone play, such as on a parent's chest): Graded, short and frequent supervised prone exposure, starting in a comfortable position like on a parent's chest, builds tolerance and the extensor strength he lacks. Holding him in prone through distress is unpleasant and may increase his aversion. Propped sitting and bouncer seats do not train antigravity extension in the way prone play does."}, {"id": 1179, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Four weeks later Arjun is eight months old and his parents are keen to keep going with the home programme. They phone with new concerns. Which new report would most clearly warrant urgent medical review instead of continuing the current physiotherapy plan?", "options": {"A": "He has become more unsettled and cries sooner in tummy time, refusing it on most days", "B": "He prefers looking to the right and a flat patch is developing at the back", "C": "He is rolling later than expected even though head lifting in prone is improving each week", "D": "He has stopped bringing his hands together and seems less alert and responsive than before"}, "cluster_id": "CHS55", "cluster_text": "Arjun Singh is a 7 month old boy, born at term, referred by the child health nurse to a community health physiotherapy clinic because he is not rolling and holds his head poorly. His parents attend. He feeds and sleeps well, smiles and vocalises. His parents rarely place him on his tummy because he cries and they worry he is distressed. Findings: in supine he brings his hands to midline only occasionally and does not reach for toys. In prone he lifts his head briefly to about 45 degrees and does not bear weight through his forearms. Pull to sit shows marked head lag. Muscle tone is low and symmetrical, reflexes are present, and he tracks a face and a toy well. The service manager proposes that an allied health assistant provide his weekly home visits.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (He has stopped bringing his hands together and seems less alert and responsive than before): Loss of previously acquired skills together with reduced alertness is a red flag for an acute or progressive condition and needs urgent medical review. Increasing unsettled behaviour in prone, a head turning preference with a flat patch, and slower rolling despite weekly gains in head lifting are common and can be managed within the current physiotherapy plan."}, {"id": 1180, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "The service manager asks that an allied health assistant deliver Arjun's weekly home visits and decide how to progress his activities as he improves. The physiotherapist has completed the assessment and plan. What is the most appropriate response to this request?", "options": {"A": "Decline delegation, as developmental programmes for infants fall outside what an assistant can safely deliver", "B": "Delegate defined activities with written instructions and review, keeping reassessment and progression with the physiotherapist", "C": "Delegate the whole programme including progression, since the assistant is experienced and parents will report concerns", "D": "Delegate after the assistant observes one session, relying on parent feedback to monitor his response"}, "cluster_id": "CHS55", "cluster_text": "Arjun Singh is a 7 month old boy, born at term, referred by the child health nurse to a community health physiotherapy clinic because he is not rolling and holds his head poorly. His parents attend. He feeds and sleeps well, smiles and vocalises. His parents rarely place him on his tummy because he cries and they worry he is distressed. Findings: in supine he brings his hands to midline only occasionally and does not reach for toys. In prone he lifts his head briefly to about 45 degrees and does not bear weight through his forearms. Pull to sit shows marked head lag. Muscle tone is low and symmetrical, reflexes are present, and he tracks a face and a toy well. The service manager proposes that an allied health assistant provide his weekly home visits.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Delegate defined activities with written instructions and review, keeping reassessment and progression with the physiotherapist): A physiotherapist remains accountable for assessment, clinical reasoning and changes to the plan, and can delegate clearly defined tasks with instruction and supervision. Handing over progression removes that oversight. Refusing delegation altogether is unnecessary, and relying on parent feedback alone is not adequate supervision."}, {"id": 1181, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Amara's mother asks why she limps. On observation, Amara leans her trunk over the right hip during right stance, and right hip abduction is limited compared with the left. Which interpretation best explains this gait pattern in a child with treated developmental dysplasia of the hip?", "options": {"A": "A true femoral length difference, producing a trunk lean to offset the shorter limb on stance", "B": "Residual left clubfoot stiffness shortens the left step, so she leans over the right hip", "C": "An antalgic gait from right hip pain, shifting her trunk over the painful side during stance", "D": "Inefficient right hip abductors from residual dysplasia, giving a compensated Trendelenburg gait pattern"}, "cluster_id": "CHS56", "cluster_text": "Amara Okonkwo, 20 months, is seen at a community child health centre. Born at term, breech presentation. Left idiopathic talipes equinovarus was treated with Ponseti casting and heel cord tenotomy at 3 weeks, and she now wears boots and bar at night and sleep times only. Right hip dysplasia was found at 6 weeks, treated in a Pavlik harness, and is under ultrasound and X-ray surveillance. She walked independently at 17 months. Her mother reports she tires on walks and the left foot turns in. Findings: left ankle dorsiflexion 5 degrees with the knee straight, right 15 degrees; right hip abduction 40 degrees, left 55 degrees; mild limp with trunk lean towards the right in right stance; no pain behaviours; skin intact. The clinic's wheeled push trolley has no wheel locks and staff have noticed it rolling on the vinyl floor.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Inefficient right hip abductors from residual dysplasia, giving a compensated Trendelenburg gait pattern): A trunk lean over the stance hip with reduced abduction fits a compensated Trendelenburg gait, because residual dysplasia places the abductors at a mechanical disadvantage. A limb length difference would not explain the reduced abduction, clubfoot stiffness alters foot position and push off rather than creating a lean over the hip, and an antalgic gait is not supported when she shows no pain behaviours."}, {"id": 1182, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Amara's left ankle dorsiflexion is 5 degrees with the knee straight, and she wears boots and bar only at night. Her mother asks what to do during the day to maintain the corrected foot position. What is the most appropriate advice?", "options": {"A": "Add rigid ankle foot orthoses for daytime wear to hold the foot position while she walks", "B": "Stop the boots and bar now she walks and rely on stretching to hold correction", "C": "Keep to the bracing schedule and add gentle stretching and play that encourages dorsiflexion", "D": "Teach firm passive overpressure stretches several times daily by her mother to keep the foot corrected"}, "cluster_id": "CHS56", "cluster_text": "Amara Okonkwo, 20 months, is seen at a community child health centre. Born at term, breech presentation. Left idiopathic talipes equinovarus was treated with Ponseti casting and heel cord tenotomy at 3 weeks, and she now wears boots and bar at night and sleep times only. Right hip dysplasia was found at 6 weeks, treated in a Pavlik harness, and is under ultrasound and X-ray surveillance. She walked independently at 17 months. Her mother reports she tires on walks and the left foot turns in. Findings: left ankle dorsiflexion 5 degrees with the knee straight, right 15 degrees; right hip abduction 40 degrees, left 55 degrees; mild limp with trunk lean towards the right in right stance; no pain behaviours; skin intact. The clinic's wheeled push trolley has no wheel locks and staff have noticed it rolling on the vinyl floor.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Keep to the bracing schedule and add gentle stretching and play that encourages dorsiflexion): Adherence to the abduction brace is the strongest protection against relapse in the first years after Ponseti treatment, so it continues as prescribed, with gentle stretching built into play. Stopping early raises relapse risk, and forceful stretching risks pain, skin damage and a stiff foot. Daytime rigid orthoses are not indicated without a specialist decision."}, {"id": 1183, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "At a later visit Amara's mother reports a red area over the left heel after nights in the boots and bar, which is not blanching quickly. The skin is unbroken and warm. She asks whether to keep using the brace. What is the best physiotherapy response?", "options": {"A": "Continue nightly use as prescribed and recheck the heel at the next appointment in a month", "B": "Contact the orthotist and treating team promptly for a skin review and refit of the brace", "C": "Stop the brace until the redness settles, then restart it at the same fitting as before", "D": "Add a thick sock under the boot to cushion the heel and keep the nightly schedule"}, "cluster_id": "CHS56", "cluster_text": "Amara Okonkwo, 20 months, is seen at a community child health centre. Born at term, breech presentation. Left idiopathic talipes equinovarus was treated with Ponseti casting and heel cord tenotomy at 3 weeks, and she now wears boots and bar at night and sleep times only. Right hip dysplasia was found at 6 weeks, treated in a Pavlik harness, and is under ultrasound and X-ray surveillance. She walked independently at 17 months. Her mother reports she tires on walks and the left foot turns in. Findings: left ankle dorsiflexion 5 degrees with the knee straight, right 15 degrees; right hip abduction 40 degrees, left 55 degrees; mild limp with trunk lean towards the right in right stance; no pain behaviours; skin intact. The clinic's wheeled push trolley has no wheel locks and staff have noticed it rolling on the vinyl floor.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Contact the orthotist and treating team promptly for a skin review and refit of the brace): Persistent redness that does not blanch is an early sign of pressure injury, and the brace fit needs review before skin breaks down. Contacting the orthotist and treating team keeps the child on the relapse prevention plan while the fit is corrected. Padding under the boot changes the fit and can raise pressure, and restarting an unchanged brace repeats the problem."}, {"id": 1184, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "During play, Amara pulls up on the unlocked wheeled trolley, which rolls away. She falls and bumps her forehead, cries briefly, then settles. She is alert, there is a small bruise, and no vomiting. After attending to her, what should the physiotherapist do?", "options": {"A": "Record the fall in the clinical notes only, as there was no injury needing medical treatment", "B": "Notify AHPRA of the event because a child was harmed while in the physiotherapist's care today", "C": "Tell her mother and continue the session, mentioning the fall verbally to the team leader later", "D": "Tell her mother, give head injury advice, lodge an incident report and take the trolley out"}, "cluster_id": "CHS56", "cluster_text": "Amara Okonkwo, 20 months, is seen at a community child health centre. Born at term, breech presentation. Left idiopathic talipes equinovarus was treated with Ponseti casting and heel cord tenotomy at 3 weeks, and she now wears boots and bar at night and sleep times only. Right hip dysplasia was found at 6 weeks, treated in a Pavlik harness, and is under ultrasound and X-ray surveillance. She walked independently at 17 months. Her mother reports she tires on walks and the left foot turns in. Findings: left ankle dorsiflexion 5 degrees with the knee straight, right 15 degrees; right hip abduction 40 degrees, left 55 degrees; mild limp with trunk lean towards the right in right stance; no pain behaviours; skin intact. The clinic's wheeled push trolley has no wheel locks and staff have noticed it rolling on the vinyl floor.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Tell her mother, give head injury advice, lodge an incident report and take the trolley out): Even an event with minor harm needs open disclosure to the carer, monitoring advice and an incident report under organisational policy, so the hazard can be investigated and removed before it injures another child. A note in the clinical file alone does not start that process. AHPRA notifications concern a practitioner's conduct or health, not routine workplace incidents."}, {"id": 1185, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Antoniou choked on thin fluids while sitting upright at lunch three days ago. Chest examination now shows coarse crackles over the right lower zone. Which lung region is aspirated material most likely to have reached in this position, and why?", "options": {"A": "Apical segment of the right upper lobe, because material settles into the highest zone when upright", "B": "Lingula of the left upper lobe, because the left main bronchus is wider and more vertical", "C": "Basal segments of the right lower lobe, as the right main bronchus is wider and straighter", "D": "Posterior segment of the right upper lobe, which receives material when a person is supine"}, "cluster_id": "CHS57", "cluster_text": "Maria Antoniou, 86, permanent resident of a residential aged care home. History: Parkinson disease, mild dementia, osteoporosis. She choked on thin tea at lunch three days ago while sitting upright. Since then: fever, wet cough and drowsiness in the afternoons. The GP diagnosed aspiration pneumonia and is treating her in the home with oral antibiotics, in line with her advance care plan, which prefers no hospital transfer. Observations today: temperature 37.9 degrees, HR 96, RR 24, BP 118/70, SpO2 92 percent on room air. She has a wet voice, a weak voluntary cough, coarse crackles over the right lower zone and sits slumped in her chair. She follows only one-step commands. Her daughter holds enduring power of attorney and visits daily.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Basal segments of the right lower lobe, as the right main bronchus is wider and straighter): In an upright person, aspirated material follows gravity and the wider, more vertical right main bronchus into the basal segments of the right lower lobe, which matches her examination. The upper lobe segments are more typical targets when a person is lying down, and the left side is less often affected because of the bronchial anatomy."}, {"id": 1186, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Antoniou has coarse crackles over the right lower zone, a weak cough and cannot follow multi-step instructions. Her SpO2 is 92 percent on room air and she has eaten nothing for two hours. Which treatment approach is most appropriate for her today?", "options": {"A": "Tip the bed head-down for fifteen minutes and apply percussion over the right lower zone", "B": "Lay her on her right side to ventilate the affected area, with assisted coughs and monitoring", "C": "Position her in supported left side-lying, cue simple huffs, assist coughing, and monitor SpO2 throughout", "D": "Sit her upright and teach active cycle of breathing technique with deep thoracic expansion exercises"}, "cluster_id": "CHS57", "cluster_text": "Maria Antoniou, 86, permanent resident of a residential aged care home. History: Parkinson disease, mild dementia, osteoporosis. She choked on thin tea at lunch three days ago while sitting upright. Since then: fever, wet cough and drowsiness in the afternoons. The GP diagnosed aspiration pneumonia and is treating her in the home with oral antibiotics, in line with her advance care plan, which prefers no hospital transfer. Observations today: temperature 37.9 degrees, HR 96, RR 24, BP 118/70, SpO2 92 percent on room air. She has a wet voice, a weak voluntary cough, coarse crackles over the right lower zone and sits slumped in her chair. She follows only one-step commands. Her daughter holds enduring power of attorney and visits daily.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Position her in supported left side-lying, cue simple huffs, assist coughing, and monitor SpO2 throughout): Side-lying with the affected right lung uppermost uses gravity gently, and one-step cues with an assisted cough suit her cognition and weak cough while SpO2 is watched. Lying on the affected side reduces ventilation to that area, head-down tipping raises aspiration risk in a frail older person, and a multi-step breathing technique exceeds her ability to follow."}, {"id": 1187, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "The next day Mrs Antoniou is more drowsy with RR 30, and SpO2 88 percent on room air. Her advance care plan, agreed with her family, states treatment within the facility and no hospital transfer. The nurse asks what physiotherapy can do. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Report the deterioration to the registered nurse and GP now, and offer gentle positioning for comfort", "B": "Arrange urgent ambulance transfer to the emergency department, because hypoxia overrides the advance care plan", "C": "Continue the planned airway clearance and review her observations again at the next scheduled session", "D": "Increase treatment to three sessions daily with assisted coughing, aiming to return her saturation to baseline"}, "cluster_id": "CHS57", "cluster_text": "Maria Antoniou, 86, permanent resident of a residential aged care home. History: Parkinson disease, mild dementia, osteoporosis. She choked on thin tea at lunch three days ago while sitting upright. Since then: fever, wet cough and drowsiness in the afternoons. The GP diagnosed aspiration pneumonia and is treating her in the home with oral antibiotics, in line with her advance care plan, which prefers no hospital transfer. Observations today: temperature 37.9 degrees, HR 96, RR 24, BP 118/70, SpO2 92 percent on room air. She has a wet voice, a weak voluntary cough, coarse crackles over the right lower zone and sits slumped in her chair. She follows only one-step commands. Her daughter holds enduring power of attorney and visits daily.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report the deterioration to the registered nurse and GP now, and offer gentle positioning for comfort): Her advance care plan limits transfer but does not limit communication: a clear deterioration must reach the nurse and GP promptly so the medical plan and comfort measures can be revisited. Physiotherapy then supports comfort. Overriding the plan disregards her documented wishes, and intensifying or simply continuing treatment ignores that she has become more unwell."}, {"id": 1188, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Antoniou recovers over two weeks. Her daughter asks whether Medicare or the NDIS will now pay for ongoing weekly physiotherapy for her 86 year old mother's Parkinson disease and chest problems. How should the physiotherapist respond to this funding question?", "options": {"A": "Confirm that a GP referral will secure Medicare rebated sessions, so the facility will not contribute", "B": "Explain that resident funding differs, then direct her to the facility manager or My Aged Care", "C": "Offer private sessions at a reduced fee, keeping the arrangement separate from the facility's care planning", "D": "Advise her to apply to the NDIS, since Parkinson disease with dysphagia is a qualifying disability"}, "cluster_id": "CHS57", "cluster_text": "Maria Antoniou, 86, permanent resident of a residential aged care home. History: Parkinson disease, mild dementia, osteoporosis. She choked on thin tea at lunch three days ago while sitting upright. Since then: fever, wet cough and drowsiness in the afternoons. The GP diagnosed aspiration pneumonia and is treating her in the home with oral antibiotics, in line with her advance care plan, which prefers no hospital transfer. Observations today: temperature 37.9 degrees, HR 96, RR 24, BP 118/70, SpO2 92 percent on room air. She has a wet voice, a weak voluntary cough, coarse crackles over the right lower zone and sits slumped in her chair. She follows only one-step commands. Her daughter holds enduring power of attorney and visits daily.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Explain that resident funding differs, then direct her to the facility manager or My Aged Care): Funding for physiotherapy for permanent residents depends on the facility's own arrangements, so the honest answer is to say it varies and send the family to the people who can confirm entitlements. Promising Medicare rebates or the NDIS is unreliable, because NDIS access generally applies to people who first enter the scheme before age 65. A private side arrangement also bypasses the facility's care planning."}, {"id": 1189, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Murray's 6 minute walk test covered 280 metres, with SpO2 falling from 94 to 84 percent, peak HR 118 and Borg breathlessness 6. He is not on home oxygen. Which interpretation is most appropriate for planning his ongoing physiotherapy care?", "options": {"A": "A mild fall within expected limits for his age, so exercise can continue with routine monitoring", "B": "Marked exertional desaturation, typical of fibrotic lung disease, needing medical review of oxygen and monitored exercise", "C": "Cardiac limitation rather than lung disease, so exercise waits until an echocardiogram is done", "D": "Expected desaturation in fibrotic disease, so the physiotherapist should start supplemental oxygen during exercise today"}, "cluster_id": "CHS58", "cluster_text": "Walter Murray, 68, is a Gamilaraay man and former shearer living in a regional NSW town. His GP and the Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service (ACCHS) referred him to community physiotherapy for idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis diagnosed 18 months ago. He has a dry cough, bibasal fine end-inspiratory crackles and finger clubbing, and is not on home oxygen. Resting SpO2 is 94 percent, HR 78, RR 18. In the clinic corridor he completed a 6 minute walk test of 280 metres, with SpO2 falling to 84 percent, peak HR 118 and Borg breathlessness 6. He stops to rest twice walking to his letterbox. He lives with his niece, who drove him today and is waiting outside. An Aboriginal health worker, Janelle Cooper, works at the ACCHS clinic, which has a small gym. He told you quietly, \"I don't like being tested like I'm on show.\"", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Marked exertional desaturation, typical of fibrotic lung disease, needing medical review of oxygen and monitored exercise): In pulmonary fibrosis, impaired gas transfer worsens with exertion, so a fall to the mid 80s is expected and clinically important, calling for medical review of oxygen needs and exercise with SpO2 monitoring. Treating it as a mild change misses its significance, starting oxygen without a prescription falls outside physiotherapy scope, and the clubbing, crackles and diagnosis make a cardiac cause unlikely."}, {"id": 1190, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Murray agrees to start a pulmonary rehabilitation style programme at the ACCHS gym twice weekly. He becomes breathless quickly and desaturates on walking. His GP is reviewing his oxygen needs separately. Which exercise approach is most appropriate to begin with?", "options": {"A": "Daily living activity only, because exercise training accelerates fibrosis and damages lung tissue", "B": "Continuous moderate walking until the planned distance is complete, to build tolerance to breathlessness over time", "C": "Interval walking and strength exercise guided by Borg score and SpO2, with rest breaks and pacing", "D": "Inspiratory muscle training with a threshold device alone, since limb exercise is unsuited to lung disease"}, "cluster_id": "CHS58", "cluster_text": "Walter Murray, 68, is a Gamilaraay man and former shearer living in a regional NSW town. His GP and the Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service (ACCHS) referred him to community physiotherapy for idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis diagnosed 18 months ago. He has a dry cough, bibasal fine end-inspiratory crackles and finger clubbing, and is not on home oxygen. Resting SpO2 is 94 percent, HR 78, RR 18. In the clinic corridor he completed a 6 minute walk test of 280 metres, with SpO2 falling to 84 percent, peak HR 118 and Borg breathlessness 6. He stops to rest twice walking to his letterbox. He lives with his niece, who drove him today and is waiting outside. An Aboriginal health worker, Janelle Cooper, works at the ACCHS clinic, which has a small gym. He told you quietly, \"I don't like being tested like I'm on show.\"", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Interval walking and strength exercise guided by Borg score and SpO2, with rest breaks and pacing): Exercise training improves function and symptoms in interstitial lung disease, and interval work with rests lets him accumulate activity while limiting desaturation and breathlessness. Intensity is titrated to Borg score and SpO2. Continuous walking drives him into deep desaturation, and avoiding exercise does not protect the lungs but leads to further deconditioning."}, {"id": 1191, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "During a supervised walk along the clinic corridor at his own pace, Mr Murray's SpO2 falls to 82 percent, HR is 120 and Borg breathlessness is 7. He denies chest pain and can speak in short phrases. What should the physiotherapist do next?", "options": {"A": "Lay him flat on a plinth to recover, then resume a shortened walk once breathing eases", "B": "Slow his pace and cue pursed lip breathing while he continues walking to settle his saturations", "C": "Stop the walk, seat him with pursed lip breathing, and monitor until SpO2 recovers", "D": "Stop the walk, rest him standing at the wall, then continue the planned distance once better"}, "cluster_id": "CHS58", "cluster_text": "Walter Murray, 68, is a Gamilaraay man and former shearer living in a regional NSW town. His GP and the Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service (ACCHS) referred him to community physiotherapy for idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis diagnosed 18 months ago. He has a dry cough, bibasal fine end-inspiratory crackles and finger clubbing, and is not on home oxygen. Resting SpO2 is 94 percent, HR 78, RR 18. In the clinic corridor he completed a 6 minute walk test of 280 metres, with SpO2 falling to 84 percent, peak HR 118 and Borg breathlessness 6. He stops to rest twice walking to his letterbox. He lives with his niece, who drove him today and is waiting outside. An Aboriginal health worker, Janelle Cooper, works at the ACCHS clinic, which has a small gym. He told you quietly, \"I don't like being tested like I'm on show.\"", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop the walk, seat him with pursed lip breathing, and monitor until SpO2 recovers): A saturation of 82 percent with severe breathlessness is a clear stop point for exercise. He should be seated upright with pursed lip breathing and monitored until values recover, which also informs the next session and the medical review. Slowing down while walking or resuming the distance after a standing rest keeps him exercising while desaturated, and lying flat increases breathlessness in lung disease."}, {"id": 1192, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Murray says quietly that he does not like being tested as though he is on show. The assessment so far took place in an open gym area where other clients could watch. His niece is waiting outside. Which response best supports culturally safe practice?", "options": {"A": "Ask where and with whom he prefers assessment, and offer to involve the Aboriginal health worker", "B": "Explain that the tests are standard for all clients and continue in the gym as planned", "C": "Book the remaining measures in a private room at the physiotherapy clinic, away from the ACCHS", "D": "Limit assessment to verbal history from now on, avoiding exercise measures to prevent further discomfort"}, "cluster_id": "CHS58", "cluster_text": "Walter Murray, 68, is a Gamilaraay man and former shearer living in a regional NSW town. His GP and the Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service (ACCHS) referred him to community physiotherapy for idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis diagnosed 18 months ago. He has a dry cough, bibasal fine end-inspiratory crackles and finger clubbing, and is not on home oxygen. Resting SpO2 is 94 percent, HR 78, RR 18. In the clinic corridor he completed a 6 minute walk test of 280 metres, with SpO2 falling to 84 percent, peak HR 118 and Borg breathlessness 6. He stops to rest twice walking to his letterbox. He lives with his niece, who drove him today and is waiting outside. An Aboriginal health worker, Janelle Cooper, works at the ACCHS clinic, which has a small gym. He told you quietly, \"I don't like being tested like I'm on show.\"", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ask where and with whom he prefers assessment, and offer to involve the Aboriginal health worker): Culturally safe practice starts by listening, asking his preferences about setting and who is present, and offering support from the Aboriginal health worker. Making assumptions about what suits him, even kindly, removes his control. Insisting on routine, or dropping the exercise measures that his care depends on, fails to address his concern or his clinical needs."}, {"id": 1193, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "The consult is by video and she is alone at home, so hands-on examination and the flexion-rotation test cannot be done reliably. Which assessment would best and safely examine the cervical contribution to her headache within these limits during this consult?", "options": {"A": "Self-applied overpressure at end-range rotation held until her headache is fully reproduced, repeated on both sides", "B": "Resisted isometric neck testing against her own hand at end range, aiming to reproduce her headache", "C": "Patient-applied sustained pressure to the upper cervical joints, rating pain response to guide treatment selection", "D": "Guided active neck movements with symptom response, plus observed resting and working posture"}, "cluster_id": "CHS59", "cluster_text": "Ms Sunita Banerjee, 34, a graphic designer in regional Queensland, attends by video. For five months she has had right-sided headaches starting at the base of the skull and spreading behind the right eye, three to four days a week, usually 6/10, provoked by long screen sessions and by turning her head. There is no aura, nausea or photophobia, and her GP has excluded other causes with no red flags. By phone camera measure, active right rotation is about 50 degrees and reproduces her headache. Flexion-rotation testing and hands-on examination cannot be done reliably by video. She works on a laptop at a dining table. The clinic has no routine outcome measures and wants to introduce them.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Guided active neck movements with symptom response, plus observed resting and working posture): Active movements with symptom response and observed posture can be done safely and meaningfully by video. Self-applied pressure, resisted end-range testing and overpressure cannot be controlled or graded remotely, so they risk provoking symptoms without giving reliable information."}, {"id": 1194, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "The clinic wants to routinely collect outcome measures for headache and neck patients delivered by video. Ms Banerjee completes questionnaires at baseline and week six. Which approach to selecting and using measures best supports meaningful quality improvement for the service?", "options": {"A": "Choose clinician-designed rating scales for each patient and compare scores between individual physiotherapists to rank performance", "B": "Track headache days in whichever format each clinician prefers and file them in individual records", "C": "Record validated patient-reported measures at set intervals and review pooled results for service changes", "D": "Collect patient satisfaction ratings after each video session and report the average to the owner"}, "cluster_id": "CHS59", "cluster_text": "Ms Sunita Banerjee, 34, a graphic designer in regional Queensland, attends by video. For five months she has had right-sided headaches starting at the base of the skull and spreading behind the right eye, three to four days a week, usually 6/10, provoked by long screen sessions and by turning her head. There is no aura, nausea or photophobia, and her GP has excluded other causes with no red flags. By phone camera measure, active right rotation is about 50 degrees and reproduces her headache. Flexion-rotation testing and hands-on examination cannot be done reliably by video. She works on a laptop at a dining table. The clinic has no routine outcome measures and wants to introduce them.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Record validated patient-reported measures at set intervals and review pooled results for service changes): Validated patient-reported measures collected at set times allow results from many patients to be pooled and used to guide service improvement. Satisfaction scores alone do not show clinical change, and ranking individuals or using non-standard formats makes comparison and learning unreliable."}, {"id": 1195, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Ms Banerjee's headache is unilateral, begins at the base of the skull and varies with posture, without aura, nausea or photophobia. Which feature of her presentation most strongly supports a cervicogenic source rather than a primary headache disorder such as migraine?", "options": {"A": "Provocation of her usual headache by cervical movement, with restricted range on that side", "B": "The frequency of three to four headaches weekly, as frequent headache points to a cervical source", "C": "The absence of nausea and photophobia, because these features rule out every primary headache type", "D": "The link to sustained screen work, because posture-related triggers are specific to cervical headache"}, "cluster_id": "CHS59", "cluster_text": "Ms Sunita Banerjee, 34, a graphic designer in regional Queensland, attends by video. For five months she has had right-sided headaches starting at the base of the skull and spreading behind the right eye, three to four days a week, usually 6/10, provoked by long screen sessions and by turning her head. There is no aura, nausea or photophobia, and her GP has excluded other causes with no red flags. By phone camera measure, active right rotation is about 50 degrees and reproduces her headache. Flexion-rotation testing and hands-on examination cannot be done reliably by video. She works on a laptop at a dining table. The clinic has no routine outcome measures and wants to introduce them.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Provocation of her usual headache by cervical movement, with restricted range on that side): Reproduction of her usual headache by neck movement, together with restricted range, is the key clinical link to a cervical source. Unilateral pain, absence of nausea and screen-related triggers also occur in other headache types, so they do not discriminate on their own."}, {"id": 1196, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Midway through the video consult Ms Banerjee reports a sudden severe headache unlike her usual pattern, with double vision and slurred speech starting minutes ago. She is alone at home. What is the most appropriate first action for the physiotherapist to take while still connected by video?", "options": {"A": "Confirm her location, call 000 and stay on the video link until help arrives", "B": "Advise her to arrange a lift to the emergency department and call the clinic afterwards", "C": "Ask her to book an urgent same-day GP appointment and send the GP today's findings", "D": "Ask her to call 000 herself, then end the video link so she can talk freely"}, "cluster_id": "CHS59", "cluster_text": "Ms Sunita Banerjee, 34, a graphic designer in regional Queensland, attends by video. For five months she has had right-sided headaches starting at the base of the skull and spreading behind the right eye, three to four days a week, usually 6/10, provoked by long screen sessions and by turning her head. There is no aura, nausea or photophobia, and her GP has excluded other causes with no red flags. By phone camera measure, active right rotation is about 50 degrees and reproduces her headache. Flexion-rotation testing and hands-on examination cannot be done reliably by video. She works on a laptop at a dining table. The clinic has no routine outcome measures and wants to introduce them.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Confirm her location, call 000 and stay on the video link until help arrives): Sudden severe headache with new double vision and slurred speech suggests a possible stroke or other intracranial emergency, so an ambulance is needed straight away. The physiotherapist confirms her location and stays connected because she is alone and may deteriorate. Asking a patient with stroke symptoms to make the call herself, arrange a lift, or see a GP delays time-critical care."}, {"id": 1197, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Between fortnightly visits Desmond needs frequent, task-specific practice to improve walking. The family is keen, local staff include an Aboriginal health practitioner, and there is no gym. Funding for extra visits is limited. Which approach to organising his rehabilitation is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Concentrate therapy into the fortnightly visits with longer sessions, delivered by the physiotherapist directly", "B": "Provide a written home exercise sheet in English and review it at each fortnightly visit", "C": "Co-design daily practice with Desmond and family, supported by local staff and reviewed each visit", "D": "Advise rest and fall avoidance until a place in a metropolitan inpatient rehabilitation unit opens"}, "cluster_id": "CHS60", "cluster_text": "Desmond Marrawal, 58, is an Aboriginal man living in a remote community about 400 km from the nearest rehabilitation service. Six weeks ago he had a right middle cerebral artery ischaemic stroke and has returned home from a metropolitan hospital. He has left hemiparesis: shoulder flexion 3/5, hip flexion 3/5, ankle dorsiflexion 2/5, increased tone (modified Ashworth 1+) in the left calf, and reduced attention to his left side. He walks 30 metres with a quad stick and close supervision, and is at risk of falls. His hypertension is managed by the local Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service. The physiotherapist visits fortnightly by plane. His wife, Maureen, and adult daughter help daily and want to be involved. The physiotherapist finds a small uncontrolled study suggesting a costly robotic gait device improves walking after stroke.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Co-design daily practice with Desmond and family, supported by local staff and reviewed each visit): Recovery after stroke depends on the amount of practice between visits, which in a remote setting means building a plan with Desmond, his family and local staff. Concentrating therapy into visits gives too little total practice. A written English sheet alone ignores language, culture and the need for hands-on guidance."}, {"id": 1198, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Australian clinical guidelines for stroke management support task-specific, repetitive and sufficiently intense practice for walking recovery. Desmond currently walks about ten minutes daily, only when staff are present. How should the physiotherapist apply this recommendation to his program this week?", "options": {"A": "Increase walking practice across the day with trained family supervision, graded goals and a falls plan", "B": "Increase walking practice during fortnightly visits, where the physiotherapist can supervise each step closely", "C": "Add a daily upper limb exercise session and keep walking at ten minutes to limit fatigue", "D": "Seek the robotic gait device from the small uncontrolled study, with telehealth review between fortnightly visits"}, "cluster_id": "CHS60", "cluster_text": "Desmond Marrawal, 58, is an Aboriginal man living in a remote community about 400 km from the nearest rehabilitation service. Six weeks ago he had a right middle cerebral artery ischaemic stroke and has returned home from a metropolitan hospital. He has left hemiparesis: shoulder flexion 3/5, hip flexion 3/5, ankle dorsiflexion 2/5, increased tone (modified Ashworth 1+) in the left calf, and reduced attention to his left side. He walks 30 metres with a quad stick and close supervision, and is at risk of falls. His hypertension is managed by the local Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service. The physiotherapist visits fortnightly by plane. His wife, Maureen, and adult daughter help daily and want to be involved. The physiotherapist finds a small uncontrolled study suggesting a costly robotic gait device improves walking after stroke.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Increase walking practice across the day with trained family supervision, graded goals and a falls plan): The guideline principle is more task-specific walking practice, so the dose should rise across the day, made safe by training his family and adding graded goals and a falls plan. Limiting practice to fortnightly visits keeps the total dose low, upper limb work does not address walking, and a costly device supported only by a small uncontrolled study is not the guideline-supported response."}, {"id": 1199, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "The small uncontrolled study of a costly robotic gait device reports improved walking speed in twelve participants from a metropolitan hospital, none from remote communities, without a comparison group. Which appraisal is most appropriate before the physiotherapist recommends it for Desmond's community?", "options": {"A": "Walking speed is an objective measure, so the result reliably supports recommending the device", "B": "Publication in a journal confirms peer review, so the finding can be adopted if funding allows", "C": "The sample is too small to inform practice, so clinical experience alone should guide the decision", "D": "Weak design cannot separate device benefit from natural recovery, and remote relevance is unproven"}, "cluster_id": "CHS60", "cluster_text": "Desmond Marrawal, 58, is an Aboriginal man living in a remote community about 400 km from the nearest rehabilitation service. Six weeks ago he had a right middle cerebral artery ischaemic stroke and has returned home from a metropolitan hospital. He has left hemiparesis: shoulder flexion 3/5, hip flexion 3/5, ankle dorsiflexion 2/5, increased tone (modified Ashworth 1+) in the left calf, and reduced attention to his left side. He walks 30 metres with a quad stick and close supervision, and is at risk of falls. His hypertension is managed by the local Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service. The physiotherapist visits fortnightly by plane. His wife, Maureen, and adult daughter help daily and want to be involved. The physiotherapist finds a small uncontrolled study suggesting a costly robotic gait device improves walking after stroke.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Weak design cannot separate device benefit from natural recovery, and remote relevance is unproven): Without a comparison group, any gain cannot be separated from spontaneous recovery after stroke, and a metropolitan hospital sample may not reflect a remote community. An objective outcome does not repair a weak design, and publication does not guarantee quality. Small samples limit confidence, but the study should be appraised alongside the wider evidence rather than dismissed in favour of experience alone."}, {"id": 1200, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Desmond often bumps his left side on the doorframe and leaves food on the left of his plate. He has reduced awareness of the left side but is otherwise alert. Which strategy should the physiotherapist teach the family to support him during daily activities?", "options": {"A": "Have family do left sided tasks for him, reducing bumps and spills while the problem persists", "B": "Reassure the family that awareness usually returns alone within weeks, so prompting him is unnecessary", "C": "Cue him to look left and gradually place items and approach him from the left", "D": "Place items on his right side so he reaches them easily and avoids repeated frustration"}, "cluster_id": "CHS60", "cluster_text": "Desmond Marrawal, 58, is an Aboriginal man living in a remote community about 400 km from the nearest rehabilitation service. Six weeks ago he had a right middle cerebral artery ischaemic stroke and has returned home from a metropolitan hospital. He has left hemiparesis: shoulder flexion 3/5, hip flexion 3/5, ankle dorsiflexion 2/5, increased tone (modified Ashworth 1+) in the left calf, and reduced attention to his left side. He walks 30 metres with a quad stick and close supervision, and is at risk of falls. His hypertension is managed by the local Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service. The physiotherapist visits fortnightly by plane. His wife, Maureen, and adult daughter help daily and want to be involved. The physiotherapist finds a small uncontrolled study suggesting a costly robotic gait device improves walking after stroke.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Cue him to look left and gradually place items and approach him from the left): Cueing and presenting items from the affected side draws attention towards the neglected space and practises scanning, which is the basis of neglect rehabilitation. Placing items on the right reinforces the neglect, and doing tasks for him removes learning opportunities. Waiting for spontaneous recovery without prompting leaves him at risk of bumps and falls."}, {"id": 1201, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Kim has no effusion, locking or giving way, and her pain is provoked by stairs and squatting during a growth spurt. Single leg squat shows dynamic knee valgus and hip abduction strength is 4/5. Which factor is most appropriate to target in her rehabilitation?", "options": {"A": "Fixed patellar maltracking from bony shape, which needs imaging before any exercise programme is started", "B": "Hip and quadriceps strength and movement control, together with recent increases in training load", "C": "Iliotibial band tightness compressing the lateral patella, needing manual release before any strengthening is started", "D": "Patellar tendon tissue damage from jumping, which needs activity stopped until imaging has been completed"}, "cluster_id": "CHS61", "cluster_text": "Kim Le, 15, plays school netball and basketball and is seen at the school sport clinic with three months of bilateral anterior knee pain, worse on stairs, squatting, jumping and after sitting. She grew 6 cm this year. Her mother, Mrs Le, attends the appointment; she speaks Vietnamese and very little English, while Kim is fluent in both. Findings: no effusion, locking or giving way; tender around the lateral patellar border; single leg squat shows dynamic knee valgus with pain at 5/10; hip abduction strength 4/5 bilaterally; hamstring and quadriceps flexibility reduced; patellar glide is within normal limits and the apprehension test is negative; ligament tests normal. The school wants Kim to keep playing if safe. Kim would like her mother to understand and agree to the plan before starting.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hip and quadriceps strength and movement control, together with recent increases in training load): Patellofemoral pain in a growing athlete is usually load related and associated with reduced hip and thigh strength and poor frontal plane control, which are modifiable by exercise. Her normal glide and negative apprehension test give no reason to delay exercise for imaging or surgery. Tightness may be present but it does not need to be released before strengthening can start."}, {"id": 1202, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Kim wants to keep playing netball. Pain is 5/10 on single leg squat and she has no instability. Her training load has risen with school trials and she has two matches weekly. Which treatment plan is most appropriate for her patellofemoral pain at this stage?", "options": {"A": "Hip and quadriceps strengthening, movement retraining and load changes while she stays in sport", "B": "Patellar taping for six weeks with rest from sport, deferring exercise until her pain has settled", "C": "Quadriceps stretching and foam rolling of the iliotibial band as the main treatment, reviewed monthly", "D": "Stop all jumping and running sports for three months, then restart full training"}, "cluster_id": "CHS61", "cluster_text": "Kim Le, 15, plays school netball and basketball and is seen at the school sport clinic with three months of bilateral anterior knee pain, worse on stairs, squatting, jumping and after sitting. She grew 6 cm this year. Her mother, Mrs Le, attends the appointment; she speaks Vietnamese and very little English, while Kim is fluent in both. Findings: no effusion, locking or giving way; tender around the lateral patellar border; single leg squat shows dynamic knee valgus with pain at 5/10; hip abduction strength 4/5 bilaterally; hamstring and quadriceps flexibility reduced; patellar glide is within normal limits and the apprehension test is negative; ligament tests normal. The school wants Kim to keep playing if safe. Kim would like her mother to understand and agree to the plan before starting.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hip and quadriceps strengthening, movement retraining and load changes while she stays in sport): Exercise therapy combined with managing load is the best supported approach, and adolescents can usually remain in sport when load is adjusted to symptoms. Long periods of rest cause deconditioning and a difficult return. Taping or stretching alone does not build the strength and control she lacks."}, {"id": 1203, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "After six weeks of well tolerated strengthening, Kim says her kneecap shifted sideways during a netball landing, then returned by itself, and the knee swelled that evening. Examination shows a positive patellar apprehension test and a mild effusion. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Use ice and compression for swelling, then allow match play once she walks without pain", "B": "Continue the strengthening programme unchanged, since apprehension commonly accompanies patellofemoral pain in growing adolescents", "C": "Modify loading, arrange medical review for suspected patellar dislocation, and hold return to jumping", "D": "Add lateral retinaculum stretching to reduce further episodes, and continue sport with a brace"}, "cluster_id": "CHS61", "cluster_text": "Kim Le, 15, plays school netball and basketball and is seen at the school sport clinic with three months of bilateral anterior knee pain, worse on stairs, squatting, jumping and after sitting. She grew 6 cm this year. Her mother, Mrs Le, attends the appointment; she speaks Vietnamese and very little English, while Kim is fluent in both. Findings: no effusion, locking or giving way; tender around the lateral patellar border; single leg squat shows dynamic knee valgus with pain at 5/10; hip abduction strength 4/5 bilaterally; hamstring and quadriceps flexibility reduced; patellar glide is within normal limits and the apprehension test is negative; ligament tests normal. The school wants Kim to keep playing if safe. Kim would like her mother to understand and agree to the plan before starting.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Modify loading, arrange medical review for suspected patellar dislocation, and hold return to jumping): A sideways shift with an effusion and new apprehension suggests a patellar dislocation or subluxation, which can damage cartilage and the medial restraint and needs medical assessment. Continuing as before or returning to play on pain alone ignores that risk. Stretching and bracing are not a safe substitute for review."}, {"id": 1204, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Le speaks little English and needs to understand and agree to the exercise plan. Kim is fluent in both languages and offers to translate. The consultation includes explaining risks, alternatives and what to expect. What should the physiotherapist do to obtain valid consent?", "options": {"A": "Book a professional interpreter, explain the plan, and check Mrs Le understands before consent", "B": "Let Kim translate, since she knows the plan best and can explain it to her mother", "C": "Give Mrs Le an English handout of the risks, then obtain her signature at the end", "D": "Ask a bilingual school staff member who is free to translate during the consultation"}, "cluster_id": "CHS61", "cluster_text": "Kim Le, 15, plays school netball and basketball and is seen at the school sport clinic with three months of bilateral anterior knee pain, worse on stairs, squatting, jumping and after sitting. She grew 6 cm this year. Her mother, Mrs Le, attends the appointment; she speaks Vietnamese and very little English, while Kim is fluent in both. Findings: no effusion, locking or giving way; tender around the lateral patellar border; single leg squat shows dynamic knee valgus with pain at 5/10; hip abduction strength 4/5 bilaterally; hamstring and quadriceps flexibility reduced; patellar glide is within normal limits and the apprehension test is negative; ligament tests normal. The school wants Kim to keep playing if safe. Kim would like her mother to understand and agree to the plan before starting.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Book a professional interpreter, explain the plan, and check Mrs Le understands before consent): Valid consent requires that the person understands the information, and a qualified interpreter provides accurate, impartial translation of clinical content. A child interpreting for a parent, or an untrained staff member, risks errors and confidentiality problems. A signature on an English handout does not show Mrs Le understood anything."}, {"id": 1205, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "On direct testing Ms Tupou has marked left hip extension weakness, yet the examiner feels normal downward pressure under her left heel when she lifts the right leg against resistance. This is recorded as a clinical sign. What does it indicate about the weakness?", "options": {"A": "Pain inhibition of the muscle, because effort varies with her anxiety and fear of movement", "B": "Inconsistent weakness, because the muscle contracts normally when attention is directed to another task", "C": "Nerve root compression at L5, because strength changes with the position of the contralateral limb", "D": "Upper motor neurone weakness, because crossed flexor and extensor synergies commonly appear in the opposite limb"}, "cluster_id": "CHS62", "cluster_text": "Ms Grace Tupou, 31, early childhood educator, was referred by her GP to community physiotherapy after three months of left leg weakness and difficulty walking that began after a viral illness. A neurologist diagnosed functional neurological disorder (functional limb weakness) after normal MRI and blood tests. Examination: dragging gait with the left foot, no spasticity, normal reflexes, no sensory level. Left hip extension is 2/5 on direct testing, but the examiner's hand under her left heel feels normal downward pressure when she lifts the right leg against resistance. Ankle dorsiflexion is 1/5 on testing, yet she walks on her heels briefly while distracted. She walks 50 metres with a stick before stopping. She is anxious and tearful, and says family and friends doubt her. Timed up and go is 18 seconds. Between visits she sent a friend request to the physiotherapist's personal social media account, writing that no one else believes her.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Inconsistent weakness, because the muscle contracts normally when attention is directed to another task): Normal automatic hip extension during contralateral effort shows the movement pathway is intact and the weakness is variable, which is a positive sign of functional weakness rather than structural damage. Root compression or upper motor neurone weakness would not normalise with a change of task. Pain inhibition does not explain the preserved automatic contraction."}, {"id": 1206, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Ms Tupou asks what is wrong with her and whether the weakness is 'all in my head'. The neurologist has already confirmed the diagnosis. The physiotherapist wants to reinforce a clear, positive explanation. Her distress is obvious during the session. Which explanation is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "The weakness is real but caused by stress, so relaxation and reassurance should settle it", "B": "Her nervous system is structurally intact but functioning abnormally, and retraining movement can reverse this", "C": "Nerve damage too subtle for scans is likely, so activity should be limited for now", "D": "The cause is unclear, so we will trial several approaches to see which one helps most"}, "cluster_id": "CHS62", "cluster_text": "Ms Grace Tupou, 31, early childhood educator, was referred by her GP to community physiotherapy after three months of left leg weakness and difficulty walking that began after a viral illness. A neurologist diagnosed functional neurological disorder (functional limb weakness) after normal MRI and blood tests. Examination: dragging gait with the left foot, no spasticity, normal reflexes, no sensory level. Left hip extension is 2/5 on direct testing, but the examiner's hand under her left heel feels normal downward pressure when she lifts the right leg against resistance. Ankle dorsiflexion is 1/5 on testing, yet she walks on her heels briefly while distracted. She walks 50 metres with a stick before stopping. She is anxious and tearful, and says family and friends doubt her. Timed up and go is 18 seconds. Between visits she sent a friend request to the physiotherapist's personal social media account, writing that no one else believes her.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Her nervous system is structurally intact but functioning abnormally, and retraining movement can reverse this): Good explanation names the disorder as real, involuntary and reversible, with the problem lying in function rather than structure. Attributing it to stress alone can sound dismissive and is not required for diagnosis. Suggesting hidden damage reinforces fear and avoidance, and an uncertain explanation undermines confidence in rehabilitation."}, {"id": 1207, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Ms Tupou walks 50 metres with a stick but drags her left foot, and her walking improves when she carries a tray or talks while moving. She wants to begin gait retraining. The physiotherapist plans a graded programme. Which strategy best targets her gait problem?", "options": {"A": "Provide a wheelchair for community distances and a frame indoors to conserve energy until strength returns", "B": "Break walking into conscious parts, cueing heel strike and knee position on each stride", "C": "Use resisted strengthening of the left leg in lying to rebuild muscle bulk and power", "D": "Practise walking with attention on an external target or task, building repetitions gradually"}, "cluster_id": "CHS62", "cluster_text": "Ms Grace Tupou, 31, early childhood educator, was referred by her GP to community physiotherapy after three months of left leg weakness and difficulty walking that began after a viral illness. A neurologist diagnosed functional neurological disorder (functional limb weakness) after normal MRI and blood tests. Examination: dragging gait with the left foot, no spasticity, normal reflexes, no sensory level. Left hip extension is 2/5 on direct testing, but the examiner's hand under her left heel feels normal downward pressure when she lifts the right leg against resistance. Ankle dorsiflexion is 1/5 on testing, yet she walks on her heels briefly while distracted. She walks 50 metres with a stick before stopping. She is anxious and tearful, and says family and friends doubt her. Timed up and go is 18 seconds. Between visits she sent a friend request to the physiotherapist's personal social media account, writing that no one else believes her.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Practise walking with attention on an external target or task, building repetitions gradually): Automatic movement is better preserved in functional movement disorders, so directing attention outward lets her walk more normally and practise it. Conscious step-by-step cueing increases self-monitoring and usually worsens the pattern. Aids and isolated strengthening reinforce a belief in structural weakness and do not address the underlying disorder."}, {"id": 1208, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Ms Tupou appears distressed and reliant on the sessions, and the friend request remains unanswered. The physiotherapist wants to protect the therapeutic relationship and her wellbeing. Her next appointment is in three days at the community clinic. What is the most appropriate professional response?", "options": {"A": "Decline kindly, explain the professional boundary at her next visit, and document the exchange", "B": "Accept the request with restricted visibility, since refusing could damage her trust and her recovery", "C": "Transfer her care to a colleague, because the boundary has been crossed and trust is compromised", "D": "Ignore the message and continue treatment as usual, so responding does not encourage further contact"}, "cluster_id": "CHS62", "cluster_text": "Ms Grace Tupou, 31, early childhood educator, was referred by her GP to community physiotherapy after three months of left leg weakness and difficulty walking that began after a viral illness. A neurologist diagnosed functional neurological disorder (functional limb weakness) after normal MRI and blood tests. Examination: dragging gait with the left foot, no spasticity, normal reflexes, no sensory level. Left hip extension is 2/5 on direct testing, but the examiner's hand under her left heel feels normal downward pressure when she lifts the right leg against resistance. Ankle dorsiflexion is 1/5 on testing, yet she walks on her heels briefly while distracted. She walks 50 metres with a stick before stopping. She is anxious and tearful, and says family and friends doubt her. Timed up and go is 18 seconds. Between visits she sent a friend request to the physiotherapist's personal social media account, writing that no one else believes her.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Decline kindly, explain the professional boundary at her next visit, and document the exchange): A clear, respectful explanation keeps the relationship professional while maintaining her care, and records show the issue was handled. Accepting blurs the boundary even with privacy settings. Silence leaves her uncertain, and transfer is a disproportionate response to a request that can be managed within the existing therapeutic relationship."}, {"id": 1209, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Tevita's left knee opens more than the right on valgus stress at 30 degrees of flexion, with a firm endpoint and pain, but is stable in full extension. Lachman and posterior drawer tests are negative. Which interpretation of these findings is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Isolated medial meniscus tear producing apparent ligament laxity through pain at the medial joint line", "B": "Partial tear of the superficial medial collateral ligament with the cruciate ligaments likely intact", "C": "Anterior cruciate ligament injury with secondary stretching of the medial restraints from the valgus mechanism", "D": "Complete medial collateral ligament rupture with posteromedial capsule injury, needing early surgical referral for repair"}, "cluster_id": "CHS63", "cluster_text": "Tevita Salesa, 27, plays rugby union as a winger and presents to your private practice four days after a tackler landed on the outside of his planted left knee. He felt immediate medial knee pain, finished the match, and the knee swelled mildly overnight. Findings: mild effusion; tender over the medial femoral epicondyle and along the medial joint line; active range 5 to 110 degrees with pain at end range; Lachman and posterior drawer tests negative; valgus stress at 30 degrees of flexion shows greater opening than the right with a firm endpoint and pain, but the knee is stable in full extension; McMurray test is uncomfortable without a click. He walks with a mild limp. At his second visit, during a band exercise, the wall anchor for his resistance band pulled out of the plasterboard and the band recoiled onto his forearm, leaving a red mark with no skin break. He says he is fine.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Partial tear of the superficial medial collateral ligament with the cruciate ligaments likely intact): Laxity at 30 degrees with a firm endpoint indicates a partial injury, and stability in extension suggests the deeper posteromedial structures are intact. Negative Lachman and drawer tests argue against cruciate involvement. A complete rupture would usually show a soft endpoint and laxity in extension as well."}, {"id": 1210, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Tevita has a mild effusion, range of 5 to 110 degrees and a limp, and wants to return to training as soon as possible. He has been running four times weekly and has no other injuries. Which approach is most appropriate for the first two weeks of managing his medial collateral ligament sprain?", "options": {"A": "Avoid weight bearing on crutches for two weeks, then start range of motion and strengthening", "B": "Begin straight line jogging and light agility work this week, progressing as soreness allows", "C": "Prioritise hamstring and calf stretching and ice, delaying quadriceps loading until swelling has fully settled", "D": "Protected weight bearing, early range of motion and quadriceps activation, with a hinged brace if needed"}, "cluster_id": "CHS63", "cluster_text": "Tevita Salesa, 27, plays rugby union as a winger and presents to your private practice four days after a tackler landed on the outside of his planted left knee. He felt immediate medial knee pain, finished the match, and the knee swelled mildly overnight. Findings: mild effusion; tender over the medial femoral epicondyle and along the medial joint line; active range 5 to 110 degrees with pain at end range; Lachman and posterior drawer tests negative; valgus stress at 30 degrees of flexion shows greater opening than the right with a firm endpoint and pain, but the knee is stable in full extension; McMurray test is uncomfortable without a click. He walks with a mild limp. At his second visit, during a band exercise, the wall anchor for his resistance band pulled out of the plasterboard and the band recoiled onto his forearm, leaving a red mark with no skin break. He says he is fine.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Protected weight bearing, early range of motion and quadriceps activation, with a hinged brace if needed): Partial medial collateral ligament injuries heal well with protected function: early motion, weight bearing and muscle activation limit stiffness and wasting without stressing the ligament, and a hinged brace can add support. Non-weight bearing and delaying quadriceps loading cause wasting and stiffness, and starting running and agility work this soon overloads healing fibres while swelling and restricted range remain."}, {"id": 1211, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "At the next visit Tevita reports that his knee catches and briefly locks short of full extension after he pivoted on it at home, and swelling has increased overnight. His ligament findings are unchanged. How should this new information change the management plan?", "options": {"A": "Continue the programme but avoid rotation, because locking is an expected feature of medial collateral sprains", "B": "Pause progressive loading and arrange medical review and imaging for a suspected meniscal injury", "C": "Perform a gentle manipulation under traction to unlock the joint and restore extension within the session", "D": "Reduce exercise intensity and reassess in two weeks, since locking often settles as the swelling resolves"}, "cluster_id": "CHS63", "cluster_text": "Tevita Salesa, 27, plays rugby union as a winger and presents to your private practice four days after a tackler landed on the outside of his planted left knee. He felt immediate medial knee pain, finished the match, and the knee swelled mildly overnight. Findings: mild effusion; tender over the medial femoral epicondyle and along the medial joint line; active range 5 to 110 degrees with pain at end range; Lachman and posterior drawer tests negative; valgus stress at 30 degrees of flexion shows greater opening than the right with a firm endpoint and pain, but the knee is stable in full extension; McMurray test is uncomfortable without a click. He walks with a mild limp. At his second visit, during a band exercise, the wall anchor for his resistance band pulled out of the plasterboard and the band recoiled onto his forearm, leaving a red mark with no skin break. He says he is fine.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Pause progressive loading and arrange medical review and imaging for a suspected meniscal injury): A new mechanical block to extension with increased effusion suggests a displaced meniscal tear, which an isolated collateral sprain does not explain. Loading should pause and he needs medical review and imaging. Manipulating a locked knee risks further damage, and waiting or continuing delays the decision on whether surgery is needed."}, {"id": 1212, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "During Tevita's session the wall anchor for his resistance band pulled out and the band recoiled onto his forearm, leaving a red mark without a skin break. He says he is fine and wants to continue. What should the physiotherapist do?", "options": {"A": "Refit the anchor and tell Tevita, reporting only if he later develops symptoms or complains", "B": "Phone the insurer first and let them decide what is recorded and who is told", "C": "Check his arm, document factually, use the incident register, and remove the anchor", "D": "Record nothing since the skin was intact, then refix the anchor before his next session"}, "cluster_id": "CHS63", "cluster_text": "Tevita Salesa, 27, plays rugby union as a winger and presents to your private practice four days after a tackler landed on the outside of his planted left knee. He felt immediate medial knee pain, finished the match, and the knee swelled mildly overnight. Findings: mild effusion; tender over the medial femoral epicondyle and along the medial joint line; active range 5 to 110 degrees with pain at end range; Lachman and posterior drawer tests negative; valgus stress at 30 degrees of flexion shows greater opening than the right with a firm endpoint and pain, but the knee is stable in full extension; McMurray test is uncomfortable without a click. He walks with a mild limp. At his second visit, during a band exercise, the wall anchor for his resistance band pulled out of the plasterboard and the band recoiled onto his forearm, leaving a red mark with no skin break. He says he is fine.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Check his arm, document factually, use the incident register, and remove the anchor): An equipment failure that touched a patient is a reportable incident even when harm is minor, and recording it lets the clinic find and fix the hazard. The patient's wish to carry on does not remove that duty. Quietly refixing the anchor or leaving the reporting to an insurer skips the clinic's own risk management process."}, {"id": 1213, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "The ward team wants a consistent way to track whether Mrs Maddox' fluid overload is resolving as diuretic treatment continues over the coming days. Which single measure, recorded the same way each day, is most useful for the ward team to follow?", "options": {"A": "A resting oxygen saturation reading each morning, as it falls when lung congestion worsens", "B": "Resting heart rate measured by pulse each morning, as it falls when the fluid load resolves", "C": "Daily weight on the same scale at the same time, interpreted with oedema and symptoms", "D": "Distance walked on the ward each day, which rises in step with falling fluid load"}, "cluster_id": "CHS64", "cluster_text": "Mrs Wendy Maddox, 81, widowed, lives alone in a two-storey house in regional Victoria. She is on day 3 of admission with acute decompensated heart failure (ejection fraction 35 percent) and atrial fibrillation. Admission weight 74 kg; her usual dry weight is about 69 kg. Findings: bilateral pitting oedema to the knees, bibasal crackles, RR 22, SpO2 93 percent on room air, HR 88, BP 108/64 mmHg. She needs two pillows to sleep. She is on intravenous frusemide with a fluid restriction in place. Previously independent with a single-point stick. Today she walked 10 metres with the stick and stand-by assistance and needed a seated rest because of breathlessness. Her daughter lives interstate. She has never had a My Aged Care assessment and has no home services. She wants to go home.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Daily weight on the same scale at the same time, interpreted with oedema and symptoms): Body weight tracks fluid gain and loss more directly than other bedside measures, provided the same scale, time of day and clothing are used, and it is read alongside oedema and breathlessness. Oxygen saturation, heart rate and walking distance are influenced by many other factors, including atrial fibrillation, deconditioning and effort, so they are less specific for fluid status."}, {"id": 1214, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Maddox is breathless after short walks but stable at rest, and she spends most of the day in bed, with persisting oedema and a reduced ejection fraction. Which exercise approach is most appropriate to start safely rebuilding her function on the ward?", "options": {"A": "Short frequent walks with seated rests, keeping breathlessness moderate and monitoring SpO2 and BP", "B": "Resistance training of the legs using bands twice daily, deferring walking until she is euvolaemic", "C": "Continuous walking for twenty minutes to mobilise fluid, stopping only if she becomes very breathless", "D": "Bed rest with leg elevation until the oedema resolves, to reduce the workload on her heart"}, "cluster_id": "CHS64", "cluster_text": "Mrs Wendy Maddox, 81, widowed, lives alone in a two-storey house in regional Victoria. She is on day 3 of admission with acute decompensated heart failure (ejection fraction 35 percent) and atrial fibrillation. Admission weight 74 kg; her usual dry weight is about 69 kg. Findings: bilateral pitting oedema to the knees, bibasal crackles, RR 22, SpO2 93 percent on room air, HR 88, BP 108/64 mmHg. She needs two pillows to sleep. She is on intravenous frusemide with a fluid restriction in place. Previously independent with a single-point stick. Today she walked 10 metres with the stick and stand-by assistance and needed a seated rest because of breathlessness. Her daughter lives interstate. She has never had a My Aged Care assessment and has no home services. She wants to go home.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Short frequent walks with seated rests, keeping breathlessness moderate and monitoring SpO2 and BP): Heart failure rehabilitation begins with low-intensity, interval-style activity and rests, keeping breathlessness at a moderate level while symptoms and observations are watched, because bed rest drives deconditioning without improving outcomes. Continuous prolonged walking is too demanding now, and delaying walking until she is euvolaemic wastes the chance to prevent further decline."}, {"id": 1215, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Maddox becomes distressed and breathless when lying flat in bed at night, with bilateral basal crackles on auscultation and bilateral leg oedema. The nurse asks for a positioning strategy. Which is most appropriate to ease her breathing while resting?", "options": {"A": "Semi-reclined at a low angle with one pillow, to rest her back and keep her relaxed", "B": "Side lying on her left with legs bent, to keep her comfortable and conserve her energy", "C": "Lying flat with a single pillow and oxygen at the bedside, to rest the respiratory muscles", "D": "Upright supported sitting in bed or a chair, leaning forward on a table with pillows"}, "cluster_id": "CHS64", "cluster_text": "Mrs Wendy Maddox, 81, widowed, lives alone in a two-storey house in regional Victoria. She is on day 3 of admission with acute decompensated heart failure (ejection fraction 35 percent) and atrial fibrillation. Admission weight 74 kg; her usual dry weight is about 69 kg. Findings: bilateral pitting oedema to the knees, bibasal crackles, RR 22, SpO2 93 percent on room air, HR 88, BP 108/64 mmHg. She needs two pillows to sleep. She is on intravenous frusemide with a fluid restriction in place. Previously independent with a single-point stick. Today she walked 10 metres with the stick and stand-by assistance and needed a seated rest because of breathlessness. Her daughter lives interstate. She has never had a My Aged Care assessment and has no home services. She wants to go home.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Upright supported sitting in bed or a chair, leaning forward on a table with pillows): Orthopnoea eases when she sits well upright, because fluid shifts away from the lungs and the diaphragm works against less load. Leaning forward on a table also supports her accessory muscles. A low semi-reclined angle or side lying still allows too much venous return to the chest, and lying flat is the position that triggered her distress, even with oxygen nearby."}, {"id": 1216, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Maddox will return home alone and needs ongoing help with showering, meals and exercise, but she has no aged care assessment and her daughter lives interstate. What is the most appropriate step for organising services before her discharge from hospital?", "options": {"A": "Give a written exercise sheet and ask her daughter to arrange services by phone after discharge", "B": "Lodge an NDIS access request, because her ongoing functional limitations at home need funded supports", "C": "Raise a My Aged Care assessment referral with the team and arrange interim supports", "D": "Recommend self-funded private physiotherapy and home help, since public pathways take too long to start"}, "cluster_id": "CHS64", "cluster_text": "Mrs Wendy Maddox, 81, widowed, lives alone in a two-storey house in regional Victoria. She is on day 3 of admission with acute decompensated heart failure (ejection fraction 35 percent) and atrial fibrillation. Admission weight 74 kg; her usual dry weight is about 69 kg. Findings: bilateral pitting oedema to the knees, bibasal crackles, RR 22, SpO2 93 percent on room air, HR 88, BP 108/64 mmHg. She needs two pillows to sleep. She is on intravenous frusemide with a fluid restriction in place. Previously independent with a single-point stick. Today she walked 10 metres with the stick and stand-by assistance and needed a seated rest because of breathlessness. Her daughter lives interstate. She has never had a My Aged Care assessment and has no home services. She wants to go home.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Raise a My Aged Care assessment referral with the team and arrange interim supports): Older people who need support with daily living are directed to the aged care system through My Aged Care, so the team should start that referral and organise interim supports so she is not discharged without help. NDIS access requests generally have to be made before age 65, so that scheme is not the right route. Relying on her daughter or private funding leaves a gap."}, {"id": 1217, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Silvana is now drowsy and confused. Observations: RR 28, HR 124, BP 88/50, temperature 38.9 degrees, SpO2 90 percent on 2 litres. These readings meet the ward's rapid response criteria. Her nurse is in another bay. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Sit her upright, stay with her and ask a colleague to inform the nurse in charge", "B": "Keep her safe, stay with her and activate the rapid response call per hospital policy", "C": "Stay with her, give oxygen as charted and ask the nurse to call the doctor", "D": "Support her back to bed, take a full set of repeat observations and then escalate"}, "cluster_id": "CHS65", "cluster_text": "Silvana Rinaldi, 59, retired teacher, is on day two after a laparoscopic right hemicolectomy. The physiotherapist is seeing her for routine post-operative chest review and early mobilisation. History: type 2 diabetes, BMI 33. At the start of the session: RR 18, HR 96, BP 118/70, temperature 37.4, SpO2 95 percent on 2 L/min nasal prongs, alert and orientated, pain 3 out of 10. Her son-in-law Daniel, who plays in the same football club as the physiotherapist, is visiting. Twenty minutes into the session, while sitting on the edge of the bed, Silvana becomes drowsy and confused. The ward uses an early warning track and trigger chart, and a rapid response call can be made by any staff member.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Keep her safe, stay with her and activate the rapid response call per hospital policy): Her observations already meet the rapid response criteria, so the physiotherapist should stay with her and activate the call straight away, as any staff member is authorised to do. Routing the message through a colleague, the nurse or the doctor adds steps between a deteriorating patient and the response team. Repeating a full set of observations before escalating only confirms what is already clear and delays senior review of possible sepsis."}, {"id": 1218, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "After the call, the medical registrar asks the physiotherapist what these findings suggest. Silvana has fever, tachycardia, low blood pressure, falling saturation and new confusion on day two after bowel surgery. Which interpretation of this combination of findings is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Possible sepsis with early organ dysfunction, for example from an anastomotic leak, needing urgent review", "B": "A hypoglycaemic episode related to diabetes, which usually settles quickly after oral or intravenous glucose", "C": "Retained secretions causing atelectasis, which would be expected to improve with airway clearance and mobilisation", "D": "Postoperative pain with anxiety causing hyperventilation, which would be expected to settle with reassurance and analgesia"}, "cluster_id": "CHS65", "cluster_text": "Silvana Rinaldi, 59, retired teacher, is on day two after a laparoscopic right hemicolectomy. The physiotherapist is seeing her for routine post-operative chest review and early mobilisation. History: type 2 diabetes, BMI 33. At the start of the session: RR 18, HR 96, BP 118/70, temperature 37.4, SpO2 95 percent on 2 L/min nasal prongs, alert and orientated, pain 3 out of 10. Her son-in-law Daniel, who plays in the same football club as the physiotherapist, is visiting. Twenty minutes into the session, while sitting on the edge of the bed, Silvana becomes drowsy and confused. The ward uses an early warning track and trigger chart, and a rapid response call can be made by any staff member.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Possible sepsis with early organ dysfunction, for example from an anastomotic leak, needing urgent review): Fever with tachycardia, hypotension, hypoxia and altered mental state after abdominal surgery points to systemic infection with early organ dysfunction rather than a problem physiotherapy treatment alone would settle. Atelectasis does not usually produce hypotension and confusion. Hypoglycaemia and anxiety do not account for the fever and low blood pressure."}, {"id": 1219, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "The registrar arrives and asks for a handover. The physiotherapist is the only staff member who saw Silvana deteriorate, and her nurse has not yet returned with the observation chart. Which communication approach is most appropriate to give the team the information they need quickly?", "options": {"A": "Describe the full physiotherapy assessment findings in chronological order so the registrar sees the whole picture", "B": "Summarise only the respiratory findings and treatment, since other systems are outside the physiotherapist's scope", "C": "Wait for questions, because the registrar will examine her and interpret the observations independently", "D": "Give a concise ISBAR handover with current observations, the trend, treatment given and your concern"}, "cluster_id": "CHS65", "cluster_text": "Silvana Rinaldi, 59, retired teacher, is on day two after a laparoscopic right hemicolectomy. The physiotherapist is seeing her for routine post-operative chest review and early mobilisation. History: type 2 diabetes, BMI 33. At the start of the session: RR 18, HR 96, BP 118/70, temperature 37.4, SpO2 95 percent on 2 L/min nasal prongs, alert and orientated, pain 3 out of 10. Her son-in-law Daniel, who plays in the same football club as the physiotherapist, is visiting. Twenty minutes into the session, while sitting on the edge of the bed, Silvana becomes drowsy and confused. The ward uses an early warning track and trigger chart, and a rapid response call can be made by any staff member.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Give a concise ISBAR handover with current observations, the trend, treatment given and your concern): A structured, concise handover that includes the trend, what has been done and what concerns the clinician allows the team to act quickly, and the physiotherapist holds unique information about the change. A long chronological account buries the key points. Waiting passively or limiting the report to respiratory findings withholds relevant observations the team needs."}, {"id": 1220, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Later that day Daniel, the physiotherapist's football club teammate and Silvana's son-in-law, asks in the corridor what her observations showed and whether she could die. Silvana is being moved to a monitored bed, and her husband has not yet arrived. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Share the observations briefly, since he is family and a friend and will soon find out", "B": "Express concern, explain the treating team will update the family, and avoid sharing clinical details yourself", "C": "Decline to speak with him and say any request for information must be made in writing", "D": "Share only the physiotherapy findings, because interpreting medical significance is outside the physiotherapist's scope"}, "cluster_id": "CHS65", "cluster_text": "Silvana Rinaldi, 59, retired teacher, is on day two after a laparoscopic right hemicolectomy. The physiotherapist is seeing her for routine post-operative chest review and early mobilisation. History: type 2 diabetes, BMI 33. At the start of the session: RR 18, HR 96, BP 118/70, temperature 37.4, SpO2 95 percent on 2 L/min nasal prongs, alert and orientated, pain 3 out of 10. Her son-in-law Daniel, who plays in the same football club as the physiotherapist, is visiting. Twenty minutes into the session, while sitting on the edge of the bed, Silvana becomes drowsy and confused. The ward uses an early warning track and trigger chart, and a rapid response call can be made by any staff member.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Express concern, explain the treating team will update the family, and avoid sharing clinical details yourself): A personal friendship does not change the duty of confidentiality or the need for information to come through the treating team, who can discuss prognosis accurately and with her consent where possible. A warm, honest explanation of the process respects the relationship without breaching boundaries. Sharing partial findings is still a disclosure, and a blunt written-request response is unhelpful to a distressed relative."}, {"id": 1221, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Nia cannot extend her wrist or fingers and has reduced sensation over the back of her hand, but elbow extension and finger flexion are preserved. Which nerve and lesion level does this pattern most strongly indicate in the context of her humeral shaft fracture?", "options": {"A": "Radial nerve injury distal to the triceps branches, consistent with damage at the humeral shaft", "B": "Radial nerve injury proximal to the triceps branches, as occurs with axillary compression or crutch use", "C": "Posterior interosseous nerve injury in the proximal forearm, affecting the finger extensors and hand sensation", "D": "Axillary nerve injury at the shoulder, producing loss of shoulder abduction and sensation over the deltoid"}, "cluster_id": "CHS66", "cluster_text": "Nia Kamara, 14, fell from her scooter yesterday and has a right humeral shaft fracture, fixed with a plate this morning. Since the injury she cannot lift her right wrist or extend her fingers. Examination on day 1 shows wrist extension 0/5, finger extension at the knuckles 0/5, elbow extension 4/5, elbow flexion 4/5 limited by pain, and reduced sensation over the back of the hand and the first web space. Finger flexion and thumb opposition are intact and the hand is warm with normal capillary refill. The operative report states the nerve was seen to be in continuity. She lives with her parents and plays netball, and she is worried about returning to school and sport. Her parents want to know what they can do to help her recover.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Radial nerve injury distal to the triceps branches, consistent with damage at the humeral shaft): Loss of wrist and finger extension with sensory loss over the back of the hand fits the radial nerve, and preserved elbow extension shows the triceps branches are intact, placing the lesion distal to them at the humeral shaft. A lesion proximal to those branches would weaken elbow extension. A posterior interosseous lesion spares sensation, and an axillary injury would affect the shoulder."}, {"id": 1222, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Nia's wrist drops and her fingers cannot extend, and the surgeon wants the paralysed muscles protected while the nerve recovers. Her wound is closed and her arm is in a sling. Which hand management is most appropriate to begin on day one?", "options": {"A": "Begin resisted wrist and finger strengthening with putty to maintain muscle bulk during recovery", "B": "Leave the hand free and teach active finger flexion exercises, expecting the extensors to recover unaided", "C": "Fit a splint holding the wrist in slight extension, with regular passive finger movements", "D": "Splint the wrist in flexion to rest the paralysed extensors, with hand movement avoided"}, "cluster_id": "CHS66", "cluster_text": "Nia Kamara, 14, fell from her scooter yesterday and has a right humeral shaft fracture, fixed with a plate this morning. Since the injury she cannot lift her right wrist or extend her fingers. Examination on day 1 shows wrist extension 0/5, finger extension at the knuckles 0/5, elbow extension 4/5, elbow flexion 4/5 limited by pain, and reduced sensation over the back of the hand and the first web space. Finger flexion and thumb opposition are intact and the hand is warm with normal capillary refill. The operative report states the nerve was seen to be in continuity. She lives with her parents and plays netball, and she is worried about returning to school and sport. Her parents want to know what they can do to help her recover.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Fit a splint holding the wrist in slight extension, with regular passive finger movements): A splint supports the wrist in slight extension, protecting the weak extensors from being overstretched by gravity and keeping the hand functional, while passive movements maintain finger and wrist range. Flexion splinting stretches the extensors, resisted exercise cannot work paralysed muscle, and leaving the hand unsupported risks overstretch and stiffness."}, {"id": 1223, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Three months after injury Nia still has no wrist or finger extension, and Tinel's sign has not advanced down the forearm since the previous review. Sensation over the first web space is unchanged. Which course of action is best for the physiotherapist at this stage?", "options": {"A": "Continue the current programme for six more months, since nerve recovery is slow, and review then", "B": "Report the lack of recovery to the surgeon for nerve studies, and continue splinting meanwhile", "C": "Discharge her with a home programme and splint, as physiotherapy cannot influence nerve recovery", "D": "Begin progressive resisted strengthening of the wrist extensors to stimulate the nerve to regenerate faster"}, "cluster_id": "CHS66", "cluster_text": "Nia Kamara, 14, fell from her scooter yesterday and has a right humeral shaft fracture, fixed with a plate this morning. Since the injury she cannot lift her right wrist or extend her fingers. Examination on day 1 shows wrist extension 0/5, finger extension at the knuckles 0/5, elbow extension 4/5, elbow flexion 4/5 limited by pain, and reduced sensation over the back of the hand and the first web space. Finger flexion and thumb opposition are intact and the hand is warm with normal capillary refill. The operative report states the nerve was seen to be in continuity. She lives with her parents and plays netball, and she is worried about returning to school and sport. Her parents want to know what they can do to help her recover.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report the lack of recovery to the surgeon for nerve studies, and continue splinting meanwhile): No clinical recovery and no advancing Tinel's sign at about three months suggests the nerve may not be regenerating, so the surgeon should know to consider nerve conduction studies or exploration. Continuing to wait delays a decision. Resisted strengthening cannot make paralysed muscle respond, and discharge ignores ongoing risks to the joints and skin."}, {"id": 1224, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Nia has reduced sensation over the back of her hand and is worried about school and netball. Her parents ask how they can help at home each day. Which self-management plan best supports her recovery and safety while the nerve heals?", "options": {"A": "Keep the hand rested in the sling until sensation returns, with finger movements done weekly", "B": "Avoid netball and school for six months, and rely on the splint to protect the hand", "C": "Check the numb skin daily, protect it from heat and cuts, and do passive movements", "D": "Squeeze a firm ball and lift light weights daily to keep the hand strong"}, "cluster_id": "CHS66", "cluster_text": "Nia Kamara, 14, fell from her scooter yesterday and has a right humeral shaft fracture, fixed with a plate this morning. Since the injury she cannot lift her right wrist or extend her fingers. Examination on day 1 shows wrist extension 0/5, finger extension at the knuckles 0/5, elbow extension 4/5, elbow flexion 4/5 limited by pain, and reduced sensation over the back of the hand and the first web space. Finger flexion and thumb opposition are intact and the hand is warm with normal capillary refill. The operative report states the nerve was seen to be in continuity. She lives with her parents and plays netball, and she is worried about returning to school and sport. Her parents want to know what they can do to help her recover.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Check the numb skin daily, protect it from heat and cuts, and do passive movements): She has reduced sensation, so daily skin checks and protection from heat and cuts prevent unnoticed injury, while regular passive movements keep joints supple. Strengthening paralysed muscles is ineffective, and prolonged rest or missing school removes participation she can safely keep with sensible precautions."}, {"id": 1225, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Pettigrew asks which everyday movements to avoid while the T11 fracture heals over the coming weeks. He is worried about making the fracture worse at home and in the garden. Which advice best reflects safe management for his vertebral compression fracture and osteoporosis?", "options": {"A": "Limit walking to household distances for six weeks because impact forces can collapse the vertebra further", "B": "Avoid loaded forward bending and twisting, and hinge at the hips when lifting light items", "C": "Rest in bed with the head raised until the pain settles, then restart walking gradually", "D": "Avoid any gentle back extension, since extension loads the fractured vertebral body and delays union"}, "cluster_id": "CHS67", "cluster_text": "Graham Pettigrew, 68, a retired bus driver, attends private practice 10 days after sudden sharp mid-back pain while lifting a bag of potting mix. He has taken oral prednisolone for polymyalgia rheumatica for six years. X-ray shows a T11 wedge compression fracture with about 30 percent height loss. A DXA scan confirms osteoporosis and his GP has started medication, calcium and vitamin D. Findings: pain 6/10 on rising from sitting, increased thoracic kyphosis, localised tenderness over T11, lower limb strength 5/5, sensation and reflexes normal, no bladder or bowel change. He walks 10 minutes before pain stops him. Timed Up and Go 13 seconds. His daughter drives him to appointments and has left a message at the clinic asking for his diagnosis and plan.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Avoid loaded forward bending and twisting, and hinge at the hips when lifting light items): Flexion and twisting concentrate load on the anterior vertebral body, which is already wedged, so hip hinging protects the fracture while it heals. Prolonged bed rest accelerates bone loss and deconditioning, and short regular walking is encouraged in osteoporosis. Gentle extension is helpful rather than harmful."}, {"id": 1226, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Three weeks on, pain is 3/10 and Mr Pettigrew wants to start a home exercise programme. He has no neurological signs and the GP has cleared him to exercise. Which programme is most appropriate for his osteoporosis and healing T11 fracture?", "options": {"A": "Flexion-based Williams exercises including knee to chest stretches to ease pain and mobilise the stiff spine", "B": "Low height jumping and hopping drills to load the skeleton, introduced gradually over four weeks", "C": "Progressive back extensor and hip strengthening in neutral spine, plus daily walking and balance work", "D": "Abdominal curl-ups and sit-ups to build trunk flexor strength and support the thoracic spine"}, "cluster_id": "CHS67", "cluster_text": "Graham Pettigrew, 68, a retired bus driver, attends private practice 10 days after sudden sharp mid-back pain while lifting a bag of potting mix. He has taken oral prednisolone for polymyalgia rheumatica for six years. X-ray shows a T11 wedge compression fracture with about 30 percent height loss. A DXA scan confirms osteoporosis and his GP has started medication, calcium and vitamin D. Findings: pain 6/10 on rising from sitting, increased thoracic kyphosis, localised tenderness over T11, lower limb strength 5/5, sensation and reflexes normal, no bladder or bowel change. He walks 10 minutes before pain stops him. Timed Up and Go 13 seconds. His daughter drives him to appointments and has left a message at the clinic asking for his diagnosis and plan.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Progressive back extensor and hip strengthening in neutral spine, plus daily walking and balance work): Extensor and hip strengthening with balance training reduces fall risk and loads the skeleton safely while keeping the spine neutral. Repeated trunk flexion such as curl-ups raises compressive load on the anterior vertebral body and risks further wedging. High impact drills are too early after a recent vertebral fracture."}, {"id": 1227, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Five weeks later he reports a new band of pain around his lower ribs after coughing, plus tingling in both feet. Today he mentions difficulty starting his urine stream. Lower limb power is 4/5 bilaterally, previously 5/5. What should the physiotherapist do next?", "options": {"A": "Stop treatment and urgently phone his GP to request same day review and repeat spinal imaging", "B": "Stop treatment and send him to an emergency department today for suspected neural compromise", "C": "Cease exercise, arrange urgent MRI through his GP the same week and review him afterwards", "D": "Restrict to gentle walking, record the new findings and contact his GP next morning"}, "cluster_id": "CHS67", "cluster_text": "Graham Pettigrew, 68, a retired bus driver, attends private practice 10 days after sudden sharp mid-back pain while lifting a bag of potting mix. He has taken oral prednisolone for polymyalgia rheumatica for six years. X-ray shows a T11 wedge compression fracture with about 30 percent height loss. A DXA scan confirms osteoporosis and his GP has started medication, calcium and vitamin D. Findings: pain 6/10 on rising from sitting, increased thoracic kyphosis, localised tenderness over T11, lower limb strength 5/5, sensation and reflexes normal, no bladder or bowel change. He walks 10 minutes before pain stops him. Timed Up and Go 13 seconds. His daughter drives him to appointments and has left a message at the clinic asking for his diagnosis and plan.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stop treatment and send him to an emergency department today for suspected neural compromise): A new motor deficit, band like trunk pain, foot paraesthesia and bladder hesitancy after a vertebral fracture suggest cord or cauda equina compromise and need emergency department assessment and imaging today. Asking the GP for a same day review or a scan within the week still sends him through a slower route than the emergency department can provide. Gentle walking with next morning contact leaves a possible cord injury unassessed overnight."}, {"id": 1228, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Pettigrew's daughter Sandra phones the clinic. She drives him to sessions and asks for his diagnosis, fracture details and exercise plan so she can help. The file holds no record of him authorising disclosure to family. How should the physiotherapist respond?", "options": {"A": "Explain that sharing needs his consent, offer to ask him, and record any permission", "B": "Post a written summary to the family address marked confidential and note this in his file", "C": "Share the diagnosis and exercise plan only, since she transports him and supports him at home", "D": "Verify her identity by asking his date of birth and address, then share the treatment plan"}, "cluster_id": "CHS67", "cluster_text": "Graham Pettigrew, 68, a retired bus driver, attends private practice 10 days after sudden sharp mid-back pain while lifting a bag of potting mix. He has taken oral prednisolone for polymyalgia rheumatica for six years. X-ray shows a T11 wedge compression fracture with about 30 percent height loss. A DXA scan confirms osteoporosis and his GP has started medication, calcium and vitamin D. Findings: pain 6/10 on rising from sitting, increased thoracic kyphosis, localised tenderness over T11, lower limb strength 5/5, sensation and reflexes normal, no bladder or bowel change. He walks 10 minutes before pain stops him. Timed Up and Go 13 seconds. His daughter drives him to appointments and has left a message at the clinic asking for his diagnosis and plan.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Explain that sharing needs his consent, offer to ask him, and record any permission): An adult with capacity controls who receives his health information, and being a relative, carer or driver does not give automatic access. Checking her identity confirms who she is, not that he agrees to disclosure. Recording the consent, or its absence, in the notes protects both patient and clinician."}, {"id": 1229, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Reviewing the morning, you find the assistant mobilised Mr Okonkwo without having seen the bed rest instruction, and he nearly fell. He is now stable. No harm has resulted and he is in no distress. What should the physiotherapist do about this event?", "options": {"A": "Document the near-fall in the clinical notes only, since incident systems are reserved for harm events", "B": "Email the ward manager asking for the assistant to be moved to another area pending investigation", "C": "Speak privately with the assistant about the error and keep it informal, as nobody was harmed", "D": "Complete an incident report in the hospital system and discuss the handover gap with the team"}, "cluster_id": "CHS68", "cluster_text": "Chidi Okonkwo, 52, warehouse supervisor, day 3 after right total knee replacement, BMI 34. A prophylactic anticoagulant is charted. Handover notes say the right calf is tender and swollen, an ultrasound is booked for this afternoon, and the nurse has written bed rest until the scan. This morning an allied health assistant, who had not seen the note, walked him to the bathroom and he nearly fell when the knee gave way. Now: right calf 3 cm larger than the left, warm and tender to palpation. HR 92, BP 126/80, RR 20, SpO2 96 percent on room air. No chest pain or breathlessness reported. Knee flexion 70 degrees, wound dry.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Complete an incident report in the hospital system and discuss the handover gap with the team): Near misses are reported through the incident system so the hospital can fix the communication gap that caused them, whether or not anyone was hurt. A private word or a note in the chart does not feed organisational learning. Reassigning an individual before any review focuses on blame rather than the system fault."}, {"id": 1230, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Okonkwo's right calf is swollen, warm and tender after knee surgery. He has no chest symptoms now but you are planning his later sessions. Which additional history or finding would most strongly raise concern that a clot has already travelled to his lungs?", "options": {"A": "Increasing calf swelling and warmth with a low grade temperature and rising inflammatory markers", "B": "Sudden pleuritic chest pain with new breathlessness and a rising heart rate", "C": "Sudden severe pain behind the knee with a palpable cord and new ankle swelling", "D": "Lightheadedness on sitting up with a falling blood pressure and pale cool skin"}, "cluster_id": "CHS68", "cluster_text": "Chidi Okonkwo, 52, warehouse supervisor, day 3 after right total knee replacement, BMI 34. A prophylactic anticoagulant is charted. Handover notes say the right calf is tender and swollen, an ultrasound is booked for this afternoon, and the nurse has written bed rest until the scan. This morning an allied health assistant, who had not seen the note, walked him to the bathroom and he nearly fell when the knee gave way. Now: right calf 3 cm larger than the left, warm and tender to palpation. HR 92, BP 126/80, RR 20, SpO2 96 percent on room air. No chest pain or breathlessness reported. Knee flexion 70 degrees, wound dry.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Sudden pleuritic chest pain with new breathlessness and a rising heart rate): Abrupt pleuritic chest pain with new breathlessness and a rising heart rate is the typical pattern of a clot reaching the lungs. Increasing calf swelling with warmth and fever suggests an extending clot or infection in the leg rather than embolism. A painful cord behind the knee points to local venous or cyst problems, and lightheadedness with falling pressure and pale skin points to blood loss or dehydration."}, {"id": 1231, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "The ultrasound has not yet been done and the calf is swollen and tender. Mr Okonkwo asks to walk to the chair because he is bored and wants to move. What is the most appropriate physiotherapy management until a clot has been excluded?", "options": {"A": "Perform knee range exercises in bed with the leg elevated, then walk after one hour", "B": "Apply firm calf massage and a compression bandage to reduce the swelling before the scan", "C": "Walk him with a frame to the chair, since early mobilisation lowers further clot risk", "D": "Withhold walking, keep him resting in bed and confirm the plan with the medical team"}, "cluster_id": "CHS68", "cluster_text": "Chidi Okonkwo, 52, warehouse supervisor, day 3 after right total knee replacement, BMI 34. A prophylactic anticoagulant is charted. Handover notes say the right calf is tender and swollen, an ultrasound is booked for this afternoon, and the nurse has written bed rest until the scan. This morning an allied health assistant, who had not seen the note, walked him to the bathroom and he nearly fell when the knee gave way. Now: right calf 3 cm larger than the left, warm and tender to palpation. HR 92, BP 126/80, RR 20, SpO2 96 percent on room air. No chest pain or breathlessness reported. Knee flexion 70 degrees, wound dry.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Withhold walking, keep him resting in bed and confirm the plan with the medical team): While a deep vein thrombosis is suspected, walking and active leg work could dislodge a clot, so activity is held until the scan result is known and the plan is agreed with the team. Massage and firm compression are inappropriate over a possible clot. Early mobilisation is the usual goal after surgery but only once thrombosis is excluded."}, {"id": 1232, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "While you assist upper limb exercises in bed, Mr Okonkwo suddenly reports sharp right-sided chest pain and breathlessness. RR is 30, HR 118, SpO2 88 percent on room air and BP 108/70. He looks frightened. What is the most appropriate immediate action?", "options": {"A": "Stop, sit him upright, stay with him and ask the nurse to arrange urgent medical review", "B": "Stop, apply oxygen as charted, stay with him and recheck saturation in five minutes first", "C": "Stop, position him upright with support, stay with him and activate the medical emergency response", "D": "Stop, lie him flat with his legs raised, stay with him and call for help"}, "cluster_id": "CHS68", "cluster_text": "Chidi Okonkwo, 52, warehouse supervisor, day 3 after right total knee replacement, BMI 34. A prophylactic anticoagulant is charted. Handover notes say the right calf is tender and swollen, an ultrasound is booked for this afternoon, and the nurse has written bed rest until the scan. This morning an allied health assistant, who had not seen the note, walked him to the bathroom and he nearly fell when the knee gave way. Now: right calf 3 cm larger than the left, warm and tender to palpation. HR 92, BP 126/80, RR 20, SpO2 96 percent on room air. No chest pain or breathlessness reported. Knee flexion 70 degrees, wound dry.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop, position him upright with support, stay with him and activate the medical emergency response): Sudden pleuritic pain, breathlessness, tachycardia and a saturation of 88 percent in a patient with a suspected leg clot indicate probable pulmonary embolism. He needs support in an upright position and an immediate emergency response activated by the physiotherapist while staying with him. Asking the nurse to arrange review, or waiting five minutes after oxygen, delays the response. Lying him flat with legs raised worsens his breathing and still does not replace the emergency call."}, {"id": 1233, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Marsh is 12 days after a non-operatively managed surgical neck fracture and wears a sling. The surgeon permits pendulum exercises and distal joint movement only at this stage. Which exercise programme is most appropriate and safe for her now?", "options": {"A": "Gentle pendulums plus elbow, wrist and hand exercises, wearing the sling between sessions", "B": "Isometric deltoid and rotator cuff contractions with the arm at the side to maintain muscle bulk", "C": "Passive shoulder abduction by the therapist to ninety degrees daily, to limit capsular tightness and stiffness", "D": "Active-assisted shoulder flexion with a wand to prevent stiffness, starting at table height within pain tolerance"}, "cluster_id": "CHS69", "cluster_text": "Mrs Karen Marsh, 79, a retired nurse, is in a subacute rehabilitation unit 12 days after a fall at home that caused a left two-part surgical neck fracture of the proximal humerus, managed without surgery in a sling. The orthopaedic plan: sling for 3 weeks, pendulums and elbow, wrist and hand movement now, passive then active-assisted shoulder movement from week 3 to 4 only after radiological review, and no resisted work until union. She lives alone in a two-storey house, has untreated osteoporosis, mild cognitive decline, uses a walking stick in her right hand and has had two falls this year. She needs help with dressing, is independent for toileting, pain is 4/10 at rest, and elbow movement is 0 to 130 degrees. She has not had a My Aged Care assessment and asks how services at home will be paid for.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Gentle pendulums plus elbow, wrist and hand exercises, wearing the sling between sessions): Pendulums and distal joint movement maintain mobility without stressing the healing surgical neck fracture, and the sling stays on between sessions. Active-assisted elevation, passive abduction and isometric cuff work all load or stress the fracture before the surgeon has allowed it."}, {"id": 1234, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Marsh is ready for discharge in about a week and will need further physiotherapy at home plus help with dressing and meals. She has not had a My Aged Care assessment. What is the most appropriate action to help her access services?", "options": {"A": "Arrange a My Aged Care referral so she can be assessed for subsidised home supports", "B": "Advise her that Medicare will fully cover ongoing home physiotherapy once her GP writes a referral", "C": "Lodge an NDIS access request for her, since the scheme funds home rehabilitation after injury", "D": "Assume private health cover will fund home visits and ask the unit to lodge the claim"}, "cluster_id": "CHS69", "cluster_text": "Mrs Karen Marsh, 79, a retired nurse, is in a subacute rehabilitation unit 12 days after a fall at home that caused a left two-part surgical neck fracture of the proximal humerus, managed without surgery in a sling. The orthopaedic plan: sling for 3 weeks, pendulums and elbow, wrist and hand movement now, passive then active-assisted shoulder movement from week 3 to 4 only after radiological review, and no resisted work until union. She lives alone in a two-storey house, has untreated osteoporosis, mild cognitive decline, uses a walking stick in her right hand and has had two falls this year. She needs help with dressing, is independent for toileting, pain is 4/10 at rest, and elbow movement is 0 to 130 degrees. She has not had a My Aged Care assessment and asks how services at home will be paid for.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Arrange a My Aged Care referral so she can be assessed for subsidised home supports): A My Aged Care referral starts assessment for subsidised home supports, which suits a 79-year-old needing help with dressing and meals. NDIS access applies to people who are under 65 when they apply, Medicare does not fund ongoing home physiotherapy in this way, and private cover cannot be assumed."}, {"id": 1235, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "On day 16 Mrs Marsh's left forearm is more swollen, her hand is cool and pale, and she reports pins and needles and severe pain unrelieved by analgesia, although the sling fits correctly. What should the treating physiotherapist do first in this unit?", "options": {"A": "Loosen the sling, elevate the forearm and ask nursing staff to recheck circulation in an hour", "B": "Check colour, pulse and sensation, then document and report at the next medical ward round", "C": "Check colour, pulse and sensation, then urgently escalate to the on-call medical officer", "D": "Request analgesia from nursing staff and reassess pain and hand circulation after the dose takes effect"}, "cluster_id": "CHS69", "cluster_text": "Mrs Karen Marsh, 79, a retired nurse, is in a subacute rehabilitation unit 12 days after a fall at home that caused a left two-part surgical neck fracture of the proximal humerus, managed without surgery in a sling. The orthopaedic plan: sling for 3 weeks, pendulums and elbow, wrist and hand movement now, passive then active-assisted shoulder movement from week 3 to 4 only after radiological review, and no resisted work until union. She lives alone in a two-storey house, has untreated osteoporosis, mild cognitive decline, uses a walking stick in her right hand and has had two falls this year. She needs help with dressing, is independent for toileting, pain is 4/10 at rest, and elbow movement is 0 to 130 degrees. She has not had a My Aged Care assessment and asks how services at home will be paid for.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Check colour, pulse and sensation, then urgently escalate to the on-call medical officer): A cool, pale, swollen hand with pins and needles and pain that analgesia has not eased suggests vascular compromise or compartment syndrome, which threatens the limb. Quick checks of colour, pulse and sensation should be followed by urgent medical escalation. Loosening the sling and waiting an hour, reporting at a later round, or waiting for more analgesia to work all allow ischaemia to continue."}, {"id": 1236, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Marsh, aged 79 and living alone, sustained this fracture from a standing-height fall and has untreated osteoporosis. Which action is within the physiotherapist's role and most likely to reduce her risk of another fragility fracture in the longer term?", "options": {"A": "Refer her directly for a bone density scan and defer all exercise until results return", "B": "Introduce high-impact loading through the injured arm early to stimulate bone remodelling and strength", "C": "Start a calcium and vitamin D supplement at a dose you select and monitor adherence", "D": "Advise GP review of her bone health and begin balance and falls prevention training"}, "cluster_id": "CHS69", "cluster_text": "Mrs Karen Marsh, 79, a retired nurse, is in a subacute rehabilitation unit 12 days after a fall at home that caused a left two-part surgical neck fracture of the proximal humerus, managed without surgery in a sling. The orthopaedic plan: sling for 3 weeks, pendulums and elbow, wrist and hand movement now, passive then active-assisted shoulder movement from week 3 to 4 only after radiological review, and no resisted work until union. She lives alone in a two-storey house, has untreated osteoporosis, mild cognitive decline, uses a walking stick in her right hand and has had two falls this year. She needs help with dressing, is independent for toileting, pain is 4/10 at rest, and elbow movement is 0 to 130 degrees. She has not had a My Aged Care assessment and asks how services at home will be paid for.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Advise GP review of her bone health and begin balance and falls prevention training): Recommending GP review of her bone health and delivering balance and falls training are within physiotherapy scope and address both bone fragility and fall risk. Selecting supplement doses is outside scope, deferring exercise removes the main fall-prevention benefit, and loading the injured arm early endangers the fracture."}, {"id": 1237, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Ms Raghavan has worst fatigue from about 2 pm and finishes her school day exhausted, then does housework and evening errands. Her neurologist attributes this to multiple sclerosis fatigue rather than relapse. She asks how to plan her routine. Which energy management approach is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Limit exercise to gentle stretching only, to avoid triggering fatigue or a relapse", "B": "Push through fatigue on good days to bank activity, then rest on bad days", "C": "Concentrate demanding tasks into one long morning block and rest only once exhausted in the afternoon", "D": "Space demanding tasks through the day with short planned rests before fatigue builds"}, "cluster_id": "CHS70", "cluster_text": "Priya Raghavan, 38, primary school teacher with relapsing remitting multiple sclerosis, diagnosed seven years ago and stable on disease modifying treatment managed by her neurologist. Community physiotherapy home visit. Her fatigue is worst from about 2 pm. Last week she fell in the bathroom after a long hot shower, bruising her hip, with no head strike. Lower limb strength 4/5 bilaterally, mild right foot drop, no new neurological signs, normal vital signs. Six minute walk test 280 metres with one rest; Borg fatigue rating rose from 2 to 6. Her home has a step in shower and a long hallway. She is medically cleared to exercise and wants to keep working full time.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Space demanding tasks through the day with short planned rests before fatigue builds): Pacing means distributing activity and taking planned rests before fatigue becomes severe, which keeps energy steadier across the day. Boom and bust patterns and long single blocks of effort lead to deeper crashes. Exercise does not cause relapse, so cutting it back to stretching removes a proven way to reduce fatigue."}, {"id": 1238, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "After a hot shower on Tuesday Ms Raghavan's legs felt weak and she had blurred vision that settled within an hour of cooling. She now avoids exercise altogether. What is the most appropriate explanation and advice to give her about exercise?", "options": {"A": "Heat symptoms signal lasting nerve damage, so she should avoid exercise that raises body temperature", "B": "Symptoms show a new relapse, so she should stop exercise until a neurologist reviews her", "C": "Heat can transiently worsen symptoms, so exercise in cool settings and use cooling strategies", "D": "Her symptoms reflect dehydration, so she should drink more and exercise as before"}, "cluster_id": "CHS70", "cluster_text": "Priya Raghavan, 38, primary school teacher with relapsing remitting multiple sclerosis, diagnosed seven years ago and stable on disease modifying treatment managed by her neurologist. Community physiotherapy home visit. Her fatigue is worst from about 2 pm. Last week she fell in the bathroom after a long hot shower, bruising her hip, with no head strike. Lower limb strength 4/5 bilaterally, mild right foot drop, no new neurological signs, normal vital signs. Six minute walk test 280 metres with one rest; Borg fatigue rating rose from 2 to 6. Her home has a step in shower and a long hallway. She is medically cleared to exercise and wants to keep working full time.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Heat can transiently worsen symptoms, so exercise in cool settings and use cooling strategies): Temporary worsening of multiple sclerosis symptoms with a rise in body temperature settles when she cools and is not a relapse or new damage. Exercising in a cool environment with cooling aids lets her stay active safely. Treating it as dehydration ignores the heat trigger, and stopping exercise removes the benefits she needs."}, {"id": 1239, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Ms Raghavan walks 280 metres in the six minute walk test, with her Borg fatigue rating rising from 2 to 6 and one rest taken. She is medically cleared to exercise. How should her aerobic exercise be started in the community programme?", "options": {"A": "Start with continuous thirty minute sessions at a high intensity to build capacity quickly", "B": "Begin with short moderate bouts and rests, then progress gradually, watching next day fatigue", "C": "Avoid aerobic exercise until fatigue resolves, using only passive stretching meanwhile to protect energy", "D": "Begin with moderate bouts and progress duration weekly on a fixed schedule set in advance"}, "cluster_id": "CHS70", "cluster_text": "Priya Raghavan, 38, primary school teacher with relapsing remitting multiple sclerosis, diagnosed seven years ago and stable on disease modifying treatment managed by her neurologist. Community physiotherapy home visit. Her fatigue is worst from about 2 pm. Last week she fell in the bathroom after a long hot shower, bruising her hip, with no head strike. Lower limb strength 4/5 bilaterally, mild right foot drop, no new neurological signs, normal vital signs. Six minute walk test 280 metres with one rest; Borg fatigue rating rose from 2 to 6. Her home has a step in shower and a long hallway. She is medically cleared to exercise and wants to keep working full time.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Begin with short moderate bouts and rests, then progress gradually, watching next day fatigue): Starting with short moderate bouts and rest breaks, then progressing according to her response, suits fatigue limited exercise capacity. A fixed schedule ignores how she responds, and high intensity continuous work risks a fatigue crash. Waiting for fatigue to resolve is unrealistic since exercise is part of its management."}, {"id": 1240, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "During the home visit Ms Raghavan becomes dizzy walking down the hallway and lowers herself to the floor with the physiotherapist guarding. She is unhurt and recovers after cooling and rest. What is the most appropriate action regarding the event once the visit ends?", "options": {"A": "Notify only her neurologist by letter, since the dizziness was probably caused by her multiple sclerosis", "B": "Lodge an incident report, inform her GP, and review her falls and heat risk plan", "C": "Wait to see whether she reports pain in coming days, then lodge a report", "D": "Record it in her clinical notes only, because no injury occurred and no report is needed"}, "cluster_id": "CHS70", "cluster_text": "Priya Raghavan, 38, primary school teacher with relapsing remitting multiple sclerosis, diagnosed seven years ago and stable on disease modifying treatment managed by her neurologist. Community physiotherapy home visit. Her fatigue is worst from about 2 pm. Last week she fell in the bathroom after a long hot shower, bruising her hip, with no head strike. Lower limb strength 4/5 bilaterally, mild right foot drop, no new neurological signs, normal vital signs. Six minute walk test 280 metres with one rest; Borg fatigue rating rose from 2 to 6. Her home has a step in shower and a long hallway. She is medically cleared to exercise and wants to keep working full time.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Lodge an incident report, inform her GP, and review her falls and heat risk plan): A fall or controlled lowering to the floor during a visit is reportable even without injury, because reporting allows the service to learn and reduce risk. The GP should know about the dizziness, and her risk plan needs updating. Waiting for harm or limiting the matter to the notes weakens the safety process."}, {"id": 1241, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mei is 27 and has had three falls in a year, with knee pain after about 300 metres. Observation shows crouch gait with weak plantarflexors and hip extensors, short hamstrings and increasing fatigue. Which problem is most likely contributing to her knee pain and falls?", "options": {"A": "Hip adductor tightness narrowing her base of support, causing scissoring that destabilises her stance", "B": "Excessive femoral anteversion producing toe in, which shifts load onto the patellofemoral joint and raises falls", "C": "Bilateral hip abductor weakness causing pelvic drop in stance, which loads the knee and increases falls", "D": "Weak plantarflexors and short hamstrings failing to control the knee in stance, raising joint load"}, "cluster_id": "CHS71", "cluster_text": "Mei Lin Chen, 27, an administrative assistant, has spastic diplegic cerebral palsy, GMFCS level II. She walks independently indoors and outdoors on level ground and uses a stick on uneven surfaces. Over twelve months she has had three falls, and has increasing knee pain and fatigue after walking more than 300 metres. Observation shows crouch gait with excessive hip and knee flexion in stance, reduced push off and bilateral toe in. Popliteal angle is 60 degrees; passive ankle dorsiflexion with the knee extended is minus 5 degrees. Strength: hip extensors 3 plus, quadriceps 4, plantarflexors 3. Modified Ashworth 1 plus in hamstrings. Six minute walk distance is 280 metres. She has found an online article reporting a small study of a whole body vibration device with 12 participants and no comparison group, and asks whether she should buy one.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Weak plantarflexors and short hamstrings failing to control the knee in stance, raising joint load): In crouch gait, weak plantarflexors and hip extensors cannot control the tibia or extend the knee, and short hamstrings hold it flexed, which raises patellofemoral and quadriceps load and fall risk. Toe in and abductor weakness can occur in cerebral palsy but do not explain the documented crouch, and no scissoring or abductor deficit is recorded. Her quadriceps are strong, so they are not the limiting problem."}, {"id": 1242, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mei's decline in walking capacity is linked to weakness and tightness rather than a new neurological event, and she has been cleared for exercise by her general practitioner. Which exercise approach is most appropriate to address her reduced walking capacity?", "options": {"A": "Progressive strengthening of hip extensors and plantarflexors, graded walking practice and hamstring stretching", "B": "Hamstring and calf stretching with a night splint, plus low load walking practice only", "C": "Moderate aerobic cycling and balance training, with strengthening deferred until her falls have stopped", "D": "Whole body vibration sessions three times weekly alongside usual walking, as the small study suggests"}, "cluster_id": "CHS71", "cluster_text": "Mei Lin Chen, 27, an administrative assistant, has spastic diplegic cerebral palsy, GMFCS level II. She walks independently indoors and outdoors on level ground and uses a stick on uneven surfaces. Over twelve months she has had three falls, and has increasing knee pain and fatigue after walking more than 300 metres. Observation shows crouch gait with excessive hip and knee flexion in stance, reduced push off and bilateral toe in. Popliteal angle is 60 degrees; passive ankle dorsiflexion with the knee extended is minus 5 degrees. Strength: hip extensors 3 plus, quadriceps 4, plantarflexors 3. Modified Ashworth 1 plus in hamstrings. Six minute walk distance is 280 metres. She has found an online article reporting a small study of a whole body vibration device with 12 participants and no comparison group, and asks whether she should buy one.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Progressive strengthening of hip extensors and plantarflexors, graded walking practice and hamstring stretching): Progressive strengthening of the weak extensors and plantarflexors, task specific walking and stretching of shortened muscles targets each of her documented impairments. Stretching and splinting with only light walking cannot rebuild weak muscle. Deferring strengthening until falls stop leaves the cause of the falls untreated, and a device backed by one small study with no comparison group is not a sound basis for her programme."}, {"id": 1243, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "A trial of a hinged ankle foot orthosis allowing free dorsiflexion increases Mei's crouch, with more knee flexion in mid stance on observation and more knee pain during the walk. Which adjustment is the best next step to discuss with the orthotist?", "options": {"A": "A solid or ground reaction ankle foot orthosis to control tibial progression and knee flexion", "B": "A more flexible posterior leaf spring design that permits greater ankle motion throughout the stance phase", "C": "Removing the orthosis and relying on a stick on all surfaces to help with stability", "D": "A knee ankle foot orthosis locked in extension for all community walking and daily tasks"}, "cluster_id": "CHS71", "cluster_text": "Mei Lin Chen, 27, an administrative assistant, has spastic diplegic cerebral palsy, GMFCS level II. She walks independently indoors and outdoors on level ground and uses a stick on uneven surfaces. Over twelve months she has had three falls, and has increasing knee pain and fatigue after walking more than 300 metres. Observation shows crouch gait with excessive hip and knee flexion in stance, reduced push off and bilateral toe in. Popliteal angle is 60 degrees; passive ankle dorsiflexion with the knee extended is minus 5 degrees. Strength: hip extensors 3 plus, quadriceps 4, plantarflexors 3. Modified Ashworth 1 plus in hamstrings. Six minute walk distance is 280 metres. She has found an online article reporting a small study of a whole body vibration device with 12 participants and no comparison group, and asks whether she should buy one.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A solid or ground reaction ankle foot orthosis to control tibial progression and knee flexion): Allowing free dorsiflexion lets the tibia advance too far, worsening crouch. A solid or ground reaction orthosis restrains the shank and encourages knee extension in stance. A more flexible design repeats the problem, and a locked knee device is excessive for an independent walker."}, {"id": 1244, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mei asks whether to buy a whole body vibration device after reading an online article about a study of 12 participants with no comparison group. She says it sounds promising. What is the most appropriate response from the treating physiotherapist?", "options": {"A": "Suggest asking the manufacturer for further trial data first, as they hold the most complete evidence", "B": "Support the purchase, as small positive studies show promise and the device seems low risk", "C": "Dismiss the article because uncontrolled studies cannot inform practice, and continue her current programme unchanged", "D": "Explain the limits of that study design and review higher quality trials before recommending it"}, "cluster_id": "CHS71", "cluster_text": "Mei Lin Chen, 27, an administrative assistant, has spastic diplegic cerebral palsy, GMFCS level II. She walks independently indoors and outdoors on level ground and uses a stick on uneven surfaces. Over twelve months she has had three falls, and has increasing knee pain and fatigue after walking more than 300 metres. Observation shows crouch gait with excessive hip and knee flexion in stance, reduced push off and bilateral toe in. Popliteal angle is 60 degrees; passive ankle dorsiflexion with the knee extended is minus 5 degrees. Strength: hip extensors 3 plus, quadriceps 4, plantarflexors 3. Modified Ashworth 1 plus in hamstrings. Six minute walk distance is 280 metres. She has found an online article reporting a small study of a whole body vibration device with 12 participants and no comparison group, and asks whether she should buy one.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain the limits of that study design and review higher quality trials before recommending it): A small uncontrolled study gives weak evidence, so the sensible step is to look for systematic reviews or controlled trials and share the appraisal with her. Endorsing on hope ignores bias, dismissing all weak evidence closes the question, and manufacturer data carries a conflict of interest."}, {"id": 1245, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Andrew has been off work for five weeks. When planning his rehabilitation, which factor in his presentation is the strongest predictor of delayed recovery and prolonged work absence that the physiotherapist should address? Consider the whole assessment, not only his physical impairments.", "options": {"A": "His age of forty-five years and manual occupation, since these fixed factors determine recovery time", "B": "Referral of pain into the left posterior thigh, because leg symptoms predict a poor outcome", "C": "His belief that lifting will damage his discs, with high fear of re-injury on screening", "D": "Restricted lumbar flexion to mid shin level, which limits his ability to bend at work"}, "cluster_id": "CHS72", "cluster_text": "Andrew Nguyen, 45, warehouse storeperson, felt immediate low back pain five weeks ago while lifting a pallet of boxes. His workers compensation claim is accepted for eight approved physiotherapy sessions, and the insurer's case manager has said further sessions need prior approval with a progress report. Pain is 5 out of 10, centred in the lower back with some left posterior thigh pain, worse with bending and lifting. Examination: lumbar flexion to mid shin limited by pain, extension full, left straight leg raise 60 degrees reproducing thigh pain, reflexes, power and sensation normal. No red flags. He has had no imaging. He scores high on a screening questionnaire for risk of persistent disability, and tells you he believes any lifting will damage his discs. He has been off work since the injury. His GP certificate supports suitable duties, and his employer has offered a modified role.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (His belief that lifting will damage his discs, with high fear of re-injury on screening): Unhelpful beliefs and fear of movement are the strongest modifiable predictors of persistent disability and work absence, and they are the ones the physiotherapist can change through education and graded exposure. Reduced flexion and thigh pain reflect impairment that usually improves, and age and occupation cannot be changed."}, {"id": 1246, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Andrew's neurological examination is normal, pain is 5 out of 10 and he avoids bending and lifting at home. Imaging has not been requested and there are no red flags. Which approach is most appropriate to begin his rehabilitation in the first session?", "options": {"A": "Request an MRI to rule out disc injury and keep him resting until the results return", "B": "Use passive mobilisation and heat for two weeks first, adding exercise once pain has settled", "C": "Provide a lumbar brace and advise several days of relative rest to protect the injured disc", "D": "Reassure about prognosis, encourage staying active and start graded movement and activity exercises"}, "cluster_id": "CHS72", "cluster_text": "Andrew Nguyen, 45, warehouse storeperson, felt immediate low back pain five weeks ago while lifting a pallet of boxes. His workers compensation claim is accepted for eight approved physiotherapy sessions, and the insurer's case manager has said further sessions need prior approval with a progress report. Pain is 5 out of 10, centred in the lower back with some left posterior thigh pain, worse with bending and lifting. Examination: lumbar flexion to mid shin limited by pain, extension full, left straight leg raise 60 degrees reproducing thigh pain, reflexes, power and sensation normal. No red flags. He has had no imaging. He scores high on a screening questionnaire for risk of persistent disability, and tells you he believes any lifting will damage his discs. He has been off work since the injury. His GP certificate supports suitable duties, and his employer has offered a modified role.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reassure about prognosis, encourage staying active and start graded movement and activity exercises): With no red flags and normal neurology, current guidance supports reassurance, advice to remain active and graded exercise. Imaging without indication reinforces a structural fear, and prolonged rest or bracing builds disability. Passive treatment first delays the active loading he needs."}, {"id": 1247, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "The employer offers four hours a day of bench sorting with a 5 kg lift limit, which the GP certificate supports. Andrew fears this will aggravate his back and asks to wait until he is pain free. What advice is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Wait until pain has settled, then return to full duties to prevent flare-ups from partial work", "B": "Begin the supported duties, progress hours gradually and review his symptom response at each visit", "C": "Support the offer only if the insurer approves in writing, otherwise keep him off work", "D": "Return him to pre-injury duties straight away with a back brace so he keeps his job"}, "cluster_id": "CHS72", "cluster_text": "Andrew Nguyen, 45, warehouse storeperson, felt immediate low back pain five weeks ago while lifting a pallet of boxes. His workers compensation claim is accepted for eight approved physiotherapy sessions, and the insurer's case manager has said further sessions need prior approval with a progress report. Pain is 5 out of 10, centred in the lower back with some left posterior thigh pain, worse with bending and lifting. Examination: lumbar flexion to mid shin limited by pain, extension full, left straight leg raise 60 degrees reproducing thigh pain, reflexes, power and sensation normal. No red flags. He has had no imaging. He scores high on a screening questionnaire for risk of persistent disability, and tells you he believes any lifting will damage his discs. He has been off work since the injury. His GP certificate supports suitable duties, and his employer has offered a modified role.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Begin the supported duties, progress hours gradually and review his symptom response at each visit): Early graded return with modified duties, agreed with the employer and monitored, is associated with better outcomes than waiting for full pain relief. Delaying until he is pain free prolongs absence, and an immediate return to full duties exceeds his capacity. The treating GP certificate already authorises suitable duties, so an insurer sign-off is not a precondition for starting."}, {"id": 1248, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Andrew has used his eight approved sessions and still needs supervised graded activity. The insurer's case manager says further treatment requires prior approval supported by a progress report. What should the physiotherapist do to keep his care safe and continuous?", "options": {"A": "Send a progress report with outcome measures and goals, requesting approval for further sessions", "B": "Phone Andrew's employer to request extra sessions, because they hold the compensation policy", "C": "Continue treatment and bill the insurer afterwards, since the claim is already accepted for his injury", "D": "Discharge him with a home program and advise claiming further sessions through Medicare instead"}, "cluster_id": "CHS72", "cluster_text": "Andrew Nguyen, 45, warehouse storeperson, felt immediate low back pain five weeks ago while lifting a pallet of boxes. His workers compensation claim is accepted for eight approved physiotherapy sessions, and the insurer's case manager has said further sessions need prior approval with a progress report. Pain is 5 out of 10, centred in the lower back with some left posterior thigh pain, worse with bending and lifting. Examination: lumbar flexion to mid shin limited by pain, extension full, left straight leg raise 60 degrees reproducing thigh pain, reflexes, power and sensation normal. No red flags. He has had no imaging. He scores high on a screening questionnaire for risk of persistent disability, and tells you he believes any lifting will damage his discs. He has been off work since the injury. His GP certificate supports suitable duties, and his employer has offered a modified role.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Send a progress report with outcome measures and goals, requesting approval for further sessions): The scheme sets the rule, so the practitioner supplies an objective progress report with measures and goals to justify more sessions before delivering them. Treating first risks unpaid services. Medicare is not the funder for an accepted compensable claim, and the employer does not authorise treatment, the insurer does."}, {"id": 1249, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Manu's GP spirometry shows a post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC ratio of 0.62. He has a long smoking history, a chronic cough and exertional breathlessness. His symptoms are persistent and not limited to certain times of the day. What does this result most strongly indicate?", "options": {"A": "Persistent airflow limitation that remains after bronchodilator, consistent with COPD in this clinical context", "B": "A restrictive defect, because the reduced ratio reflects small lung volumes and stiff lung tissue", "C": "Normal lung function for a man his age, since the ratio falls naturally with ageing", "D": "Reversible airflow limitation consistent with asthma, as the ratio was taken after bronchodilator"}, "cluster_id": "CHS73", "cluster_text": "Manu Pouono, 52, a bricklayer, is referred by his GP to community physiotherapy for breathlessness on exertion. He smokes 15 cigarettes a day (about 30 pack years) and has a chronic morning cough with small amounts of white sputum. His breathlessness is mMRC grade 2 and his six minute walk distance today is 380 metres. Resting HR 78, BP 134/86, RR 16, SpO2 94 percent on room air, with scattered wheeze and no crackles. His GP has sent spirometry results. He says he has thought about quitting but is worried about gaining weight and being teased by his workmates, and he has not decided. The community health centre has limited physiotherapy hours, and he is booked for 30 minutes at 2 pm.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Persistent airflow limitation that remains after bronchodilator, consistent with COPD in this clinical context): A post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC below 0.7 shows airflow limitation that persists despite treatment, and with his smoking history and symptoms this is consistent with COPD. In asthma the ratio would largely normalise after bronchodilator, and restrictive disease keeps the ratio normal or high because both volumes fall together."}, {"id": 1250, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Before Manu's 2 pm appointment the physiotherapist has a voicemail from another client reporting new chest pain and breathlessness, an overdue discharge letter, a routine review request and a new referral to triage. How should the physiotherapist prioritise these tasks?", "options": {"A": "Keep Manu's appointment on time for continuity, then return the voicemail after the clinic day", "B": "Return the chest pain call first and arrange urgent care, then triage the new referral", "C": "Text the caller to see her GP today, then complete the discharge letter before Manu arrives", "D": "Delegate the voicemail to reception to relay a safety message, then see Manu on time"}, "cluster_id": "CHS73", "cluster_text": "Manu Pouono, 52, a bricklayer, is referred by his GP to community physiotherapy for breathlessness on exertion. He smokes 15 cigarettes a day (about 30 pack years) and has a chronic morning cough with small amounts of white sputum. His breathlessness is mMRC grade 2 and his six minute walk distance today is 380 metres. Resting HR 78, BP 134/86, RR 16, SpO2 94 percent on room air, with scattered wheeze and no crackles. His GP has sent spirometry results. He says he has thought about quitting but is worried about gaining weight and being teased by his workmates, and he has not decided. The community health centre has limited physiotherapy hours, and he is booked for 30 minutes at 2 pm.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Return the chest pain call first and arrange urgent care, then triage the new referral): A caller reporting new chest pain and breathlessness may have a cardiac or pulmonary emergency, so the clinician who holds the message must respond personally and arrange urgent care first. Keeping the booked appointment, sending a text, or passing a safety message through reception all leave the risk unassessed by someone qualified. The letter, the review request and the referral can each wait a few minutes without harm."}, {"id": 1251, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Manu says he has thought about quitting but worries about weight gain and teasing from workmates, and has not decided. The physiotherapist has about ten minutes left in the appointment for this topic and wants to use them well. What is the most appropriate approach?", "options": {"A": "Defer discussing smoking until he has attended the exercise programme consistently and built trust with staff", "B": "Explore his concerns without pressure, give brief advice, and offer Quitline and GP support", "C": "Advise him to quit today and set a quit date, as delay risks further lung decline", "D": "Give written information on the harms of smoking and leave the decision with him"}, "cluster_id": "CHS73", "cluster_text": "Manu Pouono, 52, a bricklayer, is referred by his GP to community physiotherapy for breathlessness on exertion. He smokes 15 cigarettes a day (about 30 pack years) and has a chronic morning cough with small amounts of white sputum. His breathlessness is mMRC grade 2 and his six minute walk distance today is 380 metres. Resting HR 78, BP 134/86, RR 16, SpO2 94 percent on room air, with scattered wheeze and no crackles. His GP has sent spirometry results. He says he has thought about quitting but is worried about gaining weight and being teased by his workmates, and he has not decided. The community health centre has limited physiotherapy hours, and he is booked for 30 minutes at 2 pm.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Explore his concerns without pressure, give brief advice, and offer Quitline and GP support): Manu is ambivalent, so a non-judgemental exploration of his worries about weight and teasing, with brief advice and referral options, respects his readiness to change. Pushing a quit date can create resistance, deferring the topic wastes a good opportunity, and leaflets alone rarely change behaviour."}, {"id": 1252, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Manu's six minute walk test ended with SpO2 falling from 94 to 91 percent and Borg breathlessness of 5. He works physically and wants to return to full duties. He asks to begin exercise. Which prescription is most appropriate to start?", "options": {"A": "Supervised moderate intensity walking and strength training, keeping breathlessness around 3 to 4 on Borg", "B": "High intensity interval cycling to severe breathlessness, since greater loads give faster gains in capacity", "C": "A home programme of breathing exercises, deferring aerobic training until he has stopped smoking successfully", "D": "Low intensity walking, stopping at the first breathlessness, because exertional symptoms signal ongoing lung damage"}, "cluster_id": "CHS73", "cluster_text": "Manu Pouono, 52, a bricklayer, is referred by his GP to community physiotherapy for breathlessness on exertion. He smokes 15 cigarettes a day (about 30 pack years) and has a chronic morning cough with small amounts of white sputum. His breathlessness is mMRC grade 2 and his six minute walk distance today is 380 metres. Resting HR 78, BP 134/86, RR 16, SpO2 94 percent on room air, with scattered wheeze and no crackles. His GP has sent spirometry results. He says he has thought about quitting but is worried about gaining weight and being teased by his workmates, and he has not decided. The community health centre has limited physiotherapy hours, and he is booked for 30 minutes at 2 pm.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Supervised moderate intensity walking and strength training, keeping breathlessness around 3 to 4 on Borg): Pulmonary rehabilitation in COPD uses supervised aerobic and strength training at moderate intensity, with breathlessness around 3 to 4, progressed over time. Avoiding exertion does not train him, severe breathlessness is unnecessary and poorly tolerated, and exercise should not wait for smoking cessation."}, {"id": 1253, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mei Ling's hand symptoms follow the median nerve distribution, Tinel's and Phalen's tests are positive, and her cervical examination is unremarkable. The upper limb neurodynamic test reproduces symptoms. Which interpretation best fits all of these findings at this early stage?", "options": {"A": "Thoracic outlet compression affecting the lower trunk, with symptoms that vary with arm elevation and posture", "B": "Median nerve compression at the carpal tunnel with sensitised nerve, without cervical radicular involvement", "C": "C6 radiculopathy from foraminal narrowing, producing thumb and index symptoms that cervical movements would normally reproduce", "D": "Pronator teres syndrome with forearm median nerve compression, producing night symptoms and a positive Phalen's test"}, "cluster_id": "CHS74", "cluster_text": "Mei Ling Chen, 41, an office administrator and keen badminton player, attends your private practice with six weeks of numbness and tingling in the right thumb, index and middle fingers, worse at night and when holding her phone. There was no trauma. Examination: Phalen's test reproduces tingling within 30 seconds, Tinel's sign at the wrist is positive, thenar bulk is normal, thumb abduction is 5/5, and light touch is reduced in the median nerve distribution of the hand. Cervical rotation is full and pain-free and Spurling's test is negative. The median-bias upper limb neurodynamic test reproduces her hand symptoms and they increase with contralateral neck lateral flexion. Night pain is 4/10. She mentions she knows your landlord socially and wants to repay you for the help.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Median nerve compression at the carpal tunnel with sensitised nerve, without cervical radicular involvement): Symptoms in the median distribution with positive wrist provocation tests and a clear cervical screen point to compression at the carpal tunnel. The neurodynamic test shows the nerve is mechanosensitive, which does not require a root lesion. A lower trunk problem would involve the ring and little fingers, and pronator syndrome typically spares wrist provocation tests and night symptoms."}, {"id": 1254, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mei Ling's symptoms are mild to moderate with no thenar wasting or weakness. She types for much of the day and wakes at night with tingling. What is the most appropriate initial conservative management plan for the physiotherapist to offer?", "options": {"A": "Immediate referral for nerve conduction studies and surgical opinion before any physiotherapy treatment is started", "B": "Aggressive nerve tensioning into full symptom reproduction twice daily, combined with wrist extension stretching", "C": "Neutral wrist night splinting, activity modification and nerve gliding exercises, reviewed after a few weeks", "D": "Cervical traction and mobilisation of the lower cervical spine, followed by review of the hand symptoms"}, "cluster_id": "CHS74", "cluster_text": "Mei Ling Chen, 41, an office administrator and keen badminton player, attends your private practice with six weeks of numbness and tingling in the right thumb, index and middle fingers, worse at night and when holding her phone. There was no trauma. Examination: Phalen's test reproduces tingling within 30 seconds, Tinel's sign at the wrist is positive, thenar bulk is normal, thumb abduction is 5/5, and light touch is reduced in the median nerve distribution of the hand. Cervical rotation is full and pain-free and Spurling's test is negative. The median-bias upper limb neurodynamic test reproduces her hand symptoms and they increase with contralateral neck lateral flexion. Night pain is 4/10. She mentions she knows your landlord socially and wants to repay you for the help.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Neutral wrist night splinting, activity modification and nerve gliding exercises, reviewed after a few weeks): For mild to moderate carpal tunnel syndrome without motor loss, a neutral wrist night splint, load modification and gentle nerve gliding are well supported first line care with planned review. Forceful tensioning and wrist stretching can irritate a sensitised nerve. Her neck is not the source, and surgical referral is reserved for failed conservative care or progressive motor loss."}, {"id": 1255, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "At the third visit, despite splinting and exercise, Mei Ling reports her thumb has become weak when opening jars, and she has dropped two cups at work. Examination now shows thenar wasting and thumb abduction 3/5. How should the physiotherapist proceed?", "options": {"A": "Refer promptly for medical assessment and nerve studies, as new motor loss signals compression", "B": "Continue the plan for six more weeks, as conservative care is first line in mild cases", "C": "Progress the thenar strengthening programme and reassess in two weeks, as weakness often reflects disuse", "D": "Wear the splint during the day as well and add therapeutic ultrasound, reviewing after four sessions"}, "cluster_id": "CHS74", "cluster_text": "Mei Ling Chen, 41, an office administrator and keen badminton player, attends your private practice with six weeks of numbness and tingling in the right thumb, index and middle fingers, worse at night and when holding her phone. There was no trauma. Examination: Phalen's test reproduces tingling within 30 seconds, Tinel's sign at the wrist is positive, thenar bulk is normal, thumb abduction is 5/5, and light touch is reduced in the median nerve distribution of the hand. Cervical rotation is full and pain-free and Spurling's test is negative. The median-bias upper limb neurodynamic test reproduces her hand symptoms and they increase with contralateral neck lateral flexion. Night pain is 4/10. She mentions she knows your landlord socially and wants to repay you for the help.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Refer promptly for medical assessment and nerve studies, as new motor loss signals compression): Thenar wasting and measurable weakness signal axonal compromise, which makes this no longer a mild case and warrants prompt medical assessment, because delay risks permanent loss of hand function. More splinting, ultrasound or strengthening treats a problem she has outgrown. Continuing conservative care is only appropriate while motor function is preserved."}, {"id": 1256, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "At the end of the session, Mei Ling says she feels you are the only person who understands her, invites you to dinner with her friends, and asks whether you could treat her at her home without charge. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Decline the invitation and free home visits, keeping the relationship professional and treatment in clinic", "B": "Accept the dinner as a one off with friends present, but decline free home visits", "C": "Suggest transferring her care to a colleague so a friendship and the dinner can proceed", "D": "Accept neither offer, but raise the boundary issue with her once treatment has finished"}, "cluster_id": "CHS74", "cluster_text": "Mei Ling Chen, 41, an office administrator and keen badminton player, attends your private practice with six weeks of numbness and tingling in the right thumb, index and middle fingers, worse at night and when holding her phone. There was no trauma. Examination: Phalen's test reproduces tingling within 30 seconds, Tinel's sign at the wrist is positive, thenar bulk is normal, thumb abduction is 5/5, and light touch is reduced in the median nerve distribution of the hand. Cervical rotation is full and pain-free and Spurling's test is negative. The median-bias upper limb neurodynamic test reproduces her hand symptoms and they increase with contralateral neck lateral flexion. Night pain is 4/10. She mentions she knows your landlord socially and wants to repay you for the help.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Decline the invitation and free home visits, keeping the relationship professional and treatment in clinic): While she is an active patient, the physiotherapist should decline both the social invitation and the free home treatment, and keep the therapeutic relationship in the clinic. Attending a dinner even once blurs the boundary during treatment. Transferring care so that a friendship can begin is not a professional reason to end a therapeutic relationship, and waiting until treatment ends leaves her request without a clear and timely answer."}, {"id": 1257, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "The team wants an outcome measure to track Eleni's walking over the next weeks. She cannot walk continuously for more than two minutes and needs close contact guarding. It should be feasible to repeat weekly. Which measure is most appropriate to use at this stage?", "options": {"A": "A functional reach test in standing, recording the distance reached with her unaffected arm without stepping", "B": "A timed up and go test without her aid, recording the total time taken to finish", "C": "A six minute walk test with rest breaks allowed, recording distance and Borg rating", "D": "A ten metre walk test using her usual aid, recording time and assistance needed"}, "cluster_id": "CHS75", "cluster_text": "Eleni Papadakis, 79, a retired seamstress, is three weeks after a left middle cerebral artery ischaemic stroke and is in an inpatient rehabilitation unit. She has right hemiparesis (hip extension 3/5, ankle dorsiflexion 2/5), mild expressive aphasia, and intact cognition on screening. She stands from a chair with one assistant and walks 8 metres with a hemi-walker and close contact guarding, but cannot yet walk continuously for more than two minutes. Her Berg Balance Scale score today is 22 out of 56. Vital signs are stable. She has nominated her daughter as the only person who may receive information about her care. A neighbour who works as a nurse at another hospital telephones the ward and asks the physiotherapist for an update on her progress. The team will review her goals at the weekly meeting.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A ten metre walk test using her usual aid, recording time and assistance needed): At this stage she walks short distances with contact guarding, so a short walk test gives a safe, repeatable measure of speed and records the assistance level. A six minute test is too long for her and would show a floor effect. Walking without her aid is unsafe, and a reach test measures balance rather than walking."}, {"id": 1258, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Eleni's Berg Balance Scale score is 22 out of 56 today. The physiotherapist wants to document what a change on this scale means after one week. The test was repeated by a different assessor. Which statement about interpreting a change in her score is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Change should be judged against the average rise seen in other stroke patients on the unit", "B": "Change matters only when the score crosses a cut-off that separates fallers from non-fallers", "C": "Any rise in score shows genuine recovery, because the scale is scored objectively by trained clinicians", "D": "A change should exceed the scale's measurement error before it is called real improvement"}, "cluster_id": "CHS75", "cluster_text": "Eleni Papadakis, 79, a retired seamstress, is three weeks after a left middle cerebral artery ischaemic stroke and is in an inpatient rehabilitation unit. She has right hemiparesis (hip extension 3/5, ankle dorsiflexion 2/5), mild expressive aphasia, and intact cognition on screening. She stands from a chair with one assistant and walks 8 metres with a hemi-walker and close contact guarding, but cannot yet walk continuously for more than two minutes. Her Berg Balance Scale score today is 22 out of 56. Vital signs are stable. She has nominated her daughter as the only person who may receive information about her care. A neighbour who works as a nurse at another hospital telephones the ward and asks the physiotherapist for an update on her progress. The team will review her goals at the weekly meeting.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A change should exceed the scale's measurement error before it is called real improvement): A difference between two scores is only meaningful if it is larger than the measurement error of the scale, and a different assessor adds further variation. A small rise may simply reflect error. A fallers cut-off describes risk rather than change, and comparison with other patients does not show whether Eleni herself has improved."}, {"id": 1259, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "The physiotherapist documents today's session in Eleni's medical record. The notes will be read by the team, by Eleni's daughter on request, and possibly reviewed later for audit. Which entry is the most appropriate way to record her walking performance today?", "options": {"A": "Walked 8 m with hemi-walker, abnormal gait, told the daughter that recovery prospects look poor", "B": "Walking was limited today, patient seemed unmotivated and tired, and will need encouragement to try harder", "C": "Walked 8 m with hemi-walker and contact guarding, stopped at two minutes with fatigue", "D": "Gait pattern improved since admission, walked with hemi-walker as usual, no concerns noted today"}, "cluster_id": "CHS75", "cluster_text": "Eleni Papadakis, 79, a retired seamstress, is three weeks after a left middle cerebral artery ischaemic stroke and is in an inpatient rehabilitation unit. She has right hemiparesis (hip extension 3/5, ankle dorsiflexion 2/5), mild expressive aphasia, and intact cognition on screening. She stands from a chair with one assistant and walks 8 metres with a hemi-walker and close contact guarding, but cannot yet walk continuously for more than two minutes. Her Berg Balance Scale score today is 22 out of 56. Vital signs are stable. She has nominated her daughter as the only person who may receive information about her care. A neighbour who works as a nurse at another hospital telephones the ward and asks the physiotherapist for an update on her progress. The team will review her goals at the weekly meeting.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Walked 8 m with hemi-walker and contact guarding, stopped at two minutes with fatigue): Good documentation is objective, specific and measurable, giving distance, aid, assistance level and the reason for stopping. Judgements about motivation and unsupported claims of improvement cannot be verified by another clinician. Prognostic statements about recovery do not belong in notes unless they are supported by assessment and agreed through the team."}, {"id": 1260, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "A neighbour who works as a nurse at another hospital phones the ward and asks the physiotherapist how Eleni is progressing. Eleni has said only her daughter may receive information. The neighbour says she is only worried and knows Eleni well. What should the physiotherapist do?", "options": {"A": "Decline to give information, suggest contacting the daughter, and note the call in the record", "B": "Confirm the caller's identity and workplace, then share an update limited to walking progress", "C": "Share general progress only, since the caller is a health professional who understands confidentiality obligations", "D": "Transfer the call to the nurse in charge so the ward team can give an update"}, "cluster_id": "CHS75", "cluster_text": "Eleni Papadakis, 79, a retired seamstress, is three weeks after a left middle cerebral artery ischaemic stroke and is in an inpatient rehabilitation unit. She has right hemiparesis (hip extension 3/5, ankle dorsiflexion 2/5), mild expressive aphasia, and intact cognition on screening. She stands from a chair with one assistant and walks 8 metres with a hemi-walker and close contact guarding, but cannot yet walk continuously for more than two minutes. Her Berg Balance Scale score today is 22 out of 56. Vital signs are stable. She has nominated her daughter as the only person who may receive information about her care. A neighbour who works as a nurse at another hospital telephones the ward and asks the physiotherapist for an update on her progress. The team will review her goals at the weekly meeting.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Decline to give information, suggest contacting the daughter, and note the call in the record): Eleni has named her daughter as the only person to receive information, so nothing may be disclosed to the neighbour, even a colleague from another hospital. Checking identity, limiting the content or passing the call on would each still lead to disclosure. Politely declining, redirecting to the daughter and noting the call respects her wishes."}, {"id": 1261, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Lui sways when sitting unsupported, is unsteady standing and walks with a wide-based lurching gait, yet has full limb power and intact sensation. Which interpretation best explains this pattern of impairment given his recent right vermis infarct and these examination findings?", "options": {"A": "Corticospinal tract damage is causing weakness of the trunk and hip muscles, which makes gait unstable", "B": "Proprioceptive pathway loss in the dorsal columns is removing joint position feedback needed for balance", "C": "Peripheral vestibular loss is removing balance input, so he widens his base to compensate for it", "D": "Vermis damage is disrupting trunk control and postural stability, causing truncal ataxia"}, "cluster_id": "CHS76", "cluster_text": "Lui Vaka, 41, a forklift driver, is on day 21 of an inpatient rehabilitation stay after a right cerebellar infarct involving the vermis. He lives with his wife and three children in a two-storey house. Findings today: sits unsupported but sways with trunk titubation; stands only with moderate assistance of one person; wide-based, lurching gait with a gait belt and one assistant; dysmetria on finger-nose testing, right worse than left; intention tremor; impaired heel-shin testing. Power is 5/5 throughout and sensation is intact. Berg Balance Scale 19/56. Vision clear, no nystagmus today. Resting observations: BP 128/78 mmHg, HR 76, SpO2 98 percent on room air. He is impulsive and tends to stand without calling. Overnight, nursing staff found him walking to the bathroom alone, and the morning nurse has asked what level of help he now needs.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Vermis damage is disrupting trunk control and postural stability, causing truncal ataxia): The vermis supports axial posture and gait, so its infarction produces truncal ataxia and a wide base even when limb power is normal. Intact sensation argues against dorsal column loss, 5/5 power argues against corticospinal weakness, and nystagmus is absent, making a peripheral vestibular cause unlikely."}, {"id": 1262, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "The morning nurse asks what level of help Lui needs after finding him walking alone overnight. He currently needs one person and a gait belt, and he tends to stand without calling. What is the best way for the physiotherapist to communicate this?", "options": {"A": "Give a structured verbal handover and update the bedside mobility plan and falls risk", "B": "Write a detailed progress note and send the nurse in charge an electronic message", "C": "Brief his wife on the assistance level and ask her to remind staff at visiting time", "D": "Place a sign above the bed stating that one person and a gait belt are needed"}, "cluster_id": "CHS76", "cluster_text": "Lui Vaka, 41, a forklift driver, is on day 21 of an inpatient rehabilitation stay after a right cerebellar infarct involving the vermis. He lives with his wife and three children in a two-storey house. Findings today: sits unsupported but sways with trunk titubation; stands only with moderate assistance of one person; wide-based, lurching gait with a gait belt and one assistant; dysmetria on finger-nose testing, right worse than left; intention tremor; impaired heel-shin testing. Power is 5/5 throughout and sensation is intact. Berg Balance Scale 19/56. Vision clear, no nystagmus today. Resting observations: BP 128/78 mmHg, HR 76, SpO2 98 percent on room air. He is impulsive and tends to stand without calling. Overnight, nursing staff found him walking to the bathroom alone, and the morning nurse has asked what level of help he now needs.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Give a structured verbal handover and update the bedside mobility plan and falls risk): A structured verbal handover lets the nurse ask questions, and an updated bedside mobility plan keeps the assistance level and falls risk visible at the point of care. A written note and message are one way and may not be read before he next stands. A sign alone gives no handover or reasoning, and relying on his wife places a staffing responsibility on family."}, {"id": 1263, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Lui's goals are to walk safely at home and return to driving. His Berg score is 19/56 and he sways when sitting unsupported. Which treatment approach is most appropriate to prioritise in this session to improve his ataxia and balance?", "options": {"A": "High-load resistance training to fatigue for the trunk and hips to offset his poor coordination", "B": "Task-specific trunk and balance practice, progressed from supported positions under close supervision", "C": "Therapist handling to guide each movement passively, avoiding independent attempts until his coordination has returned", "D": "Standing and walking practice with eyes closed to force greater reliance on his proprioceptive feedback"}, "cluster_id": "CHS76", "cluster_text": "Lui Vaka, 41, a forklift driver, is on day 21 of an inpatient rehabilitation stay after a right cerebellar infarct involving the vermis. He lives with his wife and three children in a two-storey house. Findings today: sits unsupported but sways with trunk titubation; stands only with moderate assistance of one person; wide-based, lurching gait with a gait belt and one assistant; dysmetria on finger-nose testing, right worse than left; intention tremor; impaired heel-shin testing. Power is 5/5 throughout and sensation is intact. Berg Balance Scale 19/56. Vision clear, no nystagmus today. Resting observations: BP 128/78 mmHg, HR 76, SpO2 98 percent on room air. He is impulsive and tends to stand without calling. Overnight, nursing staff found him walking to the bathroom alone, and the morning nurse has asked what level of help he now needs.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Task-specific trunk and balance practice, progressed from supported positions under close supervision): Cerebellar ataxia improves with high-repetition, task-specific practice that is graded in difficulty and kept safe through supervision. Power is already 5/5, so heavy strengthening is not the limiting factor. Removing vision increases ataxia and fall risk, and passive handling prevents the motor learning he needs."}, {"id": 1264, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Midway through standing practice Lui becomes drowsy and reports a sudden severe occipital headache with vomiting. His speech is newly slurred and he cannot be roused fully and his heart rate has fallen to 52 beats per minute. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Stop the session, sit him in a chair with nursing observation and reassess in fifteen minutes", "B": "Return him to bed, stop the session and request routine nursing observations in the next hour", "C": "Return him safely to bed, stop the session, and urgently alert the medical team", "D": "Lie him flat, check blood pressure and glucose, and alert the team if either is abnormal"}, "cluster_id": "CHS76", "cluster_text": "Lui Vaka, 41, a forklift driver, is on day 21 of an inpatient rehabilitation stay after a right cerebellar infarct involving the vermis. He lives with his wife and three children in a two-storey house. Findings today: sits unsupported but sways with trunk titubation; stands only with moderate assistance of one person; wide-based, lurching gait with a gait belt and one assistant; dysmetria on finger-nose testing, right worse than left; intention tremor; impaired heel-shin testing. Power is 5/5 throughout and sensation is intact. Berg Balance Scale 19/56. Vision clear, no nystagmus today. Resting observations: BP 128/78 mmHg, HR 76, SpO2 98 percent on room air. He is impulsive and tends to stand without calling. Overnight, nursing staff found him walking to the bathroom alone, and the morning nurse has asked what level of help he now needs.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Return him safely to bed, stop the session, and urgently alert the medical team): Sudden severe occipital headache, vomiting, falling consciousness, new slurred speech and bradycardia after a cerebellar infarct suggest swelling or hydrocephalus compressing the brainstem. He needs to be returned safely to bed and the medical team alerted urgently. Watching him in a chair, requesting routine observations, or checking blood pressure and glucose first all postpone escalation for a patient with signs of raised intracranial pressure."}, {"id": 1265, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Hoa has pain on resisted knee flexion, tenderness over the mid biceps femoris, reduced right straight leg raise, a negative slump test and normal lumbar clearance. Which interpretation of these findings is most appropriate at this stage, four days after a sprinting injury?", "options": {"A": "A low grade biceps femoris muscle strain without neural or lumbar spine involvement", "B": "A complete proximal hamstring tendon avulsion needing urgent orthopaedic referral before any loading is started", "C": "Delayed onset muscle soreness from sprint training, which is expected to settle with relative rest", "D": "Adverse neural tension of the sciatic nerve, causing posterior thigh pain on straight leg raise testing"}, "cluster_id": "CHS77", "cluster_text": "Hoa Dang, 16, plays school netball and runs 400 m for her athletics team. Four days ago she felt a sudden pull at the back of her right thigh while sprinting at training and stopped at once. She limped for the rest of the day. No medical history and no regular medications. Today at the school sport clinic: mild bruising over the posterior thigh, no palpable defect, tender over the mid biceps femoris. Pain is 4/10 walking and 6/10 on resisted knee flexion. Passive straight leg raise is 60 degrees on the right with posterior thigh pain and 85 degrees on the left. Hip extension strength is 4+/5. Slump test is negative and lumbar clearance is normal. Hoa hopes to play in a final in two weeks. Her coach has sent a published case series about a ten day rehabilitation programme and wants you to follow it.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A low grade biceps femoris muscle strain without neural or lumbar spine involvement): Sudden onset during sprinting, local tenderness, pain on resisted knee flexion and a painful but intact range fit a low grade muscle strain. The negative slump test and normal lumbar clearance argue against a neural or spinal source. A tendon avulsion would usually show a palpable defect and marked weakness, and soreness would not begin with a sudden pull."}, {"id": 1266, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Four days after the injury Hoa walks without a limp and her pain on walking is 2/10, though resisted knee flexion still provokes pain. She asks to start exercising today. Which rehabilitation approach is most appropriate to begin now for her hamstring?", "options": {"A": "Stop all sport and rest the tendon for several weeks until the pain has gone", "B": "Eccentric Nordic hamstring curls at full intensity to build strength quickly and reduce reinjury risk later", "C": "Aggressive static stretching into the pain to restore straight leg raise range before any strengthening begins", "D": "Pain-limited isometric loading with progressive range, avoiding aggressive stretching and sprinting for now"}, "cluster_id": "CHS77", "cluster_text": "Hoa Dang, 16, plays school netball and runs 400 m for her athletics team. Four days ago she felt a sudden pull at the back of her right thigh while sprinting at training and stopped at once. She limped for the rest of the day. No medical history and no regular medications. Today at the school sport clinic: mild bruising over the posterior thigh, no palpable defect, tender over the mid biceps femoris. Pain is 4/10 walking and 6/10 on resisted knee flexion. Passive straight leg raise is 60 degrees on the right with posterior thigh pain and 85 degrees on the left. Hip extension strength is 4+/5. Slump test is negative and lumbar clearance is normal. Hoa hopes to play in a final in two weeks. Her coach has sent a published case series about a ten day rehabilitation programme and wants you to follow it.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Pain-limited isometric loading with progressive range, avoiding aggressive stretching and sprinting for now): Early rehabilitation of a muscle strain uses pain-limited loading and gradual range, which promotes healing without overloading the tissue. Prolonged rest leads to deconditioning and poor tissue remodelling. Stretching into pain and maximal eccentric work are later or inappropriate steps at this stage and risk aggravating the injury."}, {"id": 1267, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Hoa's coach sends a published case series describing a ten day rehabilitation programme tested in eleven players at one club, with no comparison group, and asks you to follow it exactly. How should the physiotherapist best weigh this evidence for Hoa?", "options": {"A": "Follow the programme exactly because published results show a ten day return is achievable in athletes", "B": "Dismiss it because a case series cannot inform practice, and rely on clinical experience alone", "C": "Treat it as weak evidence and progress Hoa by her clinical criteria and wider research", "D": "Adopt its exercises but ignore its timeline, since the authors' clinical judgement is reliable evidence"}, "cluster_id": "CHS77", "cluster_text": "Hoa Dang, 16, plays school netball and runs 400 m for her athletics team. Four days ago she felt a sudden pull at the back of her right thigh while sprinting at training and stopped at once. She limped for the rest of the day. No medical history and no regular medications. Today at the school sport clinic: mild bruising over the posterior thigh, no palpable defect, tender over the mid biceps femoris. Pain is 4/10 walking and 6/10 on resisted knee flexion. Passive straight leg raise is 60 degrees on the right with posterior thigh pain and 85 degrees on the left. Hip extension strength is 4+/5. Slump test is negative and lumbar clearance is normal. Hoa hopes to play in a final in two weeks. Her coach has sent a published case series about a ten day rehabilitation programme and wants you to follow it.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Treat it as weak evidence and progress Hoa by her clinical criteria and wider research): A small uncontrolled case series sits low in the evidence hierarchy and cannot show that the programme caused the outcomes. It can still be considered alongside higher quality research, clinical reasoning and Hoa's own findings. Following a fixed timeline ignores individual progress, while rejecting all such evidence discards useful information."}, {"id": 1268, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Two weeks later Hoa jogs pain free, but sprinting at full speed still provokes mild posterior thigh tightness. Her coach wants her selected for tomorrow's final. She has completed her strengthening programme. Which factor should most determine whether she returns to full competition?", "options": {"A": "Pain free full speed sprinting with strength and function tests meeting agreed criteria", "B": "Hoa's report of feeling ready, since athlete confidence is the best single predictor of safe return", "C": "Passive straight leg raise matching the other side, since restored flexibility is what prevents reinjury", "D": "Time elapsed since injury, because tissue healing time is the main driver of readiness for sprinting"}, "cluster_id": "CHS77", "cluster_text": "Hoa Dang, 16, plays school netball and runs 400 m for her athletics team. Four days ago she felt a sudden pull at the back of her right thigh while sprinting at training and stopped at once. She limped for the rest of the day. No medical history and no regular medications. Today at the school sport clinic: mild bruising over the posterior thigh, no palpable defect, tender over the mid biceps femoris. Pain is 4/10 walking and 6/10 on resisted knee flexion. Passive straight leg raise is 60 degrees on the right with posterior thigh pain and 85 degrees on the left. Hip extension strength is 4+/5. Slump test is negative and lumbar clearance is normal. Hoa hopes to play in a final in two weeks. Her coach has sent a published case series about a ten day rehabilitation programme and wants you to follow it.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Pain free full speed sprinting with strength and function tests meeting agreed criteria): Return to sport should rest on criteria that show the muscle tolerates the demands of the sport, including sprinting, strength and function. Elapsed time and confidence are useful but do not show the tissue is ready. Equal passive range alone does not predict whether the hamstring can cope with high speed loading."}, {"id": 1269, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Rachel's right hand is swollen, shiny, mottled and warmer than the left, with burning pain and pain to light touch that is out of proportion to a united fracture. Which interpretation of this combination of findings is most appropriate for the physiotherapist to record?", "options": {"A": "Simple disuse stiffness after cast immobilisation, which explains the pain and should settle with regular stretching", "B": "Combined sensory, vasomotor and trophic changes suggest complex regional pain syndrome, which a doctor must confirm", "C": "Early heterotopic ossification around the wrist, which commonly produces warmth, swelling and pain with light touch", "D": "Median nerve compression at the carpal tunnel, which explains the skin colour and temperature changes"}, "cluster_id": "CHS78", "cluster_text": "Rachel Farrell, 58, bookkeeper, right-hand dominant. She fell onto an outstretched hand 14 weeks ago and had a distal radius fracture managed in a cast for six weeks, with no surgery. Her GP referred her to outpatient physiotherapy for persisting pain and stiffness. Pain is 8/10, burning, and worse with light touch on the back of the hand. The right hand is swollen compared with the left, the skin is shiny and mottled red and blue, the hand feels warmer, and nail and hair growth look altered. Wrist flexion and extension are 15 degrees each, finger flexion is half range, and grip cannot be tested. A cotton ball causes marked pain. She keeps the arm in a sling. X-ray report: fracture united, patchy bone loss. Medications: paracetamol and a neuropathic pain medication started by her GP last week. Today she seems sleepy and unsure of the date, though she says she wants to start therapy.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Combined sensory, vasomotor and trophic changes suggest complex regional pain syndrome, which a doctor must confirm): Pain out of proportion to the injury, together with swelling, skin colour and temperature change and altered nail and hair growth, spans several categories of change and fits complex regional pain syndrome, which a doctor confirms. Simple disuse stiffness would not produce pain to light touch, mottled skin or a warm hand."}, {"id": 1270, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Rachel cannot tolerate any hand use because of pain and light touch sensitivity, and she keeps the arm guarded in a sling. The physiotherapist is planning the first treatment approach for this stage. Which approach is most appropriate to begin with?", "options": {"A": "Passive end-range wrist mobilisation with firm manual stretching, to regain range before the tissues stiffen further", "B": "Splinting the hand in a rigid support until swelling and touch sensitivity resolve, then starting movement", "C": "Weighted grip strengthening done daily to rebuild muscle bulk and encourage normal use of the hand", "D": "Education, graded desensitisation and gentle active movement within tolerable pain, with a short home programme"}, "cluster_id": "CHS78", "cluster_text": "Rachel Farrell, 58, bookkeeper, right-hand dominant. She fell onto an outstretched hand 14 weeks ago and had a distal radius fracture managed in a cast for six weeks, with no surgery. Her GP referred her to outpatient physiotherapy for persisting pain and stiffness. Pain is 8/10, burning, and worse with light touch on the back of the hand. The right hand is swollen compared with the left, the skin is shiny and mottled red and blue, the hand feels warmer, and nail and hair growth look altered. Wrist flexion and extension are 15 degrees each, finger flexion is half range, and grip cannot be tested. A cotton ball causes marked pain. She keeps the arm in a sling. X-ray report: fracture united, patchy bone loss. Medications: paracetamol and a neuropathic pain medication started by her GP last week. Today she seems sleepy and unsure of the date, though she says she wants to start therapy.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Education, graded desensitisation and gentle active movement within tolerable pain, with a short home programme): Current practice favours explaining the condition and using graded, pain-limited movement and desensitisation so the hand is gradually used again. Forceful passive stretching and heavy loading tend to provoke flares, and prolonged immobilisation worsens stiffness and disuse."}, {"id": 1271, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "At review two weeks later, Rachel says her pain, swelling and colour change were much worse for a full day after the last session, when passive stretching to the wrist was included. Pain is now 9/10. How should the physiotherapist adjust the plan?", "options": {"A": "Pause all treatment for several weeks until the flare settles, to avoid sensitising the hand further", "B": "Progress to resisted exercise, because flares show tissues need a stronger stimulus to adapt", "C": "Remove passive stretching and use brief, frequent graded activity below the flare threshold, with pacing", "D": "Continue the stretching but add ice packs afterwards to settle swelling and consolidate the range gained"}, "cluster_id": "CHS78", "cluster_text": "Rachel Farrell, 58, bookkeeper, right-hand dominant. She fell onto an outstretched hand 14 weeks ago and had a distal radius fracture managed in a cast for six weeks, with no surgery. Her GP referred her to outpatient physiotherapy for persisting pain and stiffness. Pain is 8/10, burning, and worse with light touch on the back of the hand. The right hand is swollen compared with the left, the skin is shiny and mottled red and blue, the hand feels warmer, and nail and hair growth look altered. Wrist flexion and extension are 15 degrees each, finger flexion is half range, and grip cannot be tested. A cotton ball causes marked pain. She keeps the arm in a sling. X-ray report: fracture united, patchy bone loss. Medications: paracetamol and a neuropathic pain medication started by her GP last week. Today she seems sleepy and unsure of the date, though she says she wants to start therapy.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Remove passive stretching and use brief, frequent graded activity below the flare threshold, with pacing): A marked flare after passive stretching shows the dose was too provocative, so the plan should lower intensity and keep activity frequent and graded. Stopping treatment entirely risks more stiffness and disuse, and increasing load would repeat the provocation."}, {"id": 1272, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Rachel looks drowsy and cannot state today's date after her new medication was started, yet she says she wants to begin the graded programme and offers to sign the consent form. What should the physiotherapist do about consent at this session?", "options": {"A": "Defer consent, reassess understanding when she is alert, and tell her GP about the drowsiness", "B": "Proceed with the usual low risk programme and record her consent in the notes afterwards", "C": "Accept her wish as valid consent because she is asking for treatment, and shorten the session", "D": "Ask her daughter by phone to sign on her behalf as next of kin for today"}, "cluster_id": "CHS78", "cluster_text": "Rachel Farrell, 58, bookkeeper, right-hand dominant. She fell onto an outstretched hand 14 weeks ago and had a distal radius fracture managed in a cast for six weeks, with no surgery. Her GP referred her to outpatient physiotherapy for persisting pain and stiffness. Pain is 8/10, burning, and worse with light touch on the back of the hand. The right hand is swollen compared with the left, the skin is shiny and mottled red and blue, the hand feels warmer, and nail and hair growth look altered. Wrist flexion and extension are 15 degrees each, finger flexion is half range, and grip cannot be tested. A cotton ball causes marked pain. She keeps the arm in a sling. X-ray report: fracture united, patchy bone loss. Medications: paracetamol and a neuropathic pain medication started by her GP last week. Today she seems sleepy and unsure of the date, though she says she wants to start therapy.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Defer consent, reassess understanding when she is alert, and tell her GP about the drowsiness): Valid consent needs capacity at the time it is given, and drowsiness and disorientation call that into question. The physiotherapist should wait and reassess, and also report a possible medication effect to the GP. A relative cannot sign for an adult who may regain capacity within hours."}, {"id": 1273, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Banda's vital capacity has fallen steadily on the nursing chart while his limb weakness has progressed. During your session, which additional finding would most strongly suggest developing respiratory muscle weakness that needs prompt medical attention rather than routine physiotherapy monitoring on the ward today?", "options": {"A": "Persistent calf aching and pins and needles that worsen at night in bed", "B": "A weak cough and difficulty finishing a sentence within a single breath", "C": "Absent ankle reflexes together with mild bilateral facial weakness when he smiles", "D": "Fatigue developing after about five minutes of upper limb exercise while sitting up"}, "cluster_id": "CHS79", "cluster_text": "Baraka Banda, 52, bus driver. Day 6 of admission to a general medical ward with Guillain-Barre syndrome after a recent gastroenteritis illness; receiving intravenous immunoglobulin. Examination: hip flexion and knee extension 2/5 bilaterally, ankle dorsiflexion 2/5, shoulder abduction 4/5, grip 4/5, reflexes absent at knees and ankles, mild bilateral facial weakness. He reports calf aching and pins and needles in his feet. Fatigues after five minutes of exercise. Observations: HR 88, BP 128/78, RR 20, SpO2 96% on room air. The nursing vital capacity chart shows a gradual fall over three days. The medical team reviews him daily. Physiotherapy is ordered for positioning, passive range and breathing exercises. He is anxious and asks whether he should push harder in therapy.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A weak cough and difficulty finishing a sentence within a single breath): A weak cough and speech interrupted by breathlessness reflect failing inspiratory and expiratory muscles, and ventilatory failure can progress quickly in this condition. Neuropathic pain, absent reflexes and facial weakness are expected features that do not by themselves signal ventilatory compromise. Fatigue is common and is managed by pacing rather than urgent review."}, {"id": 1274, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Banda is anxious and asks to start heavy resisted strengthening to speed his recovery, as he has always worked hard in the gym. His muscles fatigue within minutes. Which exercise approach is most appropriate for him at this acute stage of the illness?", "options": {"A": "Progressive high-load resistance training three times daily, aiming to build the weakest muscle groups quickly", "B": "Endurance training on a bike ergometer to fatigue, since aerobic work protects against overwork weakness", "C": "Short low-resistance active exercise with rests, avoiding fatigue and checking his response afterwards", "D": "Passive movements only, withholding all active effort until strength has returned on neurological testing"}, "cluster_id": "CHS79", "cluster_text": "Baraka Banda, 52, bus driver. Day 6 of admission to a general medical ward with Guillain-Barre syndrome after a recent gastroenteritis illness; receiving intravenous immunoglobulin. Examination: hip flexion and knee extension 2/5 bilaterally, ankle dorsiflexion 2/5, shoulder abduction 4/5, grip 4/5, reflexes absent at knees and ankles, mild bilateral facial weakness. He reports calf aching and pins and needles in his feet. Fatigues after five minutes of exercise. Observations: HR 88, BP 128/78, RR 20, SpO2 96% on room air. The nursing vital capacity chart shows a gradual fall over three days. The medical team reviews him daily. Physiotherapy is ordered for positioning, passive range and breathing exercises. He is anxious and asks whether he should push harder in therapy.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Short low-resistance active exercise with rests, avoiding fatigue and checking his response afterwards): In the acute phase, gentle submaximal active exercise with rest breaks maintains strength without exhausting weakened, demyelinated nerves, and his response should be checked later. Heavy or fatiguing work risks overwork weakness and prolonged setback. Complete avoidance of active effort is also unnecessary and delays useful activation."}, {"id": 1275, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "At your afternoon session Mr Banda says he feels breathless when talking. His voice is quiet, he cannot finish sentences without pausing, HR is 104 and RR is 26, and SpO2 is 95% on room air. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Record the oxygen saturation, since it remains normal, and re-check his observations again in one hour", "B": "Stop the session, sit him upright, and ask the nurse to arrange a vital capacity measurement", "C": "Sit him upright, teach breathing control and pacing, and report the change at handover", "D": "Stop the session, stay with him and call the medical team for urgent respiratory review"}, "cluster_id": "CHS79", "cluster_text": "Baraka Banda, 52, bus driver. Day 6 of admission to a general medical ward with Guillain-Barre syndrome after a recent gastroenteritis illness; receiving intravenous immunoglobulin. Examination: hip flexion and knee extension 2/5 bilaterally, ankle dorsiflexion 2/5, shoulder abduction 4/5, grip 4/5, reflexes absent at knees and ankles, mild bilateral facial weakness. He reports calf aching and pins and needles in his feet. Fatigues after five minutes of exercise. Observations: HR 88, BP 128/78, RR 20, SpO2 96% on room air. The nursing vital capacity chart shows a gradual fall over three days. The medical team reviews him daily. Physiotherapy is ordered for positioning, passive range and breathing exercises. He is anxious and asks whether he should push harder in therapy.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stop the session, stay with him and call the medical team for urgent respiratory review): Breathlessness with broken speech, a rising heart rate and respiratory rate suggest failing respiratory muscles in Guillain-Barre syndrome, which needs urgent medical review. A normal saturation is a late sign, so a recheck an hour later is unsafe. A vital capacity measurement is useful but should not replace immediate escalation, and breathing control or handover reporting treats this as a minor problem."}, {"id": 1276, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "When you sit Mr Banda over the edge of the bed during treatment, he becomes pale and dizzy. His BP falls from 128/78 to 88/50 and HR rises to 112. Autonomic involvement is suspected. What is the most appropriate immediate action?", "options": {"A": "Apply a firm compression bandage to both legs and continue upright sitting for ten minutes", "B": "Lie him back with legs raised, monitor observations until settled and inform the nursing staff", "C": "Keep him seated and encourage slow deep breaths for two minutes while observations are rechecked", "D": "Stand him with support to improve venous return, then progress to marching on the spot"}, "cluster_id": "CHS79", "cluster_text": "Baraka Banda, 52, bus driver. Day 6 of admission to a general medical ward with Guillain-Barre syndrome after a recent gastroenteritis illness; receiving intravenous immunoglobulin. Examination: hip flexion and knee extension 2/5 bilaterally, ankle dorsiflexion 2/5, shoulder abduction 4/5, grip 4/5, reflexes absent at knees and ankles, mild bilateral facial weakness. He reports calf aching and pins and needles in his feet. Fatigues after five minutes of exercise. Observations: HR 88, BP 128/78, RR 20, SpO2 96% on room air. The nursing vital capacity chart shows a gradual fall over three days. The medical team reviews him daily. Physiotherapy is ordered for positioning, passive range and breathing exercises. He is anxious and asks whether he should push harder in therapy.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Lie him back with legs raised, monitor observations until settled and inform the nursing staff): Symptomatic postural hypotension from autonomic dysfunction is managed by returning him to a position that restores cerebral perfusion, then monitoring and informing the team so the plan can be changed. Staying upright or standing him risks syncope. Compression may help later, but not as a substitute for lying him back."}, {"id": 1277, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Jorge wants to lift heavy soil bags. The surgical team's lifting restrictions are not in the referral, and his sternum feels stable. He is keen to start gardening next week. What should the physiotherapist do before prescribing resistance or lifting tasks?", "options": {"A": "Rely on stable sternal palpation and progress lifting by pain response since healing is confirmed", "B": "Test a maximal lift with soil bags in clinic, then advise a weight from that result", "C": "Apply a blanket sternotomy lifting restriction and delay all resistance work until he is discharged", "D": "Obtain the surgeon's current sternal guidance, then build loads gradually within those limits"}, "cluster_id": "CHS80", "cluster_text": "Jorge Ramirez, 71, retired mechanic, is five weeks after mechanical aortic valve replacement through a median sternotomy. Medications: warfarin, bisoprolol, perindopril, paracetamol. Sternum is stable with no clicking, wound healed. Resting HR 70 bpm regular, BP 132/78 mmHg, SpO2 96 percent. Walks 20 minutes daily without symptoms, rating 11 on Borg 6 to 20. Mild bruising on his forearms. He had a brief episode of post-operative delirium in hospital and his wife reports he still forgets conversations. He is alert, oriented and speaks fluently today. His wife attends and says he will 'agree to anything'. He wants to return to gardening and lifting bags of soil. Hospital advice about lifting restrictions is not in the referral.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Obtain the surgeon's current sternal guidance, then build loads gradually within those limits): Lifting and sternal loading limits after cardiac surgery come from the surgical team and the type of closure, so current guidance should be obtained before loading is prescribed. A stable sternum on palpation does not replace that advice. Testing a maximal lift risks excessive sternal and blood pressure load and is not appropriate at this stage."}, {"id": 1278, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Jorge's wife says he will agree to anything and offers to sign the exercise plan for him. Jorge appears alert but cannot explain back what the plan involves after it is explained. What is the most appropriate action regarding consent for this plan?", "options": {"A": "Explain again simply and obtain consent from his wife as his nominated support person", "B": "Proceed on implied consent because he attended willingly, documenting that his wife agreed to the plan", "C": "Delay treatment until a formal cognitive assessment is completed by a neuropsychologist, then seek his consent", "D": "Explain again simply, assess his decision-making capacity, and seek his own consent"}, "cluster_id": "CHS80", "cluster_text": "Jorge Ramirez, 71, retired mechanic, is five weeks after mechanical aortic valve replacement through a median sternotomy. Medications: warfarin, bisoprolol, perindopril, paracetamol. Sternum is stable with no clicking, wound healed. Resting HR 70 bpm regular, BP 132/78 mmHg, SpO2 96 percent. Walks 20 minutes daily without symptoms, rating 11 on Borg 6 to 20. Mild bruising on his forearms. He had a brief episode of post-operative delirium in hospital and his wife reports he still forgets conversations. He is alert, oriented and speaks fluently today. His wife attends and says he will 'agree to anything'. He wants to return to gardening and lifting bags of soil. Hospital advice about lifting restrictions is not in the referral.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain again simply, assess his decision-making capacity, and seek his own consent): Adults are presumed to have capacity, and capacity is judged for each decision. The physiotherapist should explain again in plain language, check whether he can understand, retain and weigh the information, and seek his own consent. A spouse can support him but cannot give consent for him without legal authority, and attendance alone does not amount to informed consent. Postponing all care for a formal specialist assessment is out of proportion to this decision."}, {"id": 1279, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Jorge's mechanical valve requires long-term warfarin. Which implication matters most when the physiotherapist plans treatment, including any hands-on technique or exercise with fall risk? He has mild forearm bruising and plans to garden on uneven ground. His last INR was reported as therapeutic.", "options": {"A": "Bleeding risk is raised, so avoid resistance exercise and restrict him to light walking only", "B": "Thrombosis risk is raised, so avoid sustained postures and check calf tenderness before every session", "C": "Bleeding risk is raised, so avoid deep soft tissue techniques and minimise falls", "D": "INR should be rechecked by the physiotherapist each session so intensity is matched to it"}, "cluster_id": "CHS80", "cluster_text": "Jorge Ramirez, 71, retired mechanic, is five weeks after mechanical aortic valve replacement through a median sternotomy. Medications: warfarin, bisoprolol, perindopril, paracetamol. Sternum is stable with no clicking, wound healed. Resting HR 70 bpm regular, BP 132/78 mmHg, SpO2 96 percent. Walks 20 minutes daily without symptoms, rating 11 on Borg 6 to 20. Mild bruising on his forearms. He had a brief episode of post-operative delirium in hospital and his wife reports he still forgets conversations. He is alert, oriented and speaks fluently today. His wife attends and says he will 'agree to anything'. He wants to return to gardening and lifting bags of soil. Hospital advice about lifting restrictions is not in the referral.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Bleeding risk is raised, so avoid deep soft tissue techniques and minimise falls): Warfarin lowers clotting, so deep soft tissue work and falls carry a higher risk of significant bruising or bleeding, which suits his forearm bruising and plans to garden on uneven ground. Strength and aerobic training remain valuable after valve surgery, so removing resistance exercise goes too far. His valve is protected by warfarin, so thrombosis is not the hazard being managed here, and the INR is monitored by the medical team rather than used to set exercise intensity."}, {"id": 1280, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "At the next visit Jorge reports new breathlessness and light-headedness climbing stairs over three days, plus a feeling of fever. Resting HR is 104 bpm, BP 104/62 mmHg, and he is clammy. His exercise plan today was to progress walking. What is the best decision?", "options": {"A": "Withhold exercise and arrange same-day medical review for possible valve or infection problems", "B": "Continue at lower intensity, advise rest and fluids, and ask him to phone if symptoms persist", "C": "Treat the breathlessness as deconditioning and add breathing control exercises with graded stair practice at home", "D": "Repeat measurements after ten minutes of seated rest and proceed with the plan if values normalise"}, "cluster_id": "CHS80", "cluster_text": "Jorge Ramirez, 71, retired mechanic, is five weeks after mechanical aortic valve replacement through a median sternotomy. Medications: warfarin, bisoprolol, perindopril, paracetamol. Sternum is stable with no clicking, wound healed. Resting HR 70 bpm regular, BP 132/78 mmHg, SpO2 96 percent. Walks 20 minutes daily without symptoms, rating 11 on Borg 6 to 20. Mild bruising on his forearms. He had a brief episode of post-operative delirium in hospital and his wife reports he still forgets conversations. He is alert, oriented and speaks fluently today. His wife attends and says he will 'agree to anything'. He wants to return to gardening and lifting bags of soil. Hospital advice about lifting restrictions is not in the referral.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Withhold exercise and arrange same-day medical review for possible valve or infection problems): New exertional symptoms, tachycardia, relative hypotension and possible fever after valve surgery raise concern for prosthetic valve dysfunction, endocarditis or bleeding, all needing same-day medical assessment. Waiting for values to settle or attributing the picture to deconditioning risks delaying urgent care. Exercise should be withheld until a medical review is complete."}, {"id": 1281, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Nursing records the Agitated Behaviour Scale each shift and the Glasgow Coma Scale daily, and the team now adds the Westmead PTA Scale, scoring Bindi 9 of 12 this morning. The ward manager asks what the Westmead PTA Scale is mainly used for in his care.", "options": {"A": "Grading eye, verbal and motor responses to judge depth of impaired consciousness and monitor deterioration", "B": "Tracking orientation and memory over time to judge when post-traumatic amnesia has resolved", "C": "Rating the severity of restlessness and aggression across each shift to guide nursing and environmental responses", "D": "Staging overall cognitive and behavioural recovery to match the type of rehabilitation environment and support"}, "cluster_id": "CHS81", "cluster_text": "Bindi Quinn, 22, a station hand, fell from a moving ute 5 days ago and sustained a severe traumatic brain injury with a left temporal contusion and a fractured left clavicle managed in a sling. He is in a single room with a low bed on an acute ward. Westmead PTA Scale today is 9 of 12 and he is disoriented. Nursing records the Agitated Behaviour Scale each shift; scores are highest after 3 pm, when he tries to climb out of bed and pulls at his nasogastric tube. He is calm and cooperative for short periods in the morning. Formal strength testing is unreliable, but he moves all limbs against gravity. His mother, Lorraine, stays with him most days. The ward manager asks physiotherapy to help choose outcome measures and review whether the unit's mobility programme improves results.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tracking orientation and memory over time to judge when post-traumatic amnesia has resolved): The Westmead PTA Scale is a brief orientation and memory test repeated daily to show when post-traumatic amnesia ends. Rating agitation is the job of the Agitated Behaviour Scale, depth of consciousness is graded by the Glasgow Coma Scale, and broad staging of cognitive recovery is done with different tools."}, {"id": 1282, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Bindi is calm and cooperative from about 9 am but increasingly restless from 3 pm. His mother Lorraine is available most days. The physiotherapist is planning a daily mobility session for the week. Which timing and set-up is most appropriate for these sessions?", "options": {"A": "Treat during the morning calm period in a quiet space, in short bouts, with Lorraine present", "B": "Treat in the busy gym alongside other patients, using social cues and activity to settle him", "C": "Group therapy into one long morning block, protecting rest for the remainder of the day", "D": "Treat in the late afternoon, using his restlessness to drive longer walking with extra staff"}, "cluster_id": "CHS81", "cluster_text": "Bindi Quinn, 22, a station hand, fell from a moving ute 5 days ago and sustained a severe traumatic brain injury with a left temporal contusion and a fractured left clavicle managed in a sling. He is in a single room with a low bed on an acute ward. Westmead PTA Scale today is 9 of 12 and he is disoriented. Nursing records the Agitated Behaviour Scale each shift; scores are highest after 3 pm, when he tries to climb out of bed and pulls at his nasogastric tube. He is calm and cooperative for short periods in the morning. Formal strength testing is unreliable, but he moves all limbs against gravity. His mother, Lorraine, stays with him most days. The ward manager asks physiotherapy to help choose outcome measures and review whether the unit's mobility programme improves results.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Treat during the morning calm period in a quiet space, in short bouts, with Lorraine present): Agitation after brain injury is eased by low stimulation, predictability and a familiar person, and treating when he is calmest gives the best participation. Using restlessness in the afternoon or a busy gym adds stimulation and risk. One long block would exceed his tolerance and increase fatigue and agitation."}, {"id": 1283, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "During a walking session Bindi becomes increasingly agitated, shouting and trying to remove his sling. He is disoriented, cannot be reasoned with, and has a fractured left clavicle in a sling. The physiotherapist is beside him with a gait belt. What is the best immediate response?", "options": {"A": "Steady him by the arms and finish the walk to maintain the routine established this week", "B": "Gently orient him with clear facts about the accident and hospital, then resume the walk", "C": "Pause the activity, reduce stimulation, reassure calmly, and return him to a safe seated position", "D": "Pause the walk, have his mother talk with him about home, and resume once settled"}, "cluster_id": "CHS81", "cluster_text": "Bindi Quinn, 22, a station hand, fell from a moving ute 5 days ago and sustained a severe traumatic brain injury with a left temporal contusion and a fractured left clavicle managed in a sling. He is in a single room with a low bed on an acute ward. Westmead PTA Scale today is 9 of 12 and he is disoriented. Nursing records the Agitated Behaviour Scale each shift; scores are highest after 3 pm, when he tries to climb out of bed and pulls at his nasogastric tube. He is calm and cooperative for short periods in the morning. Formal strength testing is unreliable, but he moves all limbs against gravity. His mother, Lorraine, stays with him most days. The ward manager asks physiotherapy to help choose outcome measures and review whether the unit's mobility programme improves results.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Pause the activity, reduce stimulation, reassure calmly, and return him to a safe seated position): During post-traumatic amnesia, rising agitation is best managed by stopping the trigger, reducing stimulation, reassuring him calmly and returning him to a safe position. Holding him to finish the walk risks injury and escalation. Explaining the accident asks him to process facts he cannot retain, and resuming the walk soon after a conversation with his mother keeps him in a stimulating task before he has settled."}, {"id": 1284, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "The ward manager wants to know whether the unit's early mobility programme improves outcomes for patients like Bindi. Patients vary from agitated and amnesic to fully oriented across a stay. Which approach to selecting and using outcome measures is most appropriate for the physiotherapy team to adopt?", "options": {"A": "Choose valid measures suited to recovery stage, record them at set times, and review the results", "B": "Count treatment sessions delivered each week, since delivery reflects programme quality and is easy to audit", "C": "Rely on clinical impressions and nursing notes alone, because formal measures are unreliable during post-traumatic amnesia", "D": "Apply the Berg Balance Scale to every patient on admission, allowing comparison with published data"}, "cluster_id": "CHS81", "cluster_text": "Bindi Quinn, 22, a station hand, fell from a moving ute 5 days ago and sustained a severe traumatic brain injury with a left temporal contusion and a fractured left clavicle managed in a sling. He is in a single room with a low bed on an acute ward. Westmead PTA Scale today is 9 of 12 and he is disoriented. Nursing records the Agitated Behaviour Scale each shift; scores are highest after 3 pm, when he tries to climb out of bed and pulls at his nasogastric tube. He is calm and cooperative for short periods in the morning. Formal strength testing is unreliable, but he moves all limbs against gravity. His mother, Lorraine, stays with him most days. The ward manager asks physiotherapy to help choose outcome measures and review whether the unit's mobility programme improves results.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Choose valid measures suited to recovery stage, record them at set times, and review the results): Outcome measures must suit what the patient can do at each stage, be valid and reliable, and be recorded consistently so change can be compared and fed back to the team. A balance scale needing instruction-following is unsuitable in early amnesia. Impressions alone cannot be audited, and counting sessions describes activity rather than results."}, {"id": 1285, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Leonard's shoulder was reduced yesterday, with no fracture and intact neurovascular status today. He is 74, this is his third dislocation, and prolonged immobilisation risks stiffness at his age. Which early physiotherapy management is most appropriate for the next few days?", "options": {"A": "Brief sling use, distal joint exercises, and gentle pendulums while avoiding abduction with external rotation", "B": "Start passive external rotation stretching to restore range early and prevent capsular stiffness in older adults", "C": "Begin resisted rotator cuff strengthening with bands from day two to restore dynamic stability early", "D": "Immobilise in the sling for six weeks to allow the capsule to heal before any movement"}, "cluster_id": "CHS82", "cluster_text": "Leonard Wilson, 74, is an Aboriginal man from a remote community about 600 km away, admitted to an orthopaedic ward after tripping on a step. He has a right anterior shoulder dislocation, reduced in the emergency department yesterday; X-ray shows no fracture. This is his third dislocation in two years. Medical history: type 2 diabetes and hypertension. He is right-hand dominant. Findings today: arm in a sling, pain 5/10 at rest, radial pulse present, sensation over the lateral upper arm normal, deltoid contracts isometrically, elbow, wrist and hand move fully. Active shoulder elevation has not yet been tested. He speaks quietly and answers most questions with 'yeah'. His niece, who travelled with him, says the family wants to be involved, and an Aboriginal Liaison Officer is available on the ward.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Brief sling use, distal joint exercises, and gentle pendulums while avoiding abduction with external rotation): In older adults prolonged immobilisation causes more stiffness than it prevents redislocation, so brief sling use with early gentle movement is preferred. Abduction combined with external rotation stresses the anterior capsule and is avoided early. Resisted cuff work and passive external rotation stretching are too early and provocative at this stage."}, {"id": 1286, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "On day four Leonard cannot actively raise his arm past 30 degrees, but passive elevation reaches nearly full range. Lateral deltoid sensation and isometric deltoid contraction remain normal, and pain is now 2/10. What is the most appropriate next action for the physiotherapist?", "options": {"A": "Attribute the weakness to pain inhibition and progress active assisted elevation until power returns spontaneously", "B": "Suspect a rotator cuff tear and ask the orthopaedic team to arrange imaging before strengthening", "C": "Treat as early adhesive capsulitis with joint mobilisation into elevation and a home stretching programme", "D": "Diagnose an axillary nerve palsy and begin electrical stimulation to the deltoid to prevent atrophy"}, "cluster_id": "CHS82", "cluster_text": "Leonard Wilson, 74, is an Aboriginal man from a remote community about 600 km away, admitted to an orthopaedic ward after tripping on a step. He has a right anterior shoulder dislocation, reduced in the emergency department yesterday; X-ray shows no fracture. This is his third dislocation in two years. Medical history: type 2 diabetes and hypertension. He is right-hand dominant. Findings today: arm in a sling, pain 5/10 at rest, radial pulse present, sensation over the lateral upper arm normal, deltoid contracts isometrically, elbow, wrist and hand move fully. Active shoulder elevation has not yet been tested. He speaks quietly and answers most questions with 'yeah'. His niece, who travelled with him, says the family wants to be involved, and an Aboriginal Liaison Officer is available on the ward.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Suspect a rotator cuff tear and ask the orthopaedic team to arrange imaging before strengthening): Rotator cuff tears are common after shoulder dislocation in people over 60 and present as loss of active elevation with near full passive range once pain settles. Intact deltoid sensation and contraction make an axillary nerve injury unlikely. Progressing exercise or mobilising before the tear is confirmed risks worsening it, and early capsulitis would limit passive range."}, {"id": 1287, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "On day five nursing staff report that Leonard's right hand feels cold and tingling, and he reports an aching arm. The physiotherapist finds a weak radial pulse and capillary refill of 4 seconds in the fingers, with pain 7/10. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Loosen the sling, encourage finger exercises to improve circulation, and recheck the pulse in an hour", "B": "Position the arm comfortably unloaded and urgently alert the medical team for vascular assessment", "C": "Document the findings, elevate the arm and raise them at the next ward round", "D": "Ask nursing staff to apply a warm pack and monitor hand colour and pulse hourly"}, "cluster_id": "CHS82", "cluster_text": "Leonard Wilson, 74, is an Aboriginal man from a remote community about 600 km away, admitted to an orthopaedic ward after tripping on a step. He has a right anterior shoulder dislocation, reduced in the emergency department yesterday; X-ray shows no fracture. This is his third dislocation in two years. Medical history: type 2 diabetes and hypertension. He is right-hand dominant. Findings today: arm in a sling, pain 5/10 at rest, radial pulse present, sensation over the lateral upper arm normal, deltoid contracts isometrically, elbow, wrist and hand move fully. Active shoulder elevation has not yet been tested. He speaks quietly and answers most questions with 'yeah'. His niece, who travelled with him, says the family wants to be involved, and an Aboriginal Liaison Officer is available on the ward.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Position the arm comfortably unloaded and urgently alert the medical team for vascular assessment): A weak radial pulse, slow capillary refill, a cold tingling hand and rising pain after a shoulder dislocation suggest vascular injury, which needs prompt medical assessment. The arm should be rested in an unloaded position while the team is alerted urgently. Loosening the sling and waiting an hour, or raising the issue at a later round, delay assessment, and a warm pack with hourly checks monitors the problem without treating it. Elevation also reduces arterial inflow."}, {"id": 1288, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Leonard says 'yeah' to most questions and looks away when asked about going home. His niece wants to be involved, and an Aboriginal Liaison Officer is on the ward. How should the physiotherapist plan discharge education and decisions in a culturally safe way?", "options": {"A": "Ask Leonard who he wants involved, offer the liaison officer, and check his understanding", "B": "Direct questions to his niece as family spokesperson so she can make decisions on his behalf", "C": "Use the standard discharge assessment in one efficient visit and book a review near his home", "D": "Accept his short replies as agreement, give a written handout and confirm with his niece"}, "cluster_id": "CHS82", "cluster_text": "Leonard Wilson, 74, is an Aboriginal man from a remote community about 600 km away, admitted to an orthopaedic ward after tripping on a step. He has a right anterior shoulder dislocation, reduced in the emergency department yesterday; X-ray shows no fracture. This is his third dislocation in two years. Medical history: type 2 diabetes and hypertension. He is right-hand dominant. Findings today: arm in a sling, pain 5/10 at rest, radial pulse present, sensation over the lateral upper arm normal, deltoid contracts isometrically, elbow, wrist and hand move fully. Active shoulder elevation has not yet been tested. He speaks quietly and answers most questions with 'yeah'. His niece, who travelled with him, says the family wants to be involved, and an Aboriginal Liaison Officer is available on the ward.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ask Leonard who he wants involved, offer the liaison officer, and check his understanding): Culturally safe care starts with asking the patient how he wants to be involved, offering the liaison officer, and allowing time for his own views, rather than assuming who speaks for him. Short replies or reduced eye contact may reflect respect, anxiety or discomfort rather than agreement or understanding. Checking understanding supports a safe discharge to a remote community."}, {"id": 1289, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Foster has had night pain in his foot for two weeks, relieved by hanging the leg down. He also has an ankle brachial index of 0.5, absent pulses and a non-healing ulcer. What do these findings most strongly suggest, and what is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Neuro-ischaemic diabetic ulcer: continue offloading and wound care, with vascular review at a routine GP visit", "B": "Intermittent claudication progressing slowly: start a supervised walking programme and review the ulcer weekly", "C": "Painful diabetic neuropathy: refer to the GP for medication review and continue offloading and ulcer care", "D": "Possible chronic limb-threatening ischaemia: arrange urgent medical and vascular review through the GP or hospital"}, "cluster_id": "CHS83", "cluster_text": "Tyrone Foster, 58, an Aboriginal man and farm contractor, attends a rural community health clinic where you are the only physiotherapist. He has had type 2 diabetes for 15 years, with peripheral neuropathy and peripheral arterial disease. He has a 1 cm plantar ulcer under the first metatarsal head for three weeks, with a thin rim of callus and no pus or spreading redness. Monofilament sensation is absent at the forefoot. Pedal pulses are not palpable, the foot is cool, capillary refill is slow, and the practice nurse recorded an ankle brachial index of 0.5 last week. He gets calf pain after 100 metres of walking, and for two weeks has woken at night with foot pain relieved by hanging the leg over the bed. A neighbour who works at clinic reception has asked you for an update on how he is going.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Possible chronic limb-threatening ischaemia: arrange urgent medical and vascular review through the GP or hospital): Night pain relieved by hanging the leg down, an ankle brachial index of 0.5, absent pulses and a non-healing ulcer suggest chronic limb-threatening ischaemia, which needs urgent vascular review. Recognising an ischaemic ulcer but waiting for a routine GP visit underestimates the urgency. Stable claudication does not cause rest pain and tissue loss, and neuropathic pain is not relieved by dependency, so it does not explain this pattern."}, {"id": 1290, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "While awaiting vascular review, Mr Foster asks what he can safely do for the forefoot ulcer and for staying active. He has an open ulcer and neuropathy, and his farm work involves long periods on his feet. What is the most appropriate advice?", "options": {"A": "Offload the forefoot, limit time on his feet, and use seated or non-weight-bearing exercise", "B": "Continue daily farm walking in cushioned protective boots with a forefoot pad, because activity supports circulation", "C": "Rest in bed with the leg raised on pillows and stop activity until the ulcer closes", "D": "Wear a total contact cast and continue usual farm work, since casting offloads the ulcer"}, "cluster_id": "CHS83", "cluster_text": "Tyrone Foster, 58, an Aboriginal man and farm contractor, attends a rural community health clinic where you are the only physiotherapist. He has had type 2 diabetes for 15 years, with peripheral neuropathy and peripheral arterial disease. He has a 1 cm plantar ulcer under the first metatarsal head for three weeks, with a thin rim of callus and no pus or spreading redness. Monofilament sensation is absent at the forefoot. Pedal pulses are not palpable, the foot is cool, capillary refill is slow, and the practice nurse recorded an ankle brachial index of 0.5 last week. He gets calf pain after 100 metres of walking, and for two weeks has woken at night with foot pain relieved by hanging the leg over the bed. A neighbour who works at clinic reception has asked you for an update on how he is going.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Offload the forefoot, limit time on his feet, and use seated or non-weight-bearing exercise): With an open plantar ulcer, neuropathy and poor perfusion, forefoot pressure must be reduced, time on his feet limited, and exercise kept to seated or non-weight-bearing forms. Continued farm walking, even in padded boots, still loads the wound for long periods. A total contact cast is a strong offloading method but is generally avoided when circulation is critically reduced. Bed rest with the leg raised lowers perfusion in a foot that is already short of blood supply."}, {"id": 1291, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Before vascular review, Mr Foster often walks barefoot at home on hot concrete and checks his feet only when they hurt. Which foot self-care advice is most important for the physiotherapist to give, given his neuropathy and reduced blood flow?", "options": {"A": "Inspect his feet each morning by touch, wear protective footwear outdoors, and report any new pain", "B": "Moisturise soles and between the toes nightly, and keep the feet warm with a heat pack", "C": "Check his feet at the clinic fortnightly, wear thick socks at home, and report any blisters", "D": "Inspect both feet daily including soles, wear protective footwear indoors, and report new skin damage early"}, "cluster_id": "CHS83", "cluster_text": "Tyrone Foster, 58, an Aboriginal man and farm contractor, attends a rural community health clinic where you are the only physiotherapist. He has had type 2 diabetes for 15 years, with peripheral neuropathy and peripheral arterial disease. He has a 1 cm plantar ulcer under the first metatarsal head for three weeks, with a thin rim of callus and no pus or spreading redness. Monofilament sensation is absent at the forefoot. Pedal pulses are not palpable, the foot is cool, capillary refill is slow, and the practice nurse recorded an ankle brachial index of 0.5 last week. He gets calf pain after 100 metres of walking, and for two weeks has woken at night with foot pain relieved by hanging the leg over the bed. A neighbour who works at clinic reception has asked you for an update on how he is going.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Inspect both feet daily including soles, wear protective footwear indoors, and report new skin damage early): Neuropathy removes the pain warning, so he needs daily visual inspection of the whole foot including the soles, protective footwear indoors as well as outdoors, and early reporting of skin damage. Checking by touch, relying on pain or occasional clinic checks misses injuries that are not felt. Heat on a numb, poorly perfused foot risks burns, and moisture between the toes encourages skin breakdown."}, {"id": 1292, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Foster's neighbour, who works at clinic reception, asks you for an update on how his foot is going because she wants to help him. Mr Foster has not given permission. What should the physiotherapist do, and what should the record show?", "options": {"A": "Share only general progress, since she is a clinic colleague, and record that she was informed", "B": "Give no details but leave the request out of his notes, to keep the record concise", "C": "Share the findings as she works in the clinic and may coordinate his care", "D": "Decline to share details without his consent, offer to ask him, and record the request"}, "cluster_id": "CHS83", "cluster_text": "Tyrone Foster, 58, an Aboriginal man and farm contractor, attends a rural community health clinic where you are the only physiotherapist. He has had type 2 diabetes for 15 years, with peripheral neuropathy and peripheral arterial disease. He has a 1 cm plantar ulcer under the first metatarsal head for three weeks, with a thin rim of callus and no pus or spreading redness. Monofilament sensation is absent at the forefoot. Pedal pulses are not palpable, the foot is cool, capillary refill is slow, and the practice nurse recorded an ankle brachial index of 0.5 last week. He gets calf pain after 100 metres of walking, and for two weeks has woken at night with foot pain relieved by hanging the leg over the bed. A neighbour who works at clinic reception has asked you for an update on how he is going.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Decline to share details without his consent, offer to ask him, and record the request): Health information may be shared only with the person's consent or for a recognised care purpose, and working at the clinic does not give a staff member a right to his details. Asking Mr Foster respects his choice and privacy, and recording the request and response gives an accurate account. Sharing general progress still discloses information, and leaving the request out removes evidence of how privacy was handled."}, {"id": 1293, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Mutasa has bilateral great toe extensor weakness, absent ankle reflexes, reduced sensation over the perineum and new difficulty starting to pass urine over the past day. Which interpretation of these findings should most influence the physiotherapist's next step in the department?", "options": {"A": "Features indicate a urinary tract infection with referred pain, needing urinalysis before any neurological concern", "B": "Features suggest possible cauda equina compression, a surgical emergency needing urgent medical review and imaging", "C": "Findings suggest peripheral neuropathy, best investigated through outpatient nerve conduction studies and GP follow up", "D": "Findings fit bilateral lumbar radiculopathy from disc herniation, suitable for conservative physiotherapy management first"}, "cluster_id": "CHS84", "cluster_text": "Emeka Mutasa, 45, warehouse worker, presents to the emergency department with three days of low back pain, now with heaviness in both legs. Over the past day he has had difficulty starting to pass urine and numbness when wiping. No trauma, no known cancer, no fever. Pain 6/10. You are the physiotherapist in the department's primary contact service. Observations: HR 82, BP 128/76 mmHg, temperature 36.8 C, SpO2 98 percent. Neurological findings: bilateral great toe extension 3/5, ankle reflexes absent on both sides, reduced light touch over the perineum, positive right straight leg raise. He lies on a trolley in a corridor bay. The registrar is attending a resuscitation and the department is busy. An experienced allied health assistant is working alongside you.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Features suggest possible cauda equina compression, a surgical emergency needing urgent medical review and imaging): Bilateral leg signs with perineal sensory change and new bladder difficulty is the classic red flag cluster for cauda equina compression, where delay risks permanent loss of bladder, bowel and sexual function. Radiculopathy and neuropathy do not explain the saddle and bladder features, and attributing symptoms to infection delays the neurological work up."}, {"id": 1294, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "The physiotherapist suspects cauda equina compression in Mr Mutasa, who is lying on a trolley in a corridor bay. The medical registrar is attending a resuscitation. Mr Mutasa's bladder symptoms are progressing. What is the most appropriate immediate action for the physiotherapist to take?", "options": {"A": "Interrupt the resuscitation team to request the registrar, because delay to decompression worsens outcomes", "B": "Ask the allied health assistant to monitor his legs and bladder and report any change", "C": "Complete a full neurological examination first, including reflexes and myotomes, so the escalation is thoroughly documented", "D": "Escalate directly to the nurse in charge or senior doctor, stating concern for cauda equina"}, "cluster_id": "CHS84", "cluster_text": "Emeka Mutasa, 45, warehouse worker, presents to the emergency department with three days of low back pain, now with heaviness in both legs. Over the past day he has had difficulty starting to pass urine and numbness when wiping. No trauma, no known cancer, no fever. Pain 6/10. You are the physiotherapist in the department's primary contact service. Observations: HR 82, BP 128/76 mmHg, temperature 36.8 C, SpO2 98 percent. Neurological findings: bilateral great toe extension 3/5, ankle reflexes absent on both sides, reduced light touch over the perineum, positive right straight leg raise. He lies on a trolley in a corridor bay. The registrar is attending a resuscitation and the department is busy. An experienced allied health assistant is working alongside you.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Escalate directly to the nurse in charge or senior doctor, stating concern for cauda equina): A suspected surgical emergency needs prompt escalation to the nearest available senior clinician, naming cauda equina so it is triaged correctly. Interrupting a resuscitation pulls the registrar from a patient who needs them more urgently, and the nurse in charge or another senior doctor can act. Delegating monitoring to an assistant or extending the examination delays the escalation that matters most."}, {"id": 1295, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Mutasa reports reduced bladder fullness awareness and bilateral leg heaviness. He is alert, cooperative and haemodynamically stable. While medical review is arranged, the physiotherapist wants to document a further examination finding. Which finding would most strongly support cauda equina syndrome?", "options": {"A": "Marked loss of lumbar flexion range with visible paraspinal muscle guarding on observation", "B": "Pain that centralises toward the spine with repeated movements into lumbar extension", "C": "Positive straight leg raise on the right with leg pain reproduced at a low angle", "D": "Reduced light touch and pinprick sensation over the saddle area on both sides"}, "cluster_id": "CHS84", "cluster_text": "Emeka Mutasa, 45, warehouse worker, presents to the emergency department with three days of low back pain, now with heaviness in both legs. Over the past day he has had difficulty starting to pass urine and numbness when wiping. No trauma, no known cancer, no fever. Pain 6/10. You are the physiotherapist in the department's primary contact service. Observations: HR 82, BP 128/76 mmHg, temperature 36.8 C, SpO2 98 percent. Neurological findings: bilateral great toe extension 3/5, ankle reflexes absent on both sides, reduced light touch over the perineum, positive right straight leg raise. He lies on a trolley in a corridor bay. The registrar is attending a resuscitation and the department is busy. An experienced allied health assistant is working alongside you.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reduced light touch and pinprick sensation over the saddle area on both sides): Bilateral saddle sensory loss reflects compromise of the sacral nerve roots and is a key supporting sign. A unilateral positive straight leg raise points to a single root, guarding and reduced flexion are nonspecific mechanical findings, and centralisation of pain suggests a mechanical disc problem with a good prognosis."}, {"id": 1296, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "A nurse asks that the allied health assistant, who is experienced in mobility, walk Mr Mutasa to the toilet and check his leg strength afterwards. His neurological signs are evolving. What is the most appropriate decision regarding this proposed delegation?", "options": {"A": "Accept the delegation only if the nurse takes full responsibility for the outcome of the activity", "B": "Delegate the walk with clear written instructions and reassess his strength personally on return", "C": "Decline, because his condition is changing and uncertain, and clarify mobility with the medical team", "D": "Delegate the task, because an experienced assistant can recognise any deterioration and call for help"}, "cluster_id": "CHS84", "cluster_text": "Emeka Mutasa, 45, warehouse worker, presents to the emergency department with three days of low back pain, now with heaviness in both legs. Over the past day he has had difficulty starting to pass urine and numbness when wiping. No trauma, no known cancer, no fever. Pain 6/10. You are the physiotherapist in the department's primary contact service. Observations: HR 82, BP 128/76 mmHg, temperature 36.8 C, SpO2 98 percent. Neurological findings: bilateral great toe extension 3/5, ankle reflexes absent on both sides, reduced light touch over the perineum, positive right straight leg raise. He lies on a trolley in a corridor bay. The registrar is attending a resuscitation and the department is busy. An experienced allied health assistant is working alongside you.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Decline, because his condition is changing and uncertain, and clarify mobility with the medical team): Delegation suits stable patients with predictable needs. A patient with evolving neurological deficit needs clinician level judgement, and safe mobility should be agreed with the medical team first. The physiotherapist remains accountable for the decision to delegate, so written instructions, an assistant's experience or the nurse accepting responsibility do not make it appropriate."}, {"id": 1297, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Ngo has tenderness over the greater trochanter, pain within seconds of single leg stance, painful resisted abduction and a full, pain-free passive hip range. Which interpretation of these findings is most appropriate for her presentation at this stage of the assessment?", "options": {"A": "Acute bursal inflammation is the main cause, so rest from walking should be the first step", "B": "Gluteal tendon pain at the greater trochanter is more likely than an intra-articular hip source", "C": "Referred pain from an irritated lumbar nerve root is likely despite the clear lumbar examination", "D": "Joint space narrowing on the X-ray confirms hip osteoarthritis as the main source of her pain"}, "cluster_id": "CHS85", "cluster_text": "Lan Ngo, 58, a Vietnamese-speaking cleaner, attends a public hospital outpatient clinic with 4 months of right lateral hip pain. Pain is worst lying on the right side at night, climbing stairs and after standing through a shift. No trauma. BMI 31. A hip X-ray arranged by her GP showed mild joint space narrowing. Examination: tender over the right greater trochanter; single leg stance on the right reproduces lateral hip pain within 20 seconds; resisted hip abduction is 4/5 and painful; passive hip flexion and internal rotation are full and pain-free; lumbar movements are clear; neurological screen is normal. A professional telephone interpreter has been booked. Her daughter, who came with her, says she is happy to translate everything so the session will be faster.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Gluteal tendon pain at the greater trochanter is more likely than an intra-articular hip source): Lateral tenderness, a positive single leg stance test and painful resisted abduction with a normal passive hip range point to the gluteal tendons rather than the joint. Mild narrowing on imaging is common at her age and does not explain a pain pattern that the passive hip movements do not reproduce. Lumbar referral is unlikely when lumbar movements and the neurological screen are clear."}, {"id": 1298, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Ngo sleeps on her right side and often stands with her weight shifted onto the right hip. She asks what she can change at home straight away. Which advice best reduces compressive load on the painful lateral hip tendons during her daily positions?", "options": {"A": "Place a pillow between her knees when side-lying and avoid crossing her legs", "B": "Stay off stairs and walking until the lateral hip pain has fully settled over several weeks", "C": "Sleep on the right side to desensitise the tendon through gradual exposure to pressure", "D": "Stretch the iliotibial band by pulling the right knee across the body several times daily"}, "cluster_id": "CHS85", "cluster_text": "Lan Ngo, 58, a Vietnamese-speaking cleaner, attends a public hospital outpatient clinic with 4 months of right lateral hip pain. Pain is worst lying on the right side at night, climbing stairs and after standing through a shift. No trauma. BMI 31. A hip X-ray arranged by her GP showed mild joint space narrowing. Examination: tender over the right greater trochanter; single leg stance on the right reproduces lateral hip pain within 20 seconds; resisted hip abduction is 4/5 and painful; passive hip flexion and internal rotation are full and pain-free; lumbar movements are clear; neurological screen is normal. A professional telephone interpreter has been booked. Her daughter, who came with her, says she is happy to translate everything so the session will be faster.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Place a pillow between her knees when side-lying and avoid crossing her legs): Hip adduction squeezes the gluteal tendons against the greater trochanter, so keeping the thigh from drifting across the midline in lying, sitting and standing reduces compression. Stretching into adduction increases the same compression and tends to aggravate the pain. Lying on the painful side adds direct pressure, and prolonged avoidance of walking reduces tendon capacity instead of building it."}, {"id": 1299, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "After education, Mrs Ngo is ready to begin exercise. Her pain is 6 out of 10 on single leg stance, it wakes her at night, and she has done no regular exercise for years. Which first loading approach is most appropriate for her gluteal tendon pain?", "options": {"A": "Single leg hopping drills to restore tendon capacity before slower strengthening is attempted", "B": "Side-lying hip abduction with ankle weights, lowering the leg across the midline on each repetition", "C": "Deep gluteal stretching into hip adduction to lengthen the painful tendon before any strengthening starts", "D": "Pain-tolerable isometric hip abduction holds in neutral, then slow isotonic loading later"}, "cluster_id": "CHS85", "cluster_text": "Lan Ngo, 58, a Vietnamese-speaking cleaner, attends a public hospital outpatient clinic with 4 months of right lateral hip pain. Pain is worst lying on the right side at night, climbing stairs and after standing through a shift. No trauma. BMI 31. A hip X-ray arranged by her GP showed mild joint space narrowing. Examination: tender over the right greater trochanter; single leg stance on the right reproduces lateral hip pain within 20 seconds; resisted hip abduction is 4/5 and painful; passive hip flexion and internal rotation are full and pain-free; lumbar movements are clear; neurological screen is normal. A professional telephone interpreter has been booked. Her daughter, who came with her, says she is happy to translate everything so the session will be faster.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Pain-tolerable isometric hip abduction holds in neutral, then slow isotonic loading later): An irritable, night-waking tendon pain in a deconditioned patient calls for low-compression isometric loading in neutral that she can tolerate, with progression to slow heavy loading as symptoms allow. Lowering the leg across the midline and stretching into adduction both add compression to the tendon. Hopping is a late-stage energy-storage task and is too demanding for her current capacity."}, {"id": 1300, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "The telephone interpreter is connected. Mrs Ngo's daughter again offers to interpret and says her mother does not need a stranger hearing private health details. Mrs Ngo has not stated her own preference. What is the most appropriate way to proceed with the session?", "options": {"A": "Accept the daughter as interpreter, since she knows the history and sessions will run faster", "B": "Ask the daughter to translate while the interpreter listens in and corrects any errors afterwards", "C": "Use the interpreter for consent only, then let the daughter translate the clinical questions and exercises", "D": "Use the interpreter to explain confidentiality and ask Mrs Ngo directly what she prefers"}, "cluster_id": "CHS85", "cluster_text": "Lan Ngo, 58, a Vietnamese-speaking cleaner, attends a public hospital outpatient clinic with 4 months of right lateral hip pain. Pain is worst lying on the right side at night, climbing stairs and after standing through a shift. No trauma. BMI 31. A hip X-ray arranged by her GP showed mild joint space narrowing. Examination: tender over the right greater trochanter; single leg stance on the right reproduces lateral hip pain within 20 seconds; resisted hip abduction is 4/5 and painful; passive hip flexion and internal rotation are full and pain-free; lumbar movements are clear; neurological screen is normal. A professional telephone interpreter has been booked. Her daughter, who came with her, says she is happy to translate everything so the session will be faster.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Use the interpreter to explain confidentiality and ask Mrs Ngo directly what she prefers): The patient's own wishes decide who assists, and she can only give them if a professional interpreter explains her options and confidentiality in her language. Family members may filter, shorten or soften information and may not be appropriate for sensitive history or consent. Limiting the interpreter to consent leaves the assessment and exercise teaching open to the same errors."}, {"id": 1301, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "The physiotherapist screens Gail for early complications of a displaced distal radius fracture and reduction before discharge. Which structure is most commonly compressed after this injury, and is therefore checked by asking about tingling and testing sensation across the thumb, index and middle fingers?", "options": {"A": "The ulnar nerve at the cubital tunnel, from prolonged elbow flexion inside the back slab", "B": "The superficial radial nerve over the distal radius, from swelling beneath a tight bandage", "C": "The median nerve within the carpal tunnel, from swelling and the immobilised wrist position", "D": "The anterior interosseous nerve at the proximal forearm, causing weak pinch but preserved skin sensation"}, "cluster_id": "CHS86", "cluster_text": "Gail Drummond, 74, a retired schoolteacher, slipped on wet bathroom tiles at home and landed on her outstretched right (dominant) hand. In the emergency department, X-ray shows a dorsally displaced, extra-articular distal radius fracture. The fracture was reduced under a local anaesthetic block and a below-elbow back slab was applied. Before reduction she had a 'dinner fork' deformity and pain of 8/10; now pain is 3/10. Radial pulse is present, capillary refill is 2 seconds, and she can wiggle her fingers. HR 80 bpm, BP 136/82 mmHg. She has lost height over the years and has never had a bone density scan. She is a widow, lives alone in a single-storey home, and her son lives interstate. She holds a Medicare card only, with no private health insurance and no aged care services. She no longer drives and is anxious about paying for therapy and getting to appointments. The physiotherapist sees her before discharge.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The median nerve within the carpal tunnel, from swelling and the immobilised wrist position): The median nerve passes through the carpal tunnel, and swelling or a flexed immobilised wrist after a distal radius fracture commonly compresses it, producing tingling across the thumb, index and middle fingers. The ulnar nerve supplies the little finger side and a below-elbow slab does not hold the elbow flexed. The superficial radial nerve supplies the back of the thumb web, not the palmar fingers tested, and the anterior interosseous nerve is purely motor."}, {"id": 1302, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Gail asks what she can do while the slab stays on for the next few weeks. She is worried about her fingers and shoulder becoming stiff. Which advice should the physiotherapist give about movement and positioning of the injured arm at home?", "options": {"A": "Rest the arm in a sling and start finger movements once swelling has settled", "B": "Remove the slab each day for gentle wrist movements to prevent stiffness while the fracture heals", "C": "Use the injured hand firmly for light household tasks to maintain grip strength during immobilisation", "D": "Raise the hand and move fingers, elbow and shoulder through full range regularly each day"}, "cluster_id": "CHS86", "cluster_text": "Gail Drummond, 74, a retired schoolteacher, slipped on wet bathroom tiles at home and landed on her outstretched right (dominant) hand. In the emergency department, X-ray shows a dorsally displaced, extra-articular distal radius fracture. The fracture was reduced under a local anaesthetic block and a below-elbow back slab was applied. Before reduction she had a 'dinner fork' deformity and pain of 8/10; now pain is 3/10. Radial pulse is present, capillary refill is 2 seconds, and she can wiggle her fingers. HR 80 bpm, BP 136/82 mmHg. She has lost height over the years and has never had a bone density scan. She is a widow, lives alone in a single-storey home, and her son lives interstate. She holds a Medicare card only, with no private health insurance and no aged care services. She no longer drives and is anxious about paying for therapy and getting to appointments. The physiotherapist sees her before discharge.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Raise the hand and move fingers, elbow and shoulder through full range regularly each day): Elevation limits swelling, and regular full range movement of the uncovered joints prevents the finger and shoulder stiffness that is common in older adults after wrist fractures. Prolonged sling use invites a stiff shoulder, and delaying finger movement lets oedema stiffen the hand. The slab protects the reduction, so removing it or loading the wrist risks losing the position."}, {"id": 1303, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Two days later Gail phones the clinic. She reports burning pain that is worsening despite analgesia, tight swollen fingers, and pins and needles. Her fingers look pale, and moving them is very painful. The slab feels tight. What should the physiotherapist advise or do first?", "options": {"A": "Loosen the outer bandage layers herself, elevate the arm, and phone again if symptoms persist", "B": "Advise elevation and analgesia, then arrange a clinic visit the next morning to check the slab", "C": "Direct her to return to the emergency department urgently for assessment of the slab", "D": "Recheck her finger colour and sensation by phone after thirty minutes of elevation and analgesia"}, "cluster_id": "CHS86", "cluster_text": "Gail Drummond, 74, a retired schoolteacher, slipped on wet bathroom tiles at home and landed on her outstretched right (dominant) hand. In the emergency department, X-ray shows a dorsally displaced, extra-articular distal radius fracture. The fracture was reduced under a local anaesthetic block and a below-elbow back slab was applied. Before reduction she had a 'dinner fork' deformity and pain of 8/10; now pain is 3/10. Radial pulse is present, capillary refill is 2 seconds, and she can wiggle her fingers. HR 80 bpm, BP 136/82 mmHg. She has lost height over the years and has never had a bone density scan. She is a widow, lives alone in a single-storey home, and her son lives interstate. She holds a Medicare card only, with no private health insurance and no aged care services. She no longer drives and is anxious about paying for therapy and getting to appointments. The physiotherapist sees her before discharge.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Direct her to return to the emergency department urgently for assessment of the slab): Worsening pain despite analgesia, pallor, pins and needles and pain on finger movement under a tight slab are red flags for a constrictive cast or developing compartment syndrome, which threatens limb viability and needs same day medical assessment. Self-loosening, elevation and a later recheck are reasonable for mild swelling but delay care when these signs are already present. A clinic visit tomorrow or a phone recheck gives the compartment too long to deteriorate."}, {"id": 1304, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Gail says she cannot afford private physiotherapy and no longer drives. She wants to recover hand function but is considering skipping rehabilitation. As an entry-level physiotherapist, which response best helps her access appropriate, affordable care after the slab comes off?", "options": {"A": "Explain Medicare rebates private physiotherapy, so she can book a clinic and claim back", "B": "Provide a written home exercise sheet and rely on self-directed recovery, to avoid travel and cost", "C": "Advise her to apply for NDIS funding, since a wrist injury creates a disability needing therapy", "D": "Refer her to public outpatient hand therapy and suggest a My Aged Care assessment"}, "cluster_id": "CHS86", "cluster_text": "Gail Drummond, 74, a retired schoolteacher, slipped on wet bathroom tiles at home and landed on her outstretched right (dominant) hand. In the emergency department, X-ray shows a dorsally displaced, extra-articular distal radius fracture. The fracture was reduced under a local anaesthetic block and a below-elbow back slab was applied. Before reduction she had a 'dinner fork' deformity and pain of 8/10; now pain is 3/10. Radial pulse is present, capillary refill is 2 seconds, and she can wiggle her fingers. HR 80 bpm, BP 136/82 mmHg. She has lost height over the years and has never had a bone density scan. She is a widow, lives alone in a single-storey home, and her son lives interstate. She holds a Medicare card only, with no private health insurance and no aged care services. She no longer drives and is anxious about paying for therapy and getting to appointments. The physiotherapist sees her before discharge.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Refer her to public outpatient hand therapy and suggest a My Aged Care assessment): A public hospital outpatient service removes the fee barrier, and a My Aged Care assessment can open home support and transport help for someone over 65 who lives alone. Medicare does not generally rebate private physiotherapy sessions. NDIS access is for people who acquire a permanent disability before 65, so a recent fracture would not qualify. Self-directed recovery alone leaves her at risk of a stiff, weak hand."}, {"id": 1305, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Graham's cast has just been removed after six weeks of immobilisation. His hand is swollen and stiff, his fist cannot close fully, and wrist movement is limited in every direction. What is the most appropriate focus of treatment at this first session after the cast comes off?", "options": {"A": "Radiocarpal mobilisation alone, leaving finger exercises until wrist range returns, to avoid overloading healing bone", "B": "Resisted grip work with therapy putty, to rebuild forearm muscle lost while in plaster", "C": "Oedema control with elevation plus active finger, thumb, wrist and forearm movement in comfortable range", "D": "Firm passive wrist stretching into extension with overpressure, to regain lost range before skin heals"}, "cluster_id": "CHS87", "cluster_text": "Graham Whitlock, 64, sheep farmer, attends the outpatient clinic of a rural hospital. Six weeks ago he fell from a ute tray onto his outstretched right (dominant) hand, sustaining a dorsally displaced distal radius fracture (Colles) that was reduced in the emergency department and immobilised in plaster. The cast came off today. Findings: dorsal hand and wrist oedema, fingertips 3 cm short of the palm in a fist, wrist flexion 25 degrees, extension 20 degrees, supination 50 degrees, pronation 60 degrees, pain 4/10 on movement, light touch intact over median and ulnar territories, radial pulse present, dry peeling skin. He has well-controlled hypertension. Graham is the physiotherapist's neighbour. He wants to resume lambing in six weeks and offers cash and a leg of lamb to be seen at his farm gate in the evenings.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Oedema control with elevation plus active finger, thumb, wrist and forearm movement in comfortable range): After cast removal the early priorities are reducing swelling and restoring active movement of the whole limb, because oedema and stiff fingers limit later function. Forceful passive stretching and early resisted work load a recently healed fracture and can aggravate pain and swelling. Delaying finger movement leaves the stiffest joints neglected."}, {"id": 1306, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Two weeks later Graham reports constant burning pain that is out of proportion to what he does. His hand has shiny skin and a colour change, and even light clothing touching it is painful. How should the physiotherapist interpret and manage this new presentation at this review?", "options": {"A": "Suspect complex regional pain syndrome, tell the doctor promptly, and continue gentle graded movement", "B": "Suspect carpal tunnel compression, splint the wrist in neutral full time, and review in a fortnight", "C": "Suspect a deep vein thrombosis in the arm, stop all movement and arrange imaging today", "D": "Treat it as expected stiffness and increase passive stretching, since pain usually settles once range improves"}, "cluster_id": "CHS87", "cluster_text": "Graham Whitlock, 64, sheep farmer, attends the outpatient clinic of a rural hospital. Six weeks ago he fell from a ute tray onto his outstretched right (dominant) hand, sustaining a dorsally displaced distal radius fracture (Colles) that was reduced in the emergency department and immobilised in plaster. The cast came off today. Findings: dorsal hand and wrist oedema, fingertips 3 cm short of the palm in a fist, wrist flexion 25 degrees, extension 20 degrees, supination 50 degrees, pronation 60 degrees, pain 4/10 on movement, light touch intact over median and ulnar territories, radial pulse present, dry peeling skin. He has well-controlled hypertension. Graham is the physiotherapist's neighbour. He wants to resume lambing in six weeks and offers cash and a leg of lamb to be seen at his farm gate in the evenings.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Suspect complex regional pain syndrome, tell the doctor promptly, and continue gentle graded movement): Disproportionate burning pain, skin changes and allodynia after a fracture suggest complex regional pain syndrome, which needs early medical input alongside gentle graded movement and desensitisation. Aggressive stretching tends to worsen it. Splinting full time and stopping movement both promote further stiffness and do not address the picture."}, {"id": 1307, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Graham again asks to be seen at his farm gate after hours for cash, and invites the physiotherapist to a family barbecue as thanks. Everyone in the small town knows them both. What is the most appropriate response to this offer from a current patient?", "options": {"A": "Accept cash for evening visits because rural access is limited, and keep a private record", "B": "Say care continues in the hospital service, decline payment and gifts, and stay professional", "C": "Agree to evening visits but decline the barbecue, since a modest declared gift is acceptable", "D": "Transfer him to another clinician and end contact, since treating a neighbour is prohibited"}, "cluster_id": "CHS87", "cluster_text": "Graham Whitlock, 64, sheep farmer, attends the outpatient clinic of a rural hospital. Six weeks ago he fell from a ute tray onto his outstretched right (dominant) hand, sustaining a dorsally displaced distal radius fracture (Colles) that was reduced in the emergency department and immobilised in plaster. The cast came off today. Findings: dorsal hand and wrist oedema, fingertips 3 cm short of the palm in a fist, wrist flexion 25 degrees, extension 20 degrees, supination 50 degrees, pronation 60 degrees, pain 4/10 on movement, light touch intact over median and ulnar territories, radial pulse present, dry peeling skin. He has well-controlled hypertension. Graham is the physiotherapist's neighbour. He wants to resume lambing in six weeks and offers cash and a leg of lamb to be seen at his farm gate in the evenings.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Say care continues in the hospital service, decline payment and gifts, and stay professional): In small communities dual relationships are common, and the duty is to manage them by keeping care within the service, avoiding private arrangements and payments, and documenting appropriately. Private cash visits fall outside the employer's governance and notes. Treating neighbours is not prohibited, so an automatic transfer is unnecessary."}, {"id": 1308, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Four weeks later radiographs confirm union, pain is 2/10 and wrist extension has reached 50 degrees. Graham still needs to lift lambs and drag feed bags before the lambing season starts. What is the most appropriate way to progress his rehabilitation now?", "options": {"A": "Introduce graded resisted grip and forearm loading, then build towards lambing-type tasks", "B": "Limit him to isometric holds for another twelve weeks, until the doctor repeats the radiographs", "C": "Delay resistance until passive range is full in every plane, because loading risks refracture", "D": "Begin heavy lamb-lifting simulation now, since confirmed union means the bone tolerates full loading"}, "cluster_id": "CHS87", "cluster_text": "Graham Whitlock, 64, sheep farmer, attends the outpatient clinic of a rural hospital. Six weeks ago he fell from a ute tray onto his outstretched right (dominant) hand, sustaining a dorsally displaced distal radius fracture (Colles) that was reduced in the emergency department and immobilised in plaster. The cast came off today. Findings: dorsal hand and wrist oedema, fingertips 3 cm short of the palm in a fist, wrist flexion 25 degrees, extension 20 degrees, supination 50 degrees, pronation 60 degrees, pain 4/10 on movement, light touch intact over median and ulnar territories, radial pulse present, dry peeling skin. He has well-controlled hypertension. Graham is the physiotherapist's neighbour. He wants to resume lambing in six weeks and offers cash and a leg of lamb to be seen at his farm gate in the evenings.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Introduce graded resisted grip and forearm loading, then build towards lambing-type tasks): Once union is confirmed and pain has settled, progressive loading is the way to restore strength and work capacity, and it should be graded towards his specific tasks. Waiting for full passive range delays strengthening needlessly. Jumping to full heavy loading ignores the need for staged conditioning."}, {"id": 1309, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Karim's right ankle brachial index is 0.5, his foot is cool and the foot pulses are absent. What does this finding most strongly indicate about his ulcer and the planning of his treatment? He asks you what it means for healing.", "options": {"A": "Venous return is impaired, so graded compression and leg elevation should be started to promote healing", "B": "Plantar pressure is the main barrier, so total contact casting should begin before any vascular opinion", "C": "Arterial supply is significantly reduced, so healing is at risk and vascular review is needed", "D": "Neuropathy is the main driver, so footwear change and offloading will determine the healing outcome"}, "cluster_id": "CHS88", "cluster_text": "Abdul Karim, 69, retired bus driver, referred by his GP to outpatient physiotherapy for a 1.5 cm plantar ulcer under the right first metatarsal head, present for five weeks. Type 2 diabetes for 20 years, peripheral neuropathy (cannot feel a 10 g monofilament on either forefoot) and peripheral arterial disease. Right ankle brachial index 0.5, foot pulses not palpable, foot cool with dependent rubor. He walks about 50 metres before calf pain forces him to stop. He wears flat slippers at home. A wound nurse dresses the ulcer weekly. No fever, no spreading redness, no odour today. He lives with his wife, Amal. His son Hassan has phoned reception asking for an update on his father's care.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Arterial supply is significantly reduced, so healing is at risk and vascular review is needed): A low ankle brachial index with absent pulses and a cool foot points to significant arterial insufficiency, which limits oxygen delivery to the wound and calls for vascular review before healing can be expected. Compression and elevation suit venous disease and can worsen ischaemia here. Offloading and casting address pressure from neuropathy, but casting over a poorly perfused foot is risky and neither explains the perfusion findings."}, {"id": 1310, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Hassan phones reception and asks the physiotherapist for details of his father's ulcer and treatment plan, saying he is worried. Reception has passed the message to you. Mr Karim has not named anyone to receive his health information. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Confirm only that the appointment occurred and describe the exercise advice given, leaving out the wound", "B": "Ask Hassan for written proof of his relationship and then release the clinical notes to him", "C": "Share the plan briefly, as a close family member has an implied right to updates", "D": "Decline to share details, offer to ask Mr Karim about consent, and document the call"}, "cluster_id": "CHS88", "cluster_text": "Abdul Karim, 69, retired bus driver, referred by his GP to outpatient physiotherapy for a 1.5 cm plantar ulcer under the right first metatarsal head, present for five weeks. Type 2 diabetes for 20 years, peripheral neuropathy (cannot feel a 10 g monofilament on either forefoot) and peripheral arterial disease. Right ankle brachial index 0.5, foot pulses not palpable, foot cool with dependent rubor. He walks about 50 metres before calf pain forces him to stop. He wears flat slippers at home. A wound nurse dresses the ulcer weekly. No fever, no spreading redness, no odour today. He lives with his wife, Amal. His son Hassan has phoned reception asking for an update on his father's care.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Decline to share details, offer to ask Mr Karim about consent, and document the call): Health information belongs to the patient, and disclosure to a relative needs his consent unless a specific legal exception applies. Confirming attendance or part of the plan is still a disclosure. Proof of kinship does not replace consent, so you protect privacy, seek permission from Mr Karim and record what happened."}, {"id": 1311, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Karim wants to keep walking for his claudication, but the ulcer sits under the first metatarsal head. He is keen to stay active and has an upcoming appointment with the podiatrist. What is the most appropriate advice about walking and footwear at this stage?", "options": {"A": "Stop all walking until the ulcer heals and use a wheelchair, since foot loading delays healing", "B": "Continue unrestricted daily walking to his claudication limit, as exercise improves wound circulation", "C": "Walk in soft-soled slippers indoors and sturdy shoes outdoors, with no other changes", "D": "Wear a prescribed offloading device, keep weight-bearing walking short and rest at moderate calf pain"}, "cluster_id": "CHS88", "cluster_text": "Abdul Karim, 69, retired bus driver, referred by his GP to outpatient physiotherapy for a 1.5 cm plantar ulcer under the right first metatarsal head, present for five weeks. Type 2 diabetes for 20 years, peripheral neuropathy (cannot feel a 10 g monofilament on either forefoot) and peripheral arterial disease. Right ankle brachial index 0.5, foot pulses not palpable, foot cool with dependent rubor. He walks about 50 metres before calf pain forces him to stop. He wears flat slippers at home. A wound nurse dresses the ulcer weekly. No fever, no spreading redness, no odour today. He lives with his wife, Amal. His son Hassan has phoned reception asking for an update on his father's care.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Wear a prescribed offloading device, keep weight-bearing walking short and rest at moderate calf pain): Offloading protects the ulcer from repetitive pressure while limited walking keeps some activity for his vascular condition. Unrestricted walking increases pressure on the wound. Complete avoidance of walking causes deconditioning, and ordinary footwear does not redistribute pressure away from the metatarsal head."}, {"id": 1312, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "At review, the ulcer has purulent drainage and redness spreading up the dorsum of the foot. Mr Karim has a temperature of 38.4 degrees Celsius, HR 104 and feels shivery. His wife says he has been unwell since yesterday. What is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Dress the wound, advise rest and elevation, and rebook in a week to reassess", "B": "Ask his GP to prescribe oral antibiotics and continue the offloading program until review", "C": "Arrange same-day urgent medical assessment, as this suggests a limb-threatening infection", "D": "Ask the wound nurse to bring the dressing forward and swab the ulcer"}, "cluster_id": "CHS88", "cluster_text": "Abdul Karim, 69, retired bus driver, referred by his GP to outpatient physiotherapy for a 1.5 cm plantar ulcer under the right first metatarsal head, present for five weeks. Type 2 diabetes for 20 years, peripheral neuropathy (cannot feel a 10 g monofilament on either forefoot) and peripheral arterial disease. Right ankle brachial index 0.5, foot pulses not palpable, foot cool with dependent rubor. He walks about 50 metres before calf pain forces him to stop. He wears flat slippers at home. A wound nurse dresses the ulcer weekly. No fever, no spreading redness, no odour today. He lives with his wife, Amal. His son Hassan has phoned reception asking for an update on his father's care.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Arrange same-day urgent medical assessment, as this suggests a limb-threatening infection): Spreading redness, pus, fever and a raised heart rate in a person with diabetes and poor arterial supply indicate a serious infection that can threaten the limb within days. He needs a face to face medical assessment the same day, with the possibility of admission and intravenous treatment. Prescribing by phone, bringing the dressing forward or rebooking a review all keep him in the community while systemic signs progress."}, {"id": 1313, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Kalinowski's auscultation findings are localised to the right lower zone after she aspirated while sitting upright at dinner. The medical team asks why the right lower lobe is the most likely site of aspiration pneumonia in this position. Which explanation is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "The right lung has greater volume and surface area, so more aspirated material settles there", "B": "The right main bronchus is wider and more vertical, directing aspirate into the right lower lobe", "C": "In upright sitting, gravity carries aspirate to the posterior segment of the right upper lobe", "D": "Weak cough and slowed mucociliary clearance in Parkinson's disease leave secretions pooling in the right base"}, "cluster_id": "CHS89", "cluster_text": "Mrs Dorothy Kalinowski, 84, widowed, lives alone in a unit. Admitted four days ago with aspiration pneumonia after choking at dinner. History: mild Parkinson's disease, hypertension. Today: RR 24, HR 96, temperature 37.8, SpO2 93% on 2 L/min nasal prongs. Auscultation: coarse crackles right lower zone, reduced air entry at the right base. Her cough is weak and wet, and she tires after ten minutes sitting out of bed. Speech pathology has modified her diet. Before admission she walked independently with a stick and had no formal services. Her daughter lives interstate. She is alert, orientated and keen to go home. Discharge is planned in three days. She asks how she could pay for physiotherapy and help at home, and whether the government can assist. She is not a veteran and has no compensable claim.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The right main bronchus is wider and more vertical, directing aspirate into the right lower lobe): The right main bronchus is shorter, wider and more vertical than the left, so inhaled material usually enters it, and in upright sitting it falls to the dependent basal segments of the lower lobe. The upper lobe posterior segment is the usual site when the person is lying flat. Parkinson's disease weakens the cough on both sides, so it adds risk but does not explain the right sided location, and lung size does not direct the aspirate."}, {"id": 1314, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "On review Mrs Kalinowski is sitting out of bed, SpO2 93% on 2 L/min, with a weak wet cough and coarse crackles at the right base. She tires after ten minutes upright. What is the most appropriate physiotherapy treatment for this session?", "options": {"A": "Incentive spirometry in high sitting, ten deep breaths hourly, to reopen the right base", "B": "Head-down tilt in left side-lying with percussion for fifteen minutes to drain the right lower lobe", "C": "Left side-lying, then active cycle of breathing with supported huffs, pausing to rest and check SpO2", "D": "Manual hyperinflation with a bagging circuit while lying flat to mobilise secretions from the right base"}, "cluster_id": "CHS89", "cluster_text": "Mrs Dorothy Kalinowski, 84, widowed, lives alone in a unit. Admitted four days ago with aspiration pneumonia after choking at dinner. History: mild Parkinson's disease, hypertension. Today: RR 24, HR 96, temperature 37.8, SpO2 93% on 2 L/min nasal prongs. Auscultation: coarse crackles right lower zone, reduced air entry at the right base. Her cough is weak and wet, and she tires after ten minutes sitting out of bed. Speech pathology has modified her diet. Before admission she walked independently with a stick and had no formal services. Her daughter lives interstate. She is alert, orientated and keen to go home. Discharge is planned in three days. She asks how she could pay for physiotherapy and help at home, and whether the government can assist. She is not a veteran and has no compensable claim.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Left side-lying, then active cycle of breathing with supported huffs, pausing to rest and check SpO2): Left side-lying puts the affected right lower lobe uppermost so secretions drain, and active cycle of breathing with supported huffs suits an awake patient with a weak cough. Rests and saturation checks respect her fatigue. Head-down tilt raises aspiration risk in a patient with swallowing difficulty, and manual hyperinflation is for ventilated patients."}, {"id": 1315, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Later that day during mobilisation, Mrs Kalinowski becomes drowsy and confused. SpO2 falls to 89% on her usual oxygen, respiratory rate is 30, heart rate 114 and she feels hot. She was alert this morning. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Stop activity, sit her upright and activate the ward's deterioration response for urgent medical review", "B": "Return her to bed, start supervised airway clearance, and reassess her observations after twenty minutes", "C": "Increase her oxygen flow, continue mobilising with a seated rest, and recheck observations in ten minutes", "D": "Stop activity, sit her upright and tell the nurse caring for her to review her shortly"}, "cluster_id": "CHS89", "cluster_text": "Mrs Dorothy Kalinowski, 84, widowed, lives alone in a unit. Admitted four days ago with aspiration pneumonia after choking at dinner. History: mild Parkinson's disease, hypertension. Today: RR 24, HR 96, temperature 37.8, SpO2 93% on 2 L/min nasal prongs. Auscultation: coarse crackles right lower zone, reduced air entry at the right base. Her cough is weak and wet, and she tires after ten minutes sitting out of bed. Speech pathology has modified her diet. Before admission she walked independently with a stick and had no formal services. Her daughter lives interstate. She is alert, orientated and keen to go home. Discharge is planned in three days. She asks how she could pay for physiotherapy and help at home, and whether the government can assist. She is not a veteran and has no compensable claim.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stop activity, sit her upright and activate the ward's deterioration response for urgent medical review): New confusion with hypoxaemia, tachypnoea, tachycardia and fever signals acute clinical deterioration, possibly sepsis, and needs immediate escalation through the local deterioration response so a medical team attends. Telling her nurse to review later correctly stops activity but does not trigger urgent medical review. Airway clearance or further mobilising adds stress to an unstable patient, and waiting to recheck delays treatment."}, {"id": 1316, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Kalinowski will leave hospital in three days and lives alone, aged 84, with no compensable claim or veteran status. Her daughter lives interstate. She asks how she could receive physiotherapy and help at home afterwards. Which advice is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Ask the hospital to fund community physiotherapy because the aspiration episode makes it responsible afterwards", "B": "Start a My Aged Care referral so she can be assessed for subsidised home support", "C": "Lodge an NDIS access request, since it funds physiotherapy for adults with significant functional decline", "D": "Advise that only private fee-for-service physiotherapy exists, and give her a list of local private clinics"}, "cluster_id": "CHS89", "cluster_text": "Mrs Dorothy Kalinowski, 84, widowed, lives alone in a unit. Admitted four days ago with aspiration pneumonia after choking at dinner. History: mild Parkinson's disease, hypertension. Today: RR 24, HR 96, temperature 37.8, SpO2 93% on 2 L/min nasal prongs. Auscultation: coarse crackles right lower zone, reduced air entry at the right base. Her cough is weak and wet, and she tires after ten minutes sitting out of bed. Speech pathology has modified her diet. Before admission she walked independently with a stick and had no formal services. Her daughter lives interstate. She is alert, orientated and keen to go home. Discharge is planned in three days. She asks how she could pay for physiotherapy and help at home, and whether the government can assist. She is not a veteran and has no compensable claim.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Start a My Aged Care referral so she can be assessed for subsidised home support): My Aged Care is the Australian gateway for assessment and subsidised in-home and community services for older people. NDIS access is generally for people who apply before 65, so it does not suit her. Hospitals do not fund ongoing community care, and limiting her to private clinics ignores available subsidised services."}, {"id": 1317, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Tran's pain spreads to both buttocks and legs without a dermatomal pattern, is worse with stress and poor sleep, and she has tenderness to light touch over wide areas. Imaging shows mild degeneration. Which interpretation of these features is most appropriate for the physiotherapist to take?", "options": {"A": "An undetected disc herniation is compressing a nerve root and needs repeat imaging before treatment", "B": "Distress is the only driver, so physical findings can be set aside when planning treatment", "C": "Progressive degenerative change at multiple levels is the main driver and predicts steady worsening ahead", "D": "Altered central pain processing is likely contributing, and imaging changes do not fully explain it"}, "cluster_id": "CHS90", "cluster_text": "Thuy Tran, 52, a former cleaner who arrived from Vietnam 12 years ago, attends an outpatient clinic with low back pain for three years and no injury. Pain averages 7/10, spreads to both buttocks and legs without a clear nerve root pattern, and is worse with stress and poor sleep. MRI shows mild multilevel disc degeneration. She has had many passive treatments and asks for another scan and an injection. Findings: lumbar flexion limited by fear, tenderness to light touch over wide areas, neurological examination normal, straight leg raise negative, no red flags. She sleeps about four hours a night, has not worked for six months, and her Pain Self-Efficacy Questionnaire score is low. She speaks limited English. Her adult son has come with her and offers to interpret; a professional Vietnamese telephone interpreter can be booked.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Altered central pain processing is likely contributing, and imaging changes do not fully explain it): Non-dermatomal spread, sensitivity to light touch and links with stress and sleep point to altered central pain processing, which fits mild imaging findings that are common in people without pain. A disc herniation would produce a root pattern with neurological signs, which are absent. Degeneration on imaging does not predict steady worsening."}, {"id": 1318, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Tran has had many passive treatments without lasting relief and fears that bending will damage her spine. Examination found no red flags or neurological deficit, and she hopes to return to light work. Which initial treatment approach is most appropriate for her persistent pain?", "options": {"A": "Explain pain science simply, then start graded activity and sleep strategies toward her work goals", "B": "Prescribe a lumbar brace and flexion avoidance advice to protect the degenerated discs during daily tasks", "C": "Start a heavy lumbar strengthening programme three times weekly to build trunk capacity quickly", "D": "Arrange a course of spinal manipulation and heat to reduce pain before introducing any exercise"}, "cluster_id": "CHS90", "cluster_text": "Thuy Tran, 52, a former cleaner who arrived from Vietnam 12 years ago, attends an outpatient clinic with low back pain for three years and no injury. Pain averages 7/10, spreads to both buttocks and legs without a clear nerve root pattern, and is worse with stress and poor sleep. MRI shows mild multilevel disc degeneration. She has had many passive treatments and asks for another scan and an injection. Findings: lumbar flexion limited by fear, tenderness to light touch over wide areas, neurological examination normal, straight leg raise negative, no red flags. She sleeps about four hours a night, has not worked for six months, and her Pain Self-Efficacy Questionnaire score is low. She speaks limited English. Her adult son has come with her and offers to interpret; a professional Vietnamese telephone interpreter can be booked.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Explain pain science simply, then start graded activity and sleep strategies toward her work goals): With persistent pain, central sensitisation and fear of movement, education that reframes pain, combined with graded activity and attention to sleep, is supported by current practice and addresses her goals. More passive care repeats an approach that has not helped, and bracing reinforces fear. Heavy loading too early is likely to provoke a flare and deepen fear."}, {"id": 1319, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Three weeks into graded walking, Mrs Tran reports two days of 8/10 pain after a longer walk. There are no new neurological symptoms, fever or weight loss, and she wants to stop exercising and see a doctor about an injection. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Reassure her, rescreen, ease walking to a tolerable level, then rebuild using a flare plan", "B": "Request urgent lumbar imaging and hold exercise until a doctor has reviewed the results", "C": "Increase the walking distance further, since flares show the nervous system is adapting to loading", "D": "Pause exercise until pain returns to its baseline, then restart at the level before the flare"}, "cluster_id": "CHS90", "cluster_text": "Thuy Tran, 52, a former cleaner who arrived from Vietnam 12 years ago, attends an outpatient clinic with low back pain for three years and no injury. Pain averages 7/10, spreads to both buttocks and legs without a clear nerve root pattern, and is worse with stress and poor sleep. MRI shows mild multilevel disc degeneration. She has had many passive treatments and asks for another scan and an injection. Findings: lumbar flexion limited by fear, tenderness to light touch over wide areas, neurological examination normal, straight leg raise negative, no red flags. She sleeps about four hours a night, has not worked for six months, and her Pain Self-Efficacy Questionnaire score is low. She speaks limited English. Her adult son has come with her and offers to interpret; a professional Vietnamese telephone interpreter can be booked.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reassure her, rescreen, ease walking to a tolerable level, then rebuild using a flare plan): Flares are common when activity increases in persistent pain, and without red flags the best response is reassurance, temporary pacing and a planned return to the programme. Complete rest tends to reinforce fear and deconditioning. Imaging is not indicated without new warning signs, and pushing the distance up during a flare is likely to deepen distress."}, {"id": 1320, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Tran's adult son offers to interpret to save time and says his mother does not need a stranger involved. She speaks limited English, and the assessment includes sensitive questions about mood, work and finances. How should the physiotherapist proceed today?", "options": {"A": "Ask the Vietnamese speaking receptionist to interpret, since she is available and knows the clinic", "B": "Accept her son as interpreter once she agrees, since he knows her history and saves time", "C": "Use a professional interpreter, explain why, and let her son stay as support if she agrees", "D": "Use simple English with gestures and handouts translated online, checking comprehension through her nodding"}, "cluster_id": "CHS90", "cluster_text": "Thuy Tran, 52, a former cleaner who arrived from Vietnam 12 years ago, attends an outpatient clinic with low back pain for three years and no injury. Pain averages 7/10, spreads to both buttocks and legs without a clear nerve root pattern, and is worse with stress and poor sleep. MRI shows mild multilevel disc degeneration. She has had many passive treatments and asks for another scan and an injection. Findings: lumbar flexion limited by fear, tenderness to light touch over wide areas, neurological examination normal, straight leg raise negative, no red flags. She sleeps about four hours a night, has not worked for six months, and her Pain Self-Efficacy Questionnaire score is low. She speaks limited English. Her adult son has come with her and offers to interpret; a professional Vietnamese telephone interpreter can be booked.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use a professional interpreter, explain why, and let her son stay as support if she agrees): A qualified interpreter keeps meaning accurate, protects confidentiality and avoids a family member filtering sensitive topics, so one should be used whenever English is limited and the content is complex or personal. Her son can stay as support only if she chooses. Bilingual staff who are not trained interpreters, and online translation of handouts, can distort clinical meaning."}, {"id": 1321, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Before starting standing strength exercises, Mrs Karagiannis becomes dizzy when standing for three minutes and her blood pressure falls from 138/78 to 112/66. She has two blood pressure tablets and a night sedative. What is the most appropriate management at this session?", "options": {"A": "Fit compression stockings and continue standing exercise, as they correct the pressure drop on their own", "B": "Continue standing strength work, as dizziness on standing is common with ageing and improves with training", "C": "Advise her to limit fluids during the day so she wakes less often to pass urine", "D": "Report the postural drop to her GP, teach staged rising, and keep exercise supported"}, "cluster_id": "CHS91", "cluster_text": "Mrs Eleni Karagiannis, 82, lives alone in her own unit with a Home Care Package arranged through My Aged Care, and attends a community aged care program where you work. She had two falls in six months, both when rising at night, with no injury. She is frail, walks with a four wheeled walker and has osteoarthritis of both knees. Medications include two blood pressure tablets and a night time sedative, reviewed by her GP last month. Timed Up and Go is 19 seconds and she completes 6 chair stands in 30 seconds. Supine BP is 138/78. After standing for three minutes it is 112/66 and she reports feeling dizzy. Her goal is to stay at home and garden. Her package manager reports that the monthly package budget for allied health is almost used.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report the postural drop to her GP, teach staged rising, and keep exercise supported): A drop on standing with dizziness suggests orthostatic hypotension, which raises falls risk and may relate to her tablets, so the prescriber needs to know. Supported exercise and staged rising keep her active safely in the meantime. Assuming it improves with training, or treating it with stockings alone, leaves the cause unexamined."}, {"id": 1322, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Karagiannis wants a plan that reduces her falls and helps her garden. Her postural drop has been reviewed and a medication change made. Which exercise prescription is best supported by evidence for her frailty and falls risk over coming months?", "options": {"A": "A daily supervised walk at a comfortable pace along a flat path, as the main intervention", "B": "Heavy resistance training of the upper limbs only, to improve the strength needed for gardening tasks", "C": "Daily seated range of movement exercises at low effort, to prevent injury and maintain flexibility safely", "D": "Progressive leg strength and balance training several times weekly, made harder as she improves"}, "cluster_id": "CHS91", "cluster_text": "Mrs Eleni Karagiannis, 82, lives alone in her own unit with a Home Care Package arranged through My Aged Care, and attends a community aged care program where you work. She had two falls in six months, both when rising at night, with no injury. She is frail, walks with a four wheeled walker and has osteoarthritis of both knees. Medications include two blood pressure tablets and a night time sedative, reviewed by her GP last month. Timed Up and Go is 19 seconds and she completes 6 chair stands in 30 seconds. Supine BP is 138/78. After standing for three minutes it is 112/66 and she reports feeling dizzy. Her goal is to stay at home and garden. Her package manager reports that the monthly package budget for allied health is almost used.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Progressive leg strength and balance training several times weekly, made harder as she improves): Progressive resistance and challenging balance training, done regularly and progressed, has the strongest evidence for reducing falls and frailty. Low effort seated movement, simple walking and upper limb work do not load the leg muscles or challenge balance enough to change her risk."}, {"id": 1323, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "The care team asks you to summarise what evidence supports for preventing falls in older people living at home. Mrs Karagiannis has several risk factors, including weak legs, poor balance, sedating medication and night time rising. Which combination of strategies is best supported?", "options": {"A": "Balance and strength exercise, medication review and home hazard assessment, matched to her risks", "B": "Home hazard modification and a medication review, with exercise deferred until her falls have stopped", "C": "Seated group exercise and vitamin D supplements, with home hazard checks left to her package manager", "D": "A personal alarm pendant, hip protectors and supervised walking, with exercise limited to lower exposure"}, "cluster_id": "CHS91", "cluster_text": "Mrs Eleni Karagiannis, 82, lives alone in her own unit with a Home Care Package arranged through My Aged Care, and attends a community aged care program where you work. She had two falls in six months, both when rising at night, with no injury. She is frail, walks with a four wheeled walker and has osteoarthritis of both knees. Medications include two blood pressure tablets and a night time sedative, reviewed by her GP last month. Timed Up and Go is 19 seconds and she completes 6 chair stands in 30 seconds. Supine BP is 138/78. After standing for three minutes it is 112/66 and she reports feeling dizzy. Her goal is to stay at home and garden. Her package manager reports that the monthly package budget for allied health is almost used.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Balance and strength exercise, medication review and home hazard assessment, matched to her risks): Falls have several causes, and the best supported prevention for people at home combines exercise that challenges balance and strength with medication review and home hazard assessment matched to individual risk. Hazard and medication changes help but deferring exercise leaves her leg weakness and poor balance untreated. Seated classes and vitamin D do not train balance, and alarms and protectors reduce harm from a fall rather than preventing it."}, {"id": 1324, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Her package manager says the allied health budget for this month is almost spent, but Mrs Karagiannis needs ongoing sessions to continue her programme. She asks what you can do to keep exercising. What is the most appropriate approach given the funding limit?", "options": {"A": "Review fund use with her and the manager, then plan fewer visits plus a home programme", "B": "Discharge her this month because funding has run out, and suggest she re-refer later", "C": "Ask her family to pay privately for sessions and leave the package budget for other services", "D": "Continue weekly sessions as usual, then invoice her for the amount over budget at month end"}, "cluster_id": "CHS91", "cluster_text": "Mrs Eleni Karagiannis, 82, lives alone in her own unit with a Home Care Package arranged through My Aged Care, and attends a community aged care program where you work. She had two falls in six months, both when rising at night, with no injury. She is frail, walks with a four wheeled walker and has osteoarthritis of both knees. Medications include two blood pressure tablets and a night time sedative, reviewed by her GP last month. Timed Up and Go is 19 seconds and she completes 6 chair stands in 30 seconds. Supine BP is 138/78. After standing for three minutes it is 112/66 and she reports feeling dizzy. Her goal is to stay at home and garden. Her package manager reports that the monthly package budget for allied health is almost used.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Review fund use with her and the manager, then plan fewer visits plus a home programme): Package funds belong to the client, so she should help decide how they are used, and a home programme with fewer supervised visits extends her access. Billing after the fact, discharging her or shifting costs to family without agreement fails to put her informed decision first."}, {"id": 1325, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Deepa's pain is out of proportion to her mild MRI findings, with widespread tenderness, pain to light brush and poor sleep. Her neurological examination is normal and there are no red flags. Which interpretation best supports a pain mechanism involving central sensitisation?", "options": {"A": "Muscle deconditioning from sick leave, with tender muscles and guarding accounting for the symptoms found", "B": "Ongoing tissue damage at L4-5, as the degenerative disc explains the widespread pain and tenderness", "C": "Nerve root irritation at L4-5 with referred pain, explaining the thigh symptoms and skin sensitivity", "D": "Heightened nervous system responsiveness shown by allodynia and widespread tenderness, with sleep loss"}, "cluster_id": "CHS92", "cluster_text": "Deepa Desai, 38, warehouse supervisor, has had low back pain for 18 months with no clear injury. MRI showed mild disc degeneration at L4-5 with no nerve compression. Pain is 7/10, spreads across both buttocks and thighs, and varies from day to day. She sleeps about four hours a night, has been on sick leave for four months, and avoids bending and lifting because she fears causing damage. Examination: widespread tenderness to light palpation over the lumbar and gluteal regions, pain with a light brush over the skin, lumbar movement limited by guarding, normal neurological examination and no red flags. Her Pain Self-Efficacy Questionnaire score is low and her Fear-Avoidance Beliefs Questionnaire work score is high. She asks what is wrong with her back. The pain clinic team includes a GP and a psychologist, who have not yet met her.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Heightened nervous system responsiveness shown by allodynia and widespread tenderness, with sleep loss): Pain to light touch, widespread tenderness, disproportion to tissue findings and poor sleep are typical of a sensitised pain system. A degenerative disc is common in people without pain and does not explain skin sensitivity. A normal neurological examination argues against root irritation, and deconditioning alone does not produce allodynia."}, {"id": 1326, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Deepa asks what is wrong with her back and whether exercise could damage it. She has read that her MRI shows degeneration and fears her disc is crumbling. You decide to start with pain education. Which explanation is most appropriate for her?", "options": {"A": "The disc degeneration is the cause, so protect the back by avoiding bending until pain eases", "B": "Her pain system has become oversensitive, and graded movement is safe for her back", "C": "Her pain reflects weak core muscles, so stabiliser exercises should come before any activity", "D": "Her pain reflects stress and mood, so psychological treatment should come first while exercise is postponed"}, "cluster_id": "CHS92", "cluster_text": "Deepa Desai, 38, warehouse supervisor, has had low back pain for 18 months with no clear injury. MRI showed mild disc degeneration at L4-5 with no nerve compression. Pain is 7/10, spreads across both buttocks and thighs, and varies from day to day. She sleeps about four hours a night, has been on sick leave for four months, and avoids bending and lifting because she fears causing damage. Examination: widespread tenderness to light palpation over the lumbar and gluteal regions, pain with a light brush over the skin, lumbar movement limited by guarding, normal neurological examination and no red flags. Her Pain Self-Efficacy Questionnaire score is low and her Fear-Avoidance Beliefs Questionnaire work score is high. She asks what is wrong with her back. The pain clinic team includes a GP and a psychologist, who have not yet met her.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Her pain system has become oversensitive, and graded movement is safe for her back): Pain education reframes persistent pain as an oversensitive protective system rather than ongoing damage, which reduces fear and supports activity. Blaming the disc reinforces avoidance, and a core-first approach implies the spine is fragile. Attributing pain to mood alone dismisses her experience and delays the active management that helps."}, {"id": 1327, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Deepa agrees to start exercising. Her pain varies from day to day, and on bad days she stops all activity and rests for several days. Her goal is returning to supervising at work. Which exercise strategy is most appropriate for her?", "options": {"A": "Passive manual therapy and heat first to reduce sensitivity, introducing active exercise once pain is lower", "B": "Intensive lumbar stabilisation sessions three times weekly, increasing load once pain has resolved each week", "C": "Graded, time-based activity toward her work goal, agreed with her and kept up on bad days", "D": "Pain-contingent exercise, stopping each task at the first rise in pain and resuming when it settles"}, "cluster_id": "CHS92", "cluster_text": "Deepa Desai, 38, warehouse supervisor, has had low back pain for 18 months with no clear injury. MRI showed mild disc degeneration at L4-5 with no nerve compression. Pain is 7/10, spreads across both buttocks and thighs, and varies from day to day. She sleeps about four hours a night, has been on sick leave for four months, and avoids bending and lifting because she fears causing damage. Examination: widespread tenderness to light palpation over the lumbar and gluteal regions, pain with a light brush over the skin, lumbar movement limited by guarding, normal neurological examination and no red flags. Her Pain Self-Efficacy Questionnaire score is low and her Fear-Avoidance Beliefs Questionnaire work score is high. She asks what is wrong with her back. The pain clinic team includes a GP and a psychologist, who have not yet met her.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Graded, time-based activity toward her work goal, agreed with her and kept up on bad days): Time-based graded activity links progression to a plan rather than to pain, which breaks the boom and bust cycle common in persistent pain. Stopping at the first rise in pain reinforces fear and avoidance. Waiting for pain to resolve or relying on passive care keeps her dependent and does not build confidence in movement."}, {"id": 1328, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "After four sessions you will hand over to the pain clinic team ahead of Deepa's case conference. The GP and psychologist have not met her. You must pass on her status, what you have done and what you recommend. Which handover approach is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Send the full clinic notes and outcome questionnaires beforehand, then answer questions at the case conference", "B": "Give a short verbal summary of pain, physical findings and treatment, leaving scores in the notes", "C": "Email a written pain history to the GP and psychologist, with recommendations left for the meeting", "D": "Use ISBAR, giving concise findings, treatment so far, outcome scores and clear recommendations"}, "cluster_id": "CHS92", "cluster_text": "Deepa Desai, 38, warehouse supervisor, has had low back pain for 18 months with no clear injury. MRI showed mild disc degeneration at L4-5 with no nerve compression. Pain is 7/10, spreads across both buttocks and thighs, and varies from day to day. She sleeps about four hours a night, has been on sick leave for four months, and avoids bending and lifting because she fears causing damage. Examination: widespread tenderness to light palpation over the lumbar and gluteal regions, pain with a light brush over the skin, lumbar movement limited by guarding, normal neurological examination and no red flags. Her Pain Self-Efficacy Questionnaire score is low and her Fear-Avoidance Beliefs Questionnaire work score is high. She asks what is wrong with her back. The pain clinic team includes a GP and a psychologist, who have not yet met her.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Use ISBAR, giving concise findings, treatment so far, outcome scores and clear recommendations): A structured format such as ISBAR (identify, situation, background, assessment, recommendation) gives the whole team the key information in a predictable order, including outcome scores and what is recommended next. Sending full notes pushes the work of prioritising onto people who have not met her. A short verbal summary that omits scores, or a history without recommendations, leaves gaps the team needs to plan together."}, {"id": 1329, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Kai's pain began after training volume increased before a state squad trial, and it settles once warm. He wants to keep playing. Hop testing provokes pain of 5/10 and morning stiffness lasts only minutes. Which load management advice is most appropriate at this stage?", "options": {"A": "Modify jumping and running load so pain stays tolerable, while staying active", "B": "Stop all sport and rest the tendon for several weeks until the pain has gone", "C": "Keep training as before, add heel lifts to his shoes, and ice the tendon after sessions", "D": "Continue full training, because pain that eases with warm up shows the tendon tolerates the load"}, "cluster_id": "CHS93", "cluster_text": "Kai Tupou, 14, plays soccer and basketball and trains four times a week for a state squad. Over six weeks he has developed pain in the right mid Achilles tendon, worse for the first steps in the morning and at the start of training, easing once warm. The pain began after training volume increased before a squad trial. No injury event, no other medical history. Examination: thickening about 4 cm above the heel, tender on palpation with a positive arc sign; no tenderness at the insertion or over the calf. Single leg heel raises: 14 on the right with pain, 28 on the left. Thompson test negative. Hop test provokes pain of 5/10. Weight bearing lunge to wall: 8 cm right, 10 cm left. Kai's parents are present. The clinic employs an allied health assistant, Priya, who runs supervised gym sessions and holds current competencies for the gym equipment. You are asked to hand Kai's exercises over to her while you see other patients.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Modify jumping and running load so pain stays tolerable, while staying active): Tendinopathy usually follows a load spike, so the aim is to reduce and modify the aggravating load rather than remove all load, which keeps the tendon conditioned. Complete rest deconditions the tendon, and carrying on unchanged ignores the cause. Easing with warm up is typical of the condition and does not show the load is safe."}, {"id": 1330, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Kai's single leg heel raise is 14 on the right with pain and 28 on the left. Exercise pain is currently 3/10 and settles within a day. You plan a loading programme for his Achilles. Which exercise approach is most appropriate to begin?", "options": {"A": "Fast plyometric hopping drills in the first sessions to restore tendon stiffness and speed of force", "B": "Progressive calf loading, starting with isometric holds then slow heavy raises", "C": "Calf stretching on a step held for long periods, repeated several times through the day", "D": "Deep friction massage and ultrasound three times weekly, with no loading until the thickening has reduced"}, "cluster_id": "CHS93", "cluster_text": "Kai Tupou, 14, plays soccer and basketball and trains four times a week for a state squad. Over six weeks he has developed pain in the right mid Achilles tendon, worse for the first steps in the morning and at the start of training, easing once warm. The pain began after training volume increased before a squad trial. No injury event, no other medical history. Examination: thickening about 4 cm above the heel, tender on palpation with a positive arc sign; no tenderness at the insertion or over the calf. Single leg heel raises: 14 on the right with pain, 28 on the left. Thompson test negative. Hop test provokes pain of 5/10. Weight bearing lunge to wall: 8 cm right, 10 cm left. Kai's parents are present. The clinic employs an allied health assistant, Priya, who runs supervised gym sessions and holds current competencies for the gym equipment. You are asked to hand Kai's exercises over to her while you see other patients.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Progressive calf loading, starting with isometric holds then slow heavy raises): Progressive tendon loading, beginning with isometrics and moving to slow heavy resistance, is the core of management and the reduced heel raise capacity shows the calf complex needs strengthening. Stretching and passive modalities do not build load capacity, and fast plyometrics are a later stage that would overload a tendon still painful on hopping."}, {"id": 1331, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "You will see another patient and ask allied health assistant Priya to supervise Kai's gym sessions. Kai's pain is variable, and loading progression depends on his response to each session. Priya's competencies for the gym equipment are current. Which delegation decision is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Do not delegate anything, because exercise supervision for an adolescent must be done by the physiotherapist", "B": "Hand over the whole programme including progressions, since Priya's sign off covers the equipment and exercises", "C": "Delegate supervising the set exercises within pain limits, and keep progression decisions yourself", "D": "Delegate the sessions and let Priya adjust loads herself, then review her notes weekly"}, "cluster_id": "CHS93", "cluster_text": "Kai Tupou, 14, plays soccer and basketball and trains four times a week for a state squad. Over six weeks he has developed pain in the right mid Achilles tendon, worse for the first steps in the morning and at the start of training, easing once warm. The pain began after training volume increased before a squad trial. No injury event, no other medical history. Examination: thickening about 4 cm above the heel, tender on palpation with a positive arc sign; no tenderness at the insertion or over the calf. Single leg heel raises: 14 on the right with pain, 28 on the left. Thompson test negative. Hop test provokes pain of 5/10. Weight bearing lunge to wall: 8 cm right, 10 cm left. Kai's parents are present. The clinic employs an allied health assistant, Priya, who runs supervised gym sessions and holds current competencies for the gym equipment. You are asked to hand Kai's exercises over to her while you see other patients.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Delegate supervising the set exercises within pain limits, and keep progression decisions yourself): A physiotherapist can delegate a defined task to a competent assistant while remaining accountable for assessment, clinical reasoning and progression. Because Kai's response varies, those decisions need professional judgement. Handing over everything or letting the assistant adjust loads exceeds her role, whereas refusing to delegate at all is unnecessary when the task is clear and she is competent."}, {"id": 1332, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Kai's tendon is thickened 4 cm above the calcaneus, tender with a positive arc sign, and painful on single leg heel raises. The Thompson test is negative. He has no pain at the heel insertion. Which diagnosis do these findings most strongly support?", "options": {"A": "Insertional Achilles tendinopathy with an associated retrocalcaneal bursitis causing the posterior heel pain", "B": "Mid portion Achilles tendinopathy, with the tendon continuity intact and no sign of rupture", "C": "Calcaneal apophysitis, common in active adolescents, causing pain at the back of the heel", "D": "A partial Achilles tendon tear, since thickening and a negative Thompson test indicate fibre disruption"}, "cluster_id": "CHS93", "cluster_text": "Kai Tupou, 14, plays soccer and basketball and trains four times a week for a state squad. Over six weeks he has developed pain in the right mid Achilles tendon, worse for the first steps in the morning and at the start of training, easing once warm. The pain began after training volume increased before a squad trial. No injury event, no other medical history. Examination: thickening about 4 cm above the heel, tender on palpation with a positive arc sign; no tenderness at the insertion or over the calf. Single leg heel raises: 14 on the right with pain, 28 on the left. Thompson test negative. Hop test provokes pain of 5/10. Weight bearing lunge to wall: 8 cm right, 10 cm left. Kai's parents are present. The clinic employs an allied health assistant, Priya, who runs supervised gym sessions and holds current competencies for the gym equipment. You are asked to hand Kai's exercises over to her while you see other patients.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Mid portion Achilles tendinopathy, with the tendon continuity intact and no sign of rupture): Thickening a few centimetres above the insertion with a positive arc sign and load related pain is characteristic of mid portion tendinopathy, and a negative Thompson test shows the tendon is in continuity. Apophysitis and insertional problems cause pain at the heel itself, which Kai does not have."}, {"id": 1333, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Aisha, admitted after a viral cold, has bilateral expiratory wheeze, mild accessory muscle use, no crackles, no sputum, no fever and speaks in full sentences. The registrar requested chest physiotherapy for chest tightness. Which interpretation of these findings should guide the physiotherapist's plan?", "options": {"A": "Early viral pneumonia with developing consolidation, needing airway clearance alongside medical review", "B": "Mucus plugging with segmental collapse that needs manual techniques to mobilise secretions from the lower zones", "C": "Anxiety-driven overbreathing and air gulping, best addressed with breathing control and reassurance from staff", "D": "Airflow obstruction from bronchospasm and airway inflammation rather than a problem of retained secretions"}, "cluster_id": "CHS94", "cluster_text": "Aisha Rahman, 7, was admitted to the paediatric ward yesterday with an acute asthma exacerbation after a viral cold. She has known asthma and uses a preventer and a reliever by spacer at home. The registrar has requested chest physiotherapy for chest tightness. Today: RR 30, HR 118, SpO2 94% on room air, afebrile. She speaks in full sentences with mild accessory muscle use. Auscultation shows bilateral expiratory wheeze with no crackles and no sputum. Nursing staff give salbutamol through a spacer. Her mother says she gets frightened when her chest is tight and tends to gulp air through her mouth. A student on placement shows a small uncontrolled case series in which chest physiotherapy was said to shorten asthma admissions, and asks to include it in her plan.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Airflow obstruction from bronchospasm and airway inflammation rather than a problem of retained secretions): Expiratory wheeze without crackles, sputum or fever points to narrowed airways from bronchospasm and inflammation, not retained secretions, so airway clearance is not the priority. Pneumonia or mucus plugging with collapse would usually bring crackles, reduced air entry or fever. Her fear and gulping are real and worth coaching, but they do not explain the wheeze and accessory muscle use."}, {"id": 1334, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Aisha's mother asks how to give the reliever through the spacer correctly when Aisha is breathless and frightened at home, and says Aisha tends to gulp air. Which instruction reflects accepted technique for a young child using a spacer with a mask or mouthpiece?", "options": {"A": "Shake it, give one puff, then wait until the mist clears before she breathes in", "B": "Give one puff, then ask her to take one deep, fast breath and hold it", "C": "Fire several puffs into the spacer together to save time, then have her breathe normally", "D": "Shake it, give one puff at a time into the spacer, and let her breathe normally"}, "cluster_id": "CHS94", "cluster_text": "Aisha Rahman, 7, was admitted to the paediatric ward yesterday with an acute asthma exacerbation after a viral cold. She has known asthma and uses a preventer and a reliever by spacer at home. The registrar has requested chest physiotherapy for chest tightness. Today: RR 30, HR 118, SpO2 94% on room air, afebrile. She speaks in full sentences with mild accessory muscle use. Auscultation shows bilateral expiratory wheeze with no crackles and no sputum. Nursing staff give salbutamol through a spacer. Her mother says she gets frightened when her chest is tight and tends to gulp air through her mouth. A student on placement shows a small uncontrolled case series in which chest physiotherapy was said to shorten asthma admissions, and asks to include it in her plan.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Shake it, give one puff at a time into the spacer, and let her breathe normally): Current practice for a young child is to shake the inhaler, give a single puff into the spacer and let the child breathe normally from it, repeating for each puff. Firing several puffs together reduces the dose reaching the lungs. Waiting lets the medicine settle on the spacer walls, and a forced deep fast breath is hard for a frightened child and increases deposition in the throat."}, {"id": 1335, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "During a later review Aisha is drowsy and exhausted, can say only single words, and her chest sounds almost silent on auscultation, with SpO2 88 percent and a respiratory rate of 40. Her mother says she was less wheezy earlier. What is the most appropriate physiotherapist action?", "options": {"A": "Start gentle percussion in a side lying position to reopen her airways before help arrives", "B": "Recheck her observations in thirty minutes and ask nursing to give another reliever first", "C": "Call urgently for medical help, as the quieter chest suggests worsening airflow rather than improvement", "D": "Reassure the mother that quieter breathing shows improvement and continue with the planned breathing exercises"}, "cluster_id": "CHS94", "cluster_text": "Aisha Rahman, 7, was admitted to the paediatric ward yesterday with an acute asthma exacerbation after a viral cold. She has known asthma and uses a preventer and a reliever by spacer at home. The registrar has requested chest physiotherapy for chest tightness. Today: RR 30, HR 118, SpO2 94% on room air, afebrile. She speaks in full sentences with mild accessory muscle use. Auscultation shows bilateral expiratory wheeze with no crackles and no sputum. Nursing staff give salbutamol through a spacer. Her mother says she gets frightened when her chest is tight and tends to gulp air through her mouth. A student on placement shows a small uncontrolled case series in which chest physiotherapy was said to shorten asthma admissions, and asks to include it in her plan.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Call urgently for medical help, as the quieter chest suggests worsening airflow rather than improvement): A silent chest with drowsiness, single words and falling saturation indicates life threatening asthma, where little air is moving. Reduced wheeze here reflects severity, not recovery. Immediate medical escalation is required before any further physiotherapy, and percussion would add distress without benefit."}, {"id": 1336, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "The student suggests adding airway clearance to Aisha's plan because her small uncontrolled case series reported shorter asthma admissions with chest physiotherapy. The ward guideline and the registrar's request do not mention it. How should the physiotherapist respond when deciding whether to change Aisha's plan?", "options": {"A": "Appraise the design for bias, then compare it with current asthma guideline recommendations", "B": "Trial airway clearance with Aisha and compare her length of stay with the ward average", "C": "Check the paper reports a statistically significant shorter stay, then adopt the technique if so", "D": "Set the paper aside, as lower level evidence should not influence physiotherapy decisions in paediatric asthma"}, "cluster_id": "CHS94", "cluster_text": "Aisha Rahman, 7, was admitted to the paediatric ward yesterday with an acute asthma exacerbation after a viral cold. She has known asthma and uses a preventer and a reliever by spacer at home. The registrar has requested chest physiotherapy for chest tightness. Today: RR 30, HR 118, SpO2 94% on room air, afebrile. She speaks in full sentences with mild accessory muscle use. Auscultation shows bilateral expiratory wheeze with no crackles and no sputum. Nursing staff give salbutamol through a spacer. Her mother says she gets frightened when her chest is tight and tends to gulp air through her mouth. A student on placement shows a small uncontrolled case series in which chest physiotherapy was said to shorten asthma admissions, and asks to include it in her plan.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Appraise the design for bias, then compare it with current asthma guideline recommendations): An uncontrolled case series cannot show that a treatment caused shorter stays, because children recover naturally and there is no comparison group. The sensible response is to appraise the design for bias and check it against current asthma guidelines before changing the plan. A significant result does not remove bias, a single child's stay cannot test the technique, and dismissing low level evidence outright wastes a prompt to look at the guidelines."}, {"id": 1337, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Margaret can raise her arm only to about 70 degrees actively with shoulder hitching, yet passively reaches 150 degrees, and she shows an external rotation lag. What do these findings most likely indicate about the cause of her restricted active movement?", "options": {"A": "Subacromial bursitis causing reflex pain inhibition that settles quickly with relative rest and analgesia", "B": "Loss of active cuff control from a tendon tear, rather than capsular stiffness", "C": "Suprascapular nerve entrapment causing isolated infraspinatus wasting with a normal passive range and painful arc", "D": "Adhesive capsulitis limiting active and passive elevation equally in a capsular pattern of restriction"}, "cluster_id": "CHS95", "cluster_text": "Margaret Kowalski, 71, a retired teacher, attends an outpatient clinic with six weeks of right shoulder pain and weakness after lifting a suitcase onto a luggage rack. She is right hand dominant, wakes at night when lying on the shoulder, and has type 2 diabetes and hypertension, both well controlled. Findings: active elevation 70 degrees with marked shoulder hitching; passive elevation 150 degrees with a soft end feel; drop arm test positive; external rotation lag sign positive; supraspinatus strength 2/5, infraspinatus 3/5; neurological screen normal; pain 6/10 on active elevation. Ultrasound shows a 2 cm full thickness supraspinatus tear. After discussion Margaret chooses a trial of non-operative rehabilitation, with a surgical opinion if she does not improve. The clinic has an allied health assistant who runs supervised gym sessions, and Margaret will attend twice weekly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Loss of active cuff control from a tendon tear, rather than capsular stiffness): A large gap between active and passive elevation with a lag sign points to failure of the cuff to control the humerus, as in a full thickness tear. A stiff capsule would limit passive range as much as active range. Bursitis rarely produces a true lag, and a nerve lesion would show a neurological pattern that was absent here."}, {"id": 1338, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Margaret chooses non-operative care for a 2 cm full thickness tear. She has pain of 6/10 with active elevation and no neurological loss, sleeps poorly and tires quickly with effort. Which initial exercise approach is most appropriate for her first weeks of rehabilitation?", "options": {"A": "Pulley elevation to full range with light resistance band external rotation from the first week", "B": "Pain-limited assisted elevation, scapular setting and gentle isometrics kept below shoulder height", "C": "Sling rest with pendulum swings for the first six weeks, delaying any active elevation or isometrics", "D": "Joint mobilisation into end range elevation and stretching to prevent frozen shoulder, with rest from strengthening"}, "cluster_id": "CHS95", "cluster_text": "Margaret Kowalski, 71, a retired teacher, attends an outpatient clinic with six weeks of right shoulder pain and weakness after lifting a suitcase onto a luggage rack. She is right hand dominant, wakes at night when lying on the shoulder, and has type 2 diabetes and hypertension, both well controlled. Findings: active elevation 70 degrees with marked shoulder hitching; passive elevation 150 degrees with a soft end feel; drop arm test positive; external rotation lag sign positive; supraspinatus strength 2/5, infraspinatus 3/5; neurological screen normal; pain 6/10 on active elevation. Ultrasound shows a 2 cm full thickness supraspinatus tear. After discussion Margaret chooses a trial of non-operative rehabilitation, with a surgical opinion if she does not improve. The clinic has an allied health assistant who runs supervised gym sessions, and Margaret will attend twice weekly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Pain-limited assisted elevation, scapular setting and gentle isometrics kept below shoulder height): Early rehabilitation of a painful cuff tear uses low load, pain-limited movement and scapular control to build tolerance before strengthening is progressed. Full range pulley work with band resistance loads a 2/5 cuff too soon. A prolonged sling rest risks stiffness and weakness, and end range stretching irritates a painful shoulder that has soft end feel and no capsular restriction."}, {"id": 1339, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "After eight weeks of rehabilitation Margaret's pain has eased to 2/10, but active elevation remains about 80 degrees with a persistent lag despite good adherence, and she cannot lift a kettle. She wants to return to gardening. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Continue the same programme unchanged for a further twelve weeks to allow strength to return gradually", "B": "Discharge her with a home programme, since persisting weakness means further input will not help", "C": "Ask the GP for a corticosteroid injection so she can start stronger resisted work without pain", "D": "Discuss her progress and arrange referral back to the GP or an orthopaedic surgeon about repair"}, "cluster_id": "CHS95", "cluster_text": "Margaret Kowalski, 71, a retired teacher, attends an outpatient clinic with six weeks of right shoulder pain and weakness after lifting a suitcase onto a luggage rack. She is right hand dominant, wakes at night when lying on the shoulder, and has type 2 diabetes and hypertension, both well controlled. Findings: active elevation 70 degrees with marked shoulder hitching; passive elevation 150 degrees with a soft end feel; drop arm test positive; external rotation lag sign positive; supraspinatus strength 2/5, infraspinatus 3/5; neurological screen normal; pain 6/10 on active elevation. Ultrasound shows a 2 cm full thickness supraspinatus tear. After discussion Margaret chooses a trial of non-operative rehabilitation, with a surgical opinion if she does not improve. The clinic has an allied health assistant who runs supervised gym sessions, and Margaret will attend twice weekly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Discuss her progress and arrange referral back to the GP or an orthopaedic surgeon about repair): Her pain has improved but function has not, which was the agreed trigger for a surgical opinion. Referring on respects her original plan and her goals. Repeating an unchanged programme ignores the lack of progress, and discharging her without options leaves a functional deficit unaddressed."}, {"id": 1340, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "The clinic's allied health assistant will supervise Margaret's gym sessions twice weekly for the next month. Margaret is medically stable with controlled diabetes, but her shoulder irritability changes with load and her programme is likely to need progression. How should the physiotherapist delegate this task?", "options": {"A": "Give written parameters and stop criteria, review her regularly, and keep responsibility for progressions", "B": "Delegate after the assistant observes one session, leaving progression decisions to the assistant as pain changes", "C": "Hand over the whole programme including progressions, since the assistant works under the clinic's general supervision", "D": "Ask Margaret to report problems to the assistant, who may adjust exercises when her pain changes"}, "cluster_id": "CHS95", "cluster_text": "Margaret Kowalski, 71, a retired teacher, attends an outpatient clinic with six weeks of right shoulder pain and weakness after lifting a suitcase onto a luggage rack. She is right hand dominant, wakes at night when lying on the shoulder, and has type 2 diabetes and hypertension, both well controlled. Findings: active elevation 70 degrees with marked shoulder hitching; passive elevation 150 degrees with a soft end feel; drop arm test positive; external rotation lag sign positive; supraspinatus strength 2/5, infraspinatus 3/5; neurological screen normal; pain 6/10 on active elevation. Ultrasound shows a 2 cm full thickness supraspinatus tear. After discussion Margaret chooses a trial of non-operative rehabilitation, with a surgical opinion if she does not improve. The clinic has an allied health assistant who runs supervised gym sessions, and Margaret will attend twice weekly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Give written parameters and stop criteria, review her regularly, and keep responsibility for progressions): A physiotherapist remains accountable for a delegated task and for decisions needing clinical judgement, such as progression when irritability varies. The assistant can run a clear, defined program with stop criteria while the physiotherapist reviews the patient. Handing over progressions or adjustments exceeds the assistant's role, however much general supervision exists."}, {"id": 1341, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Cooper has a positive left straight leg raise, mild left dorsiflexion weakness and lumbar muscle spasm, together with new urinary hesitancy and reduced perianal sensation. Which interpretation is the most important for deciding how urgently his condition must be managed today?", "options": {"A": "Dorsiflexion weakness with a positive straight leg raise suggests L5 radiculopathy, which sets the urgency", "B": "Bladder and saddle sensory changes suggest possible cauda equina compression needing urgent assessment", "C": "Onset after heavy lifting suggests disc herniation, so review in a few days is reasonable", "D": "Urinary hesitancy at his age is likely prostatic, so a GP review next week suffices"}, "cluster_id": "CHS96", "cluster_text": "Darren Cooper, 47, a station hand, attends a remote health clinic 400 km from the nearest hospital. Three days ago he lifted a heavy gate and felt sudden central low back pain, now 7 out of 10 with left buttock and posterior leg pain. Since last night he has had difficulty starting urination and feels numbness in the area that sits on a saddle. Observations: HR 78, BP 135/85, temperature 36.8, SpO2 98 percent. Examination: left ankle dorsiflexion 4/5, left straight leg raise positive at 40 degrees, reduced light touch sensation around the perianal area, and lower lumbar muscle spasm. A remote area nurse is on site, a GP is available by telehealth, and retrieval would be through the Royal Flying Doctor Service. You are the visiting physiotherapist.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Bladder and saddle sensory changes suggest possible cauda equina compression needing urgent assessment): New urinary hesitancy with reduced perianal sensation is the hallmark of possible cauda equina compression, which can cause lasting loss of bladder, bowel and sexual function if decompression is delayed. The straight leg raise and mild L5 weakness fit radiculopathy and alone would be managed conservatively. A lifting history does not reduce the urgency, and attributing bladder symptoms to age or prostate misses a surgical emergency."}, {"id": 1342, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Cooper's findings raise concern for cauda equina syndrome. The remote area nurse asks what should happen next, and a medical officer is reachable by telehealth. The closest hospital is several hours away by road. What is the most appropriate immediate step for the physiotherapist?", "options": {"A": "Complete the screen of reflexes and myotomes, then phone the medical officer with the full results", "B": "Ask the telehealth medical officer for an outpatient MRI and send him home with return advice", "C": "Treat as severe radiculopathy with nurse-led pain management and review his bladder symptoms in two days", "D": "Stop examining, keep him resting, and arrange urgent medical contact for emergency retrieval"}, "cluster_id": "CHS96", "cluster_text": "Darren Cooper, 47, a station hand, attends a remote health clinic 400 km from the nearest hospital. Three days ago he lifted a heavy gate and felt sudden central low back pain, now 7 out of 10 with left buttock and posterior leg pain. Since last night he has had difficulty starting urination and feels numbness in the area that sits on a saddle. Observations: HR 78, BP 135/85, temperature 36.8, SpO2 98 percent. Examination: left ankle dorsiflexion 4/5, left straight leg raise positive at 40 degrees, reduced light touch sensation around the perianal area, and lower lumbar muscle spasm. A remote area nurse is on site, a GP is available by telehealth, and retrieval would be through the Royal Flying Doctor Service. You are the visiting physiotherapist.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stop examining, keep him resting, and arrange urgent medical contact for emergency retrieval): Suspected cauda equina compression is time-critical, so further testing stops and the medical officer is contacted at once to organise emergency retrieval for imaging and surgical review. Finishing the screen first adds delay when the signs already justify escalation. Sending him home for an outpatient scan or reviewing in two days leaves him hours from care while decompression is delayed."}, {"id": 1343, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Retrieval will take about three hours. While waiting, Mr Cooper asks whether he can walk around or have his back loosened to ease the pain. He remains neurologically stable and the nurse is recording his observations. Which physiotherapy action is most appropriate during the wait?", "options": {"A": "Apply gentle lumbar mobilisation to reduce spasm, which may lessen pressure on the irritated nerve roots", "B": "Apply a heat pack to the lumbar spasm and encourage short supported walks to ease stiffness", "C": "Support a comfortable position, avoid manual therapy, and repeat neurological and bladder checks regularly", "D": "Maintain a comfortable position and avoid manual therapy, then reassess strength and sensation once retrieval arrives"}, "cluster_id": "CHS96", "cluster_text": "Darren Cooper, 47, a station hand, attends a remote health clinic 400 km from the nearest hospital. Three days ago he lifted a heavy gate and felt sudden central low back pain, now 7 out of 10 with left buttock and posterior leg pain. Since last night he has had difficulty starting urination and feels numbness in the area that sits on a saddle. Observations: HR 78, BP 135/85, temperature 36.8, SpO2 98 percent. Examination: left ankle dorsiflexion 4/5, left straight leg raise positive at 40 degrees, reduced light touch sensation around the perianal area, and lower lumbar muscle spasm. A remote area nurse is on site, a GP is available by telehealth, and retrieval would be through the Royal Flying Doctor Service. You are the visiting physiotherapist.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Support a comfortable position, avoid manual therapy, and repeat neurological and bladder checks regularly): While waiting for emergency transfer the aim is comfort and early detection of deterioration, so he rests in a tolerable position and neurological and bladder status are rechecked regularly so changes can be handed over. Waiting until retrieval arrives to reassess could miss progression for hours. Mobilisation risks increasing compression, and heat and walking add a fall risk if weakness progresses."}, {"id": 1344, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "The medical officer joins by telehealth and asks the physiotherapist to give the clinical handover for Mr Cooper. Time is limited and the situation is urgent, with retrieval not yet arranged. Which structure for the verbal handover is most appropriate for the physiotherapist to use?", "options": {"A": "Begin with the neurological findings in detail, then identity, history and the retrieval request", "B": "Open with identity and urgent concern, then background, findings and the retrieval request", "C": "Open with identity and a full history of the lifting injury, then findings and the request", "D": "Open with identity and urgent concern, then findings, leaving the retrieval request for a later call"}, "cluster_id": "CHS96", "cluster_text": "Darren Cooper, 47, a station hand, attends a remote health clinic 400 km from the nearest hospital. Three days ago he lifted a heavy gate and felt sudden central low back pain, now 7 out of 10 with left buttock and posterior leg pain. Since last night he has had difficulty starting urination and feels numbness in the area that sits on a saddle. Observations: HR 78, BP 135/85, temperature 36.8, SpO2 98 percent. Examination: left ankle dorsiflexion 4/5, left straight leg raise positive at 40 degrees, reduced light touch sensation around the perianal area, and lower lumbar muscle spasm. A remote area nurse is on site, a GP is available by telehealth, and retrieval would be through the Royal Flying Doctor Service. You are the visiting physiotherapist.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Open with identity and urgent concern, then background, findings and the retrieval request): A structured handover such as ISBAR puts identity and the urgent concern first, then background and findings, so the receiver understands the priority and can act on the retrieval request immediately. Opening with detailed findings before identity and concern forces the officer to work out why they matter. A long history buries the red flags, and holding the request for a later call delays a time-critical decision."}, {"id": 1345, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Tanya has dynamic knee valgus on single leg squat, right hip abduction strength of 4/5, a positive step down test and lateral facet tenderness, with normal tilt and no effusion. Which impairment is the most likely modifiable contributor to her pain?", "options": {"A": "Quadriceps wasting from disuse in pregnancy, which accounts for the anterior knee pain on stairs", "B": "A tight iliotibial band pulling the patella laterally, which explains the lateral facet tenderness on palpation", "C": "Hypermobility of the patella from postpartum ligament laxity, causing maltracking during the step down test", "D": "Weak hip abductors allowing the femur to drift inward and overload the patellofemoral joint"}, "cluster_id": "CHS97", "cluster_text": "Tanya Roberts, 31, a Wiradjuri woman, attends a community health centre 12 weeks after the vaginal birth of her second child. She has had four months of right anterior knee pain, worse on stairs, squatting and sitting for long periods while breastfeeding. She wants to walk with the pram and play with her older son. No injury, no medical history, no regular medications. Examination: dynamic knee valgus on single leg squat with pain 5/10, right hip abduction strength 4/5, positive step down test, tender over the lateral patellar facet, patellar tilt normal, no effusion. Hip and lumbar screens are clear. Tanya says she felt rushed and unheard at a previous clinic. Her aunt has come with her, and an Aboriginal liaison officer works at the centre. Her family lives two hours away.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Weak hip abductors allowing the femur to drift inward and overload the patellofemoral joint): Reduced hip abductor strength with dynamic valgus on loading points to proximal control as a trainable contributor to patellofemoral load. The normal tilt test argues against a primary patellar hypermobility problem, and nothing in the findings shows quadriceps wasting. Lateral tenderness alone does not prove the iliotibial band is the driver."}, {"id": 1346, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Tanya wants to walk with her pram and look after her son. Her pain is 5/10 on stairs. She is breastfeeding and can attend only occasionally as her family live far away. Which initial management plan is most appropriate to begin?", "options": {"A": "A patellar brace worn all day, with isometric quadriceps exercise only and review in six weeks", "B": "Complete rest from stairs, squatting and walking until the pain has resolved, then return to activity", "C": "Load education and a home hip and quadriceps programme she can do independently", "D": "Weekly clinic manual therapy and taping alone, adding exercise only once the pain has settled"}, "cluster_id": "CHS97", "cluster_text": "Tanya Roberts, 31, a Wiradjuri woman, attends a community health centre 12 weeks after the vaginal birth of her second child. She has had four months of right anterior knee pain, worse on stairs, squatting and sitting for long periods while breastfeeding. She wants to walk with the pram and play with her older son. No injury, no medical history, no regular medications. Examination: dynamic knee valgus on single leg squat with pain 5/10, right hip abduction strength 4/5, positive step down test, tender over the lateral patellar facet, patellar tilt normal, no effusion. Hip and lumbar screens are clear. Tanya says she felt rushed and unheard at a previous clinic. Her aunt has come with her, and an Aboriginal liaison officer works at the centre. Her family lives two hours away.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Load education and a home hip and quadriceps programme she can do independently): Patellofemoral pain responds best to education, load management and hip and knee strengthening, and a home programme fits her limited ability to attend. Passive treatment alone is less effective and depends on frequent visits. Total rest and prolonged bracing reduce activity she needs for daily life and care of her children."}, {"id": 1347, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Tanya says she felt rushed and unheard at a previous clinic and is hesitant to speak. Her aunt came with her and a local Aboriginal liaison officer is available. How can the physiotherapist best provide culturally safe care at this first appointment?", "options": {"A": "Let Tanya choose who attends, offer the liaison officer, and take time to build trust", "B": "Follow the standard assessment script, noting her cultural background on the intake form for later reference", "C": "Invite her aunt to speak about Tanya's goals, since family guides health decisions in her community", "D": "Ask the liaison officer to lead the appointment and involve her aunt in all decisions"}, "cluster_id": "CHS97", "cluster_text": "Tanya Roberts, 31, a Wiradjuri woman, attends a community health centre 12 weeks after the vaginal birth of her second child. She has had four months of right anterior knee pain, worse on stairs, squatting and sitting for long periods while breastfeeding. She wants to walk with the pram and play with her older son. No injury, no medical history, no regular medications. Examination: dynamic knee valgus on single leg squat with pain 5/10, right hip abduction strength 4/5, positive step down test, tender over the lateral patellar facet, patellar tilt normal, no effusion. Hip and lumbar screens are clear. Tanya says she felt rushed and unheard at a previous clinic. Her aunt has come with her, and an Aboriginal liaison officer works at the centre. Her family lives two hours away.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Let Tanya choose who attends, offer the liaison officer, and take time to build trust): Culturally safe practice puts the patient in control, so Tanya decides who supports her, with time to build trust after a previous experience of feeling unheard. Speaking through her aunt or handing the visit to the liaison officer assumes roles she may not want. Following a standard script and only recording her background treats culture as a form field rather than something shaping how care is delivered."}, {"id": 1348, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "At review six weeks later Tanya has pain of 2/10 on stairs, a better single leg squat and hip abduction of 4+/5. She is breastfeeding and wants to start jogging again with her pram. What should the physiotherapist advise about progression?", "options": {"A": "Wait until she has stopped breastfeeding and her pain is 0/10 before any running is attempted", "B": "Stop strengthening now that pain has improved and use jogging itself to build the remaining strength", "C": "Begin continuous jogging for twenty minutes on alternate days, as pain of 2/10 is acceptable", "D": "Continue strengthening, then add graded walk jog intervals guided by pain response"}, "cluster_id": "CHS97", "cluster_text": "Tanya Roberts, 31, a Wiradjuri woman, attends a community health centre 12 weeks after the vaginal birth of her second child. She has had four months of right anterior knee pain, worse on stairs, squatting and sitting for long periods while breastfeeding. She wants to walk with the pram and play with her older son. No injury, no medical history, no regular medications. Examination: dynamic knee valgus on single leg squat with pain 5/10, right hip abduction strength 4/5, positive step down test, tender over the lateral patellar facet, patellar tilt normal, no effusion. Hip and lumbar screens are clear. Tanya says she felt rushed and unheard at a previous clinic. Her aunt has come with her, and an Aboriginal liaison officer works at the centre. Her family lives two hours away.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Continue strengthening, then add graded walk jog intervals guided by pain response): Return to running should build on continued strength work with a gradual exposure to impact, using symptoms to guide progression. Breastfeeding does not prevent running, and pain need not be zero to start a graded plan. Jumping straight to continuous jogging or dropping strengthening removes the capacity that is still developing."}, {"id": 1349, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Anderson's new atrial fibrillation was found on ECG after his transient symptoms. A junior colleague asks why this finding is treated as an important cause of cardioembolic stroke despite his raised blood pressure and smoking. Which explanation is the most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Irregular rhythm lowers cerebral perfusion pressure and causes repeated watershed ischaemia between arterial territories", "B": "Clots form in the left ventricle after hypertensive thickening and travel to the cerebral arteries", "C": "Clots can form in the fibrillating atria and travel to the cerebral arteries", "D": "Atrial fibrillation speeds atherosclerosis in the carotid arteries, which then narrows and occludes the lumen"}, "cluster_id": "CHS98", "cluster_text": "Mr Jack Anderson, 62, truck driver, presented to the emergency department 4 hours ago after 20 minutes of right arm weakness and slurred speech that fully resolved before arrival. BP 168/96 mmHg, HR 92 beats per minute and irregular, SpO2 97 percent on room air. ECG shows new atrial fibrillation. CT brain shows no haemorrhage. Neurological examination now normal: power 5/5, sensation intact, speech clear, NIHSS 0. He smokes 20 cigarettes a day, has a BMI of 33, drinks four to five beers most nights and does no exercise. The medical team is admitting him for observation and has asked physiotherapy to screen his mobility and give lifestyle advice. He wants to leave soon and return to work.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Clots can form in the fibrillating atria and travel to the cerebral arteries): Ineffective atrial contraction lets blood stagnate and form thrombus, which can embolise to the brain and cause transient or complete arterial occlusion. Hypertension and smoking contribute through ventricular and large artery disease, but those are not how the new rhythm causes stroke. Hypoperfusion and carotid atherosclerosis describe different stroke mechanisms from the cardioembolic one."}, {"id": 1350, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Before screening his mobility, you measure resting BP of 168/96 mmHg and an irregular HR of 92. He has no symptoms and a normal neurological examination. What is the most appropriate approach to physical screening today, given that no activity restrictions have been ordered?", "options": {"A": "Withhold physical activity until the cardiology review because exercise raises embolic risk in new atrial fibrillation", "B": "Defer mobility screening until his blood pressure has been lowered by medication and stays stable overnight", "C": "Supervised low intensity walking with symptom and observation monitoring, progressing after medical review", "D": "Commence a moderate to vigorous aerobic programme, because exercise is the key treatment for blood pressure"}, "cluster_id": "CHS98", "cluster_text": "Mr Jack Anderson, 62, truck driver, presented to the emergency department 4 hours ago after 20 minutes of right arm weakness and slurred speech that fully resolved before arrival. BP 168/96 mmHg, HR 92 beats per minute and irregular, SpO2 97 percent on room air. ECG shows new atrial fibrillation. CT brain shows no haemorrhage. Neurological examination now normal: power 5/5, sensation intact, speech clear, NIHSS 0. He smokes 20 cigarettes a day, has a BMI of 33, drinks four to five beers most nights and does no exercise. The medical team is admitting him for observation and has asked physiotherapy to screen his mobility and give lifestyle advice. He wants to leave soon and return to work.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Supervised low intensity walking with symptom and observation monitoring, progressing after medical review): Early, supervised low intensity activity with monitoring is safe in a neurologically stable patient and gives useful information for discharge planning. Vigorous exercise is premature before medical review of his rhythm and blood pressure. Withholding all activity or waiting for medication effects is unnecessarily restrictive when he is asymptomatic."}, {"id": 1351, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "During a supervised walk on the ward, Mr Anderson suddenly stops, says his words feel jumbled, and his right hand drops the cup he is holding. He appears alert and stands with support from the physiotherapist. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Sit him safely, recheck his blood pressure and monitor him for ten minutes before deciding", "B": "Sit him safely, note the time symptoms began, and call the medical emergency team", "C": "Complete a full neurological examination first, then report the findings to the nurse in charge afterwards", "D": "Lie him flat and reassure him that earlier symptoms resolved quickly, then resume walking once steadier"}, "cluster_id": "CHS98", "cluster_text": "Mr Jack Anderson, 62, truck driver, presented to the emergency department 4 hours ago after 20 minutes of right arm weakness and slurred speech that fully resolved before arrival. BP 168/96 mmHg, HR 92 beats per minute and irregular, SpO2 97 percent on room air. ECG shows new atrial fibrillation. CT brain shows no haemorrhage. Neurological examination now normal: power 5/5, sensation intact, speech clear, NIHSS 0. He smokes 20 cigarettes a day, has a BMI of 33, drinks four to five beers most nights and does no exercise. The medical team is admitting him for observation and has asked physiotherapy to screen his mobility and give lifestyle advice. He wants to leave soon and return to work.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Sit him safely, note the time symptoms began, and call the medical emergency team): New speech disturbance and arm weakness are stroke warning signs and need immediate medical escalation, with the onset time recorded because treatment decisions depend on it. Delaying to observe, examine in full or resume walking wastes time that may be critical. Reassurance based on the earlier attack is not appropriate."}, {"id": 1352, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Anderson says he smokes 20 a day, drinks most nights and does no exercise, but believes it was just a funny turn. He wants to leave and return to driving his truck. What is the most appropriate approach to support his self-management?", "options": {"A": "Explore his goals, give brief stroke risk information, and agree one achievable change", "B": "Give him a detailed written plan covering smoking, alcohol, diet and exercise for him to follow", "C": "Warn him firmly about disabling stroke risk so that fear motivates him to change his habits", "D": "Defer lifestyle advice to the GP after discharge because the acute setting suits only acute care"}, "cluster_id": "CHS98", "cluster_text": "Mr Jack Anderson, 62, truck driver, presented to the emergency department 4 hours ago after 20 minutes of right arm weakness and slurred speech that fully resolved before arrival. BP 168/96 mmHg, HR 92 beats per minute and irregular, SpO2 97 percent on room air. ECG shows new atrial fibrillation. CT brain shows no haemorrhage. Neurological examination now normal: power 5/5, sensation intact, speech clear, NIHSS 0. He smokes 20 cigarettes a day, has a BMI of 33, drinks four to five beers most nights and does no exercise. The medical team is admitting him for observation and has asked physiotherapy to screen his mobility and give lifestyle advice. He wants to leave soon and return to work.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Explore his goals, give brief stroke risk information, and agree one achievable change): A person-centred, brief intervention that builds on his own goals is more effective than a long list of instructions or fear tactics. One agreed, achievable change is realistic for someone who minimises the event. Waiting until after discharge wastes a teachable moment when he is already engaged with health services."}, {"id": 1353, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Warren asks why he keeps rolling his ankles and tripping on uneven ground at work, and the physiotherapist wants to explain it accurately. Considering his strength, foot posture and gait findings, which explanation best accounts for these problems in this man with Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease?", "options": {"A": "Cerebellar dysfunction produces a wide-based unsteady gait with poor limb coordination on uneven surfaces", "B": "Generalised ligament laxity from a connective tissue disorder allows the ankles to roll inward easily", "C": "Weak dorsiflexors and evertors with cavus alignment reduce toe clearance and ankle stability", "D": "Upper motor neuron spasticity in the calf muscles limits dorsiflexion and makes the ankle stiff"}, "cluster_id": "CHS99", "cluster_text": "Warren Cubillo, 34, is an Aboriginal man and a council groundsman who attends an Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service for physiotherapy. He has genetically confirmed Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease, as did his late father. He reports tripping on uneven ground, rolling both ankles often, and tiring by mid-afternoon at work. He wants to keep working and playing community football. His aunt came to the last appointment and has come again. Findings: pes cavus bilaterally with hammer toes and a high-stepping gait. Ankle dorsiflexion strength is 2/5 bilaterally, plantarflexion 4/5, knee extension 5/5. Mild weakness of hand intrinsics. Ankle reflexes absent, knee reflexes reduced. Vibration sense is reduced at both great toes. Calf girth is reduced bilaterally. Resting observations are normal. He walks 400 metres before reporting leg fatigue (Borg 5/10). He says he would like to yarn about his week first and asks if his aunt can sit in on the whole session.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Weak dorsiflexors and evertors with cavus alignment reduce toe clearance and ankle stability): Length-dependent motor neuropathy weakens the dorsiflexors and evertors, and the cavus foot gives a small, unstable base, so toes catch and ankles roll. Absent ankle reflexes and wasted calves point to lower motor neuron loss, not spasticity. Laxity and cerebellar signs are not present in his examination."}, {"id": 1354, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Warren wants to strengthen his legs so he can last a full shift. His dorsiflexors are 2/5 and he reports leg fatigue at Borg 5/10 after 400 metres of walking. Which exercise prescription is most appropriate to begin with for his Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease?", "options": {"A": "Rest from resistance exercise and use only passive stretching to protect weak muscles from overuse damage", "B": "High-load eccentric strengthening to fatigue three times weekly to maximise strength gains in the weak dorsiflexors", "C": "Submaximal strengthening and moderate aerobic exercise with rest days, stopping short of exhaustion", "D": "Isolated dorsiflexor strengthening only, since aerobic exercise accelerates nerve degeneration and muscle loss in the disease"}, "cluster_id": "CHS99", "cluster_text": "Warren Cubillo, 34, is an Aboriginal man and a council groundsman who attends an Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service for physiotherapy. He has genetically confirmed Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease, as did his late father. He reports tripping on uneven ground, rolling both ankles often, and tiring by mid-afternoon at work. He wants to keep working and playing community football. His aunt came to the last appointment and has come again. Findings: pes cavus bilaterally with hammer toes and a high-stepping gait. Ankle dorsiflexion strength is 2/5 bilaterally, plantarflexion 4/5, knee extension 5/5. Mild weakness of hand intrinsics. Ankle reflexes absent, knee reflexes reduced. Vibration sense is reduced at both great toes. Calf girth is reduced bilaterally. Resting observations are normal. He walks 400 metres before reporting leg fatigue (Borg 5/10). He says he would like to yarn about his week first and asks if his aunt can sit in on the whole session.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Submaximal strengthening and moderate aerobic exercise with rest days, stopping short of exhaustion): In slowly progressive neuropathy, moderate resistance and aerobic training are safe and beneficial, but working weak, denervated muscle to exhaustion risks overwork weakness. Complete avoidance of resistance exercise leads to deconditioning, and aerobic exercise does not accelerate the disease."}, {"id": 1355, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Warren's high-stepping gait and 2/5 dorsiflexion cause tripping, and he needs to walk on rough ground at work in heavy boots all day. Which intervention is most likely to improve his toe clearance and safety on uneven ground in the short term?", "options": {"A": "Functional electrical stimulation to the peroneal nerve during walking, as the weak muscles can be reactivated", "B": "Orthotist referral for custom ankle foot orthoses to hold the foot clear during swing", "C": "A four-wheeled walker for all outdoor walking to widen his base of support on rough ground", "D": "Rigid ankle taping applied each morning to replace the missing dorsiflexor strength during the work shift"}, "cluster_id": "CHS99", "cluster_text": "Warren Cubillo, 34, is an Aboriginal man and a council groundsman who attends an Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service for physiotherapy. He has genetically confirmed Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease, as did his late father. He reports tripping on uneven ground, rolling both ankles often, and tiring by mid-afternoon at work. He wants to keep working and playing community football. His aunt came to the last appointment and has come again. Findings: pes cavus bilaterally with hammer toes and a high-stepping gait. Ankle dorsiflexion strength is 2/5 bilaterally, plantarflexion 4/5, knee extension 5/5. Mild weakness of hand intrinsics. Ankle reflexes absent, knee reflexes reduced. Vibration sense is reduced at both great toes. Calf girth is reduced bilaterally. Resting observations are normal. He walks 400 metres before reporting leg fatigue (Borg 5/10). He says he would like to yarn about his week first and asks if his aunt can sit in on the whole session.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Orthotist referral for custom ankle foot orthoses to hold the foot clear during swing): An ankle foot orthosis substitutes for absent dorsiflexion and can be fitted with accommodation for the cavus foot. Stimulation depends on a viable motor nerve and muscle, which is limited here. Tape loses tension and cannot replace 2/5 power, and a walker is impractical for his work."}, {"id": 1356, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Warren asks whether his aunt can stay for the whole session and says he would like to yarn first before any examination. An Aboriginal health worker is also available on site today. What is the most culturally safe response by the physiotherapist?", "options": {"A": "Begin the standard assessment as planned, then invite his aunt in and discuss his goals afterwards", "B": "Invite his aunt to speak for Warren about goals, since family elders decide health matters together", "C": "Hand the session to the Aboriginal health worker and ask his aunt to wait in reception", "D": "Welcome his aunt, let Warren set the pace, and offer the health worker's support"}, "cluster_id": "CHS99", "cluster_text": "Warren Cubillo, 34, is an Aboriginal man and a council groundsman who attends an Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service for physiotherapy. He has genetically confirmed Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease, as did his late father. He reports tripping on uneven ground, rolling both ankles often, and tiring by mid-afternoon at work. He wants to keep working and playing community football. His aunt came to the last appointment and has come again. Findings: pes cavus bilaterally with hammer toes and a high-stepping gait. Ankle dorsiflexion strength is 2/5 bilaterally, plantarflexion 4/5, knee extension 5/5. Mild weakness of hand intrinsics. Ankle reflexes absent, knee reflexes reduced. Vibration sense is reduced at both great toes. Calf girth is reduced bilaterally. Resting observations are normal. He walks 400 metres before reporting leg fatigue (Borg 5/10). He says he would like to yarn about his week first and asks if his aunt can sit in on the whole session.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Welcome his aunt, let Warren set the pace, and offer the health worker's support): Cultural safety means the client defines what feels safe, so Warren decides who is present and how the session begins, with the health worker offered as support he can accept. Starting the standard assessment first and asking his aunt to wait both override his stated preference. Inviting his aunt to speak for him assumes a family decision process he did not ask for, and handing the session to the health worker removes his say."}, {"id": 1357, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Kalogeros is distressed by breathlessness at rest, with RR 26, SpO2 90 percent, a quiet chest and prolonged expiration while flat in bed. Her care goals prioritise comfort. Which first non-pharmacological strategy is most appropriate for her comfort at this time?", "options": {"A": "Teach slow diaphragmatic breathing lying supine with a hand on her abdomen to settle her rate", "B": "Commence short seated exercise with oximetry to build her tolerance of washing and dressing", "C": "Support her upright, leaning forward on pillows, and direct a handheld fan towards her face", "D": "Apply low flow oxygen, since her saturation is below normal and she is distressed"}, "cluster_id": "CHS100", "cluster_text": "Mrs Maria Kalogeros, 86, lives in a residential aged care facility with end stage COPD. Her advance care plan, agreed with her family, prioritises comfort and avoiding hospital admission. She reports distressing breathlessness while washing and dressing and feels panicky at night. Today: RR 26, HR 98, SpO2 90 percent on room air, using accessory muscles, lying flat in bed with one pillow and a hand over her mouth. Her chest is quiet with prolonged expiration. She uses regular inhalers. The GP has ordered a palliative care review but no breathlessness medication is charted. Her saturation does not meet criteria for home oxygen. Her daughter says she wishes she could do more. The registered nurse is on duty and a night carer will assist Mrs Kalogeros tonight.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Support her upright, leaning forward on pillows, and direct a handheld fan towards her face): Upright forward leaning positions reduce the work of breathing, and a fan on the face is a simple comfort measure for breathlessness at rest. Supine diaphragmatic breathing worsens her mechanics because lying flat disadvantages a hyperinflated chest. Exercise practice does not suit distress at rest and her comfort focused goals. Oxygen is not indicated at this saturation and is outside physiotherapy prescribing, so non-drug measures come first."}, {"id": 1358, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "A carer asks why directing a handheld fan at Mrs Kalogeros's face seems to ease her breathlessness although her oxygen saturation does not change, and she looks calmer within minutes. What is the most accurate explanation to give the carer in plain language?", "options": {"A": "Airflow stimulates facial receptors supplied by the trigeminal nerve, easing the sensation of breathlessness", "B": "Cool air improves oxygen delivery to the lungs, even though the oximeter reading stays similar", "C": "Airflow relaxes the neck accessory muscles directly, which lowers her respiratory rate and work of breathing", "D": "Moving air dries upper airway secretions, which reduces cough and the sensation of mucus build up"}, "cluster_id": "CHS100", "cluster_text": "Mrs Maria Kalogeros, 86, lives in a residential aged care facility with end stage COPD. Her advance care plan, agreed with her family, prioritises comfort and avoiding hospital admission. She reports distressing breathlessness while washing and dressing and feels panicky at night. Today: RR 26, HR 98, SpO2 90 percent on room air, using accessory muscles, lying flat in bed with one pillow and a hand over her mouth. Her chest is quiet with prolonged expiration. She uses regular inhalers. The GP has ordered a palliative care review but no breathlessness medication is charted. Her saturation does not meet criteria for home oxygen. Her daughter says she wishes she could do more. The registered nurse is on duty and a night carer will assist Mrs Kalogeros tonight.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Airflow stimulates facial receptors supplied by the trigeminal nerve, easing the sensation of breathlessness): Cool airflow across the face activates trigeminal nerve receptors and changes the brain's perception of breathlessness, without altering oxygen levels. It does not increase oxygen delivery or directly relax muscles. Drying of secretions is not the mechanism and would not explain the calming effect."}, {"id": 1359, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Kalogeros wants to keep washing herself but becomes very breathless and anxious midway. She has a good morning energy pattern and a shower chair. Her daughter suggests carers should now bathe her fully in bed. Which approach to the task is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Delay washing until her saturation returns to the normal range before any further exertion is attempted", "B": "Encourage her to complete the whole wash without rests to build tolerance across the coming week", "C": "Break washing into short seated steps with rests, in the morning, keeping items within reach", "D": "Agree to bed bathing by carers, because any exertion in advanced disease will worsen her breathlessness"}, "cluster_id": "CHS100", "cluster_text": "Mrs Maria Kalogeros, 86, lives in a residential aged care facility with end stage COPD. Her advance care plan, agreed with her family, prioritises comfort and avoiding hospital admission. She reports distressing breathlessness while washing and dressing and feels panicky at night. Today: RR 26, HR 98, SpO2 90 percent on room air, using accessory muscles, lying flat in bed with one pillow and a hand over her mouth. Her chest is quiet with prolonged expiration. She uses regular inhalers. The GP has ordered a palliative care review but no breathlessness medication is charted. Her saturation does not meet criteria for home oxygen. Her daughter says she wishes she could do more. The registered nurse is on duty and a night carer will assist Mrs Kalogeros tonight.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Break washing into short seated steps with rests, in the morning, keeping items within reach): Pacing, sitting, planning rests and using her best time of day let her keep valued independence with less breathlessness, which fits palliative goals. Removing the task entirely takes away her choice and function. Pushing through without rests adds distress, and waiting for a normal saturation is unrealistic in her disease."}, {"id": 1360, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "You finish your shift with Mrs Kalogeros still panicking at night, and the registered nurse is on duty. The GP has ordered a palliative care review but no breathlessness medication is charted. Which handover to the registered nurse is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Write detailed notes flagging the unmet breathlessness need for the GP's next scheduled round", "B": "Advise the registered nurse of a suitable breathlessness medication and dose for the GP to chart", "C": "Give a structured verbal handover of findings and effective strategies, and request an urgent medication review", "D": "Brief the night carer directly on strategies and leave medication to the palliative care review"}, "cluster_id": "CHS100", "cluster_text": "Mrs Maria Kalogeros, 86, lives in a residential aged care facility with end stage COPD. Her advance care plan, agreed with her family, prioritises comfort and avoiding hospital admission. She reports distressing breathlessness while washing and dressing and feels panicky at night. Today: RR 26, HR 98, SpO2 90 percent on room air, using accessory muscles, lying flat in bed with one pillow and a hand over her mouth. Her chest is quiet with prolonged expiration. She uses regular inhalers. The GP has ordered a palliative care review but no breathlessness medication is charted. Her saturation does not meet criteria for home oxygen. Her daughter says she wishes she could do more. The registered nurse is on duty and a night carer will assist Mrs Kalogeros tonight.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Give a structured verbal handover of findings and effective strategies, and request an urgent medication review): A structured, direct handover to the responsible nurse shares what helps tonight and flags the unmet need so the prescriber can review her symptom relief promptly. Notes alone may not be read or acted on before the night, and waiting for a scheduled round delays relief. The night carer cannot arrange a medication review, so briefing only the carer leaves the gap open. Recommending a drug and dose is outside physiotherapy scope."}, {"id": 1361, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "On day one, Mr Vaipulu's underwater seal shows fluid swinging with breathing and intermittent bubbles only during forceful coughing, with 80 mL serous drainage over six hours. The drain is unkinked and he is comfortable at rest. What does this most likely indicate?", "options": {"A": "A patent drain with a small air leak on coughing, which needs monitoring and documentation", "B": "A blocked drain with pleural fluid collecting, requiring urgent stripping of the tubing to restore flow", "C": "Normal pleural drainage with no air leak, so the drain can be removed once drainage slows", "D": "A large persistent air leak from a failing lung repair, requiring immediate surgical review before mobilising"}, "cluster_id": "CHS101", "cluster_text": "Tomasi Vaipulu, 58, Samoan-Australian van driver, day 1 after a right video-assisted thoracoscopic lower lobectomy for lung cancer. Smoker for 30 pack-years, mild COPD. Analgesia: patient-controlled opioid and regular paracetamol. One right chest drain is connected to an underwater seal bottle without suction. Observations: HR 92, RR 22, BP 128/76, SpO2 93 percent on 3 L/min via nasal prongs; pain 7/10 with coughing and 3/10 at rest. Air entry is reduced at the right base, left clear. Drain: 80 mL serous in 6 hours, fluid tidals with respiration, no bubbling at rest but intermittent bubbles on a forceful cough. He is drowsy after a recent opioid dose; when asked, he agrees to sit out, but minutes later cannot recall what you explained. His wife is not present.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A patent drain with a small air leak on coughing, which needs monitoring and documentation): Tidalling confirms the drain communicates with the pleural space and is patent, and bubbles only on forceful cough represent a small leak that is monitored and recorded. A blocked drain would stop swinging, a large continuous leak would bubble at rest, and bubbling of any kind means the lung is not yet fully sealed."}, {"id": 1362, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Vaipulu is alert and consents to walk after analgesia has taken effect. His drain is connected to an underwater seal bottle with no suction, and he also has a PCA pump and intravenous line attached. Which approach to the drainage system is most appropriate during mobilisation?", "options": {"A": "Clamp the tubing near the chest wall during walking to prevent air entering the pleural space", "B": "Lay the bottle on its side in a bag so he can walk hands free", "C": "Keep the bottle upright below chest level, carry it beside him, and avoid pulling the tubing", "D": "Rest the bottle on the bed beside him during transfers, keeping the tubing slack"}, "cluster_id": "CHS101", "cluster_text": "Tomasi Vaipulu, 58, Samoan-Australian van driver, day 1 after a right video-assisted thoracoscopic lower lobectomy for lung cancer. Smoker for 30 pack-years, mild COPD. Analgesia: patient-controlled opioid and regular paracetamol. One right chest drain is connected to an underwater seal bottle without suction. Observations: HR 92, RR 22, BP 128/76, SpO2 93 percent on 3 L/min via nasal prongs; pain 7/10 with coughing and 3/10 at rest. Air entry is reduced at the right base, left clear. Drain: 80 mL serous in 6 hours, fluid tidals with respiration, no bubbling at rest but intermittent bubbles on a forceful cough. He is drowsy after a recent opioid dose; when asked, he agrees to sit out, but minutes later cannot recall what you explained. His wife is not present.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Keep the bottle upright below chest level, carry it beside him, and avoid pulling the tubing): The bottle must stay upright and below the chest so the underwater seal works and fluid cannot siphon back, with the tubing kept free of tension. Clamping risks a tension pneumothorax because he has an air leak on cough. Lying the bottle on its side breaks the seal. Resting it on the bed raises it close to or above chest level during transfers, allowing drainage to flow back toward the chest."}, {"id": 1363, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "During his walk Mr Vaipulu steps on the tubing and the chest drain slips out of the chest wall. The site is oozing serous fluid and air can be heard moving at the wound. He is anxious but SpO2 is 92 percent. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Push the drain back to its marked depth and tape it securely until the nurse arrives", "B": "Help him back to bed, then leave to fetch the nurse and document the event later", "C": "Lie him flat, seal the site with an occlusive dressing taped on all sides, then reassess", "D": "Cover the site with a sterile dressing, sit him down, and call urgently for help"}, "cluster_id": "CHS101", "cluster_text": "Tomasi Vaipulu, 58, Samoan-Australian van driver, day 1 after a right video-assisted thoracoscopic lower lobectomy for lung cancer. Smoker for 30 pack-years, mild COPD. Analgesia: patient-controlled opioid and regular paracetamol. One right chest drain is connected to an underwater seal bottle without suction. Observations: HR 92, RR 22, BP 128/76, SpO2 93 percent on 3 L/min via nasal prongs; pain 7/10 with coughing and 3/10 at rest. Air entry is reduced at the right base, left clear. Drain: 80 mL serous in 6 hours, fluid tidals with respiration, no bubbling at rest but intermittent bubbles on a forceful cough. He is drowsy after a recent opioid dose; when asked, he agrees to sit out, but minutes later cannot recall what you explained. His wife is not present.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Cover the site with a sterile dressing, sit him down, and call urgently for help): A dislodged drain needs the wound covered with a sterile dressing, the patient kept calm and seated, and immediate medical and nursing help. Reinserting a contaminated drain risks infection and injury, and a fully sealed dressing can trap air and cause a tension pneumothorax in a lung that is still leaking. Leaving him alone delays care."}, {"id": 1364, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Two hours later he is drowsier after further opioid analgesia and again agrees to sit out, but cannot repeat what you proposed or why. His wife is unavailable and no substitute decision-maker has been appointed. How should the physiotherapist proceed with this treatment?", "options": {"A": "Defer non-urgent mobilisation, reassess his understanding when alert, and document the capacity assessment", "B": "Continue with a gentler version, since agreement from a drowsy patient suffices for low risk care", "C": "Proceed with sitting him out on his verbal agreement, since consent given earlier covers later sessions", "D": "Phone his wife for verbal consent to the session, and proceed as next of kin"}, "cluster_id": "CHS101", "cluster_text": "Tomasi Vaipulu, 58, Samoan-Australian van driver, day 1 after a right video-assisted thoracoscopic lower lobectomy for lung cancer. Smoker for 30 pack-years, mild COPD. Analgesia: patient-controlled opioid and regular paracetamol. One right chest drain is connected to an underwater seal bottle without suction. Observations: HR 92, RR 22, BP 128/76, SpO2 93 percent on 3 L/min via nasal prongs; pain 7/10 with coughing and 3/10 at rest. Air entry is reduced at the right base, left clear. Drain: 80 mL serous in 6 hours, fluid tidals with respiration, no bubbling at rest but intermittent bubbles on a forceful cough. He is drowsy after a recent opioid dose; when asked, he agrees to sit out, but minutes later cannot recall what you explained. His wife is not present.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Defer non-urgent mobilisation, reassess his understanding when alert, and document the capacity assessment): Valid consent needs understanding, and his capacity is fluctuating with sedation, so the sensible step is to defer non urgent treatment and reassess when he is alert, recording what was found. Agreement from a drowsy patient who cannot recall the explanation is not informed consent, and earlier agreement does not carry forward once capacity is in doubt. His wife is not an appointed substitute decision maker, and the treatment can safely wait."}, {"id": 1365, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Kevin cannot recall yesterday's session with the physiotherapist and is disoriented to place and date. The team wants a way to track whether his post-traumatic amnesia is resolving. Staff can test him each morning, when he is calmest. Which approach is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Family reports of how settled he seems, with PTA judged resolved once he is calmer", "B": "Glasgow Coma Scale scoring each session, because improving eye opening reflects the return of memory", "C": "A weekly cognitive screen such as the Montreal Cognitive Assessment, accepting one high score as resolution", "D": "Daily orientation and recall testing with a validated tool such as the Westmead PTA Scale"}, "cluster_id": "CHS102", "cluster_text": "Mr Kevin Oakes, 45, plasterer, fell from a ladder 12 days ago and sustained a moderate traumatic brain injury with frontal contusions. He is on a rehabilitation unit. He cannot recall yesterday's session and is disoriented to place and date, so post-traumatic amnesia continues. He becomes agitated and shouts in noisy, crowded settings, and has tried to leave the unit twice at night. Findings: Glasgow Coma Scale 14, power 5/5 in all limbs, mild unsteadiness on tandem walking, HR 76, BP 126/78. His wife Sara visits daily and wants to help. She works part time and is anxious about managing him at home. He drinks alcohol most weekends and sleeps poorly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Daily orientation and recall testing with a validated tool such as the Westmead PTA Scale): PTA is monitored by repeated serial testing of orientation and new memory, and the Westmead PTA Scale is a recognised tool for this. A single high score on a cognitive screen can give false reassurance, and the Glasgow Coma Scale measures conscious level rather than memory. Family impressions of calmness do not show that amnesia has resolved."}, {"id": 1366, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Kevin becomes agitated and shouts when approached in the busy gym, and claims he is due at a building site. He is physically well. His wife Sara is waiting in the corridor. What is the most appropriate way to manage this during therapy?", "options": {"A": "Reduce stimulation, use a calm low voice, and redirect him to a simple familiar task", "B": "Move him to a quieter corner of the gym and continue the planned treadmill session", "C": "Gently correct his statements and re-orient him to date and place until he accepts it", "D": "Bring Sara into the busy gym to reassure him, and continue the planned session"}, "cluster_id": "CHS102", "cluster_text": "Mr Kevin Oakes, 45, plasterer, fell from a ladder 12 days ago and sustained a moderate traumatic brain injury with frontal contusions. He is on a rehabilitation unit. He cannot recall yesterday's session and is disoriented to place and date, so post-traumatic amnesia continues. He becomes agitated and shouts in noisy, crowded settings, and has tried to leave the unit twice at night. Findings: Glasgow Coma Scale 14, power 5/5 in all limbs, mild unsteadiness on tandem walking, HR 76, BP 126/78. His wife Sara visits daily and wants to help. She works part time and is anxious about managing him at home. He drinks alcohol most weekends and sleeps poorly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reduce stimulation, use a calm low voice, and redirect him to a simple familiar task): During post-traumatic amnesia the brain is easily overloaded, so reducing noise and demands, speaking calmly and redirecting to a familiar activity settles agitation without confrontation. Repeated correcting and re-orienting raises distress because he cannot retain the information. A quieter corner still keeps the same demanding task. Having his wife join in the busy gym does not remove the overstimulating environment that triggered the agitation."}, {"id": 1367, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Midway through a corridor walk Kevin vomits twice, complains that his headache is getting worse and is more drowsy than this morning. His Glasgow Coma Scale has fallen from 14 to 12. Nursing staff are on the ward nearby. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Stop treatment, position him safely lying down, and notify the medical team urgently", "B": "Document the change and ask nursing staff to monitor him closely until the next planned review", "C": "Stop the session, let him rest in his room with nursing checks and review alertness later", "D": "Sit him down and repeat his Glasgow Coma Scale in ten minutes before deciding to escalate"}, "cluster_id": "CHS102", "cluster_text": "Mr Kevin Oakes, 45, plasterer, fell from a ladder 12 days ago and sustained a moderate traumatic brain injury with frontal contusions. He is on a rehabilitation unit. He cannot recall yesterday's session and is disoriented to place and date, so post-traumatic amnesia continues. He becomes agitated and shouts in noisy, crowded settings, and has tried to leave the unit twice at night. Findings: Glasgow Coma Scale 14, power 5/5 in all limbs, mild unsteadiness on tandem walking, HR 76, BP 126/78. His wife Sara visits daily and wants to help. She works part time and is anxious about managing him at home. He drinks alcohol most weekends and sleeps poorly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stop treatment, position him safely lying down, and notify the medical team urgently): Worsening headache, repeated vomiting and a fall in conscious level after a brain injury can indicate raised intracranial pressure or a new bleed, which is an emergency. Therapy stops and he is made safe while the medical team is called urgently. Documenting for later review, resting with routine checks, or waiting ten minutes to recheck would each delay time critical treatment when the score has already fallen."}, {"id": 1368, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Three weeks later Kevin's post-traumatic amnesia has resolved and he plans to go home. He tires by mid-afternoon and drinks alcohol most weekends. Sara asks how to support his recovery. He is keen to return to work soon. Which self-management advice is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Keep a daily diary of symptoms and exercise, and cut back alcohol only if symptoms worsen", "B": "Push through fatigue with longer daily exercise to rebuild stamina, since rest slows neurological recovery", "C": "Limit activity to short household tasks until fatigue settles, then plan a gradual return to exercise", "D": "Pace activities with rests, keep a regular sleep routine, and discuss alcohol with his doctor"}, "cluster_id": "CHS102", "cluster_text": "Mr Kevin Oakes, 45, plasterer, fell from a ladder 12 days ago and sustained a moderate traumatic brain injury with frontal contusions. He is on a rehabilitation unit. He cannot recall yesterday's session and is disoriented to place and date, so post-traumatic amnesia continues. He becomes agitated and shouts in noisy, crowded settings, and has tried to leave the unit twice at night. Findings: Glasgow Coma Scale 14, power 5/5 in all limbs, mild unsteadiness on tandem walking, HR 76, BP 126/78. His wife Sara visits daily and wants to help. She works part time and is anxious about managing him at home. He drinks alcohol most weekends and sleeps poorly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Pace activities with rests, keep a regular sleep routine, and discuss alcohol with his doctor): Fatigue after brain injury is best managed by pacing, planned rests and good sleep, and alcohol can impair recovery and increase seizure and fall risk, so it should be discussed with his doctor. Pushing through fatigue tends to worsen symptoms, and prolonged restriction promotes deconditioning. A diary is useful but should not delay advice about alcohol."}, {"id": 1369, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "During a later session Mia has a generalised tonic-clonic seizure while standing near the plinth and falls to the floor. She is jerking and unresponsive. Nothing like this has happened in clinic before. What is the most appropriate immediate management by the physiotherapist?", "options": {"A": "Lift her onto the plinth to keep her safe, then monitor her pulse and breathing closely", "B": "Begin chest compressions because she is unresponsive, and ask a colleague to fetch the defibrillator", "C": "Note the time, protect her head, clear hazards, and turn her onto her side afterwards", "D": "Gently restrain her limbs to limit injury and place a soft object between her teeth"}, "cluster_id": "CHS103", "cluster_text": "Mia Tuilagi, 15, is a Year 10 student seen in a community physiotherapy clinic for anterior knee pain after joining the school athletics squad. She has focal epilepsy with occasional tonic-clonic seizures, the last one four months ago, managed with regular prescribed medication. Her mother reports that Mia has missed several doses this month because of late nights, and says Mia has had two short episodes of staring and unresponsiveness at school this week. Mia wants to start swimming training and cross-country running. Knee findings: pain 4/10 on stairs, mild patellofemoral pain on squatting, quadriceps strength 4+/5, no effusion. Her mother asks whether the physiotherapist can tell her to take a double dose to catch up, and whether swimming is safe.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Note the time, protect her head, clear hazards, and turn her onto her side afterwards): The aim during a tonic-clonic seizure is to prevent injury: note the time, protect the head, clear hazards and turn her onto her side once the jerking stops. Restraint can injure her and objects in the mouth risk airway obstruction or dental injury. Compressions are not indicated while she is still breathing."}, {"id": 1370, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mia's seizure stops after about two minutes. She is drowsy and confused, breathing normally, and slowly recovering on her side. She has no injuries and this is consistent with her known epilepsy pattern. What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Move her to a quiet room to rest alone and check on her every few minutes", "B": "Sit her up, offer water, and continue the knee session once she says she feels ready", "C": "Call an ambulance now, because a seizure in a clinic needs assessment in the emergency department", "D": "Stay with her, monitor recovery, and call an ambulance if breathing or consciousness worsens"}, "cluster_id": "CHS103", "cluster_text": "Mia Tuilagi, 15, is a Year 10 student seen in a community physiotherapy clinic for anterior knee pain after joining the school athletics squad. She has focal epilepsy with occasional tonic-clonic seizures, the last one four months ago, managed with regular prescribed medication. Her mother reports that Mia has missed several doses this month because of late nights, and says Mia has had two short episodes of staring and unresponsiveness at school this week. Mia wants to start swimming training and cross-country running. Knee findings: pain 4/10 on stairs, mild patellofemoral pain on squatting, quadriceps strength 4+/5, no effusion. Her mother asks whether the physiotherapist can tell her to take a double dose to catch up, and whether swimming is safe.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stay with her, monitor recovery, and call an ambulance if breathing or consciousness worsens): A single typical seizure of short duration, ending with steady recovery in a person with known epilepsy and no injury, does not usually need an ambulance. Monitoring continues and help is called if breathing or consciousness worsens or seizures repeat. Resuming exercise or leaving her alone removes safe observation during the drowsy recovery phase."}, {"id": 1371, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mia has recently missed medication and had two staring episodes. She wants to join swimming training and the cross-country team, and her past seizures have occurred without warning. Which advice about exercise participation is most appropriate for the physiotherapist to give?", "options": {"A": "Advise avoiding swimming and running, since exercise commonly provokes seizures and lowers the seizure threshold", "B": "Encourage exercise, with close supervision in water and a running buddy until control improves", "C": "Allow normal participation in swimming and running because her seizures are usually brief and self-limiting", "D": "Permit running, and swimming only in a shallow pool, since shallow water reduces the drowning risk"}, "cluster_id": "CHS103", "cluster_text": "Mia Tuilagi, 15, is a Year 10 student seen in a community physiotherapy clinic for anterior knee pain after joining the school athletics squad. She has focal epilepsy with occasional tonic-clonic seizures, the last one four months ago, managed with regular prescribed medication. Her mother reports that Mia has missed several doses this month because of late nights, and says Mia has had two short episodes of staring and unresponsiveness at school this week. Mia wants to start swimming training and cross-country running. Knee findings: pain 4/10 on stairs, mild patellofemoral pain on squatting, quadriceps strength 4+/5, no effusion. Her mother asks whether the physiotherapist can tell her to take a double dose to catch up, and whether swimming is safe.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Encourage exercise, with close supervision in water and a running buddy until control improves): Exercise is encouraged in epilepsy and does not generally provoke seizures, but swimming and running carry added risk when seizures occur without warning, so supervision is sensible while control is uncertain. Shallow water still carries drowning risk. Avoiding exercise is unnecessary and harms health, while unrestricted participation ignores her recent missed doses."}, {"id": 1372, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mia's mother asks whether Mia should take a double dose of medication to catch up on missed doses, and whether the recent staring episodes matter. The clinic holds the details of Mia's GP. What is the most appropriate response by the physiotherapist?", "options": {"A": "Suggest she follows the packet instructions and report further staring episodes at the next physiotherapy visit", "B": "Advise one extra dose is usually safe after a missed dose, and record the staring episodes", "C": "Reassure that brief staring is common in teenagers and continue the knee program as planned", "D": "Explain dosing is outside physiotherapy scope and recommend a prompt GP or neurologist review"}, "cluster_id": "CHS103", "cluster_text": "Mia Tuilagi, 15, is a Year 10 student seen in a community physiotherapy clinic for anterior knee pain after joining the school athletics squad. She has focal epilepsy with occasional tonic-clonic seizures, the last one four months ago, managed with regular prescribed medication. Her mother reports that Mia has missed several doses this month because of late nights, and says Mia has had two short episodes of staring and unresponsiveness at school this week. Mia wants to start swimming training and cross-country running. Knee findings: pain 4/10 on stairs, mild patellofemoral pain on squatting, quadriceps strength 4+/5, no effusion. Her mother asks whether the physiotherapist can tell her to take a double dose to catch up, and whether swimming is safe.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain dosing is outside physiotherapy scope and recommend a prompt GP or neurologist review): Medication dosing is outside physiotherapy scope and an extra dose could be harmful, so the physiotherapist declines to advise and directs the family to the prescriber. New staring episodes may signal poorer seizure control and need timely medical review. Recording them helps, but reassurance or waiting for the next visit delays proper assessment."}, {"id": 1373, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Ms Raman has been cleared by the cardiology team to mobilise today, with her radial puncture site checked and her observations stable after her recent infarct. Which graded plan is most appropriate and safe for her first physiotherapy session after this infarct?", "options": {"A": "Short supervised walks with monitoring, avoiding weight bearing or heavy gripping through the right wrist", "B": "Bed rest with the right leg kept straight until the access site is cleared by cardiology", "C": "Short supervised walks with monitoring, using a four-wheeled frame loaded through both hands for stability", "D": "Seated exercises and walking within the room only, deferring all activity until cardiac rehabilitation starts"}, "cluster_id": "CHS104", "cluster_text": "Ms Kavitha Raman, 55, a primary school teacher, is on day 1 after primary percutaneous coronary intervention with a stent to the left anterior descending artery for an anterior STEMI. Access was via the right radial artery. The compression band has been removed, a small bruise is visible, the radial pulse is present and the hand is warm. Troponin is trending down. Resting HR 72, BP 128/76 mmHg, SpO2 97 percent on room air, no chest pain, ECG sinus rhythm. Echocardiogram shows mildly reduced left ventricular function. She lives with her husband and is keen to return to work. The nurse asks you to mobilise her and begin education. She tells you she is very grateful and is on the public waiting list for outpatient cardiac rehabilitation, which is several weeks away.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Short supervised walks with monitoring, avoiding weight bearing or heavy gripping through the right wrist): After radial access the wrist is protected from loading and heavy gripping while the puncture site heals, but the rest of the body can be mobilised early with monitoring. Leg restrictions belong to femoral access and do not apply here. A frame loaded through the hands puts weight through the access wrist. Waiting for outpatient rehabilitation weeks away is needlessly conservative for a stable patient cleared to mobilise."}, {"id": 1374, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "During her first walk on the ward, after 50 metres, Ms Raman reports new central chest tightness rated 4 out of 10 and feels slightly nauseated. HR is 98, BP 124/74 mmHg and SpO2 96 percent. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Stop, sit her down, recheck observations and alert the nurse before any further exercise", "B": "Rest for two minutes and resume walking if the tightness eases, then report afterwards", "C": "Return her to bed, apply her own prescribed glyceryl trinitrate spray, and document the walk distance", "D": "Slow the pace and continue the walk, as her vital signs remain within acceptable ranges"}, "cluster_id": "CHS104", "cluster_text": "Ms Kavitha Raman, 55, a primary school teacher, is on day 1 after primary percutaneous coronary intervention with a stent to the left anterior descending artery for an anterior STEMI. Access was via the right radial artery. The compression band has been removed, a small bruise is visible, the radial pulse is present and the hand is warm. Troponin is trending down. Resting HR 72, BP 128/76 mmHg, SpO2 97 percent on room air, no chest pain, ECG sinus rhythm. Echocardiogram shows mildly reduced left ventricular function. She lives with her husband and is keen to return to work. The nurse asks you to mobilise her and begin education. She tells you she is very grateful and is on the public waiting list for outpatient cardiac rehabilitation, which is several weeks away.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stop, sit her down, recheck observations and alert the nurse before any further exercise): New chest tightness with nausea soon after a myocardial infarction may represent ischaemia, so exercise stops immediately, she sits and the nursing and medical team are informed even though her vital signs look reassuring. Resuming after a brief rest or continuing slowly misses a possible acute event, and a physiotherapist does not administer medication for her."}, {"id": 1375, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "The next morning Ms Raman's resting heart rate is 118 and irregular, BP 96/60 mmHg, and she feels dizzy sitting at the bedside. Yesterday her observations were normal and her troponin continues to fall. What is the most appropriate physiotherapy decision?", "options": {"A": "Walk her in the corridor with a nurse beside her, since the falling troponin shows recovery", "B": "Limit her to seated limb exercises with observations repeated every five minutes, then reassess", "C": "Defer exercise, keep her resting and report the findings to the medical team promptly", "D": "Postpone review until after the next scheduled medical round, avoiding exercise in the meantime"}, "cluster_id": "CHS104", "cluster_text": "Ms Kavitha Raman, 55, a primary school teacher, is on day 1 after primary percutaneous coronary intervention with a stent to the left anterior descending artery for an anterior STEMI. Access was via the right radial artery. The compression band has been removed, a small bruise is visible, the radial pulse is present and the hand is warm. Troponin is trending down. Resting HR 72, BP 128/76 mmHg, SpO2 97 percent on room air, no chest pain, ECG sinus rhythm. Echocardiogram shows mildly reduced left ventricular function. She lives with her husband and is keen to return to work. The nurse asks you to mobilise her and begin education. She tells you she is very grateful and is on the public waiting list for outpatient cardiac rehabilitation, which is several weeks away.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Defer exercise, keep her resting and report the findings to the medical team promptly): New irregular tachycardia with hypotension and dizziness is an unstable change that may indicate a new arrhythmia or haemodynamic compromise, so exercise is withheld and the doctor is informed promptly. Seated exercise or accompanied walking still raises demand on an unstable circulation, and delaying the report delays assessment. A falling troponin does not exclude a new arrhythmia."}, {"id": 1376, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Before discharge Ms Raman, grateful for her care, offers you a large gift voucher and asks if you could see her privately at your home gym for cash to avoid the cardiac rehabilitation waiting list. How should you respond as her treating physiotherapist?", "options": {"A": "Accept the voucher politely but decline home sessions, and refer her through the usual waiting list", "B": "Decline the voucher and the private sessions, and mention the offer to a colleague informally", "C": "Agree to see her privately once her inpatient episode ends, since she remains clinically suitable", "D": "Decline the arrangement, explain the referral pathway, and record the offer as hospital policy requires"}, "cluster_id": "CHS104", "cluster_text": "Ms Kavitha Raman, 55, a primary school teacher, is on day 1 after primary percutaneous coronary intervention with a stent to the left anterior descending artery for an anterior STEMI. Access was via the right radial artery. The compression band has been removed, a small bruise is visible, the radial pulse is present and the hand is warm. Troponin is trending down. Resting HR 72, BP 128/76 mmHg, SpO2 97 percent on room air, no chest pain, ECG sinus rhythm. Echocardiogram shows mildly reduced left ventricular function. She lives with her husband and is keen to return to work. The nurse asks you to mobilise her and begin education. She tells you she is very grateful and is on the public waiting list for outpatient cardiac rehabilitation, which is several weeks away.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Decline the arrangement, explain the referral pathway, and record the offer as hospital policy requires): Accepting a valuable gift or moving a current patient into a private cash arrangement blurs professional boundaries, creates a conflict of interest with the public waiting list and may fall outside employer governance and insurance. The physiotherapist should decline kindly, explain the usual referral pathway and record the offer as policy requires. Keeping the voucher still compromises the boundary, and an informal mention does not meet the recording requirement."}, {"id": 1377, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "The registrar wonders whether this is only a bad contusion. Which finding in Daniel's presentation is the most reliable early clinical indicator of evolving acute compartment syndrome, and should be weighed above the normal X-ray when deciding what to do next in the department?", "options": {"A": "The palpable pedal pulse and normal capillary refill, which exclude raised compartment pressure now", "B": "Reduced sensation in the first web space, which develops earlier and more reliably than pain", "C": "The absence of fracture on X-ray, which makes a severe contusion the more likely explanation", "D": "Disproportionate pain worsened by passive toe stretch, while pulses often remain normal"}, "cluster_id": "CHS105", "cluster_text": "Daniel Kovac, 24, plays semi-professional football. Six hours ago an opponent's boot struck his right anterior shin. He was brought to the emergency department with pain that has increased despite ice and oral analgesia. Observations: HR 96, BP 128/78, RR 16, SpO2 99 percent, afebrile. Right lower leg: tense, shiny, swollen anterior compartment, pain 9 out of 10, increased by passive toe flexion; reduced sensation in the first web space; weak, painful ankle dorsiflexion. Dorsalis pedis pulse palpable, capillary refill 2 seconds. X-ray shows no fracture. The registrar has asked for physiotherapy review for crutch training and a home program. Your other referrals today are a day 1 total hip replacement due for discharge this afternoon and a rib fracture patient with a weak cough.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Disproportionate pain worsened by passive toe stretch, while pulses often remain normal): Pain that is far greater than the injury suggests and is provoked by passive stretch of the muscles in the compartment is the earliest and most dependable sign. Sensory change is a useful warning but usually follows the pain and is less reliable early on. Distal pulses and capillary refill usually stay intact until very late, so reassurance from them is dangerous. A normal X-ray only excludes a fracture and says nothing about pressure within the compartment."}, {"id": 1378, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Daniel asks to practise crutches so he can go home. His pain has risen to 10 out of 10 over the last hour and his toes feel numb. The leg remains tense and the nurse reports no change in his observations. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Apply a firm compression bandage to the shin to limit further bleeding into the muscle", "B": "Stop mobilising and urgently escalate to the treating medical team for compartment assessment", "C": "Stop mobilising, remove compression, keep the leg level with the heart, and reassess in thirty minutes", "D": "Elevate the leg well above heart level and apply ice, then reassess pain after twenty minutes"}, "cluster_id": "CHS105", "cluster_text": "Daniel Kovac, 24, plays semi-professional football. Six hours ago an opponent's boot struck his right anterior shin. He was brought to the emergency department with pain that has increased despite ice and oral analgesia. Observations: HR 96, BP 128/78, RR 16, SpO2 99 percent, afebrile. Right lower leg: tense, shiny, swollen anterior compartment, pain 9 out of 10, increased by passive toe flexion; reduced sensation in the first web space; weak, painful ankle dorsiflexion. Dorsalis pedis pulse palpable, capillary refill 2 seconds. X-ray shows no fracture. The registrar has asked for physiotherapy review for crutch training and a home program. Your other referrals today are a day 1 total hip replacement due for discharge this afternoon and a rib fracture patient with a weak cough.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stop mobilising and urgently escalate to the treating medical team for compartment assessment): Rising pain with new numbness in a tense compartment is a limb threatening emergency that needs immediate medical assessment, so activity stops and the team is alerted at once. Positioning at heart level is sensible first aid but waiting thirty minutes to reassess delays the decision to operate. Elevation above the heart lowers arterial inflow and worsens perfusion, and a tight bandage raises pressure further."}, {"id": 1379, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Two weeks after urgent fasciotomy, Daniel's wound has healed and surgeons clear him for active rehabilitation of the anterior compartment. He has mild ankle swelling after walking and is keen to return to football training quickly. Which approach to loading is most appropriate now?", "options": {"A": "Rest in a compression sleeve until all ankle swelling has settled before beginning any active loading", "B": "Start plyometric hopping and sprint drills to restore explosive power before rebuilding basic muscle strength", "C": "Use deep friction massage and intensive passive stretching to remodel scar tissue before starting strengthening", "D": "Progress from isometric to concentric then eccentric loading, tracking swelling and pain after sessions"}, "cluster_id": "CHS105", "cluster_text": "Daniel Kovac, 24, plays semi-professional football. Six hours ago an opponent's boot struck his right anterior shin. He was brought to the emergency department with pain that has increased despite ice and oral analgesia. Observations: HR 96, BP 128/78, RR 16, SpO2 99 percent, afebrile. Right lower leg: tense, shiny, swollen anterior compartment, pain 9 out of 10, increased by passive toe flexion; reduced sensation in the first web space; weak, painful ankle dorsiflexion. Dorsalis pedis pulse palpable, capillary refill 2 seconds. X-ray shows no fracture. The registrar has asked for physiotherapy review for crutch training and a home program. Your other referrals today are a day 1 total hip replacement due for discharge this afternoon and a rib fracture patient with a weak cough.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Progress from isometric to concentric then eccentric loading, tracking swelling and pain after sessions): Graded loading from low demand isometric work towards eccentric work rebuilds the injured muscle while the swelling and pain response guides each step. Jumping and sprinting before strength returns overloads healing tissue. Prolonged rest delays recovery, and aggressive friction or stretching risks irritating the healing wound and muscle."}, {"id": 1380, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Having escalated Daniel's findings, you must prioritise the rest of your list. The rib fracture patient has a weak cough and SpO2 falling from 96 to 93 percent, and the hip replacement patient needs mobilising before discharge this afternoon. Which plan for the next hour is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Stay with Daniel until the medical team decides, then see the other two patients in order", "B": "See the rib fracture patient now, remain contactable for Daniel, then mobilise the hip patient", "C": "Mobilise the hip patient first to avoid delaying discharge, then review the rib fracture patient", "D": "Delegate the hip patient's mobilisation to the assistant and see the rib fracture patient after lunch"}, "cluster_id": "CHS105", "cluster_text": "Daniel Kovac, 24, plays semi-professional football. Six hours ago an opponent's boot struck his right anterior shin. He was brought to the emergency department with pain that has increased despite ice and oral analgesia. Observations: HR 96, BP 128/78, RR 16, SpO2 99 percent, afebrile. Right lower leg: tense, shiny, swollen anterior compartment, pain 9 out of 10, increased by passive toe flexion; reduced sensation in the first web space; weak, painful ankle dorsiflexion. Dorsalis pedis pulse palpable, capillary refill 2 seconds. X-ray shows no fracture. The registrar has asked for physiotherapy review for crutch training and a home program. Your other referrals today are a day 1 total hip replacement due for discharge this afternoon and a rib fracture patient with a weak cough.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (See the rib fracture patient now, remain contactable for Daniel, then mobilise the hip patient): Daniel is already in the hands of the medical team after escalation, and the patient whose oxygen saturation is falling with a weak cough is the next most urgent and is where physiotherapy skills are most needed. Discharge timing is a lower clinical priority than deterioration. Staying with Daniel adds little once the team is acting, and deferring the respiratory patient until after lunch is unsafe."}, {"id": 1381, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Marlee's left hip abduction is now 15 degrees, half the range on the right, with new groin pain after walking and no fever or fall. Her last hip X-ray was 18 months ago. Which interpretation of these findings is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Muscle fatigue after walking, managed by reducing walker use and observing symptoms over coming weeks", "B": "Expected adductor tightness for GMFCS level III, managed by stretching and routine review.", "C": "Possible hip migration or displacement, which needs imaging soon rather than continued routine monitoring", "D": "Hamstring shortening referred to the groin, treated with sustained hamstring stretches before reassessing the hip"}, "cluster_id": "CHS106", "cluster_text": "Marlee Johnson, 6, Aboriginal girl with spastic diplegic cerebral palsy (GMFCS level III), lives in a remote community 700 km from the nearest paediatric centre. Her aunt, Aunty Rhonda, is her main carer. A visiting physiotherapist attends every three months; an Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander health practitioner works in the local clinic and telehealth is available. Marlee walks with a posterior walker at home and school and uses a wheelchair for longer distances. Today she limps more and complains of left groin and thigh pain after walking. No fever or fall. Left hip abduction is 15 degrees compared with 30 degrees on the right. Popliteal angle is 60 degrees bilaterally. Her last hip X-ray was 18 months ago. The clinic doctor visits fortnightly. Aunty Rhonda is worried and says the family prefers to decide together.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Possible hip migration or displacement, which needs imaging soon rather than continued routine monitoring): Children with bilateral spastic cerebral palsy are at risk of hip migration, and a new asymmetric loss of abduction with groin pain is a recognised warning that surveillance imaging is overdue. Treating it as routine tightness or fatigue delays diagnosis. The symmetrical hamstring angle does not explain a one-sided hip restriction."}, {"id": 1382, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "The physiotherapist suspects hip migration. The next visit is three months away, the doctor visits fortnightly, and a paediatrician can be reached by telehealth. Aunty Rhonda is anxious and wants the family involved. What is the most appropriate action to take now?", "options": {"A": "Contact the doctor and paediatrician by telehealth to arrange hip imaging and inform the family", "B": "Arrange evacuation through the Royal Flying Doctor Service, because suspected hip migration is a medical emergency", "C": "Teach Aunty Rhonda abduction stretching and standing programmes, then book an X-ray.", "D": "Ask the NDIS planner to fund a walker and hip brace, then await the plan review"}, "cluster_id": "CHS106", "cluster_text": "Marlee Johnson, 6, Aboriginal girl with spastic diplegic cerebral palsy (GMFCS level III), lives in a remote community 700 km from the nearest paediatric centre. Her aunt, Aunty Rhonda, is her main carer. A visiting physiotherapist attends every three months; an Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander health practitioner works in the local clinic and telehealth is available. Marlee walks with a posterior walker at home and school and uses a wheelchair for longer distances. Today she limps more and complains of left groin and thigh pain after walking. No fever or fall. Left hip abduction is 15 degrees compared with 30 degrees on the right. Popliteal angle is 60 degrees bilaterally. Her last hip X-ray was 18 months ago. The clinic doctor visits fortnightly. Aunty Rhonda is worried and says the family prefers to decide together.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Contact the doctor and paediatrician by telehealth to arrange hip imaging and inform the family): Imaging and medical review are outside physiotherapy scope but need to happen promptly, so the physiotherapist escalates through the available telehealth and clinic doctor pathway and keeps the family informed. Waiting three months is unsafe. Emergency evacuation is disproportionate for a child without acute signs, and funding applications do not address the clinical question."}, {"id": 1383, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "The family prefers to decide together. Marlee needs a simple daily programme for the three months until the next physiotherapy visit, with help from the local clinic. Aunty Rhonda has limited transport and works part-time. Which approach to the home programme is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Build a short routine with the family and health practitioner, using play and daily activities", "B": "Give a written sheet of twelve stretches and strength tasks, requesting fortnightly progress updates by phone", "C": "Plan clinic sessions with the practitioner each week, with Aunty Rhonda attending to learn the exercises", "D": "Teach Aunty Rhonda a short routine, then review progress at the next visit in three months"}, "cluster_id": "CHS106", "cluster_text": "Marlee Johnson, 6, Aboriginal girl with spastic diplegic cerebral palsy (GMFCS level III), lives in a remote community 700 km from the nearest paediatric centre. Her aunt, Aunty Rhonda, is her main carer. A visiting physiotherapist attends every three months; an Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander health practitioner works in the local clinic and telehealth is available. Marlee walks with a posterior walker at home and school and uses a wheelchair for longer distances. Today she limps more and complains of left groin and thigh pain after walking. No fever or fall. Left hip abduction is 15 degrees compared with 30 degrees on the right. Popliteal angle is 60 degrees bilaterally. Her last hip X-ray was 18 months ago. The clinic doctor visits fortnightly. Aunty Rhonda is worried and says the family prefers to decide together.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Build a short routine with the family and health practitioner, using play and daily activities): Culturally safe practice involves the carer and family in shared decisions and fits therapy into everyday routines and play, with the local health practitioner as a continuing support. A long written list is hard to sustain and suits neither the family's circumstances nor how a young child learns. Weekly clinic attendance ignores limited transport and work commitments. Teaching one carer and then leaving the programme unsupported for three months removes the local support and shared decision making."}, {"id": 1384, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "While waiting for imaging, Marlee still walks to school with her posterior walker. She is not in pain at rest. Aunty Rhonda asks what the family should do about her walking and stretching in the meantime. Which advice is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Encourage walking without the walker to load the hip, since weight bearing promotes normal acetabular development", "B": "Apply sustained daily passive hip abduction stretching to regain range before the X-ray can be taken", "C": "Stop all walking and standing and use the wheelchair full time until the paediatrician reviews her", "D": "Continue walker use as comfortable, but avoid forceful hip abduction stretching until imaging is reviewed"}, "cluster_id": "CHS106", "cluster_text": "Marlee Johnson, 6, Aboriginal girl with spastic diplegic cerebral palsy (GMFCS level III), lives in a remote community 700 km from the nearest paediatric centre. Her aunt, Aunty Rhonda, is her main carer. A visiting physiotherapist attends every three months; an Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander health practitioner works in the local clinic and telehealth is available. Marlee walks with a posterior walker at home and school and uses a wheelchair for longer distances. Today she limps more and complains of left groin and thigh pain after walking. No fever or fall. Left hip abduction is 15 degrees compared with 30 degrees on the right. Popliteal angle is 60 degrees bilaterally. Her last hip X-ray was 18 months ago. The clinic doctor visits fortnightly. Aunty Rhonda is worried and says the family prefers to decide together.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Continue walker use as comfortable, but avoid forceful hip abduction stretching until imaging is reviewed): Comfortable activity keeps her mobile and participating, while forceful stretching of a possibly unstable hip could cause pain or harm before its position is known. Complete restriction is unnecessary without acute signs and has its own costs. Forcing range or removing her walker adds risk without any benefit before imaging."}, {"id": 1385, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Bennett's daughter says his memory and thinking were normal before admission, and nurses describe him as alert one hour and drowsy the next. A colleague asks which aspect of the picture most strongly points to delirium rather than a new dementia diagnosis. What is it?", "options": {"A": "Abrupt onset during acute illness with fluctuating alertness and inattention across the day", "B": "Disorientation to the date and the hospital, a finding specific to delirium among older inpatients", "C": "Agitation with pulling at lines, since hyperactive behaviour is required before delirium can be diagnosed", "D": "Hearing loss and absent glasses, which alone explain the confusion seen on the ward"}, "cluster_id": "CHS107", "cluster_text": "Harold Bennett, 78, a retired bus driver, is on day 4 in intensive care after emergency laparotomy for bowel obstruction. History: type 2 diabetes and mild hearing loss; his daughter reports no memory or thinking problems before admission. He was extubated yesterday. Overnight nursing notes record fluctuating confusion, pulling at his intravenous line, and drowsiness by day. This morning: heart rate 96, blood pressure 128/74, respiratory rate 20, SpO2 95 percent on 2 L/min nasal prongs, afebrile. A delirium screening tool completed by the nurse is positive. He cannot state the date or the hospital and is hyperactive at times, then lethargic. His hearing aids and glasses are at the bedside but not in use. Limb power is grade 4 throughout, though he tires after two repetitions. The abdominal wound is intact with a drain in place. The nurse in charge asks whether he can be mobilised, and the intensivist will round in 30 minutes.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Abrupt onset during acute illness with fluctuating alertness and inattention across the day): Delirium is defined by acute onset, a fluctuating course and disturbed attention and alertness, usually triggered by illness, surgery or medications. Disorientation occurs in dementia as well, so it does not separate the two, and hypoactive delirium shows that agitation is not required. Sensory impairment is a risk factor and should be corrected, but it does not by itself account for fluctuating alertness."}, {"id": 1386, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Bennett is drowsy but rousable and cooperates for short periods. His observations are stable, the drain is secured and his lines are in place. The nurse asks whether to mobilise him now. Which approach to mobilisation best suits his delirium and current status?", "options": {"A": "Run a prolonged resistance exercise session in bed, using firm repeated commands to secure his cooperation", "B": "Early supervised sit out of bed and short walk with glasses and hearing aids in place", "C": "Defer all mobilisation until the delirium screen is negative, to avoid distressing him further", "D": "Limit input to passive range of movement in bed until he can follow multistep commands reliably"}, "cluster_id": "CHS107", "cluster_text": "Harold Bennett, 78, a retired bus driver, is on day 4 in intensive care after emergency laparotomy for bowel obstruction. History: type 2 diabetes and mild hearing loss; his daughter reports no memory or thinking problems before admission. He was extubated yesterday. Overnight nursing notes record fluctuating confusion, pulling at his intravenous line, and drowsiness by day. This morning: heart rate 96, blood pressure 128/74, respiratory rate 20, SpO2 95 percent on 2 L/min nasal prongs, afebrile. A delirium screening tool completed by the nurse is positive. He cannot state the date or the hospital and is hyperactive at times, then lethargic. His hearing aids and glasses are at the bedside but not in use. Limb power is grade 4 throughout, though he tires after two repetitions. The abdominal wound is intact with a drain in place. The nurse in charge asks whether he can be mobilised, and the intensivist will round in 30 minutes.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Early supervised sit out of bed and short walk with glasses and hearing aids in place): Early, safe mobilisation with sensory aids supports orientation, the sleep and wake rhythm and recovery, and is a recognised non-drug approach in delirium. He is haemodynamically stable, so waiting for a negative screen would only prolong immobility. Forceful or bed-bound exercise ignores his limited attention and risks distress, and passive movement alone gives little functional benefit."}, {"id": 1387, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "During a second session he becomes abruptly more drowsy while sitting out. His heart rate is 118, respiratory rate 28 and temperature 38.6 degrees Celsius, up from 96, 20 and afebrile this morning. Which immediate action by the physiotherapist is most appropriate here?", "options": {"A": "Reorient him calmly and reassess in an hour, as delirium was already identified this morning", "B": "Continue the session gently while monitoring, as raised observations are expected with exertion in delirium", "C": "Return him to bed, document the changes, and mention them to the nurse at handover", "D": "Return him to bed, stay with him and call the nurse for urgent medical review"}, "cluster_id": "CHS107", "cluster_text": "Harold Bennett, 78, a retired bus driver, is on day 4 in intensive care after emergency laparotomy for bowel obstruction. History: type 2 diabetes and mild hearing loss; his daughter reports no memory or thinking problems before admission. He was extubated yesterday. Overnight nursing notes record fluctuating confusion, pulling at his intravenous line, and drowsiness by day. This morning: heart rate 96, blood pressure 128/74, respiratory rate 20, SpO2 95 percent on 2 L/min nasal prongs, afebrile. A delirium screening tool completed by the nurse is positive. He cannot state the date or the hospital and is hyperactive at times, then lethargic. His hearing aids and glasses are at the bedside but not in use. Limb power is grade 4 throughout, though he tires after two repetitions. The abdominal wound is intact with a drain in place. The nurse in charge asks whether he can be mobilised, and the intensivist will round in 30 minutes.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Return him to bed, stay with him and call the nurse for urgent medical review): An abrupt change in alertness with tachycardia, tachypnoea and fever suggests a new acute problem such as sepsis or a surgical complication, which needs urgent medical assessment. The physiotherapist stops activity, keeps him safe and escalates immediately. Attributing the change to existing delirium, waiting an hour, or continuing gently risks delayed treatment of a deteriorating patient. Raising it only at handover is far too late."}, {"id": 1388, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "The intensivist has now arrived at the bedside, and Mr Bennett has been returned to bed with the nurse present. You are asked for a handover about the session. Which approach to this verbal handover best supports the team's urgent decision making?", "options": {"A": "Write a detailed note and ask the nurse to relay the concerns to the team", "B": "Give a structured summary: the change, observations before and after activity, and your actions", "C": "Describe the whole session chronologically from first contact, including each exercise and his spoken comments", "D": "Report the abnormal vital signs and give your opinion on the likely medical diagnosis"}, "cluster_id": "CHS107", "cluster_text": "Harold Bennett, 78, a retired bus driver, is on day 4 in intensive care after emergency laparotomy for bowel obstruction. History: type 2 diabetes and mild hearing loss; his daughter reports no memory or thinking problems before admission. He was extubated yesterday. Overnight nursing notes record fluctuating confusion, pulling at his intravenous line, and drowsiness by day. This morning: heart rate 96, blood pressure 128/74, respiratory rate 20, SpO2 95 percent on 2 L/min nasal prongs, afebrile. A delirium screening tool completed by the nurse is positive. He cannot state the date or the hospital and is hyperactive at times, then lethargic. His hearing aids and glasses are at the bedside but not in use. Limb power is grade 4 throughout, though he tires after two repetitions. The abdominal wound is intact with a drain in place. The nurse in charge asks whether he can be mobilised, and the intensivist will round in 30 minutes.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Give a structured summary: the change, observations before and after activity, and your actions): A structured handover covering what changed, objective data before and after activity, and the actions taken gives the intensivist what is needed for urgent decisions and reduces information loss. A long chronological account buries the key point, and passing concerns on second hand delays action. Diagnosing is outside the physiotherapist's role, although reporting objective findings is essential."}, {"id": 1389, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Lewandowski has an SpO2 of 91 percent on high-flow oxygen, reduced right basal air entry, no sputum, no fever and no previous lung disease. His cough is weak and he avoids deep breaths. Which mechanism most likely explains his hypoxaemia at this stage?", "options": {"A": "Pulmonary embolism from immobility producing a ventilation to perfusion mismatch with clear lung fields on auscultation", "B": "Pain limited breathing with loss of right basal lung volume and underlying contusion", "C": "Retained secretions in the right lower lobe from impaired mucociliary clearance, causing airway plugging", "D": "Residual pneumothorax with an inadequately functioning drain, producing reduced air entry at the right base"}, "cluster_id": "CHS108", "cluster_text": "Goran Lewandowski, 58, a builder, fell from a ladder two days ago. He has right rib fractures 4 to 9 with a flail segment and a small right pneumothorax managed with an intercostal catheter on underwater seal. He is on day 2 in intensive care with an epidural running. Non-smoker, no lung disease, afebrile. High-flow nasal oxygen 40 L/min, FiO2 0.5. RR 26, SpO2 91%, HR 102, BP 128/76. Pain is 7/10 on coughing and 3/10 at rest. He breathes shallowly with paradoxical movement of the right chest wall, and holds his breath to avoid coughing. Air entry is reduced at the right base with no added wet sounds, and he has produced no sputum. The drain swings with a small intermittent bubble on coughing. Chest X-ray shows right basal atelectasis and lung contusion. The nurse reports that his epidural block seems patchy.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Pain limited breathing with loss of right basal lung volume and underlying contusion): Without sputum, fever or smoking history, the picture is pain-driven shallow breathing, a mobile chest wall and contused, collapsed basal lung, matching the chest X-ray findings. Secretion plugging is tempting because cough is weak, but there is no sputum or wet sound to support it. A drain that swings and bubbles on cough argues against a significant residual pneumothorax. Embolism is possible after immobility but does not explain the basal collapse and contusion. Treatment should aim at lung volume and effective analgesia first."}, {"id": 1390, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Analgesia has been reviewed and he now reports pain of 4/10 on coughing. The physiotherapist wants to improve lung volume and cough effectiveness over the injured right side without disturbing the fractures or the drain. Which technique is most appropriate to teach him now?", "options": {"A": "Manual percussion over the right lateral chest to loosen secretions before he attempts a deep breath", "B": "Incentive spirometry with long maximal inspirations performed lying flat and without chest wall support", "C": "Mechanical insufflation-exsufflation to boost cough effectiveness, delivered at high pressures with the chest unsupported", "D": "Deep breathing with a firm folded towel held against the right chest wall during coughing"}, "cluster_id": "CHS108", "cluster_text": "Goran Lewandowski, 58, a builder, fell from a ladder two days ago. He has right rib fractures 4 to 9 with a flail segment and a small right pneumothorax managed with an intercostal catheter on underwater seal. He is on day 2 in intensive care with an epidural running. Non-smoker, no lung disease, afebrile. High-flow nasal oxygen 40 L/min, FiO2 0.5. RR 26, SpO2 91%, HR 102, BP 128/76. Pain is 7/10 on coughing and 3/10 at rest. He breathes shallowly with paradoxical movement of the right chest wall, and holds his breath to avoid coughing. Air entry is reduced at the right base with no added wet sounds, and he has produced no sputum. The drain swings with a small intermittent bubble on coughing. Chest X-ray shows right basal atelectasis and lung contusion. The nurse reports that his epidural block seems patchy.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Deep breathing with a firm folded towel held against the right chest wall during coughing): Supporting the chest wall by hand or with a rolled towel reduces pain and paradoxical movement while he takes deep breaths and coughs. Percussion over a flail segment risks displacing fractures and worsens pain, and he has no secretions to loosen. Spirometry lying flat without support leaves the painful segment unsplinted. High pressure assisted cough adds risk to an unstable chest wall and a drain with an air leak."}, {"id": 1391, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "During the session his SpO2 falls from 91 to 84 percent, RR rises to 34, the drain stops swinging, and crackling swelling spreads rapidly over his neck and chest wall. He becomes distressed and unable to speak in sentences. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Clamp the drain near the chest wall while a nurse checks the connections", "B": "Check the tubing for kinks and gently milk it to restore the swing, then reassess", "C": "Sit him forward, increase his oxygen, and ask the nurse to check the drain shortly", "D": "Stop treatment, keep him upright on oxygen, and call urgently for medical review of the drain"}, "cluster_id": "CHS108", "cluster_text": "Goran Lewandowski, 58, a builder, fell from a ladder two days ago. He has right rib fractures 4 to 9 with a flail segment and a small right pneumothorax managed with an intercostal catheter on underwater seal. He is on day 2 in intensive care with an epidural running. Non-smoker, no lung disease, afebrile. High-flow nasal oxygen 40 L/min, FiO2 0.5. RR 26, SpO2 91%, HR 102, BP 128/76. Pain is 7/10 on coughing and 3/10 at rest. He breathes shallowly with paradoxical movement of the right chest wall, and holds his breath to avoid coughing. Air entry is reduced at the right base with no added wet sounds, and he has produced no sputum. The drain swings with a small intermittent bubble on coughing. Chest X-ray shows right basal atelectasis and lung contusion. The nurse reports that his epidural block seems patchy.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stop treatment, keep him upright on oxygen, and call urgently for medical review of the drain): Rapid desaturation with a stagnant drain and spreading surgical emphysema suggests a blocked or displaced drain and possible tension physiology, which needs immediate medical assessment. Clamping a drain in a patient with an air leak can convert a pneumothorax into a tension pneumothorax. Checking and milking the tubing, or asking for a nurse check shortly, spends critical minutes on a problem that needs a doctor now."}, {"id": 1392, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "A nurse says the epidural seems patchy and asks the physiotherapist to increase the infusion rate so the afternoon session can proceed. Changing infusions is outside physiotherapy scope in this hospital, and the patient's pain is limiting treatment. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Decline to change the rate, explain the scope limit, and ask the pain service to review", "B": "Decline to change the rate and treat as planned, then report the patchy block at handover", "C": "Increase the rate a small step at the nurse's direction and note it in the record", "D": "Delay the session by an hour and wait to see whether the patchy block settles"}, "cluster_id": "CHS108", "cluster_text": "Goran Lewandowski, 58, a builder, fell from a ladder two days ago. He has right rib fractures 4 to 9 with a flail segment and a small right pneumothorax managed with an intercostal catheter on underwater seal. He is on day 2 in intensive care with an epidural running. Non-smoker, no lung disease, afebrile. High-flow nasal oxygen 40 L/min, FiO2 0.5. RR 26, SpO2 91%, HR 102, BP 128/76. Pain is 7/10 on coughing and 3/10 at rest. He breathes shallowly with paradoxical movement of the right chest wall, and holds his breath to avoid coughing. Air entry is reduced at the right base with no added wet sounds, and he has produced no sputum. The drain swings with a small intermittent bubble on coughing. Chest X-ray shows right basal atelectasis and lung contusion. The nurse reports that his epidural block seems patchy.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Decline to change the rate, explain the scope limit, and ask the pain service to review): Changing analgesic infusions sits outside physiotherapy scope, and a nurse's request does not extend it. The physiotherapist should communicate the clinical need, that pain is limiting treatment, to the team who can adjust medication, and do so before treating. Treating through poorly controlled pain compromises cough effectiveness, and reporting only at handover leaves the problem unaddressed. Waiting for the block to settle without involving the pain service delays relief."}, {"id": 1393, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Nguyen has SpO2 97 percent on 6 L/min, RR 12 and slight drowsiness. Her prescription is 2 L/min with a target of 88 to 92 percent, and she retained carbon dioxide last year. Which concern is the most important in this situation?", "options": {"A": "Her saturation is reassuringly high, so drowsiness likely reflects tiredness from the exacerbation and needs rest", "B": "Her saturation exceeds target, so oxygen should be stopped and her saturation rechecked on room air", "C": "Excess oxygen may be worsening carbon dioxide retention, explaining her drowsiness and slow respiratory rate", "D": "Drowsiness suggests developing sepsis, so the oxygen flow can wait until after the medical review"}, "cluster_id": "CHS109", "cluster_text": "Dorothy Nguyen, 79, is on day 2 of an acute medical admission for a COPD exacerbation. She uses long term oxygen at home, and last year she needed non-invasive ventilation for carbon dioxide retention. The medical orders state a target SpO2 of 88 to 92 percent with prescribed oxygen of 2 L/min via nasal prongs. When you arrive for a mobility session, the wall flowmeter reads 6 L/min. She is sitting in a chair, slightly drowsy but easy to rouse. At rest on that flow: SpO2 97 percent, RR 12, HR 80, BP 130/76. Once the flow is returned to 2 L/min and she settles, SpO2 is 91 percent. Walking 10 metres with a frame: SpO2 86 percent, RR 26, Borg breathlessness 5, recovering to 90 percent after two minutes seated. She has mild bilateral wheeze and a productive cough. The nurse in the next bay is available.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Excess oxygen may be worsening carbon dioxide retention, explaining her drowsiness and slow respiratory rate): In people at risk of hypercapnia, oxygen above the prescribed target can reduce ventilatory drive and raise carbon dioxide, producing drowsiness and a slowing respiratory rate. A high saturation is therefore not reassuring here. Stopping oxygen altogether is wrong for a long term oxygen user, because the aim is to return to the prescribed flow and target range. Attributing the picture to tiredness or to sepsis first would miss a correctable and potentially dangerous cause."}, {"id": 1394, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "On finding the 6 L/min flow and Mrs Nguyen slightly drowsy but rousable, the physiotherapist needs to act before starting the planned mobility session. Her airway is clear and the nurse is in the next bay. What should be done first?", "options": {"A": "Turn the oxygen off to see whether she holds target saturation on room air before mobilising", "B": "Return the flow to the prescribed rate, recheck alertness and SpO2, and inform the nurse", "C": "Leave the flow as found and sit her upright, since higher flow supports breathing during activity", "D": "Proceed with mobility at the current flow and report the flow setting to the nurse afterwards"}, "cluster_id": "CHS109", "cluster_text": "Dorothy Nguyen, 79, is on day 2 of an acute medical admission for a COPD exacerbation. She uses long term oxygen at home, and last year she needed non-invasive ventilation for carbon dioxide retention. The medical orders state a target SpO2 of 88 to 92 percent with prescribed oxygen of 2 L/min via nasal prongs. When you arrive for a mobility session, the wall flowmeter reads 6 L/min. She is sitting in a chair, slightly drowsy but easy to rouse. At rest on that flow: SpO2 97 percent, RR 12, HR 80, BP 130/76. Once the flow is returned to 2 L/min and she settles, SpO2 is 91 percent. Walking 10 metres with a frame: SpO2 86 percent, RR 26, Borg breathlessness 5, recovering to 90 percent after two minutes seated. She has mild bilateral wheeze and a productive cough. The nurse in the next bay is available.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Return the flow to the prescribed rate, recheck alertness and SpO2, and inform the nurse): The flow exceeds the prescription and she has signs that suggest carbon dioxide retention, so the flow is corrected to the prescribed rate and her state rechecked against the target range, with the nurse told. Switching oxygen off abruptly risks hypoxaemia in a patient on long term oxygen. Starting exercise on excess oxygen would also mask early deterioration."}, {"id": 1395, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Back on 2 L/min, Mrs Nguyen walks 10 metres with her frame. SpO2 falls to 86 percent and RR rises to 26, with Borg breathlessness 5, then recovers to 90 percent after two minutes seated. Her target is 88 to 92 percent. How should future walking be managed?", "options": {"A": "Limit her to bed exercises, because desaturation below 88 percent on exertion contraindicates walking", "B": "Short walks with seated rests and pursed lip breathing, and ask the team about exertional oxygen", "C": "Stop walking until her resting saturation holds above 94 percent, to prevent any desaturation on exertion", "D": "Raise oxygen during walking to keep SpO2 above 95 percent, the usual walking target"}, "cluster_id": "CHS109", "cluster_text": "Dorothy Nguyen, 79, is on day 2 of an acute medical admission for a COPD exacerbation. She uses long term oxygen at home, and last year she needed non-invasive ventilation for carbon dioxide retention. The medical orders state a target SpO2 of 88 to 92 percent with prescribed oxygen of 2 L/min via nasal prongs. When you arrive for a mobility session, the wall flowmeter reads 6 L/min. She is sitting in a chair, slightly drowsy but easy to rouse. At rest on that flow: SpO2 97 percent, RR 12, HR 80, BP 130/76. Once the flow is returned to 2 L/min and she settles, SpO2 is 91 percent. Walking 10 metres with a frame: SpO2 86 percent, RR 26, Borg breathlessness 5, recovering to 90 percent after two minutes seated. She has mild bilateral wheeze and a productive cough. The nurse in the next bay is available.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Short walks with seated rests and pursed lip breathing, and ask the team about exertional oxygen): Brief desaturation on exertion with quick recovery is common in COPD and is managed with pacing, rests and breathing control while the medical team decides whether the oxygen prescription should change for activity. Her target stays 88 to 92 percent, so pushing saturations above 95 percent risks carbon dioxide retention. Stopping walking altogether would accelerate deconditioning."}, {"id": 1396, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "The flowmeter was found at 6 L/min against a prescription of 2 L/min, and Mrs Nguyen was drowsy. Once she is stable and the flow is corrected, what is the most appropriate risk management step for the physiotherapist to take next?", "options": {"A": "Lodge an incident report in the hospital system and record the facts and her response", "B": "Monitor her over the next day and report formally only if she comes to harm", "C": "Note the event in your own work diary so the nurse can review it at handover", "D": "Tell the nurse verbally and leave it to the team to decide on a written report"}, "cluster_id": "CHS109", "cluster_text": "Dorothy Nguyen, 79, is on day 2 of an acute medical admission for a COPD exacerbation. She uses long term oxygen at home, and last year she needed non-invasive ventilation for carbon dioxide retention. The medical orders state a target SpO2 of 88 to 92 percent with prescribed oxygen of 2 L/min via nasal prongs. When you arrive for a mobility session, the wall flowmeter reads 6 L/min. She is sitting in a chair, slightly drowsy but easy to rouse. At rest on that flow: SpO2 97 percent, RR 12, HR 80, BP 130/76. Once the flow is returned to 2 L/min and she settles, SpO2 is 91 percent. Walking 10 metres with a frame: SpO2 86 percent, RR 26, Borg breathlessness 5, recovering to 90 percent after two minutes seated. She has mild bilateral wheeze and a productive cough. The nurse in the next bay is available.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Lodge an incident report in the hospital system and record the facts and her response): An event that could have harmed a patient should be reported through the hospital incident system so the cause can be investigated and repeated errors prevented, whether or not harm resulted. Factual documentation of what was found and how she responded supports patient care. A verbal remark or a private diary note leaves no auditable trail, and waiting for harm defeats the purpose of near miss reporting."}, {"id": 1397, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Karras wants to add heavy leg press and deadlifts to the group session. He feels well and has no pain. Considering his aneurysm and the blood pressure limit set by his vascular team, which resistance training approach is most appropriate for the physiotherapist to prescribe?", "options": {"A": "Machine based exercises only, performed to muscular failure each set with blood pressure measured once afterwards", "B": "Moderate loads, higher repetitions, steady breathing without breath holding, and blood pressure checks between sets", "C": "Isometric holds such as wall sits and planks for sustained periods, as these avoid rapid movements", "D": "Maximal loads for few repetitions with a firm breath hold for trunk stability and longer rests"}, "cluster_id": "CHS110", "cluster_text": "Giorgos Karras, 74, a retired welder, attends a community exercise group run by a local health service. He has a known infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm under surveillance, hypertension, and stopped smoking ten years ago. His vascular team has advised that his systolic blood pressure should stay below 160 mmHg during exercise. Today, seated at rest: BP 148/88 mmHg, HR 70, SpO2 96 percent. He feels well and reports no abdominal or back pain. He walks 30 minutes daily and does light weights twice weekly. He wants to add heavy leg press and deadlifts, and says he feels dizzy when he stands. He asks you whether he should halve his blood pressure tablet because of the dizziness.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Moderate loads, higher repetitions, steady breathing without breath holding, and blood pressure checks between sets): Moderate loads with more repetitions and exhaling through effort keep pressure spikes lower, which matters with an aneurysm and a set systolic limit. Checking blood pressure between sets confirms the limit is respected. Maximal efforts, sustained isometric holds and training to failure all drive large pressure rises, even when they feel controlled or avoid fast movement."}, {"id": 1398, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "During a brisk walking circuit, Mr Karras says he feels fine and is not short of breath, but a reading taken immediately after the second lap is 174/92 mmHg. His vascular team set a limit below 160 systolic. What should the physiotherapist do now?", "options": {"A": "Continue the circuit as he has no symptoms, rechecking pressure after the next lap", "B": "End the session and send him home, advising a GP review of his tablets", "C": "Stop walking, seat him, recheck after rest, and resume at lower intensity once under the limit", "D": "Slow the pace and keep walking, since stopping abruptly can cause blood pooling and dizziness"}, "cluster_id": "CHS110", "cluster_text": "Giorgos Karras, 74, a retired welder, attends a community exercise group run by a local health service. He has a known infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm under surveillance, hypertension, and stopped smoking ten years ago. His vascular team has advised that his systolic blood pressure should stay below 160 mmHg during exercise. Today, seated at rest: BP 148/88 mmHg, HR 70, SpO2 96 percent. He feels well and reports no abdominal or back pain. He walks 30 minutes daily and does light weights twice weekly. He wants to add heavy leg press and deadlifts, and says he feels dizzy when he stands. He asks you whether he should halve his blood pressure tablet because of the dizziness.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop walking, seat him, recheck after rest, and resume at lower intensity once under the limit): Exceeding the pressure ceiling set by the vascular team is a reason to stop exercise even though he feels well, because stress on the aneurysm wall is not felt. A seated rest and recheck shows whether pressure settles, after which a reduced intensity can be trialled. Continuing or only slowing risks sustained high pressure, while sending him home without monitoring recovery is unnecessarily abrupt."}, {"id": 1399, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Weeks later Mr Karras arrives for the group session and reports sudden severe central abdominal pain radiating to his back, starting an hour ago. He looks pale and sweaty, his pulse is fast and his blood pressure is low. What is the most appropriate immediate action?", "options": {"A": "Lie him down, give sips of water, and arrange an urgent GP review within the hour", "B": "Call 000, keep him lying still, give nothing by mouth, and monitor until paramedics arrive", "C": "Phone his vascular surgeon for advice first, then call 000 if his pain has not eased", "D": "Move him to the clinic couch for abdominal palpation, then phone his vascular surgeon"}, "cluster_id": "CHS110", "cluster_text": "Giorgos Karras, 74, a retired welder, attends a community exercise group run by a local health service. He has a known infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm under surveillance, hypertension, and stopped smoking ten years ago. His vascular team has advised that his systolic blood pressure should stay below 160 mmHg during exercise. Today, seated at rest: BP 148/88 mmHg, HR 70, SpO2 96 percent. He feels well and reports no abdominal or back pain. He walks 30 minutes daily and does light weights twice weekly. He wants to add heavy leg press and deadlifts, and says he feels dizzy when he stands. He asks you whether he should halve his blood pressure tablet because of the dizziness.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Call 000, keep him lying still, give nothing by mouth, and monitor until paramedics arrive): Sudden severe abdominal and back pain with shock in a person with a known aneurysm suggests rupture, which is a life-threatening emergency needing an ambulance and surgical care. Palpating an aneurysm risks further harm. Seeking advice first, or arranging a GP visit, delays definitive care and removes paramedic monitoring. He should stay still, receive nothing by mouth in case of theatre, and be observed until help arrives."}, {"id": 1400, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Karras says he is dizzy on standing and asks the physiotherapist whether to halve his blood pressure tablet dose. He takes the tablets as prescribed and his seated reading is 148/88 mmHg. What is the most appropriate response within the physiotherapist's scope of practice?", "options": {"A": "Explain that dizziness on standing is normal at his age and monitor pressure during exercise", "B": "Check standing pressure, tell him to keep taking the tablets as prescribed, and review next week", "C": "Suggest he skips the tablet on exercise days, as his seated reading is within the limit", "D": "Check standing pressure, advise against changing the dose, and refer him to his GP promptly"}, "cluster_id": "CHS110", "cluster_text": "Giorgos Karras, 74, a retired welder, attends a community exercise group run by a local health service. He has a known infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm under surveillance, hypertension, and stopped smoking ten years ago. His vascular team has advised that his systolic blood pressure should stay below 160 mmHg during exercise. Today, seated at rest: BP 148/88 mmHg, HR 70, SpO2 96 percent. He feels well and reports no abdominal or back pain. He walks 30 minutes daily and does light weights twice weekly. He wants to add heavy leg press and deadlifts, and says he feels dizzy when he stands. He asks you whether he should halve his blood pressure tablet because of the dizziness.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Check standing pressure, advise against changing the dose, and refer him to his GP promptly): Measuring standing pressure documents the problem objectively, but adjusting antihypertensive medication belongs to the prescribing doctor, so the physiotherapist should advise no change and refer to the GP. Dizziness on standing may signal postural hypotension that needs review, and treating it as normal ignores a safety risk. Reviewing again next week without referral leaves the possible cause unassessed. Advising him to skip tablets is a medication decision outside scope."}, {"id": 1401, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "During a cough, bubbles pass through the water of Mr Menon's underwater seal chamber, and the water column swings with each breath. Drainage is serous and the drain is secure. What does the bubbling on coughing most likely indicate about his lung?", "options": {"A": "A small air leak from the lung surface, which is common early after lung resection", "B": "Normal tidalling of the water column as pleural pressure changes, which can produce bubbling on coughing", "C": "A loose connection in the tubing between patient and bottle, which needs the connections checked urgently", "D": "Air entering around the drain at the skin site, which commonly causes bubbling on coughing"}, "cluster_id": "CHS111", "cluster_text": "Mr Rahul Menon, 58, accountant, day one after a right video-assisted thoracoscopic lobectomy for lung cancer. Ex-smoker, 30 pack-years, uses salbutamol as needed. A single intercostal drain on the right connects to an underwater seal on low-pressure suction. Drainage overnight was serous. Observations: RR 20, HR 88, BP 124/78, SpO2 94% on 2 L/min. Auscultation: reduced air entry at the right base with a few transmitted secretions. Pain is 6 out of 10 with coughing and he splints poorly. He is alert and orientated and can describe his operation and the risks of staying in bed, but he declines to get out of bed this morning because he fears the drain will fall out. His wife is visiting.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A small air leak from the lung surface, which is common early after lung resection): Bubbling during coughing, with a swinging water column, points to a small air leak from the staple line or lung surface, which is common after resection. Tidalling alone is the normal swing of the water column and does not create bubbles passing through the water. A loose connection would usually produce continuous bubbling and loss of the swing, and the secure drain with an intact skin site argues against air entering at the skin."}, {"id": 1402, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Menon has agreed to sit on the edge of the bed. His drain is connected to the underwater seal with suction. He rates pain 6 out of 10 on movement and is anxious about the tubing. How should the physiotherapist manage the drain system while he moves?", "options": {"A": "Keep the chamber upright and below chest level, and check the tubing for kinks", "B": "Disconnect suction first, then rest the chamber on the bed beside him while he moves", "C": "Keep the chamber upright and level with his chest, and check the tubing for kinks", "D": "Ask the nurse to briefly clamp the tube near the chest wall while he transfers"}, "cluster_id": "CHS111", "cluster_text": "Mr Rahul Menon, 58, accountant, day one after a right video-assisted thoracoscopic lobectomy for lung cancer. Ex-smoker, 30 pack-years, uses salbutamol as needed. A single intercostal drain on the right connects to an underwater seal on low-pressure suction. Drainage overnight was serous. Observations: RR 20, HR 88, BP 124/78, SpO2 94% on 2 L/min. Auscultation: reduced air entry at the right base with a few transmitted secretions. Pain is 6 out of 10 with coughing and he splints poorly. He is alert and orientated and can describe his operation and the risks of staying in bed, but he declines to get out of bed this morning because he fears the drain will fall out. His wife is visiting.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Keep the chamber upright and below chest level, and check the tubing for kinks): The chamber must stay upright and below the chest so fluid and air drain one way and the water seal is preserved, and a kink would stop drainage. Holding the chamber level with the chest or resting it on the bed lets fluid run back toward the pleural space. Clamping the tube risks a tension pneumothorax if there is an air leak, and disconnecting suction without direction is not the physiotherapist's decision."}, {"id": 1403, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "On day 2, Mr Menon reports new tightness in his throat. Crackling swelling is palpable across his neck and chest wall, the drain has stopped swinging and drainage is minimal. SpO2 is 91%. What action should the physiotherapist take next in response to these findings?", "options": {"A": "Reassure him that surgical emphysema is common after resection and recheck observations at the next review", "B": "Stop treatment, keep him upright and urgently notify the medical team of possible drain blockage", "C": "Continue treatment with gentle supported deep breathing, and notify the nurse after the session ends", "D": "Lay him flat to reduce the swelling and ask the nurse to check the tubing"}, "cluster_id": "CHS111", "cluster_text": "Mr Rahul Menon, 58, accountant, day one after a right video-assisted thoracoscopic lobectomy for lung cancer. Ex-smoker, 30 pack-years, uses salbutamol as needed. A single intercostal drain on the right connects to an underwater seal on low-pressure suction. Drainage overnight was serous. Observations: RR 20, HR 88, BP 124/78, SpO2 94% on 2 L/min. Auscultation: reduced air entry at the right base with a few transmitted secretions. Pain is 6 out of 10 with coughing and he splints poorly. He is alert and orientated and can describe his operation and the risks of staying in bed, but he declines to get out of bed this morning because he fears the drain will fall out. His wife is visiting.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stop treatment, keep him upright and urgently notify the medical team of possible drain blockage): Spreading subcutaneous emphysema with a drain that no longer swings suggests the drain is blocked or displaced and air is tracking into the tissues, with a risk of tension pneumothorax. This needs urgent medical assessment, so treatment stops and he stays upright to ease breathing. Reassurance and a later review delay escalation, continuing treatment adds pressure to a leaking pleural space, and lying flat worsens breathing while a nurse check alone does not bring the medical team."}, {"id": 1404, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Menon is alert and orientated. He can explain his surgery and the risk of pneumonia, but still declines to leave bed this morning, fearing the drain will dislodge. Which response best respects his decision-making rights while still supporting his recovery?", "options": {"A": "Request a formal capacity assessment before continuing, because refusing recommended treatment suggests impaired decision making", "B": "Accept his refusal, explain the drain safety, offer bed exercises, document it and revisit later", "C": "Accept his refusal for today, document it, and ask the nurse to mobilise him later", "D": "Explain the risks of staying in bed, and ask his wife to encourage him to move"}, "cluster_id": "CHS111", "cluster_text": "Mr Rahul Menon, 58, accountant, day one after a right video-assisted thoracoscopic lobectomy for lung cancer. Ex-smoker, 30 pack-years, uses salbutamol as needed. A single intercostal drain on the right connects to an underwater seal on low-pressure suction. Drainage overnight was serous. Observations: RR 20, HR 88, BP 124/78, SpO2 94% on 2 L/min. Auscultation: reduced air entry at the right base with a few transmitted secretions. Pain is 6 out of 10 with coughing and he splints poorly. He is alert and orientated and can describe his operation and the risks of staying in bed, but he declines to get out of bed this morning because he fears the drain will fall out. His wife is visiting.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Accept his refusal, explain the drain safety, offer bed exercises, document it and revisit later): Someone who understands the information and can weigh risks has capacity and may refuse treatment, even when the decision looks unwise. Addressing his fear of the drain, offering safe bed exercises, documenting and returning later keeps the therapeutic relationship and his care going. Refusal alone is not evidence of lost capacity, simply handing him on does not address his fear, and recruiting family to pressure him undermines his autonomy."}, {"id": 1405, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Julie's saturation is 97 percent on 4 L/min nasal prongs, she is drowsier, and her gas shows a raised carbon dioxide level with mild acidosis. No target range is charted. What should the physiotherapist do first, before starting any chest treatment?", "options": {"A": "Reduce oxygen to 2 L/min yourself, recheck saturation after ten minutes, then document the change", "B": "Start airway clearance now but monitor saturation closely, and report the carbon dioxide result afterwards", "C": "Fit a Venturi mask in place of the prongs and then begin airway clearance", "D": "Alert the nurse and doctor to the oxygen and drowsiness and request a charted target range"}, "cluster_id": "CHS112", "cluster_text": "Julie Gibson, 81, is on day two of an admission to an acute medical ward with an infective exacerbation of COPD. She lives alone and walks with a stick. History: long term COPD, a previous admission with hypercapnic respiratory failure, and hypertension. This morning she is drowsier than yesterday. She is on nasal prongs at 4 L/min. Observations: RR 22, HR 98, BP 142/80, SpO2 97 percent. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.31, PaCO2 62 mmHg, bicarbonate raised. Coarse crackles at the right base, a weak cough and sticky sputum she cannot clear. The medical chart does not state a target saturation range. The ward manager has asked the physiotherapy team to help audit oxygen practice this month.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Alert the nurse and doctor to the oxygen and drowsiness and request a charted target range): Raised carbon dioxide with drowsiness and a saturation above the usual 88 to 92 percent range for hypercapnic COPD suggests excess oxygen may be depressing her drive to breathe. The nurse and doctor need to know so oxygen can be titrated to a charted target. Changing the flow or the delivery device is a prescribing decision outside the physiotherapist's role, and treating before reporting leaves an unrecognised risk in place."}, {"id": 1406, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "The medical team sets a target saturation of 88 to 92 percent. Julie needs oxygen delivered at a predictable low concentration, and she breathes through her mouth at times. Which device is the most appropriate for delivering this controlled oxygen therapy safely?", "options": {"A": "Nasal prongs at 2 L/min, which are comfortable and deliver a low concentration she can tolerate", "B": "A non rebreather mask with a reservoir bag, keeping her saturation from falling below target", "C": "A simple face mask at 5 L/min, which gives a more consistent concentration than prongs", "D": "A Venturi mask set to a low fixed percentage, titrated to keep her within target"}, "cluster_id": "CHS112", "cluster_text": "Julie Gibson, 81, is on day two of an admission to an acute medical ward with an infective exacerbation of COPD. She lives alone and walks with a stick. History: long term COPD, a previous admission with hypercapnic respiratory failure, and hypertension. This morning she is drowsier than yesterday. She is on nasal prongs at 4 L/min. Observations: RR 22, HR 98, BP 142/80, SpO2 97 percent. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.31, PaCO2 62 mmHg, bicarbonate raised. Coarse crackles at the right base, a weak cough and sticky sputum she cannot clear. The medical chart does not state a target saturation range. The ward manager has asked the physiotherapy team to help audit oxygen practice this month.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A Venturi mask set to a low fixed percentage, titrated to keep her within target): A Venturi mask entrains air to deliver a fixed, predictable oxygen percentage whatever her breathing pattern, so it can be titrated to target. Prongs and simple masks give a variable concentration, especially with mouth breathing. A non rebreather delivers high concentrations that risk worsening carbon dioxide retention."}, {"id": 1407, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Julie's sputum is sticky and she cannot clear it, with a weak cough even when supported. Her oxygen is now by Venturi mask at a low percentage. Which approach is most appropriate to help her clear secretions safely and comfortably in this situation?", "options": {"A": "Nebulised normal saline before treatment, then manual percussion in high side-lying to dislodge secretions", "B": "Heated humidification with attention to hydration, then supported airway clearance such as huffing", "C": "Cold bubble humidifier attached to the oxygen supply, followed by supported huffing and coughing", "D": "Nasopharyngeal suction as the first treatment, then humidification, because her cough is weak"}, "cluster_id": "CHS112", "cluster_text": "Julie Gibson, 81, is on day two of an admission to an acute medical ward with an infective exacerbation of COPD. She lives alone and walks with a stick. History: long term COPD, a previous admission with hypercapnic respiratory failure, and hypertension. This morning she is drowsier than yesterday. She is on nasal prongs at 4 L/min. Observations: RR 22, HR 98, BP 142/80, SpO2 97 percent. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.31, PaCO2 62 mmHg, bicarbonate raised. Coarse crackles at the right base, a weak cough and sticky sputum she cannot clear. The medical chart does not state a target saturation range. The ward manager has asked the physiotherapy team to help audit oxygen practice this month.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Heated humidification with attention to hydration, then supported airway clearance such as huffing): Heated humidification with good hydration thins tenacious secretions, and a supported technique then makes clearance easier and more comfortable. A cold bubble bottle adds little moisture at low flows, so it does not treat thick secretions. Suction is uncomfortable and not a first step for a patient who can still huff with support, and percussion is poorly tolerated by breathless older adults with COPD."}, {"id": 1408, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "The ward wants to audit oxygen practice for patients with COPD and previous hypercapnic failure. The physiotherapy team must choose an outcome measure that reflects safe, appropriate oxygen delivery. Which measure is the most appropriate for the audit this month?", "options": {"A": "Percentage of oxygen orders that include a prescribed flow rate, reviewed weekly across the ward", "B": "Percentage of patients with COPD who receive physiotherapy review within one day of admission", "C": "Percentage of patients with a charted target whose saturations stay within that range", "D": "Number of rapid response calls for oxygen concerns each month, compared with last year"}, "cluster_id": "CHS112", "cluster_text": "Julie Gibson, 81, is on day two of an admission to an acute medical ward with an infective exacerbation of COPD. She lives alone and walks with a stick. History: long term COPD, a previous admission with hypercapnic respiratory failure, and hypertension. This morning she is drowsier than yesterday. She is on nasal prongs at 4 L/min. Observations: RR 22, HR 98, BP 142/80, SpO2 97 percent. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.31, PaCO2 62 mmHg, bicarbonate raised. Coarse crackles at the right base, a weak cough and sticky sputum she cannot clear. The medical chart does not state a target saturation range. The ward manager has asked the physiotherapy team to help audit oxygen practice this month.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Percentage of patients with a charted target whose saturations stay within that range): Measuring whether saturations stay within a charted target range links directly to the harm being prevented and shows whether practice follows the standard. A flow rate order records documentation, not whether the patient stayed in a safe range. Timely physiotherapy review measures access to the service, and emergency call numbers are rare, affected by many other factors and slow to show change."}, {"id": 1409, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Dunstan's shin has become tense and shiny over several hours, and he now reports tingling in the first web space. The bruising lies directly over the impact site. Which finding most strongly supports evolving compartment syndrome rather than a simple contusion in this limb?", "options": {"A": "Bruising that has spread distally towards the ankle, with local tenderness over the point of impact", "B": "Palpable tenderness and warmth over the shin, with pain on resisted ankle movement", "C": "Reduced active ankle dorsiflexion range, limited by swelling and guarding around the injured muscle belly", "D": "Worsening pain despite analgesia, provoked by passive stretch of the muscles in that compartment"}, "cluster_id": "CHS113", "cluster_text": "Mr Jack Dunstan, 24, is a farmhand admitted to a rural hospital six hours ago after a cow kicked his left shin. X-ray shows no fracture. Regular analgesia has been given. The medical officer has asked for physiotherapy to start early ankle mobilisation. Findings at 4 pm: the anterior shin is tense, shiny and swollen, with bruising over the impact site. Pain was 6 out of 10 at 1 pm and is now 9 out of 10 despite analgesia. Pain increases markedly with passive plantarflexion of the toes and ankle. He reports tingling in the first web space of the left foot. Dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses are palpable and the toes are warm. HR 98 bpm, BP 128/80 mmHg, afebrile. The ward nurse says he is probably just sore and needs to move. The nearest tertiary hospital is two hours away by road, and the medical officer is currently attending an emergency in the department.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Worsening pain despite analgesia, provoked by passive stretch of the muscles in that compartment): Pain that escalates despite analgesia and is provoked by passive stretch of the involved muscles reflects rising compartment pressure and ischaemia, which a contusion alone does not produce. Spreading bruising, restricted range and local tenderness are expected after direct impact and do not separate the two conditions."}, {"id": 1410, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "The physiotherapist suspects compartment syndrome. The ward plan includes active ankle mobilisation, ice and elevation of the limb, and the medical officer has not yet reviewed Mr Dunstan since his pain escalated severely this afternoon. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Apply ice and rest the limb flat, then ask the nurse to monitor his pain", "B": "Stop mobilising, keep the limb level with the heart, and urgently alert the doctor", "C": "Continue gentle ankle pumping to aid venous return and recheck neurovascular status in ten minutes", "D": "Apply compression and elevate the limb above the heart, then reassess his pain in an hour"}, "cluster_id": "CHS113", "cluster_text": "Mr Jack Dunstan, 24, is a farmhand admitted to a rural hospital six hours ago after a cow kicked his left shin. X-ray shows no fracture. Regular analgesia has been given. The medical officer has asked for physiotherapy to start early ankle mobilisation. Findings at 4 pm: the anterior shin is tense, shiny and swollen, with bruising over the impact site. Pain was 6 out of 10 at 1 pm and is now 9 out of 10 despite analgesia. Pain increases markedly with passive plantarflexion of the toes and ankle. He reports tingling in the first web space of the left foot. Dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses are palpable and the toes are warm. HR 98 bpm, BP 128/80 mmHg, afebrile. The ward nurse says he is probably just sore and needs to move. The nearest tertiary hospital is two hours away by road, and the medical officer is currently attending an emergency in the department.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stop mobilising, keep the limb level with the heart, and urgently alert the doctor): Compartment pressure is worsened by compression, by elevation above the heart that reduces arterial inflow, and by active muscle work, so treatment stops and the limb is kept level while a doctor assesses urgently. Ice and gentle pumping feel harmless but delay recognition of a limb-threatening problem."}, {"id": 1411, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "The medical officer phones back and says compartment syndrome is unlikely because Mr Dunstan's foot pulses are easily palpable and his toes are warm. The physiotherapist remains concerned about the tense shin and escalating pain. Why is a palpable pulse unreliable for excluding compartment syndrome?", "options": {"A": "Venous obstruction weakens distal pulses early, so a strong pulse favours a straightforward contusion with bleeding", "B": "Arterial pressure usually exceeds compartment pressure, so pulses persist while muscle perfusion fails", "C": "Pulses return once pain has peaked, so palpable pulses suggest the compartment pressure is settling", "D": "Pulse palpation is imprecise in swollen limbs, so Doppler assessment must precede any clinical decision here"}, "cluster_id": "CHS113", "cluster_text": "Mr Jack Dunstan, 24, is a farmhand admitted to a rural hospital six hours ago after a cow kicked his left shin. X-ray shows no fracture. Regular analgesia has been given. The medical officer has asked for physiotherapy to start early ankle mobilisation. Findings at 4 pm: the anterior shin is tense, shiny and swollen, with bruising over the impact site. Pain was 6 out of 10 at 1 pm and is now 9 out of 10 despite analgesia. Pain increases markedly with passive plantarflexion of the toes and ankle. He reports tingling in the first web space of the left foot. Dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses are palpable and the toes are warm. HR 98 bpm, BP 128/80 mmHg, afebrile. The ward nurse says he is probably just sore and needs to move. The nearest tertiary hospital is two hours away by road, and the medical officer is currently attending an emergency in the department.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Arterial pressure usually exceeds compartment pressure, so pulses persist while muscle perfusion fails): Compartment pressure usually rises enough to impair capillary flow, muscle and nerve perfusion while arterial pressure is still higher, so pulses and warm toes are late changes. Pain out of proportion, tension and altered sensation are the earlier warnings, and these are present, so urgent medical review is still needed."}, {"id": 1412, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "The physiotherapist needs to phone the medical officer between emergencies. Mr Dunstan's observations, pain trend and neurological changes must be conveyed quickly and unambiguously so the doctor can decide about transfer. Which approach to this telephone handover is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Phone and describe the tense swollen shin, then ask the doctor to review after the emergency", "B": "Ask the nurse in charge to relay the findings and request medical review soon", "C": "Email the orthopaedic registrar the findings and X-ray report, requesting advice about transfer by road", "D": "Use ISBAR, giving timed findings and the pain trend, and request an urgent review"}, "cluster_id": "CHS113", "cluster_text": "Mr Jack Dunstan, 24, is a farmhand admitted to a rural hospital six hours ago after a cow kicked his left shin. X-ray shows no fracture. Regular analgesia has been given. The medical officer has asked for physiotherapy to start early ankle mobilisation. Findings at 4 pm: the anterior shin is tense, shiny and swollen, with bruising over the impact site. Pain was 6 out of 10 at 1 pm and is now 9 out of 10 despite analgesia. Pain increases markedly with passive plantarflexion of the toes and ankle. He reports tingling in the first web space of the left foot. Dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses are palpable and the toes are warm. HR 98 bpm, BP 128/80 mmHg, afebrile. The ward nurse says he is probably just sore and needs to move. The nearest tertiary hospital is two hours away by road, and the medical officer is currently attending an emergency in the department.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Use ISBAR, giving timed findings and the pain trend, and request an urgent review): A structured handover such as ISBAR puts the situation, the timed trend and the request first, which lets a busy doctor grasp urgency in seconds. A phone call without the pain trend and neurological change does not convey risk, and a message passed through the nurse can lose detail. An email to a registrar may be read late, so it cannot support a decision that cannot wait."}, {"id": 1413, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Dimitri has breathlessness lying flat, morning headaches, paradoxical abdominal movement when supine and a falling FVC, yet his daytime SpO2 is 95 percent. Which interpretation of these findings is most appropriate for guiding his physiotherapy and referral at this stage?", "options": {"A": "Airway infection with early pneumonia, needing a sputum culture and antibiotic review by his general practitioner", "B": "Anxiety related hyperventilation, best managed with breathing control and relaxation strategies taught at home", "C": "Reduced cardiac output from heart failure, needing urgent cardiology referral before further respiratory input", "D": "Diaphragm weakness with nocturnal hypoventilation, needing prompt respiratory team review for ventilation assessment"}, "cluster_id": "CHS114", "cluster_text": "Dimitri Karagiannis, 64, a retired builder, has motor neurone disease diagnosed 14 months ago with limb onset and clear speech. He lives with his wife Eleni in a single storey home and is seen at home on referral from the multidisciplinary MND clinic. He reports breathlessness lying flat, waking with morning headaches, a weak cough and thick saliva. RR 20, SpO2 95 percent on room air while awake. Breathing is shallow with paradoxical abdominal movement when supine. Upper limb strength is 3 out of 5 and lower limb 4 minus. He cannot clear secretions by cough. His FVC has fallen from 70 to 55 percent of predicted over three months. The clinic is considering non invasive ventilation. Eleni says the family has little savings and notes Dimitri turns 65 in four months. He wishes to stay at home.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Diaphragm weakness with nocturnal hypoventilation, needing prompt respiratory team review for ventilation assessment): Orthopnoea, morning headaches and paradoxical abdominal movement point to diaphragm weakness with overnight carbon dioxide retention, which a normal daytime saturation does not exclude. A falling FVC supports this and makes respiratory team review for ventilation the priority over infection or anxiety explanations."}, {"id": 1414, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Dimitri cannot generate an effective cough and secretions are pooling in his upper airway, although his chest is currently clear on auscultation and his speech and swallow control are good. Which intervention should the physiotherapist prioritise to assist his airway clearance?", "options": {"A": "Teach manual assisted cough with lung volume recruitment, and discuss a mechanical cough assist device", "B": "Percussion and vibration in side lying with forced expiration technique to mobilise secretions from the periphery", "C": "Active cycle of breathing technique with vigorous huffing, repeated hourly to clear thick secretions", "D": "Incentive spirometry practised each hour to improve inspiratory depth and so strengthen his cough"}, "cluster_id": "CHS114", "cluster_text": "Dimitri Karagiannis, 64, a retired builder, has motor neurone disease diagnosed 14 months ago with limb onset and clear speech. He lives with his wife Eleni in a single storey home and is seen at home on referral from the multidisciplinary MND clinic. He reports breathlessness lying flat, waking with morning headaches, a weak cough and thick saliva. RR 20, SpO2 95 percent on room air while awake. Breathing is shallow with paradoxical abdominal movement when supine. Upper limb strength is 3 out of 5 and lower limb 4 minus. He cannot clear secretions by cough. His FVC has fallen from 70 to 55 percent of predicted over three months. The clinic is considering non invasive ventilation. Eleni says the family has little savings and notes Dimitri turns 65 in four months. He wishes to stay at home.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Teach manual assisted cough with lung volume recruitment, and discuss a mechanical cough assist device): His cough fails because expiratory and inspiratory muscles are weak, so it needs external assistance and lung volume recruitment to raise cough flow. Good bulbar function makes recruitment technique feasible. Percussion, active huffing and spirometry all rely on muscle strength he no longer has and do not address the cause."}, {"id": 1415, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "The MND clinic wants a measure the physiotherapist can repeat at home to detect progressive inspiratory muscle weakness between clinic visits, as Dimitri's FVC is already falling and his clinic visits are several weeks apart. Which measure is most useful for this purpose?", "options": {"A": "Peak expiratory flow, which reflects airway calibre and tends to fall early with inspiratory weakness", "B": "Sniff nasal inspiratory pressure, which reflects diaphragm strength and can be repeated at home", "C": "Respiratory rate counted over one minute, which rises before vital capacity declines noticeably", "D": "Resting oxygen saturation by pulse oximetry, which falls early when the diaphragm begins to weaken"}, "cluster_id": "CHS114", "cluster_text": "Dimitri Karagiannis, 64, a retired builder, has motor neurone disease diagnosed 14 months ago with limb onset and clear speech. He lives with his wife Eleni in a single storey home and is seen at home on referral from the multidisciplinary MND clinic. He reports breathlessness lying flat, waking with morning headaches, a weak cough and thick saliva. RR 20, SpO2 95 percent on room air while awake. Breathing is shallow with paradoxical abdominal movement when supine. Upper limb strength is 3 out of 5 and lower limb 4 minus. He cannot clear secretions by cough. His FVC has fallen from 70 to 55 percent of predicted over three months. The clinic is considering non invasive ventilation. Eleni says the family has little savings and notes Dimitri turns 65 in four months. He wishes to stay at home.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Sniff nasal inspiratory pressure, which reflects diaphragm strength and can be repeated at home): Sniff nasal inspiratory pressure is a portable test of inspiratory muscle strength used in motor neurone disease and is sensitive to diaphragm weakness. Oxygen saturation often stays normal while carbon dioxide rises, and peak flow and respiratory rate are not reliable early markers of this problem."}, {"id": 1416, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Eleni explains the family has limited savings and Dimitri turns 65 in four months, while the team wants a cough assist device and a hospital bed at home for him. What is the most appropriate funding advice from the physiotherapist to give her?", "options": {"A": "Wait until he turns 65 and apply through My Aged Care, which funds equipment more broadly", "B": "Apply to the NDIS after his birthday, using the aged care assessment as supporting evidence", "C": "Support an NDIS access request now, since it must be lodged before he turns 65", "D": "Rely on Medicare chronic disease management items to fund the cough assist device and the bed"}, "cluster_id": "CHS114", "cluster_text": "Dimitri Karagiannis, 64, a retired builder, has motor neurone disease diagnosed 14 months ago with limb onset and clear speech. He lives with his wife Eleni in a single storey home and is seen at home on referral from the multidisciplinary MND clinic. He reports breathlessness lying flat, waking with morning headaches, a weak cough and thick saliva. RR 20, SpO2 95 percent on room air while awake. Breathing is shallow with paradoxical abdominal movement when supine. Upper limb strength is 3 out of 5 and lower limb 4 minus. He cannot clear secretions by cough. His FVC has fallen from 70 to 55 percent of predicted over three months. The clinic is considering non invasive ventilation. Eleni says the family has little savings and notes Dimitri turns 65 in four months. He wishes to stay at home.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Support an NDIS access request now, since it must be lodged before he turns 65): NDIS access depends on applying before the 65th birthday, so a timely request with supporting reports from the MND clinic protects his options for equipment and home supports. Waiting moves him to the aged care system, and Medicare items cover limited consultations rather than expensive equipment."}, {"id": 1417, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Compared with yesterday, Mr Kowalski's observations have changed across several systems. Which feature of his current presentation is the strongest single indicator that he is acutely deteriorating and needs urgent medical review rather than continued routine physiotherapy treatment on the ward today?", "options": {"A": "A temperature of 38.6 degrees, suggesting infection that warrants a request for medical review", "B": "A distended tender abdomen, suggesting a surgical complication that the surgical team should review", "C": "Fine bibasal crackles, suggesting retained secretions or early fluid overload needing physiotherapy review", "D": "New confusion and drowsiness, suggesting reduced cerebral perfusion or oxygenation alongside a falling blood pressure"}, "cluster_id": "CHS115", "cluster_text": "Walter Kowalski, 78, retired bricklayer, day 3 after an open bowel resection for diverticular disease. History: hypertension, type 2 diabetes, ex-smoker. Referred for deep breathing and mobilisation. Yesterday he walked 40 metres with a frame. Today, seated at the bed edge: HR 118 bpm, RR 28, BP 92/58 mmHg, SpO2 89 percent on 2 L/min nasal prongs (95 percent yesterday), temperature 38.6 degrees. He is drowsy and newly confused, answering slowly. His abdomen is distended and tender. Fine crackles at both bases. The observation chart shows a red zone trigger from the morning round. He grips your arm and says, 'Please don't tell the nurses I feel this bad, they will keep me in longer.'", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (New confusion and drowsiness, suggesting reduced cerebral perfusion or oxygenation alongside a falling blood pressure): A change in mental state together with hypotension, tachycardia, tachypnoea and a falling saturation signals poor organ perfusion and is a core trigger for urgent medical review. Fever, abdominal distension and crackles are all worth reporting, but each can occur in a patient who is otherwise stable. New confusion alongside a falling blood pressure shows the whole body is failing to compensate, which makes it the strongest single sign."}, {"id": 1418, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Kowalski remains drowsy, tachypnoeic and hypotensive, and his chart is already in the red zone. Treatment has been stopped and he is sitting at the bed edge. His bedside nurse is attending another patient in the next bay. Following local escalation procedures, what should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Stay with him, support him upright, and activate the rapid response call at the bedside", "B": "Lie him flat with his legs raised, then briefly leave to find the nurse", "C": "Stay with him and ask a passing staff member to find the nurse, then keep monitoring", "D": "Return him to bed with oxygen running, and press the nurse call bell to request review"}, "cluster_id": "CHS115", "cluster_text": "Walter Kowalski, 78, retired bricklayer, day 3 after an open bowel resection for diverticular disease. History: hypertension, type 2 diabetes, ex-smoker. Referred for deep breathing and mobilisation. Yesterday he walked 40 metres with a frame. Today, seated at the bed edge: HR 118 bpm, RR 28, BP 92/58 mmHg, SpO2 89 percent on 2 L/min nasal prongs (95 percent yesterday), temperature 38.6 degrees. He is drowsy and newly confused, answering slowly. His abdomen is distended and tender. Fine crackles at both bases. The observation chart shows a red zone trigger from the morning round. He grips your arm and says, 'Please don't tell the nurses I feel this bad, they will keep me in longer.'", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stay with him, support him upright, and activate the rapid response call at the bedside): A patient meeting red zone criteria needs immediate escalation through the local rapid response system, and the physiotherapist must not leave an unstable patient alone. Staying upright eases his work of breathing while help is summoned. Lying him flat or leaving him alone is unsafe, and asking a bystander or ringing the call bell only brings a nurse who is already busy, rather than the team the red zone requires."}, {"id": 1419, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "The rapid response team is on the way. Mr Kowalski's SpO2 has fallen to 87 percent on his nasal prongs and he is using accessory muscles. A colleague has arrived to help you. What is the most appropriate physiotherapy action while waiting for the team?", "options": {"A": "Lie him flat to improve perfusion while checking his pulse, and prepare a structured handover", "B": "Keep him upright, check oxygen is connected and flowing, and prepare a structured handover", "C": "Increase the oxygen flow yourself until his saturation improves, as oxygen therapy is within scope", "D": "Encourage deep breathing with supported huffing in sitting to reopen the bases until the team arrives"}, "cluster_id": "CHS115", "cluster_text": "Walter Kowalski, 78, retired bricklayer, day 3 after an open bowel resection for diverticular disease. History: hypertension, type 2 diabetes, ex-smoker. Referred for deep breathing and mobilisation. Yesterday he walked 40 metres with a frame. Today, seated at the bed edge: HR 118 bpm, RR 28, BP 92/58 mmHg, SpO2 89 percent on 2 L/min nasal prongs (95 percent yesterday), temperature 38.6 degrees. He is drowsy and newly confused, answering slowly. His abdomen is distended and tender. Fine crackles at both bases. The observation chart shows a red zone trigger from the morning round. He grips your arm and says, 'Please don't tell the nurses I feel this bad, they will keep me in longer.'", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Keep him upright, check oxygen is connected and flowing, and prepare a structured handover): While waiting, the physiotherapist keeps the airway and breathing position supported, confirms the existing oxygen delivery is working, and organises clear information for the team. Lying flat would increase his work of breathing even though he is hypotensive. Changing oxygen flow is not the physiotherapist's decision in this setting, and active breathing techniques add effort for a patient who is already tiring."}, {"id": 1420, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Later, alert and settled on the high dependency unit, Mr Kowalski says you broke his trust by raising the alarm after he asked you to keep his symptoms secret. He looks upset. How should the physiotherapist best respond to his comment about trust?", "options": {"A": "Apologise and agree to withhold similar information from staff in future when he asks you clearly", "B": "Explain that sharing within the care team is permitted practice, then move on to his exercises", "C": "Acknowledge his worry, explain that sharing a safety concern was necessary, and stay supportive", "D": "Explain that nurses had already noticed his chart, so your report changed nothing for him"}, "cluster_id": "CHS115", "cluster_text": "Walter Kowalski, 78, retired bricklayer, day 3 after an open bowel resection for diverticular disease. History: hypertension, type 2 diabetes, ex-smoker. Referred for deep breathing and mobilisation. Yesterday he walked 40 metres with a frame. Today, seated at the bed edge: HR 118 bpm, RR 28, BP 92/58 mmHg, SpO2 89 percent on 2 L/min nasal prongs (95 percent yesterday), temperature 38.6 degrees. He is drowsy and newly confused, answering slowly. His abdomen is distended and tender. Fine crackles at both bases. The observation chart shows a red zone trigger from the morning round. He grips your arm and says, 'Please don't tell the nurses I feel this bad, they will keep me in longer.'", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Acknowledge his worry, explain that sharing a safety concern was necessary, and stay supportive): Duty of care to the patient's safety outweighs a request to conceal a serious deterioration, and the physiotherapist should explain this honestly while recognising his fear of a longer stay. Promising secrecy in future would breach that duty, and brushing aside his feelings or minimising the report damages the therapeutic relationship."}, {"id": 1421, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "During the six minute walk test Ms Tuilagi's SpO2 falls to 87% on room air and she rates breathlessness 5 on the Borg scale, recovering within two minutes of seated rest. How should the physiotherapist best use this result when planning her exercise programme?", "options": {"A": "Defer the programme until her walking oxygen saturation stays normal on repeated testing, then restart", "B": "Prescribe a monitored starting intensity from the walk test and inform her doctor of the desaturation", "C": "Commence supplemental oxygen during exercise from the service's portable supply to keep saturation up", "D": "Treat the desaturation as expected in obesity and begin group exercise at a moderate intensity"}, "cluster_id": "CHS116", "cluster_text": "Ms Losana Tuilagi, 46, drives a school bus. BMI is 46. She has obesity hypoventilation syndrome and has been prescribed nocturnal non-invasive ventilation (NIV), which she uses about three nights a week because of mask leak and a dry nose. She reports morning headaches, daytime sleepiness and breathlessness on stairs. Medical history: hypertension; no known lung disease. Observations at rest: SpO2 92% on room air, RR 18, HR 82, BP 148/90, mild bilateral ankle oedema. Six minute walk test: 290 metres, with SpO2 falling to 87% on room air and a Borg breathlessness rating of 5, recovering within two minutes of seated rest. She is keen to start an outpatient exercise programme. The service has an allied health assistant who can supervise group exercise sessions, and the physiotherapist has been asked to decide which tasks the assistant can take on.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Prescribe a monitored starting intensity from the walk test and inform her doctor of the desaturation): Exercise is part of treatment, so she should start at an intensity guided by the test with oximetry monitoring, and the desaturation should be reported to the doctor, who can decide on any oxygen need. Waiting for normal saturations would withhold a beneficial treatment, and dismissing the fall as normal removes necessary monitoring. Starting oxygen needs a medical order, not a physiotherapy decision."}, {"id": 1422, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Ms Tuilagi says she removes the mask at night because of leak and a dry nose, and she has headaches most mornings. She asks whether regular exercise will make the night ventilator unnecessary. What is the most appropriate response from the physiotherapist to her question?", "options": {"A": "Adjust the ventilator pressure to reduce leak, and record the change in her notes", "B": "Accept part-time use for now while she exercises, and review her symptoms after six weeks", "C": "Reinforce nightly use and refer to the sleep service for mask refit and humidification", "D": "Refer her back to her GP to review the ventilator prescription before exercise starts"}, "cluster_id": "CHS116", "cluster_text": "Ms Losana Tuilagi, 46, drives a school bus. BMI is 46. She has obesity hypoventilation syndrome and has been prescribed nocturnal non-invasive ventilation (NIV), which she uses about three nights a week because of mask leak and a dry nose. She reports morning headaches, daytime sleepiness and breathlessness on stairs. Medical history: hypertension; no known lung disease. Observations at rest: SpO2 92% on room air, RR 18, HR 82, BP 148/90, mild bilateral ankle oedema. Six minute walk test: 290 metres, with SpO2 falling to 87% on room air and a Borg breathlessness rating of 5, recovering within two minutes of seated rest. She is keen to start an outpatient exercise programme. The service has an allied health assistant who can supervise group exercise sessions, and the physiotherapist has been asked to decide which tasks the assistant can take on.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reinforce nightly use and refer to the sleep service for mask refit and humidification): Morning headaches and sleepiness suggest ventilation is not yet adequate, and exercise does not replace it. The leak and dry nose are common, fixable problems that the sleep service can address by refitting the mask and adjusting humidification. Changing pressure settings is outside the physiotherapist's role, part-time use leaves her under-ventilated, and sending her to her GP delays the service that manages the device."}, {"id": 1423, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "The service plans group exercise sessions, led by an allied health assistant with the physiotherapist available on site, starting next week. Her exercise plan has been written. Which task is most appropriate to delegate to the assistant for Ms Tuilagi's sessions?", "options": {"A": "Repeating the six minute walk test and deciding whether her result shows meaningful improvement", "B": "Helping her troubleshoot ventilator mask leak during sessions by changing the device settings", "C": "Supervising her walking and cycling within the intensity and limits written in the plan", "D": "Progressing her exercise intensity when she reports less breathlessness than at the previous session"}, "cluster_id": "CHS116", "cluster_text": "Ms Losana Tuilagi, 46, drives a school bus. BMI is 46. She has obesity hypoventilation syndrome and has been prescribed nocturnal non-invasive ventilation (NIV), which she uses about three nights a week because of mask leak and a dry nose. She reports morning headaches, daytime sleepiness and breathlessness on stairs. Medical history: hypertension; no known lung disease. Observations at rest: SpO2 92% on room air, RR 18, HR 82, BP 148/90, mild bilateral ankle oedema. Six minute walk test: 290 metres, with SpO2 falling to 87% on room air and a Borg breathlessness rating of 5, recovering within two minutes of seated rest. She is keen to start an outpatient exercise programme. The service has an allied health assistant who can supervise group exercise sessions, and the physiotherapist has been asked to decide which tasks the assistant can take on.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Supervising her walking and cycling within the intensity and limits written in the plan): An assistant can safely deliver a defined programme with clear limits and a stated point at which to stop and call the physiotherapist. Deciding whether results show improvement, changing device settings and progressing intensity all need clinical reasoning and remain the physiotherapist's responsibility. The physiotherapist stays accountable for the delegated work and supervises it."}, {"id": 1424, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "At week three the assistant reports that Ms Tuilagi's ankles are more swollen, she has gained weight, and she feels short of breath lying flat. Resting SpO2 is 90% and heart rate 96. What should the physiotherapist do first in response?", "options": {"A": "Add leg elevation and ankle pumps to the programme and review at her next session", "B": "Continue at a reduced intensity, since ankle swelling is common after walking in people with obesity", "C": "Ask the assistant to repeat her weight and oxygen readings after ten minutes of seated rest", "D": "Hold exercise today, assess her, and arrange prompt medical review for possible fluid overload"}, "cluster_id": "CHS116", "cluster_text": "Ms Losana Tuilagi, 46, drives a school bus. BMI is 46. She has obesity hypoventilation syndrome and has been prescribed nocturnal non-invasive ventilation (NIV), which she uses about three nights a week because of mask leak and a dry nose. She reports morning headaches, daytime sleepiness and breathlessness on stairs. Medical history: hypertension; no known lung disease. Observations at rest: SpO2 92% on room air, RR 18, HR 82, BP 148/90, mild bilateral ankle oedema. Six minute walk test: 290 metres, with SpO2 falling to 87% on room air and a Borg breathlessness rating of 5, recovering within two minutes of seated rest. She is keen to start an outpatient exercise programme. The service has an allied health assistant who can supervise group exercise sessions, and the physiotherapist has been asked to decide which tasks the assistant can take on.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hold exercise today, assess her, and arrange prompt medical review for possible fluid overload): Rising oedema, weight gain and breathlessness lying flat suggest fluid retention, possibly from right heart strain in obesity hypoventilation, and this needs prompt medical assessment before further exercise. Reduced intensity ignores a red flag, and adding exercises or repeating readings delays escalation without addressing the cause."}, {"id": 1425, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Jabbour, anxious and guarding his right side on the trolley, has fractures of ribs 4 to 9 on the right. Which examination finding most specifically indicates a flail segment rather than simple multiple rib fractures when you observe his chest wall during breathing?", "options": {"A": "Marked tenderness and crepitus over the fractured ribs that increases with palpation and deep breathing", "B": "Visibly reduced chest expansion on the injured side, with muscle splinting and shallow, rapid respiratory effort", "C": "Asymmetrical chest wall movement with the injured side lagging behind the uninjured side during inspiration", "D": "A chest wall section moving inward on inspiration and outward on expiration, opposite the rest"}, "cluster_id": "CHS117", "cluster_text": "Joseph Jabbour, 71, retired taxi driver, fell from a ladder this morning. Right rib fractures 4 to 9 with a flail segment: three adjacent ribs broken in two places, with paradoxical movement on the right. Chest X-ray: right lower lobe contusion, no pneumothorax. History: type 2 diabetes, ex-smoker, not anticoagulated. Observations in the emergency department: RR 26, HR 104, BP 138/82, SpO2 91 percent on 4 L/min via nasal prongs. Pain 8/10 on inspiration, shallow breathing, weak ineffective cough. He is receiving intravenous paracetamol and an opioid; the anaesthetic team is organising a regional block. The ED registrar is overstretched, the department is short of beds, and a physiotherapist is the only allied health clinician currently on shift.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A chest wall section moving inward on inspiration and outward on expiration, opposite the rest): A flail segment is defined by paradoxical movement, where the free section is pulled in during inspiration by negative pressure and pushed out during expiration. Tenderness, crepitus, reduced expansion and splinting occur with any painful rib fracture. A side that simply lags is moving in the same direction as the rest of the chest, so it shows reduced movement rather than a free segment."}, {"id": 1426, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Jabbour's pain is 8/10 on inspiration, his cough is weak, and he is breathing at 26 breaths per minute with SpO2 91 percent on 4 L/min. A regional anaesthetic block is being organised but has not yet been performed. What is the most appropriate physiotherapy action now?", "options": {"A": "Request that analgesia be optimised first, then teach supported deep breathing and cough in high sitting", "B": "Teach supported deep breathing and cough in high sitting straight away, as delaying treatment risks collapse", "C": "Apply a firm elastic chest binder to stabilise the segment and reduce pain during breathing", "D": "Commence mobilising out of bed with oxygen, since walking will improve ventilation and cough effort"}, "cluster_id": "CHS117", "cluster_text": "Joseph Jabbour, 71, retired taxi driver, fell from a ladder this morning. Right rib fractures 4 to 9 with a flail segment: three adjacent ribs broken in two places, with paradoxical movement on the right. Chest X-ray: right lower lobe contusion, no pneumothorax. History: type 2 diabetes, ex-smoker, not anticoagulated. Observations in the emergency department: RR 26, HR 104, BP 138/82, SpO2 91 percent on 4 L/min via nasal prongs. Pain 8/10 on inspiration, shallow breathing, weak ineffective cough. He is receiving intravenous paracetamol and an opioid; the anaesthetic team is organising a regional block. The ED registrar is overstretched, the department is short of beds, and a physiotherapist is the only allied health clinician currently on shift.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Request that analgesia be optimised first, then teach supported deep breathing and cough in high sitting): Effective pain control is the foundation of respiratory physiotherapy after rib trauma, so the physiotherapist liaises with the team first and then uses upright positioning with supported breathing and cough. Starting the right technique before pain is controlled produces shallow, ineffective efforts. Binders restrict expansion and encourage atelectasis, and getting up with pain at this level and a flail segment is unsafe."}, {"id": 1427, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "On day two the regional block is working and his pain is 3/10, yet he still breathes shallowly with reduced air entry at the right base and a chest X-ray showing basal atelectasis. SpO2 is 94 percent on 2 L/min. Which plan best improves right basal ventilation?", "options": {"A": "Rest him in right side-lying for extended periods so the mattress splints the injured chest wall", "B": "Use manual percussion over the right base in left side-lying, followed by forced expiration technique cycles", "C": "Sit upright with supported deep breathing and incentive spirometry, then progress to walking with oxygen", "D": "Sit upright with relaxed tidal breathing and oxygen, delaying incentive spirometry until the ribs have healed"}, "cluster_id": "CHS117", "cluster_text": "Joseph Jabbour, 71, retired taxi driver, fell from a ladder this morning. Right rib fractures 4 to 9 with a flail segment: three adjacent ribs broken in two places, with paradoxical movement on the right. Chest X-ray: right lower lobe contusion, no pneumothorax. History: type 2 diabetes, ex-smoker, not anticoagulated. Observations in the emergency department: RR 26, HR 104, BP 138/82, SpO2 91 percent on 4 L/min via nasal prongs. Pain 8/10 on inspiration, shallow breathing, weak ineffective cough. He is receiving intravenous paracetamol and an opioid; the anaesthetic team is organising a regional block. The ED registrar is overstretched, the department is short of beds, and a physiotherapist is the only allied health clinician currently on shift.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Sit upright with supported deep breathing and incentive spirometry, then progress to walking with oxygen): With pain controlled, upright positioning, supported deep breaths, incentive spirometry and early mobilisation expand the lung bases and reduce atelectasis. Lying on the injured side reduces ventilation of the affected lung. Quiet tidal breathing alone does not reopen collapsed lung, and percussion with forced expirations over healing fractures is poorly tolerated."}, {"id": 1428, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "The ED registrar, worried about bed shortages, asks the physiotherapist to decide whether Mr Jabbour needs intensive care admission and to arrange the transfer. His RR has risen to 32 and SpO2 is 90 percent on 6 L/min. How should the physiotherapist respond?", "options": {"A": "Make the admission decision using your respiratory findings, then document it and inform the registrar", "B": "Share the objective respiratory trend, explain admission is a medical decision, and request senior review", "C": "Call the intensive care unit and give a verbal referral so the transfer is not delayed", "D": "Decline involvement because respiratory deterioration lies outside physiotherapy practice, and continue with your scheduled treatments"}, "cluster_id": "CHS117", "cluster_text": "Joseph Jabbour, 71, retired taxi driver, fell from a ladder this morning. Right rib fractures 4 to 9 with a flail segment: three adjacent ribs broken in two places, with paradoxical movement on the right. Chest X-ray: right lower lobe contusion, no pneumothorax. History: type 2 diabetes, ex-smoker, not anticoagulated. Observations in the emergency department: RR 26, HR 104, BP 138/82, SpO2 91 percent on 4 L/min via nasal prongs. Pain 8/10 on inspiration, shallow breathing, weak ineffective cough. He is receiving intravenous paracetamol and an opioid; the anaesthetic team is organising a regional block. The ED registrar is overstretched, the department is short of beds, and a physiotherapist is the only allied health clinician currently on shift.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Share the objective respiratory trend, explain admission is a medical decision, and request senior review): Admission to intensive care is a medical decision, but the physiotherapist has a duty to contribute clear respiratory assessment data and to push for senior review when a patient is worsening. Making the decision alone exceeds scope, and declining to engage while continuing routine treatment ignores the deterioration. Direct referral bypasses the responsible medical team."}, {"id": 1429, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Long reports urinary hesitancy since yesterday and numbness when wiping, with bilateral posterior thigh tingling and mild left dorsiflexion weakness. Pain is worsening despite rest. Observations are stable and he is lying on a trolley in the corridor. What is the physiotherapist's most appropriate immediate action?", "options": {"A": "Complete the neurological screen with myotomes and reflexes, then report findings to the doctor", "B": "Stop assessing and alert the emergency doctor now, as suspected cauda equina needs urgent medical review", "C": "Begin gentle neural mobilisation for the left sciatic features and recheck the bladder symptoms after treatment", "D": "Teach pain relieving positions and walking, with outpatient review if bladder symptoms persist"}, "cluster_id": "CHS118", "cluster_text": "Long Ly, 46, delivery driver, presents to the emergency department with three days of low back pain after lifting a pallet. Triage: pain 8/10, temperature normal, HR 82, BP 134/84, RR 16, SpO2 98 percent on air. Since yesterday he has had two episodes of urinary hesitancy and says it feels numb when he wipes. He also reports tingling in both posterior thighs. Examination: left ankle dorsiflexion 4/5, straight leg raise positive on the left at 40 degrees, gait slow and antalgic. He takes no regular medication and has no history of cancer. The physiotherapist has been asked to see him by the emergency team, and four other referrals are waiting on the ward and in the department.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stop assessing and alert the emergency doctor now, as suspected cauda equina needs urgent medical review): Bladder disturbance with saddle numbness and bilateral leg symptoms suggests cauda equina syndrome, a surgical emergency where delay risks permanent loss of function. The physiotherapist's role is to escalate immediately and avoid spending time on further testing or treatment. Finishing a full screen first would delay the decision for a patient whose observations are not the concern."}, {"id": 1430, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "The doctor asks the physiotherapist to help document neurological findings because cauda equina is suspected. Long is lying on his side with pain and can tolerate only brief, gentle testing. Which examination finding would add the most weight to this suspicion?", "options": {"A": "Reproduction of left leg pain on slump testing, indicating dural tension from the disc", "B": "A reduced left Achilles reflex with weakness on repeated single leg heel raises", "C": "Marked paraspinal muscle spasm with loss of lumbar lordosis and reduced forward flexion range", "D": "Loss of light touch and pinprick sensation across the saddle region bilaterally"}, "cluster_id": "CHS118", "cluster_text": "Long Ly, 46, delivery driver, presents to the emergency department with three days of low back pain after lifting a pallet. Triage: pain 8/10, temperature normal, HR 82, BP 134/84, RR 16, SpO2 98 percent on air. Since yesterday he has had two episodes of urinary hesitancy and says it feels numb when he wipes. He also reports tingling in both posterior thighs. Examination: left ankle dorsiflexion 4/5, straight leg raise positive on the left at 40 degrees, gait slow and antalgic. He takes no regular medication and has no history of cancer. The physiotherapist has been asked to see him by the emergency team, and four other referrals are waiting on the ward and in the department.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Loss of light touch and pinprick sensation across the saddle region bilaterally): Reduced saddle sensation is a cardinal sign of compression of the sacral nerve roots and fits the bladder symptoms. Slump findings, a reduced ankle reflex and muscle spasm are common with disc related back pain and do not distinguish a cauda equina presentation from a simple radiculopathy."}, {"id": 1431, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "With Long now under medical care, the physiotherapist has four other referrals: post operative hip mobilisation, a stable ankle sprain needing crutches, a discharge mobility check, and a patient with rib fractures whose breathing has become shallow with SpO2 falling to 91 percent on air. Which referral should be seen first?", "options": {"A": "The rib fracture patient, to assess breathing and address the falling oxygen saturation", "B": "The discharge mobility check, because the bed is needed for a patient waiting in the department", "C": "The post operative hip patient, because delayed early mobilisation raises the risk of complications later today", "D": "The ankle sprain patient, because untreated swelling and weight bearing risk further ligament injury today"}, "cluster_id": "CHS118", "cluster_text": "Long Ly, 46, delivery driver, presents to the emergency department with three days of low back pain after lifting a pallet. Triage: pain 8/10, temperature normal, HR 82, BP 134/84, RR 16, SpO2 98 percent on air. Since yesterday he has had two episodes of urinary hesitancy and says it feels numb when he wipes. He also reports tingling in both posterior thighs. Examination: left ankle dorsiflexion 4/5, straight leg raise positive on the left at 40 degrees, gait slow and antalgic. He takes no regular medication and has no history of cancer. The physiotherapist has been asked to see him by the emergency team, and four other referrals are waiting on the ward and in the department.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The rib fracture patient, to assess breathing and address the falling oxygen saturation): Prioritising by clinical risk puts the patient with deteriorating respiratory status first, since shallow breathing with falling saturation risks pneumonia and respiratory failure. The other referrals matter for flow and recovery, but none carries the same immediate risk of harm. A bed need, delayed mobilisation or a stable sprain can wait a short time without serious consequences."}, {"id": 1432, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "After urgent surgical decompression, Long is day one post operative with an indwelling catheter, reduced saddle sensation and left foot dorsiflexion weakness. His wound is dry, observations are stable and pain is 4 out of 10. Which early mobilisation approach is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Mobilise early with supervision, support the weak foot, and check skin over numb areas", "B": "Keep Long on bed rest until the catheter is removed, with ankle pump exercises for circulation", "C": "Mobilise early with supervision and add lumbar flexion stretches after each walk to limit wound stiffness", "D": "Begin with supervised sitting and standing only for two days, before any walking is attempted"}, "cluster_id": "CHS118", "cluster_text": "Long Ly, 46, delivery driver, presents to the emergency department with three days of low back pain after lifting a pallet. Triage: pain 8/10, temperature normal, HR 82, BP 134/84, RR 16, SpO2 98 percent on air. Since yesterday he has had two episodes of urinary hesitancy and says it feels numb when he wipes. He also reports tingling in both posterior thighs. Examination: left ankle dorsiflexion 4/5, straight leg raise positive on the left at 40 degrees, gait slow and antalgic. He takes no regular medication and has no history of cancer. The physiotherapist has been asked to see him by the emergency team, and four other referrals are waiting on the ward and in the department.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Mobilise early with supervision, support the weak foot, and check skin over numb areas): Early supervised mobilisation lowers the risk of complications of bed rest, while support for the weak foot and checking skin over numb areas protects against falls and pressure injury. Bed rest until the catheter is removed delays recovery. Spinal flexion stretches too soon after decompression do not suit a healing wound, and holding him to sitting and standing for days is overly cautious for a stable patient."}, {"id": 1433, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Nyah's usual airway clearance is a PEP mask followed by huffing, but her device is cracked. The clinic nurse asks why PEP is used in a child with bronchiectasis. Which statement best describes the main effect of positive expiratory pressure on secretion clearance?", "options": {"A": "It delivers humidity that thins the sputum so she can clear it with a gentle cough", "B": "It holds airways open on expiration, so air passes behind secretions and moves them centrally", "C": "It increases inspiratory flow, which stretches the airway wall and loosens secretions before huffing begins", "D": "It draws secretions upward by applying negative pressure to the airway as she breathes out"}, "cluster_id": "CHS119", "cluster_text": "Nyah Roberts, 7, lives in a remote community and attends the community clinic during the physiotherapist's fortnightly outreach visit. She has cystic fibrosis with bronchiectasis in both lower lobes and chronic Pseudomonas infection. Her grandmother is her main carer; she speaks Pitjantjatjara as her first language and English as a second language. Nyah's usual airway clearance is a positive expiratory pressure (PEP) mask followed by huffing, twice a day, but the mask is cracked and the nearest pharmacy is hours away. Today she has a wet cough with three days of increased green sputum. She is afebrile, SpO2 is 94 percent, RR 26, with crackles at both bases and unchanged weight. A nurse practitioner works at the clinic daily and the Royal Flying Doctor Service visits weekly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It holds airways open on expiration, so air passes behind secretions and moves them centrally): PEP creates back pressure on expiration that keeps small airways open, so air can move behind secretions through collateral channels and bring them to larger airways where huffing clears them. It does not rely on greater inspiratory flow, negative suction or humidification, which belong to other therapies."}, {"id": 1434, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "The physiotherapist needs to teach Nyah's grandmother how to supervise airway clearance. She nods throughout but asks no questions, and English is her second language. A health worker who is a distant relative offers to translate. What is the most appropriate approach?", "options": {"A": "Book an accredited interpreter, by phone if needed, and confirm understanding with teach-back", "B": "Speak slowly in plain English with gestures and a picture handout, taking her nodding as understanding", "C": "Ask Nyah to relay the instructions in English, as children often hold the household's language skills", "D": "Use the relative to interpret, as family trust improves engagement, and check understanding at the end"}, "cluster_id": "CHS119", "cluster_text": "Nyah Roberts, 7, lives in a remote community and attends the community clinic during the physiotherapist's fortnightly outreach visit. She has cystic fibrosis with bronchiectasis in both lower lobes and chronic Pseudomonas infection. Her grandmother is her main carer; she speaks Pitjantjatjara as her first language and English as a second language. Nyah's usual airway clearance is a positive expiratory pressure (PEP) mask followed by huffing, twice a day, but the mask is cracked and the nearest pharmacy is hours away. Today she has a wet cough with three days of increased green sputum. She is afebrile, SpO2 is 94 percent, RR 26, with crackles at both bases and unchanged weight. A nurse practitioner works at the clinic daily and the Royal Flying Doctor Service visits weekly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Book an accredited interpreter, by phone if needed, and confirm understanding with teach-back): An accredited interpreter keeps messages accurate, neutral and confidential, whereas a relative may filter information or feel uncomfortable discussing sensitive topics. Teach-back with demonstration shows what the grandmother actually understood, because nodding is not evidence of comprehension. Using a child to interpret is inappropriate."}, {"id": 1435, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Nyah is afebrile and her saturations are stable, but her sputum has been green for three days. Her replacement PEP device will take two weeks to arrive and the nearest pharmacy is hours away. Which interim airway clearance plan is most appropriate while she waits?", "options": {"A": "Pause airway clearance until the new device arrives because techniques without equipment are ineffective in bronchiectasis", "B": "Use active cycle of breathing with huffing in supported sitting, framed as a play-based routine", "C": "Encourage prolonged forceful coughing sessions twice daily, since huffing is unsuitable for children of her age", "D": "Use manual percussion over both bases in a head-down position for ten minutes, twice each day"}, "cluster_id": "CHS119", "cluster_text": "Nyah Roberts, 7, lives in a remote community and attends the community clinic during the physiotherapist's fortnightly outreach visit. She has cystic fibrosis with bronchiectasis in both lower lobes and chronic Pseudomonas infection. Her grandmother is her main carer; she speaks Pitjantjatjara as her first language and English as a second language. Nyah's usual airway clearance is a positive expiratory pressure (PEP) mask followed by huffing, twice a day, but the mask is cracked and the nearest pharmacy is hours away. Today she has a wet cough with three days of increased green sputum. She is afebrile, SpO2 is 94 percent, RR 26, with crackles at both bases and unchanged weight. A nurse practitioner works at the clinic daily and the Royal Flying Doctor Service visits weekly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use active cycle of breathing with huffing in supported sitting, framed as a play-based routine): Active cycle of breathing with huffing needs no equipment, can be taught as play and keeps airway clearance going while the device is replaced. Stopping clearance risks sputum retention, head-down percussion is outdated and uncomfortable, and prolonged forceful coughing tires a child and can collapse airways."}, {"id": 1436, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "At the next fortnightly visit, Nyah has a fever of 38.4 degrees, a respiratory rate of 34, SpO2 of 90 percent on room air and reduced appetite. Her grandmother says she has been coughing more for a week. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Intensify airway clearance with longer sessions twice daily and recheck her saturations in a few days", "B": "Test her tolerance with supervised exercise in the yard and send her home if saturations hold", "C": "Leave a home programme and review at the next outreach visit, as physiotherapy can settle exacerbations", "D": "Alert the nurse practitioner for urgent medical review, keeping airway clearance gentle until assessed"}, "cluster_id": "CHS119", "cluster_text": "Nyah Roberts, 7, lives in a remote community and attends the community clinic during the physiotherapist's fortnightly outreach visit. She has cystic fibrosis with bronchiectasis in both lower lobes and chronic Pseudomonas infection. Her grandmother is her main carer; she speaks Pitjantjatjara as her first language and English as a second language. Nyah's usual airway clearance is a positive expiratory pressure (PEP) mask followed by huffing, twice a day, but the mask is cracked and the nearest pharmacy is hours away. Today she has a wet cough with three days of increased green sputum. She is afebrile, SpO2 is 94 percent, RR 26, with crackles at both bases and unchanged weight. A nurse practitioner works at the clinic daily and the Royal Flying Doctor Service visits weekly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Alert the nurse practitioner for urgent medical review, keeping airway clearance gentle until assessed): Fever, a high respiratory rate, falling saturations and a week of worsening cough suggest a pulmonary exacerbation needing medical assessment and probably antibiotics. The nurse practitioner is on site and must be told first. Longer physiotherapy sessions or exercise testing delay that review and could exhaust an unwell child."}, {"id": 1437, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "The medical orders limit systolic blood pressure during activity for Mr Vo, who is recovering from pneumonia on the ward. What is the main physiological reason such a limit applies to a patient with an infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm during walking?", "options": {"A": "Rising pressure raises intracranial pressure through the carotid arteries, increasing his stroke risk during exertion", "B": "Rising pressure increases wall tension on the weakened aortic segment, raising the risk of expansion", "C": "Rising pressure increases left ventricular work, which would worsen gas exchange from his pneumonia", "D": "Rising pressure reduces perfusion of the lower limbs, so claudication would stop him exercising early"}, "cluster_id": "CHS120", "cluster_text": "Tam Vo, 74, retired taxi driver, day 4 of admission to the vascular ward with community-acquired pneumonia. Known 5.4 cm infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm under surveillance by the vascular team. History of hypertension, COPD and 40 pack-years of smoking. Medical orders: mobilise daily, keep systolic BP under 160 mmHg during activity, report any new abdominal or back pain. Today: afebrile, resting HR 84, BP 148/86, RR 20, SpO2 94 percent on room air, productive cough, coarse crackles at the right base. Yesterday he walked 40 metres with a frame and stopped with breathlessness (Borg 4). He tells you his blood pressure tablet makes him tired and he wants to stop it. He also asks whether walking could make his aneurysm burst.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Rising pressure increases wall tension on the weakened aortic segment, raising the risk of expansion): Wall tension in a dilated vessel increases with pressure, so keeping exertional pressure within the set limit protects an aneurysmal segment that is already weak. Limb perfusion and cardiac workload may change with exertion but they are not the reason the ceiling is ordered here. Stroke risk is not the target of this particular limit."}, {"id": 1438, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Vo asks whether he can stop his blood pressure tablet and whether walking could burst his aneurysm. You are not authorised to alter medications and the vascular team knows his imaging. He seems anxious and wants an answer today. How should the physiotherapist respond?", "options": {"A": "Reassure him the aneurysm is stable and suggest raising the tablets at his next GP visit", "B": "Explain walking is safe within his ordered limits, and encourage him to persist with the tablet", "C": "Advise him to avoid walking until the vascular team has reviewed his imaging and the tablet", "D": "Explain the activity limits that protect him, and refer the medication question to the medical team"}, "cluster_id": "CHS120", "cluster_text": "Tam Vo, 74, retired taxi driver, day 4 of admission to the vascular ward with community-acquired pneumonia. Known 5.4 cm infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm under surveillance by the vascular team. History of hypertension, COPD and 40 pack-years of smoking. Medical orders: mobilise daily, keep systolic BP under 160 mmHg during activity, report any new abdominal or back pain. Today: afebrile, resting HR 84, BP 148/86, RR 20, SpO2 94 percent on room air, productive cough, coarse crackles at the right base. Yesterday he walked 40 metres with a frame and stopped with breathlessness (Borg 4). He tells you his blood pressure tablet makes him tired and he wants to stop it. He also asks whether walking could make his aneurysm burst.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain the activity limits that protect him, and refer the medication question to the medical team): Entry-level scope includes explaining safe exercise limits, but advising on medication belongs to the prescriber, so his side effect concern goes to the medical team. Telling him to persist with the tablet gives medication advice and ignores his complaint. Reassuring him about stability goes beyond what you know, and withholding walking contradicts the medical orders to mobilise within a blood pressure limit."}, {"id": 1439, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Vo is cleared to walk with the frame. His resting BP is 148/86 and the documented ceiling during activity is 160 systolic. He has walked 40 metres before with a frame and has a productive cough. Which monitoring plan is most appropriate for this walking session?", "options": {"A": "Record BP at rest, at a seated pause midway, and after the walk", "B": "Rely on heart rate and Borg rating alone, since pulse tracks blood pressure closely during walking", "C": "Check BP only before starting and after finishing, as intermediate readings disturb the walking pattern", "D": "Use oxygen saturation alone to guide intensity, since pneumonia rather than the aneurysm limits his walking"}, "cluster_id": "CHS120", "cluster_text": "Tam Vo, 74, retired taxi driver, day 4 of admission to the vascular ward with community-acquired pneumonia. Known 5.4 cm infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm under surveillance by the vascular team. History of hypertension, COPD and 40 pack-years of smoking. Medical orders: mobilise daily, keep systolic BP under 160 mmHg during activity, report any new abdominal or back pain. Today: afebrile, resting HR 84, BP 148/86, RR 20, SpO2 94 percent on room air, productive cough, coarse crackles at the right base. Yesterday he walked 40 metres with a frame and stopped with breathlessness (Borg 4). He tells you his blood pressure tablet makes him tired and he wants to stop it. He also asks whether walking could make his aneurysm burst.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Record BP at rest, at a seated pause midway, and after the walk): A set pressure ceiling can only be respected by measuring pressure during the effort, so a planned seated pause with a reading is needed as well as before and after readings. Heart rate and Borg do not reliably reflect arterial pressure, and saturation does not show whether the ceiling is exceeded."}, {"id": 1440, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Later that day Mr Vo is sitting on the edge of his bed and reports sudden severe pain across his back and lower abdomen. He looks pale and clammy, with HR 112 and BP 100/60 mmHg. What is the most appropriate immediate action?", "options": {"A": "Lay him down, stay with him, and urgently call the medical emergency team", "B": "Help him into bed, check his observations again, and report to the nurse in charge afterwards", "C": "Stay with him, ask the nurse to give analgesia, and delay physiotherapy until pain settles", "D": "Sit him upright in the chair, apply oxygen, and ask a colleague to notify the registrar"}, "cluster_id": "CHS120", "cluster_text": "Tam Vo, 74, retired taxi driver, day 4 of admission to the vascular ward with community-acquired pneumonia. Known 5.4 cm infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm under surveillance by the vascular team. History of hypertension, COPD and 40 pack-years of smoking. Medical orders: mobilise daily, keep systolic BP under 160 mmHg during activity, report any new abdominal or back pain. Today: afebrile, resting HR 84, BP 148/86, RR 20, SpO2 94 percent on room air, productive cough, coarse crackles at the right base. Yesterday he walked 40 metres with a frame and stopped with breathlessness (Borg 4). He tells you his blood pressure tablet makes him tired and he wants to stop it. He also asks whether walking could make his aneurysm burst.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Lay him down, stay with him, and urgently call the medical emergency team): Sudden severe back or abdominal pain with signs of shock in a patient with a known aneurysm suggests rupture, which is a surgical emergency. The priority is a rapid emergency call while you stay with him lying down. Reporting after repeating observations loses time, analgesia first treats the symptom and not the cause, and sitting him up with a message to one doctor delays the full emergency response."}, {"id": 1441, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Ngoc's mother asks why hypertonic saline is nebulised before the airway clearance session rather than afterwards, since it seems to waste time. What is the most accurate explanation of its role in cystic fibrosis airway disease that the physiotherapist can give her?", "options": {"A": "It relaxes airway smooth muscle, so the clearance technique then needs less effort", "B": "It hydrates airway mucus so secretions are easier to move during the session", "C": "It kills the Pseudomonas in the lungs, so clearing afterwards removes the dead bacteria and debris", "D": "It reduces airway inflammation, so the child coughs less and needs fewer clearance techniques"}, "cluster_id": "CHS121", "cluster_text": "Ngoc Truong, 9, has cystic fibrosis (homozygous F508del), pancreatic insufficiency and chronic Pseudomonas aeruginosa colonisation. She attends outpatient physiotherapy with her mother, Linh, for a routine review and is clinically well. FEV1 is 78 percent predicted, SpO2 97 percent on room air, RR 20, and her chest has mild scattered crackles in the right upper zone. Her home regimen is salbutamol by spacer, nebulised hypertonic saline, then the active cycle of breathing technique with huffing, using a positive expiratory pressure (PEP) device she prefers, twice daily for about 20 minutes. Ngoc says she skips the evening session because it is tedious while her brother plays video games. She plays netball twice a week. The team leader has asked Peter, an allied health assistant with basic respiratory training, to run Ngoc's weekly supervised airway clearance and exercise session.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It hydrates airway mucus so secretions are easier to move during the session): Hypertonic saline draws water onto the airway surface and hydrates thick secretions, so it is most useful when given just before clearance. Relaxing airway smooth muscle is the job of the salbutamol taken earlier. It is not an antibiotic and is not given mainly to reduce inflammation."}, {"id": 1442, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Ngoc skips her evening airway clearance because the session is tedious, but she enjoys netball twice a week and prefers her PEP device. She is clinically stable today. What is the most appropriate way to adjust her airway clearance plan with her?", "options": {"A": "Drop to one session daily, since she is stable and adherence matters more than frequency", "B": "Keep the full twice daily ACBT plan and ask her mother to supervise every session closely", "C": "Co-design the plan with Ngoc, keeping her preferred PEP device and counting exercise as an adjunct", "D": "Replace the evening session with netball on training days, as exercise gives equivalent clearance for children"}, "cluster_id": "CHS121", "cluster_text": "Ngoc Truong, 9, has cystic fibrosis (homozygous F508del), pancreatic insufficiency and chronic Pseudomonas aeruginosa colonisation. She attends outpatient physiotherapy with her mother, Linh, for a routine review and is clinically well. FEV1 is 78 percent predicted, SpO2 97 percent on room air, RR 20, and her chest has mild scattered crackles in the right upper zone. Her home regimen is salbutamol by spacer, nebulised hypertonic saline, then the active cycle of breathing technique with huffing, using a positive expiratory pressure (PEP) device she prefers, twice daily for about 20 minutes. Ngoc says she skips the evening session because it is tedious while her brother plays video games. She plays netball twice a week. The team leader has asked Peter, an allied health assistant with basic respiratory training, to run Ngoc's weekly supervised airway clearance and exercise session.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Co-design the plan with Ngoc, keeping her preferred PEP device and counting exercise as an adjunct): Involving a child in designing her plan, and using a device she prefers, improves adherence while keeping airway clearance in place. Exercise supports clearance but does not replace it. Cutting the frequency or tightening supervision does not address why she skips the session."}, {"id": 1443, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Two weeks later Ngoc has a new wet cough, increased green sputum, reduced netball tolerance, SpO2 94 percent and a spirometry fall of about 10 percent from her usual baseline. Her appetite has also dropped. What should the physiotherapist do next?", "options": {"A": "Increase high intensity exercise to stimulate coughing, and review sputum colour at the next session", "B": "Intensify airway clearance at home this week, and review with the CF team if no improvement", "C": "Send a sputum sample to the laboratory and wait for results before changing her regimen", "D": "Contact her CF team promptly about a possible exacerbation and intensify airway clearance"}, "cluster_id": "CHS121", "cluster_text": "Ngoc Truong, 9, has cystic fibrosis (homozygous F508del), pancreatic insufficiency and chronic Pseudomonas aeruginosa colonisation. She attends outpatient physiotherapy with her mother, Linh, for a routine review and is clinically well. FEV1 is 78 percent predicted, SpO2 97 percent on room air, RR 20, and her chest has mild scattered crackles in the right upper zone. Her home regimen is salbutamol by spacer, nebulised hypertonic saline, then the active cycle of breathing technique with huffing, using a positive expiratory pressure (PEP) device she prefers, twice daily for about 20 minutes. Ngoc says she skips the evening session because it is tedious while her brother plays video games. She plays netball twice a week. The team leader has asked Peter, an allied health assistant with basic respiratory training, to run Ngoc's weekly supervised airway clearance and exercise session.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Contact her CF team promptly about a possible exacerbation and intensify airway clearance): More sputum, a fall in lung function, lower exercise tolerance and reduced appetite suggest a pulmonary exacerbation, which needs timely review by the CF team as well as more intensive clearance. Waiting a week or for laboratory results delays treatment and risks lasting loss of lung function. Exercise alone is not an adequate response, and a physiotherapist does not order sputum tests independently of the team."}, {"id": 1444, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "The team leader asks Peter, an allied health assistant with basic respiratory training, to run Ngoc's weekly supervised airway clearance and exercise session. Ngoc is currently stable. Which approach to delegation is most appropriate for the physiotherapist who assessed Ngoc?", "options": {"A": "Delegate exercise and let Peter modify her airway clearance technique if Ngoc finds it hard", "B": "Decline to delegate, since only a physiotherapist may supervise children with chronic respiratory disease", "C": "Check Peter's competence, delegate defined tasks with escalation triggers, and keep responsibility", "D": "Delegate the entire session because the team leader approved it, and ask Peter for a summary"}, "cluster_id": "CHS121", "cluster_text": "Ngoc Truong, 9, has cystic fibrosis (homozygous F508del), pancreatic insufficiency and chronic Pseudomonas aeruginosa colonisation. She attends outpatient physiotherapy with her mother, Linh, for a routine review and is clinically well. FEV1 is 78 percent predicted, SpO2 97 percent on room air, RR 20, and her chest has mild scattered crackles in the right upper zone. Her home regimen is salbutamol by spacer, nebulised hypertonic saline, then the active cycle of breathing technique with huffing, using a positive expiratory pressure (PEP) device she prefers, twice daily for about 20 minutes. Ngoc says she skips the evening session because it is tedious while her brother plays video games. She plays netball twice a week. The team leader has asked Peter, an allied health assistant with basic respiratory training, to run Ngoc's weekly supervised airway clearance and exercise session.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Check Peter's competence, delegate defined tasks with escalation triggers, and keep responsibility): The delegating physiotherapist remains accountable, so they confirm the assistant's competence, define the tasks, set clear triggers for calling back and review the outcomes. A manager's approval does not transfer that duty. Allowing the assistant to change the technique goes beyond a delegated task, while refusing all delegation is not required."}, {"id": 1445, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Mulladad is ready to mobilise for the first time on day 2 after his repair. His surgeon has ordered non weight bearing through the right leg for two weeks, in a boot with heel wedges. Which approach to this first mobilisation session is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Supervised frame mobilisation on the left leg only, with the wedged boot staying on", "B": "Frame mobilisation non weight bearing after removing the wedges so the ankle rests in neutral", "C": "Frame mobilisation with partial weight through the right heel as pain allows, boot kept on", "D": "Gentle assisted active dorsiflexion in bed first, then frame mobilisation without the boot on"}, "cluster_id": "CHS122", "cluster_text": "Dennis Mulladad is a 68 year old Aboriginal man from a rural town, now on day 2 after surgical repair of a right Achilles tendon rupture sustained while playing with his grandchildren. He has type 2 diabetes and hypertension. His wife and two adult children sit with him, and the hospital's Aboriginal Liaison Officer visited this morning. He speaks quietly and his wife answers some of your questions for him. The surgeon's protocol: removable boot with heel wedges and non weight bearing for two weeks. Findings: pain 3/10 at rest, toes warm and pink, capillary refill 2 seconds, sensation intact, HR 78, BP 138/84. He needs to practise transfers and mobilising with a walking frame before discharge planning begins.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Supervised frame mobilisation on the left leg only, with the wedged boot staying on): The surgeon's protocol protects the repair by keeping the tendon slack in plantarflexion and off load, so the wedges and boot stay on and no weight goes through the leg. Letting the ankle move toward neutral or dorsiflexion stretches the new repair, and partial loading breaches the order."}, {"id": 1446, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "On day 3 Mr Mulladad reports pins and needles in his right toes and says the boot feels tight. His toes look pale and cool, capillary refill is now 4 seconds, and swelling has increased since yesterday. What is the most appropriate immediate action?", "options": {"A": "Encourage active toe movements to improve circulation and review the toes again tomorrow", "B": "Loosen the boot straps, keep the leg level and urgently notify the surgical team", "C": "Elevate the leg and recheck toe colour and sensation after an hour before deciding on escalation", "D": "Remove the boot, apply a cold pack to reduce the swelling and monitor over the afternoon"}, "cluster_id": "CHS122", "cluster_text": "Dennis Mulladad is a 68 year old Aboriginal man from a rural town, now on day 2 after surgical repair of a right Achilles tendon rupture sustained while playing with his grandchildren. He has type 2 diabetes and hypertension. His wife and two adult children sit with him, and the hospital's Aboriginal Liaison Officer visited this morning. He speaks quietly and his wife answers some of your questions for him. The surgeon's protocol: removable boot with heel wedges and non weight bearing for two weeks. Findings: pain 3/10 at rest, toes warm and pink, capillary refill 2 seconds, sensation intact, HR 78, BP 138/84. He needs to practise transfers and mobilising with a walking frame before discharge planning begins.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Loosen the boot straps, keep the leg level and urgently notify the surgical team): Pale cool toes, slow capillary refill and altered sensation suggest compromised circulation or developing compartment problems, which are time critical. Relieving external pressure, keeping the leg level and escalating at once is safest. Waiting, delaying review or taking the boot off without medical direction risks tissue damage and breaches the protocol."}, {"id": 1447, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Mulladad is quiet and his wife answers many of your questions. Before tomorrow's discharge planning meeting you need to teach him the weight bearing rules and discuss his goals for caring for his grandchildren. Which approach is most culturally safe?", "options": {"A": "Teach with his whole family present, and give his wife the written instructions to manage", "B": "Teach him one to one in a private room, since privacy suits a quiet patient", "C": "Ask who he wants present, offer Aboriginal Liaison Officer support and teach at his pace", "D": "Ask the Aboriginal Liaison Officer to run the session, then confirm his understanding at discharge"}, "cluster_id": "CHS122", "cluster_text": "Dennis Mulladad is a 68 year old Aboriginal man from a rural town, now on day 2 after surgical repair of a right Achilles tendon rupture sustained while playing with his grandchildren. He has type 2 diabetes and hypertension. His wife and two adult children sit with him, and the hospital's Aboriginal Liaison Officer visited this morning. He speaks quietly and his wife answers some of your questions for him. The surgeon's protocol: removable boot with heel wedges and non weight bearing for two weeks. Findings: pain 3/10 at rest, toes warm and pink, capillary refill 2 seconds, sensation intact, HR 78, BP 138/84. He needs to practise transfers and mobilising with a walking frame before discharge planning begins.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ask who he wants present, offer Aboriginal Liaison Officer support and teach at his pace): Culturally safe practice lets the patient decide who is involved and offers, rather than presumes, support from the Aboriginal Liaison Officer. Handing instructions to his wife removes his own decision making, and a private one to one session may exclude family he values. The liaison officer supports communication but does not replace the physiotherapist's responsibility to teach him the weight bearing rules."}, {"id": 1448, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "A student physiotherapist asks why Mr Mulladad's calf and foot on the operated leg are checked at every session. He has diabetes and will be non weight bearing in a boot for two weeks after surgery. Which explanation is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Achilles repairs commonly fail early when swelling rises, so any swelling signals a repair breakdown", "B": "Diabetes causes foot ulcers under the boot, so skin checks replace any need for calf checks", "C": "Immobility and surgery raise his risk of venous thromboembolism, and diabetes can delay wound healing", "D": "Weight bearing restrictions mainly risk muscle wasting, so calf girth is measured to track atrophy"}, "cluster_id": "CHS122", "cluster_text": "Dennis Mulladad is a 68 year old Aboriginal man from a rural town, now on day 2 after surgical repair of a right Achilles tendon rupture sustained while playing with his grandchildren. He has type 2 diabetes and hypertension. His wife and two adult children sit with him, and the hospital's Aboriginal Liaison Officer visited this morning. He speaks quietly and his wife answers some of your questions for him. The surgeon's protocol: removable boot with heel wedges and non weight bearing for two weeks. Findings: pain 3/10 at rest, toes warm and pink, capillary refill 2 seconds, sensation intact, HR 78, BP 138/84. He needs to practise transfers and mobilising with a walking frame before discharge planning begins.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Immobility and surgery raise his risk of venous thromboembolism, and diabetes can delay wound healing): Recent surgery and reduced mobility are recognised risk factors for venous thromboembolism, and diabetes adds risk of poor healing and altered sensation. Some swelling is expected after repair and does not alone signal failure, and skin checks complement rather than replace monitoring for clot signs."}, {"id": 1449, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Alex plans treadmill walking and reports that heat and fatigue worsen their weakness. They want to build endurance without a relapse-like flare of symptoms. The gym is warm today. Which exercise approach is most appropriate for the physiotherapist to set up?", "options": {"A": "Avoid treadmill walking and prescribe only seated exercise, since aerobic exercise raises relapse risk", "B": "Use short intermittent bouts with rest breaks in a cool space while monitoring symptoms", "C": "Use high intensity interval walking with heat acclimatisation, increasing room temperature gradually to build tolerance", "D": "Use one continuous moderate session to maximise endurance gains, stopping only if fatigue becomes severe"}, "cluster_id": "CHS123", "cluster_text": "Alex Kourakis, 36, is a non-binary person who uses the name Alex and they and them pronouns, although their Medicare record lists a different legal name and sex. Alex has relapsing remitting multiple sclerosis diagnosed seven years ago, works as a graphic designer, and attends outpatient physiotherapy for leg weakness and fatigue. Findings: hip flexion 4/5 bilaterally, ankle dorsiflexion 4/5 on the right and 3+/5 on the left, mild plantarflexor spasticity (modified Ashworth 1), timed 25 foot walk slower than six months ago, and a high score on a fatigue questionnaire. Alex reports symptoms worsen in hot weather and after hot showers. The last relapse was eight months ago. Today they plan treadmill walking in the clinic gym at a moderate pace. The gym temperature is 27 degrees.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use short intermittent bouts with rest breaks in a cool space while monitoring symptoms): Heat and fatigue temporarily worsen symptoms in multiple sclerosis, so short intermittent bouts with rest in a cool space let Alex build endurance safely. Aerobic exercise does not increase relapse risk, so avoiding it is unnecessary. Continuous or heated training would push symptoms up and limit participation."}, {"id": 1450, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Halfway through walking, Alex reports blurred vision and heavier legs. After cooling and ten minutes of rest all symptoms fully settle. Between sessions Alex has no new symptoms and no fever or illness. Alex asks if this is a relapse. Which interpretation is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "A temporary heat-related worsening of existing symptoms, so adjust cooling and pacing next time", "B": "Ordinary deconditioning fatigue, so reduce intensity and expect the blurred vision to ease with fitness", "C": "A new relapse, because visual and motor symptoms together indicate active demyelination needing urgent medical review", "D": "A sign of disease progression, so exercise should stop and the neurologist should be told"}, "cluster_id": "CHS123", "cluster_text": "Alex Kourakis, 36, is a non-binary person who uses the name Alex and they and them pronouns, although their Medicare record lists a different legal name and sex. Alex has relapsing remitting multiple sclerosis diagnosed seven years ago, works as a graphic designer, and attends outpatient physiotherapy for leg weakness and fatigue. Findings: hip flexion 4/5 bilaterally, ankle dorsiflexion 4/5 on the right and 3+/5 on the left, mild plantarflexor spasticity (modified Ashworth 1), timed 25 foot walk slower than six months ago, and a high score on a fatigue questionnaire. Alex reports symptoms worsen in hot weather and after hot showers. The last relapse was eight months ago. Today they plan treadmill walking in the clinic gym at a moderate pace. The gym temperature is 27 degrees.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A temporary heat-related worsening of existing symptoms, so adjust cooling and pacing next time): Symptoms that appear with heat or exertion and settle fully with cooling and rest are a temporary worsening of old deficits, not a new relapse, which would last longer than a day. Treating it as a relapse or progression would cause needless alarm and stop beneficial exercise. Deconditioning does not usually explain blurred vision."}, {"id": 1451, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Later, Alex steps off the treadmill, loses balance, and falls, grazing their elbow. No head strike occurred and Alex stands with help. The treadmill safety clip was found not attached. Alex is upset. After immediate care, what is the most appropriate professional action regarding the incident?", "options": {"A": "Disclose openly what happened, complete an incident report, and review safety clip use", "B": "Record the fall in clinical notes only, because a graze is too minor for incident reporting", "C": "Reattach the clip, continue if Alex agrees, and mention the fall to the manager later", "D": "Explain that the fall reflects Alex's balance problems, then file a report once the graze heals"}, "cluster_id": "CHS123", "cluster_text": "Alex Kourakis, 36, is a non-binary person who uses the name Alex and they and them pronouns, although their Medicare record lists a different legal name and sex. Alex has relapsing remitting multiple sclerosis diagnosed seven years ago, works as a graphic designer, and attends outpatient physiotherapy for leg weakness and fatigue. Findings: hip flexion 4/5 bilaterally, ankle dorsiflexion 4/5 on the right and 3+/5 on the left, mild plantarflexor spasticity (modified Ashworth 1), timed 25 foot walk slower than six months ago, and a high score on a fatigue questionnaire. Alex reports symptoms worsen in hot weather and after hot showers. The last relapse was eight months ago. Today they plan treadmill walking in the clinic gym at a moderate pace. The gym temperature is 27 degrees.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Disclose openly what happened, complete an incident report, and review safety clip use): A fall with injury and a safety device not in use should be openly disclosed to the patient and reported promptly through the organisation's incident system so the cause can be reviewed. Notes alone do not trigger system review, and attributing the fall to the patient is inaccurate and unprofessional. Continuing the session before reporting delays the response."}, {"id": 1452, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "At the front desk, the receptionist calls Alex by the legal name on their Medicare record in the waiting room. Alex looks uncomfortable. Alex has not yet been asked how they wish to be addressed. The physiotherapist wants to ensure respectful, safe care going forward. Which action is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Use the legal name in documentation and speech to avoid billing and identification errors", "B": "Choose a neutral nickname for Alex in the clinic system, and avoid asking to prevent distress", "C": "Apologise to Alex in the waiting room and ask reception to use first names only", "D": "Ask Alex privately for their name and pronouns, record them, and brief reception staff"}, "cluster_id": "CHS123", "cluster_text": "Alex Kourakis, 36, is a non-binary person who uses the name Alex and they and them pronouns, although their Medicare record lists a different legal name and sex. Alex has relapsing remitting multiple sclerosis diagnosed seven years ago, works as a graphic designer, and attends outpatient physiotherapy for leg weakness and fatigue. Findings: hip flexion 4/5 bilaterally, ankle dorsiflexion 4/5 on the right and 3+/5 on the left, mild plantarflexor spasticity (modified Ashworth 1), timed 25 foot walk slower than six months ago, and a high score on a fatigue questionnaire. Alex reports symptoms worsen in hot weather and after hot showers. The last relapse was eight months ago. Today they plan treadmill walking in the clinic gym at a moderate pace. The gym temperature is 27 degrees.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ask Alex privately for their name and pronouns, record them, and brief reception staff): Asking privately avoids drawing attention and lets Alex choose the name and pronouns, which are then recorded and shared with staff on a need to know basis. Using only the legal name ignores Alex's identity, while a public apology or a guessed nickname both skip asking the person. Billing requirements can be met separately from everyday address."}, {"id": 1453, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Late in the afternoon, the physiotherapist still has to complete the discharge plan for a post-stroke inpatient leaving tomorrow, return a non-urgent call, finish documentation for Sipho, and update a waiting-list audit. How should these tasks be prioritised before the end of the day?", "options": {"A": "Return the call first for good rapport, then notes, then the discharge plan, then the audit", "B": "Write Sipho's notes first while details are fresh, then the audit, with the discharge plan tomorrow", "C": "Update the audit first because it is quick, then the discharge plan, notes and the call", "D": "Complete the time-critical discharge plan first, then clinical notes, then the call, then the audit"}, "cluster_id": "CHS124", "cluster_text": "Sipho Nkosi, 34, a secondary school teacher, attends a hospital outpatient clinic 12 weeks after resection of a right cerebellar haemangioblastoma. He reports two near falls on school stairs and spills drinks when carrying them. Examination: gaze-evoked nystagmus on right lateral gaze, right dysmetria on finger-to-nose, impaired rapid alternating movements on the right, and an intention tremor. He walks with a wide base and veers to the right. Strength is 5/5 throughout and sensation is intact. Berg Balance Scale 41/56. He is medically stable and takes no sedating medication. The physiotherapist also covers ward referrals and has a full afternoon list, including a post-stroke inpatient whose discharge is planned for tomorrow, plus a non-urgent call and a waiting-list audit due today.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Complete the time-critical discharge plan first, then clinical notes, then the call, then the audit): Tasks are ranked by clinical urgency and consequence of delay. A discharge planned for tomorrow needs equipment, services and family arranged in advance, so it cannot wait. Doing quick administrative jobs first feels efficient but risks an unsafe or delayed discharge, while notes and calls can be finished once the time-critical work is done."}, {"id": 1454, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "On today's examination Sipho's strength is full and sensation intact, yet he overshoots targets on the right, has a tremor that worsens as his hand nears a target, and veers to the right when walking. Which interpretation best explains these findings?", "options": {"A": "Right cerebellar hemisphere dysfunction causing ipsilateral incoordination, not weakness or lost sensory feedback", "B": "Right corticospinal tract involvement causing spasticity and slowed voluntary movement of the limb", "C": "Right sided proprioceptive loss from dorsal column damage, causing sensory ataxia that worsens with eyes closed", "D": "Left cerebellar vermis dysfunction causing truncal instability but sparing limb coordination on testing"}, "cluster_id": "CHS124", "cluster_text": "Sipho Nkosi, 34, a secondary school teacher, attends a hospital outpatient clinic 12 weeks after resection of a right cerebellar haemangioblastoma. He reports two near falls on school stairs and spills drinks when carrying them. Examination: gaze-evoked nystagmus on right lateral gaze, right dysmetria on finger-to-nose, impaired rapid alternating movements on the right, and an intention tremor. He walks with a wide base and veers to the right. Strength is 5/5 throughout and sensation is intact. Berg Balance Scale 41/56. He is medically stable and takes no sedating medication. The physiotherapist also covers ward referrals and has a full afternoon list, including a post-stroke inpatient whose discharge is planned for tomorrow, plus a non-urgent call and a waiting-list audit due today.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Right cerebellar hemisphere dysfunction causing ipsilateral incoordination, not weakness or lost sensory feedback): Cerebellar hemisphere lesions cause incoordination on the same side as the lesion, with dysmetria, intention tremor and veering toward the affected side. Strength and sensation are normal here, which argues against corticospinal or dorsal column involvement. Sensory ataxia would improve with vision and worsen markedly when his eyes are closed, which is not described."}, {"id": 1455, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Sipho is keen to return to teaching and to walk more steadily on school stairs. His dysmetria and intention tremor are consistent across tasks, and he is otherwise medically stable. Which exercise approach should the physiotherapist begin with in clinic to improve his function?", "options": {"A": "Frenkel-style exercises alone in supine and sitting, deferring standing and walking tasks until coordination normalises", "B": "Strength training of the right limbs first, since weakness is the main driver of his incoordination", "C": "Eyes-closed balance drills on a firm surface to retrain reliance on proprioception over vision", "D": "Task-specific, graded balance and gait practice with repetition and feedback on movement quality"}, "cluster_id": "CHS124", "cluster_text": "Sipho Nkosi, 34, a secondary school teacher, attends a hospital outpatient clinic 12 weeks after resection of a right cerebellar haemangioblastoma. He reports two near falls on school stairs and spills drinks when carrying them. Examination: gaze-evoked nystagmus on right lateral gaze, right dysmetria on finger-to-nose, impaired rapid alternating movements on the right, and an intention tremor. He walks with a wide base and veers to the right. Strength is 5/5 throughout and sensation is intact. Berg Balance Scale 41/56. He is medically stable and takes no sedating medication. The physiotherapist also covers ward referrals and has a full afternoon list, including a post-stroke inpatient whose discharge is planned for tomorrow, plus a non-urgent call and a waiting-list audit due today.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Task-specific, graded balance and gait practice with repetition and feedback on movement quality): Evidence for cerebellar ataxia supports intensive, task-specific, progressively challenging balance and coordination practice in the activities the person needs. His strength is already full, so strengthening alone does not address the deficit. Deferring upright tasks delays carry-over to real function, and removing vision makes an already unsteady person less safe."}, {"id": 1456, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "At the next session, three months after tumour surgery, Sipho reports a new severe occipital headache, two episodes of vomiting this morning and increasing unsteadiness over three days. He seems more drowsy than usual. His heart rate and blood pressure are stable. What should the physiotherapist do?", "options": {"A": "Check his blood pressure and heart rate, and continue treatment if both are within normal limits", "B": "Cease treatment and arrange same-day medical review, communicating the neurological change to the doctor", "C": "Document the symptoms and reassess at the next scheduled session, adding vestibular exercises for the unsteadiness", "D": "Continue with a reduced-intensity balance session and advise him to see his GP if symptoms persist"}, "cluster_id": "CHS124", "cluster_text": "Sipho Nkosi, 34, a secondary school teacher, attends a hospital outpatient clinic 12 weeks after resection of a right cerebellar haemangioblastoma. He reports two near falls on school stairs and spills drinks when carrying them. Examination: gaze-evoked nystagmus on right lateral gaze, right dysmetria on finger-to-nose, impaired rapid alternating movements on the right, and an intention tremor. He walks with a wide base and veers to the right. Strength is 5/5 throughout and sensation is intact. Berg Balance Scale 41/56. He is medically stable and takes no sedating medication. The physiotherapist also covers ward referrals and has a full afternoon list, including a post-stroke inpatient whose discharge is planned for tomorrow, plus a non-urgent call and a waiting-list audit due today.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Cease treatment and arrange same-day medical review, communicating the neurological change to the doctor): New severe headache, vomiting, drowsiness and worsening ataxia after posterior fossa surgery suggest raised intracranial pressure or hydrocephalus, which is an emergency. Stable heart rate and blood pressure do not exclude it. Treatment stops and a medical team is engaged the same day, rather than waiting for symptoms to persist or adding exercise."}, {"id": 1457, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Dorothy has gained weight since discharge, sleeps propped on three pillows, and has bibasal crackles. Her pulse and blood pressure are unchanged from discharge, and she feels well in herself. She asks to begin her walking program today. What is the most appropriate plan for this visit?", "options": {"A": "Perform chest percussion and airway clearance techniques to clear the crackles, then reassess her oxygen saturation", "B": "Teach pursed-lip breathing and positioning, then review next week since oedema is expected in heart failure", "C": "Defer exercise and contact her GP or heart failure nurse today about the congestion", "D": "Begin gentle walking indoors with rests, and mention the weight gain at her next GP visit"}, "cluster_id": "CHS125", "cluster_text": "Dorothy Kostas, 81, lives alone in a single-storey home and was discharged four days ago after admission with decompensated heart failure (reduced ejection fraction). Medications: frusemide, perindopril, carvedilol, spironolactone. Today: breathless after walking from bedroom to kitchen, resting RR 20, HR 88 regular, BP 108/64 mmHg, SpO2 94 percent on room air. Bilateral pitting oedema to mid-shin. Weight 63.4 kg; discharge weight was 62.0 kg. She sleeps on three pillows. Bibasal crackles on auscultation. Her GP's chronic disease management plan covers a set number of subsidised physiotherapy visits per year, and she has one remaining. She has no home support services. She wants to stay at home. Her daughter lives interstate.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Defer exercise and contact her GP or heart failure nurse today about the congestion): Weight gain since discharge, orthopnoea and bibasal crackles point to recurring fluid overload, which needs medical review the same day because diuretic changes are outside physiotherapy scope. Starting a walking program on top of worsening congestion adds risk. Crackles from fluid are not secretions, so airway clearance will not help."}, {"id": 1458, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Dorothy asks how she can tell whether her condition is getting worse between visits. Her daughter phones daily. She struggles to read small print. Which self-monitoring advice should the physiotherapist reinforce in line with her heart failure action plan and daily routine?", "options": {"A": "Weigh herself each morning, record it, and report rapid gain or worsening breathlessness", "B": "Use the Borg scale during chores and limit activity whenever she scores above a moderate rating", "C": "Track resting pulse each evening and report it only if it rises above her usual range", "D": "Measure ankle circumference weekly with a tape and report any increase at her next appointment"}, "cluster_id": "CHS125", "cluster_text": "Dorothy Kostas, 81, lives alone in a single-storey home and was discharged four days ago after admission with decompensated heart failure (reduced ejection fraction). Medications: frusemide, perindopril, carvedilol, spironolactone. Today: breathless after walking from bedroom to kitchen, resting RR 20, HR 88 regular, BP 108/64 mmHg, SpO2 94 percent on room air. Bilateral pitting oedema to mid-shin. Weight 63.4 kg; discharge weight was 62.0 kg. She sleeps on three pillows. Bibasal crackles on auscultation. Her GP's chronic disease management plan covers a set number of subsidised physiotherapy visits per year, and she has one remaining. She has no home support services. She wants to stay at home. Her daughter lives interstate.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Weigh herself each morning, record it, and report rapid gain or worsening breathlessness): Daily morning weights taken under the same conditions are the most sensitive home indicator of fluid retention, and rapid gain or worsening breathlessness should prompt same-day contact. Weekly ankle measurements, pulse alone and effort ratings each miss early fluid accumulation. A large-print chart helps her record weights given her reading difficulty."}, {"id": 1459, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Dorothy is at her final subsidised physiotherapy visit under her GP's plan, wants supervised exercise to continue at home, and has no support services. Her daughter asks how ongoing help could be funded. Cost is a concern for the family. What is the most appropriate advice?", "options": {"A": "Explain the visit limit and help her arrange a My Aged Care assessment", "B": "Ask the GP to issue a new chronic disease management plan, which restarts subsidised visits immediately", "C": "Recommend she pay privately for weekly visits as other funded options need a compensable injury claim", "D": "Suggest an NDIS application, since that scheme funds ongoing physiotherapy for people with chronic heart conditions"}, "cluster_id": "CHS125", "cluster_text": "Dorothy Kostas, 81, lives alone in a single-storey home and was discharged four days ago after admission with decompensated heart failure (reduced ejection fraction). Medications: frusemide, perindopril, carvedilol, spironolactone. Today: breathless after walking from bedroom to kitchen, resting RR 20, HR 88 regular, BP 108/64 mmHg, SpO2 94 percent on room air. Bilateral pitting oedema to mid-shin. Weight 63.4 kg; discharge weight was 62.0 kg. She sleeps on three pillows. Bibasal crackles on auscultation. Her GP's chronic disease management plan covers a set number of subsidised physiotherapy visits per year, and she has one remaining. She has no home support services. She wants to stay at home. Her daughter lives interstate.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Explain the visit limit and help her arrange a My Aged Care assessment): Subsidised visits under a GP management plan are capped per calendar year, so a new plan does not restart them. A My Aged Care assessment is the usual pathway to government-subsidised home support for an older person. The NDIS generally does not take new participants who first need support after age 65, and private payment alone ignores available funding."}, {"id": 1460, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Dorothy becomes breathless lying back for the assessment and feels better when upright, leaning forward on a table with arms supported. Her GP has since adjusted her medication. Which explanation best accounts for this response and guides her positioning at home?", "options": {"A": "Forward lean stretches the pectoral muscles, which loosens the chest wall and reduces rib cage stiffness", "B": "Upright posture lowers oxygen demand by reducing muscle work, so it is useful only during exertion", "C": "Upright posture reduces venous return and lung congestion, easing the work of breathing", "D": "Lying flat increases diaphragmatic excursion, so any discomfort suggests lower lobe collapse needing airway clearance"}, "cluster_id": "CHS125", "cluster_text": "Dorothy Kostas, 81, lives alone in a single-storey home and was discharged four days ago after admission with decompensated heart failure (reduced ejection fraction). Medications: frusemide, perindopril, carvedilol, spironolactone. Today: breathless after walking from bedroom to kitchen, resting RR 20, HR 88 regular, BP 108/64 mmHg, SpO2 94 percent on room air. Bilateral pitting oedema to mid-shin. Weight 63.4 kg; discharge weight was 62.0 kg. She sleeps on three pillows. Bibasal crackles on auscultation. Her GP's chronic disease management plan covers a set number of subsidised physiotherapy visits per year, and she has one remaining. She has no home support services. She wants to stay at home. Her daughter lives interstate.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Upright posture reduces venous return and lung congestion, easing the work of breathing): In heart failure, lying flat increases venous return to a failing ventricle and worsens pulmonary congestion, so upright or forward-leaning supported postures reduce dyspnoea. This is a haemodynamic effect rather than a musculoskeletal or secretion problem. Her home setup should include supports so she can adopt these positions comfortably."}, {"id": 1461, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Tran instinctively leans forward over the bedside table when breathless. Her daughter asks whether the posture is harmful and whether she should be made to lie back. She is otherwise calm and her observations are unchanged. How should the physiotherapist respond?", "options": {"A": "Forward leaning flattens the diaphragm unhelpfully, so teach her to sit upright against pillows instead", "B": "Lying flat rests the accessory muscles and eases effort, so recommend it during episodes of breathlessness", "C": "Supported forward leaning eases breathlessness, so encourage it with arms resting and shoulders relaxed", "D": "The posture reduces perfusion of the lung apices, so limit it to very brief periods"}, "cluster_id": "CHS126", "cluster_text": "Thanh Tran, 79, a retired seamstress, has metastatic lung cancer with a right pleural effusion and is in an inpatient palliative care unit. Her goals are comfort and to sit in the courtyard with her daughter. At rest she is calm: RR 22, HR 96, SpO2 92 percent on room air. Walking six metres to the bathroom brings on Borg breathlessness of 7 out of 10 with anxiety, and she recovers over about eight minutes. She leans forward over the bedside table when breathless. A regular opioid is prescribed by the medical team. A handheld fan sits on her locker, but she says it looks silly. The day nurse is about to leave, the evening nurse is starting, and the notes contain no plan for managing her breathlessness.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Supported forward leaning eases breathlessness, so encourage it with arms resting and shoulders relaxed): Leaning forward on a firm surface fixes the shoulder girdle so the accessory muscles can lift the chest more effectively, and it is a recognised position of ease. Encouraging it is appropriate and comforting. Lying flat usually increases breathlessness in someone with a pleural effusion and tiring respiratory muscles."}, {"id": 1462, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Tran wants to sit in the courtyard with her daughter, about 40 metres from her room, but becomes breathless after six metres. She says the handheld fan looks silly. Her goals are comfort-focused and her energy is limited. Which plan best supports her goal?", "options": {"A": "Plan short walking stages with seats, trial the fan during recovery, and extend distances gradually", "B": "Restrict activity to seated exercises in her room so breathlessness is avoided and energy saved", "C": "Walk her to the courtyard in one effort with oxygen, as reaching it matters most", "D": "Walk her daily until breathlessness reaches Borg 7, to build tolerance and reduce her fear"}, "cluster_id": "CHS126", "cluster_text": "Thanh Tran, 79, a retired seamstress, has metastatic lung cancer with a right pleural effusion and is in an inpatient palliative care unit. Her goals are comfort and to sit in the courtyard with her daughter. At rest she is calm: RR 22, HR 96, SpO2 92 percent on room air. Walking six metres to the bathroom brings on Borg breathlessness of 7 out of 10 with anxiety, and she recovers over about eight minutes. She leans forward over the bedside table when breathless. A regular opioid is prescribed by the medical team. A handheld fan sits on her locker, but she says it looks silly. The day nurse is about to leave, the evening nurse is starting, and the notes contain no plan for managing her breathlessness.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Plan short walking stages with seats, trial the fan during recovery, and extend distances gradually): Her goal is comfort and time with her daughter, so staged walks with seats, a trial of airflow from the fan for recovery, and gradual progression suit her. A single long effort or pushing to severe breathlessness adds distress, while restricting her to room exercises removes the activity that matters most to her."}, {"id": 1463, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "At 3 pm the day nurse leaves and the evening nurse starts. The physiotherapist has just learned Mrs Tran recovers within eight minutes when she leans forward and uses the fan, and that she becomes anxious when alone. Which handover to the evening nurse is most effective?", "options": {"A": "Report the distance walked and exercises completed, leaving symptom plans to the palliative care doctor", "B": "Brief the nurse in person on her triggers, positions, fan use and recovery time, then document", "C": "Write a detailed note and leave, because the written record serves as the formal handover", "D": "Mention her breathlessness on walking and ask the nurse to read the full notes later"}, "cluster_id": "CHS126", "cluster_text": "Thanh Tran, 79, a retired seamstress, has metastatic lung cancer with a right pleural effusion and is in an inpatient palliative care unit. Her goals are comfort and to sit in the courtyard with her daughter. At rest she is calm: RR 22, HR 96, SpO2 92 percent on room air. Walking six metres to the bathroom brings on Borg breathlessness of 7 out of 10 with anxiety, and she recovers over about eight minutes. She leans forward over the bedside table when breathless. A regular opioid is prescribed by the medical team. A handheld fan sits on her locker, but she says it looks silly. The day nurse is about to leave, the evening nurse is starting, and the notes contain no plan for managing her breathlessness.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Brief the nurse in person on her triggers, positions, fan use and recovery time, then document): Effective handover is timely, face to face and specific: triggers, helpful positions, what works and how long recovery takes, followed by documentation. A note alone or a vague mention leaves the evening nurse without the plan when distress occurs, and symptom strategies belong to the whole team, not only the doctor."}, {"id": 1464, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "The next morning Mrs Tran suddenly becomes severely breathless with new chest pain during a visit. Her RR is 34, HR 120 and SpO2 87 percent on room air. Her goals of care document comfort-focused treatment with no transfer to hospital. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Lay her flat and apply high flow oxygen by mask, then resume treatment once she settles", "B": "Leave to find the doctor for review, since managing acute symptoms is outside physiotherapy practice", "C": "Stop activity, support her leaning forward with the fan on, and call the nurse to attend", "D": "Continue the planned walk with breathing control coaching, as her saturations should improve with activity"}, "cluster_id": "CHS126", "cluster_text": "Thanh Tran, 79, a retired seamstress, has metastatic lung cancer with a right pleural effusion and is in an inpatient palliative care unit. Her goals are comfort and to sit in the courtyard with her daughter. At rest she is calm: RR 22, HR 96, SpO2 92 percent on room air. Walking six metres to the bathroom brings on Borg breathlessness of 7 out of 10 with anxiety, and she recovers over about eight minutes. She leans forward over the bedside table when breathless. A regular opioid is prescribed by the medical team. A handheld fan sits on her locker, but she says it looks silly. The day nurse is about to leave, the evening nurse is starting, and the notes contain no plan for managing her breathlessness.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop activity, support her leaning forward with the fan on, and call the nurse to attend): She is in acute distress and should not be left alone. Stopping activity, supporting her in a position of ease, using the fan and calling the nurse or doctor fits comfort-focused goals. Continuing a walk or lying her flat on high flow oxygen ignores her distress and the agreed care plan."}, {"id": 1465, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Khoury asks what the usual first steps are for hip osteoarthritis, and says she hopes to avoid surgery. The physiotherapist outlines recommended care based on Australian clinical guidelines. Which combination best describes the first-line management for her hip osteoarthritis before considering surgical referral?", "options": {"A": "Intra-articular corticosteroid injection and a walking aid first, with exercise added after pain settles", "B": "Activity pacing and a walking aid first, to protect the joint until surgery is reviewed", "C": "Education, individualised exercise and weight management, with surgical review if function stays poor", "D": "Manual therapy and short term analgesia first, adding exercise only after an orthopaedic opinion"}, "cluster_id": "CHS127", "cluster_text": "Samira Khoury, 68, a retired seamstress, has had right hip pain for two years. Pain is 5/10 on walking and stairs, with 20 minutes of morning stiffness. X-ray shows moderate joint space narrowing and osteophytes. There is no history of trauma and no night pain at rest. BMI 31. She walks less than before and sits much of the day. Examination: hip flexion 100 degrees, internal rotation 10 degrees with pain at end range, right hip abduction strength 4-/5, mild Trendelenburg sign on single leg stance, and 9 repetitions on a 30 second sit to stand. Neurological screen is normal. She wants to avoid surgery. Her daughter Rima drove her to the appointment and is waiting outside. The physiotherapist records the assessment in the clinic's electronic record.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Education, individualised exercise and weight management, with surgical review if function stays poor): Australian guidelines for hip osteoarthritis place education, tailored exercise and weight management first, with surgical review when function stays poor despite them. Injections, analgesia and manual therapy can ease symptoms but do not replace active self-management. Reducing activity and relying on a walking aid early lets the hip muscles weaken further."}, {"id": 1466, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Khoury is worried that her exercises will damage her hip because they cause some discomfort. During the first week she reports pain of 4/10 after exercising that is gone by the next morning. What advice about exercise-related pain is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Exercise only on days with no pain, to prevent flares and protect the joint surface", "B": "Temporary soreness that fades within a day is acceptable; continue and build up slowly", "C": "Stop any exercise that causes joint pain, because pain shows ongoing cartilage damage from joint loading", "D": "Push through the soreness and increase the load every session to build tolerance quickly"}, "cluster_id": "CHS127", "cluster_text": "Samira Khoury, 68, a retired seamstress, has had right hip pain for two years. Pain is 5/10 on walking and stairs, with 20 minutes of morning stiffness. X-ray shows moderate joint space narrowing and osteophytes. There is no history of trauma and no night pain at rest. BMI 31. She walks less than before and sits much of the day. Examination: hip flexion 100 degrees, internal rotation 10 degrees with pain at end range, right hip abduction strength 4-/5, mild Trendelenburg sign on single leg stance, and 9 repetitions on a 30 second sit to stand. Neurological screen is normal. She wants to avoid surgery. Her daughter Rima drove her to the appointment and is waiting outside. The physiotherapist records the assessment in the clinic's electronic record.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Temporary soreness that fades within a day is acceptable; continue and build up slowly): Mild to moderate soreness that settles within about a day is a normal response to loading and does not indicate harm, so she can continue and progress gradually. Stopping whenever the joint hurts reinforces fear and deconditioning. Exercising only on pain free days limits her training, and rapid load increases risk a prolonged flare."}, {"id": 1467, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Khoury's mild Trendelenburg sign and 4-/5 abduction strength suggest weak right hip abductors. She can stand on one leg briefly with a pelvic drop. She also has end range pain with internal rotation. Which exercise prescription is the most appropriate starting point for this weakness?", "options": {"A": "Static hip capsule stretching into internal rotation, then abductor strengthening only once range normalises", "B": "Resisted hip extension with a bridge progression, as gluteus maximus controls pelvic drop in stance", "C": "Supported weight shift and side-lying abduction, progressing to resisted standing abduction with pelvis level", "D": "Core stability training with trunk bracing, progressing to lumbar extension exercises for pelvic control"}, "cluster_id": "CHS127", "cluster_text": "Samira Khoury, 68, a retired seamstress, has had right hip pain for two years. Pain is 5/10 on walking and stairs, with 20 minutes of morning stiffness. X-ray shows moderate joint space narrowing and osteophytes. There is no history of trauma and no night pain at rest. BMI 31. She walks less than before and sits much of the day. Examination: hip flexion 100 degrees, internal rotation 10 degrees with pain at end range, right hip abduction strength 4-/5, mild Trendelenburg sign on single leg stance, and 9 repetitions on a 30 second sit to stand. Neurological screen is normal. She wants to avoid surgery. Her daughter Rima drove her to the appointment and is waiting outside. The physiotherapist records the assessment in the clinic's electronic record.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Supported weight shift and side-lying abduction, progressing to resisted standing abduction with pelvis level): The pelvic drop on single leg stance points to weak hip abductors, so graded abductor strengthening that progresses from supported to standing positions targets the deficit. Stretching into painful end range delays strengthening and may irritate the joint. Gluteus maximus work and trunk bracing are reasonable general exercises but do not directly train the abductors that hold the pelvis level."}, {"id": 1468, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "After the session, Rima phones the clinic asking for Mrs Khoury's diagnosis, assessment results and exercise plan so she can help at home. Mrs Khoury did not discuss sharing her health information with family during the visit. How should the physiotherapist respond?", "options": {"A": "Confirm Rima's identity with date of birth details, then release the information as a family contact", "B": "Release the notes if Rima completes a request form and states she is the nearest relative", "C": "Decline to share details without Mrs Khoury's consent, and offer to seek her permission", "D": "Share the general exercise plan only, since Rima helps with transport and supports her at home"}, "cluster_id": "CHS127", "cluster_text": "Samira Khoury, 68, a retired seamstress, has had right hip pain for two years. Pain is 5/10 on walking and stairs, with 20 minutes of morning stiffness. X-ray shows moderate joint space narrowing and osteophytes. There is no history of trauma and no night pain at rest. BMI 31. She walks less than before and sits much of the day. Examination: hip flexion 100 degrees, internal rotation 10 degrees with pain at end range, right hip abduction strength 4-/5, mild Trendelenburg sign on single leg stance, and 9 repetitions on a 30 second sit to stand. Neurological screen is normal. She wants to avoid surgery. Her daughter Rima drove her to the appointment and is waiting outside. The physiotherapist records the assessment in the clinic's electronic record.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Decline to share details without Mrs Khoury's consent, and offer to seek her permission): Health information is confidential and can be disclosed to family only with the patient's consent or another lawful basis, which a family relationship and a helpful role alone do not provide. The physiotherapist should decline politely and offer to ask Mrs Khoury, and record any consent given. Verifying identity or completing a form does not replace the patient's permission."}, {"id": 1469, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Yusuf is on NIV with supplemental oxygen bled in, and a later check shows her saturation at 96%. She has a history of hypercapnic respiratory failure and her morning gas showed pH 7.35 with raised PaCO2. Which physiotherapy action is most appropriate now?", "options": {"A": "Switch her to a high flow mask, since 96 percent shows NIV is no longer needed", "B": "Ask nursing to titrate oxygen down to the 88 to 92 percent target for hypercapnic COPD", "C": "Continue treatment and recheck later, since her pH has normalised and higher saturations are now safe", "D": "Leave the oxygen unchanged, since a saturation above 95 percent protects her heart and brain"}, "cluster_id": "CHS128", "cluster_text": "Mrs Amina Yusuf, 73, retired seamstress, admitted two days ago with an infective exacerbation of COPD and hypercapnic respiratory failure (pH 7.29 on admission). Ex-smoker, uses tiotropium and salbutamol, and managed her own shopping before admission. NIV was started and she has used it for most of the last 30 hours. Today: pH 7.35, RR 26, HR 104, SpO2 91% on NIV with oxygen bled in. Thick green sputum, bilateral wheeze, coarse crackles at the right base. She tires quickly and her cough is weak. She is alert and makes her own decisions. Her adult son, who lives in another suburb, has been calling the ward for updates. She has not named anyone to receive information about her care.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ask nursing to titrate oxygen down to the 88 to 92 percent target for hypercapnic COPD): In patients at risk of hypercapnia, Australian COPD practice targets a saturation of 88 to 92 percent, because excess oxygen can worsen carbon dioxide retention. A near normal pH does not remove that risk. A high saturation on NIV reflects the oxygen being delivered and is not a reason to stop ventilatory support."}, {"id": 1470, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Yusuf has thick green sputum, bilateral wheeze, coarse crackles at the right base and is tiring on NIV. Her gas has improved. She cannot clear secretions unaided. Medical review is complete. Which physiotherapy plan is most appropriate to help clear her secretions?", "options": {"A": "Take her off NIV for a thirty minute session of percussion and drainage, then restart", "B": "Keep NIV running continuously and perform routine hourly nasopharyngeal suction instead of active techniques", "C": "Add nebulised hypertonic saline through the NIV circuit before treatment, then perform vigorous chest percussion", "D": "Use brief NIV breaks for active cycle of breathing techniques in sitting, resuming NIV between sets"}, "cluster_id": "CHS128", "cluster_text": "Mrs Amina Yusuf, 73, retired seamstress, admitted two days ago with an infective exacerbation of COPD and hypercapnic respiratory failure (pH 7.29 on admission). Ex-smoker, uses tiotropium and salbutamol, and managed her own shopping before admission. NIV was started and she has used it for most of the last 30 hours. Today: pH 7.35, RR 26, HR 104, SpO2 91% on NIV with oxygen bled in. Thick green sputum, bilateral wheeze, coarse crackles at the right base. She tires quickly and her cough is weak. She is alert and makes her own decisions. Her adult son, who lives in another suburb, has been calling the ward for updates. She has not named anyone to receive information about her care.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Use brief NIV breaks for active cycle of breathing techniques in sitting, resuming NIV between sets): Short planned breaks from NIV for active techniques in sitting clear secretions while keeping her ventilatory support for most of the time, and she recovers on the machine between sets. A prolonged break risks tiring her and retaining carbon dioxide. Routine suction is invasive and hypertonic saline with percussion can provoke bronchospasm in wheezy COPD."}, {"id": 1471, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "After eight hours of NIV, Mrs Yusuf has a red, non-blanching area over the bridge of her nose, and air leaks around the mask into her eyes. She is distressed but her gas results are stable. What is the most appropriate physiotherapy action?", "options": {"A": "Apply thick moisturiser to the bridge, refit the same mask, and review the skin tomorrow", "B": "Check mask size and fit, pad the nasal bridge, and report the skin change to nursing", "C": "Remove NIV until the skin heals, give oxygen by nasal prongs, and reassess her gases tomorrow", "D": "Tighten the headgear to stop the leak, since a firmer seal prevents air reaching her eyes"}, "cluster_id": "CHS128", "cluster_text": "Mrs Amina Yusuf, 73, retired seamstress, admitted two days ago with an infective exacerbation of COPD and hypercapnic respiratory failure (pH 7.29 on admission). Ex-smoker, uses tiotropium and salbutamol, and managed her own shopping before admission. NIV was started and she has used it for most of the last 30 hours. Today: pH 7.35, RR 26, HR 104, SpO2 91% on NIV with oxygen bled in. Thick green sputum, bilateral wheeze, coarse crackles at the right base. She tires quickly and her cough is weak. She is alert and makes her own decisions. Her adult son, who lives in another suburb, has been calling the ward for updates. She has not named anyone to receive information about her care.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Check mask size and fit, pad the nasal bridge, and report the skin change to nursing): A non-blanching red area is early pressure injury, so the mask fit should be corrected, the bridge protected and the team informed. Tightening straps increases pressure on damaged skin. Stopping NIV puts a hypercapnic patient at risk of relapse, and moisturiser under the same ill-fitting mask does not relieve pressure."}, {"id": 1472, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Yusuf's adult son phones the ward and asks the physiotherapist to explain her diagnosis, oxygen needs and likely outlook. She is alert and able to decide, and has not nominated anyone to receive her information. How should the physiotherapist respond?", "options": {"A": "Withhold details, offer to ask Mrs Yusuf about sharing information, and document the call", "B": "Confirm she is on the ward, then tell him the doctor will update him when convenient", "C": "Share only her diagnosis and oxygen needs, since close family may receive basic ward information", "D": "Verify his identity using her date of birth, then give him a summary of her condition"}, "cluster_id": "CHS128", "cluster_text": "Mrs Amina Yusuf, 73, retired seamstress, admitted two days ago with an infective exacerbation of COPD and hypercapnic respiratory failure (pH 7.29 on admission). Ex-smoker, uses tiotropium and salbutamol, and managed her own shopping before admission. NIV was started and she has used it for most of the last 30 hours. Today: pH 7.35, RR 26, HR 104, SpO2 91% on NIV with oxygen bled in. Thick green sputum, bilateral wheeze, coarse crackles at the right base. She tires quickly and her cough is weak. She is alert and makes her own decisions. Her adult son, who lives in another suburb, has been calling the ward for updates. She has not named anyone to receive information about her care.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Withhold details, offer to ask Mrs Yusuf about sharing information, and document the call): Health information belongs to the patient, who has capacity and has not authorised disclosure, so none can be given until she consents. Asking her and recording the call respects privacy and keeps the record accurate. Family relationship or identity checks do not create a right to information, and even confirming details can breach confidentiality."}, {"id": 1473, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mia's mother asks what GMFCS level II tells her about how Mia will move around school and the community, and whether the level is likely to change as Mia grows. She has noticed Mia walks well at home but struggles on slopes and in crowds. Which explanation is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "She walks everywhere with only reduced speed and balance, and her level usually stays stable", "B": "She walks in most places, limited on rough ground, and her level usually stays stable", "C": "She needs a handheld device indoors and a wheelchair outdoors, and her level usually stays stable", "D": "She walks in most places, limited on rough ground, though her level typically worsens with growth"}, "cluster_id": "CHS129", "cluster_text": "Mia Thompson, 8 years, attends outpatient physiotherapy with her mother. She has bilateral spastic cerebral palsy, classified GMFCS level II, and attends a mainstream school. She walks indoors without a device but tires on the 400 metre walk between classrooms and avoids uneven playground surfaces. Findings: crouch pattern in mid stance, popliteal angle 60 degrees bilaterally, passive ankle dorsiflexion with the knee extended reaching neutral only, Modified Ashworth grade 1+ at gastrocnemius, knee extensor strength 4/5, gluteal strength 3+/5, no pain. Her goal is to walk between classrooms without resting. Today, while stepping over low obstacles in the gym, she tripped, fell onto a crash mat and grazed her knee. She did not strike her head, was alert, and bore weight normally afterwards. Her mother was present and is upset.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (She walks in most places, limited on rough ground, and her level usually stays stable): GMFCS level II describes walking in most settings with limitations on uneven surfaces, slopes, crowds and longer distances. The classification is designed to be broadly stable across childhood, so a rise in level with growth is not the expected pattern. Level I would describe walking without limitation, and a handheld device indoors describes level III."}, {"id": 1474, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Considering Mia's crouch in mid stance, hamstring tightness, and her knee extensor and gluteal strength, which physiotherapy programme best supports her goal of walking between classrooms without resting? She attends weekly and her mother can supervise short home practice between sessions.", "options": {"A": "Prolonged passive hamstring stretching as the main treatment, because weakness is not a limiting factor here", "B": "Treadmill walking in a body weight support harness, avoiding resisted tasks to limit fatigue", "C": "Task specific gluteal and quadriceps strengthening with hamstring stretching, practised in walking and stair tasks", "D": "Standing frame sessions to reduce tone before active exercise, adding walking practice only once tone settles"}, "cluster_id": "CHS129", "cluster_text": "Mia Thompson, 8 years, attends outpatient physiotherapy with her mother. She has bilateral spastic cerebral palsy, classified GMFCS level II, and attends a mainstream school. She walks indoors without a device but tires on the 400 metre walk between classrooms and avoids uneven playground surfaces. Findings: crouch pattern in mid stance, popliteal angle 60 degrees bilaterally, passive ankle dorsiflexion with the knee extended reaching neutral only, Modified Ashworth grade 1+ at gastrocnemius, knee extensor strength 4/5, gluteal strength 3+/5, no pain. Her goal is to walk between classrooms without resting. Today, while stepping over low obstacles in the gym, she tripped, fell onto a crash mat and grazed her knee. She did not strike her head, was alert, and bore weight normally afterwards. Her mother was present and is upset.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Task specific gluteal and quadriceps strengthening with hamstring stretching, practised in walking and stair tasks): Mia has reduced gluteal and quadriceps strength alongside hamstring tightness, so strengthening within functional tasks combined with stretching addresses the crouch. Stretching alone ignores the weakness that contributes to the crouch pattern. Standing frames and unloaded treadmill walking do not train the muscles she uses in the school corridor."}, {"id": 1475, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mia's goal is to walk the 400 metres between classrooms without resting, and her physiotherapist wants a measure that can be repeated each term to show change in this specific ability. Which outcome measure is most appropriate to choose for this purpose?", "options": {"A": "The six minute walk test, repeated under standardised conditions, to track walking endurance over time", "B": "The Timed Up and Go, because short distance mobility speed reflects endurance across a school day", "C": "The Modified Ashworth Scale at the calf each term, since reduced tone predicts better walking distance", "D": "A repeat GMFCS classification each term, because a change of level shows improved walking capacity"}, "cluster_id": "CHS129", "cluster_text": "Mia Thompson, 8 years, attends outpatient physiotherapy with her mother. She has bilateral spastic cerebral palsy, classified GMFCS level II, and attends a mainstream school. She walks indoors without a device but tires on the 400 metre walk between classrooms and avoids uneven playground surfaces. Findings: crouch pattern in mid stance, popliteal angle 60 degrees bilaterally, passive ankle dorsiflexion with the knee extended reaching neutral only, Modified Ashworth grade 1+ at gastrocnemius, knee extensor strength 4/5, gluteal strength 3+/5, no pain. Her goal is to walk between classrooms without resting. Today, while stepping over low obstacles in the gym, she tripped, fell onto a crash mat and grazed her knee. She did not strike her head, was alert, and bore weight normally afterwards. Her mother was present and is upset.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The six minute walk test, repeated under standardised conditions, to track walking endurance over time): Her goal is about walking distance and endurance, which the six minute walk test measures and which can be repeated in the same conditions. The Timed Up and Go reflects short mobility tasks, not endurance. Tone grades and GMFCS level are not sensitive measures of change in a specific walking goal."}, {"id": 1476, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "After the fall, Mia is comfortable and weight bearing normally, with a small graze that has been cleaned and dressed. The fall happened during supervised gym practice with the physiotherapist beside her. Her mother is upset but calm. Which action best meets risk management and documentation requirements for this event?", "options": {"A": "File the incident report and leave the fall out of the notes, keeping the records separate", "B": "Document the fall in the notes only, because a minor graze falls below the reporting threshold", "C": "Record the facts in clinical notes, file the incident report separately, and openly tell her mother", "D": "Record the facts and note that an incident report was filed, attaching a copy"}, "cluster_id": "CHS129", "cluster_text": "Mia Thompson, 8 years, attends outpatient physiotherapy with her mother. She has bilateral spastic cerebral palsy, classified GMFCS level II, and attends a mainstream school. She walks indoors without a device but tires on the 400 metre walk between classrooms and avoids uneven playground surfaces. Findings: crouch pattern in mid stance, popliteal angle 60 degrees bilaterally, passive ankle dorsiflexion with the knee extended reaching neutral only, Modified Ashworth grade 1+ at gastrocnemius, knee extensor strength 4/5, gluteal strength 3+/5, no pain. Her goal is to walk between classrooms without resting. Today, while stepping over low obstacles in the gym, she tripped, fell onto a crash mat and grazed her knee. She did not strike her head, was alert, and bore weight normally afterwards. Her mother was present and is upset.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Record the facts in clinical notes, file the incident report separately, and openly tell her mother): The clinical record should hold a factual account of the event and care given, while the incident report is a separate quality process and is not referred to in the notes. Open disclosure to the family is part of good practice. Skipping the report because the injury is minor, or omitting the fall from the notes, both leave the record incomplete."}, {"id": 1477, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "A junior colleague asks which part of Nikos's current status most strongly supports starting out-of-bed mobilisation today. Considering the usual safety criteria for mobilising ventilated adults, such as cardiovascular, respiratory and neurological stability, which single finding is most relevant here?", "options": {"A": "His limb strength is grade 3 to 4, which shows his legs can bear weight safely", "B": "He is off sedation, so cognition is clear and he can follow a full exercise program", "C": "He is alert and obeying commands, with stable circulation off vasopressors and modest ventilator support", "D": "His FiO2 of 0.35 and PEEP of 5 are low, so respiratory support alone clears him"}, "cluster_id": "CHS130", "cluster_text": "Nikos Kyriakou, 58, a builder, is on day 6 of ventilation in the high dependency unit after severe community-acquired pneumonia with septic shock. He is breathing on pressure support through an endotracheal tube: pressure support 8 cmH2O, PEEP 5 cmH2O, FiO2 0.35, RR 20, SpO2 96 percent. HR 96 in sinus rhythm, MAP 78 mmHg, off noradrenaline for 24 hours. Sedation has been ceased: he is alert, follows commands and has a weak but effective cough. MRC grades: shoulder flexion 4, grip 4, knee extension 3, ankle dorsiflexion 4. He has arterial and central lines in situ and a nasogastric tube. The physiotherapist is covering four other referrals this morning because a colleague is on leave, and Nikos is booked for mobilisation at 0900.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (He is alert and obeying commands, with stable circulation off vasopressors and modest ventilator support): Screening for mobilising ventilated patients combines cardiovascular stability, acceptable ventilator settings and the ability to cooperate. Nikos is alert, follows commands and is off vasopressors with modest support. Limb strength alone does not clear him, being off sedation says little about circulation, and low oxygen settings are only one part of the safety picture."}, {"id": 1478, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Nikos sits on the edge of the bed with assistance. After three minutes his SpO2 falls from 96 to 91 percent, HR rises from 96 to 128, RR rises from 20 to 32, and he reports breathlessness at 7 on the Borg scale. What is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Lay him flat and ask nursing staff to increase his ventilator support before any further activity", "B": "Keep him sitting with feet supported and continue the session while watching his oxygen saturation", "C": "Continue sitting for two more minutes to allow adaptation, since exertional stress is part of weaning", "D": "Stop the activity, return him to a supported position, and reassess before deciding how to progress"}, "cluster_id": "CHS130", "cluster_text": "Nikos Kyriakou, 58, a builder, is on day 6 of ventilation in the high dependency unit after severe community-acquired pneumonia with septic shock. He is breathing on pressure support through an endotracheal tube: pressure support 8 cmH2O, PEEP 5 cmH2O, FiO2 0.35, RR 20, SpO2 96 percent. HR 96 in sinus rhythm, MAP 78 mmHg, off noradrenaline for 24 hours. Sedation has been ceased: he is alert, follows commands and has a weak but effective cough. MRC grades: shoulder flexion 4, grip 4, knee extension 3, ankle dorsiflexion 4. He has arterial and central lines in situ and a nasogastric tube. The physiotherapist is covering four other referrals this morning because a colleague is on leave, and Nikos is booked for mobilisation at 0900.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stop the activity, return him to a supported position, and reassess before deciding how to progress): The combined fall in saturation, tachycardia, tachypnoea and high perceived breathlessness show intolerance, so activity should stop and he should be supported while recovery is observed. Pushing on risks further decompensation. Changing ventilator settings is outside physiotherapy scope, and lying flat would worsen his breathing mechanics."}, {"id": 1479, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "At 0850 a nurse reports that a post-laparotomy patient has audible secretions and a falling saturation of 89 percent despite oxygen. Nikos's 0900 session, a stroke review and two routine referrals are also waiting. How should the physiotherapist prioritise the morning?", "options": {"A": "Complete Nikos's booked session first to protect his weaning progress, then attend the post-laparotomy patient", "B": "Delegate the post-laparotomy patient to an allied health assistant so both sessions can run on schedule", "C": "See the deteriorating patient first, tell Nikos's nurse about the delay, and reschedule his session later", "D": "Ask the nurse to arrange suction and reassess later, so the planned list stays on time"}, "cluster_id": "CHS130", "cluster_text": "Nikos Kyriakou, 58, a builder, is on day 6 of ventilation in the high dependency unit after severe community-acquired pneumonia with septic shock. He is breathing on pressure support through an endotracheal tube: pressure support 8 cmH2O, PEEP 5 cmH2O, FiO2 0.35, RR 20, SpO2 96 percent. HR 96 in sinus rhythm, MAP 78 mmHg, off noradrenaline for 24 hours. Sedation has been ceased: he is alert, follows commands and has a weak but effective cough. MRC grades: shoulder flexion 4, grip 4, knee extension 3, ankle dorsiflexion 4. He has arterial and central lines in situ and a nasogastric tube. The physiotherapist is covering four other referrals this morning because a colleague is on leave, and Nikos is booked for mobilisation at 0900.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (See the deteriorating patient first, tell Nikos's nurse about the delay, and reschedule his session later): An acutely deteriorating patient with a treatable respiratory problem outranks a planned, stable mobilisation, and the delay should be communicated to the nursing team. Delegating an unstable patient to an allied health assistant is outside their scope. Postponing assessment to keep a timetable puts the sicker patient at risk."}, {"id": 1480, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Nikos is extubated to high-flow nasal oxygen the following day. He is alert with a weak cough, fatigues when talking, and sits at the bed edge with SpO2 94 percent. Secretions are audible in the upper chest. Which strategy best supports his recovery over the next 24 hours?", "options": {"A": "Intermittent positive pressure breathing every two hours, as mobilisation adds fatigue before his cough recovers", "B": "Prolonged bed rest on high-flow oxygen for a day to conserve respiratory effort before beginning activity", "C": "Hourly percussion and vibration to prevent secretion build-up, with mobilisation delayed until secretions clear", "D": "Upright positioning with assisted cough, then graded mobilisation monitored for fatigue and oxygen saturation"}, "cluster_id": "CHS130", "cluster_text": "Nikos Kyriakou, 58, a builder, is on day 6 of ventilation in the high dependency unit after severe community-acquired pneumonia with septic shock. He is breathing on pressure support through an endotracheal tube: pressure support 8 cmH2O, PEEP 5 cmH2O, FiO2 0.35, RR 20, SpO2 96 percent. HR 96 in sinus rhythm, MAP 78 mmHg, off noradrenaline for 24 hours. Sedation has been ceased: he is alert, follows commands and has a weak but effective cough. MRC grades: shoulder flexion 4, grip 4, knee extension 3, ankle dorsiflexion 4. He has arterial and central lines in situ and a nasogastric tube. The physiotherapist is covering four other referrals this morning because a colleague is on leave, and Nikos is booked for mobilisation at 0900.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Upright positioning with assisted cough, then graded mobilisation monitored for fatigue and oxygen saturation): Upright positioning and an assisted cough address his weak cough, and early graded mobilisation counters ICU-acquired weakness while he is monitored for fatigue. Bed rest worsens deconditioning and secretion clearance. Routine percussion and fixed-interval pressure breathing do not target his main deficit and add burden."}, {"id": 1481, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Bui omits food on the left of her tray and her line bisection is deviated to the right, but formal visual field testing is not yet available. Which observation would most strongly support unilateral spatial neglect rather than a left homonymous hemianopia?", "options": {"A": "She does not scan left even when cued and ignores her left arm", "B": "She has left sided weakness and reduced sitting balance, both common after a right hemisphere stroke", "C": "She bumps armrests into door frames only in dim corridors and not in bright light", "D": "She is aware of missing the left side and turns her head left to compensate"}, "cluster_id": "CHS131", "cluster_text": "Mrs Thuy Bui, 76, retired nurse, right parietal infarct 3 weeks ago, in inpatient rehabilitation. Left hemiparesis (leg strength 3/5, arm 2/5). She sits with her head and gaze turned to the right, leaves food on the left of her tray, and bumps her left wheelchair armrest into door frames. Line bisection is markedly deviated to the right. Other cognition is fair but she has no insight: 'there is nothing wrong with my left'. She sits unsupported for 30 seconds and needs moderate assistance of one to stand. History of atrial fibrillation (on an anticoagulant) and osteoporosis. She tried to stand alone yesterday. Your morning also includes a new admission assessment due before 10 am and a family meeting at 1 pm.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (She does not scan left even when cued and ignores her left arm): Neglect is an attentional and representational problem, so the person fails to explore the affected side even when prompted and often disregards the limb as well. Awareness and compensatory head turning point towards a field cut. Weakness and balance loss are common but do not separate the two conditions."}, {"id": 1482, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Bui sits with her gaze fixed to the right and has no insight into her left sided omissions. For her next session which approach is most appropriate to begin reducing the effect of neglect on her function during meals and mobility?", "options": {"A": "Explain the deficit repeatedly and ask her to practise remembering to look left on her own", "B": "Use graded cues and a visual anchor to draw her attention leftward", "C": "Place all her belongings and the call bell on her right so she can reach them", "D": "Focus on left leg strengthening in standing first, as motor recovery will resolve the spatial problem"}, "cluster_id": "CHS131", "cluster_text": "Mrs Thuy Bui, 76, retired nurse, right parietal infarct 3 weeks ago, in inpatient rehabilitation. Left hemiparesis (leg strength 3/5, arm 2/5). She sits with her head and gaze turned to the right, leaves food on the left of her tray, and bumps her left wheelchair armrest into door frames. Line bisection is markedly deviated to the right. Other cognition is fair but she has no insight: 'there is nothing wrong with my left'. She sits unsupported for 30 seconds and needs moderate assistance of one to stand. History of atrial fibrillation (on an anticoagulant) and osteoporosis. She tried to stand alone yesterday. Your morning also includes a new admission assessment due before 10 am and a family meeting at 1 pm.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use graded cues and a visual anchor to draw her attention leftward): Graded cueing with an anchor on the neglected side trains her to explore that space and can be faded as awareness improves. Moving everything to the right makes tasks easier but reinforces the neglect. Because she lacks insight, explanation alone will not change behaviour, and strength gains do not fix the attentional deficit."}, {"id": 1483, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Bui is anticoagulated, has osteoporosis and tried to stand alone yesterday while ignoring her left leg and having no insight into her deficits. She is due for sit to stand practice. What is the best way to manage the risk during the session?", "options": {"A": "Stand on her left side to guard and cue attention to the left leg", "B": "Stand on her stronger right side, as this is where she attends and responds to instructions", "C": "Guard from behind with a gait belt and avoid cueing so she focuses on the movement", "D": "Ask her to rehearse the movement mentally first, then complete the sit to stand unaided"}, "cluster_id": "CHS131", "cluster_text": "Mrs Thuy Bui, 76, retired nurse, right parietal infarct 3 weeks ago, in inpatient rehabilitation. Left hemiparesis (leg strength 3/5, arm 2/5). She sits with her head and gaze turned to the right, leaves food on the left of her tray, and bumps her left wheelchair armrest into door frames. Line bisection is markedly deviated to the right. Other cognition is fair but she has no insight: 'there is nothing wrong with my left'. She sits unsupported for 30 seconds and needs moderate assistance of one to stand. History of atrial fibrillation (on an anticoagulant) and osteoporosis. She tried to stand alone yesterday. Your morning also includes a new admission assessment due before 10 am and a family meeting at 1 pm.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stand on her left side to guard and cue attention to the left leg): Positioning on the neglected side allows hands on guarding where the risk is greatest and draws her attention to that leg. Standing on the attended side tends to reinforce neglect and leaves the weak leg unprotected. A fall on an anticoagulant with osteoporosis could be serious, so unaided attempts or removing cues are not acceptable."}, {"id": 1484, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Your 9 am slot is Mrs Bui's session, but the ward manager asks for a new admission assessment before 10 am. Nursing has just reported Mrs Bui tried to get up alone again, and a family meeting is booked at 1 pm. What is the most appropriate first action?", "options": {"A": "Complete Mrs Bui's booked session first and do the admission assessment after the family meeting", "B": "Agree a safety plan with nursing, then do the admission assessment before 10 am", "C": "Ask a colleague to take the admission and cancel the family meeting to focus on her", "D": "Do the admission assessment first, as new patients take priority over existing patients with known risks"}, "cluster_id": "CHS131", "cluster_text": "Mrs Thuy Bui, 76, retired nurse, right parietal infarct 3 weeks ago, in inpatient rehabilitation. Left hemiparesis (leg strength 3/5, arm 2/5). She sits with her head and gaze turned to the right, leaves food on the left of her tray, and bumps her left wheelchair armrest into door frames. Line bisection is markedly deviated to the right. Other cognition is fair but she has no insight: 'there is nothing wrong with my left'. She sits unsupported for 30 seconds and needs moderate assistance of one to stand. History of atrial fibrillation (on an anticoagulant) and osteoporosis. She tried to stand alone yesterday. Your morning also includes a new admission assessment due before 10 am and a family meeting at 1 pm.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Agree a safety plan with nursing, then do the admission assessment before 10 am): An immediate falls risk takes priority, but it can be addressed quickly through a shared plan, which leaves time to meet the admission deadline. Pushing the new patient back or putting the new patient ahead of a known risk ignores one of the two demands. Cancelling a family meeting is unnecessary and removes a useful opportunity to involve family."}, {"id": 1485, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "During the Trendelenburg test Dorothea stands on her right leg and her pelvis drops on the left side. She is five weeks after a right hemiarthroplasty and has reduced hip strength. Which impairment best explains this finding on testing in her home?", "options": {"A": "Weakness of the left hip abductors on the dropping side, which cannot lift the free leg", "B": "Weakness of the right hip flexors, which cannot support the weight of the swinging leg", "C": "Weakness of the right hip abductors, which fail to hold the pelvis level", "D": "Shortening of the right hip adductors, which pull the pelvis down towards the stance side"}, "cluster_id": "CHS132", "cluster_text": "Dorothea Papadakis, 84, widow, lives alone in a single-storey house in a regional town. Five weeks ago she fell in her kitchen and had a right cemented hemiarthroplasty for an intracapsular neck of femur fracture through a lateral approach. She may weight bear as tolerated with no movement restrictions. Home visit findings: Timed Up and Go 24 seconds with a four-wheeled walker, right hip abduction 3/5, flexion 3+/5, positive Trendelenburg sign on the right, pain 2/10, two falls in the past year, osteoporosis. Blood pressure 130/78 mmHg lying and 108/66 mmHg standing, with dizziness on standing. She is anxious about falling again. The subsidised allied health visits available this year under her GP's plan are used up, she has had no My Aged Care assessment, and her daughter asks what further therapy will cost.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Weakness of the right hip abductors, which fail to hold the pelvis level): The stance-side abductors, mainly gluteus medius, must hold the pelvis level in single-leg stance, so a drop on the opposite side points to weakness on the right. The left abductors are not working in this position. Tight adductors and weak flexors do not produce this specific pelvic drop."}, {"id": 1486, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Dorothea still needs supervised therapy for balance and strength, but her subsidised visits are used up and her daughter is worried about the cost of further sessions. What is the most appropriate way for the physiotherapist to respond to this situation?", "options": {"A": "Outline costs and alternatives, and help her contact My Aged Care for a funding assessment", "B": "Offer a private fee package of discounted sessions, explaining her daughter can fund further visits", "C": "Provide a progressed home exercise program and phone review, then discharge until funding becomes available", "D": "Ask the GP for a new plan so further subsidised visits continue this year"}, "cluster_id": "CHS132", "cluster_text": "Dorothea Papadakis, 84, widow, lives alone in a single-storey house in a regional town. Five weeks ago she fell in her kitchen and had a right cemented hemiarthroplasty for an intracapsular neck of femur fracture through a lateral approach. She may weight bear as tolerated with no movement restrictions. Home visit findings: Timed Up and Go 24 seconds with a four-wheeled walker, right hip abduction 3/5, flexion 3+/5, positive Trendelenburg sign on the right, pain 2/10, two falls in the past year, osteoporosis. Blood pressure 130/78 mmHg lying and 108/66 mmHg standing, with dizziness on standing. She is anxious about falling again. The subsidised allied health visits available this year under her GP's plan are used up, she has had no My Aged Care assessment, and her daughter asks what further therapy will cost.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Outline costs and alternatives, and help her contact My Aged Care for a funding assessment): Clients should be told the likely costs and alternatives, and helped to reach publicly funded supports, with a My Aged Care assessment as the route for an older person. Moving straight to private payment skips the funding options that exist. Discharging with a program leaves a continuing need unsupervised, and a new GP plan does not renew the capped subsidised visits within the same year."}, {"id": 1487, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Standing blood pressure has dropped about 22 mmHg from lying, with dizziness, and Dorothea is due to progress her walking program this week. What should the physiotherapist do before progressing standing and walking activities with her at the next visit?", "options": {"A": "Report the postural drop to the GP, teach staged position changes, and keep exercise supported", "B": "Progress outdoor walking as planned and recheck pressure next week, since drops this size are common", "C": "Advise more fluid, salt and compression stockings, and continue the standing program as before", "D": "Withhold all standing exercise and use bed-based exercise until the GP gives written clearance"}, "cluster_id": "CHS132", "cluster_text": "Dorothea Papadakis, 84, widow, lives alone in a single-storey house in a regional town. Five weeks ago she fell in her kitchen and had a right cemented hemiarthroplasty for an intracapsular neck of femur fracture through a lateral approach. She may weight bear as tolerated with no movement restrictions. Home visit findings: Timed Up and Go 24 seconds with a four-wheeled walker, right hip abduction 3/5, flexion 3+/5, positive Trendelenburg sign on the right, pain 2/10, two falls in the past year, osteoporosis. Blood pressure 130/78 mmHg lying and 108/66 mmHg standing, with dizziness on standing. She is anxious about falling again. The subsidised allied health visits available this year under her GP's plan are used up, she has had no My Aged Care assessment, and her daughter asks what further therapy will cost.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report the postural drop to the GP, teach staged position changes, and keep exercise supported): A symptomatic postural drop raises falls risk and needs medical review, while the physiotherapist can teach safe transitions and keep activity supported and graded. Pushing on ignores a falls risk. Dietary and stocking advice falls outside physiotherapy scope, and removing standing work altogether causes avoidable deconditioning."}, {"id": 1488, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Her blood pressure has been reviewed and the GP has cleared exercise. Dorothea scores 24 seconds on Timed Up and Go, has two falls in a year and fears falling again. Which program is most appropriate for the next phase of her rehabilitation?", "options": {"A": "A progressive walking program with her walker, increasing distance weekly and including uneven ground outdoors", "B": "Hip abductor and quadriceps strengthening in sitting and supported standing, delaying balance challenge until confidence returns", "C": "A video based home program with weekly phone check ins and progression of balance tasks", "D": "Supervised progressive balance and strength training that challenges stability, with support nearby"}, "cluster_id": "CHS132", "cluster_text": "Dorothea Papadakis, 84, widow, lives alone in a single-storey house in a regional town. Five weeks ago she fell in her kitchen and had a right cemented hemiarthroplasty for an intracapsular neck of femur fracture through a lateral approach. She may weight bear as tolerated with no movement restrictions. Home visit findings: Timed Up and Go 24 seconds with a four-wheeled walker, right hip abduction 3/5, flexion 3+/5, positive Trendelenburg sign on the right, pain 2/10, two falls in the past year, osteoporosis. Blood pressure 130/78 mmHg lying and 108/66 mmHg standing, with dizziness on standing. She is anxious about falling again. The subsidised allied health visits available this year under her GP's plan are used up, she has had no My Aged Care assessment, and her daughter asks what further therapy will cost.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Supervised progressive balance and strength training that challenges stability, with support nearby): Falls prevention in older people works best with supervised, progressive balance and strength training that challenges stability, with safety support nearby. Walking improves fitness but does not specifically train balance. Strengthening without balance challenge misses the key deficit, and a video program with phone contact lacks hands on supervision for someone with her falls history."}, {"id": 1489, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Zain's parents want to know whether therapy is changing his movement abilities across lying, sitting, standing and walking over six months. He is seven years old with cerebral palsy at GMFCS level III. Which outcome measure is most appropriate to track this?", "options": {"A": "Gross Motor Function Measure, which is validated to detect change in children with cerebral palsy", "B": "Modified Ashworth Scale, because spasticity is the main driver of his movement ability and function", "C": "Berg Balance Scale, because it scores balance across functional tasks and predicts falls in this group", "D": "Six minute walk test, because it captures walking distance and endurance across all his movement skills"}, "cluster_id": "CHS133", "cluster_text": "Zain Hussain, 7, has bilateral spastic cerebral palsy, GMFCS level III. He walks indoors with a forward walker and uses a wheelchair for longer distances at school. He attends weekly outpatient physiotherapy and is followed by a paediatrician. His parents' goal is for him to walk from the classroom to the library with the walker. Findings: crouch gait with scissoring on the right, 10 metre walk test 22 seconds with the walker, hip adductor tone Modified Ashworth 2 bilaterally. Passive hip abduction with the knees extended is 15 degrees on the right and 30 degrees on the left. Over three weeks Zain has reported right groin and thigh pain on walking, and the groin is tender to palpation. His last pelvic radiograph was two years ago. His mother, Parveen, asks whether the hip is coming out of joint and whether his botulinum toxin injections should be increased.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Gross Motor Function Measure, which is validated to detect change in children with cerebral palsy): The Gross Motor Function Measure is a standardised, validated measure covering lying to walking skills and is sensitive to change in children with cerebral palsy, so it fits the parents' question. The walk test and balance scale each capture a narrow part of function, and spasticity grading describes impairment rather than ability. The measure chosen should match the question being asked."}, {"id": 1490, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "The physiotherapist adds a six minute walk test to track endurance, and Zain walks with his forward walker. The test will be repeated in six months by a colleague. How should the test be performed and recorded so the two results can be compared?", "options": {"A": "Remove the walker to show his true ability, and record distance walked before the first rest", "B": "Let him choose the walker or wheelchair during the test, and record total distance covered", "C": "Use the same walker, course and instructions, and record distance, rests taken and the device used", "D": "Repeat the test twice on the day and record the better distance as his typical result"}, "cluster_id": "CHS133", "cluster_text": "Zain Hussain, 7, has bilateral spastic cerebral palsy, GMFCS level III. He walks indoors with a forward walker and uses a wheelchair for longer distances at school. He attends weekly outpatient physiotherapy and is followed by a paediatrician. His parents' goal is for him to walk from the classroom to the library with the walker. Findings: crouch gait with scissoring on the right, 10 metre walk test 22 seconds with the walker, hip adductor tone Modified Ashworth 2 bilaterally. Passive hip abduction with the knees extended is 15 degrees on the right and 30 degrees on the left. Over three weeks Zain has reported right groin and thigh pain on walking, and the groin is tender to palpation. His last pelvic radiograph was two years ago. His mother, Parveen, asks whether the hip is coming out of joint and whether his botulinum toxin injections should be increased.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use the same walker, course and instructions, and record distance, rests taken and the device used): Repeat testing is only comparable when conditions are standardised, so the device, course, instructions and rests must be consistent and documented. Removing the walker or letting him change device alters the task and no longer reflects his usual method of walking. A better-of-two approach hides day to day variation and does not describe his typical performance."}, {"id": 1491, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Zain has had three weeks of right groin pain on walking, reduced right hip abduction and a tender groin. His last pelvic radiograph was two years ago. Parveen asks whether the hip is moving out of joint. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Reassure her this is common muscle tightness, and continue the program while monitoring pain weekly", "B": "Perform hip provocation tests today, then reassess in four weeks if the pain persists", "C": "Explain that imaging and medical review are needed, and contact his paediatrician promptly", "D": "Tell her the hip is likely subluxing and advise increasing his botulinum toxin to reduce pull"}, "cluster_id": "CHS133", "cluster_text": "Zain Hussain, 7, has bilateral spastic cerebral palsy, GMFCS level III. He walks indoors with a forward walker and uses a wheelchair for longer distances at school. He attends weekly outpatient physiotherapy and is followed by a paediatrician. His parents' goal is for him to walk from the classroom to the library with the walker. Findings: crouch gait with scissoring on the right, 10 metre walk test 22 seconds with the walker, hip adductor tone Modified Ashworth 2 bilaterally. Passive hip abduction with the knees extended is 15 degrees on the right and 30 degrees on the left. Over three weeks Zain has reported right groin and thigh pain on walking, and the groin is tender to palpation. His last pelvic radiograph was two years ago. His mother, Parveen, asks whether the hip is coming out of joint and whether his botulinum toxin injections should be increased.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Explain that imaging and medical review are needed, and contact his paediatrician promptly): New hip pain with reduced abduction in a child with bilateral spastic cerebral palsy may signal hip migration, which is confirmed by radiograph and managed by the medical team, so prompt referral for imaging is needed. The physiotherapist identifies and communicates the concern but does not diagnose displacement or change injection plans. Waiting four weeks, or increasing injections without imaging, delays appropriate care."}, {"id": 1492, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Zain's school asks the physiotherapist to explain his GMFCS level III classification to staff planning excursions. Staff want to know when he can walk and when he will need wheeled mobility on outings. Which description of mobility best matches this level for a child of his age?", "options": {"A": "Has very limited self-mobility, and is transported in a manual wheelchair or uses powered mobility outdoors", "B": "Walks without a device indoors but uses a rail on stairs and struggles on uneven ground", "C": "Walks without a device indoors and outdoors but has limits in speed, balance and coordination", "D": "Walks with a hand-held mobility device in most indoor settings and often uses wheeled mobility outdoors"}, "cluster_id": "CHS133", "cluster_text": "Zain Hussain, 7, has bilateral spastic cerebral palsy, GMFCS level III. He walks indoors with a forward walker and uses a wheelchair for longer distances at school. He attends weekly outpatient physiotherapy and is followed by a paediatrician. His parents' goal is for him to walk from the classroom to the library with the walker. Findings: crouch gait with scissoring on the right, 10 metre walk test 22 seconds with the walker, hip adductor tone Modified Ashworth 2 bilaterally. Passive hip abduction with the knees extended is 15 degrees on the right and 30 degrees on the left. Over three weeks Zain has reported right groin and thigh pain on walking, and the groin is tender to palpation. His last pelvic radiograph was two years ago. His mother, Parveen, asks whether the hip is coming out of joint and whether his botulinum toxin injections should be increased.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Walks with a hand-held mobility device in most indoor settings and often uses wheeled mobility outdoors): GMFCS level III describes a child who walks with a hand-held device in most indoor settings and relies on wheeled mobility for longer distances or rough terrain. Walking without a device describes levels I and II, while limited self-mobility with transport in a wheelchair describes level IV. Knowing the level helps staff plan realistic access for outings."}, {"id": 1493, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Arjun has intermittent inspiratory stridor even when settled in his mother's arms, mild recession, a barking cough and only transmitted upper airway noise on auscultation. Considering these findings together, what is the most appropriate interpretation for the physiotherapist to record in the notes?", "options": {"A": "Early bacterial pneumonia with consolidation, so active airway clearance and upright positioning are the priority", "B": "Reactive airway narrowing from bronchospasm, so positioning and breathing control techniques are the main treatment priority", "C": "Subglottic swelling causing upper airway obstruction, not retained secretions, so protecting the airway is the priority", "D": "Lower airway secretion retention in dependent lobes, so manual techniques and suction should begin first"}, "cluster_id": "CHS134", "cluster_text": "Arjun Patel, 14 months, was admitted to the paediatric ward from the emergency department with a two day coryzal illness, a barking cough and a hoarse cry. He is fully immunised, was born at term and was previously well. The medical team diagnosed croup and have started corticosteroid treatment. The physiotherapist is asked to review his noisy breathing before the afternoon round. Observations: HR 150, RR 48, SpO2 96 percent in air, temperature 37.8. Inspiratory stridor is audible when he cries and intermittently when settled in his mother's arms, with mild suprasternal and intercostal recession. Auscultation: transmitted upper airway noise only, no focal crackles. He is alert, feeding poorly and becomes distressed when strangers approach. His mother is anxious and has not left his side. The registrar wants a clear physiotherapy opinion and a plan.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Subglottic swelling causing upper airway obstruction, not retained secretions, so protecting the airway is the priority): Stridor when settled with recession points to subglottic swelling narrowing the upper airway, so the physiotherapist's role is monitoring and protecting it. Auscultation showing only transmitted noise argues against lower airway secretions, which is where chest physiotherapy techniques would help. Active clearance would agitate him and could worsen the obstruction."}, {"id": 1494, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Arjun cries and his stridor worsens whenever staff approach, though he settles in his mother's arms. The physiotherapist needs to complete the respiratory assessment safely before the medical round. Which approach to the assessment is most appropriate for this child at this stage?", "options": {"A": "Perform nasopharyngeal suction first to clear the noisy breathing, then reassess his stridor and recession", "B": "Observe and auscultate while his mother holds him, and defer handling likely to distress him", "C": "Lay him supine for a full chest examination, then return him to his mother to settle", "D": "Apply gentle chest percussion while his mother distracts him, then reassess his breath sounds"}, "cluster_id": "CHS134", "cluster_text": "Arjun Patel, 14 months, was admitted to the paediatric ward from the emergency department with a two day coryzal illness, a barking cough and a hoarse cry. He is fully immunised, was born at term and was previously well. The medical team diagnosed croup and have started corticosteroid treatment. The physiotherapist is asked to review his noisy breathing before the afternoon round. Observations: HR 150, RR 48, SpO2 96 percent in air, temperature 37.8. Inspiratory stridor is audible when he cries and intermittently when settled in his mother's arms, with mild suprasternal and intercostal recession. Auscultation: transmitted upper airway noise only, no focal crackles. He is alert, feeding poorly and becomes distressed when strangers approach. His mother is anxious and has not left his side. The registrar wants a clear physiotherapy opinion and a plan.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Observe and auscultate while his mother holds him, and defer handling likely to distress him): Agitation increases airflow turbulence and can convert partial obstruction into a critical one, so assessment is best done by observation and listening while he stays calm. Suction or percussion would not treat upper airway swelling and would provoke distress. A supine examination also removes the comfort and position he has chosen."}, {"id": 1495, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Two hours later Arjun has marked recession and louder stridor at rest. His SpO2 is 90 percent in air, and he is now drowsy and less responsive to his mother. His mother asks the physiotherapist whether she should wait for the registrar. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Stay with him in a position of comfort and urgently call the medical emergency team", "B": "Stay with him and call the registrar urgently, as the registrar knows his case best", "C": "Start oxygen by mask, repeat his observations in ten minutes and ask the registrar to review", "D": "Ask the nurse to give nebulised adrenaline, then reassess his stridor and saturation in fifteen minutes"}, "cluster_id": "CHS134", "cluster_text": "Arjun Patel, 14 months, was admitted to the paediatric ward from the emergency department with a two day coryzal illness, a barking cough and a hoarse cry. He is fully immunised, was born at term and was previously well. The medical team diagnosed croup and have started corticosteroid treatment. The physiotherapist is asked to review his noisy breathing before the afternoon round. Observations: HR 150, RR 48, SpO2 96 percent in air, temperature 37.8. Inspiratory stridor is audible when he cries and intermittently when settled in his mother's arms, with mild suprasternal and intercostal recession. Auscultation: transmitted upper airway noise only, no focal crackles. He is alert, feeding poorly and becomes distressed when strangers approach. His mother is anxious and has not left his side. The registrar wants a clear physiotherapy opinion and a plan.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stay with him in a position of comfort and urgently call the medical emergency team): Drowsiness, falling saturation and stridor at rest signal impending airway failure, which needs the medical emergency team immediately while the child is kept calm and supported. Calling only the registrar may delay a full resuscitation response. Mask oxygen can distress him and waiting ten minutes is unsafe, and requesting medication and reassessing later also lets time pass in a tiring child."}, {"id": 1496, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Before leaving the ward the physiotherapist hands over the review to the registrar and the nurse in charge. Which handover best reflects effective interprofessional communication for a child like Arjun who has deteriorated and then stabilised after the emergency response?", "options": {"A": "A structured handover of findings, the airway concern, actions taken and monitoring advice, with written notes", "B": "A verbal handover to the registrar only, covering the airway concern, with notes written later", "C": "A brief verbal update that his saturation recovered and his chest is clear, with notes later", "D": "A detailed chart entry of findings and actions, left for colleagues to read later"}, "cluster_id": "CHS134", "cluster_text": "Arjun Patel, 14 months, was admitted to the paediatric ward from the emergency department with a two day coryzal illness, a barking cough and a hoarse cry. He is fully immunised, was born at term and was previously well. The medical team diagnosed croup and have started corticosteroid treatment. The physiotherapist is asked to review his noisy breathing before the afternoon round. Observations: HR 150, RR 48, SpO2 96 percent in air, temperature 37.8. Inspiratory stridor is audible when he cries and intermittently when settled in his mother's arms, with mild suprasternal and intercostal recession. Auscultation: transmitted upper airway noise only, no focal crackles. He is alert, feeding poorly and becomes distressed when strangers approach. His mother is anxious and has not left his side. The registrar wants a clear physiotherapy opinion and a plan.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A structured handover of findings, the airway concern, actions taken and monitoring advice, with written notes): The structured handover such as ISBAR gives the whole receiving team the situation, the key airway concern, what was done and what to monitor. Briefing one person leaves the nurse in charge without the plan, and a short update about a clear chest hides the airway concern. The chart entry alone depends on colleagues finding and reading it in time."}, {"id": 1497, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Staff are unsure whether Mrs O'Sullivan's confusion simply reflects her known mild cognitive impairment or is something new after her surgery. Which feature of her presentation over the past day most strongly suggests delirium rather than progression of that underlying condition?", "options": {"A": "Disorientation to date and place that remains stable across repeated assessments throughout the day", "B": "Word-finding difficulty and reduced speech fluency while attention and alertness remain otherwise preserved", "C": "Acute onset of fluctuating alertness and inattention, clearly different from her usual baseline", "D": "Slowly progressive loss of recent memory with alertness that stays consistent throughout the day"}, "cluster_id": "CHS135", "cluster_text": "Mrs Margaret O'Sullivan, 84, is on day 2 after a posterior approach hemiarthroplasty for a fractured neck of femur. She has mild cognitive impairment and lives alone. Since last night she has been intermittently drowsy, then agitated, pulling at her IV line and catheter, and unable to follow a conversation. Her daughter says this is very unlike her. Her glasses and hearing aids are in her locker. Observations: temperature 37.9 degrees C, HR 98, BP 105/60 mmHg, RR 20, SpO2 93 percent on room air, pain 6/10 on movement. She has not opened her bowels since admission. Orders: weight bear as tolerated, hip precautions per surgeon. Your morning list also includes a patient due for discharge home by midday who needs stair practice, a day-one total knee replacement awaiting first mobilisation, and a stable patient due for routine review.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Acute onset of fluctuating alertness and inattention, clearly different from her usual baseline): Delirium is defined by an acute change with fluctuating attention and level of alertness, and her daughter confirms it is out of character. Gradual memory loss, stable disorientation and isolated language difficulty fit chronic cognitive impairment, where attention and alertness are typically preserved."}, {"id": 1498, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs O'Sullivan is agitated but currently alert enough to try standing, and her weight bearing is as tolerated. Her glasses and hearing aids are still in her locker. Which approach to mobilisation is most appropriate for this session, given her delirium and hip precautions?", "options": {"A": "Defer all mobilisation until the delirium resolves, since standing may increase her distress and fall risk", "B": "Mobilise briefly with glasses and hearing aids on, using short single-step instructions", "C": "Seat her in the chair with a lap belt fitted so she cannot stand alone", "D": "Run the standard session and reorient her repeatedly by quizzing her on date and place"}, "cluster_id": "CHS135", "cluster_text": "Mrs Margaret O'Sullivan, 84, is on day 2 after a posterior approach hemiarthroplasty for a fractured neck of femur. She has mild cognitive impairment and lives alone. Since last night she has been intermittently drowsy, then agitated, pulling at her IV line and catheter, and unable to follow a conversation. Her daughter says this is very unlike her. Her glasses and hearing aids are in her locker. Observations: temperature 37.9 degrees C, HR 98, BP 105/60 mmHg, RR 20, SpO2 93 percent on room air, pain 6/10 on movement. She has not opened her bowels since admission. Orders: weight bear as tolerated, hip precautions per surgeon. Your morning list also includes a patient due for discharge home by midday who needs stair practice, a day-one total knee replacement awaiting first mobilisation, and a stable patient due for routine review.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Mobilise briefly with glasses and hearing aids on, using short single-step instructions): Sensory aids, simple single-step commands and brief supervised activity reduce confusion and keep her moving, which lowers delirium duration and deconditioning. Prolonged bed rest worsens outcomes, and repeated quizzing increases agitation. A lap belt is a restraint and should not be used to manage safety."}, {"id": 1499, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Four patients need you before midday: a discharge needing stair practice, a day-one knee replacement awaiting first mobilisation, a routine review, and Mrs O'Sullivan, whose new confusion and temperature of 37.9 degrees C have not been escalated. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Treat Mrs O'Sullivan first while she is alert enough to participate, then escalate her observations afterwards", "B": "Report her acute change to the nurse in charge, then reprioritise the other patients", "C": "Mobilise the knee replacement patient first, since early mobilisation reduces postoperative complications and shortens stay", "D": "See the discharge patient first, because transport is booked and a delayed bed affects patient flow"}, "cluster_id": "CHS135", "cluster_text": "Mrs Margaret O'Sullivan, 84, is on day 2 after a posterior approach hemiarthroplasty for a fractured neck of femur. She has mild cognitive impairment and lives alone. Since last night she has been intermittently drowsy, then agitated, pulling at her IV line and catheter, and unable to follow a conversation. Her daughter says this is very unlike her. Her glasses and hearing aids are in her locker. Observations: temperature 37.9 degrees C, HR 98, BP 105/60 mmHg, RR 20, SpO2 93 percent on room air, pain 6/10 on movement. She has not opened her bowels since admission. Orders: weight bear as tolerated, hip precautions per surgeon. Your morning list also includes a patient due for discharge home by midday who needs stair practice, a day-one total knee replacement awaiting first mobilisation, and a stable patient due for routine review.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report her acute change to the nurse in charge, then reprioritise the other patients): A sudden change in mental state with a raised temperature and borderline observations may reflect infection, hypoxia or another serious cause, so medical review is the time-critical action and takes only minutes. The other tasks are planned and can be sequenced afterwards. Treating her before escalation delays diagnosis."}, {"id": 1500, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs O'Sullivan has posterior hip precautions ordered but cannot retain instructions while delirious on the ward, and she keeps trying to cross her legs and bend forward in bed. Which approach to managing her hip precautions is most appropriate right now?", "options": {"A": "Cease the precautions because her confusion makes adherence unrealistic and rely on early mobilisation instead", "B": "Teach the precautions again each session with a written card and expect her to follow them", "C": "Maintain precautions through positioning, an abduction pillow and staff supervised handling", "D": "Keep her flat in bed with the hip still until the delirium has cleared"}, "cluster_id": "CHS135", "cluster_text": "Mrs Margaret O'Sullivan, 84, is on day 2 after a posterior approach hemiarthroplasty for a fractured neck of femur. She has mild cognitive impairment and lives alone. Since last night she has been intermittently drowsy, then agitated, pulling at her IV line and catheter, and unable to follow a conversation. Her daughter says this is very unlike her. Her glasses and hearing aids are in her locker. Observations: temperature 37.9 degrees C, HR 98, BP 105/60 mmHg, RR 20, SpO2 93 percent on room air, pain 6/10 on movement. She has not opened her bowels since admission. Orders: weight bear as tolerated, hip precautions per surgeon. Your morning list also includes a patient due for discharge home by midday who needs stair practice, a day-one total knee replacement awaiting first mobilisation, and a stable patient due for routine review.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Maintain precautions through positioning, an abduction pillow and staff supervised handling): When a patient cannot recall instructions, protection of the joint must come from the environment and from staff, using positioning aids and supervised transfers. Relying on her memory fails, stopping the surgeon's precautions is outside physiotherapy scope, and bed rest raises the risk of complications and prolongs delirium."}, {"id": 1501, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Bennell has C6 tetraplegia (ASIA impairment scale A), coarse crackles at the right base and a weak cough that cannot clear secretions. His diaphragm contracts well on palpation. Which mechanism most directly explains why his cough is currently ineffective?", "options": {"A": "Lost airway sensation from the spinal injury, so secretions go undetected and no cough starts", "B": "Vocal cord weakness, which stops the glottis closing and building pressure before the cough", "C": "Paralysed abdominal and intercostal muscles cannot generate the forceful expiration a cough needs", "D": "Diaphragm paralysis that prevents the deep inspiration he needs before he can attempt an effective cough"}, "cluster_id": "CHS136", "cluster_text": "Mr Jarrah Bennell, 31, is a Wiradjuri man from a town several hours from the rehabilitation unit. He is six weeks after a motor vehicle crash with C6 tetraplegia (ASIA impairment scale A). Assessment on the spinal ward: unable to cough effectively on request, abdominal muscles paralysed, diaphragm contracts well on palpation, paradoxical upper chest movement on inspiration, coarse crackles and reduced air entry at the right base, audible secretions he cannot clear. RR 22, HR 62, BP 98/58 supine, SpO2 94% on room air. He becomes dizzy on sitting up and wears an abdominal binder in the chair. He has an indwelling urinary catheter. His mother and an uncle visit most days. His uncle usually sits in on discussions, and staff have mostly directed questions to his mother, while Mr Bennell is quiet and looks away. The hospital Aboriginal Liaison Officer is available today.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Paralysed abdominal and intercostal muscles cannot generate the forceful expiration a cough needs): An effective cough needs a deep breath followed by a forceful expiration driven by the abdominal and intercostal muscles, which are paralysed at this level. His diaphragm is innervated above the injury and works, so inspiration is not the main problem. Vocal cord function and airway sensation are not what is lost in a cervical injury."}, {"id": 1502, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Bennell's uncle usually sits in on discussions, and staff have been directing most questions to his mother. Mr Bennell is quiet and looks away. The Aboriginal Liaison Officer is on the ward today. Before planning treatment, what is the most culturally safe approach for the physiotherapist?", "options": {"A": "Direct explanations to his mother and uncle as senior family, then summarise briefly for him", "B": "Speak with Mr Bennell directly, ask how he wants family involved, and offer liaison support", "C": "Ask the Aboriginal Liaison Officer to speak for him and agree the plan with his family", "D": "Use the standard consent form, keep the session brief, and check his understanding the next day"}, "cluster_id": "CHS136", "cluster_text": "Mr Jarrah Bennell, 31, is a Wiradjuri man from a town several hours from the rehabilitation unit. He is six weeks after a motor vehicle crash with C6 tetraplegia (ASIA impairment scale A). Assessment on the spinal ward: unable to cough effectively on request, abdominal muscles paralysed, diaphragm contracts well on palpation, paradoxical upper chest movement on inspiration, coarse crackles and reduced air entry at the right base, audible secretions he cannot clear. RR 22, HR 62, BP 98/58 supine, SpO2 94% on room air. He becomes dizzy on sitting up and wears an abdominal binder in the chair. He has an indwelling urinary catheter. His mother and an uncle visit most days. His uncle usually sits in on discussions, and staff have mostly directed questions to his mother, while Mr Bennell is quiet and looks away. The hospital Aboriginal Liaison Officer is available today.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Speak with Mr Bennell directly, ask how he wants family involved, and offer liaison support): Culturally safe practice keeps the patient at the centre: he decides who is involved, and the liaison officer supports him and the team rather than speaking for him. Directing information to relatives sidelines his consent, and handing decisions to a liaison officer or family does the same. Looking away should not be read as disinterest, and a rushed standard process does not build trust."}, {"id": 1503, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Twenty minutes after lunch, Mr Bennell's secretions are audible, his effort has increased and SpO2 has fallen to 91% on room air. He still cannot cough effectively. Nursing staff are available to help and his abdominal binder is in place. Which approach to clearing secretions is most appropriate now?", "options": {"A": "Use a gentle costal assisted cough with positioning, avoiding abdominal thrusts until the meal settles", "B": "Position him in left side lying with the head down and percuss the right base", "C": "Defer secretion clearance until the next scheduled session and ask nursing to monitor his saturation", "D": "Give a timed abdominal thrust assisted cough, as clearing secretions outweighs the reflux risk after lunch"}, "cluster_id": "CHS136", "cluster_text": "Mr Jarrah Bennell, 31, is a Wiradjuri man from a town several hours from the rehabilitation unit. He is six weeks after a motor vehicle crash with C6 tetraplegia (ASIA impairment scale A). Assessment on the spinal ward: unable to cough effectively on request, abdominal muscles paralysed, diaphragm contracts well on palpation, paradoxical upper chest movement on inspiration, coarse crackles and reduced air entry at the right base, audible secretions he cannot clear. RR 22, HR 62, BP 98/58 supine, SpO2 94% on room air. He becomes dizzy on sitting up and wears an abdominal binder in the chair. He has an indwelling urinary catheter. His mother and an uncle visit most days. His uncle usually sits in on discussions, and staff have mostly directed questions to his mother, while Mr Bennell is quiet and looks away. The hospital Aboriginal Liaison Officer is available today.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use a gentle costal assisted cough with positioning, avoiding abdominal thrusts until the meal settles): He needs help to clear secretions now because his saturation is falling, but an abdominal thrust soon after a meal risks regurgitation and aspiration, so a costal technique with suitable positioning is the safer way to assist. Waiting for the next session leaves a worsening problem untreated. A head down position is poorly tolerated after a meal and with his low blood pressure."}, {"id": 1504, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "During a sitting session, Mr Bennell develops a pounding headache, flushing and sweating above the level of injury, and goose bumps below it. BP is 168/102, though his usual is about 98/58. What should the physiotherapist do first in response?", "options": {"A": "Lie him flat with his legs raised to restore pressure, then ask a nurse to attend", "B": "Stop the session, place him in side lying, and recheck his blood pressure every few minutes", "C": "Keep him upright, loosen the binder, check his catheter for blockage, and call a nurse", "D": "Hoist him back to bed to rest flat, then ask a nurse to check his observations"}, "cluster_id": "CHS136", "cluster_text": "Mr Jarrah Bennell, 31, is a Wiradjuri man from a town several hours from the rehabilitation unit. He is six weeks after a motor vehicle crash with C6 tetraplegia (ASIA impairment scale A). Assessment on the spinal ward: unable to cough effectively on request, abdominal muscles paralysed, diaphragm contracts well on palpation, paradoxical upper chest movement on inspiration, coarse crackles and reduced air entry at the right base, audible secretions he cannot clear. RR 22, HR 62, BP 98/58 supine, SpO2 94% on room air. He becomes dizzy on sitting up and wears an abdominal binder in the chair. He has an indwelling urinary catheter. His mother and an uncle visit most days. His uncle usually sits in on discussions, and staff have mostly directed questions to his mother, while Mr Bennell is quiet and looks away. The hospital Aboriginal Liaison Officer is available today.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Keep him upright, loosen the binder, check his catheter for blockage, and call a nurse): These features and a pressure far above his baseline indicate autonomic dysreflexia, a medical emergency in injuries at this level. Staying upright helps lower the pressure, and the physiotherapist removes likely triggers such as a tight binder or blocked catheter while calling for help. Lying flat raises pressure further, and monitoring or moving him without removing triggers delays treatment."}, {"id": 1505, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Rajinder finds the low lounge chair and toilet difficult, and his hip bends beyond a right angle whenever he sits down or rises. He is on day 10 after surgery with six weeks of precautions ahead. Which modification best supports his posterior hip precautions?", "options": {"A": "Raise seats with firm cushions and a toilet raiser, keeping hips above knees", "B": "Keep the low lounge chair and add a pillow behind his back to support posture", "C": "Place a soft low cushion on the seat and sit with knees apart, toes pointing outwards", "D": "Replace the lounge chair with a stool, letting him lean forward on his frame to stand"}, "cluster_id": "CHS137", "cluster_text": "Rajinder Singh, 71, retired bus driver, is on day 10 after a right total hip replacement for osteoarthritis through a posterior approach. He lives with his wife in a two-storey home with the bedroom upstairs. The surgeon ordered posterior hip precautions for six weeks and weight bearing as tolerated. He has type 2 diabetes and takes prescribed anticoagulant tablets to prevent clots. At today's community home visit the wound is dry with steri-strips and there is bruising over the thigh. Active right hip flexion is 80 degrees, abduction strength is 3/5, and pain is 3/10. He walks with a two-wheeled frame. Both calves are soft and non-tender. He sits in a low lounge chair, uses a low toilet, and is worried about the bruising. He asks whether he can stop the tablets.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Raise seats with firm cushions and a toilet raiser, keeping hips above knees): Posterior precautions limit hip flexion beyond about a right angle, adduction past midline and internal rotation, so firm raised seating with a toilet raiser keeps the hip in a safe range. A pillow behind his back does not raise the seat, and a soft low cushion compresses and leaves the hips below the knees. Leaning forward from a stool pushes the hip into deep flexion."}, {"id": 1506, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Rajinder wants to climb the stairs to his bedroom for the first time, using one crutch and the handrail, now that his abductor strength is 3/5 on the operated side. Which technique is correct for stair climbing after his hip replacement?", "options": {"A": "Lead with the right leg going up and the left leg going down", "B": "Lead with the right leg in both directions while the left follows it", "C": "Lead with the left leg going up and the right leg going down", "D": "Lead with the left leg in both directions while the right follows it"}, "cluster_id": "CHS137", "cluster_text": "Rajinder Singh, 71, retired bus driver, is on day 10 after a right total hip replacement for osteoarthritis through a posterior approach. He lives with his wife in a two-storey home with the bedroom upstairs. The surgeon ordered posterior hip precautions for six weeks and weight bearing as tolerated. He has type 2 diabetes and takes prescribed anticoagulant tablets to prevent clots. At today's community home visit the wound is dry with steri-strips and there is bruising over the thigh. Active right hip flexion is 80 degrees, abduction strength is 3/5, and pain is 3/10. He walks with a two-wheeled frame. Both calves are soft and non-tender. He sits in a low lounge chair, uses a low toilet, and is worried about the bruising. He asks whether he can stop the tablets.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Lead with the left leg going up and the right leg going down): The stronger leg leads up and the operated leg leads down, so the stronger leg does the lifting and lowering work. The reversed pattern places the demand on the weaker operated hip. Leading with the same leg in both directions ignores this principle."}, {"id": 1507, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Rajinder is worried about the bruising on his thigh and asks the physiotherapist whether he can stop his anticoagulant tablets. His wife is listening and wants an answer today, so he can decide before the weekend. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Explain that medication decisions are outside physiotherapy scope, and refer him to his GP", "B": "Advise stopping the tablets for now because bruising is mild and the clot risk has passed", "C": "Reassure him that bruising is expected after surgery and tell him to keep taking the tablets", "D": "Telephone his pharmacist and ask for the dose to be reduced until bruising settles"}, "cluster_id": "CHS137", "cluster_text": "Rajinder Singh, 71, retired bus driver, is on day 10 after a right total hip replacement for osteoarthritis through a posterior approach. He lives with his wife in a two-storey home with the bedroom upstairs. The surgeon ordered posterior hip precautions for six weeks and weight bearing as tolerated. He has type 2 diabetes and takes prescribed anticoagulant tablets to prevent clots. At today's community home visit the wound is dry with steri-strips and there is bruising over the thigh. Active right hip flexion is 80 degrees, abduction strength is 3/5, and pain is 3/10. He walks with a two-wheeled frame. Both calves are soft and non-tender. He sits in a low lounge chair, uses a low toilet, and is worried about the bruising. He asks whether he can stop the tablets.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Explain that medication decisions are outside physiotherapy scope, and refer him to his GP): Physiotherapists do not advise on starting, stopping or altering medicines, so the right step is to refer him promptly to the prescriber and record the discussion. Telling him to stop could expose him to clot risk. Reassurance alone and asking a pharmacist to change the dose both pass over the prescriber's role."}, {"id": 1508, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "At the next visit Rajinder reports right calf pain on walking. The calf is warm, tender and 3 cm larger than the left, and he feels slightly breathless at rest. What is the most appropriate action for the physiotherapist to take now?", "options": {"A": "Stop walking and elevate the leg with ankle pumps, then monitor swelling until the next visit", "B": "Stop exercise, rest him, and arrange same-day medical assessment, with an ambulance if worse", "C": "Stop exercise and ask his wife to drive him to the GP the next morning", "D": "Apply a compression bandage with elevation, review in two days, and continue walking meanwhile"}, "cluster_id": "CHS137", "cluster_text": "Rajinder Singh, 71, retired bus driver, is on day 10 after a right total hip replacement for osteoarthritis through a posterior approach. He lives with his wife in a two-storey home with the bedroom upstairs. The surgeon ordered posterior hip precautions for six weeks and weight bearing as tolerated. He has type 2 diabetes and takes prescribed anticoagulant tablets to prevent clots. At today's community home visit the wound is dry with steri-strips and there is bruising over the thigh. Active right hip flexion is 80 degrees, abduction strength is 3/5, and pain is 3/10. He walks with a two-wheeled frame. Both calves are soft and non-tender. He sits in a low lounge chair, uses a low toilet, and is worried about the bruising. He asks whether he can stop the tablets.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stop exercise, rest him, and arrange same-day medical assessment, with an ambulance if worse): The warm, swollen, tender calf with breathlessness after hip surgery raises concern for deep vein thrombosis and possible pulmonary embolism, which needs exercise stopped and same day medical assessment, with an ambulance if he worsens. Elevation and ankle pumps, a bandage with continued walking, or waiting until the next morning all leave a potentially life threatening problem unassessed."}, {"id": 1509, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Morgan has a C6 AIS A injury and a forced vital capacity of 40 percent predicted. He takes a reasonable deep breath in but cannot generate an effective cough or clear the secretions in his right lower zone. Which explanation is best?", "options": {"A": "Paralysed abdominal and intercostal muscles cannot generate the forceful expiration an effective cough needs", "B": "Loss of the cough reflex after cervical injury means secretions are not sensed or expelled effectively", "C": "Phrenic nerve paralysis removes diaphragm function, so he cannot take the deep inspiration a cough needs", "D": "Lower lobe collapse has weakened his diaphragm, which is the main cause of his weak cough"}, "cluster_id": "CHS138", "cluster_text": "Mr Brandon Morgan, 34, a Wiradjuri man, sustained a C6 AIS A spinal cord injury eight months ago and has returned to his home town in regional New South Wales, living with his aunt. He reports a weak cough, thick sputum he cannot clear and two chest infections this year. Today: wet sounding breathing, coarse crackles in the right lower zone, RR 18, SpO2 95 percent on room air, afebrile. His forced vital capacity is 40 percent predicted and he takes a reasonable breath in but cannot cough effectively. Blood pressure supine 118/70, sitting in his wheelchair 96/58 with dizziness. He has an indwelling urinary catheter. An Aboriginal health worker from the local Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service attends with his aunt. Mr Morgan answers briefly and avoids eye contact.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Paralysed abdominal and intercostal muscles cannot generate the forceful expiration an effective cough needs): The diaphragm is supplied from C3 to C5 and is largely working at C6, so inspiration is possible, but the expiratory abdominal and intercostal muscles are paralysed, leaving a weak expiratory force. The cough reflex pathway is not lost by the injury itself. Atelectasis may follow the weak cough rather than cause it."}, {"id": 1510, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Morgan is lying supine, has an empty stomach and reports no abdominal pain. You plan a manually assisted cough once he has taken a deep breath and attempts to cough. Which technique is most appropriate for timing and hand placement?", "options": {"A": "Percuss firmly with a cupped hand over the right lower zone to prompt a spontaneous cough", "B": "Apply firm bilateral costal pressure during inspiration to increase his tidal volume before the cough begins", "C": "Apply an upward thrust over the xiphoid process during inspiration so the diaphragm can descend further", "D": "Apply an inward and upward abdominal thrust below the ribs as he begins to cough"}, "cluster_id": "CHS138", "cluster_text": "Mr Brandon Morgan, 34, a Wiradjuri man, sustained a C6 AIS A spinal cord injury eight months ago and has returned to his home town in regional New South Wales, living with his aunt. He reports a weak cough, thick sputum he cannot clear and two chest infections this year. Today: wet sounding breathing, coarse crackles in the right lower zone, RR 18, SpO2 95 percent on room air, afebrile. His forced vital capacity is 40 percent predicted and he takes a reasonable breath in but cannot cough effectively. Blood pressure supine 118/70, sitting in his wheelchair 96/58 with dizziness. He has an indwelling urinary catheter. An Aboriginal health worker from the local Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service attends with his aunt. Mr Morgan answers briefly and avoids eye contact.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Apply an inward and upward abdominal thrust below the ribs as he begins to cough): An assisted cough replaces the missing abdominal muscle force, so pressure is applied below the ribs, directed inward and upward, and timed with the expiratory phase of the cough. Pressure during inspiration works against the breath, and pressure over the xiphoid risks injury. Percussion does not substitute for expiratory force."}, {"id": 1511, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Morgan feels dizzy when he is sat upright in his wheelchair, with a blood pressure of 96/58 compared with 118/70 supine. His catheter drains freely and his skin is intact. He needs further treatment for secretion clearance. How should you proceed?", "options": {"A": "Position head down to drain the right lower zone, which also lifts his blood pressure", "B": "Recline him with an abdominal binder in place and monitor blood pressure and symptoms during treatment", "C": "Keep him upright because sitting gives the best cough effort, continuing if the dizziness is mild", "D": "Lie him supine without a binder to maximise diaphragm position, and check blood pressure afterwards"}, "cluster_id": "CHS138", "cluster_text": "Mr Brandon Morgan, 34, a Wiradjuri man, sustained a C6 AIS A spinal cord injury eight months ago and has returned to his home town in regional New South Wales, living with his aunt. He reports a weak cough, thick sputum he cannot clear and two chest infections this year. Today: wet sounding breathing, coarse crackles in the right lower zone, RR 18, SpO2 95 percent on room air, afebrile. His forced vital capacity is 40 percent predicted and he takes a reasonable breath in but cannot cough effectively. Blood pressure supine 118/70, sitting in his wheelchair 96/58 with dizziness. He has an indwelling urinary catheter. An Aboriginal health worker from the local Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service attends with his aunt. Mr Morgan answers briefly and avoids eye contact.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Recline him with an abdominal binder in place and monitor blood pressure and symptoms during treatment): Postural hypotension is common after cervical injury, so reclining and using an abdominal binder support blood pressure and the diaphragm while treatment continues with monitoring. Treating through dizziness risks syncope. Removing the binder and delaying measurement, or relying on head down tilt, does not provide a safe, monitored approach."}, {"id": 1512, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Morgan answers briefly, avoids direct eye contact and glances at his aunt when you ask questions. An Aboriginal health worker is present. You need to agree on a treatment plan and consent. Which approach is most culturally safe and respectful here?", "options": {"A": "Explain that eye contact is expected in healthcare, then complete the standard assessment and consent quickly", "B": "Postpone consent until an Aboriginal liaison officer can attend and lead the whole consultation", "C": "Direct questions and consent discussions to his aunt as family spokesperson to keep things efficient", "D": "Allow time, ask how he wants his aunt and health worker involved, and share decisions"}, "cluster_id": "CHS138", "cluster_text": "Mr Brandon Morgan, 34, a Wiradjuri man, sustained a C6 AIS A spinal cord injury eight months ago and has returned to his home town in regional New South Wales, living with his aunt. He reports a weak cough, thick sputum he cannot clear and two chest infections this year. Today: wet sounding breathing, coarse crackles in the right lower zone, RR 18, SpO2 95 percent on room air, afebrile. His forced vital capacity is 40 percent predicted and he takes a reasonable breath in but cannot cough effectively. Blood pressure supine 118/70, sitting in his wheelchair 96/58 with dizziness. He has an indwelling urinary catheter. An Aboriginal health worker from the local Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service attends with his aunt. Mr Morgan answers briefly and avoids eye contact.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Allow time, ask how he wants his aunt and health worker involved, and share decisions): Culturally safe care respects the person's own choices, so he is asked how he wants others involved and decisions are shared at an unhurried pace. Avoiding eye contact can be a sign of respect and should not be assumed to mean disengagement. Handing consent to relatives or delaying care removes his say in decisions."}, {"id": 1513, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Papadopoulos has palpable pedal pulses, symptoms provoked by lumbar extension and heaviness that eases with sitting. Which feature of his presentation most strongly supports neurogenic claudication rather than vascular claudication as the cause of his walking-related leg symptoms in this assessment?", "options": {"A": "Symptoms starting at a consistent walking distance on level and sloped ground alike", "B": "Calf cramping pain with cool shiny skin and hair loss over the lower legs", "C": "Settling within a minute of standing still without any need to sit down", "D": "Relief with trunk flexion, which enlarges the spinal canal and lateral recesses"}, "cluster_id": "CHS139", "cluster_text": "Gerald Papadopoulos, 71, a retired baker, attends private practice with 8 months of bilateral buttock and thigh heaviness when walking. Symptoms start after about 200 metres, ease within a few minutes of sitting or leaning on a shopping trolley, and are worse walking downhill. He can ride a stationary bike for 30 minutes without symptoms. Medical history: hypertension, well controlled, and type 2 diabetes. Examination: lumbar extension reproduces thigh heaviness and flexion relieves it; pedal pulses are palpable and symmetrical; reflexes and myotomes are normal; straight leg raise is negative; walking tolerance is 5 minutes on the clinic treadmill. It is a busy afternoon, and your next patient arrives in 10 minutes.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Relief with trunk flexion, which enlarges the spinal canal and lateral recesses): Positional relief with flexion and aggravation with extension and downhill walking reflect canal and foramen narrowing, which is typical of neurogenic claudication. Vascular claudication follows a consistent exertional distance, settles with standing rest and is accompanied by trophic and pulse changes. Palpable pulses make the vascular pattern unlikely."}, {"id": 1514, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Examination suggests lumbar spinal stenosis with neurogenic claudication, and he has no neurological deficit or red flags. He wants to keep walking with his wife and avoid surgery for now if possible. Which physiotherapy programme is the most appropriate to begin?", "options": {"A": "Central posterior-anterior mobilisations in prone to widen the narrowed canal, then walking as tolerated", "B": "Flexion-biased exercises and positioning, with graded walking using planned rests in flexed postures", "C": "Repeated lumbar extension in standing before each walk to centralise the thigh heaviness", "D": "Passive lumbar traction and avoidance of walking until the thigh symptoms have fully resolved"}, "cluster_id": "CHS139", "cluster_text": "Gerald Papadopoulos, 71, a retired baker, attends private practice with 8 months of bilateral buttock and thigh heaviness when walking. Symptoms start after about 200 metres, ease within a few minutes of sitting or leaning on a shopping trolley, and are worse walking downhill. He can ride a stationary bike for 30 minutes without symptoms. Medical history: hypertension, well controlled, and type 2 diabetes. Examination: lumbar extension reproduces thigh heaviness and flexion relieves it; pedal pulses are palpable and symmetrical; reflexes and myotomes are normal; straight leg raise is negative; walking tolerance is 5 minutes on the clinic treadmill. It is a busy afternoon, and your next patient arrives in 10 minutes.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Flexion-biased exercises and positioning, with graded walking using planned rests in flexed postures): Flexion increases canal space, so flexion-biased exercise and positioned rests let him walk further, and graded walking maintains fitness. Extension and prone mobilisations load the narrowed segments and are likely to provoke symptoms. Avoiding walking would deteriorate his fitness and diabetes control without addressing the cause."}, {"id": 1515, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Papadopoulos's walking tolerance has improved to 12 minutes. He asks which exercise to add for fitness and weight control, as his diabetes is a concern. Considering his spinal condition and symptoms, which form of aerobic activity is most appropriate to prioritise?", "options": {"A": "Upright cross trainer sessions with a tall posture to build endurance without leg impact", "B": "Breaststroke swimming with the head up, since water unloads the spine during sustained exercise", "C": "Brisk downhill walking on bush trails, which lengthens stride and builds tolerance quickly", "D": "Stationary or recumbent cycling, progressing duration, because flexion reduces canal narrowing"}, "cluster_id": "CHS139", "cluster_text": "Gerald Papadopoulos, 71, a retired baker, attends private practice with 8 months of bilateral buttock and thigh heaviness when walking. Symptoms start after about 200 metres, ease within a few minutes of sitting or leaning on a shopping trolley, and are worse walking downhill. He can ride a stationary bike for 30 minutes without symptoms. Medical history: hypertension, well controlled, and type 2 diabetes. Examination: lumbar extension reproduces thigh heaviness and flexion relieves it; pedal pulses are palpable and symmetrical; reflexes and myotomes are normal; straight leg raise is negative; walking tolerance is 5 minutes on the clinic treadmill. It is a busy afternoon, and your next patient arrives in 10 minutes.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stationary or recumbent cycling, progressing duration, because flexion reduces canal narrowing): Cycling keeps the lumbar spine flexed, which he already tolerates, so he can accumulate enough aerobic volume for fitness and glycaemic control. Breaststroke with the head up holds the lumbar spine in extension. Downhill walking is already known to worsen his symptoms, and an upright cross trainer posture extends the lumbar spine."}, {"id": 1516, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Before the next patient arrives in 10 minutes, the physiotherapist has three tasks: finish Mr Papadopoulos's notes, order new resistance bands, and return a call from a GP about a post-operative knee patient with new calf swelling and breathlessness. Which task should be done first?", "options": {"A": "Finish Mr Papadopoulos's notes first while his findings are fresh, then call the GP between patients", "B": "Ask reception to tell the GP a physiotherapist will call back in about an hour", "C": "Advise the GP by text message to arrange a review, then continue with the notes", "D": "Return the GP call now, as the reported symptoms need urgent medical assessment"}, "cluster_id": "CHS139", "cluster_text": "Gerald Papadopoulos, 71, a retired baker, attends private practice with 8 months of bilateral buttock and thigh heaviness when walking. Symptoms start after about 200 metres, ease within a few minutes of sitting or leaning on a shopping trolley, and are worse walking downhill. He can ride a stationary bike for 30 minutes without symptoms. Medical history: hypertension, well controlled, and type 2 diabetes. Examination: lumbar extension reproduces thigh heaviness and flexion relieves it; pedal pulses are palpable and symmetrical; reflexes and myotomes are normal; straight leg raise is negative; walking tolerance is 5 minutes on the clinic treadmill. It is a busy afternoon, and your next patient arrives in 10 minutes.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Return the GP call now, as the reported symptoms need urgent medical assessment): New calf swelling and breathlessness after surgery may signal a thrombosis or embolus, so the safety critical call takes priority over administrative work. Notes can be finished once the call is made. Deferring the call by an hour, or sending a text instead of speaking to the GP, delays an urgent clinical decision and the exchange of information it needs."}, {"id": 1517, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Sinclair has walked 30 metres and the physiotherapist is watching the telemetry trace throughout. Considering his atrial fibrillation history and current medications, which ECG change during activity is a standard clinical criterion for stopping the session safely and calling nursing staff?", "options": {"A": "Sinus tachycardia that rises steadily with walking speed and falls promptly once he slows", "B": "Isolated premature atrial beats that settle within seconds once he pauses to rest", "C": "New sustained rapid irregular rhythm with a sudden rise in heart rate and light-headedness", "D": "Breathlessness of Borg 4 with SpO2 falling from 95 to 93 percent, recovering with rest"}, "cluster_id": "CHS140", "cluster_text": "Mr Glenn Sinclair, 78, retired bus driver, is on day 3 of admission for community acquired pneumonia. Past history: paroxysmal atrial fibrillation, hypertension; he takes an anticoagulant and a beta blocker. Today he is alert and wants to walk. Resting telemetry: sinus rhythm, HR 74, BP 130/78, RR 18, SpO2 95% on room air, temperature 37.2. Chest auscultation: reduced air entry at the right base, no new wheeze. Yesterday he walked 20 metres with a frame at Borg breathlessness 3. The ward has a local mobility guideline that sets heart rate limits for exercise. A junior colleague has quoted a single small trial to argue that the limits should be raised for patients taking beta blockers. The physiotherapist plans a corridor walk with the telemetry trace visible at the nursing station.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (New sustained rapid irregular rhythm with a sudden rise in heart rate and light-headedness): The new sustained fast irregular rhythm with a sudden rate rise and light headedness suggests rapid atrial fibrillation with reduced cardiac output, so exercise stops and nursing staff are told. The sinus rate that rises with workload is an expected response, and isolated ectopic beats that settle are common. The small, brief saturation dip that recovers with rest is a reason to monitor closely, not a standard stopping criterion."}, {"id": 1518, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Telemetry now shows a fast irregular rhythm at about 140 beats per minute. Mr Sinclair feels light-headed and pale, and his blood pressure falls to 92/58. He is standing beside the walking frame in the corridor. What should the physiotherapist do first to keep him safe?", "options": {"A": "Seat him in the nearby chair, stop activity, and ask a nurse to review him", "B": "Encourage slow deep breathing while he stands, and continue to the end of the corridor", "C": "Reduce the walking speed and monitor for one minute before deciding whether to carry on", "D": "Walk him back to the bedside slowly with a nurse beside him, then recheck his pressure"}, "cluster_id": "CHS140", "cluster_text": "Mr Glenn Sinclair, 78, retired bus driver, is on day 3 of admission for community acquired pneumonia. Past history: paroxysmal atrial fibrillation, hypertension; he takes an anticoagulant and a beta blocker. Today he is alert and wants to walk. Resting telemetry: sinus rhythm, HR 74, BP 130/78, RR 18, SpO2 95% on room air, temperature 37.2. Chest auscultation: reduced air entry at the right base, no new wheeze. Yesterday he walked 20 metres with a frame at Borg breathlessness 3. The ward has a local mobility guideline that sets heart rate limits for exercise. A junior colleague has quoted a single small trial to argue that the limits should be raised for patients taking beta blockers. The physiotherapist plans a corridor walk with the telemetry trace visible at the nursing station.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Seat him in the nearby chair, stop activity, and ask a nurse to review him): Symptomatic hypotension with a fast irregular rhythm means he must stop and be seated at once, with the responsible nurse or doctor reviewing him. Walking him further, or waiting to see whether it settles, delays care and risks a faint. Breathing cues do not address the circulatory problem."}, {"id": 1519, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Next morning he is back in sinus rhythm at rest, HR 70, BP 126/74, and the team has changed his medication after the event. He asks to walk again. The ward guideline lists heart rate limits. What is the most appropriate approach to the next session?", "options": {"A": "Avoid walking until the anticoagulant dose is stable, and continue bed exercises meanwhile with nursing review", "B": "Confirm medical clearance and any new exercise parameters, then restart with short walks under telemetry", "C": "Apply the ward guideline heart rate limits unchanged and add a blood pressure check afterwards", "D": "Resume the previous walking distance since his resting rhythm and observations have returned to baseline"}, "cluster_id": "CHS140", "cluster_text": "Mr Glenn Sinclair, 78, retired bus driver, is on day 3 of admission for community acquired pneumonia. Past history: paroxysmal atrial fibrillation, hypertension; he takes an anticoagulant and a beta blocker. Today he is alert and wants to walk. Resting telemetry: sinus rhythm, HR 74, BP 130/78, RR 18, SpO2 95% on room air, temperature 37.2. Chest auscultation: reduced air entry at the right base, no new wheeze. Yesterday he walked 20 metres with a frame at Borg breathlessness 3. The ward has a local mobility guideline that sets heart rate limits for exercise. A junior colleague has quoted a single small trial to argue that the limits should be raised for patients taking beta blockers. The physiotherapist plans a corridor walk with the telemetry trace visible at the nursing station.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Confirm medical clearance and any new exercise parameters, then restart with short walks under telemetry): After an arrhythmia with haemodynamic effects and a medication change, the physiotherapist should confirm clearance and any revised limits with the medical team and restart cautiously with monitoring. A normal resting rhythm does not show he will tolerate his earlier workload. The old guideline limits may no longer suit his changed medication, and withholding walking is unnecessary once cleared."}, {"id": 1520, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "A junior colleague quotes one small trial to argue that the ward's heart rate limits should be raised for patients taking beta blockers. The physiotherapist wants to decide whether practice should change. What is the best next step in using the evidence?", "options": {"A": "Ask the colleague to apply the higher limits with one patient first and record the outcome", "B": "Appraise the trial's quality and fit to this ward, then discuss changes through the team", "C": "Set the trial aside because single studies cannot inform practice, and follow the written guideline", "D": "Adopt the trial's higher limits for beta blocker patients because the results look promising"}, "cluster_id": "CHS140", "cluster_text": "Mr Glenn Sinclair, 78, retired bus driver, is on day 3 of admission for community acquired pneumonia. Past history: paroxysmal atrial fibrillation, hypertension; he takes an anticoagulant and a beta blocker. Today he is alert and wants to walk. Resting telemetry: sinus rhythm, HR 74, BP 130/78, RR 18, SpO2 95% on room air, temperature 37.2. Chest auscultation: reduced air entry at the right base, no new wheeze. Yesterday he walked 20 metres with a frame at Borg breathlessness 3. The ward has a local mobility guideline that sets heart rate limits for exercise. A junior colleague has quoted a single small trial to argue that the limits should be raised for patients taking beta blockers. The physiotherapist plans a corridor walk with the telemetry trace visible at the nursing station.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Appraise the trial's quality and fit to this ward, then discuss changes through the team): Evidence informed practice means appraising the design, size, bias and applicability of the study, then taking any proposed change through the ward's guideline process with the team. Adopting findings from one small trial risks harm, and trialling it on a patient bypasses governance. Dismissing the study outright also fails, because it may be worth appraising for a later guideline review."}, {"id": 1521, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "On examination of his right arm, Mr Dang has a reduced biceps reflex, 4/5 wrist extension and reduced thumb pulp sensation. Which nerve root level do these findings most closely indicate in this patient, and therefore what should be rechecked at each review to monitor neurological change?", "options": {"A": "C5 nerve root: recheck shoulder abduction strength and lateral upper arm sensation each visit", "B": "C7 nerve root: recheck triceps strength and middle finger sensation along with the triceps reflex", "C": "C6 nerve root: recheck wrist extension strength and thumb sensation each visit", "D": "C8 nerve root: recheck finger flexor and intrinsic hand strength and little finger sensation"}, "cluster_id": "CHS141", "cluster_text": "Mr Long Dang, 47, is a call-centre team leader who smokes about 10 cigarettes a day. He has had 3 weeks of right neck and arm pain after a heavy deadline period, with tingling in his thumb and index finger. Arm pain is 7/10, worse looking up and turning right, and eased with his right hand resting on his head. Right cervical rotation is 40 degrees and reproduces arm pain. Spurling's test is positive on the right and manual distraction relieves symptoms. Biceps reflex is reduced on the right, wrist extension is 4/5, triceps and grip are 5/5, and thumb pulp sensation is reduced. Hoffmann's sign is negative, gait is normal and there are no bladder or bilateral symptoms. Neck Disability Index is 22 out of 50.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (C6 nerve root: recheck wrist extension strength and thumb sensation each visit): Reduced biceps reflex, wrist extension weakness and thumb sensory loss map to C6, so these are the signs that best track change in that root. C5 would affect shoulder abduction and lateral upper arm sensation, and the preserved triceps and grip argue against C7 or C8 involvement."}, {"id": 1522, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Dang's arm pain is irritable, distraction eases it, and his neurological deficit is mild with no upper motor neurone signs. At the first session, which treatment approach is most appropriate to begin with while neurological status is monitored each visit?", "options": {"A": "Sustained end-range cervical traction with the arm in the provoking position to centralise the symptoms", "B": "Progressive loaded strengthening of the deep neck flexors and scapular muscles, starting from the first session", "C": "Education, symptom-easing positions and gentle active neck movement within comfortable symptom tolerance", "D": "High-velocity rotation manipulation of the lower cervical spine to restore rotation and settle arm pain"}, "cluster_id": "CHS141", "cluster_text": "Mr Long Dang, 47, is a call-centre team leader who smokes about 10 cigarettes a day. He has had 3 weeks of right neck and arm pain after a heavy deadline period, with tingling in his thumb and index finger. Arm pain is 7/10, worse looking up and turning right, and eased with his right hand resting on his head. Right cervical rotation is 40 degrees and reproduces arm pain. Spurling's test is positive on the right and manual distraction relieves symptoms. Biceps reflex is reduced on the right, wrist extension is 4/5, triceps and grip are 5/5, and thumb pulp sensation is reduced. Hoffmann's sign is negative, gait is normal and there are no bladder or bilateral symptoms. Neck Disability Index is 22 out of 50.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Education, symptom-easing positions and gentle active neck movement within comfortable symptom tolerance): With an irritable, mild radiculopathy and no upper motor neurone signs, education, easing positions and gentle active movement settle symptoms while allowing neurological monitoring. Sustained end-range traction or manipulation risks aggravating an irritable root, and loaded strengthening is better introduced once irritability has settled."}, {"id": 1523, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Dang asks what he can do himself between visits. He sits for most of his shift at a desk with a laptop screen set low, and smokes about ten cigarettes daily. Which advice best supports self-management and long-term recovery while his symptoms are settling?", "options": {"A": "Wear a soft collar for several weeks and avoid neck movement until arm symptoms resolve", "B": "Raise the screen, break up sitting often and offer smoking cessation advice and support", "C": "Begin graded neck and shoulder strengthening now, increasing to heavy overhead loads within the week", "D": "Take two weeks off desk work to rest the neck, then return gradually to usual duties"}, "cluster_id": "CHS141", "cluster_text": "Mr Long Dang, 47, is a call-centre team leader who smokes about 10 cigarettes a day. He has had 3 weeks of right neck and arm pain after a heavy deadline period, with tingling in his thumb and index finger. Arm pain is 7/10, worse looking up and turning right, and eased with his right hand resting on his head. Right cervical rotation is 40 degrees and reproduces arm pain. Spurling's test is positive on the right and manual distraction relieves symptoms. Biceps reflex is reduced on the right, wrist extension is 4/5, triceps and grip are 5/5, and thumb pulp sensation is reduced. Hoffmann's sign is negative, gait is normal and there are no bladder or bilateral symptoms. Neck Disability Index is 22 out of 50.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Raise the screen, break up sitting often and offer smoking cessation advice and support): Workstation changes, breaking up sitting and support to stop smoking address the modifiable factors behind recovery and recurrence, which is the core of self-management here. A collar and prolonged rest encourage stiffness and dependence. Strengthening is useful later, but rapid progression to heavy overhead loading is premature for an irritable cervical root."}, {"id": 1524, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "At review in week 2, Mr Dang reports new difficulty gripping his coffee cup, his wrist extension is now 3/5 and the biceps reflex is absent. Pain has eased to 4/10 since the first visit. What is the most appropriate management decision?", "options": {"A": "Add sustained cervical traction and neural mobilisation to target the weakness, rechecking strength next week", "B": "Continue the programme as pain is easing, and recheck strength and reflexes in a month.", "C": "Contact the GP promptly to review the progressing motor deficit and need for imaging", "D": "Begin resisted wrist extensor strengthening to counter the weakness and review progress again in two weeks"}, "cluster_id": "CHS141", "cluster_text": "Mr Long Dang, 47, is a call-centre team leader who smokes about 10 cigarettes a day. He has had 3 weeks of right neck and arm pain after a heavy deadline period, with tingling in his thumb and index finger. Arm pain is 7/10, worse looking up and turning right, and eased with his right hand resting on his head. Right cervical rotation is 40 degrees and reproduces arm pain. Spurling's test is positive on the right and manual distraction relieves symptoms. Biceps reflex is reduced on the right, wrist extension is 4/5, triceps and grip are 5/5, and thumb pulp sensation is reduced. Hoffmann's sign is negative, gait is normal and there are no bladder or bilateral symptoms. Neck Disability Index is 22 out of 50.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Contact the GP promptly to review the progressing motor deficit and need for imaging): Wrist extension falling from 4/5 to 3/5 with an absent reflex shows progressing motor loss, which needs medical review even though pain is improving. Falling pain can accompany worsening nerve damage, so continuing or intensifying physiotherapy alone delays imaging and possible specialist input."}, {"id": 1525, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Observing Mr Lombardi's chest at rest, the physiotherapist sees the right lateral segment move inward during inspiration and outward during expiration while the rest of the chest wall moves normally. Which description best explains this movement and its effect on ventilation?", "options": {"A": "Diaphragmatic paralysis on the right causes paradoxical inward motion, which impairs the lower chest's expansion", "B": "Pain-related guarding restricts right rib expansion, causing asymmetric movement without structural instability of the chest wall", "C": "Subcutaneous emphysema stiffens the chest wall locally and pulls it inward during inspiration, reducing tidal volume", "D": "A flail segment moves paradoxically because it is detached from the chest wall, reducing effective ventilation"}, "cluster_id": "CHS142", "cluster_text": "Mr Dino Lombardi, 81, retired builder, lives with his wife and was independent. He fell from a ladder two days ago. Fractures of right ribs 4 to 9 with a flail segment, mild right pulmonary contusion, no pneumothorax on the latest chest X-ray. History: atrial fibrillation on an anticoagulant, type 2 diabetes. Analgesia: regular paracetamol and oxycodone as needed; the acute pain service is reviewing a regional block. Observations: RR 26, HR 98 irregular, BP 128/74, SpO2 92% on 4 L/min via Hudson mask. Shallow breathing, paradoxical movement of the right lateral chest, splinting with one hand, weak ineffective cough, crackles right base. Pain 8 out of 10 on coughing. The ward nurse has asked the physiotherapist about his oxycodone dose.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A flail segment moves paradoxically because it is detached from the chest wall, reducing effective ventilation): Adjacent ribs broken in two places leave a free segment that is drawn in by negative pleural pressure on inspiration and pushed out on expiration. This wastes tidal volume and increases work of breathing. Guarding reduces movement but does not reverse its direction, and the case gives no evidence of diaphragm paralysis or emphysema."}, {"id": 1526, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Pain is 8 out of 10 on coughing and Mr Lombardi refuses deep breathing. Analgesia was given an hour ago. SpO2 is 92% on supplemental oxygen. He is in high sitting and holds his right side with one hand. Which physiotherapy intervention is most appropriate for this treatment?", "options": {"A": "Use ten minutes of continuous positive expiratory pressure therapy, accepting some pain to gain sustained effort", "B": "Teach splinted deep breathing and supported cough with a pillow, in short, frequent sessions", "C": "Apply firm chest wall percussion over the fracture sites to loosen secretions before attempting any huffing", "D": "Lie him on the right side to splint fractures and defer breathing exercises until pain settles"}, "cluster_id": "CHS142", "cluster_text": "Mr Dino Lombardi, 81, retired builder, lives with his wife and was independent. He fell from a ladder two days ago. Fractures of right ribs 4 to 9 with a flail segment, mild right pulmonary contusion, no pneumothorax on the latest chest X-ray. History: atrial fibrillation on an anticoagulant, type 2 diabetes. Analgesia: regular paracetamol and oxycodone as needed; the acute pain service is reviewing a regional block. Observations: RR 26, HR 98 irregular, BP 128/74, SpO2 92% on 4 L/min via Hudson mask. Shallow breathing, paradoxical movement of the right lateral chest, splinting with one hand, weak ineffective cough, crackles right base. Pain 8 out of 10 on coughing. The ward nurse has asked the physiotherapist about his oxycodone dose.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Teach splinted deep breathing and supported cough with a pillow, in short, frequent sessions): Supported cough and splinted breathing with a pillow let him clear secretions with less pain, and short frequent sessions suit his fatigue and pain. Percussion over fractures is contraindicated. Pushing through pain reduces effort and cooperation, and lying on the injured side with no exercises allows basal collapse to worsen."}, {"id": 1527, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Six hours later Mr Lombardi is exhausted. Respiratory rate is 34, SpO2 is 88% despite increased oxygen, he is drowsy and the flail segment movement has increased. He can no longer perform his breathing exercises. What is the physiotherapist's most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Apply a firm chest binder around the flail segment and continue his planned breathing programme", "B": "Stop treatment and urgently escalate to the medical team and outreach service for respiratory support", "C": "Sit him upright, increase oxygen further, and review him again within thirty minutes before escalating", "D": "Start manually assisted cough over the fracture, then reassess saturation and respiratory rate in an hour"}, "cluster_id": "CHS142", "cluster_text": "Mr Dino Lombardi, 81, retired builder, lives with his wife and was independent. He fell from a ladder two days ago. Fractures of right ribs 4 to 9 with a flail segment, mild right pulmonary contusion, no pneumothorax on the latest chest X-ray. History: atrial fibrillation on an anticoagulant, type 2 diabetes. Analgesia: regular paracetamol and oxycodone as needed; the acute pain service is reviewing a regional block. Observations: RR 26, HR 98 irregular, BP 128/74, SpO2 92% on 4 L/min via Hudson mask. Shallow breathing, paradoxical movement of the right lateral chest, splinting with one hand, weak ineffective cough, crackles right base. Pain 8 out of 10 on coughing. The ward nurse has asked the physiotherapist about his oxycodone dose.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stop treatment and urgently escalate to the medical team and outreach service for respiratory support): Exhaustion, drowsiness, a very high respiratory rate and falling saturation despite oxygen indicate impending respiratory failure, which needs urgent medical and critical care input for ventilatory support. Further physiotherapy or delay adds risk. A tight binder restricts ventilation and is not recommended for flail chest."}, {"id": 1528, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "The ward nurse asks the physiotherapist to increase Mr Lombardi's oxycodone dose because pain limits his treatment, noting the physiotherapist knows his response best. The physiotherapist has not been given prescribing authority in this hospital. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Withhold all physiotherapy until the next ward round so the doctor can review his analgesia then", "B": "Pass the request to the allied health assistant, who can administer extra analgesia under physiotherapy delegation", "C": "Decline to alter the dose, and report his pain-limited treatment to the medical or pain team", "D": "Agree and suggest the dose to the nurse, since physiotherapists assess the functional effect of analgesia"}, "cluster_id": "CHS142", "cluster_text": "Mr Dino Lombardi, 81, retired builder, lives with his wife and was independent. He fell from a ladder two days ago. Fractures of right ribs 4 to 9 with a flail segment, mild right pulmonary contusion, no pneumothorax on the latest chest X-ray. History: atrial fibrillation on an anticoagulant, type 2 diabetes. Analgesia: regular paracetamol and oxycodone as needed; the acute pain service is reviewing a regional block. Observations: RR 26, HR 98 irregular, BP 128/74, SpO2 92% on 4 L/min via Hudson mask. Shallow breathing, paradoxical movement of the right lateral chest, splinting with one hand, weak ineffective cough, crackles right base. Pain 8 out of 10 on coughing. The ward nurse has asked the physiotherapist about his oxycodone dose.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Decline to alter the dose, and report his pain-limited treatment to the medical or pain team): Without prescribing authority, changing or recommending an opioid dose is outside the physiotherapist's scope. The role is to report how pain limits function so the treating or pain team can review promptly. Delegating drug administration to an assistant is also outside scope, and withholding treatment until the ward round delays care."}, {"id": 1529, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "A speaking valve trial is planned this afternoon with the speech pathologist. Leilani is alert and settled, with a strong cough and moderate secretions. Before applying the one-way valve to her tracheostomy, which check must the physiotherapist confirm to make the trial safe?", "options": {"A": "Her cough is strong and her secretions are thin, so the valve is safe to try", "B": "The cuff is inflated to seal the airway, so the valve sends air through the cords", "C": "No cuff is inflated, and she can exhale around the tube through her upper airway", "D": "Suction has just cleared the tube and humidification is ceased, so mucus cannot block the valve"}, "cluster_id": "CHS143", "cluster_text": "Leilani Fonoti, 6, is in the paediatric intensive care unit after prolonged ventilation for severe adenoviral pneumonia and now has a cuffless tracheostomy tube, placed three weeks ago. She breathes spontaneously through the tube with humidification. SpO2 97 percent in air, RR 24, HR 102. Her cough is strong, with moderate thin white secretions. The ENT team has confirmed a patent upper airway on assessment and the speech pathologist has requested a speaking valve trial with physiotherapy present. Her parents, who speak Samoan at home and some English, hope to take her home within weeks. A Samoan interpreter is available by phone. Her mother asks what she must learn to keep Leilani safe, and her father is worried about being blamed if something goes wrong.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (No cuff is inflated, and she can exhale around the tube through her upper airway): The one way valve closes on inspiration and blocks exhalation through the tube, so all exhaled air must pass around the tube and through the upper airway. That needs the cuff absent or fully deflated and a patent route, which ENT has confirmed. The strong cough and thin secretions are helpful but do not confirm exhalation is possible, an inflated cuff would cause dangerous obstruction, and stopping humidification does not make the trial safe."}, {"id": 1530, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Thirty seconds after the valve is placed, Leilani cannot voice, her work of breathing rises with chest retraction, her SpO2 falls to 92 percent and she appears frightened. The speech pathologist suggests encouraging her to persevere. What should the physiotherapist do next?", "options": {"A": "Remove the valve at once, reassess her breathing, and inform the team before any retrial", "B": "Switch to a heat moisture exchanger and continue the trial, because initial exhalation resistance is expected", "C": "Coach her to breathe out against the valve for a minute, as anxiety explains the difficulty", "D": "Suction through the tube and then reapply the valve, as secretions may be blocking exhaled airflow"}, "cluster_id": "CHS143", "cluster_text": "Leilani Fonoti, 6, is in the paediatric intensive care unit after prolonged ventilation for severe adenoviral pneumonia and now has a cuffless tracheostomy tube, placed three weeks ago. She breathes spontaneously through the tube with humidification. SpO2 97 percent in air, RR 24, HR 102. Her cough is strong, with moderate thin white secretions. The ENT team has confirmed a patent upper airway on assessment and the speech pathologist has requested a speaking valve trial with physiotherapy present. Her parents, who speak Samoan at home and some English, hope to take her home within weeks. A Samoan interpreter is available by phone. Her mother asks what she must learn to keep Leilani safe, and her father is worried about being blamed if something goes wrong.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Remove the valve at once, reassess her breathing, and inform the team before any retrial): Retraction, falling saturation and silence indicate she cannot exhale adequately past the tube, which is an airway safety problem. The valve must come off immediately so she can breathe through the tube, then the cause is reassessed with ENT. Persevering or reapplying the valve risks air trapping and further distress."}, {"id": 1531, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "A junior physiotherapist asks why a speaking valve must not be placed on a tracheostomy tube while its cuff is inflated, pointing out that the valve opens easily on inspiration. Which explanation of the risk to the child is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "The valve adds dead space, and with an inflated cuff carbon dioxide is retained", "B": "The valve closing raises cuff pressure sharply, which can injure the tracheal lining within minutes", "C": "Exhaled air cannot pass the inflated cuff and reach the upper airway, so exhalation is blocked", "D": "A cuff prevents the valve sealing on the tube hub, so air leaks at the connection"}, "cluster_id": "CHS143", "cluster_text": "Leilani Fonoti, 6, is in the paediatric intensive care unit after prolonged ventilation for severe adenoviral pneumonia and now has a cuffless tracheostomy tube, placed three weeks ago. She breathes spontaneously through the tube with humidification. SpO2 97 percent in air, RR 24, HR 102. Her cough is strong, with moderate thin white secretions. The ENT team has confirmed a patent upper airway on assessment and the speech pathologist has requested a speaking valve trial with physiotherapy present. Her parents, who speak Samoan at home and some English, hope to take her home within weeks. A Samoan interpreter is available by phone. Her mother asks what she must learn to keep Leilani safe, and her father is worried about being blamed if something goes wrong.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Exhaled air cannot pass the inflated cuff and reach the upper airway, so exhalation is blocked): An inflated cuff occupies the airway around the tube, so once the valve closes on expiration the air has nowhere to go and the child cannot breathe out. The valve's opening on inspiration is not the concern. Cuff injury, hub leaks and dead space are not the mechanism of this hazard."}, {"id": 1532, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Leilani's parents plan to manage her tracheostomy at home with community nursing support. Her mother speaks mainly Samoan and her father fears blame if an emergency occurs. Which teaching approach best supports safe, confident self-management for both parents before discharge?", "options": {"A": "One careful bedside demonstration of each skill, with refresher teaching if the parents report difficulty", "B": "Interpreter-supported hands-on practice of suction and emergency care by both parents, checked with teach-back", "C": "Training the mother as primary carer only, to avoid mixed instructions and added anxiety", "D": "Written and video resources in English and Samoan, with questions reviewed at the first community visit"}, "cluster_id": "CHS143", "cluster_text": "Leilani Fonoti, 6, is in the paediatric intensive care unit after prolonged ventilation for severe adenoviral pneumonia and now has a cuffless tracheostomy tube, placed three weeks ago. She breathes spontaneously through the tube with humidification. SpO2 97 percent in air, RR 24, HR 102. Her cough is strong, with moderate thin white secretions. The ENT team has confirmed a patent upper airway on assessment and the speech pathologist has requested a speaking valve trial with physiotherapy present. Her parents, who speak Samoan at home and some English, hope to take her home within weeks. A Samoan interpreter is available by phone. Her mother asks what she must learn to keep Leilani safe, and her father is worried about being blamed if something goes wrong.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Interpreter-supported hands-on practice of suction and emergency care by both parents, checked with teach-back): Competence in emergency tube care comes from supervised repeated practice by every carer, with an interpreter and teach-back confirming understanding. Written resources and single demonstrations rely on the parents to self-identify gaps. Training one parent leaves the family exposed when the other is alone with Leilani and may deepen the father's anxiety."}, {"id": 1533, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Sami's mouth opens to 30 mm with a click on opening and a softer click on closing. The jaw deviates to the right then returns to midline, and he has never locked. Which interpretation of this pattern is most consistent with these findings?", "options": {"A": "The disc stays displaced and blocks opening, which produces a closed lock with reduced range", "B": "Early osteoarthritic change in the joint surfaces, with the click arising from bony roughness", "C": "Capsular tightness restricting the right condyle, producing deviation to the affected side", "D": "The disc displaces then recaptures on opening, with masticatory muscle pain also contributing"}, "cluster_id": "CHS144", "cluster_text": "Sami Haddad, 15, a Year 10 student and school trumpet player, is referred by his dentist for right sided jaw pain lasting four months. He reports clicking when opening, pain when chewing tougher foods, and morning jaw tightness. His mother says he clenches his teeth during exams, which are coming up. Active mouth opening is 30 mm with a click on opening and a softer click on closing, and the jaw deviates to the right then returns to the midline. Palpation reproduces his pain in the right masseter and temporalis. Cervical rotation is full. There is no history of trauma, fever, swelling or locking. His dentist is considering a night splint. Sami attends with his mother and says he would like to understand what is going on.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The disc displaces then recaptures on opening, with masticatory muscle pain also contributing): A click on opening and a softer one on closing, with deviation that returns to midline, suggests a disc that displaces and then recaptures, with muscle pain adding to the picture. A displaced disc that blocks opening would limit range and lock the jaw, which he has not reported. Restriction from capsular tightness would leave the jaw deviated toward the affected side."}, {"id": 1534, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Sami's pain is mainly muscular, linked to clenching during study and exams, and his joint clicks on opening. His mother asks what he can do at home before any splint is fitted. Which advice is most appropriate as his initial self-management plan?", "options": {"A": "Use gentle jaw stretches with a finger assisted wide opening several times daily to improve range", "B": "Keep teeth apart at rest, eat softer foods, avoid gum chewing, and practise jaw relaxation", "C": "Keep the jaw still on a soft diet and avoid talking and yawning until pain settles", "D": "Wear a firm over the counter mouthguard day and night until the dentist fits a splint"}, "cluster_id": "CHS144", "cluster_text": "Sami Haddad, 15, a Year 10 student and school trumpet player, is referred by his dentist for right sided jaw pain lasting four months. He reports clicking when opening, pain when chewing tougher foods, and morning jaw tightness. His mother says he clenches his teeth during exams, which are coming up. Active mouth opening is 30 mm with a click on opening and a softer click on closing, and the jaw deviates to the right then returns to the midline. Palpation reproduces his pain in the right masseter and temporalis. Cervical rotation is full. There is no history of trauma, fever, swelling or locking. His dentist is considering a night splint. Sami attends with his mother and says he would like to understand what is going on.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Keep teeth apart at rest, eat softer foods, avoid gum chewing, and practise jaw relaxation): Resting with the teeth apart reduces clenching load, softer foods and no gum lessen strain, and relaxation addresses the stress driver. Forced stretching through a click aggravates irritable muscles. Complete jaw rest and avoiding all movement promotes stiffness, and a self-fitted guard worn constantly may increase clenching and should wait for the dentist's assessment."}, {"id": 1535, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "At review Sami has a warm, swollen right cheek in front of the ear, a temperature of 38.4 degrees Celsius and increasing jaw pain, and he cannot open past 20 mm. Symptoms began two days ago. What should the physiotherapist do next?", "options": {"A": "Apply gentle heat and ultrasound to the joint, then review the swelling in two days", "B": "Send his dentist an urgent letter describing the swelling, and pause jaw exercises meanwhile", "C": "Stop treatment and arrange same day medical review for possible infection of the joint", "D": "Advise rest, soft diet and simple pain relief, then recheck the swelling in three days"}, "cluster_id": "CHS144", "cluster_text": "Sami Haddad, 15, a Year 10 student and school trumpet player, is referred by his dentist for right sided jaw pain lasting four months. He reports clicking when opening, pain when chewing tougher foods, and morning jaw tightness. His mother says he clenches his teeth during exams, which are coming up. Active mouth opening is 30 mm with a click on opening and a softer click on closing, and the jaw deviates to the right then returns to the midline. Palpation reproduces his pain in the right masseter and temporalis. Cervical rotation is full. There is no history of trauma, fever, swelling or locking. His dentist is considering a night splint. Sami attends with his mother and says he would like to understand what is going on.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop treatment and arrange same day medical review for possible infection of the joint): The warm swollen cheek, fever and rapidly worsening pain with reduced opening suggest infection or another serious condition needing same day medical assessment, so physiotherapy stops. Heat and ultrasound could worsen infection and delay care. The letter to the dentist does not match the urgency, and self care with a recheck in days leaves a possible joint infection untreated."}, {"id": 1536, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Sami's dentist has asked whether a night splint should be fitted after physiotherapy assessment. Sami and his mother have consented to information being shared. What is the most appropriate content and structure for the physiotherapist's written handover to the dentist?", "options": {"A": "Key findings, working impression, treatment so far, and a clear question about the splint", "B": "Copies of the complete clinical notes and every outcome measure, so the dentist has full background", "C": "A telephone call only, because written records about adolescents should stay within the physiotherapy clinic", "D": "A brief note stating the pain is muscular and recommending a splint, without treatment details"}, "cluster_id": "CHS144", "cluster_text": "Sami Haddad, 15, a Year 10 student and school trumpet player, is referred by his dentist for right sided jaw pain lasting four months. He reports clicking when opening, pain when chewing tougher foods, and morning jaw tightness. His mother says he clenches his teeth during exams, which are coming up. Active mouth opening is 30 mm with a click on opening and a softer click on closing, and the jaw deviates to the right then returns to the midline. Palpation reproduces his pain in the right masseter and temporalis. Cervical rotation is full. There is no history of trauma, fever, swelling or locking. His dentist is considering a night splint. Sami attends with his mother and says he would like to understand what is going on.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Key findings, working impression, treatment so far, and a clear question about the splint): A good handover is concise and structured: objective findings, impression, what was done, response and a clear question. This lets the dentist decide about a splint. Sending all notes shares more than is needed, a vague note gives the dentist nothing to act on, and a call alone leaves no record."}, {"id": 1537, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "The rheumatologist asks which features of Tariq's history best distinguish inflammatory back pain from mechanical back pain, to guide the content of his education. Considering his age of onset and symptom behaviour, which combination is most characteristic of an inflammatory pattern?", "options": {"A": "Insidious onset before forty, relief with exercise, worse with rest, and long morning stiffness", "B": "Onset after fifty, pain radiating below the knee and worsening with prolonged standing and walking", "C": "Sudden onset after heavy lifting, pain that eases with rest and worsens with repeated flexion", "D": "Gradual onset in his thirties, pain relieved by lying flat, and morning stiffness settling within minutes"}, "cluster_id": "CHS145", "cluster_text": "Tariq Hussain, 26, call centre worker, is referred by his rheumatologist with a two year history of insidious low back and buttock pain. Pain is worse after rest, with morning stiffness of about 90 minutes, and eases with exercise. Night pain wakes him in the second half of the night. NSAIDs help. He is HLA-B27 positive and MRI shows sacroiliitis, consistent with axial spondyloarthritis. Examination: reduced lumbar flexion on modified Schober's test, reduced chest expansion, mildly flexed thoracic posture, occiput to wall distance 3 cm. Neurological examination is normal. BASDAI 6.5 out of 10 and BASFI 5 out of 10. Pain today is 5 out of 10 and settles with movement. Your clinic is starting to collect outcome measures to evaluate its axial spondyloarthritis program, but staff currently record only a pain score at the first visit.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Insidious onset before forty, relief with exercise, worse with rest, and long morning stiffness): Inflammatory back pain typically begins insidiously in younger adults, improves with movement, worsens with rest and brings prolonged morning stiffness. The other patterns describe mechanical strain, degenerative or neurogenic leg symptoms, or a rest-relieved picture, which is the opposite of what Tariq reports."}, {"id": 1538, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Tariq asks what exercise will best help his stiffness and posture. He has a mildly flexed thoracic posture and reduced chest expansion, with no neurological signs. Pain is 5 out of 10 and settles with movement. Which program is most appropriate to begin?", "options": {"A": "Rest the spine until pain settles, then begin gentle isolated hamstring and hip flexor stretching", "B": "Regular thrust manipulation of the stiff thoracic and lumbar segments to restore spinal movement between visits", "C": "Trunk flexor strengthening with sit-ups and loaded spinal flexion to improve his core control", "D": "Daily spinal mobility, thoracic extension, deep breathing, postural strengthening and regular aerobic exercise"}, "cluster_id": "CHS145", "cluster_text": "Tariq Hussain, 26, call centre worker, is referred by his rheumatologist with a two year history of insidious low back and buttock pain. Pain is worse after rest, with morning stiffness of about 90 minutes, and eases with exercise. Night pain wakes him in the second half of the night. NSAIDs help. He is HLA-B27 positive and MRI shows sacroiliitis, consistent with axial spondyloarthritis. Examination: reduced lumbar flexion on modified Schober's test, reduced chest expansion, mildly flexed thoracic posture, occiput to wall distance 3 cm. Neurological examination is normal. BASDAI 6.5 out of 10 and BASFI 5 out of 10. Pain today is 5 out of 10 and settles with movement. Your clinic is starting to collect outcome measures to evaluate its axial spondyloarthritis program, but staff currently record only a pain score at the first visit.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Daily spinal mobility, thoracic extension, deep breathing, postural strengthening and regular aerobic exercise): Regular mobility, extension, chest expansion and postural work, with aerobic exercise, maintain spinal movement and counter the drift into flexion. Resting until pain settles encourages more stiffness since his pain eases with movement. Flexion loading reinforces his posture, and thrust techniques carry fracture risk in a stiffened, osteopenic spine."}, {"id": 1539, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "At review in four weeks Tariq reports two days of a painful red left eye, blurred vision and discomfort in bright light. He has no trauma history and no eye drops. He works long hours at a screen. Which action is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Advise same-day medical review for possible uveitis and let the rheumatologist know about the symptoms", "B": "Suggest he rest his eyes and use lubricating drops, then recheck at the next appointment", "C": "Suggest regular breaks from screen work for a week, since the symptoms suggest digital eye strain", "D": "Book an optometrist visit within the fortnight for glasses, as blurred vision suggests a refractive change"}, "cluster_id": "CHS145", "cluster_text": "Tariq Hussain, 26, call centre worker, is referred by his rheumatologist with a two year history of insidious low back and buttock pain. Pain is worse after rest, with morning stiffness of about 90 minutes, and eases with exercise. Night pain wakes him in the second half of the night. NSAIDs help. He is HLA-B27 positive and MRI shows sacroiliitis, consistent with axial spondyloarthritis. Examination: reduced lumbar flexion on modified Schober's test, reduced chest expansion, mildly flexed thoracic posture, occiput to wall distance 3 cm. Neurological examination is normal. BASDAI 6.5 out of 10 and BASFI 5 out of 10. Pain today is 5 out of 10 and settles with movement. Your clinic is starting to collect outcome measures to evaluate its axial spondyloarthritis program, but staff currently record only a pain score at the first visit.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Advise same-day medical review for possible uveitis and let the rheumatologist know about the symptoms): Acute anterior uveitis is a recognised extra-articular feature of axial spondyloarthritis, and a painful red eye with light sensitivity and blurred vision needs prompt medical assessment to protect vision. Eye strain and refractive change do not produce a red, painful, photophobic eye. Lubricating drops and waiting until the next appointment delay the treatment he needs."}, {"id": 1540, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Your clinic wants to evaluate its axial spondyloarthritis program using Tariq's baseline BASDAI of 6.5 and BASFI of 5. Staff currently record only a numerical pain score at the first visit. Which approach to outcome measurement is most appropriate for service quality improvement?", "options": {"A": "Let each therapist choose their preferred measure so assessment is tailored, then compare clinic averages", "B": "Collect measures only from patients who complete the full program and report their average improvement", "C": "Repeat the same validated measures at set intervals and review pooled results at team meetings", "D": "Record only patient satisfaction scores at discharge, since they summarise the quality of the whole service"}, "cluster_id": "CHS145", "cluster_text": "Tariq Hussain, 26, call centre worker, is referred by his rheumatologist with a two year history of insidious low back and buttock pain. Pain is worse after rest, with morning stiffness of about 90 minutes, and eases with exercise. Night pain wakes him in the second half of the night. NSAIDs help. He is HLA-B27 positive and MRI shows sacroiliitis, consistent with axial spondyloarthritis. Examination: reduced lumbar flexion on modified Schober's test, reduced chest expansion, mildly flexed thoracic posture, occiput to wall distance 3 cm. Neurological examination is normal. BASDAI 6.5 out of 10 and BASFI 5 out of 10. Pain today is 5 out of 10 and settles with movement. Your clinic is starting to collect outcome measures to evaluate its axial spondyloarthritis program, but staff currently record only a pain score at the first visit.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Repeat the same validated measures at set intervals and review pooled results at team meetings): Using the same validated tools at planned time points for every patient allows meaningful change to be tracked and the pooled data to inform service improvement. Different measures per therapist cannot be compared. Satisfaction alone says nothing about function or disease activity, and counting only completers hides dropouts and overstates benefit."}, {"id": 1541, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Rinaldi has known chronic COPD and was breathless on arrival. His arterial gas before NIV showed pH 7.28, PaCO2 68 mmHg and HCO3 30 mmol/L. The registrar asks the team to describe the acid base state. Which interpretation best fits these results?", "options": {"A": "Acute metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation from hyperventilation driven by hypoxaemia", "B": "Acute on chronic respiratory acidosis with compensatory bicarbonate retention already present", "C": "Mixed metabolic alkalosis due to excess bicarbonate and underlying renal impairment", "D": "Chronic compensated respiratory alkalosis with a normal pH and low carbon dioxide"}, "cluster_id": "CHS146", "cluster_text": "Salvatore Rinaldi, 76, retired mechanic, presented to the emergency department with three days of worsening breathlessness and increased green sputum. Known COPD, ex-smoker with a 50 pack-year history. Non-invasive ventilation (bilevel) was started two hours ago. Arterial gas before NIV: pH 7.28, PaCO2 68 mmHg, HCO3 30 mmol/L. Now RR 28, SpO2 91%, HR 104, alert but tired, sputum audible and he cannot clear it. Auscultation shows coarse crackles and wheeze bilaterally. He consented to updates being given to his wife only. His son, who lives interstate, phones the department and asks the physiotherapist for a full update on his father's condition and treatment plan. He lives with his wife and is independent with daily activities at baseline.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Acute on chronic respiratory acidosis with compensatory bicarbonate retention already present): The low pH with raised PaCO2 shows respiratory acidosis, and the raised bicarbonate shows the kidneys have retained buffer over time, so the picture is acute on chronic. A metabolic acidosis would have a low bicarbonate. The finding matters because it is the group in which NIV is expected to help."}, {"id": 1542, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Rinaldi remains on NIV with audible sputum he cannot clear, but he is alert, cooperative and his SpO2 is 91 percent on current settings. The doctor has agreed to short planned breaks from the mask. Which approach is most appropriate for airway clearance?", "options": {"A": "Sit him upright and use brief mask breaks for huffing and coughing, with nasal prong oxygen", "B": "Keep NIV running and use manual techniques in a modified postural drainage position with percussion", "C": "Remove the mask for a fifteen minute airway clearance session to maximise time off pressure", "D": "Change to nasal prong oxygen and use suction for clearance, then restart NIV when settled"}, "cluster_id": "CHS146", "cluster_text": "Salvatore Rinaldi, 76, retired mechanic, presented to the emergency department with three days of worsening breathlessness and increased green sputum. Known COPD, ex-smoker with a 50 pack-year history. Non-invasive ventilation (bilevel) was started two hours ago. Arterial gas before NIV: pH 7.28, PaCO2 68 mmHg, HCO3 30 mmol/L. Now RR 28, SpO2 91%, HR 104, alert but tired, sputum audible and he cannot clear it. Auscultation shows coarse crackles and wheeze bilaterally. He consented to updates being given to his wife only. His son, who lives interstate, phones the department and asks the physiotherapist for a full update on his father's condition and treatment plan. He lives with his wife and is independent with daily activities at baseline.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Sit him upright and use brief mask breaks for huffing and coughing, with nasal prong oxygen): Short planned breaks let him huff and cough while supported by oxygen and observed for fatigue and desaturation. The long break risks a rapid carbon dioxide rise and tiring. Head down positions raise work of breathing in severe COPD, and stopping ventilation for suction removes support while ignoring his ability to clear secretions with coaching."}, {"id": 1543, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "One hour later his repeat gas shows pH 7.25 and PaCO2 72 mmHg. He is now drowsy and slow to answer despite a well fitting mask and unchanged settings. The physiotherapist is next due to treat him and his sputum remains audible. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Allow him to sleep for a few hours and repeat the assessment at the next shift", "B": "Raise the oxygen flow to the highest setting to correct the drowsiness and then continue treatment", "C": "Inform the treating doctor promptly and withhold treatment until escalation has been reviewed", "D": "Go ahead with the planned airway clearance, since clearing sputum should lower his carbon dioxide level"}, "cluster_id": "CHS146", "cluster_text": "Salvatore Rinaldi, 76, retired mechanic, presented to the emergency department with three days of worsening breathlessness and increased green sputum. Known COPD, ex-smoker with a 50 pack-year history. Non-invasive ventilation (bilevel) was started two hours ago. Arterial gas before NIV: pH 7.28, PaCO2 68 mmHg, HCO3 30 mmol/L. Now RR 28, SpO2 91%, HR 104, alert but tired, sputum audible and he cannot clear it. Auscultation shows coarse crackles and wheeze bilaterally. He consented to updates being given to his wife only. His son, who lives interstate, phones the department and asks the physiotherapist for a full update on his father's condition and treatment plan. He lives with his wife and is independent with daily activities at baseline.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Inform the treating doctor promptly and withhold treatment until escalation has been reviewed): Worsening acidosis and drowsiness on NIV signal possible NIV failure, which needs medical decisions about settings and escalation. Giving more oxygen can worsen carbon dioxide retention in COPD. Treatment that tires or removes the mask from a drowsy patient is unsafe until the team has reviewed the plan."}, {"id": 1544, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "His son, who lives interstate, phones and asks for full details about the diagnosis and plan, saying his mother is too upset to explain. Mr Rinaldi agreed only to updates to his wife, and the son is not named in his consent. What should the physiotherapist do?", "options": {"A": "Pass the call to a nurse who can share details without noting it in the record", "B": "Give a general summary, as close family of a patient have an automatic right to know", "C": "Share the plan after the son confirms his identity and states the relationship to the patient", "D": "Politely withhold details, explain the consent limit, and document the call and the response given"}, "cluster_id": "CHS146", "cluster_text": "Salvatore Rinaldi, 76, retired mechanic, presented to the emergency department with three days of worsening breathlessness and increased green sputum. Known COPD, ex-smoker with a 50 pack-year history. Non-invasive ventilation (bilevel) was started two hours ago. Arterial gas before NIV: pH 7.28, PaCO2 68 mmHg, HCO3 30 mmol/L. Now RR 28, SpO2 91%, HR 104, alert but tired, sputum audible and he cannot clear it. Auscultation shows coarse crackles and wheeze bilaterally. He consented to updates being given to his wife only. His son, who lives interstate, phones the department and asks the physiotherapist for a full update on his father's condition and treatment plan. He lives with his wife and is independent with daily activities at baseline.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Politely withhold details, explain the consent limit, and document the call and the response given): Information may only be shared as the patient has agreed, and identity checking does not create that permission. The physiotherapist can invite the son to speak with the patient or the treating team about consent. Documenting the call, what was disclosed and why, protects the patient and the clinician."}, {"id": 1545, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "The physiotherapist needs to decide what level of the nervous system explains Mr Menon's clumsy hands and unsteady walking. Which combination of his findings most strongly indicates cervical spinal cord compression rather than a nerve root or peripheral nerve problem?", "options": {"A": "Bilateral Hoffmann signs, brisk lower limb reflexes with clonus, and an impaired tandem gait", "B": "Reduced cervical extension, tingling in the hands, and failure to respond to previous neck manipulation", "C": "Urinary urgency, type 2 diabetes, and reduced light touch over the fingertips of both hands", "D": "Tingling in both hands on neck extension, reduced fingertip light touch, and slow grip and release"}, "cluster_id": "CHS147", "cluster_text": "Rajesh Menon, 62, an accountant, presents to a private practice with four months of clumsy hands and unsteady walking. He has difficulty with buttons and handwriting, and says his legs feel stiff and heavy. No trauma. Occasional urinary urgency over the last month. A previous physiotherapist provided neck manipulation, which did not help. Medical history: type 2 diabetes. He lives in a two-storey home with the bedroom upstairs. Findings: cervical extension reduced and provokes tingling in both hands. Hoffmann sign positive bilaterally. Knee and ankle reflexes brisk with three beats of clonus at the right ankle. Right plantar response extensor. Tandem walking impaired with a wide-based gait. Finger grip and release slow. Reduced light touch over the fingertips. Power 4+/5 in grip and hip flexion. He plays golf weekly and wants to keep working. He asks what he can safely do himself.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Bilateral Hoffmann signs, brisk lower limb reflexes with clonus, and an impaired tandem gait): Upper motor neuron signs below the level of compression, such as Hoffmann signs, hyperreflexia, clonus and gait ataxia, localise to the cord. Hand tingling and fingertip sensory loss can occur with root or peripheral problems, and diabetes could explain neuropathy, but none of these explain the brisk reflexes and clonus."}, {"id": 1546, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Menon's examination suggests myelopathy, and his previous physiotherapist used neck manipulation. He asks for a plan and is keen to continue with hands-on neck treatment today as before. What is the most appropriate next step for the physiotherapist to take today?", "options": {"A": "Begin a dexterity and balance programme, referring on only if no gain occurs after six weeks", "B": "Refer promptly to his GP for urgent imaging and a spinal opinion, avoiding cervical manual therapy", "C": "Refer for nerve conduction studies to confirm bilateral carpal tunnel syndrome before any further neurological assessment", "D": "Start gentle cervical mobilisation and deep neck flexor strengthening, and review progress after four weeks"}, "cluster_id": "CHS147", "cluster_text": "Rajesh Menon, 62, an accountant, presents to a private practice with four months of clumsy hands and unsteady walking. He has difficulty with buttons and handwriting, and says his legs feel stiff and heavy. No trauma. Occasional urinary urgency over the last month. A previous physiotherapist provided neck manipulation, which did not help. Medical history: type 2 diabetes. He lives in a two-storey home with the bedroom upstairs. Findings: cervical extension reduced and provokes tingling in both hands. Hoffmann sign positive bilaterally. Knee and ankle reflexes brisk with three beats of clonus at the right ankle. Right plantar response extensor. Tandem walking impaired with a wide-based gait. Finger grip and release slow. Reduced light touch over the fingertips. Power 4+/5 in grip and hip flexion. He plays golf weekly and wants to keep working. He asks what he can safely do himself.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Refer promptly to his GP for urgent imaging and a spinal opinion, avoiding cervical manual therapy): Progressive signs of cord compression with early bladder symptoms need timely imaging and surgical opinion, because delay risks irreversible deficit. Manual therapy to the cervical spine adds risk, and a trial of therapy delays referral. Carpal tunnel syndrome would not explain his leg and reflex findings."}, {"id": 1547, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Two days later Mr Menon telephones the clinic. Overnight his legs have become markedly weaker, he is struggling to stand, and he has been unable to pass urine since yesterday. His GP appointment is on Friday. What advice should the physiotherapist give?", "options": {"A": "Keep his booked GP appointment on Friday, asking reception to flag the request as urgent", "B": "Attend the clinic tomorrow morning for reassessment, then contact the surgeon if weakness has not settled", "C": "Rest with his legs elevated at home and ring again if the weakness continues to worsen", "D": "Go to the nearest emergency department now, arranging transport rather than driving himself"}, "cluster_id": "CHS147", "cluster_text": "Rajesh Menon, 62, an accountant, presents to a private practice with four months of clumsy hands and unsteady walking. He has difficulty with buttons and handwriting, and says his legs feel stiff and heavy. No trauma. Occasional urinary urgency over the last month. A previous physiotherapist provided neck manipulation, which did not help. Medical history: type 2 diabetes. He lives in a two-storey home with the bedroom upstairs. Findings: cervical extension reduced and provokes tingling in both hands. Hoffmann sign positive bilaterally. Knee and ankle reflexes brisk with three beats of clonus at the right ankle. Right plantar response extensor. Tandem walking impaired with a wide-based gait. Finger grip and release slow. Reduced light touch over the fingertips. Power 4+/5 in grip and hip flexion. He plays golf weekly and wants to keep working. He asks what he can safely do himself.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Go to the nearest emergency department now, arranging transport rather than driving himself): Rapid motor deterioration with urinary retention suggests acute cord compromise, which needs emergency assessment and possible decompression within hours. Waiting for a routine appointment or clinic review loses critical time, and he should not drive with new leg weakness."}, {"id": 1548, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "While Mr Menon waits for his specialist review, he asks what he can do himself to stay well and safe. He plays golf weekly and walks to work, and his bedroom is upstairs. Which advice best supports safe self-management over this period?", "options": {"A": "Continue golf and gym training as usual, wearing a soft collar for neck support", "B": "Rest at home and limit walking to essential trips to protect the spinal cord until review", "C": "Do daily firm neck stretches into extension to restore movement and relieve pressure on the cord", "D": "Keep walking daily, use handrails on stairs, avoid end-range neck extension, and report new symptoms"}, "cluster_id": "CHS147", "cluster_text": "Rajesh Menon, 62, an accountant, presents to a private practice with four months of clumsy hands and unsteady walking. He has difficulty with buttons and handwriting, and says his legs feel stiff and heavy. No trauma. Occasional urinary urgency over the last month. A previous physiotherapist provided neck manipulation, which did not help. Medical history: type 2 diabetes. He lives in a two-storey home with the bedroom upstairs. Findings: cervical extension reduced and provokes tingling in both hands. Hoffmann sign positive bilaterally. Knee and ankle reflexes brisk with three beats of clonus at the right ankle. Right plantar response extensor. Tandem walking impaired with a wide-based gait. Finger grip and release slow. Reduced light touch over the fingertips. Power 4+/5 in grip and hip flexion. He plays golf weekly and wants to keep working. He asks what he can safely do himself.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Keep walking daily, use handrails on stairs, avoid end-range neck extension, and report new symptoms): Staying active maintains function, handrails reduce fall risk from impaired balance, and avoiding end-range extension limits cord narrowing, while knowing which new symptoms to report supports early action. Extension stretching can aggravate compression, prolonged inactivity causes deconditioning, and a collar does not make high-demand activity safe."}, {"id": 1549, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "You need to explain the diagnosis, obtain informed consent for taping and exercises, and discuss changes to her market work. Mrs Huynh's daughter offers to interpret and Mrs Huynh seems comfortable with this. What is the most appropriate way to communicate in this session?", "options": {"A": "Let her daughter interpret and check understanding by asking Mrs Huynh to repeat key points", "B": "Speak slowly in plain English with translated handouts and ask her to nod if she understands", "C": "Use an accredited interpreter, and let her daughter stay as support if Mrs Huynh agrees", "D": "Use her daughter for the history and an interpreter only for the consent discussion"}, "cluster_id": "CHS148", "cluster_text": "Trang Huynh is a 71 year old woman who speaks Vietnamese and limited English. She attends a community health centre with her daughter, who offers to interpret. For four months she has had sharp pain under her left medial heel on the first steps each morning and after sitting, easing after a few minutes of walking. She stands for eight hours daily selling vegetables at a market. BMI 31. Examination: tenderness at the medial calcaneal tubercle, windlass test reproduces her pain, ankle dorsiflexion with the knee straight limited to neutral, no swelling, no night pain, normal neurovascular status and an intact Achilles tendon. Her GP has ruled out systemic causes. She wears thin flat sandals.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use an accredited interpreter, and let her daughter stay as support if Mrs Huynh agrees): Accredited interpreters keep information accurate and confidential, which matters most for consent and clinical content, and a family member can still attend as support by her choice. Family interpreting risks omissions and filtering, and nodding or handouts alone do not confirm understanding."}, {"id": 1550, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Huynh has had four months of heel pain. You are explaining to a student which features separate plantar fasciitis from other causes of heel pain. Which combination of features is most characteristic of plantar fasciitis in an older adult?", "options": {"A": "Posterior heel pain on resisted plantarflexion with tenderness just above the calcaneal insertion", "B": "Burning night pain with sole numbness and a positive Tinel sign behind the medial malleolus", "C": "Pain on first steps, localised medial calcaneal tenderness and a positive windlass test", "D": "Pain on hopping with diffuse heel swelling and tenderness on squeezing the calcaneus sides"}, "cluster_id": "CHS148", "cluster_text": "Trang Huynh is a 71 year old woman who speaks Vietnamese and limited English. She attends a community health centre with her daughter, who offers to interpret. For four months she has had sharp pain under her left medial heel on the first steps each morning and after sitting, easing after a few minutes of walking. She stands for eight hours daily selling vegetables at a market. BMI 31. Examination: tenderness at the medial calcaneal tubercle, windlass test reproduces her pain, ankle dorsiflexion with the knee straight limited to neutral, no swelling, no night pain, normal neurovascular status and an intact Achilles tendon. Her GP has ruled out systemic causes. She wears thin flat sandals.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Pain on first steps, localised medial calcaneal tenderness and a positive windlass test): Start up pain easing with walking, tenderness at the medial calcaneal tubercle and a positive windlass test are the classic pattern. The other combinations describe tarsal tunnel syndrome, a calcaneal stress injury and Achilles tendon pathology, each with different pain behaviour and examination findings."}, {"id": 1551, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Huynh must keep standing through long market shifts and wears thin flat sandals. Her dorsiflexion is restricted with the knee straight and her heel pain follows the first step pattern described in the chart. Which initial treatment plan is most appropriate for her?", "options": {"A": "Calf and plantar fascia stretches, supportive footwear or taping, and pacing of standing time", "B": "Referral for a corticosteroid injection, with stretching and footwear changes deferred until the pain has settled", "C": "Intensive eccentric calf loading to fatigue daily, with her current footwear kept unchanged", "D": "Rest from all standing for six weeks followed by a graded return to the market stall"}, "cluster_id": "CHS148", "cluster_text": "Trang Huynh is a 71 year old woman who speaks Vietnamese and limited English. She attends a community health centre with her daughter, who offers to interpret. For four months she has had sharp pain under her left medial heel on the first steps each morning and after sitting, easing after a few minutes of walking. She stands for eight hours daily selling vegetables at a market. BMI 31. Examination: tenderness at the medial calcaneal tubercle, windlass test reproduces her pain, ankle dorsiflexion with the knee straight limited to neutral, no swelling, no night pain, normal neurovascular status and an intact Achilles tendon. Her GP has ruled out systemic causes. She wears thin flat sandals.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Calf and plantar fascia stretches, supportive footwear or taping, and pacing of standing time): Her restricted dorsiflexion, high standing load and unsupportive footwear are modifiable contributors, so stretching, support and load pacing address them while she keeps earning. Long rest is impractical, an injection first skips conservative care, and heavy daily loading in poor footwear risks aggravating the fascia."}, {"id": 1552, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Three weeks later, through the interpreter, Mrs Huynh says her heel now aches at night when resting and that she has lost weight without trying. She has also felt generally tired, and the windlass test is now negative. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Reduce taping and loading to rest the fascia, and review progress at the next visit", "B": "Stop the heel program and refer her to her GP promptly for medical investigation", "C": "Increase calf stretching and reassess in a month, as the negative windlass test suggests improvement", "D": "Add a night splint and review in two weeks, as night heel pain occurs in fasciopathy"}, "cluster_id": "CHS148", "cluster_text": "Trang Huynh is a 71 year old woman who speaks Vietnamese and limited English. She attends a community health centre with her daughter, who offers to interpret. For four months she has had sharp pain under her left medial heel on the first steps each morning and after sitting, easing after a few minutes of walking. She stands for eight hours daily selling vegetables at a market. BMI 31. Examination: tenderness at the medial calcaneal tubercle, windlass test reproduces her pain, ankle dorsiflexion with the knee straight limited to neutral, no swelling, no night pain, normal neurovascular status and an intact Achilles tendon. Her GP has ruled out systemic causes. She wears thin flat sandals.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stop the heel program and refer her to her GP promptly for medical investigation): Constant night pain with unexplained weight loss and fatigue are red flags that a heel musculoskeletal diagnosis does not explain, particularly in an older adult, so prompt medical referral takes priority over continuing the program. Resting the fascia or adding a splint treats a diagnosis that no longer fits. A negative windlass test does not show recovery when the pattern of symptoms has changed."}, {"id": 1553, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Gareth currently has an inflated cuff. A one-way speaking valve lets air in through the tube but directs exhaled air through the upper airway. Before the valve is placed, which action is essential to prevent complete airway obstruction when he exhales?", "options": {"A": "Request a change to an uncuffed tube first, since valves are only used with uncuffed tubes", "B": "Suction the tube and upper airway, then place the valve with the cuff left partly inflated", "C": "Fully deflate the cuff before placing the valve, so exhaled air can pass around the tube", "D": "Confirm he can cough and clear secretions independently, then fit the valve for a trial"}, "cluster_id": "CHS149", "cluster_text": "Gareth Mitchell, 58, is on an acute ward after 3 weeks of intubation and ventilation for severe pneumonia, now 6 days after a percutaneous tracheostomy. Cuffed size 8 tube, cuff currently inflated, breathing spontaneously through a humidified circuit with 28% oxygen. He is alert and follows commands, has no voice and mouths words. RR 18, SpO2 96%, HR 80. His cough is strong, with moderate thin white secretions that he clears with suction twice per shift. The speech pathologist found his swallow safe for thin fluids on a modified diet, and the doctor has said a speaking valve trial can be considered. Gareth has smoked 20 cigarettes a day for 35 years and tells you he plans to 'sort the smoking out' only after he is home. He lives with his daughter and badly wants to speak to his grandchildren.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Fully deflate the cuff before placing the valve, so exhaled air can pass around the tube): A one-way valve closes on exhalation, so all expired air must travel around the tube and out through the larynx. With the cuff inflated no air can pass and the patient cannot exhale, which is an emergency. Secretion clearance and cough matter for candidacy, but they do not replace complete cuff deflation before placing the valve."}, {"id": 1554, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "This morning, with the cuff deflated and the tube occluded briefly by a gloved finger, Gareth exhaled comfortably through his mouth, vocalised, and kept SpO2 at 96%. Which clinical finding is most important for judging his readiness for a first speaking valve trial?", "options": {"A": "His normal saturation on supplemental oxygen shows he no longer needs humidification and tolerates a valve", "B": "Comfortable exhalation through the upper airway with the tube occluded shows adequate patency", "C": "His strong cough and thin secretions show the upper airway has no narrowing above the tube", "D": "His safe swallow of thin fluids shows his airway is patent for exhalation around the tube"}, "cluster_id": "CHS149", "cluster_text": "Gareth Mitchell, 58, is on an acute ward after 3 weeks of intubation and ventilation for severe pneumonia, now 6 days after a percutaneous tracheostomy. Cuffed size 8 tube, cuff currently inflated, breathing spontaneously through a humidified circuit with 28% oxygen. He is alert and follows commands, has no voice and mouths words. RR 18, SpO2 96%, HR 80. His cough is strong, with moderate thin white secretions that he clears with suction twice per shift. The speech pathologist found his swallow safe for thin fluids on a modified diet, and the doctor has said a speaking valve trial can be considered. Gareth has smoked 20 cigarettes a day for 35 years and tells you he plans to 'sort the smoking out' only after he is home. He lives with his daughter and badly wants to speak to his grandchildren.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Comfortable exhalation through the upper airway with the tube occluded shows adequate patency): Comfortable exhalation with the tube occluded demonstrates the upper airway can carry all expired air, which is the key safety requirement for a valve. Swallow safety, strong cough and oxygen needs are relevant to overall care but do not show that air can leave through the larynx. A valve should not be placed until this is confirmed."}, {"id": 1555, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Ten minutes into the supervised trial with the cuff fully deflated, Gareth looks distressed, grips his neck, and cannot be heard to vocalise. Chest wall movement is laboured and SpO2 falls from 96% to 90%. What should the physiotherapist do immediately?", "options": {"A": "Re-inflate the cuff with the valve in place and suction the tube to clear possible secretions", "B": "Coach slow breathing and an effortful cough, allowing two more minutes to adapt to the valve", "C": "Remove the valve, then restart the trial immediately with his head extended to open the airway", "D": "Remove the valve immediately, provide oxygen and call for help, then reassess airway patency"}, "cluster_id": "CHS149", "cluster_text": "Gareth Mitchell, 58, is on an acute ward after 3 weeks of intubation and ventilation for severe pneumonia, now 6 days after a percutaneous tracheostomy. Cuffed size 8 tube, cuff currently inflated, breathing spontaneously through a humidified circuit with 28% oxygen. He is alert and follows commands, has no voice and mouths words. RR 18, SpO2 96%, HR 80. His cough is strong, with moderate thin white secretions that he clears with suction twice per shift. The speech pathologist found his swallow safe for thin fluids on a modified diet, and the doctor has said a speaking valve trial can be considered. Gareth has smoked 20 cigarettes a day for 35 years and tells you he plans to 'sort the smoking out' only after he is home. He lives with his daughter and badly wants to speak to his grandchildren.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Remove the valve immediately, provide oxygen and call for help, then reassess airway patency): Distress, absent voice, laboured breathing and falling saturation suggest air cannot leave through the upper airway, so the valve must come off immediately to relieve obstruction, followed by oxygen, help and reassessment. Waiting, or re-inflating the cuff with the valve in place, makes obstruction worse. Restarting straight away ignores the likely cause."}, {"id": 1556, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Gareth says he will 'sort the smoking out' after he goes home. He has 35 years of smoking and has just survived severe pneumonia. He appears open to talking but unsure where to start. As part of self-management support, what is the most appropriate physiotherapist response?", "options": {"A": "Hand him a pamphlet, document advice given, and leave further discussion to the GP", "B": "Respect his timing and leave smoking to the community team, since ward time focuses on rehabilitation", "C": "Give brief advice, explore his readiness and reasons, and link him with his GP and Quitline", "D": "Explain how smoking harmed his lungs and ask him to commit to stopping before discharge"}, "cluster_id": "CHS149", "cluster_text": "Gareth Mitchell, 58, is on an acute ward after 3 weeks of intubation and ventilation for severe pneumonia, now 6 days after a percutaneous tracheostomy. Cuffed size 8 tube, cuff currently inflated, breathing spontaneously through a humidified circuit with 28% oxygen. He is alert and follows commands, has no voice and mouths words. RR 18, SpO2 96%, HR 80. His cough is strong, with moderate thin white secretions that he clears with suction twice per shift. The speech pathologist found his swallow safe for thin fluids on a modified diet, and the doctor has said a speaking valve trial can be considered. Gareth has smoked 20 cigarettes a day for 35 years and tells you he plans to 'sort the smoking out' only after he is home. He lives with his daughter and badly wants to speak to his grandchildren.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Give brief advice, explore his readiness and reasons, and link him with his GP and Quitline): Brief advice delivered respectfully, with exploration of his own motivation and a link to the GP and Quitline, uses the teachable moment after a serious illness without pressuring him. Demanding commitment can provoke resistance, and delaying the topic or handing over a pamphlet alone misses the chance for a supported plan. Smoking cessation advice is within every physiotherapist's health promotion role."}, {"id": 1557, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Bell has severe COPD with known chronic hypercapnia on a past arterial gas, and he is prescribed long term oxygen at home. His daughter asks what saturation to aim for. Which SpO2 target range is generally recommended for a patient like him on supplemental oxygen?", "options": {"A": "90 to 94 percent, as a compromise between hypoxia risk and carbon dioxide retention in COPD", "B": "92 to 96 percent, the target used for most acutely unwell adults on supplemental oxygen", "C": "94 to 98 percent, the standard range for patients without risk of hypercapnic failure", "D": "88 to 92 percent, to limit carbon dioxide retention while keeping oxygenation adequate"}, "cluster_id": "CHS150", "cluster_text": "Colin Bell, 72, retired grazier, lives on a station 300 km from the nearest hospital and attends the remote health clinic with his daughter. Severe COPD with chronic hypercapnia (PaCO2 58 mmHg on a past gas). Prescribed long term oxygen 2 L/min by nasal prongs from a home concentrator. This morning his daughter increased the flow to 4 L/min because he seemed breathless. Today in clinic: SpO2 96% on 4 L/min, RR 18, HR 88, morning headache, drowsy but rousable, mild tremor of the hands. The physiotherapist notes the clinic's backup oxygen cylinder regulator reads 2 L/min but the flowmeter ball sits near 1 L/min when checked. The clinic nurse says it has probably always done that. Mr Bell's 6 minute walk test is planned after review.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (88 to 92 percent, to limit carbon dioxide retention while keeping oxygenation adequate): Patients at risk of hypercapnic respiratory failure are given a lower target of 88 to 92 percent to limit carbon dioxide retention while keeping oxygenation adequate. The standard 94 to 98 percent range suits patients without that risk, and the other ranges sit above the recommended band for a patient with proven chronic hypercapnia, so they allow oxygen to push carbon dioxide higher."}, {"id": 1558, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Bell is drowsy with morning headache, tremor and an SpO2 of 96 percent on 4 L/min, after his daughter raised the flow this morning because he seemed breathless. He is otherwise haemodynamically stable. What is the most appropriate physiotherapist action now?", "options": {"A": "Keep 4 L/min and encourage deep breathing exercises to blow off the extra carbon dioxide", "B": "Leave the flow unchanged and delay further assessment until the walk test has been completed", "C": "Reduce the flow to the prescribed setting, recheck SpO2 and alertness, and arrange medical advice", "D": "Turn the oxygen off until his saturation settles at the level expected without COPD"}, "cluster_id": "CHS150", "cluster_text": "Colin Bell, 72, retired grazier, lives on a station 300 km from the nearest hospital and attends the remote health clinic with his daughter. Severe COPD with chronic hypercapnia (PaCO2 58 mmHg on a past gas). Prescribed long term oxygen 2 L/min by nasal prongs from a home concentrator. This morning his daughter increased the flow to 4 L/min because he seemed breathless. Today in clinic: SpO2 96% on 4 L/min, RR 18, HR 88, morning headache, drowsy but rousable, mild tremor of the hands. The physiotherapist notes the clinic's backup oxygen cylinder regulator reads 2 L/min but the flowmeter ball sits near 1 L/min when checked. The clinic nurse says it has probably always done that. Mr Bell's 6 minute walk test is planned after review.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reduce the flow to the prescribed setting, recheck SpO2 and alertness, and arrange medical advice): Drowsiness, headache and tremor after excess oxygen suggest carbon dioxide retention, so the flow should be returned to the prescribed rate and observations repeated. Removing oxygen entirely risks hypoxaemia in a severe COPD patient. Breathing exercises alone will not correct a problem caused by over-oxygenation, and medical advice is needed, possibly via telehealth."}, {"id": 1559, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Later, during the walk test on his prescribed 2 L/min, SpO2 falls to 85 percent after 3 minutes with a Borg breathlessness rating of 7. He looks pale but is fully alert, and the clinic nurse is nearby. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Stop the test, seat him to recover, and report the desaturation for oxygen review", "B": "Raise the flow to 4 L/min himself and continue the test to see if saturation recovers", "C": "Pause briefly and resume once he feels ready, since exertional dips are common in severe COPD", "D": "Stop, switch him to the clinic cylinder at 2 L/min, and repeat the walk after rest"}, "cluster_id": "CHS150", "cluster_text": "Colin Bell, 72, retired grazier, lives on a station 300 km from the nearest hospital and attends the remote health clinic with his daughter. Severe COPD with chronic hypercapnia (PaCO2 58 mmHg on a past gas). Prescribed long term oxygen 2 L/min by nasal prongs from a home concentrator. This morning his daughter increased the flow to 4 L/min because he seemed breathless. Today in clinic: SpO2 96% on 4 L/min, RR 18, HR 88, morning headache, drowsy but rousable, mild tremor of the hands. The physiotherapist notes the clinic's backup oxygen cylinder regulator reads 2 L/min but the flowmeter ball sits near 1 L/min when checked. The clinic nurse says it has probably always done that. Mr Bell's 6 minute walk test is planned after review.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stop the test, seat him to recover, and report the desaturation for oxygen review): A fall to 85 percent with a Borg rating of 7 is a reason to stop the test and let him recover, and the result needs to be reported so his oxygen prescription can be reviewed. Raising the flow himself is outside the physiotherapist's role and risks worsening hypercapnia in a man whose daughter has already shown how drowsy higher flow can make him. Resuming after a short pause ignores the desaturation, and a cylinder whose flowmeter does not match its regulator cannot be trusted to deliver the prescribed flow."}, {"id": 1560, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "The backup cylinder flowmeter reads about 1 L/min when set to 2 L/min, and the nurse says it has always done that. No patient has been harmed so far, and the clinic has an incident reporting policy. What is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Ask the nurse to turn the regulator up until the reading nears the needed flow", "B": "Remove it from use, label it, and lodge an incident report under clinic policy", "C": "Wait to see if a patient is affected before deciding whether a formal report is warranted", "D": "Keep using it and add a note on the cylinder about the difference between the readings"}, "cluster_id": "CHS150", "cluster_text": "Colin Bell, 72, retired grazier, lives on a station 300 km from the nearest hospital and attends the remote health clinic with his daughter. Severe COPD with chronic hypercapnia (PaCO2 58 mmHg on a past gas). Prescribed long term oxygen 2 L/min by nasal prongs from a home concentrator. This morning his daughter increased the flow to 4 L/min because he seemed breathless. Today in clinic: SpO2 96% on 4 L/min, RR 18, HR 88, morning headache, drowsy but rousable, mild tremor of the hands. The physiotherapist notes the clinic's backup oxygen cylinder regulator reads 2 L/min but the flowmeter ball sits near 1 L/min when checked. The clinic nurse says it has probably always done that. Mr Bell's 6 minute walk test is planned after review.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Remove it from use, label it, and lodge an incident report under clinic policy): A device that delivers less oxygen than set is a hazard even if no harm has occurred, so it should be taken out of service and reported through the incident system so it can be investigated and fixed. Working around the fault or waiting for harm defeats the purpose of near miss reporting and leaves future patients at risk."}, {"id": 1561, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Priya has neck pain, stiffness and reduced rotation with normal neurological findings and no fracture indicators. Three weeks have passed since the crash, and she reports no arm symptoms. Using the Quebec Task Force classification, which grade best describes her presentation?", "options": {"A": "Grade III, neck complaint with neurological signs such as reduced reflexes or weakness", "B": "Grade I, neck pain or stiffness with no objective physical signs on examination", "C": "Grade II, neck complaint with musculoskeletal signs but no neurological signs", "D": "Grade IV, neck complaint with fracture or dislocation needing imaging and surgical review"}, "cluster_id": "CHS151", "cluster_text": "Priya Sharma, 34, accountant, attends a compensable scheme clinic three weeks after a rear end motor vehicle crash as a belted driver. She was assessed in the emergency department on the day, and cervical spine imaging was not required after clinical screening. Current complaints: neck pain 6/10, stiffness, headaches at the base of the skull, and difficulty checking blind spots when driving. She has no arm symptoms, dizziness, visual disturbance or swallowing difficulty. Neurological screen is normal. Cervical rotation is limited to about 50 degrees bilaterally with pain at end range. Neck Disability Index at baseline is 28 percent. She is anxious about driving, worries that movement will cause damage, and sleeps poorly. A friend advised her to wear a soft collar. The insurer's case manager wants progress reports, and the clinic wants to track outcomes across its patients with whiplash.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Grade II, neck complaint with musculoskeletal signs but no neurological signs): Grade II covers neck complaints with musculoskeletal signs such as reduced range of motion and point tenderness. Priya's limited rotation is an objective sign, which excludes grade I, and her normal neurological screen and screening outcome exclude grades III and IV."}, {"id": 1562, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Priya completed the Neck Disability Index at baseline, scoring 28 percent. The clinic wants to track recovery and service quality across its patients with whiplash, and the insurer wants progress reports. Which outcome measure approach is most appropriate to use consistently at baseline and each review?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the Neck Disability Index and add the Patient Specific Functional Scale for driving", "B": "Use a visual analogue pain scale alone at each visit, since pain is her main complaint", "C": "Record cervical rotation range with a goniometer and add the Neck Disability Index only at discharge", "D": "Use the Neck Disability Index at baseline, then a global rating of change at each review"}, "cluster_id": "CHS151", "cluster_text": "Priya Sharma, 34, accountant, attends a compensable scheme clinic three weeks after a rear end motor vehicle crash as a belted driver. She was assessed in the emergency department on the day, and cervical spine imaging was not required after clinical screening. Current complaints: neck pain 6/10, stiffness, headaches at the base of the skull, and difficulty checking blind spots when driving. She has no arm symptoms, dizziness, visual disturbance or swallowing difficulty. Neurological screen is normal. Cervical rotation is limited to about 50 degrees bilaterally with pain at end range. Neck Disability Index at baseline is 28 percent. She is anxious about driving, worries that movement will cause damage, and sleeps poorly. A friend advised her to wear a soft collar. The insurer's case manager wants progress reports, and the clinic wants to track outcomes across its patients with whiplash.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Repeat the Neck Disability Index and add the Patient Specific Functional Scale for driving): Repeating the same validated disability questionnaire at every time point makes change interpretable and comparable across patients, and a patient specific measure captures her driving goal. The pain scale alone misses disability and participation. Measuring the questionnaire only at discharge leaves the insurer without progress data. The global rating of change gives no repeated disability score to compare with her baseline."}, {"id": 1563, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Priya fears movement will cause damage and is avoiding rotation. Her examination shows no red flags. She sleeps poorly, and a friend advised her to wear a soft collar. Which management approach is most consistent with current evidence for her at this stage?", "options": {"A": "High velocity manipulation of the upper cervical spine to restore rotation, then activity advice later", "B": "Reassurance, education about a good prognosis, and a graded return to neck movement and activity", "C": "Soft collar use during the day for two weeks, then gradual introduction of neck exercises", "D": "Rest from driving and desk work until pain settles to two out of ten, then restart"}, "cluster_id": "CHS151", "cluster_text": "Priya Sharma, 34, accountant, attends a compensable scheme clinic three weeks after a rear end motor vehicle crash as a belted driver. She was assessed in the emergency department on the day, and cervical spine imaging was not required after clinical screening. Current complaints: neck pain 6/10, stiffness, headaches at the base of the skull, and difficulty checking blind spots when driving. She has no arm symptoms, dizziness, visual disturbance or swallowing difficulty. Neurological screen is normal. Cervical rotation is limited to about 50 degrees bilaterally with pain at end range. Neck Disability Index at baseline is 28 percent. She is anxious about driving, worries that movement will cause damage, and sleeps poorly. A friend advised her to wear a soft collar. The insurer's case manager wants progress reports, and the clinic wants to track outcomes across its patients with whiplash.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reassurance, education about a good prognosis, and a graded return to neck movement and activity): Most people recover well from this grade of injury, and early education with graded activity addresses fear and stiffness together. Collars and prolonged rest are associated with slower recovery, and high velocity manipulation of the upper neck carries risk without clear benefit at this stage."}, {"id": 1564, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "At her next visit Priya reports a sudden, severe headache unlike her usual neck headaches, with new left facial numbness and unsteadiness since yesterday. Her neck pain is unchanged. Her observations are within normal limits. What is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Continue the graded exercise plan but avoid end range rotation, and reassess later.", "B": "Perform positional vertebral artery testing in clinic and refer onward only if the test is positive", "C": "Withhold neck treatment and arrange urgent emergency assessment for possible vascular injury", "D": "Advise rest and simple pain relief from the pharmacy, with review booked for the following week"}, "cluster_id": "CHS151", "cluster_text": "Priya Sharma, 34, accountant, attends a compensable scheme clinic three weeks after a rear end motor vehicle crash as a belted driver. She was assessed in the emergency department on the day, and cervical spine imaging was not required after clinical screening. Current complaints: neck pain 6/10, stiffness, headaches at the base of the skull, and difficulty checking blind spots when driving. She has no arm symptoms, dizziness, visual disturbance or swallowing difficulty. Neurological screen is normal. Cervical rotation is limited to about 50 degrees bilaterally with pain at end range. Neck Disability Index at baseline is 28 percent. She is anxious about driving, worries that movement will cause damage, and sleeps poorly. A friend advised her to wear a soft collar. The insurer's case manager wants progress reports, and the clinic wants to track outcomes across its patients with whiplash.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Withhold neck treatment and arrange urgent emergency assessment for possible vascular injury): A new severe headache with facial numbness and unsteadiness after neck trauma raises concern about cervical artery dissection and stroke, which needs urgent medical assessment. Provocation testing is not a valid screening tool and could cause harm, and waiting or continuing exercise delays time sensitive care."}, {"id": 1565, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Le has been awake for two days, is haemodynamically stable and follows commands on low ventilator support, although his trunk control in sitting is poor. Which approach to today's session best reflects safe early active rehabilitation in intensive care?", "options": {"A": "Limit treatment to passive range of movement in bed until the spontaneous breathing trial succeeds today", "B": "Sit him on the edge of the bed for active trunk and limb exercise with monitoring", "C": "Use a tilt table for passive standing, which loads the legs while avoiding sitting balance demands", "D": "Hoist him to a chair for passive sitting, since active exercise should wait until extubation"}, "cluster_id": "CHS152", "cluster_text": "Mr Long Le, 62, a retired bus driver, is on day 6 in the intensive care unit after severe community-acquired pneumonia. He is orally intubated on pressure support ventilation (PEEP 5, FiO2 0.35). RASS 0, he follows commands, has had no sedation infusion for 48 hours, and noradrenaline was ceased 24 hours ago. HR 92, MAP 78 mmHg, RR 20, SpO2 96 percent, afebrile. Chest: coarse crackles at the right base, moderate secretions cleared by suction. He lifts all four limbs against gravity, grip is fair, trunk control in sitting is poor. Previously independent, lives with his wife. The team plans a spontaneous breathing trial this afternoon. You have four patients to see this morning. A new ward referral has just arrived: Mrs Joan Pike, post right hemicolectomy, with a sudden increase in oxygen requirement, reduced air entry at the right base and a weak cough. Another ICU patient is stable and awaiting a routine mobility review.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Sit him on the edge of the bed for active trunk and limb exercise with monitoring): Early goal-directed active mobilisation is safe and beneficial once physiological stability criteria are met, and an endotracheal tube alone is not a barrier. He is alert, off vasopressors and on low support, so active exercise with monitoring suits him. Passive-only approaches or waiting for extubation delay recovery and add to the risk of ICU-acquired weakness."}, {"id": 1566, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Ten minutes into sitting on the edge of the bed, his SpO2 falls from 96 to 89 percent, respiratory rate rises to 32 and he looks distressed, with heart rate climbing steadily. What is the most appropriate immediate physiotherapy response?", "options": {"A": "Stop and return him to bed and settle him, then reassess at the next scheduled treatment", "B": "Pause briefly, coach slow breathing during a rest break, then resume sitting once he settles", "C": "Ask the nurse to increase the ventilator oxygen setting, then continue sitting for the planned time", "D": "Stop, return him to a supported position, reassess, and inform the nurse and team"}, "cluster_id": "CHS152", "cluster_text": "Mr Long Le, 62, a retired bus driver, is on day 6 in the intensive care unit after severe community-acquired pneumonia. He is orally intubated on pressure support ventilation (PEEP 5, FiO2 0.35). RASS 0, he follows commands, has had no sedation infusion for 48 hours, and noradrenaline was ceased 24 hours ago. HR 92, MAP 78 mmHg, RR 20, SpO2 96 percent, afebrile. Chest: coarse crackles at the right base, moderate secretions cleared by suction. He lifts all four limbs against gravity, grip is fair, trunk control in sitting is poor. Previously independent, lives with his wife. The team plans a spontaneous breathing trial this afternoon. You have four patients to see this morning. A new ward referral has just arrived: Mrs Joan Pike, post right hemicolectomy, with a sudden increase in oxygen requirement, reduced air entry at the right base and a weak cough. Another ICU patient is stable and awaiting a routine mobility review.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stop, return him to a supported position, reassess, and inform the nurse and team): A fall in saturation with a rapid respiratory rate and distress is a criterion to stop, so he is returned to a supported position, reassessed and the nurse and team are told in case the ventilator settings or a medical review need to change. Settling him in bed but waiting for the next treatment delays recognition of deterioration. Pausing briefly and resuming treats a clear intolerance as a passing dip. Asking for more oxygen and carrying on keeps loading a patient who is already decompensating."}, {"id": 1567, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "You have four patients to see and limited time. Mrs Pike has a new oxygen requirement, reduced air entry at the right base and a weak cough, while Mr Le's session is planned. Which prioritisation of your time this morning is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Review the routine patient first to clear the list, since stable patients take less time", "B": "See Mrs Pike first, then Mr Le, and defer the routine review to later today", "C": "See Mr Le first while he is stable before the breathing trial, then see Mrs Pike", "D": "Attend to patients in the order the referrals arrived, to keep the allocation transparent and fair"}, "cluster_id": "CHS152", "cluster_text": "Mr Long Le, 62, a retired bus driver, is on day 6 in the intensive care unit after severe community-acquired pneumonia. He is orally intubated on pressure support ventilation (PEEP 5, FiO2 0.35). RASS 0, he follows commands, has had no sedation infusion for 48 hours, and noradrenaline was ceased 24 hours ago. HR 92, MAP 78 mmHg, RR 20, SpO2 96 percent, afebrile. Chest: coarse crackles at the right base, moderate secretions cleared by suction. He lifts all four limbs against gravity, grip is fair, trunk control in sitting is poor. Previously independent, lives with his wife. The team plans a spontaneous breathing trial this afternoon. You have four patients to see this morning. A new ward referral has just arrived: Mrs Joan Pike, post right hemicolectomy, with a sudden increase in oxygen requirement, reduced air entry at the right base and a weak cough. Another ICU patient is stable and awaiting a routine mobility review.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (See Mrs Pike first, then Mr Le, and defer the routine review to later today): A new oxygen requirement with reduced air entry and a weak cough suggests sputum plugging or lobar collapse that physiotherapy can treat, so the acute deterioration takes priority. Mr Le is stable and can follow, and the routine review can move to later. Referral order is not a sound triage rule when clinical urgency differs between patients."}, {"id": 1568, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "After two weeks of mechanical ventilation and sepsis, a patient has symmetrical flaccid weakness of all four limbs, with normal facial and eye movements, intact sensation and reduced reflexes, first noticed as sedation was lightened. Which single clinical explanation best fits these findings in a critically ill patient?", "options": {"A": "Guillain-Barre syndrome, an acute inflammatory polyneuropathy that can follow infection and cause ascending weakness", "B": "ICU-acquired weakness, a peripheral neuromuscular complication of critical illness, sepsis and prolonged immobility", "C": "Myasthenic crisis, where fluctuating weakness worsens with repeated effort and commonly involves the eyelids and eyes", "D": "A brainstem stroke from basilar artery thrombosis, producing bilateral motor loss with preserved sensation"}, "cluster_id": "CHS152", "cluster_text": "Mr Long Le, 62, a retired bus driver, is on day 6 in the intensive care unit after severe community-acquired pneumonia. He is orally intubated on pressure support ventilation (PEEP 5, FiO2 0.35). RASS 0, he follows commands, has had no sedation infusion for 48 hours, and noradrenaline was ceased 24 hours ago. HR 92, MAP 78 mmHg, RR 20, SpO2 96 percent, afebrile. Chest: coarse crackles at the right base, moderate secretions cleared by suction. He lifts all four limbs against gravity, grip is fair, trunk control in sitting is poor. Previously independent, lives with his wife. The team plans a spontaneous breathing trial this afternoon. You have four patients to see this morning. A new ward referral has just arrived: Mrs Joan Pike, post right hemicolectomy, with a sudden increase in oxygen requirement, reduced air entry at the right base and a weak cough. Another ICU patient is stable and awaiting a routine mobility review.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (ICU-acquired weakness, a peripheral neuromuscular complication of critical illness, sepsis and prolonged immobility): Symmetrical flaccid limb weakness with intact sensation, normal cranial nerve function and reduced reflexes after sepsis and prolonged ventilation is the typical picture of ICU-acquired weakness. Guillain-Barre syndrome is the closest mimic but usually shows an ascending course with sensory or facial involvement. A brainstem lesion would affect eye movements and raise tone, and myasthenic weakness fluctuates with effort."}, {"id": 1569, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "A colleague cites one small case series suggesting intensive strengthening speeds recovery in Guillain-Barre syndrome and proposes adopting it for Mr Okonkwo's current ICU programme. The intensivist asks how the physiotherapist should use this evidence. What is the most appropriate approach?", "options": {"A": "Discard it because case series cannot inform practice, and continue the unit's usual local routine", "B": "Adopt the approach since Guillain-Barre syndrome is rare and better evidence is unlikely to be published", "C": "Appraise the design and population, then weigh it against guidelines, his presentation and preferences", "D": "Wait for a systematic review of randomised trials before discussing the paper with the team"}, "cluster_id": "CHS153", "cluster_text": "Michael Okonkwo, 42, accountant, is on day 18 in intensive care with Guillain-Barre syndrome that followed a gastrointestinal illness. He has completed immunoglobulin therapy and has a tracheostomy, ventilated with pressure support while weaning is trialled. He is alert and communicates with lip movements. Strength: MRC 2/5 at the shoulders and hips, 1/5 distally, reflexes absent, bilateral facial weakness. Early contracture signs are appearing at the ankles. He reports severe aching pain in his thighs and pins and needles in his hands. Heart rate and blood pressure have been labile with position change. At the unit meeting a colleague cites one small case series reporting faster recovery with intensive strengthening in Guillain-Barre syndrome and suggests adopting it. The intensivist asks the physiotherapist to explain how to use the available evidence.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Appraise the design and population, then weigh it against guidelines, his presentation and preferences): Evidence-based practice combines appraised research with guidelines, clinical findings and the patient's values. A case series is low-level evidence that should be weighed rather than adopted or ignored, and waiting for ideal evidence withholds a considered discussion about a rare condition."}, {"id": 1570, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Within two minutes of a first sit at the edge of the bed, Mr Okonkwo's heart rate falls to 48 and blood pressure to 85/50, and he becomes pale, clammy and drowsy. Autonomic involvement is known. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Stop the session, record the instability and ask the nurse to recheck him in an hour", "B": "Lie him flat with legs elevated and monitor until observations recover, then inform the nurse", "C": "Stop and lower him gently, then repeat the same position immediately with a slower transfer pace", "D": "Continue the sit for another minute to allow his autonomic reflexes to adapt before reassessing"}, "cluster_id": "CHS153", "cluster_text": "Michael Okonkwo, 42, accountant, is on day 18 in intensive care with Guillain-Barre syndrome that followed a gastrointestinal illness. He has completed immunoglobulin therapy and has a tracheostomy, ventilated with pressure support while weaning is trialled. He is alert and communicates with lip movements. Strength: MRC 2/5 at the shoulders and hips, 1/5 distally, reflexes absent, bilateral facial weakness. Early contracture signs are appearing at the ankles. He reports severe aching pain in his thighs and pins and needles in his hands. Heart rate and blood pressure have been labile with position change. At the unit meeting a colleague cites one small case series reporting faster recovery with intensive strengthening in Guillain-Barre syndrome and suggests adopting it. The intensivist asks the physiotherapist to explain how to use the available evidence.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Lie him flat with legs elevated and monitor until observations recover, then inform the nurse): Symptomatic hypotension and bradycardia call for immediate return to a position that restores cerebral perfusion, with monitoring until recovery and handover to nursing staff. Continuing or immediately repeating the position risks syncope or arrhythmia, and leaving him unmonitored for an hour is unsafe."}, {"id": 1571, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Okonkwo has paralysed distal muscles, with ankles falling into plantarflexion and fingers into flexion, while severe neuropathic thigh pain makes handling difficult. Strength is 1/5 distally and 2/5 proximally. Which plan best prevents contracture and avoids injuring his weak muscles?", "options": {"A": "Progressive resisted exercise with weights at each joint to preserve muscle bulk while waiting for recovery", "B": "Forceful passive stretching into end range twice daily to stop shortening before it becomes established", "C": "Gentle passive range of motion and resting splints, timed around his analgesia", "D": "Exercise to fatigue in active-assisted range every session, with splints off to maximise movement"}, "cluster_id": "CHS153", "cluster_text": "Michael Okonkwo, 42, accountant, is on day 18 in intensive care with Guillain-Barre syndrome that followed a gastrointestinal illness. He has completed immunoglobulin therapy and has a tracheostomy, ventilated with pressure support while weaning is trialled. He is alert and communicates with lip movements. Strength: MRC 2/5 at the shoulders and hips, 1/5 distally, reflexes absent, bilateral facial weakness. Early contracture signs are appearing at the ankles. He reports severe aching pain in his thighs and pins and needles in his hands. Heart rate and blood pressure have been labile with position change. At the unit meeting a colleague cites one small case series reporting faster recovery with intensive strengthening in Guillain-Barre syndrome and suggests adopting it. The intensivist asks the physiotherapist to explain how to use the available evidence.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Gentle passive range of motion and resting splints, timed around his analgesia): Gentle passive movement and splinting maintain length and joint position without overstretching painful, weak tissue, and timing around analgesia improves tolerance. Forceful stretching and resisted or fatiguing exercise risk injury and overwork weakness in denervated or recovering muscle."}, {"id": 1572, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Two weeks later Mr Okonkwo's strength is improving. After a long session of repetitive active exercise, he reports profound exhaustion, and his muscle strength is lower the next morning. He is highly motivated and wants more. How should the physiotherapist adjust the programme?", "options": {"A": "Keep the same load and add a second session later each day to counter the weakness", "B": "Lower the volume and intensity, use short sessions with rests, and monitor strength next day", "C": "Stop all active exercise for several weeks and use passive movement only until strength fully returns", "D": "Switch to heavy low-repetition resistance exercise on alternate days, since repetitive work likely caused the exhaustion"}, "cluster_id": "CHS153", "cluster_text": "Michael Okonkwo, 42, accountant, is on day 18 in intensive care with Guillain-Barre syndrome that followed a gastrointestinal illness. He has completed immunoglobulin therapy and has a tracheostomy, ventilated with pressure support while weaning is trialled. He is alert and communicates with lip movements. Strength: MRC 2/5 at the shoulders and hips, 1/5 distally, reflexes absent, bilateral facial weakness. Early contracture signs are appearing at the ankles. He reports severe aching pain in his thighs and pins and needles in his hands. Heart rate and blood pressure have been labile with position change. At the unit meeting a colleague cites one small case series reporting faster recovery with intensive strengthening in Guillain-Barre syndrome and suggests adopting it. The intensivist asks the physiotherapist to explain how to use the available evidence.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Lower the volume and intensity, use short sessions with rests, and monitor strength next day): Strength falling after exercise suggests overwork in recovering muscle, so dose is reduced and paced with rest and next day monitoring. Adding sessions deepens the problem, while stopping all active work causes avoidable deconditioning and heavy resistance still overloads fragile muscle."}, {"id": 1573, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Palmer is on NIV with RR 22 and SpO2 92 percent, but a 15 minute trial off NIV gives RR 28, SpO2 89 percent and accessory muscle use. He also has thick sputum and a weak cough. Which interpretation is most useful for planning his physiotherapy session today?", "options": {"A": "He has recovered enough to stop NIV, so sputum clearance can proceed off support as usual", "B": "The faster breathing off NIV reflects anxiety about the mask, so reassurance should guide the session", "C": "His hypoxaemia off NIV suggests a cardiac cause, so treatment should wait for a cardiology opinion", "D": "He has little reserve off NIV, so treatment needs short bouts with ventilatory support readily available"}, "cluster_id": "CHS154", "cluster_text": "Geoffrey Palmer, 74, a retired bus driver, is on day 3 in intensive care after an infective exacerbation of COPD with hypercapnic respiratory failure. He has a 50 pack-year smoking history and lives with his wife. He is weaning from bilevel non-invasive ventilation (NIV) via a full face mask to intermittent use. On NIV: HR 96, BP 128/74, RR 22, SpO2 92 percent. During a 15 minute trial off NIV: RR 28, SpO2 89 percent, using accessory muscles. He has thick sputum, a weak cough, coarse crackles at the right base and reduced air entry at the left base. He is alert but anxious about the mask. The medical team has requested physiotherapy for sputum clearance and mobilisation. His daughter telephones the unit asking for a full update on his chest condition. He has not named anyone to receive information, and his wife is not present.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (He has little reserve off NIV, so treatment needs short bouts with ventilatory support readily available): Falling SpO2, a rising respiratory rate and accessory muscle use off NIV show limited ventilatory reserve, so sessions must be brief with NIV available for rest. Anxiety may add to his breathlessness, but it does not explain the hypoxaemia. The picture fits his known respiratory failure rather than a new cardiac problem."}, {"id": 1574, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Palmer fatigues quickly and has thick sputum, a weak cough and right basal crackles. He is currently using NIV intermittently and becomes more breathless during longer treatments off the mask. Which airway clearance approach is most appropriate at this stage?", "options": {"A": "Postural drainage in head-down tilt for an extended period, since gravity assisted drainage suits copious secretions", "B": "Short bouts of airway clearance with NIV rest between, using positioning and huffing while monitoring SpO2", "C": "Active cycle of breathing technique off NIV in sitting until secretions clear, accepting a lower saturation", "D": "Chest percussion and vibration in side-lying with NIV on, repeated until the right base clears"}, "cluster_id": "CHS154", "cluster_text": "Geoffrey Palmer, 74, a retired bus driver, is on day 3 in intensive care after an infective exacerbation of COPD with hypercapnic respiratory failure. He has a 50 pack-year smoking history and lives with his wife. He is weaning from bilevel non-invasive ventilation (NIV) via a full face mask to intermittent use. On NIV: HR 96, BP 128/74, RR 22, SpO2 92 percent. During a 15 minute trial off NIV: RR 28, SpO2 89 percent, using accessory muscles. He has thick sputum, a weak cough, coarse crackles at the right base and reduced air entry at the left base. He is alert but anxious about the mask. The medical team has requested physiotherapy for sputum clearance and mobilisation. His daughter telephones the unit asking for a full update on his chest condition. He has not named anyone to receive information, and his wife is not present.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Short bouts of airway clearance with NIV rest between, using positioning and huffing while monitoring SpO2): With a weak cough and little reserve off NIV, clearance is best done in short bouts with rests back on the mask, using positioning and huffing under continuous SpO2 monitoring. Long unsupported sessions off NIV risk further desaturation and exhaustion. Prolonged head-down tilt increases the work of breathing. Repeated percussion and vibration until the chest clears gives him no recovery time and adds little over active techniques he can manage."}, {"id": 1575, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Later, while sitting over the edge of the bed on intermittent NIV, Mr Palmer becomes more breathless and drowsy. His RR rises to 34 and SpO2 falls to 85 percent despite the mask being on. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Stop activity, return him to a supported position on NIV and alert the team promptly", "B": "Coach slower breathing for another minute, then continue if saturations rise towards his target range", "C": "Stop activity and return him to bed on NIV, then report at the next handover", "D": "Stop activity, support him upright on NIV, and increase the pressure settings yourself to improve ventilation"}, "cluster_id": "CHS154", "cluster_text": "Geoffrey Palmer, 74, a retired bus driver, is on day 3 in intensive care after an infective exacerbation of COPD with hypercapnic respiratory failure. He has a 50 pack-year smoking history and lives with his wife. He is weaning from bilevel non-invasive ventilation (NIV) via a full face mask to intermittent use. On NIV: HR 96, BP 128/74, RR 22, SpO2 92 percent. During a 15 minute trial off NIV: RR 28, SpO2 89 percent, using accessory muscles. He has thick sputum, a weak cough, coarse crackles at the right base and reduced air entry at the left base. He is alert but anxious about the mask. The medical team has requested physiotherapy for sputum clearance and mobilisation. His daughter telephones the unit asking for a full update on his chest condition. He has not named anyone to receive information, and his wife is not present.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stop activity, return him to a supported position on NIV and alert the team promptly): Rising respiratory rate, falling saturation and new drowsiness in hypercapnic failure signal deterioration and possible carbon dioxide narcosis, so activity stops, he is supported on NIV and the team is alerted straight away. Coaching for another minute delays recognition. Reporting only at handover leaves a deteriorating patient unreviewed. Changing ventilator pressures is a medical and nursing decision, not a physiotherapy one."}, {"id": 1576, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Palmer's daughter telephones the unit and asks the physiotherapist for details of his chest condition and treatment plan. He has not nominated anyone to receive information about him, and his wife is not present. What is the most appropriate response and documentation?", "options": {"A": "Share general information about his breathing because she is close family, and note it in notes", "B": "Give full details once she confirms his date of birth, since identity checking satisfies privacy obligations", "C": "Decline to share details without his consent, offer to ask him, and record the call", "D": "Confirm he is in intensive care and describe today's goals, then tell the nurse in charge"}, "cluster_id": "CHS154", "cluster_text": "Geoffrey Palmer, 74, a retired bus driver, is on day 3 in intensive care after an infective exacerbation of COPD with hypercapnic respiratory failure. He has a 50 pack-year smoking history and lives with his wife. He is weaning from bilevel non-invasive ventilation (NIV) via a full face mask to intermittent use. On NIV: HR 96, BP 128/74, RR 22, SpO2 92 percent. During a 15 minute trial off NIV: RR 28, SpO2 89 percent, using accessory muscles. He has thick sputum, a weak cough, coarse crackles at the right base and reduced air entry at the left base. He is alert but anxious about the mask. The medical team has requested physiotherapy for sputum clearance and mobilisation. His daughter telephones the unit asking for a full update on his chest condition. He has not named anyone to receive information, and his wife is not present.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Decline to share details without his consent, offer to ask him, and record the call): Health information belongs to the patient, so disclosure to family needs his consent unless the law allows otherwise. Offering to ask him respects his autonomy, and recording the call keeps the clinical record accurate. Being a close relative, or giving a correct date of birth, does not create a right to his details."}, {"id": 1577, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Giorgio reports blurred vision when he turns his head quickly. The physiotherapist performs the head impulse test and the Dix-Hallpike manoeuvre in clinic as part of a vestibular examination. Which bedside finding best confirms right sided peripheral vestibular hypofunction as the cause of this problem?", "options": {"A": "Upbeating torsional nystagmus on lying right with the head hanging, settling within about a minute", "B": "A corrective saccade seen after a rapid, small head turn toward the left side only", "C": "Nystagmus appearing only on lying down that fatigues quickly with repeated testing in that position", "D": "A corrective saccade seen after a rapid, small head turn toward the right side"}, "cluster_id": "CHS155", "cluster_text": "Giorgio Rossi, 74, a retired electrician, attends outpatient physiotherapy three weeks after right vestibular neuritis. He reports blurred vision and dizziness when he turns his head quickly, and feels unsteady walking in shops. Since onset he has taken a vestibular suppressant tablet three times daily, prescribed by his GP. Findings: no spontaneous nystagmus, positive head impulse test to the right, Dix-Hallpike negative bilaterally, gait slightly wide based and worse with head turns. Lying and standing blood pressure are similar and he has no light headedness on standing. His dizziness handicap score is in the moderate range. He lives with his wife, walks daily and wants to return to his weekly bowls club.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A corrective saccade seen after a rapid, small head turn toward the right side): In a right peripheral deficit the vestibulo-ocular reflex fails during fast head turns toward the affected side, so the eyes drift off target and a corrective saccade follows. A saccade after turns to the left would indicate the other ear. The two nystagmus patterns describe positional vertigo, which his negative Dix-Hallpike has excluded."}, {"id": 1578, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Giorgio's examination has excluded positional vertigo and shows reduced vestibulo-ocular reflex gain on the right, with unsteadiness when turning his head. He lives with his wife and wants to resume bowls. Which exercise programme is most appropriate to start for his deficit?", "options": {"A": "Canalith repositioning manoeuvres repeated daily, because his symptoms arise from displaced debris in the posterior canal", "B": "Gaze stabilisation with head turns on a fixed target, progressing speed and background gradually", "C": "Brandt-Daroff habituation exercises from lying to sitting, repeated several times each day until dizziness subsides", "D": "Avoiding fast head movements for several weeks, with seated rest, to let the inner ear recover"}, "cluster_id": "CHS155", "cluster_text": "Giorgio Rossi, 74, a retired electrician, attends outpatient physiotherapy three weeks after right vestibular neuritis. He reports blurred vision and dizziness when he turns his head quickly, and feels unsteady walking in shops. Since onset he has taken a vestibular suppressant tablet three times daily, prescribed by his GP. Findings: no spontaneous nystagmus, positive head impulse test to the right, Dix-Hallpike negative bilaterally, gait slightly wide based and worse with head turns. Lying and standing blood pressure are similar and he has no light headedness on standing. His dizziness handicap score is in the moderate range. He lives with his wife, walks daily and wants to return to his weekly bowls club.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Gaze stabilisation with head turns on a fixed target, progressing speed and background gradually): Gaze stabilisation exercises drive adaptation of the vestibulo-ocular reflex and are the core treatment for peripheral hypofunction. Repositioning and Brandt-Daroff exercises treat benign paroxysmal positional vertigo, which has been excluded. Prolonged avoidance of movement delays the central compensation he needs."}, {"id": 1579, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "At review, Giorgio says moderate dizziness during his gaze exercises takes about thirty minutes to settle, so he stopped doing them after two days. He is keen to return to bowls but found the symptoms frightening. What is the most appropriate adjustment to his programme?", "options": {"A": "Shorten each bout and slow head speed, keeping daily practice so symptoms settle within minutes", "B": "Stop exercises until he is symptom free, then restart at the same level and speed", "C": "Reduce practice to one supervised session each week so unsupervised symptom flares are avoided at home", "D": "Lengthen each bout and speed up head movements so he habituates sooner despite longer lasting symptoms"}, "cluster_id": "CHS155", "cluster_text": "Giorgio Rossi, 74, a retired electrician, attends outpatient physiotherapy three weeks after right vestibular neuritis. He reports blurred vision and dizziness when he turns his head quickly, and feels unsteady walking in shops. Since onset he has taken a vestibular suppressant tablet three times daily, prescribed by his GP. Findings: no spontaneous nystagmus, positive head impulse test to the right, Dix-Hallpike negative bilaterally, gait slightly wide based and worse with head turns. Lying and standing blood pressure are similar and he has no light headedness on standing. His dizziness handicap score is in the moderate range. He lives with his wife, walks daily and wants to return to his weekly bowls club.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Shorten each bout and slow head speed, keeping daily practice so symptoms settle within minutes): Exercises should provoke mild to moderate symptoms that settle quickly, so reducing duration and speed while keeping frequent practice maintains the stimulus for compensation. Stopping altogether loses that stimulus. Increasing the load worsens tolerance, and weekly sessions are too infrequent to drive adaptation."}, {"id": 1580, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Giorgio has taken a vestibular suppressant three times daily for three weeks, and his exercises are now limited by symptoms. He sees his GP regularly and has consented to the physiotherapist sharing information. How should the physiotherapist communicate about this with the rest of his care team?", "options": {"A": "Leave medication unmentioned as it is outside scope, and record exercise progress in the physiotherapy notes", "B": "Phone the pharmacist to request a lower dose, since pharmacists manage dose changes for suppressants", "C": "Write to the GP with findings, noting suppressants may slow compensation, and request medication review", "D": "Advise Giorgio to stop the tablets because they slow compensation, then tell the GP afterwards"}, "cluster_id": "CHS155", "cluster_text": "Giorgio Rossi, 74, a retired electrician, attends outpatient physiotherapy three weeks after right vestibular neuritis. He reports blurred vision and dizziness when he turns his head quickly, and feels unsteady walking in shops. Since onset he has taken a vestibular suppressant tablet three times daily, prescribed by his GP. Findings: no spontaneous nystagmus, positive head impulse test to the right, Dix-Hallpike negative bilaterally, gait slightly wide based and worse with head turns. Lying and standing blood pressure are similar and he has no light headedness on standing. His dizziness handicap score is in the moderate range. He lives with his wife, walks daily and wants to return to his weekly bowls club.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Write to the GP with findings, noting suppressants may slow compensation, and request medication review): Prescribing decisions belong to the GP, but the physiotherapist should share relevant findings and the concern that long term suppressants can impede compensation, and request review. Telling a patient to stop a prescribed medicine oversteps scope. Silence withholds important information, and a pharmacist does not adjust prescribed doses independently."}, {"id": 1581, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Bianchi leaks only with coughing, sneezing and lifting, with no urgency, a clear urinalysis and a normal voiding pattern in her diary. You are planning the first stage of her physiotherapy programme this week. Which interpretation and plan are most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Overflow incontinence from poor emptying, so teach intermittent catheterisation before any strengthening exercise is begun", "B": "Stress incontinence, so begin supervised pelvic floor muscle training and continue the diary", "C": "Mixed incontinence needing urodynamic testing first, so defer physiotherapy until specialist assessment is completed", "D": "Urge incontinence from an overactive bladder, so start bladder retraining with timed voiding first"}, "cluster_id": "CHS156", "cluster_text": "Mrs Rosa Bianchi, 71, a retired shop owner, was referred by her GP to a community physiotherapy clinic for urine leakage. She leaks small amounts when she coughs, sneezes or lifts her grandchild, and has done so for about three years. She has no urgency, no night time voiding and no pain or blood in her urine. She has had three vaginal births, has constipation and a BMI of 31. A urinalysis arranged by her GP was clear. Her bladder diary shows six voids daily and two leaks, both with coughing. With her written consent, a pelvic floor muscle assessment found a weak squeeze, Oxford grade 2 out of 5, sustained for three seconds, with good relaxation and no prolapse symptoms. She uses vaginal oestrogen cream prescribed by her GP. She is motivated and attends weekly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stress incontinence, so begin supervised pelvic floor muscle training and continue the diary): Leaking only with raised abdominal pressure, normal voiding and a clear urinalysis point to stress incontinence, for which supervised pelvic floor muscle training is the first line physiotherapy treatment. Urge and overflow patterns need urgency or poor emptying that she does not report, and nothing here requires deferring treatment."}, {"id": 1582, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Her squeeze is Oxford grade 2 and she can hold three seconds. She asks which exercises give the quickest improvement. Considering her findings and the aim of preventing leaks with coughing, which prescription is most appropriate to start in the first weeks?", "options": {"A": "Daily abdominal crunches and sit ups to strengthen the core, which lifts the bladder neck indirectly", "B": "Fast repeated buttock squeezes and gluteal bracing during standing, to recruit the larger muscles first", "C": "Stopping urine midstream at each toilet visit as the main exercise, to train the muscle repeatedly", "D": "Maximal squeezes held for her tolerated time, repeated daily, plus a squeeze before each cough"}, "cluster_id": "CHS156", "cluster_text": "Mrs Rosa Bianchi, 71, a retired shop owner, was referred by her GP to a community physiotherapy clinic for urine leakage. She leaks small amounts when she coughs, sneezes or lifts her grandchild, and has done so for about three years. She has no urgency, no night time voiding and no pain or blood in her urine. She has had three vaginal births, has constipation and a BMI of 31. A urinalysis arranged by her GP was clear. Her bladder diary shows six voids daily and two leaks, both with coughing. With her written consent, a pelvic floor muscle assessment found a weak squeeze, Oxford grade 2 out of 5, sustained for three seconds, with good relaxation and no prolapse symptoms. She uses vaginal oestrogen cream prescribed by her GP. She is motivated and attends weekly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Maximal squeezes held for her tolerated time, repeated daily, plus a squeeze before each cough): A weak muscle responds to maximal effort squeezes held for a sustainable time and repeated through the day, and a squeeze timed before a cough protects against leaks. Habitual midstream stopping can disturb emptying, and gluteal bracing or crunches do not train the pelvic floor specifically."}, {"id": 1583, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "At her fourth visit Mrs Bianchi reports that she has seen visible blood in her urine on two occasions this week, with no burning or pain. Her leaks are unchanged. What is the most appropriate physiotherapy response to this new information?", "options": {"A": "Continue the programme, asking her to report if bleeding recurs, as her urinalysis was clear", "B": "Pause progression and refer her promptly to her GP for investigation of the haematuria", "C": "Refer her back to the GP only if bleeding continues after two more weeks of treatment", "D": "Pause progression and ask the continence nurse to review her at the next clinic visit"}, "cluster_id": "CHS156", "cluster_text": "Mrs Rosa Bianchi, 71, a retired shop owner, was referred by her GP to a community physiotherapy clinic for urine leakage. She leaks small amounts when she coughs, sneezes or lifts her grandchild, and has done so for about three years. She has no urgency, no night time voiding and no pain or blood in her urine. She has had three vaginal births, has constipation and a BMI of 31. A urinalysis arranged by her GP was clear. Her bladder diary shows six voids daily and two leaks, both with coughing. With her written consent, a pelvic floor muscle assessment found a weak squeeze, Oxford grade 2 out of 5, sustained for three seconds, with good relaxation and no prolapse symptoms. She uses vaginal oestrogen cream prescribed by her GP. She is motivated and attends weekly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Pause progression and refer her promptly to her GP for investigation of the haematuria): Visible blood in the urine is a red flag in a 71-year-old and needs prompt medical investigation, so progression is paused and she is referred to her GP. An earlier clear urinalysis does not explain new visible bleeding. Waiting two more weeks delays diagnosis of a possible serious cause. A continence nurse can advise on bladder management but cannot investigate haematuria, so the referral goes to the medical practitioner."}, {"id": 1584, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "At her fifth visit Mrs Bianchi, pleased with her progress, gives you a gift voucher worth a substantial amount and invites you to her family birthday dinner. She is still your active patient. What is the most appropriate response from you?", "options": {"A": "Accept the voucher to avoid offence, decline the dinner, and mention it to a colleague", "B": "Decline the voucher and invitation kindly, explain professional boundaries, and record the offer", "C": "Accept the dinner invitation as a social gesture, keeping any clinical discussion out of the evening", "D": "Say you will accept both once her care ends, since boundaries apply during treatment only"}, "cluster_id": "CHS156", "cluster_text": "Mrs Rosa Bianchi, 71, a retired shop owner, was referred by her GP to a community physiotherapy clinic for urine leakage. She leaks small amounts when she coughs, sneezes or lifts her grandchild, and has done so for about three years. She has no urgency, no night time voiding and no pain or blood in her urine. She has had three vaginal births, has constipation and a BMI of 31. A urinalysis arranged by her GP was clear. Her bladder diary shows six voids daily and two leaks, both with coughing. With her written consent, a pelvic floor muscle assessment found a weak squeeze, Oxford grade 2 out of 5, sustained for three seconds, with good relaxation and no prolapse symptoms. She uses vaginal oestrogen cream prescribed by her GP. She is motivated and attends weekly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Decline the voucher and invitation kindly, explain professional boundaries, and record the offer): Accepting a substantial gift or social invitation from a current patient blurs the therapeutic boundary and can compromise judgement and trust. A kind refusal that explains the reason and documents the offer protects both of you. Accepting only part, or promising to accept later, does not remove the conflict during active care."}, {"id": 1585, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Sione has shallow breathing, an ineffective cough, reduced air entry at the left base and crackles two days after rib fractures. He smokes and has mild COPD, and his pain on deep breathing is 8/10. Which problem should the physiotherapist treat as the priority?", "options": {"A": "Opioid-related drowsiness and confusion, risking reduced respiratory drive and falls", "B": "Reduced exercise tolerance from bed rest, which needs early graded walking before discharge", "C": "His refusal of breathing exercises, which needs a capacity assessment before any treatment continues", "D": "Pain-limited breathing and weak cough, risking sputum retention and left basal atelectasis"}, "cluster_id": "CHS157", "cluster_text": "Sione Taufa, 58, a truck driver, was admitted to a rural hospital two days ago after falling from his truck cab. Chest X-ray shows left rib fractures 5 to 8 with no pneumothorax. He smokes and has mild COPD. Today: pain 8/10 on deep breathing, RR 24, HR 96, BP 138/84 mmHg, SpO2 94% on 2 L/min oxygen via nasal prongs. He breathes shallowly, guards the left side, and cannot cough effectively. Auscultation shows reduced air entry at the left base with a few crackles. He takes paracetamol and prescribed oxycodone as needed, and there is no regional anaesthesia available. Nurses report he was disoriented to time overnight after oxycodone. This morning he is alert, oriented, and says he does not want to do any breathing exercises and wants to discharge himself.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Pain-limited breathing and weak cough, risking sputum retention and left basal atelectasis): Painful rib fractures, shallow breathing, a weak cough and basal crackles in a smoker with COPD put him at immediate risk of sputum retention and atelectasis, so airway clearance and lung expansion come first. Overnight disorientation after oxycodone is worth reporting but he is now alert and oriented. Reduced exercise tolerance can be addressed once breathing is safe. He is alert and oriented, so capacity is presumed and his refusal is better addressed by explaining the risks."}, {"id": 1586, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Sione refuses breathing exercises and wants to leave, but nurses note he was disoriented overnight after oxycodone. This morning he is alert, oriented and talking clearly, and he gives reasons for leaving. What should the physiotherapist do about his refusal before the next session?", "options": {"A": "Treat the refusal as invalid because of last night's disorientation, and continue treating him", "B": "Accept the refusal immediately, record it, and stop all physiotherapy input out of respect for autonomy", "C": "Ask his wife to consent for him as next of kin, given the disorientation overnight", "D": "Assess whether he can understand, retain, weigh and communicate this decision, explaining the risks"}, "cluster_id": "CHS157", "cluster_text": "Sione Taufa, 58, a truck driver, was admitted to a rural hospital two days ago after falling from his truck cab. Chest X-ray shows left rib fractures 5 to 8 with no pneumothorax. He smokes and has mild COPD. Today: pain 8/10 on deep breathing, RR 24, HR 96, BP 138/84 mmHg, SpO2 94% on 2 L/min oxygen via nasal prongs. He breathes shallowly, guards the left side, and cannot cough effectively. Auscultation shows reduced air entry at the left base with a few crackles. He takes paracetamol and prescribed oxycodone as needed, and there is no regional anaesthesia available. Nurses report he was disoriented to time overnight after oxycodone. This morning he is alert, oriented, and says he does not want to do any breathing exercises and wants to discharge himself.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Assess whether he can understand, retain, weigh and communicate this decision, explaining the risks): Capacity is decision-specific and can fluctuate, so it is assessed at the time of the decision, and an adult with capacity may refuse after the risks are explained. Past disorientation does not remove his right today. Stopping all input without discussing risk, or asking a relative to consent, both bypass a proper assessment."}, {"id": 1587, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Sione agrees to try treatment after the risks were explained. His pain is 8/10 on deep breaths and he cannot cough effectively with crackles at the left base. What is the most appropriate approach to airway clearance in his present condition?", "options": {"A": "Time sessions after analgesia, then teach supported deep breathing and coughing in upright sitting", "B": "Teach deep breathing and supported coughing in upright sitting, then request analgesia if pain persists", "C": "Use gentle positioning and humidified oxygen alone, and avoid coughing until his pain settles further", "D": "Prescribe an hourly incentive spirometer routine in supported sitting and leave secretion clearance to nursing staff"}, "cluster_id": "CHS157", "cluster_text": "Sione Taufa, 58, a truck driver, was admitted to a rural hospital two days ago after falling from his truck cab. Chest X-ray shows left rib fractures 5 to 8 with no pneumothorax. He smokes and has mild COPD. Today: pain 8/10 on deep breathing, RR 24, HR 96, BP 138/84 mmHg, SpO2 94% on 2 L/min oxygen via nasal prongs. He breathes shallowly, guards the left side, and cannot cough effectively. Auscultation shows reduced air entry at the left base with a few crackles. He takes paracetamol and prescribed oxycodone as needed, and there is no regional anaesthesia available. Nurses report he was disoriented to time overnight after oxycodone. This morning he is alert, oriented, and says he does not want to do any breathing exercises and wants to discharge himself.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Time sessions after analgesia, then teach supported deep breathing and coughing in upright sitting): Timing sessions after analgesia and using upright positions with a supported cough lets him clear secretions while his pain is controlled. Starting without analgesia makes an effective cough unlikely at this pain level. Positioning and humidified oxygen without active coughing leaves secretions in the lungs. An incentive spirometer helps lung expansion but does not teach a supported cough or address his retained secretions."}, {"id": 1588, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "While walking in the corridor Sione becomes acutely breathless, with RR 32 and SpO2 87% on oxygen. The left chest has absent breath sounds and a hyperresonant percussion note. What should the physiotherapist do first in this emergency on the ward?", "options": {"A": "Sit him in a supported breathing control position, then call the nurse if saturation stays low", "B": "Return him to bed, increase his oxygen supply, and ask the nurse to review him soon", "C": "Position him in right side-lying and perform airway clearance techniques for a suspected mucus plug", "D": "Stop activity, sit him upright with oxygen continuing, and call the emergency response team"}, "cluster_id": "CHS157", "cluster_text": "Sione Taufa, 58, a truck driver, was admitted to a rural hospital two days ago after falling from his truck cab. Chest X-ray shows left rib fractures 5 to 8 with no pneumothorax. He smokes and has mild COPD. Today: pain 8/10 on deep breathing, RR 24, HR 96, BP 138/84 mmHg, SpO2 94% on 2 L/min oxygen via nasal prongs. He breathes shallowly, guards the left side, and cannot cough effectively. Auscultation shows reduced air entry at the left base with a few crackles. He takes paracetamol and prescribed oxycodone as needed, and there is no regional anaesthesia available. Nurses report he was disoriented to time overnight after oxycodone. This morning he is alert, oriented, and says he does not want to do any breathing exercises and wants to discharge himself.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stop activity, sit him upright with oxygen continuing, and call the emergency response team): Sudden severe breathlessness with absent breath sounds and hyperresonance after rib fractures suggests a pneumothorax, which is a life-threatening problem needing an immediate medical response while oxygen continues and he is kept upright. Breathing control first delays the call. Returning him to bed and asking for a review soon, or changing oxygen without orders, does not match the urgency. Airway clearance for a mucus plug misreads the examination findings."}, {"id": 1589, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Ms Joshi asks to switch to high-intensity intervals after reading about one small trial in the news. You want to respond using evidence and guidelines for people after myocardial infarction. She is currently symptom free. What is the most appropriate approach?", "options": {"A": "Accept the findings because peer-reviewed publication confirms interval training is safe after infarction", "B": "Appraise the trial's population and design, check current guidelines, and individualise intensity with her cardiologist", "C": "Decline interval training because guidelines permit only continuous moderate exercise after a myocardial infarction", "D": "Introduce intervals gradually without further review, since her ejection fraction is normal and she tolerates effort"}, "cluster_id": "CHS158", "cluster_text": "Anjali Joshi, 58, primary school teacher, week 3 of an outpatient cardiac rehabilitation program, six weeks after an NSTEMI treated with a drug-eluting stent to the left anterior descending artery. Ejection fraction 52 percent at discharge. Medications: beta blocker, aspirin and a statin. Resting HR 62, BP 124/76, SpO2 98 percent. Resting telemetry shows sinus rhythm with occasional isolated ventricular ectopics. Her peak effort so far was Borg 12 with no symptoms. She has read a news article about a small trial of high-intensity interval training and asks to switch, saying it will get her back to work faster. A cardiac nurse is on site and a defibrillator is available in the gym.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Appraise the trial's population and design, check current guidelines, and individualise intensity with her cardiologist): A single small trial may not match her age, risk or stent history, so you appraise its design and population and compare it with current guidelines before changing intensity. Prescribing for an individual after myocardial infarction involves her cardiologist. Blanket acceptance or blanket refusal both ignore appraisal, and a normal ejection fraction alone does not make intervals safe."}, {"id": 1590, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "During cycling at Borg 12, Ms Joshi's telemetry shows a run of several consecutive fast ventricular beats and she feels light-headed. She is on a stationary bike with the nurse nearby. Which description best identifies this rhythm change and its implication for the session?", "options": {"A": "Non-sustained ventricular tachycardia, which is a criterion for stopping exercise immediately and calling the nurse", "B": "Atrial flutter with variable conduction, which needs monitoring but not an immediate stop", "C": "Frequent isolated ventricular ectopics, which are common and permit continuing at a lower intensity with monitoring", "D": "Sinus tachycardia from exertion, which is expected and allows the session to continue with monitoring"}, "cluster_id": "CHS158", "cluster_text": "Anjali Joshi, 58, primary school teacher, week 3 of an outpatient cardiac rehabilitation program, six weeks after an NSTEMI treated with a drug-eluting stent to the left anterior descending artery. Ejection fraction 52 percent at discharge. Medications: beta blocker, aspirin and a statin. Resting HR 62, BP 124/76, SpO2 98 percent. Resting telemetry shows sinus rhythm with occasional isolated ventricular ectopics. Her peak effort so far was Borg 12 with no symptoms. She has read a news article about a small trial of high-intensity interval training and asks to switch, saying it will get her back to work faster. A cardiac nurse is on site and a defibrillator is available in the gym.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Non-sustained ventricular tachycardia, which is a criterion for stopping exercise immediately and calling the nurse): Three or more consecutive ventricular beats at a fast rate is ventricular tachycardia, and with symptoms it meets stopping criteria for exercise. Isolated ectopics do not form a run. Sinus tachycardia has narrow complexes with a preceding P wave and is an expected exertional response, so it does not fit this strip."}, {"id": 1591, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Ms Joshi stopped exercising after the run of ventricular beats. Within three minutes she is seated, symptom free, in sinus rhythm with HR 78 and BP 120/74. Her last cycling effort was Borg 12. What is the most appropriate next step in the session?", "options": {"A": "Resume light cycling at a lower workload once she has been symptom free for five minutes", "B": "Complete a gentle cool-down walk to avoid blood pooling, then send her home with usual advice", "C": "Keep her seated on telemetry, inform the nurse and cardiologist, and stop exercise today", "D": "Repeat a resting ECG tomorrow before deciding whether she may join the next group session"}, "cluster_id": "CHS158", "cluster_text": "Anjali Joshi, 58, primary school teacher, week 3 of an outpatient cardiac rehabilitation program, six weeks after an NSTEMI treated with a drug-eluting stent to the left anterior descending artery. Ejection fraction 52 percent at discharge. Medications: beta blocker, aspirin and a statin. Resting HR 62, BP 124/76, SpO2 98 percent. Resting telemetry shows sinus rhythm with occasional isolated ventricular ectopics. Her peak effort so far was Borg 12 with no symptoms. She has read a news article about a small trial of high-intensity interval training and asks to switch, saying it will get her back to work faster. A cardiac nurse is on site and a defibrillator is available in the gym.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Keep her seated on telemetry, inform the nurse and cardiologist, and stop exercise today): Even with prompt recovery, a symptomatic ventricular arrhythmia during exercise needs medical review before more exertion, so the session ends and she stays monitored. Resuming exercise or walking her home skips that review. Waiting for a next-day ECG without informing the treating team leaves the event unreported."}, {"id": 1592, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Two weeks later during treadmill walking at the usual workload, she reports no chest pain, but the monitored ECG shows new horizontal ST depression absent at rest, with HR 96 and BP 130/78. She seems keen to keep going. What should the physiotherapist do?", "options": {"A": "Record the change and continue, since beta blockers commonly cause non-specific ST changes at this rate", "B": "Stop the exercise, sit her down and seek medical review, as silent ischaemia is possible", "C": "Reduce the speed by one level and continue, because she has no chest pain or breathlessness", "D": "Continue to the end of the interval and compare the ST segment in recovery before acting"}, "cluster_id": "CHS158", "cluster_text": "Anjali Joshi, 58, primary school teacher, week 3 of an outpatient cardiac rehabilitation program, six weeks after an NSTEMI treated with a drug-eluting stent to the left anterior descending artery. Ejection fraction 52 percent at discharge. Medications: beta blocker, aspirin and a statin. Resting HR 62, BP 124/76, SpO2 98 percent. Resting telemetry shows sinus rhythm with occasional isolated ventricular ectopics. Her peak effort so far was Borg 12 with no symptoms. She has read a news article about a small trial of high-intensity interval training and asks to switch, saying it will get her back to work faster. A cardiac nurse is on site and a defibrillator is available in the gym.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stop the exercise, sit her down and seek medical review, as silent ischaemia is possible): New exertional ST depression after an infarction can indicate myocardial ischaemia even without pain, and it is a standard reason to stop and seek medical review. Absence of symptoms does not make it safe to carry on. Beta blockers do not typically produce new horizontal ST depression at this low heart rate, so that explanation should not be assumed."}, {"id": 1593, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Rosa wants to keep preparing her own dinner but stops after ten minutes and then rests for hours. Her fatigue is worst mid-afternoon. Which energy conservation advice should the physiotherapist give first to help her complete the task safely at home?", "options": {"A": "Prepare the whole meal in one continuous afternoon session, then take a single long rest afterwards", "B": "Push through the tiredness to build tolerance, resting only when the fatigue becomes heavy", "C": "Rest until her energy fully returns each day, and cook only on days she feels fresh", "D": "Split the task into short parts with planned seated rests, and prepare it in the morning"}, "cluster_id": "CHS159", "cluster_text": "Rosa Greco, 78, has had secondary progressive multiple sclerosis for 30 years and lives alone, walking with a stick. This is a telehealth session on a secure video platform, using her tablet in the kitchen. Her daughter Elena has joined from the same room. Rosa reports fatigue worst in the mid-afternoon, which stops her cooking after ten minutes and leaves her resting for hours. Her legs feel weaker and her vision blurs in hot weather, settling when she cools down. She also mentions bladder urgency. On screen she walks safely across the room with the stick. She had a near fall last month when rising from a chair. Her rating of perceived exertion after five sit-to-stands in clinic was 'somewhat hard'. No consent for family attendance has been recorded yet.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Split the task into short parts with planned seated rests, and prepare it in the morning): Pacing breaks an activity into manageable parts with planned rests before fatigue builds, and puts demanding tasks at the time of day when energy is highest. Pushing through fatigue or working in one long session tends to increase fatigue for hours afterwards. Waiting for full recovery leads to deconditioning and reduces her independence."}, {"id": 1594, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Rosa notices her legs weaken and her vision blur in a hot kitchen in summer, and both settle after she cools down. She has had no new symptoms otherwise. Which explanation and advice is most appropriate for exercise and daily tasks?", "options": {"A": "This indicates a relapse, so stop exercise and arrange an urgent neurologist review this week", "B": "Heat temporarily worsens nerve conduction, so exercise in a cool room and use cooling strategies", "C": "Dehydration alone causes these symptoms, so she should drink more fluid and continue activity as usual", "D": "Heat gradually builds tolerance in damaged nerves, so exercising in warm conditions trains her tolerance"}, "cluster_id": "CHS159", "cluster_text": "Rosa Greco, 78, has had secondary progressive multiple sclerosis for 30 years and lives alone, walking with a stick. This is a telehealth session on a secure video platform, using her tablet in the kitchen. Her daughter Elena has joined from the same room. Rosa reports fatigue worst in the mid-afternoon, which stops her cooking after ten minutes and leaves her resting for hours. Her legs feel weaker and her vision blurs in hot weather, settling when she cools down. She also mentions bladder urgency. On screen she walks safely across the room with the stick. She had a near fall last month when rising from a chair. Her rating of perceived exertion after five sit-to-stands in clinic was 'somewhat hard'. No consent for family attendance has been recorded yet.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Heat temporarily worsens nerve conduction, so exercise in a cool room and use cooling strategies): Uhthoff phenomenon is a temporary worsening of symptoms in multiple sclerosis when body temperature rises, caused by reduced conduction in demyelinated nerves, and it settles on cooling. It is not a relapse, so cooling strategies and exercising in a cool environment are appropriate. Training in heat risks worsening symptoms and falls."}, {"id": 1595, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Rosa lives alone and Elena will leave in ten minutes. The physiotherapist wants a five times sit-to-stand test to estimate leg strength and falls risk. Rosa is willing to try it now using a dining chair. What is the most appropriate decision?", "options": {"A": "Do the test now while Elena is present, with the chair placed against a wall", "B": "Do the test after Elena leaves, as it should reflect how she manages when alone", "C": "Skip physical testing by telehealth, and plan the program from her self-reported fatigue score", "D": "Do the test now beside the bench, with her phone in reach to call for help"}, "cluster_id": "CHS159", "cluster_text": "Rosa Greco, 78, has had secondary progressive multiple sclerosis for 30 years and lives alone, walking with a stick. This is a telehealth session on a secure video platform, using her tablet in the kitchen. Her daughter Elena has joined from the same room. Rosa reports fatigue worst in the mid-afternoon, which stops her cooking after ten minutes and leaves her resting for hours. Her legs feel weaker and her vision blurs in hot weather, settling when she cools down. She also mentions bladder urgency. On screen she walks safely across the room with the stick. She had a near fall last month when rising from a chair. Her rating of perceived exertion after five sit-to-stands in clinic was 'somewhat hard'. No consent for family attendance has been recorded yet.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Do the test now while Elena is present, with the chair placed against a wall): Remote testing is reasonable when the physiotherapist arranges safety, and a person with MS who had a recent near fall needs someone physically present and a stable chair against a wall. A phone within reach does not prevent a fall during the test, and testing after Elena leaves removes the only safeguard. Self-report alone would leave leg strength unmeasured."}, {"id": 1596, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Rosa glances at Elena and says she would rather not talk about her bladder urgency, but Elena keeps answering for her. No consent for family attendance has been recorded. The session has twenty minutes remaining. What should the physiotherapist do next?", "options": {"A": "Ask Rosa to type her concerns into the chat and save the transcript in the record", "B": "Ask Rosa whether she wants Elena present, offer privacy, and document attendance and her consent", "C": "End the video session and rebook in person, noting video cannot protect her confidentiality", "D": "Continue with Elena present, as family input aids history, and record the bladder concern from Elena"}, "cluster_id": "CHS159", "cluster_text": "Rosa Greco, 78, has had secondary progressive multiple sclerosis for 30 years and lives alone, walking with a stick. This is a telehealth session on a secure video platform, using her tablet in the kitchen. Her daughter Elena has joined from the same room. Rosa reports fatigue worst in the mid-afternoon, which stops her cooking after ten minutes and leaves her resting for hours. Her legs feel weaker and her vision blurs in hot weather, settling when she cools down. She also mentions bladder urgency. On screen she walks safely across the room with the stick. She had a near fall last month when rising from a chair. Her rating of perceived exertion after five sit-to-stands in clinic was 'somewhat hard'. No consent for family attendance has been recorded yet.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ask Rosa whether she wants Elena present, offer privacy, and document attendance and her consent): Rosa's wish for privacy must be respected, so the physiotherapist should ask her directly about Elena's presence, offer a private moment and record who attended and what she agreed to. Taking information from Elena that Rosa wants to withhold breaches her confidentiality. Ending the session or using a typed chat does not solve the problem, since the screen may be seen by others in the room."}, {"id": 1597, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "On day one the nerve block is wearing off and pain is 6/10. Under the surgeon's protocol, active shoulder movement is not allowed. Which exercise set is most appropriate to teach Robert for the operated limb today while he remains in the sling?", "options": {"A": "Active hand, wrist and elbow movement while the shoulder stays supported and still", "B": "Active assisted shoulder flexion using a wand held in both hands, to prevent early stiffness developing", "C": "Scapular retraction and shoulder shrug exercises with light resistance to maintain posture and muscle strength", "D": "Submaximal isometric shoulder abduction and external rotation against the sling to keep the cuff muscles active"}, "cluster_id": "CHS160", "cluster_text": "Robert Haddad, 62, a retired plumber, is on day 1 after arthroscopic repair of a full thickness supraspinatus tear in his dominant right shoulder, done under general anaesthetic with an interscalene nerve block that is now wearing off. History: hypertension and type 2 diabetes, both medicated. He wears an abduction sling. The surgeon's protocol: sling on except for hygiene and exercises, passive shoulder movement only for six weeks, no active shoulder movement. Observations: BP 138/84 mmHg, HR 78, SpO2 96 percent on room air, afebrile. Pain 6/10. The dressing is dry. He can move his fingers, with some reduced sensation over the lateral forearm. The nurse reports he pushed through his right arm during a bed to chair transfer this morning. Later, the ward allied health assistant mentions the sling was off during a meal.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Active hand, wrist and elbow movement while the shoulder stays supported and still): Distal joint movement reduces swelling and stiffness without loading the repair, provided the shoulder stays supported. Active assisted movement, cuff isometrics and resisted scapular work all recruit muscles that act on the repaired tendon, which the protocol forbids in the early weeks."}, {"id": 1598, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Robert needs to transfer from bed to chair this morning. He tends to push up through his right arm and is drowsy, with reduced sensation in the forearm from the interscalene block. Which approach to the transfer is safest for the repair?", "options": {"A": "Push up with the left arm, sling on, with a staff member supervising closely", "B": "Let him push through both hands with the operated arm close to his side for stability", "C": "Remove the sling to let him move more freely and lower the risk of a fall", "D": "Use a belted stand transfer, with him steadying on a staff member using the operated hand"}, "cluster_id": "CHS160", "cluster_text": "Robert Haddad, 62, a retired plumber, is on day 1 after arthroscopic repair of a full thickness supraspinatus tear in his dominant right shoulder, done under general anaesthetic with an interscalene nerve block that is now wearing off. History: hypertension and type 2 diabetes, both medicated. He wears an abduction sling. The surgeon's protocol: sling on except for hygiene and exercises, passive shoulder movement only for six weeks, no active shoulder movement. Observations: BP 138/84 mmHg, HR 78, SpO2 96 percent on room air, afebrile. Pain 6/10. The dressing is dry. He can move his fingers, with some reduced sensation over the lateral forearm. The nurse reports he pushed through his right arm during a bed to chair transfer this morning. Later, the ward allied health assistant mentions the sling was off during a meal.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Push up with the left arm, sling on, with a staff member supervising closely): Pushing through the operated arm loads the rotator cuff repair, so he should use the other arm under close supervision with the sling protecting the shoulder. Pushing through both hands, or steadying on a staff member with the operated hand, loads the repair even when the arm is kept close. Removing the sling takes away protection while his sensation and alertness are reduced."}, {"id": 1599, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "On day three Robert's pain is 3/10 and he asks why he cannot lift his arm himself for the first six weeks, as he feels much better. He wants to return to plumbing work soon. Which explanation should the physiotherapist give?", "options": {"A": "The nerve block weakens the muscles, so lifting stays unsafe until sensation returns to the arm", "B": "Early active lifting risks adhesive capsulitis, which is the main concern for a diabetic shoulder", "C": "Muscle contraction stresses the repair before the tendon has healed into the bone", "D": "Early active movement raises blood pressure, which is unsafe given his hypertension and recent anaesthetic"}, "cluster_id": "CHS160", "cluster_text": "Robert Haddad, 62, a retired plumber, is on day 1 after arthroscopic repair of a full thickness supraspinatus tear in his dominant right shoulder, done under general anaesthetic with an interscalene nerve block that is now wearing off. History: hypertension and type 2 diabetes, both medicated. He wears an abduction sling. The surgeon's protocol: sling on except for hygiene and exercises, passive shoulder movement only for six weeks, no active shoulder movement. Observations: BP 138/84 mmHg, HR 78, SpO2 96 percent on room air, afebrile. Pain 6/10. The dressing is dry. He can move his fingers, with some reduced sensation over the lateral forearm. The nurse reports he pushed through his right arm during a bed to chair transfer this morning. Later, the ward allied health assistant mentions the sling was off during a meal.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Muscle contraction stresses the repair before the tendon has healed into the bone): A repaired tendon needs time to biologically attach to bone, and active muscle contraction pulls on it and can cause the repair to fail. Stiffness is a risk of the whole recovery, but the active restriction is protecting the repair. The block wears off within a day or two, so it does not explain a six week rule."}, {"id": 1600, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "The assistant confirms she found the sling off for about ten minutes during a meal and replaced it without telling anyone. Robert reports no new pain, and wound and neurovascular checks are unchanged. What is the most appropriate action by the physiotherapist?", "options": {"A": "Notify the surgical team, lodge an incident report, and revisit sling education with Robert", "B": "Chart the event and watch him, as no harm resulted so no report is needed", "C": "Teach Robert the sling rules again and ask him to mention it at his next appointment", "D": "Counsel the assistant privately about following the protocol, and report only if Robert's shoulder pain worsens"}, "cluster_id": "CHS160", "cluster_text": "Robert Haddad, 62, a retired plumber, is on day 1 after arthroscopic repair of a full thickness supraspinatus tear in his dominant right shoulder, done under general anaesthetic with an interscalene nerve block that is now wearing off. History: hypertension and type 2 diabetes, both medicated. He wears an abduction sling. The surgeon's protocol: sling on except for hygiene and exercises, passive shoulder movement only for six weeks, no active shoulder movement. Observations: BP 138/84 mmHg, HR 78, SpO2 96 percent on room air, afebrile. Pain 6/10. The dressing is dry. He can move his fingers, with some reduced sensation over the lateral forearm. The nurse reports he pushed through his right arm during a bed to chair transfer this morning. Later, the ward allied health assistant mentions the sling was off during a meal.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Notify the surgical team, lodge an incident report, and revisit sling education with Robert): A deviation from a surgical protocol is reportable even when no harm is apparent, because reporting allows the surgeon to assess the repair and the service to learn from the event. Private counselling or waiting for harm leaves the surgeon uninformed and the risk unmanaged. Education for Robert is useful but is part of the response, not the whole of it."}, {"id": 1601, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Ana has bilateral buttock pain and a tender symphysis pubis, and she wonders whether this is her back. Which examination finding most strongly supports pelvic girdle pain as the source of her symptoms rather than a lumbar or vascular cause?", "options": {"A": "Pain below the knee on repeated lumbar flexion with a reduced ankle reflex on that side", "B": "Provocation testing reproducing her pain, with the active straight leg raise easing under compression", "C": "Groin and inner thigh pain on resisted hip adduction, with negative pelvic provocation tests", "D": "Deep groin pain with restricted hip internal rotation and flexion, worse on single leg stance"}, "cluster_id": "CHS161", "cluster_text": "Ana Tuilagi, 16, is a school student, 28 weeks pregnant with her first baby, attending the hospital antenatal physiotherapy outpatient clinic with her mother. She reports three weeks of bilateral buttock and groin pain, worse climbing the school stairs, rolling over in bed and standing on one leg. Pain is 7 out of 10 on stairs. Her Patient-Specific Functional Scale activities are stairs, walking to school and turning in bed. Antenatal care is uneventful: normal fetal movements, normal blood pressure, clear urinalysis, no bleeding and no leg swelling. On examination, the posterior pelvic pain provocation test reproduces buttock pain on both sides, active straight leg raise is painful bilaterally and eases with manual pelvic compression, and the lumbar spine is clear with normal neurology. The symphysis pubis is tender to palpation. The clinic manager wants to review how well the pelvic girdle pain programme is working.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Provocation testing reproducing her pain, with the active straight leg raise easing under compression): Reproduction of her pain by posterior pelvic provocation and easing of the active straight leg raise under pelvic compression point to the pelvic joints and their load transfer. Leg pain with an altered reflex describes a radicular lumbar picture, which her normal neurology does not support. Adductor pain on resisted testing and a restricted, painful hip describe groin and hip sources that do not explain the provocation findings and compression relief."}, {"id": 1602, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Ana's pain limits stairs and walking to school, and compression of her pelvis eased the active straight leg raise. She has no neurological signs and attends school five days a week. Which management plan is most appropriate at this first visit?", "options": {"A": "Progressive hip abduction strengthening with resistance bands, plus a graded daily walking program for tolerance", "B": "A support belt worn high over the waist, with bed rest for most of the day", "C": "A pelvic belt worn low over the hips, activity modification and individualised muscle control exercises", "D": "Crutches for all walking, stair avoidance until after delivery, and gentle sacroiliac mobilisation by the therapist"}, "cluster_id": "CHS161", "cluster_text": "Ana Tuilagi, 16, is a school student, 28 weeks pregnant with her first baby, attending the hospital antenatal physiotherapy outpatient clinic with her mother. She reports three weeks of bilateral buttock and groin pain, worse climbing the school stairs, rolling over in bed and standing on one leg. Pain is 7 out of 10 on stairs. Her Patient-Specific Functional Scale activities are stairs, walking to school and turning in bed. Antenatal care is uneventful: normal fetal movements, normal blood pressure, clear urinalysis, no bleeding and no leg swelling. On examination, the posterior pelvic pain provocation test reproduces buttock pain on both sides, active straight leg raise is painful bilaterally and eases with manual pelvic compression, and the lumbar spine is clear with normal neurology. The symphysis pubis is tender to palpation. The clinic manager wants to review how well the pelvic girdle pain programme is working.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A pelvic belt worn low over the hips, activity modification and individualised muscle control exercises): A pelvic belt worn low over the hips, pacing of aggravating activities and individualised muscle control exercises reduce pain and improve function in pregnancy related pelvic girdle pain. A belt worn high does not compress the pelvic joints and bed rest deconditions her. Strengthening with resistance bands loads a painful pelvis on one legged tasks too early. Blanket crutch use and stair avoidance until delivery restricts school attendance more than needed."}, {"id": 1603, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "At review a week later, Ana reports sudden pain and swelling in her left calf since yesterday, with warmth over the area. Her pelvic pain is unchanged and her vital signs are stable. What should the physiotherapist do first about her calf symptoms?", "options": {"A": "Advise rest with a warm pack for the calf and book a review within two days", "B": "Perform a calf squeeze test and, if negative, continue the exercise programme as planned", "C": "Apply a graduated compression stocking and elevate the leg, then reassess swelling at next week's visit", "D": "Stop treatment, do not massage the calf, and arrange same-day assessment by a doctor"}, "cluster_id": "CHS161", "cluster_text": "Ana Tuilagi, 16, is a school student, 28 weeks pregnant with her first baby, attending the hospital antenatal physiotherapy outpatient clinic with her mother. She reports three weeks of bilateral buttock and groin pain, worse climbing the school stairs, rolling over in bed and standing on one leg. Pain is 7 out of 10 on stairs. Her Patient-Specific Functional Scale activities are stairs, walking to school and turning in bed. Antenatal care is uneventful: normal fetal movements, normal blood pressure, clear urinalysis, no bleeding and no leg swelling. On examination, the posterior pelvic pain provocation test reproduces buttock pain on both sides, active straight leg raise is painful bilaterally and eases with manual pelvic compression, and the lumbar spine is clear with normal neurology. The symphysis pubis is tender to palpation. The clinic manager wants to review how well the pelvic girdle pain programme is working.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stop treatment, do not massage the calf, and arrange same-day assessment by a doctor): Pregnancy raises the risk of venous thromboembolism, and unilateral calf pain, swelling and warmth need same-day medical assessment, with no massage or vigorous exercise that could dislodge a clot. A negative bedside test cannot exclude a clot, and compression, rest or waiting two days delays diagnosis."}, {"id": 1604, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "The clinic manager asks how the service can show whether its pelvic girdle pain programme improves function for pregnant clients. Ana's baseline pain and function scores were recorded today. Which approach to measuring outcomes is most appropriate for this quality improvement project?", "options": {"A": "Ask clients to rate satisfaction at discharge, because satisfaction shows whether function improved with the programme", "B": "Record a validated function score at discharge and compare it with norms for pregnant women", "C": "Review the clinical records of every client for notes saying improved, and calculate the proportion improved", "D": "Record pain and a functional measure at first and last visit, then audit the combined change"}, "cluster_id": "CHS161", "cluster_text": "Ana Tuilagi, 16, is a school student, 28 weeks pregnant with her first baby, attending the hospital antenatal physiotherapy outpatient clinic with her mother. She reports three weeks of bilateral buttock and groin pain, worse climbing the school stairs, rolling over in bed and standing on one leg. Pain is 7 out of 10 on stairs. Her Patient-Specific Functional Scale activities are stairs, walking to school and turning in bed. Antenatal care is uneventful: normal fetal movements, normal blood pressure, clear urinalysis, no bleeding and no leg swelling. On examination, the posterior pelvic pain provocation test reproduces buttock pain on both sides, active straight leg raise is painful bilaterally and eases with manual pelvic compression, and the lumbar spine is clear with normal neurology. The symphysis pubis is tender to palpation. The clinic manager wants to review how well the pelvic girdle pain programme is working.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Record pain and a functional measure at first and last visit, then audit the combined change): Recording pain and a validated functional measure at the first and last visit lets the service quantify change for each client and audit the combined result. Satisfaction describes the experience of care rather than function. A discharge score without a baseline cannot show change for individual clients. Counting records that say improved relies on subjective wording that is not consistently recorded."}, {"id": 1605, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Cannavaro, an older woman with diabetes, has a plantar ulcer and several foot findings recorded at this visit. Which single finding most strongly points to impaired arterial supply as a barrier to healing, rather than pressure or neuropathy as the cause?", "options": {"A": "Thickened callus ringing the ulcer margin over the first metatarsal head from repeated pressure while walking", "B": "Toe clawing with prominent metatarsal heads, which increase pressure on the forefoot while walking", "C": "Cool pale foot with absent dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses and slow capillary refill", "D": "Absent protective sensation to monofilament testing across both forefeet and the ulcer site"}, "cluster_id": "CHS162", "cluster_text": "Mrs Rosa Cannavaro, 72, widowed, lives alone in a unit and is seen at home by the community physiotherapist. History: type 2 diabetes for 20 years, peripheral arterial disease, hypertension, ex-smoker. She has a 1.5 cm plantar ulcer under the right first metatarsal head, with callus around the margin and a thin layer of slough, and no odour or surrounding redness today. Podiatry manages the wound and has fitted an offloading boot. The right foot is cool and pale with absent dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses and slow capillary refill; she has claw toes with prominent metatarsal heads, and protective sensation to monofilament is absent across both forefeet. She reports calf pain after walking about 100 metres that eases within minutes of standing still. HR 78, BP 144/80. Her daughter Elena helps with shopping and often phones the service for updates. Mrs Cannavaro has not named anyone to receive her health information.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Cool pale foot with absent dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses and slow capillary refill): A cool pale foot with absent foot pulses and slow capillary refill indicates reduced blood supply, which slows wound healing. Loss of protective sensation, callus and toe deformity all indicate neuropathic loading of the forefoot and explain why the ulcer formed, but not why it may fail to heal."}, {"id": 1606, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Cannavaro reports calf pain after walking about 100 metres that eases within minutes of standing still. Podiatry has fitted an offloading boot for her ulcer. She asks whether she should walk at all. What is the most appropriate exercise advice?", "options": {"A": "Rest from walking until the ulcer heals, and use seated calf exercises in the meantime", "B": "Walk indoors in socks on carpet until pain stops her, then rest, to avoid footwear pressure", "C": "Walk briskly through any calf pain to build tolerance, wearing the boot only when sore", "D": "Walk in the offloading boot to moderate calf pain, rest until it eases, then repeat"}, "cluster_id": "CHS162", "cluster_text": "Mrs Rosa Cannavaro, 72, widowed, lives alone in a unit and is seen at home by the community physiotherapist. History: type 2 diabetes for 20 years, peripheral arterial disease, hypertension, ex-smoker. She has a 1.5 cm plantar ulcer under the right first metatarsal head, with callus around the margin and a thin layer of slough, and no odour or surrounding redness today. Podiatry manages the wound and has fitted an offloading boot. The right foot is cool and pale with absent dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses and slow capillary refill; she has claw toes with prominent metatarsal heads, and protective sensation to monofilament is absent across both forefeet. She reports calf pain after walking about 100 metres that eases within minutes of standing still. HR 78, BP 144/80. Her daughter Elena helps with shopping and often phones the service for updates. Mrs Cannavaro has not named anyone to receive her health information.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Walk in the offloading boot to moderate calf pain, rest until it eases, then repeat): Supervised or home based interval walking to moderate claudication pain is a core treatment for peripheral arterial disease, and wearing the offloading boot protects the ulcer. Complete rest loses the benefit of walking, and walking in socks exposes the ulcer to pressure. Pushing through pain without protection harms the wound and does not improve outcomes."}, {"id": 1607, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "At the next visit the right foot is warm, swollen and red with spreading redness above the ulcer and a new offensive odour. Her temperature is 38.4 degrees, heart rate 104, and she feels unwell. What should the physiotherapist do now?", "options": {"A": "Clean and redress the wound with her usual dressing and book a review for next week", "B": "Send a note to her GP and book a review for later this week", "C": "Stop exercise, arrange same day medical review for possible infection, and document the findings", "D": "Apply compression to control the swelling and ask podiatry to review at her next appointment"}, "cluster_id": "CHS162", "cluster_text": "Mrs Rosa Cannavaro, 72, widowed, lives alone in a unit and is seen at home by the community physiotherapist. History: type 2 diabetes for 20 years, peripheral arterial disease, hypertension, ex-smoker. She has a 1.5 cm plantar ulcer under the right first metatarsal head, with callus around the margin and a thin layer of slough, and no odour or surrounding redness today. Podiatry manages the wound and has fitted an offloading boot. The right foot is cool and pale with absent dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses and slow capillary refill; she has claw toes with prominent metatarsal heads, and protective sensation to monofilament is absent across both forefeet. She reports calf pain after walking about 100 metres that eases within minutes of standing still. HR 78, BP 144/80. Her daughter Elena helps with shopping and often phones the service for updates. Mrs Cannavaro has not named anyone to receive her health information.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop exercise, arrange same day medical review for possible infection, and document the findings): Spreading redness, odour, fever and a raised heart rate suggest a spreading diabetic foot infection in an ischaemic limb, which can threaten the limb and her life and needs same day medical assessment. Redressing and waiting a week is too slow. A note to the GP with a review later in the week delays treatment of a possible systemic infection. Compression is unsafe on a limb with absent pulses, and leaving the problem for a later podiatry visit delays care."}, {"id": 1608, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Cannavaro's daughter phones the community service and asks for the ulcer findings and the walking plan. Mrs Cannavaro has not nominated anyone to receive her information. The physiotherapist knows the daughter helps with shopping. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Refer her to the medical records department to request the file under privacy legislation", "B": "Share the walking plan only, since she helps with shopping, and withhold the ulcer findings", "C": "Decline to share details until Mrs Cannavaro consents, and offer to ask her at the visit", "D": "Confirm the daughter's identity by asking for her mother's date of birth, then share the findings"}, "cluster_id": "CHS162", "cluster_text": "Mrs Rosa Cannavaro, 72, widowed, lives alone in a unit and is seen at home by the community physiotherapist. History: type 2 diabetes for 20 years, peripheral arterial disease, hypertension, ex-smoker. She has a 1.5 cm plantar ulcer under the right first metatarsal head, with callus around the margin and a thin layer of slough, and no odour or surrounding redness today. Podiatry manages the wound and has fitted an offloading boot. The right foot is cool and pale with absent dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses and slow capillary refill; she has claw toes with prominent metatarsal heads, and protective sensation to monofilament is absent across both forefeet. She reports calf pain after walking about 100 metres that eases within minutes of standing still. HR 78, BP 144/80. Her daughter Elena helps with shopping and often phones the service for updates. Mrs Cannavaro has not named anyone to receive her health information.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Decline to share details until Mrs Cannavaro consents, and offer to ask her at the visit): Health information belongs to the patient, and a family member's involvement does not give the right to it without the patient's consent. Offering to ask Mrs Cannavaro respects her choice and keeps the daughter included if she agrees. Sharing part of the plan or checking identity does not replace consent, and sending the daughter to request the file does not address that either."}, {"id": 1609, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Thornton's knee is hot and effused, his temperature is 38.1 degrees, and aspiration has not yet been done. He asks to begin knee bending exercises today because his toe gout always settled with movement. What is the most appropriate physiotherapy action at this stage?", "options": {"A": "Begin weight bearing with a frame to maintain mobility while the aspiration result is awaited", "B": "Start gentle active knee flexion within pain limits, as his gout history explains this flare", "C": "Apply a hot pack to the knee, then assisted range of movement to ease stiffness", "D": "Defer knee exercise, rest the joint, and tell the medical team about the fever"}, "cluster_id": "CHS163", "cluster_text": "Mr Peter Thornton, 78, retired bus driver, was admitted to the orthopaedic ward from the emergency department with a hot, swollen, painful right knee for 36 hours. He has a history of gout in his big toe, hypertension and chronic kidney disease, and takes a diuretic daily. Observations: temperature 38.1 degrees Celsius, HR 98, BP 132/80, RR 18, SpO2 96 percent on room air. The knee is red and warm with a large effusion. Passive flexion is limited to 40 degrees by pain, resting pain is 8/10 and he cannot weight bear. Blood tests are pending and the medical team plans joint aspiration. He lives alone and was independent with a walking stick. The ward is running a quality improvement audit of mobility outcomes, and the physiotherapy team records the de Morton Mobility Index at admission and discharge.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Defer knee exercise, rest the joint, and tell the medical team about the fever): A hot, febrile, effused joint is a possible septic arthritis until aspiration proves otherwise, and heat or movement can worsen infection and damage cartilage. His gout history makes crystals likely but does not exclude infection. Holding exercise and passing the findings to the doctors is the safe step, whereas starting movement on the assumption of gout skips that check."}, {"id": 1610, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "During handover the registrar asks you, as the physiotherapist, to explain why Mr Thornton may be prone to gout flares despite no recent toe symptoms. Considering his medical history and medications, which factor most plausibly raises his serum urate and flare risk?", "options": {"A": "His daily diuretic and reduced kidney function both lower urate excretion, favouring crystal formation", "B": "His hypertension and its treatment raise urate production in the liver, favouring crystal formation", "C": "His fever and acute inflammation raise urate by increasing cell turnover, which then triggers crystal deposition", "D": "His chronic kidney disease alone explains it, since diuretics have little effect on urate handling"}, "cluster_id": "CHS163", "cluster_text": "Mr Peter Thornton, 78, retired bus driver, was admitted to the orthopaedic ward from the emergency department with a hot, swollen, painful right knee for 36 hours. He has a history of gout in his big toe, hypertension and chronic kidney disease, and takes a diuretic daily. Observations: temperature 38.1 degrees Celsius, HR 98, BP 132/80, RR 18, SpO2 96 percent on room air. The knee is red and warm with a large effusion. Passive flexion is limited to 40 degrees by pain, resting pain is 8/10 and he cannot weight bear. Blood tests are pending and the medical team plans joint aspiration. He lives alone and was independent with a walking stick. The ward is running a quality improvement audit of mobility outcomes, and the physiotherapy team records the de Morton Mobility Index at admission and discharge.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (His daily diuretic and reduced kidney function both lower urate excretion, favouring crystal formation): Diuretics and impaired renal function both reduce urate excretion, which raises serum urate and the chance of crystal formation. Hypertension is associated with gout through reduced renal clearance rather than increased hepatic production. Fever and inflammation follow a flare rather than cause the raised urate. His kidney disease contributes, but diuretics also reduce urate excretion, so kidney disease alone is an incomplete explanation."}, {"id": 1611, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Aspiration showed urate crystals with a negative culture, and medical treatment began. Two days later his temperature is normal, resting pain is 3/10 and the effusion is smaller. He wants to walk to the bathroom independently. What is the most appropriate mobility progression?", "options": {"A": "Apply firm end range overpressure to regain flexion so he is ready for stairs at home", "B": "Start heavy resisted quadriceps training to restore strength quickly while the inflammation is still settling", "C": "Progress to supervised walking with his stick and active knee movement within comfortable range", "D": "Keep him on bed rest until the knee is pain free and the swelling has gone"}, "cluster_id": "CHS163", "cluster_text": "Mr Peter Thornton, 78, retired bus driver, was admitted to the orthopaedic ward from the emergency department with a hot, swollen, painful right knee for 36 hours. He has a history of gout in his big toe, hypertension and chronic kidney disease, and takes a diuretic daily. Observations: temperature 38.1 degrees Celsius, HR 98, BP 132/80, RR 18, SpO2 96 percent on room air. The knee is red and warm with a large effusion. Passive flexion is limited to 40 degrees by pain, resting pain is 8/10 and he cannot weight bear. Blood tests are pending and the medical team plans joint aspiration. He lives alone and was independent with a walking stick. The ward is running a quality improvement audit of mobility outcomes, and the physiotherapy team records the de Morton Mobility Index at admission and discharge.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Progress to supervised walking with his stick and active knee movement within comfortable range): Once infection is excluded and inflammation is settling, early supervised weight bearing and active movement within comfort protect strength and independence. Prolonged bed rest causes deconditioning, while heavy loading or forced end range work irritates a recently inflamed joint and delays recovery."}, {"id": 1612, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "The ward wants to know whether physiotherapy is improving mobility for medical inpatients like Mr Thornton. A therapist suggests several audit methods to compare. Which approach would give the most meaningful information about change in mobility across the ward over three months?", "options": {"A": "Compare length of stay before and after physiotherapy started, treating shorter stays as better mobility", "B": "Measure mobility with a validated tool at discharge, then compare scores between wards with similar patients", "C": "Ask treating therapists to rate each patient's mobility change at discharge as better, same or worse", "D": "Record one validated mobility measure at admission and discharge for consecutive patients and review change"}, "cluster_id": "CHS163", "cluster_text": "Mr Peter Thornton, 78, retired bus driver, was admitted to the orthopaedic ward from the emergency department with a hot, swollen, painful right knee for 36 hours. He has a history of gout in his big toe, hypertension and chronic kidney disease, and takes a diuretic daily. Observations: temperature 38.1 degrees Celsius, HR 98, BP 132/80, RR 18, SpO2 96 percent on room air. The knee is red and warm with a large effusion. Passive flexion is limited to 40 degrees by pain, resting pain is 8/10 and he cannot weight bear. Blood tests are pending and the medical team plans joint aspiration. He lives alone and was independent with a walking stick. The ward is running a quality improvement audit of mobility outcomes, and the physiotherapy team records the de Morton Mobility Index at admission and discharge.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Record one validated mobility measure at admission and discharge for consecutive patients and review change): A validated mobility measure recorded at admission and discharge for consecutive patients shows real change in the outcome of interest and can be judged against the measure's known error. Length of stay is influenced by many factors besides mobility. A discharge score alone with no baseline cannot show change. Therapist ratings are subjective and prone to bias in favour of the service."}, {"id": 1613, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mia is on day one after reconstruction with an extension deficit of 8 degrees, a moderate effusion and a barely palpable quadriceps contraction, and she can tolerate gentle exercise. Her mother asks what the physiotherapist will focus on. Which priority is most appropriate for today?", "options": {"A": "Build quadriceps strength with open chain resisted knee extension from full flexion using progressive ankle weights", "B": "Maximise knee flexion beyond 90 degrees using overpressure so early scar tissue does not restrict range", "C": "Regain full passive extension and activate the quadriceps, using elevation and active exercise within pain limits", "D": "Delay quadriceps activation until the effusion has resolved, to avoid reflex inhibition and graft strain"}, "cluster_id": "CHS164", "cluster_text": "Mia Tanaka, 16, netball player, is on day one after left ACL reconstruction with a hamstring autograft, following a landing injury four months ago. Asthma, well controlled. Her mother is at the bedside. Orders: weight bearing as tolerated, hinged brace unlocked, ice and elevation. Findings: pain 5/10 at rest and 7/10 with movement, moderate effusion, active knee flexion 40 degrees, passive extension lacking 8 degrees, unable to straight leg raise without a lag, and a barely palpable quadriceps contraction. Calf soft and non-tender, pulses present, dressing dry. The nerve block has worn off. HR 82, BP 110/68, SpO2 98%. Mia is mature, understands her care and has said she wants to control who is told about it. She is anxious about walking and worried she will never play again. Her netball coach has phoned the ward asking for an update.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Regain full passive extension and activate the quadriceps, using elevation and active exercise within pain limits): Early priorities are full extension, effusion control and quadriceps activation, since a persistent extension deficit and inhibition cause long term problems. Early resisted open chain extension loads the graft, forcing flexion is unnecessary, and delaying activation prolongs inhibition."}, {"id": 1614, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mia's regional block has worn off and she is anxious about getting out of bed. Her observations are stable and the surgeon's orders allow weight bearing as tolerated in the unlocked brace. What is the most appropriate mobility plan for her first walk?", "options": {"A": "Walk with crutches, bearing weight as tolerated in the brace, with staff close by", "B": "Walk non weight bearing on the left with crutches until the surgeon's review at week two", "C": "Walk without aids to encourage a normal gait pattern, keeping the brace locked straight for stability", "D": "Use a wheelchair for all transfers for two days to protect the graft from loading"}, "cluster_id": "CHS164", "cluster_text": "Mia Tanaka, 16, netball player, is on day one after left ACL reconstruction with a hamstring autograft, following a landing injury four months ago. Asthma, well controlled. Her mother is at the bedside. Orders: weight bearing as tolerated, hinged brace unlocked, ice and elevation. Findings: pain 5/10 at rest and 7/10 with movement, moderate effusion, active knee flexion 40 degrees, passive extension lacking 8 degrees, unable to straight leg raise without a lag, and a barely palpable quadriceps contraction. Calf soft and non-tender, pulses present, dressing dry. The nerve block has worn off. HR 82, BP 110/68, SpO2 98%. Mia is mature, understands her care and has said she wants to control who is told about it. She is anxious about walking and worried she will never play again. Her netball coach has phoned the ward asking for an update.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Walk with crutches, bearing weight as tolerated in the brace, with staff close by): The orders permit weight bearing as tolerated, and early supervised walking with crutches supports recovery and reduces her anxiety. Withholding weight bearing contradicts the orders, and walking without aids with a locked brace gives a poor gait and a falling risk."}, {"id": 1615, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "At the next session Mia has a swollen, warm, tender left calf, mild breathlessness walking to the door, SpO2 94% and HR 102. She describes a deep ache in the calf that began overnight, different from her knee pain. What should the physiotherapist do?", "options": {"A": "Stop mobilising, keep her in bed, and urgently notify the medical team of a possible clot", "B": "Continue gentle ankle pumps and walking to promote venous return, then report after the session", "C": "Stop mobilising, apply a firm compression bandage and elevate the leg, then report if swelling persists", "D": "Return her to bed with the leg elevated, and ask the nurse to recheck observations later"}, "cluster_id": "CHS164", "cluster_text": "Mia Tanaka, 16, netball player, is on day one after left ACL reconstruction with a hamstring autograft, following a landing injury four months ago. Asthma, well controlled. Her mother is at the bedside. Orders: weight bearing as tolerated, hinged brace unlocked, ice and elevation. Findings: pain 5/10 at rest and 7/10 with movement, moderate effusion, active knee flexion 40 degrees, passive extension lacking 8 degrees, unable to straight leg raise without a lag, and a barely palpable quadriceps contraction. Calf soft and non-tender, pulses present, dressing dry. The nerve block has worn off. HR 82, BP 110/68, SpO2 98%. Mia is mature, understands her care and has said she wants to control who is told about it. She is anxious about walking and worried she will never play again. Her netball coach has phoned the ward asking for an update.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stop mobilising, keep her in bed, and urgently notify the medical team of a possible clot): Calf swelling, warmth and tenderness with breathlessness and a falling SpO2 raise concern for deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism, which need urgent medical assessment, so she stops mobilising and the team is told at once. Continuing to walk could dislodge a clot. Compression and waiting to see if swelling persists delays diagnosis. Elevating the leg and asking for a later recheck treats a possible pulmonary embolism as routine."}, {"id": 1616, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mia's netball coach phones the ward, asks how the surgery went and when she can return to play, and Mia's mother is not in the room. Mia has said she wants to control who is told. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Share general progress and the likely return timeline, since the coach must plan team selection", "B": "Decline without Mia's permission, offer to ask her about an update, and document the call", "C": "Confirm the coach's identity and role first, then give a brief summary of the surgery", "D": "Take the coach's number and ask Mia's mother to decide later what information is passed on"}, "cluster_id": "CHS164", "cluster_text": "Mia Tanaka, 16, netball player, is on day one after left ACL reconstruction with a hamstring autograft, following a landing injury four months ago. Asthma, well controlled. Her mother is at the bedside. Orders: weight bearing as tolerated, hinged brace unlocked, ice and elevation. Findings: pain 5/10 at rest and 7/10 with movement, moderate effusion, active knee flexion 40 degrees, passive extension lacking 8 degrees, unable to straight leg raise without a lag, and a barely palpable quadriceps contraction. Calf soft and non-tender, pulses present, dressing dry. The nerve block has worn off. HR 82, BP 110/68, SpO2 98%. Mia is mature, understands her care and has said she wants to control who is told about it. She is anxious about walking and worried she will never play again. Her netball coach has phoned the ward asking for an update.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Decline without Mia's permission, offer to ask her about an update, and document the call): Health information belongs to the patient, and a mature adolescent who understands her care can decide who is told. Identity checks or general summaries still disclose without her agreement. Asking Mia, and recording the call, respects privacy and keeps the notes complete."}, {"id": 1617, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Singh has smear positive pulmonary tuberculosis, has been on treatment for three days and is in a negative pressure room. The physiotherapist will enter to assist his huffing and coughing. Which personal protective equipment is most appropriate for this airborne risk?", "options": {"A": "A fit-checked P2 respirator, with eye protection because coughing generates aerosols", "B": "Gloves and a gown, since the negative pressure room already removes airborne particles", "C": "A surgical mask with gloves and gown, as for droplet precautions", "D": "A surgical mask on the physiotherapist and a P2 respirator on the patient"}, "cluster_id": "CHS165", "cluster_text": "Gurpreet Singh, 46, a taxi driver who arrived from Punjab three months ago, speaks Punjabi and little English. He is in a negative pressure single room with smear positive pulmonary tuberculosis: six weeks of cough, night sweats and weight loss, with right upper lobe cavitation on chest X-ray. Anti-tuberculous treatment started three days ago. Observations: SpO2 95 percent on room air, RR 20, HR 92, temperature 37.8. He has a weak, ineffective cough with small sputum volumes, has lost muscle bulk and tires walking to the bathroom. Physiotherapy is requested for secretion clearance and rehabilitation. The ward has a telephone interpreter service. His cousin visits daily and offers to interpret, saying Mr Singh does not want others in the community to know his diagnosis.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A fit-checked P2 respirator, with eye protection because coughing generates aerosols): Tuberculosis spreads by small airborne particles, so staff need a fit checked P2 or N95 respirator, with eye protection when coughing is being assisted. Surgical masks do not filter these particles, and droplet precautions are the wrong category. The negative pressure room reduces spread but does not protect someone standing near the patient. Roles are reversed in the last statement, because the patient wears a surgical mask to contain droplets and the staff member needs the respirator."}, {"id": 1618, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Singh is medically stable on treatment but deconditioned after six weeks of illness and asks to walk in the corridor. He is still smear positive and infectious. A ward cycle ergometer could be brought to his room. What is the most appropriate mobilisation plan?", "options": {"A": "Progress walking and cycling inside the negative pressure room until he is deemed non-infectious", "B": "Keep him at rest until sputum smears are negative, because exercise raises infectious aerosol output", "C": "Use the shared rehabilitation gym during quiet times, wiping the equipment afterwards, with mask on", "D": "Walk him in the corridor wearing a surgical mask, since the physiotherapist wears a P2 respirator"}, "cluster_id": "CHS165", "cluster_text": "Gurpreet Singh, 46, a taxi driver who arrived from Punjab three months ago, speaks Punjabi and little English. He is in a negative pressure single room with smear positive pulmonary tuberculosis: six weeks of cough, night sweats and weight loss, with right upper lobe cavitation on chest X-ray. Anti-tuberculous treatment started three days ago. Observations: SpO2 95 percent on room air, RR 20, HR 92, temperature 37.8. He has a weak, ineffective cough with small sputum volumes, has lost muscle bulk and tires walking to the bathroom. Physiotherapy is requested for secretion clearance and rehabilitation. The ward has a telephone interpreter service. His cousin visits daily and offers to interpret, saying Mr Singh does not want others in the community to know his diagnosis.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Progress walking and cycling inside the negative pressure room until he is deemed non-infectious): Rehabilitation can start now and does not need to wait, so exercise is progressed inside the room where aerosols are controlled. Leaving the room for non-essential activity exposes others while he is infectious, and wiping equipment does not remove airborne risk. Bed rest would only worsen his deconditioning."}, {"id": 1619, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Singh's cousin offers to interpret during the session, but Mr Singh has asked through the nurse that his diagnosis stay private. The physiotherapist needs to explain the exercise plan in Punjabi and obtain his consent. What is the most appropriate approach?", "options": {"A": "Use simple English, gestures and demonstration, to avoid involving others in the discussion", "B": "Ask a bilingual ward nurse to translate informally, keeping the information inside the care team", "C": "Accept the cousin as interpreter because family know his preferences, and explain the plan to both", "D": "Book a professional interpreter and check with Mr Singh who he wants present"}, "cluster_id": "CHS165", "cluster_text": "Gurpreet Singh, 46, a taxi driver who arrived from Punjab three months ago, speaks Punjabi and little English. He is in a negative pressure single room with smear positive pulmonary tuberculosis: six weeks of cough, night sweats and weight loss, with right upper lobe cavitation on chest X-ray. Anti-tuberculous treatment started three days ago. Observations: SpO2 95 percent on room air, RR 20, HR 92, temperature 37.8. He has a weak, ineffective cough with small sputum volumes, has lost muscle bulk and tires walking to the bathroom. Physiotherapy is requested for secretion clearance and rehabilitation. The ward has a telephone interpreter service. His cousin visits daily and offers to interpret, saying Mr Singh does not want others in the community to know his diagnosis.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Book a professional interpreter and check with Mr Singh who he wants present): A professional interpreter, by telephone if needed, is bound by confidentiality and gives accurate information for valid consent. Mr Singh should decide who else is present, which respects his stated wish for privacy. A family member or an untrained staff member may distort the message or breach his confidence, and gestures cannot support informed consent."}, {"id": 1620, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "During a later session Mr Singh coughs up about a cupful of bright red blood and becomes anxious and breathless, with SpO2 falling to 91 percent. He has a known right upper lobe cavity. The interpreter is on the phone. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Use the interpreter to explain the episode and reassure him, and assess breath sounds before calling", "B": "Stay with him, call urgently for medical help, and support his airway and breathing", "C": "Stay with him, sit him upright, and coach controlled breathing while the interpreter reassures him", "D": "Lay him on his left side and ask a colleague to call the doctor urgently"}, "cluster_id": "CHS165", "cluster_text": "Gurpreet Singh, 46, a taxi driver who arrived from Punjab three months ago, speaks Punjabi and little English. He is in a negative pressure single room with smear positive pulmonary tuberculosis: six weeks of cough, night sweats and weight loss, with right upper lobe cavitation on chest X-ray. Anti-tuberculous treatment started three days ago. Observations: SpO2 95 percent on room air, RR 20, HR 92, temperature 37.8. He has a weak, ineffective cough with small sputum volumes, has lost muscle bulk and tires walking to the bathroom. Physiotherapy is requested for secretion clearance and rehabilitation. The ward has a telephone interpreter service. His cousin visits daily and offers to interpret, saying Mr Singh does not want others in the community to know his diagnosis.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stay with him, call urgently for medical help, and support his airway and breathing): A cupful of fresh blood from a cavity is a significant haemoptysis that can threaten the airway, so the physiotherapist stays with him, calls urgently for medical help and supports his airway and breathing. Explaining and assessing first delays escalation. Sitting him up and coaching breathing without calling for help leaves a life-threatening bleed untreated. Lying him on the side opposite the bleeding allows blood to flow into the healthy lung, since the bleeding lung should be kept lowermost."}, {"id": 1621, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Kavita describes doing a long walk on good days, then resting for two days afterwards, with fatigue peaking by early afternoon. She wants to stay active without triggering this cycle. Which change to her routine is most appropriate to recommend in this telehealth session?", "options": {"A": "Spread shorter moderate bouts across the week with planned rests before fatigue builds", "B": "Keep activity to good days and rest fully on the others so that symptoms stay settled", "C": "Progress the length of each good day walk steadily to build tolerance and reduce fatigue", "D": "Move activity into one longer late afternoon session once her teaching day has ended"}, "cluster_id": "CHS166", "cluster_text": "Kavita Patel, 38, a primary school teacher, has relapsing remitting multiple sclerosis diagnosed six years ago and has had no relapse for two years. She attends her third physiotherapy session by telehealth from home. She reports fatigue that builds from early afternoon, legs that feel heavy after a school day, and worse symptoms in hot weather. Her Fatigue Severity Scale score is 5.8 out of 7. She walks without an aid and drives. She plans to return to full time work next term. Her activity is uneven: long walks on good days followed by two days of rest. Today she says the sessions are the most helpful part of her week. She asks to message the physiotherapist on a personal mobile at night about symptoms and has sent a follow request to the physiotherapist's private social media account.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Spread shorter moderate bouts across the week with planned rests before fatigue builds): Pacing with regular short bouts and planned rests conserves energy and avoids the boom and bust cycle that deepens fatigue in multiple sclerosis. Saving activity for good days or lengthening good day walks keeps the cycle going, and a late session still lands on a body already fatigued."}, {"id": 1622, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Kavita notices her legs feel heavier and her vision blurs slightly when walking outdoors in warm weather, settling within an hour of resting in a cool room. She asks whether this means she is having a relapse. What is the best explanation and advice?", "options": {"A": "Temporary heat related worsening; exercise in cooler conditions and report symptoms lasting over a day", "B": "A likely relapse, so stop exercising and arrange same day review by her treating neurologist", "C": "Early disease progression, so refer for assessment of a walking aid and reduce activity", "D": "Normal deconditioning, so lengthen outdoor walks gradually to build her tolerance to heat over weeks"}, "cluster_id": "CHS166", "cluster_text": "Kavita Patel, 38, a primary school teacher, has relapsing remitting multiple sclerosis diagnosed six years ago and has had no relapse for two years. She attends her third physiotherapy session by telehealth from home. She reports fatigue that builds from early afternoon, legs that feel heavy after a school day, and worse symptoms in hot weather. Her Fatigue Severity Scale score is 5.8 out of 7. She walks without an aid and drives. She plans to return to full time work next term. Her activity is uneven: long walks on good days followed by two days of rest. Today she says the sessions are the most helpful part of her week. She asks to message the physiotherapist on a personal mobile at night about symptoms and has sent a follow request to the physiotherapist's private social media account.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Temporary heat related worsening; exercise in cooler conditions and report symptoms lasting over a day): Symptoms that appear with raised body temperature and resolve with cooling are a recognised heat effect in multiple sclerosis and do not signal new damage. Symptoms persisting beyond about a day would raise the possibility of a relapse and need medical review, which is why a clear report threshold is given."}, {"id": 1623, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "At the end of the session Kavita asks to message the physiotherapist on a personal mobile at night and sends a follow request to the physiotherapist's private social media account. She is friendly and means well. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Decline both requests by email without giving a reason, to avoid making the session conversation uncomfortable", "B": "Accept the request using a restricted profile and keep clinical discussion strictly off that platform", "C": "Accept personal mobile messaging for exercise advice only, and record the arrangement in her notes", "D": "Thank her, decline personal contact, and explain official contact channels and urgent after hours options"}, "cluster_id": "CHS166", "cluster_text": "Kavita Patel, 38, a primary school teacher, has relapsing remitting multiple sclerosis diagnosed six years ago and has had no relapse for two years. She attends her third physiotherapy session by telehealth from home. She reports fatigue that builds from early afternoon, legs that feel heavy after a school day, and worse symptoms in hot weather. Her Fatigue Severity Scale score is 5.8 out of 7. She walks without an aid and drives. She plans to return to full time work next term. Her activity is uneven: long walks on good days followed by two days of rest. Today she says the sessions are the most helpful part of her week. She asks to message the physiotherapist on a personal mobile at night about symptoms and has sent a follow request to the physiotherapist's private social media account.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Thank her, decline personal contact, and explain official contact channels and urgent after hours options): Maintaining a professional boundary protects the therapeutic relationship and the duty of care, and a clear explanation of official channels gives her a safe route for urgent concerns. Limited or restricted personal contact still blurs the boundary, and declining without explanation leaves her uncertain about where to turn."}, {"id": 1624, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Kavita wants to return to full time work next term and asks how she can tell whether her fatigue plan is actually helping over the coming weeks, given that her symptoms vary from day to day. Which approach is most appropriate for monitoring change?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the timed walk test alone, since walking speed reflects fatigue directly and reliably", "B": "Rely on her verbal report at each session, because fatigue cannot be measured usefully", "C": "Repeat the Fatigue Severity Scale and review an activity and fatigue diary at each session", "D": "Track the maximum heart rate reached during exercise as a marker of her fatigue level"}, "cluster_id": "CHS166", "cluster_text": "Kavita Patel, 38, a primary school teacher, has relapsing remitting multiple sclerosis diagnosed six years ago and has had no relapse for two years. She attends her third physiotherapy session by telehealth from home. She reports fatigue that builds from early afternoon, legs that feel heavy after a school day, and worse symptoms in hot weather. Her Fatigue Severity Scale score is 5.8 out of 7. She walks without an aid and drives. She plans to return to full time work next term. Her activity is uneven: long walks on good days followed by two days of rest. Today she says the sessions are the most helpful part of her week. She asks to message the physiotherapist on a personal mobile at night about symptoms and has sent a follow request to the physiotherapist's private social media account.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Repeat the Fatigue Severity Scale and review an activity and fatigue diary at each session): A validated self report scale combined with a diary captures both the experience and the pattern of fatigue that matter to her return to work. Walking speed and heart rate reflect other constructs, and dismissing measurement ignores available tools."}, {"id": 1625, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Hartley's weakness and slurred speech lasted 40 minutes and resolved completely, and today's neurological screen is normal. He feels well and considers the episode unimportant, so he plans to return to work tomorrow. Why does this event still require urgent medical assessment?", "options": {"A": "It signals a high short-term risk of stroke, so urgent specialist assessment and prevention are needed", "B": "Full recovery shows no tissue injury, but scans are needed to exclude a brain tumour first", "C": "Delayed deficits often follow a TIA, so rehabilitation should be arranged before risk review", "D": "Most events like this settle, so review at his next scheduled GP visit is adequate"}, "cluster_id": "CHS167", "cluster_text": "Mr Bevan Hartley, 58, sheep and grain farmer, attends the rural physiotherapy clinic for chronic low back pain. Yesterday at home he had 40 minutes of left arm weakness and slurred speech, which resolved completely. He did not seek care. History: smoker for 30 years, hypertension, not taking his tablets regularly. The nearest hospital is two hours away by road. A visiting GP attends once a week, and a nurse practitioner works at the clinic. Observations: BP 168/96 sitting, pulse irregularly irregular at 92 per minute, SpO2 96 percent, afebrile. Neurological screen today: normal power, sensation, speech and facial symmetry. He wants to continue his planned back exercises and return to work on the header tomorrow.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It signals a high short-term risk of stroke, so urgent specialist assessment and prevention are needed): A TIA is a warning event, and the risk of a completed stroke is highest in the first days, so prompt specialist assessment and secondary prevention are time critical. Complete recovery does not mean the cause, such as atrial fibrillation or carotid disease, has been treated. Waiting for a routine appointment leaves him exposed during the highest-risk period."}, {"id": 1626, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Palpation shows Mr Hartley's pulse is irregularly irregular at 92 per minute and his resting blood pressure is 168/96. He reports no palpitations or chest pain. The nurse practitioner is in the clinic today. What is the most appropriate physiotherapy action regarding the pulse finding?", "options": {"A": "Report it to the nurse practitioner today as a possible arrhythmia and defer exercise testing", "B": "Record it and continue the exercise assessment, as the resting heart rate is within normal limits", "C": "Teach him to check his pulse after exercise and review the pattern at his next visit", "D": "Ask him to rest for ten minutes, then repeat the pulse check before any action"}, "cluster_id": "CHS167", "cluster_text": "Mr Bevan Hartley, 58, sheep and grain farmer, attends the rural physiotherapy clinic for chronic low back pain. Yesterday at home he had 40 minutes of left arm weakness and slurred speech, which resolved completely. He did not seek care. History: smoker for 30 years, hypertension, not taking his tablets regularly. The nearest hospital is two hours away by road. A visiting GP attends once a week, and a nurse practitioner works at the clinic. Observations: BP 168/96 sitting, pulse irregularly irregular at 92 per minute, SpO2 96 percent, afebrile. Neurological screen today: normal power, sensation, speech and facial symmetry. He wants to continue his planned back exercises and return to work on the header tomorrow.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report it to the nurse practitioner today as a possible arrhythmia and defer exercise testing): An irregularly irregular pulse in a person with a recent TIA suggests atrial fibrillation, a common source of cardiac emboli, so it needs prompt medical review and a hold on exercise testing. A resting rate in the normal range does not exclude it. Self-monitoring or waiting delays diagnosis and decisions about anticoagulation."}, {"id": 1627, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "The GP has started anticoagulant medication and cleared Mr Hartley for exercise. His resting blood pressure is now 150/90, and he works around machinery and livestock. He wants to start a farm fitness program straight away. What advice about exercise is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Start vigorous interval training to lower his stroke risk quickly, and stop only if symptoms return", "B": "Begin graded moderate aerobic and light resistance exercise, monitor blood pressure, and avoid straining", "C": "Use heavy lifting with controlled breathing out on effort, because strength gains lower vascular risk fastest", "D": "Defer exercise until his resting blood pressure has been in the normal range for three months"}, "cluster_id": "CHS167", "cluster_text": "Mr Bevan Hartley, 58, sheep and grain farmer, attends the rural physiotherapy clinic for chronic low back pain. Yesterday at home he had 40 minutes of left arm weakness and slurred speech, which resolved completely. He did not seek care. History: smoker for 30 years, hypertension, not taking his tablets regularly. The nearest hospital is two hours away by road. A visiting GP attends once a week, and a nurse practitioner works at the clinic. Observations: BP 168/96 sitting, pulse irregularly irregular at 92 per minute, SpO2 96 percent, afebrile. Neurological screen today: normal power, sensation, speech and facial symmetry. He wants to continue his planned back exercises and return to work on the header tomorrow.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Begin graded moderate aerobic and light resistance exercise, monitor blood pressure, and avoid straining): Moderate aerobic activity with light resistance work improves vascular risk and suits a patient who is medically cleared, provided blood pressure is monitored and straining is avoided. Heavy lifting raises blood pressure sharply, and anticoagulation adds bleeding risk around machinery and livestock. Vigorous intervals are premature, while delaying exercise for months forfeits benefit that clearance already supports."}, {"id": 1628, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "The nurse practitioner phones asking for a quick update on Mr Hartley before the visiting GP's clinic. The call is brief and the clinic is busy. The physiotherapist has his symptom timeline, pulse and blood pressure readings in the notes. Which approach best supports a safe handover?", "options": {"A": "Give a full account of every session so no detail is lost, and let her choose", "B": "Email the clinic notes now and phone again only if she has not acknowledged them", "C": "Use a structured format such as ISBAR: timeline, pulse, blood pressure and a clear request", "D": "Lead with your impression and treatment plan, then add the observations if she asks for them"}, "cluster_id": "CHS167", "cluster_text": "Mr Bevan Hartley, 58, sheep and grain farmer, attends the rural physiotherapy clinic for chronic low back pain. Yesterday at home he had 40 minutes of left arm weakness and slurred speech, which resolved completely. He did not seek care. History: smoker for 30 years, hypertension, not taking his tablets regularly. The nearest hospital is two hours away by road. A visiting GP attends once a week, and a nurse practitioner works at the clinic. Observations: BP 168/96 sitting, pulse irregularly irregular at 92 per minute, SpO2 96 percent, afebrile. Neurological screen today: normal power, sensation, speech and facial symmetry. He wants to continue his planned back exercises and return to work on the header tomorrow.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use a structured format such as ISBAR: timeline, pulse, blood pressure and a clear request): A structured format such as ISBAR puts the key risk, the irregular pulse and blood pressure readings, and the action requested in a predictable order, which reduces omissions in a brief call. Narrative detail buries the priority, and leading with impressions before data invites assumptions. Email alone risks the urgent finding not being read in time."}, {"id": 1629, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "On line bisection Mrs Vasilakis marks well right of the true centre, and she omits most left-sided stars on cancellation, yet she turns towards touch on her left shoulder. Which interpretation of these assessment findings is most appropriate for planning her therapy?", "options": {"A": "Left arm apraxia, causing poor motor planning during the cancellation and bisection tasks", "B": "Left homonymous hemianopia, a field cut from right optic radiation damage that head turning cannot overcome", "C": "Left hemisensory loss, causing reduced awareness of the left side of her body", "D": "Left unilateral spatial neglect, an attentional deficit rather than a primary visual field loss"}, "cluster_id": "CHS168", "cluster_text": "Mrs Kalliopi Vasilakis, 76, retired seamstress, speaks Greek and understands little English. She had a right middle cerebral artery ischaemic stroke five weeks ago and lives at home with her husband Nikos, who speaks fluent English. She has been referred to community rehabilitation after hospital discharge. History: hypertension, type 2 diabetes. Findings at today's first visit: left upper limb strength 2/5, left lower limb 3/5, sits independently, walks 10 metres with a hemi-walker and supervision. She bumps into the left doorframe and leaves the food on the left of her plate. Line bisection: marks well right of centre. Star cancellation: omits most left-sided stars. She turns her head to the left when touched on the left shoulder. HR 78, BP 138/82, SpO2 97 percent on room air. Nikos wishes to interpret for her to save time.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Left unilateral spatial neglect, an attentional deficit rather than a primary visual field loss): Omitting left-sided items on bisection and cancellation while still responding to touch on the left points to neglect, an attention deficit, rather than a field cut or sensory loss. A homonymous hemianopia would largely be overcome by head turning and would not explain errors on a drawing task. Naming the problem correctly shapes the treatment plan."}, {"id": 1630, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Vasilakis walks into the left doorframe and leaves food on the left of her plate. Her husband will continue practice between visits. Which strategy is most appropriate to improve her attention to the left over time during gait and meals?", "options": {"A": "Keep everything she needs on her right, where she attends, so she completes tasks without prompts", "B": "Place cues and objects on the left and prompt her to scan towards that side", "C": "Restrict right arm use during meals, so that attention is forced towards the left", "D": "Practise walking with her eyes closed, to strengthen proprioceptive awareness of the left side"}, "cluster_id": "CHS168", "cluster_text": "Mrs Kalliopi Vasilakis, 76, retired seamstress, speaks Greek and understands little English. She had a right middle cerebral artery ischaemic stroke five weeks ago and lives at home with her husband Nikos, who speaks fluent English. She has been referred to community rehabilitation after hospital discharge. History: hypertension, type 2 diabetes. Findings at today's first visit: left upper limb strength 2/5, left lower limb 3/5, sits independently, walks 10 metres with a hemi-walker and supervision. She bumps into the left doorframe and leaves the food on the left of her plate. Line bisection: marks well right of centre. Star cancellation: omits most left-sided stars. She turns her head to the left when touched on the left shoulder. HR 78, BP 138/82, SpO2 97 percent on room air. Nikos wishes to interpret for her to save time.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Place cues and objects on the left and prompt her to scan towards that side): Cueing and scanning practice, with objects deliberately placed on the neglected side, directs attention leftwards and can be graded as she improves. Keeping everything on the right accommodates the deficit but does not reduce it. Closing the eyes removes the visual cues she needs, and restricting the right arm adds frustration without training attention."}, {"id": 1631, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Nikos offers to interpret during sessions to save time, as his wife speaks little English. Mrs Vasilakis has not yet been asked about her own preferences or goals. What is the most appropriate approach to communicating with her at today's assessment?", "options": {"A": "Use simple English, gestures and demonstration, as she follows familiar words and Nikos can clarify", "B": "Book a professional interpreter, and invite Nikos to contribute as her carer rather than interpreter", "C": "Accept Nikos as interpreter for exercises, and use a professional interpreter for consent and risk discussions", "D": "Ask the hospital for a translated written exercise sheet, and rely on Nikos for verbal explanations"}, "cluster_id": "CHS168", "cluster_text": "Mrs Kalliopi Vasilakis, 76, retired seamstress, speaks Greek and understands little English. She had a right middle cerebral artery ischaemic stroke five weeks ago and lives at home with her husband Nikos, who speaks fluent English. She has been referred to community rehabilitation after hospital discharge. History: hypertension, type 2 diabetes. Findings at today's first visit: left upper limb strength 2/5, left lower limb 3/5, sits independently, walks 10 metres with a hemi-walker and supervision. She bumps into the left doorframe and leaves the food on the left of her plate. Line bisection: marks well right of centre. Star cancellation: omits most left-sided stars. She turns her head to the left when touched on the left shoulder. HR 78, BP 138/82, SpO2 97 percent on room air. Nikos wishes to interpret for her to save time.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Book a professional interpreter, and invite Nikos to contribute as her carer rather than interpreter): A qualified interpreter protects accuracy, confidentiality and her right to take part in decisions in her own language. Family members may filter or alter information, so Nikos contributes as her carer and source of history. Splitting the session between family and professional interpretation still leaves treatment instructions open to error."}, {"id": 1632, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "At a home visit a week later, Mrs Vasilakis is newly drowsy and her left arm is weaker than before. Nikos reports a sudden severe headache this morning. Her observations are stable, and she was alert at her last visit. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Ask Nikos to drive her to the emergency department while you phone ahead to warn staff", "B": "Complete a gentler session, then report the change to her GP at the next review", "C": "Phone her GP to arrange an urgent appointment today, and advise rest and fluids until then", "D": "Call an ambulance and stay with her, recording her observations until the paramedics arrive"}, "cluster_id": "CHS168", "cluster_text": "Mrs Kalliopi Vasilakis, 76, retired seamstress, speaks Greek and understands little English. She had a right middle cerebral artery ischaemic stroke five weeks ago and lives at home with her husband Nikos, who speaks fluent English. She has been referred to community rehabilitation after hospital discharge. History: hypertension, type 2 diabetes. Findings at today's first visit: left upper limb strength 2/5, left lower limb 3/5, sits independently, walks 10 metres with a hemi-walker and supervision. She bumps into the left doorframe and leaves the food on the left of her plate. Line bisection: marks well right of centre. Star cancellation: omits most left-sided stars. She turns her head to the left when touched on the left shoulder. HR 78, BP 138/82, SpO2 97 percent on room air. Nikos wishes to interpret for her to save time.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Call an ambulance and stay with her, recording her observations until the paramedics arrive): A sudden severe headache with new drowsiness and increased weakness suggests a possible new bleed or extension of the stroke, which is a time critical emergency. An ambulance brings treatment and monitoring to her, whereas private transport or a GP visit delays care. A gentler session would be unsafe."}, {"id": 1633, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Doyle's pain is reproduced by resisted wrist extension with the elbow straight and by palpation over the common extensor origin, while cervical movement and neural tension tests are unremarkable. Which interpretation of these findings is most reasonable at this stage of the assessment?", "options": {"A": "Cervical radiculopathy at the C6 level referring pain into the lateral elbow", "B": "Radial tunnel syndrome with posterior interosseous nerve compression in the proximal forearm region", "C": "Load related tendinopathy at the common extensor origin, consistent with lateral elbow tendinopathy", "D": "Lateral collateral ligament sprain from a recent hyperextension injury during fencing work"}, "cluster_id": "CHS169", "cluster_text": "Graham Doyle is a 52 year old sheep farmer living 200 km from the nearest regional hospital. He has had right lateral elbow pain for 10 weeks, which began after a fortnight of fence building and shearing. Pain is 6/10 with gripping and lifting and settles at rest. He has no neck pain or arm paraesthesia. Examination: full elbow range, tenderness over the common extensor origin, pain on resisted wrist extension with the elbow straight, Cozen's test positive, cervical movements and upper limb neural tension tests unremarkable. Right grip strength is about 60 percent of the left. His GP referred him. You visit the local community health centre fortnightly, where an allied health assistant works daily. Graham must keep working through lambing.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Load related tendinopathy at the common extensor origin, consistent with lateral elbow tendinopathy): Pain on resisted wrist extension, local tenderness at the extensor origin and normal neural and cervical testing point to a local tendon problem. Nerve entrapment would usually bring forearm tenderness distal to the epicondyle and neural signs, and a cervical source would change with neck movement. A ligament sprain does not fit a gradual onset after repetitive work."}, {"id": 1634, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Doyle must keep working through the busy lambing season and cannot rest his arm for weeks. Examination shows no neurological signs and full elbow range. Considering his pain and work demands, which initial management plan is most appropriate to begin at this first visit?", "options": {"A": "Four weeks off all arm use in a wrist splint before any strengthening begins", "B": "Referral for a corticosteroid injection first, then stretching only until the pain has settled", "C": "Education, graded wrist extensor loading and modified grip tasks that keep him working", "D": "Deep transverse friction massage and passive stretching alone, progressing to loading only after pain subsides"}, "cluster_id": "CHS169", "cluster_text": "Graham Doyle is a 52 year old sheep farmer living 200 km from the nearest regional hospital. He has had right lateral elbow pain for 10 weeks, which began after a fortnight of fence building and shearing. Pain is 6/10 with gripping and lifting and settles at rest. He has no neck pain or arm paraesthesia. Examination: full elbow range, tenderness over the common extensor origin, pain on resisted wrist extension with the elbow straight, Cozen's test positive, cervical movements and upper limb neural tension tests unremarkable. Right grip strength is about 60 percent of the left. His GP referred him. You visit the local community health centre fortnightly, where an allied health assistant works daily. Graham must keep working through lambing.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Education, graded wrist extensor loading and modified grip tasks that keep him working): Tendinopathy responds best to progressive loading combined with load management, and this suits a man who cannot stop working. Prolonged rest and splinting deconditions the tendon and is impractical here. Injection and passive techniques alone offer short term relief at best and do not build tendon capacity."}, {"id": 1635, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Doyle's loading program is prescribed and he performed it correctly today. You will be away for two weeks and the allied health assistant at the community health centre will see him daily. Which task is appropriate to delegate to the assistant during this period?", "options": {"A": "Re-examining with Cozen's test to decide whether the program should change", "B": "Progressing the exercise load once his pain has settled over several consecutive sessions", "C": "Explaining the diagnosis and obtaining consent to revise the treatment plan", "D": "Supervising the set exercises, recording his pain response and reporting changes to you"}, "cluster_id": "CHS169", "cluster_text": "Graham Doyle is a 52 year old sheep farmer living 200 km from the nearest regional hospital. He has had right lateral elbow pain for 10 weeks, which began after a fortnight of fence building and shearing. Pain is 6/10 with gripping and lifting and settles at rest. He has no neck pain or arm paraesthesia. Examination: full elbow range, tenderness over the common extensor origin, pain on resisted wrist extension with the elbow straight, Cozen's test positive, cervical movements and upper limb neural tension tests unremarkable. Right grip strength is about 60 percent of the left. His GP referred him. You visit the local community health centre fortnightly, where an allied health assistant works daily. Graham must keep working through lambing.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Supervising the set exercises, recording his pain response and reporting changes to you): An assistant can deliver a clearly defined, already prescribed program and report observations, with the physiotherapist remaining accountable for the plan. Clinical reasoning tasks such as progressing load, reassessing with special tests and obtaining informed consent for changes cannot be delegated because they need professional judgement."}, {"id": 1636, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "After six weeks of loading, Mr Doyle's pain with gripping has improved to 3/10 but his grip is still weaker than the left. He asks whether pain returning during shearing means he is damaging the tendon. Which explanation is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Mild pain during loading is common while the tendon adapts, so adjust load rather than stop", "B": "Recurring pain shows the tendon is inflamed, so anti-inflammatory gel and rest should replace loading", "C": "Returning pain shows the program is ineffective, so passive treatments like ultrasound should replace it", "D": "Pain during gripping signals fresh tissue tearing, so he should stop loading until pain has gone"}, "cluster_id": "CHS169", "cluster_text": "Graham Doyle is a 52 year old sheep farmer living 200 km from the nearest regional hospital. He has had right lateral elbow pain for 10 weeks, which began after a fortnight of fence building and shearing. Pain is 6/10 with gripping and lifting and settles at rest. He has no neck pain or arm paraesthesia. Examination: full elbow range, tenderness over the common extensor origin, pain on resisted wrist extension with the elbow straight, Cozen's test positive, cervical movements and upper limb neural tension tests unremarkable. Right grip strength is about 60 percent of the left. His GP referred him. You visit the local community health centre fortnightly, where an allied health assistant works daily. Graham must keep working through lambing.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Mild pain during loading is common while the tendon adapts, so adjust load rather than stop): Tolerable, settling pain during rehabilitation is expected as a tendon adapts, and load is adjusted to keep symptoms acceptable. Reading pain as tearing or failure of the program would lead to unnecessary rest or a switch to passive care, which has weaker evidence for lasting benefit."}, {"id": 1637, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Russo's morning blood gas shows a near-normal pH, a high PaCO2 and a raised bicarbonate. He is clinically stable on day three and his oxygen needs have not changed. The physiotherapist is documenting the likely acid-base state before planning exercise. Which interpretation best fits these results?", "options": {"A": "Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis, since high carbon dioxide and raised bicarbonate both lower pH", "B": "Acute uncompensated respiratory acidosis from a new infection, needing urgent escalation of ventilatory support first", "C": "Chronic compensated respiratory acidosis, with the kidneys retaining bicarbonate to buffer the raised carbon dioxide", "D": "Metabolic alkalosis from diuretic use, with the raised bicarbonate driving secondary carbon dioxide retention"}, "cluster_id": "CHS170", "cluster_text": "Salvatore Russo, 54, a taxi driver, is on day 3 of admission to an acute ward for hypercapnic respiratory failure due to obesity hypoventilation syndrome (BMI 52) with untreated obstructive sleep apnoea. He uses bilevel non-invasive ventilation overnight and 2 L/min oxygen via nasal prongs by day, with a medical target saturation range of 88 to 92 percent. Resting observations: HR 92, BP 148/88, RR 22, SpO2 91 percent. Morning blood gas: pH 7.36, PaCO2 60 mmHg, bicarbonate raised. Auscultation shows reduced basal air entry and no added sounds. His ankles are oedematous. He walks 15 metres with a frame before reporting Borg breathlessness of 6 out of 10. The medical team has asked for twice daily mobilisation. The ward allied health assistant has capacity to help with the afternoon walks.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Chronic compensated respiratory acidosis, with the kidneys retaining bicarbonate to buffer the raised carbon dioxide): A near-normal pH with a raised PaCO2 and raised bicarbonate shows the kidneys have retained bicarbonate over days to weeks, which is typical of long-standing hypoventilation in obesity hypoventilation syndrome. An acute respiratory acidosis would show a clearly low pH with little bicarbonate rise. Mixed acidosis is tempting, but a raised bicarbonate buffers acid rather than adding to it."}, {"id": 1638, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "At the end of a walk Mr Russo's SpO2 reads 86 percent on 2 L/min and he is more breathless. His documented oxygen target is 88 to 92 percent. How should the physiotherapist manage his oxygen now that he is seated and resting?", "options": {"A": "Increase oxygen in small steps until within the target range, monitoring saturations and alertness", "B": "Raise oxygen flow until saturations reach 96 percent, then wean slowly once he feels settled", "C": "Apply the bilevel ventilator straight away, because non-invasive ventilation should follow each episode of exertional breathlessness", "D": "Leave oxygen unchanged and coach pursed lip breathing, since exertional desaturation usually settles on its own"}, "cluster_id": "CHS170", "cluster_text": "Salvatore Russo, 54, a taxi driver, is on day 3 of admission to an acute ward for hypercapnic respiratory failure due to obesity hypoventilation syndrome (BMI 52) with untreated obstructive sleep apnoea. He uses bilevel non-invasive ventilation overnight and 2 L/min oxygen via nasal prongs by day, with a medical target saturation range of 88 to 92 percent. Resting observations: HR 92, BP 148/88, RR 22, SpO2 91 percent. Morning blood gas: pH 7.36, PaCO2 60 mmHg, bicarbonate raised. Auscultation shows reduced basal air entry and no added sounds. His ankles are oedematous. He walks 15 metres with a frame before reporting Borg breathlessness of 6 out of 10. The medical team has asked for twice daily mobilisation. The ward allied health assistant has capacity to help with the afternoon walks.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Increase oxygen in small steps until within the target range, monitoring saturations and alertness): In chronic hypercapnia a controlled saturation range is the goal, so oxygen is titrated up in small steps and his mental state is watched for drowsiness. Driving saturations to 96 percent risks suppressing ventilatory drive and worsening carbon dioxide retention. Bilevel ventilation is prescribed for sleep by the medical team and is not applied after each walk."}, {"id": 1639, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "On day five Mr Russo is hard to rouse for his morning session, with a flushed face, a new headache and a respiratory rate of 14. Nursing staff say he took off his bilevel mask overnight. What should the physiotherapist do about his reduced responsiveness this morning?", "options": {"A": "Increase his oxygen flow to relieve the headache and continue treatment once his colour has improved", "B": "Sit him over the edge of the bed to improve ventilation, then reassess his alertness", "C": "Withhold mobilisation, check observations and oxygen delivery, and promptly inform the nurse in charge", "D": "Reapply the bilevel mask and proceed with the planned walk, as ventilation will clear carbon dioxide"}, "cluster_id": "CHS170", "cluster_text": "Salvatore Russo, 54, a taxi driver, is on day 3 of admission to an acute ward for hypercapnic respiratory failure due to obesity hypoventilation syndrome (BMI 52) with untreated obstructive sleep apnoea. He uses bilevel non-invasive ventilation overnight and 2 L/min oxygen via nasal prongs by day, with a medical target saturation range of 88 to 92 percent. Resting observations: HR 92, BP 148/88, RR 22, SpO2 91 percent. Morning blood gas: pH 7.36, PaCO2 60 mmHg, bicarbonate raised. Auscultation shows reduced basal air entry and no added sounds. His ankles are oedematous. He walks 15 metres with a frame before reporting Borg breathlessness of 6 out of 10. The medical team has asked for twice daily mobilisation. The ward allied health assistant has capacity to help with the afternoon walks.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Withhold mobilisation, check observations and oxygen delivery, and promptly inform the nurse in charge): Drowsiness, headache and a flushed face after missed ventilation suggest rising carbon dioxide, and a respiratory rate of 14 shows he is not compensating. He is unsafe to mobilise and needs prompt medical review. Raising oxygen alone can worsen hypoventilation, and sitting him up without reassessment delays escalation."}, {"id": 1640, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "The physiotherapist wants the allied health assistant to supervise Mr Russo's afternoon walks. He is stable today, with a set walking distance, a documented oxygen target and clear stop criteria in his plan. Which approach to delegation is most appropriate here?", "options": {"A": "Decline to delegate, because assistants may not work with patients receiving oxygen therapy or ventilation", "B": "Delegate with written instructions on distance, oxygen target and stop criteria, staying available for review", "C": "Hand over the whole programme including progression, letting the assistant adjust it by how he looks", "D": "Delegate by verbal handover of the distance, because the assistant has long ward experience mobilising patients"}, "cluster_id": "CHS170", "cluster_text": "Salvatore Russo, 54, a taxi driver, is on day 3 of admission to an acute ward for hypercapnic respiratory failure due to obesity hypoventilation syndrome (BMI 52) with untreated obstructive sleep apnoea. He uses bilevel non-invasive ventilation overnight and 2 L/min oxygen via nasal prongs by day, with a medical target saturation range of 88 to 92 percent. Resting observations: HR 92, BP 148/88, RR 22, SpO2 91 percent. Morning blood gas: pH 7.36, PaCO2 60 mmHg, bicarbonate raised. Auscultation shows reduced basal air entry and no added sounds. His ankles are oedematous. He walks 15 metres with a frame before reporting Borg breathlessness of 6 out of 10. The medical team has asked for twice daily mobilisation. The ward allied health assistant has capacity to help with the afternoon walks.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Delegate with written instructions on distance, oxygen target and stop criteria, staying available for review): Safe delegation needs a stable patient, a specific task, written limits and a route for escalation, while the physiotherapist keeps responsibility for assessment and progression. Verbal instructions based on experience leave gaps in safety limits. Assistants can work with patients on oxygen when the plan is clear and supervised, so declining is unnecessary."}, {"id": 1641, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Wanganeen is alert, off sedation and medically cleared for a trial of a one-way speaking valve by the multidisciplinary team. Before the valve is attached to the tracheostomy hub, which condition must be confirmed to keep the trial safe?", "options": {"A": "Suction has cleared all secretions from below the cuff and the inner cannula is removed", "B": "Cough strength has been confirmed as strong enough that suctioning is no longer needed", "C": "The cuff is partly inflated to keep some seal while air passes through the valve", "D": "The cuff is fully deflated so exhaled air can flow around the tube and out"}, "cluster_id": "CHS171", "cluster_text": "Mr Peter Wanganeen, 49, an Aboriginal man from a remote community, is on day 12 in intensive care after severe pneumonia and respiratory failure. He has had a cuffed tracheostomy for 12 days and now breathes through a humidified tracheostomy collar at FiO2 0.28: SpO2 95 percent, RR 18, HR 84. He is alert, follows commands and communicates by mouthing words. His cough is weak but effective and produces moderate thick secretions from the tube. The cuff is currently inflated. The team is considering a trial of a one-way speaking valve. He smoked about 20 cigarettes a day for 30 years, has not been asked about smoking this admission, and says he wants to get back to Country as soon as possible. His sister visits daily and the unit has an Aboriginal Hospital Liaison Officer available.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The cuff is fully deflated so exhaled air can flow around the tube and out): A one-way valve closes on exhalation, so all expired air must travel around the tube and through the upper airway. With an inflated or partly inflated cuff the patient cannot breathe out and the airway is effectively blocked. A strong cough and minimal secretions are good goals but they are not what makes the trial safe."}, {"id": 1642, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Two minutes into the trial, with the cuff reported as deflated, Mr Wanganeen becomes anxious, struggles to breathe out, makes noisy effortful breaths and his SpO2 falls to 88 percent. What is the most appropriate immediate action to take now?", "options": {"A": "Remove the valve immediately, then check the cuff is fully deflated and reassess the airway", "B": "Suction through the tube with the valve in place, then continue the trial with extra oxygen", "C": "Ask him to cough forcefully while the valve stays on to clear the obstruction", "D": "Coach slow breathing out through the valve for two minutes, since anxiety often causes these signs"}, "cluster_id": "CHS171", "cluster_text": "Mr Peter Wanganeen, 49, an Aboriginal man from a remote community, is on day 12 in intensive care after severe pneumonia and respiratory failure. He has had a cuffed tracheostomy for 12 days and now breathes through a humidified tracheostomy collar at FiO2 0.28: SpO2 95 percent, RR 18, HR 84. He is alert, follows commands and communicates by mouthing words. His cough is weak but effective and produces moderate thick secretions from the tube. The cuff is currently inflated. The team is considering a trial of a one-way speaking valve. He smoked about 20 cigarettes a day for 30 years, has not been asked about smoking this admission, and says he wants to get back to Country as soon as possible. His sister visits daily and the unit has an Aboriginal Hospital Liaison Officer available.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Remove the valve immediately, then check the cuff is fully deflated and reassess the airway): Noisy effortful expiration with desaturation signals that air cannot leave through the upper airway, so the valve comes off at once, which restores airflow through the tube. The cuff and any obstruction can then be checked before any further trial. Coaching, coughing or suctioning with the valve on delays relief of blocked outflow."}, {"id": 1643, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Wanganeen has thick secretions in the tube and a weak but effective cough that tires him. SpO2 is 94 percent on the collar and chest auscultation shows transmitted upper airway sounds only. Which management is most appropriate for the secretions this afternoon?", "options": {"A": "Suction deeply every hour on a fixed schedule, to prevent secretions pooling and tiring him later", "B": "Give manual hyperinflation with a bag through the tube to mobilise secretions toward the large airways", "C": "Humidify, position upright, teach supported huff and cough, and suction only if cough fails", "D": "Instil saline into the tube before each suction pass to loosen secretions and speed clearance"}, "cluster_id": "CHS171", "cluster_text": "Mr Peter Wanganeen, 49, an Aboriginal man from a remote community, is on day 12 in intensive care after severe pneumonia and respiratory failure. He has had a cuffed tracheostomy for 12 days and now breathes through a humidified tracheostomy collar at FiO2 0.28: SpO2 95 percent, RR 18, HR 84. He is alert, follows commands and communicates by mouthing words. His cough is weak but effective and produces moderate thick secretions from the tube. The cuff is currently inflated. The team is considering a trial of a one-way speaking valve. He smoked about 20 cigarettes a day for 30 years, has not been asked about smoking this admission, and says he wants to get back to Country as soon as possible. His sister visits daily and the unit has an Aboriginal Hospital Liaison Officer available.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Humidify, position upright, teach supported huff and cough, and suction only if cough fails): His cough is effective and the lungs sound clear, so the aim is to support his own secretion clearance with humidification, upright positioning and an assisted cough while preserving his energy. Suction is kept for when the cough fails to clear the tube. Fixed-schedule suctioning, routine saline instillation and manual hyperinflation add risk and are not indicated by these findings."}, {"id": 1644, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Wanganeen quietly asks whether smoking has made his lungs worse and says he doubts he could stop. He has not discussed smoking with any clinician this admission. What is the most appropriate way to respond directly as his physiotherapist?", "options": {"A": "Recommend nicotine replacement patches to him straight away, since cravings may worsen his breathing", "B": "Answer honestly, link brief advice to his goals, and offer liaison officer and quit support", "C": "Explain in detail how smoking damaged his lungs, using pictures, so he understands the urgency", "D": "Suggest he raise it with his GP after discharge, when recovery allows a proper conversation"}, "cluster_id": "CHS171", "cluster_text": "Mr Peter Wanganeen, 49, an Aboriginal man from a remote community, is on day 12 in intensive care after severe pneumonia and respiratory failure. He has had a cuffed tracheostomy for 12 days and now breathes through a humidified tracheostomy collar at FiO2 0.28: SpO2 95 percent, RR 18, HR 84. He is alert, follows commands and communicates by mouthing words. His cough is weak but effective and produces moderate thick secretions from the tube. The cuff is currently inflated. The team is considering a trial of a one-way speaking valve. He smoked about 20 cigarettes a day for 30 years, has not been asked about smoking this admission, and says he wants to get back to Country as soon as possible. His sister visits daily and the unit has an Aboriginal Hospital Liaison Officer available.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Answer honestly, link brief advice to his goals, and offer liaison officer and quit support): Brief, respectful advice tied to his own goal of returning to Country is an appropriate health promotion step for any clinician, and involving the Aboriginal Hospital Liaison Officer and cessation support makes it culturally safe. A detailed lecture is unlikely to build motivation, deferring the conversation misses a teachable moment, and recommending nicotine products sits outside physiotherapy scope."}, {"id": 1645, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr McAllister needs to get out of bed for his morning session while observing the surgeon's restrictions on bending and twisting. He asks whether he can sit straight up by pulling on the bed rail. Which bed mobility technique should you teach him?", "options": {"A": "Roll with trunk rotation while pulling on the rail, to stay independent and move quickly", "B": "Bridge up onto his elbows with trunk flexion, then swing both legs sideways off the bed", "C": "Sit up directly by flexing the trunk forward with abdominal effort, which strengthens the core early", "D": "Log roll to side lying, then push up with his arms while lowering the legs"}, "cluster_id": "CHS172", "cluster_text": "Mr Ian McAllister, 73, a retired farmer, is on day 10 after an L4 to L5 posterior decompression and instrumented fusion for spinal stenosis with neurogenic claudication. He is in a subacute rehabilitation unit. The wound is clean and dry. Pain is 4/10 with movement. Right ankle dorsiflexion is 4/5 and the right big toe extensor is 4/5, improved from 3/5 before surgery. Sensation is reduced over the right lateral calf. Bladder and bowel function are normal. HR 74, BP 128/76, afebrile. The surgeon has ordered no bending, lifting or twisting for six weeks and a lumbar brace when out of bed. He is oriented in the morning but becomes confused in the evenings. His wife attends daily. He says he does not want stair practice and wants to go home next week.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Log roll to side lying, then push up with his arms while lowering the legs): Log rolling keeps the spine in one line and respects the restriction on bending and twisting, and pushing up with the arms avoids trunk flexion. Sitting up with trunk flexion, rotating while pulling on a rail, or bridging onto the elbows with flexion would all load the fusion site."}, {"id": 1646, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "At the next session on day 12, Mr McAllister reports he has had trouble starting to pass urine since last night. He also has numbness around his buttocks and inner thighs and both legs feel heavy. What should you do first?", "options": {"A": "Encourage fluids and timed toileting for several days, then review a bladder diary next session", "B": "Stop treatment, note the findings, and arrange urgent medical review for possible cauda equina compression", "C": "Document the findings and report to the surgical team at the next scheduled ward round", "D": "Stop treatment, arrange a bladder scan for retention, and report results to the team that afternoon"}, "cluster_id": "CHS172", "cluster_text": "Mr Ian McAllister, 73, a retired farmer, is on day 10 after an L4 to L5 posterior decompression and instrumented fusion for spinal stenosis with neurogenic claudication. He is in a subacute rehabilitation unit. The wound is clean and dry. Pain is 4/10 with movement. Right ankle dorsiflexion is 4/5 and the right big toe extensor is 4/5, improved from 3/5 before surgery. Sensation is reduced over the right lateral calf. Bladder and bowel function are normal. HR 74, BP 128/76, afebrile. The surgeon has ordered no bending, lifting or twisting for six weeks and a lumbar brace when out of bed. He is oriented in the morning but becomes confused in the evenings. His wife attends daily. He says he does not want stair practice and wants to go home next week.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stop treatment, note the findings, and arrange urgent medical review for possible cauda equina compression): Difficulty starting urination with saddle numbness and heavy legs after lumbar surgery are signs of possible cauda equina compression, a surgical emergency where delay risks permanent damage, so treatment stops and urgent medical review is arranged. Fluids and a bladder diary treat a bladder problem and miss the cause. Reporting at the next routine round is too slow. A bladder scan can confirm retention but should not delay urgent medical review."}, {"id": 1647, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr McAllister's wife asks what activities are safe at home during the first weeks after his fusion. He is keen to help around the house. The surgeon's restrictions on bending, lifting and twisting still apply. Which activity is most consistent with those restrictions?", "options": {"A": "Resting in a firm chair for most of the day, standing only for meals and toileting", "B": "Helping with meals by lifting light pots from the stove, keeping his back straight", "C": "Gentle pelvic and trunk rotation exercises in lying, to keep his lumbar spine mobile", "D": "Short frequent walks on level ground, standing tall, building distance gradually each day"}, "cluster_id": "CHS172", "cluster_text": "Mr Ian McAllister, 73, a retired farmer, is on day 10 after an L4 to L5 posterior decompression and instrumented fusion for spinal stenosis with neurogenic claudication. He is in a subacute rehabilitation unit. The wound is clean and dry. Pain is 4/10 with movement. Right ankle dorsiflexion is 4/5 and the right big toe extensor is 4/5, improved from 3/5 before surgery. Sensation is reduced over the right lateral calf. Bladder and bowel function are normal. HR 74, BP 128/76, afebrile. The surgeon has ordered no bending, lifting or twisting for six weeks and a lumbar brace when out of bed. He is oriented in the morning but becomes confused in the evenings. His wife attends daily. He says he does not want stair practice and wants to go home next week.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Short frequent walks on level ground, standing tall, building distance gradually each day): Short walks on level ground in an upright posture build activity tolerance and circulation without bending, lifting or twisting, which is why they are the standard early activity after fusion. Long periods of chair rest promote deconditioning and stiffness. Lifting pots still loads the spine even with good technique, and rotation exercises breach the twisting restriction."}, {"id": 1648, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "In the evening Mr McAllister seems confused and his wife, who wants him home, says she consents on his behalf to stair training. In the morning he is clear but declines stairs. What is the most appropriate approach to consent for stair practice?", "options": {"A": "Treat his earlier cooperation in sessions as implied consent and proceed with stairs under supervision", "B": "Assess his understanding of this decision when he is clearest, explain risks, and respect his choice", "C": "Defer to the surgeon's order and teach stairs without discussion, as consent to surgery covered rehabilitation", "D": "Accept his wife's consent, since evening confusion means he lacks capacity to make any treatment decisions"}, "cluster_id": "CHS172", "cluster_text": "Mr Ian McAllister, 73, a retired farmer, is on day 10 after an L4 to L5 posterior decompression and instrumented fusion for spinal stenosis with neurogenic claudication. He is in a subacute rehabilitation unit. The wound is clean and dry. Pain is 4/10 with movement. Right ankle dorsiflexion is 4/5 and the right big toe extensor is 4/5, improved from 3/5 before surgery. Sensation is reduced over the right lateral calf. Bladder and bowel function are normal. HR 74, BP 128/76, afebrile. The surgeon has ordered no bending, lifting or twisting for six weeks and a lumbar brace when out of bed. He is oriented in the morning but becomes confused in the evenings. His wife attends daily. He says he does not want stair practice and wants to go home next week.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Assess his understanding of this decision when he is clearest, explain risks, and respect his choice): Capacity is decision specific and can fluctuate, so it should be assessed for stair practice at his clearest time. A relative cannot consent for a person who has capacity, and neither earlier cooperation nor consent to surgery replaces informed agreement to this particular activity."}, {"id": 1649, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Jakub has a 2 cm red, warm area on his right heel that does not blanch under pressure. The skin is intact and sensation is reduced below the knee. He wears ankle foot orthoses daily. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Pad the orthosis over the heel and shorten walking bouts, rechecking the skin at the end", "B": "Document and photograph the area, then ask his family to monitor it until the next session", "C": "Refer him urgently to the orthotist for a refit and continue walking practice in the meantime", "D": "Offload the heel, remove the right orthosis, and recheck the skin after pressure has been relieved"}, "cluster_id": "CHS173", "cluster_text": "Jakub Horvat, 14, has an L4 level myelomeningocele and a ventriculoperitoneal shunt placed in infancy, with a revision at age nine. He attends a community health centre gym for fortnightly physiotherapy. He walks around the house and school using ankle foot orthoses and forearm crutches, and uses a wheelchair for longer distances. Latex allergy is documented. Hip flexion and knee extension are 4/5, ankle dorsiflexion 2/5 and plantarflexion 0/5, with reduced sensation below the knees. Today there is a 2 cm red area on the right heel that does not blanch, feels warm and has intact skin. Last session he slipped during a mat transfer and grazed his shin, with no head strike. The only record is a brief clinical note.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Offload the heel, remove the right orthosis, and recheck the skin after pressure has been relieved): Non blanching redness over an area with reduced sensation is an early pressure injury, so the first step is to remove the load and then check the skin. Padding inside the orthosis and continuing to walk keeps loading the damaged tissue. Documenting and waiting for the family to monitor delays action, and a referral does not protect the heel today."}, {"id": 1650, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Midway through standing practice in the gym, Jakub reports a new severe headache and nausea, vomits once, and appears unusually drowsy and irritable compared with earlier in the session. He has a ventriculoperitoneal shunt in place and no recent fall. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Stop the session, monitor observations, and arrange urgent medical assessment through emergency services", "B": "Complete a lower limb neurological screen to localise the cause before deciding whether referral is needed", "C": "Phone his parents and advise a GP appointment within the next few days if symptoms persist", "D": "Offer rest and fluids, recheck his symptoms after twenty minutes, then continue with a lighter programme"}, "cluster_id": "CHS173", "cluster_text": "Jakub Horvat, 14, has an L4 level myelomeningocele and a ventriculoperitoneal shunt placed in infancy, with a revision at age nine. He attends a community health centre gym for fortnightly physiotherapy. He walks around the house and school using ankle foot orthoses and forearm crutches, and uses a wheelchair for longer distances. Latex allergy is documented. Hip flexion and knee extension are 4/5, ankle dorsiflexion 2/5 and plantarflexion 0/5, with reduced sensation below the knees. Today there is a 2 cm red area on the right heel that does not blanch, feels warm and has intact skin. Last session he slipped during a mat transfer and grazed his shin, with no head strike. The only record is a brief clinical note.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stop the session, monitor observations, and arrange urgent medical assessment through emergency services): Headache, vomiting and drowsiness in a person with a shunt suggest shunt malfunction and raised intracranial pressure, which is a medical emergency. Delay for observation or a routine appointment is unsafe, and a limb screen does not address the immediate risk."}, {"id": 1651, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Jakub wants to walk longer distances at school, but he fatigues quickly on crutches and his shoulders ache afterwards. He has strong quadriceps and weak ankle muscles. Which plan best balances his mobility goals with his long term health and energy?", "options": {"A": "Keep household walking with orthoses and crutches, and use the wheelchair for longer distances", "B": "Use the wheelchair for all mobility to prevent falls and protect his shoulders from crutch overuse", "C": "Pause all walking until the orthoses are reviewed, then restart with a wheeled walker at school", "D": "Train walking to full school distances, accepting fatigue as the effort needed to keep independence"}, "cluster_id": "CHS173", "cluster_text": "Jakub Horvat, 14, has an L4 level myelomeningocele and a ventriculoperitoneal shunt placed in infancy, with a revision at age nine. He attends a community health centre gym for fortnightly physiotherapy. He walks around the house and school using ankle foot orthoses and forearm crutches, and uses a wheelchair for longer distances. Latex allergy is documented. Hip flexion and knee extension are 4/5, ankle dorsiflexion 2/5 and plantarflexion 0/5, with reduced sensation below the knees. Today there is a 2 cm red area on the right heel that does not blanch, feels warm and has intact skin. Last session he slipped during a mat transfer and grazed his shin, with no head strike. The only record is a brief clinical note.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Keep household walking with orthoses and crutches, and use the wheelchair for longer distances): Walking maintains bone health, hip stability and function, so it should continue where it is efficient, while the wheelchair protects the upper limbs and energy over longer distances. Stopping walking loses those benefits, and pushing fatigue risks shoulder overuse and falls."}, {"id": 1652, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Last session Jakub slipped during a mat transfer and grazed his shin, with no head strike and no first aid needed. The physiotherapist wrote only a brief clinical note about it afterwards. What is the most appropriate action regarding this incident now?", "options": {"A": "Notify AHPRA, because any patient injury during treatment must be reported directly to the regulator", "B": "Record it in the notes only, as the injury was minor and needed no first aid", "C": "Complete an incident report, update the notes, and tell Jakub and his parents what occurred", "D": "Report it only if Jakub's parents ask about the graze or make a formal complaint"}, "cluster_id": "CHS173", "cluster_text": "Jakub Horvat, 14, has an L4 level myelomeningocele and a ventriculoperitoneal shunt placed in infancy, with a revision at age nine. He attends a community health centre gym for fortnightly physiotherapy. He walks around the house and school using ankle foot orthoses and forearm crutches, and uses a wheelchair for longer distances. Latex allergy is documented. Hip flexion and knee extension are 4/5, ankle dorsiflexion 2/5 and plantarflexion 0/5, with reduced sensation below the knees. Today there is a 2 cm red area on the right heel that does not blanch, feels warm and has intact skin. Last session he slipped during a mat transfer and grazed his shin, with no head strike. The only record is a brief clinical note.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Complete an incident report, update the notes, and tell Jakub and his parents what occurred): A fall during a session is a reportable incident even when harm is minor, because reporting allows the service to identify risks and prevent recurrence. Open disclosure to the patient and family is expected. AHPRA notifications relate to notifiable conduct concerns, not every minor incident."}, {"id": 1653, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "On valgus stress testing Jayden shows increased medial opening at 30 degrees of flexion with a firm end point, and none in full extension. Pain is felt over the medial joint line, and the cruciate ligament tests are negative. Which interpretation of this finding is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "An isolated medial meniscus tear, since firm end point laxity at 30 degrees implies meniscal origin", "B": "A partial medial collateral ligament injury with the posteromedial capsule and cruciates likely intact", "C": "A patellar subluxation with medial retinacular strain, as the mechanism involved a planted foot and rotation", "D": "A complete medial collateral ligament rupture with associated posteromedial capsule and anterior cruciate ligament injury"}, "cluster_id": "CHS174", "cluster_text": "Jayden Williams, 15, comes to the emergency department with his mother two hours after a rugby league tackle. His right knee was struck from the outside while his foot was planted, and he felt a pop and pain on the inner knee. No medical history. Findings: mild swelling and bruising over the medial joint line, tender over the proximal medial collateral ligament. Knee flexion is 0 to 100 degrees, limited by pain. Valgus stress at 30 degrees flexion gives increased opening with a firm end point and pain; at full extension there is no laxity. Lachman and posterior drawer tests are negative. He weight bears with an antalgic gait. Pain 5/10. Neurovascular status is normal and radiographs show no fracture. Jayden's coach has rung the department asking for details of the injury and likely return to play.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A partial medial collateral ligament injury with the posteromedial capsule and cruciates likely intact): Opening at 30 degrees with a firm end point and no laxity in extension indicates a partial injury, because the secondary restraints that tighten in extension are still working. A complete rupture would show opening in extension and a soft end point. A meniscal tear does not produce valgus laxity."}, {"id": 1654, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Radiographs show no fracture. Jayden can weight bear with an antalgic gait and has stable cruciate ligaments. Swelling is mild and neurovascular status is normal. Which initial management plan is most appropriate to start before he is discharged from the emergency department?", "options": {"A": "Weight bearing as pain allows with early pain-free movement and swelling control", "B": "Compression bandage and ice only, with rest until the knee is pain free before any movement", "C": "Non weight bearing on crutches until swelling fully resolves, with static quadriceps exercise only until then", "D": "Immobilise in a straight knee brace for six weeks before beginning any range of motion exercise"}, "cluster_id": "CHS174", "cluster_text": "Jayden Williams, 15, comes to the emergency department with his mother two hours after a rugby league tackle. His right knee was struck from the outside while his foot was planted, and he felt a pop and pain on the inner knee. No medical history. Findings: mild swelling and bruising over the medial joint line, tender over the proximal medial collateral ligament. Knee flexion is 0 to 100 degrees, limited by pain. Valgus stress at 30 degrees flexion gives increased opening with a firm end point and pain; at full extension there is no laxity. Lachman and posterior drawer tests are negative. He weight bears with an antalgic gait. Pain 5/10. Neurovascular status is normal and radiographs show no fracture. Jayden's coach has rung the department asking for details of the injury and likely return to play.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Weight bearing as pain allows with early pain-free movement and swelling control): Partial medial collateral ligament sprains heal well with early protected loading and movement, which limits stiffness and muscle loss. Prolonged immobilisation or enforced non weight bearing delays recovery. Rest until pain free also withholds the graded movement that guides ligament healing."}, {"id": 1655, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "At review one week later Jayden's swelling has increased and valgus stress now shows laxity in full extension as well as at 30 degrees, with a soft end point. He reports the knee feels unstable on stairs. What is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the valgus stress test firmly twice more to confirm laxity before deciding whether to refer", "B": "Pause progression and refer back to the doctor for imaging and orthopaedic review", "C": "Progress to closed chain strengthening and balance work, because the laxity pattern fits normal sprain recovery", "D": "Continue the programme and add a hinged brace, since laxity is expected as the ligament remodels"}, "cluster_id": "CHS174", "cluster_text": "Jayden Williams, 15, comes to the emergency department with his mother two hours after a rugby league tackle. His right knee was struck from the outside while his foot was planted, and he felt a pop and pain on the inner knee. No medical history. Findings: mild swelling and bruising over the medial joint line, tender over the proximal medial collateral ligament. Knee flexion is 0 to 100 degrees, limited by pain. Valgus stress at 30 degrees flexion gives increased opening with a firm end point and pain; at full extension there is no laxity. Lachman and posterior drawer tests are negative. He weight bears with an antalgic gait. Pain 5/10. Neurovascular status is normal and radiographs show no fracture. Jayden's coach has rung the department asking for details of the injury and likely return to play.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Pause progression and refer back to the doctor for imaging and orthopaedic review): Laxity in extension with a soft end point and new instability suggests a more extensive injury than first thought, so loading should pause and medical review with imaging is needed. Bracing and progression would load a possibly unstable knee. Repeated forceful stress testing adds risk without changing the need to refer."}, {"id": 1656, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Jayden's coach rings the emergency department and asks the physiotherapist for details of the diagnosis and when Jayden can play. Jayden is 15 and his mother is present in the department, with no recorded consent for sharing information. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Share the diagnosis and return to play, since the coach has an interest in his welfare", "B": "Withhold details until consent is given by Jayden and his mother, and document the request", "C": "Ask the coach to request in writing, then release a summary of the injury and plan", "D": "Share general injury information without naming the diagnosis, and leave the call out of the notes"}, "cluster_id": "CHS174", "cluster_text": "Jayden Williams, 15, comes to the emergency department with his mother two hours after a rugby league tackle. His right knee was struck from the outside while his foot was planted, and he felt a pop and pain on the inner knee. No medical history. Findings: mild swelling and bruising over the medial joint line, tender over the proximal medial collateral ligament. Knee flexion is 0 to 100 degrees, limited by pain. Valgus stress at 30 degrees flexion gives increased opening with a firm end point and pain; at full extension there is no laxity. Lachman and posterior drawer tests are negative. He weight bears with an antalgic gait. Pain 5/10. Neurovascular status is normal and radiographs show no fracture. Jayden's coach has rung the department asking for details of the injury and likely return to play.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Withhold details until consent is given by Jayden and his mother, and document the request): Health information is private and may only be shared with consent or where another lawful basis applies, and a coach has no automatic entitlement to it. For a young person, the physiotherapist should involve Jayden and his parent in deciding what is shared. The request and the response belong in the clinical record, and a written request does not replace consent."}, {"id": 1657, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Over two weeks Linh has more wet cough, green sputum, tiredness and a 1 kg weight loss, with coarse crackles at the right base and a respiratory rate of 24. Which interpretation and priority are most appropriate for you to act on today?", "options": {"A": "Fatigue from skipped airway clearance only, so the priority is making sessions more enjoyable for her", "B": "Normal fluctuation in bronchiectasis, so intensifying airway clearance at home is enough without medical contact", "C": "A probable pulmonary exacerbation, so her cystic fibrosis team should review her promptly for treatment", "D": "A flare of pancreatic insufficiency, so dietitian review should come before any respiratory plan changes"}, "cluster_id": "CHS175", "cluster_text": "Linh Nguyen, 8, has cystic fibrosis with bronchiectasis affecting the right middle and lower lobes. Her family runs a farm 400 km from the nearest tertiary hospital. Physiotherapy review is by video link, with a remote area nurse at the local clinic present. Her mother speaks Vietnamese and limited English. Over two weeks Linh has had more wet cough, green sputum and less energy, and her mother says she has stopped airway clearance because Linh finds it boring and tiring. The nurse reports SpO2 95 percent, RR 24, afebrile, coarse crackles at the right base and a 1 kg weight loss. Linh's twelve year old brother is present and has been translating for his mother. Prescribed treatments are an inhaled antibiotic, hypertonic saline, a bronchodilator and airway clearance.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A probable pulmonary exacerbation, so her cystic fibrosis team should review her promptly for treatment): Increased cough and purulent sputum, reduced energy, weight loss and new crackles over days are the typical features of a pulmonary exacerbation in cystic fibrosis, which usually needs medical review and often antibiotic treatment. Airway clearance alone will not treat an infection. Nutrition matters but does not explain the chest findings."}, {"id": 1658, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Linh's prescribed treatments are an inhaled antibiotic, hypertonic saline, a bronchodilator and airway clearance. Linh tires quickly and the family has only a short time each morning, so order matters. Her mother asks for the correct order to follow at home. Which sequence is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Hypertonic saline, then the bronchodilator after airway clearance, with the inhaled antibiotic given before she finishes", "B": "Inhaled antibiotic, then airway clearance, with hypertonic saline and the bronchodilator given afterwards for comfort", "C": "Airway clearance first to mobilise secretions, then hypertonic saline, the bronchodilator and the inhaled antibiotic", "D": "Bronchodilator, then hypertonic saline, then airway clearance, with the inhaled antibiotic given last"}, "cluster_id": "CHS175", "cluster_text": "Linh Nguyen, 8, has cystic fibrosis with bronchiectasis affecting the right middle and lower lobes. Her family runs a farm 400 km from the nearest tertiary hospital. Physiotherapy review is by video link, with a remote area nurse at the local clinic present. Her mother speaks Vietnamese and limited English. Over two weeks Linh has had more wet cough, green sputum and less energy, and her mother says she has stopped airway clearance because Linh finds it boring and tiring. The nurse reports SpO2 95 percent, RR 24, afebrile, coarse crackles at the right base and a 1 kg weight loss. Linh's twelve year old brother is present and has been translating for his mother. Prescribed treatments are an inhaled antibiotic, hypertonic saline, a bronchodilator and airway clearance.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Bronchodilator, then hypertonic saline, then airway clearance, with the inhaled antibiotic given last): The bronchodilator opens the airways and reduces bronchospasm that hypertonic saline can provoke, the saline then hydrates secretions, airway clearance moves them, and the inhaled antibiotic last reaches cleared airways better. Giving the antibiotic or saline before clearing reduces how well each can deposit or work."}, {"id": 1659, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Linh is eight, has a productive cough, finds airway clearance boring, the family has no specialised equipment, and you can only supervise by video. Which daily, age appropriate approach to airway clearance is most appropriate to teach and monitor remotely?", "options": {"A": "Autogenic drainage taught from written instructions alone, as it needs no demonstration or feedback", "B": "Passive postural drainage in head down tilt for thirty minutes, since it demands no effort", "C": "Manual percussion by her mother in head down positions, because it has the strongest supporting evidence", "D": "Positive expiratory pressure with huffing taught by video, made engaging with a reward chart"}, "cluster_id": "CHS175", "cluster_text": "Linh Nguyen, 8, has cystic fibrosis with bronchiectasis affecting the right middle and lower lobes. Her family runs a farm 400 km from the nearest tertiary hospital. Physiotherapy review is by video link, with a remote area nurse at the local clinic present. Her mother speaks Vietnamese and limited English. Over two weeks Linh has had more wet cough, green sputum and less energy, and her mother says she has stopped airway clearance because Linh finds it boring and tiring. The nurse reports SpO2 95 percent, RR 24, afebrile, coarse crackles at the right base and a 1 kg weight loss. Linh's twelve year old brother is present and has been translating for his mother. Prescribed treatments are an inhaled antibiotic, hypertonic saline, a bronchodilator and airway clearance.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Positive expiratory pressure with huffing taught by video, made engaging with a reward chart): A simple, child friendly technique that she can perform herself and that the clinician can observe and correct on video suits remote care, and a reward system addresses the adherence problem. Head down positions are no longer recommended routinely. Autogenic drainage is a complex skill needing demonstration and feedback."}, {"id": 1660, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Linh's twelve year old brother has been interpreting for his mother during the video review. She nods but cannot restate the plan. The remote area nurse speaks English only. How should you proceed with the family's language needs from now on?", "options": {"A": "Book a professional interpreter by phone or video and address Linh's mother directly through them", "B": "Keep using Linh's brother, who knows the routine well, and simplify the instructions further", "C": "Send written instructions from an online translation tool, and check understanding by phone next week", "D": "Ask the nurse to explain the plan in plain English with pictures and written copies"}, "cluster_id": "CHS175", "cluster_text": "Linh Nguyen, 8, has cystic fibrosis with bronchiectasis affecting the right middle and lower lobes. Her family runs a farm 400 km from the nearest tertiary hospital. Physiotherapy review is by video link, with a remote area nurse at the local clinic present. Her mother speaks Vietnamese and limited English. Over two weeks Linh has had more wet cough, green sputum and less energy, and her mother says she has stopped airway clearance because Linh finds it boring and tiring. The nurse reports SpO2 95 percent, RR 24, afebrile, coarse crackles at the right base and a 1 kg weight loss. Linh's twelve year old brother is present and has been translating for his mother. Prescribed treatments are an inhaled antibiotic, hypertonic saline, a bronchodilator and airway clearance.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Book a professional interpreter by phone or video and address Linh's mother directly through them): A qualified interpreter gives accurate, confidential communication and allows the mother to take part in the decisions about her daughter's care; a child should not interpret health information. Nodding does not show understanding, so teach back should be used. Plain English from the nurse and machine translation do not provide reliable two way communication."}, {"id": 1661, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Over the last three days Tevita's left hip flexion has fallen sharply, and the joint is warm, swollen and painful at end range, with no fracture seen on admission imaging. Which complication should the physiotherapist most suspect, given his recent brain injury?", "options": {"A": "Heterotopic ossification, which needs medical review before any further stretching of the hip", "B": "Deep vein thrombosis of the thigh, managed with rest, elevation and monitoring of limb girth", "C": "Hip flexor muscle strain from overstretching, settled with relative rest, ice and gentle active-assisted movement", "D": "Hip capsule stiffness from immobility, managed with sustained end-range stretching and heat before exercise"}, "cluster_id": "CHS176", "cluster_text": "Mr Tevita Liufau, 24, apprentice electrician, is three weeks after a motorcycle crash with moderate to severe traumatic brain injury (admission Glasgow Coma Scale 8). Post-traumatic amnesia has resolved. He is on a subacute rehabilitation ward. Findings: right limb strength about 4 out of 5, ataxic gait with a wide base, Berg Balance Scale 38/56, fatigue and distractibility after about 15 minutes of activity. Left hip flexion measured 100 degrees five days ago and is now 60 degrees, with local warmth, swelling and pain at end range. Supine HR 84, BP 122/76. He is progressing standing practice with the ward team. Today the physiotherapist has a ward round at 10:00, a booked 9:30 gait session with a stable patient, Mr Chen, and a new admission waiting for screening.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Heterotopic ossification, which needs medical review before any further stretching of the hip): Heterotopic ossification is a recognised complication after traumatic brain injury and presents with a warm, swollen, painful joint and a rapidly falling range of movement. Forceful stretching can worsen it, so the medical team should investigate first. Simple stiffness or a strain would not usually produce warmth and swelling with such a fast loss of range."}, {"id": 1662, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Tevita is distractible, becomes fatigued after about 15 minutes, and has an ataxic gait with poor balance. The gym is noisy at midday. He is motivated and keen to return to work. Which session structure is most appropriate for his balance training?", "options": {"A": "One continuous forty-five minute block in the busy gym, to build tolerance for real-life distractions", "B": "Dual-task walking with mental arithmetic from the start, to challenge his attention and balance", "C": "Short task-specific blocks in a quiet area, with rest breaks and one instruction at a time", "D": "Passive treatment only until his attention improves, then introduce active balance practice in the gym"}, "cluster_id": "CHS176", "cluster_text": "Mr Tevita Liufau, 24, apprentice electrician, is three weeks after a motorcycle crash with moderate to severe traumatic brain injury (admission Glasgow Coma Scale 8). Post-traumatic amnesia has resolved. He is on a subacute rehabilitation ward. Findings: right limb strength about 4 out of 5, ataxic gait with a wide base, Berg Balance Scale 38/56, fatigue and distractibility after about 15 minutes of activity. Left hip flexion measured 100 degrees five days ago and is now 60 degrees, with local warmth, swelling and pain at end range. Supine HR 84, BP 122/76. He is progressing standing practice with the ward team. Today the physiotherapist has a ward round at 10:00, a booked 9:30 gait session with a stable patient, Mr Chen, and a new admission waiting for screening.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Short task-specific blocks in a quiet area, with rest breaks and one instruction at a time): Impaired attention and fatigue after brain injury respond best to short, focused, repeated practice with rest, a low distraction environment and simple instructions. Prolonged sessions in a noisy gym exceed his capacity and reduce learning. Dual-task training comes later once single tasks are safe, and passive-only treatment misses the active practice needed for recovery."}, {"id": 1663, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "During supported standing Tevita becomes pale and sweaty. His heart rate rises from 84 to 118 and blood pressure falls to 88/50, but he remains conscious and talking. He has been mostly in bed for three weeks. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Call the medical emergency team and leave him standing supported until the team arrives to assess", "B": "Keep him standing and ask for slow deep breaths for one minute, as it usually settles", "C": "Lower him to supine or recline the chair, raise his legs and monitor observations", "D": "Sit him upright in a wheelchair, offer fluids and reassess when he looks better"}, "cluster_id": "CHS176", "cluster_text": "Mr Tevita Liufau, 24, apprentice electrician, is three weeks after a motorcycle crash with moderate to severe traumatic brain injury (admission Glasgow Coma Scale 8). Post-traumatic amnesia has resolved. He is on a subacute rehabilitation ward. Findings: right limb strength about 4 out of 5, ataxic gait with a wide base, Berg Balance Scale 38/56, fatigue and distractibility after about 15 minutes of activity. Left hip flexion measured 100 degrees five days ago and is now 60 degrees, with local warmth, swelling and pain at end range. Supine HR 84, BP 122/76. He is progressing standing practice with the ward team. Today the physiotherapist has a ward round at 10:00, a booked 9:30 gait session with a stable patient, Mr Chen, and a new admission waiting for screening.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Lower him to supine or recline the chair, raise his legs and monitor observations): Pallor, a rising heart rate and a fall in blood pressure after prolonged bed rest indicate orthostatic intolerance, and the immediate priority is to restore cerebral perfusion by lying him flat and raising his legs. Continuing to stand risks syncope and a fall, and sitting upright keeps him gravity dependent. Escalation can follow once he is safe, but he should not be left standing."}, {"id": 1664, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "It is 9:15 and the physiotherapist has until the 10:00 ward round. Tevita's hip is suspected to be heterotopic ossification, Mr Chen has a booked 9:30 gait session and is stable, and a new admission awaits screening. Which task should be done first?", "options": {"A": "Run the booked gait session first, as the patient is stable and delay disrupts his timetable", "B": "Alert the medical team to the suspected hip complication, hold stretching, then reorder other tasks", "C": "Screen the new admission first, so goals and a plan are ready for the ward round", "D": "Assess the hip and stretch to maintain range, then report findings at the ward round"}, "cluster_id": "CHS176", "cluster_text": "Mr Tevita Liufau, 24, apprentice electrician, is three weeks after a motorcycle crash with moderate to severe traumatic brain injury (admission Glasgow Coma Scale 8). Post-traumatic amnesia has resolved. He is on a subacute rehabilitation ward. Findings: right limb strength about 4 out of 5, ataxic gait with a wide base, Berg Balance Scale 38/56, fatigue and distractibility after about 15 minutes of activity. Left hip flexion measured 100 degrees five days ago and is now 60 degrees, with local warmth, swelling and pain at end range. Supine HR 84, BP 122/76. He is progressing standing practice with the ward team. Today the physiotherapist has a ward round at 10:00, a booked 9:30 gait session with a stable patient, Mr Chen, and a new admission waiting for screening.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Alert the medical team to the suspected hip complication, hold stretching, then reorder other tasks): A possible serious complication, where continued stretching could cause harm, takes priority, so the medical team is informed first and the plan adjusted. The stable patient's gait session and the new admission screening can be rearranged or delegated without safety risk. Stretching the suspected joint while waiting for the round could worsen the problem."}, {"id": 1665, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Brandon wants to play in the carnival, his pain is 5/10 after training, and there is no night pain or swelling. Given likely Osgood-Schlatter disease at the knee and Sever disease at the heels, which approach to load over the next three weeks is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Reduce jumping and sprinting to keep pain tolerable, with calf and quadriceps stretching and strengthening", "B": "Use a knee brace and heel lifts full time for six weeks, resting the growth plates", "C": "Continue full training with ice after sessions, as pain reflects normal growth and settles with maturity", "D": "Stop sport until pain-free for a month, then return to full training and matches straight away"}, "cluster_id": "CHS177", "cluster_text": "Brandon Simpson, 13, a Wiradjuri boy, attends the school sport clinic with his aunty, Karen, who is his primary carer. He plays football and basketball, trains five days a week, and has grown 6 cm this year. He reports 4 months of pain at the front of the left knee and pain in both heels, 5/10 after training and easing with rest. Findings: prominent, tender left tibial tuberosity; pain on resisted knee extension and single-leg hopping; no effusion; full knee range; tight quadriceps and hamstrings. Calcaneal squeeze test is painful bilaterally. Calf tightness is present, with ankle dorsiflexion 5 degrees short on both sides on lunge testing. No night pain, no systemic symptoms and no history of trauma. Brandon answers questions briefly and looks at the floor. He does not want to miss the school football carnival in three weeks.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reduce jumping and sprinting to keep pain tolerable, with calf and quadriceps stretching and strengthening): Apophysitis is managed by modifying load to a tolerable pain level while maintaining activity and improving flexibility and strength of the muscles pulling on the growth plates. Total rest followed by an abrupt return invites a flare. Ignoring pain overlooks the load problem, and long immobilisation is unnecessary and deconditions a growing athlete."}, {"id": 1666, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Brandon's heel pain, calf tightness and painful calcaneal squeeze test fit Sever disease. Karen asks why a growing child gets this and would like to explain it to his football coach as well. Which explanation of the underlying mechanism is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Compression of the tibial nerve in the tarsal tunnel as bone growth outpaces soft tissue", "B": "Repetitive traction from the Achilles tendon irritates the calcaneal growth plate during rapid growth", "C": "A stress fracture of the calcaneus from overuse, appearing when training load rises in growth spurts", "D": "Inflammation of the plantar fascia at its calcaneal origin from repeated heel strike on hard surfaces"}, "cluster_id": "CHS177", "cluster_text": "Brandon Simpson, 13, a Wiradjuri boy, attends the school sport clinic with his aunty, Karen, who is his primary carer. He plays football and basketball, trains five days a week, and has grown 6 cm this year. He reports 4 months of pain at the front of the left knee and pain in both heels, 5/10 after training and easing with rest. Findings: prominent, tender left tibial tuberosity; pain on resisted knee extension and single-leg hopping; no effusion; full knee range; tight quadriceps and hamstrings. Calcaneal squeeze test is painful bilaterally. Calf tightness is present, with ankle dorsiflexion 5 degrees short on both sides on lunge testing. No night pain, no systemic symptoms and no history of trauma. Brandon answers questions briefly and looks at the floor. He does not want to miss the school football carnival in three weeks.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Repetitive traction from the Achilles tendon irritates the calcaneal growth plate during rapid growth): Sever disease is a traction apophysitis at the calcaneal growth plate, driven by the pull of the tight Achilles and calf complex during growth and load. Plantar fasciitis and stress fracture are different conditions with different clinical signs. Tarsal tunnel compression produces neural symptoms, which Brandon does not describe."}, {"id": 1667, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Two weeks later Karen phones to say Brandon's knee pain now wakes him at night and a firm swelling has appeared over his lower thigh. He seems unusually tired. His training has not changed and there has been no fall. What should the physiotherapist advise?", "options": {"A": "Arrange prompt GP review for investigation, and pause training until the cause is known", "B": "Book an urgent clinic reassessment and repeat the knee and thigh examination before deciding on referral", "C": "Refer to a sports medicine physician at the next available routine appointment, keeping training load reduced", "D": "Treat as an Osgood-Schlatter flare with load management, and reassess the swelling next visit"}, "cluster_id": "CHS177", "cluster_text": "Brandon Simpson, 13, a Wiradjuri boy, attends the school sport clinic with his aunty, Karen, who is his primary carer. He plays football and basketball, trains five days a week, and has grown 6 cm this year. He reports 4 months of pain at the front of the left knee and pain in both heels, 5/10 after training and easing with rest. Findings: prominent, tender left tibial tuberosity; pain on resisted knee extension and single-leg hopping; no effusion; full knee range; tight quadriceps and hamstrings. Calcaneal squeeze test is painful bilaterally. Calf tightness is present, with ankle dorsiflexion 5 degrees short on both sides on lunge testing. No night pain, no systemic symptoms and no history of trauma. Brandon answers questions briefly and looks at the floor. He does not want to miss the school football carnival in three weeks.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Arrange prompt GP review for investigation, and pause training until the cause is known): Night pain, a new firm mass over the lower thigh and unusual tiredness are red flags for a bone tumour or another serious cause, so he needs prompt medical investigation and a pause in training. Reassessing in clinic first or waiting for a routine appointment adds delay when imaging is needed. Treating the picture as apophysitis ignores that the new findings are in a different site and do not fit it."}, {"id": 1668, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "At the first visit Brandon gives short answers and looks down while Karen waits quietly. You need his history, his goals and agreement on a plan. Which approach best reflects culturally safe practice with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people?", "options": {"A": "Invite Karen to stay, yarn to build trust, explain plainly, and offer Aboriginal Health Worker support", "B": "Avoid raising cultural background and treat him like other students so he is not singled out", "C": "Interview Brandon alone with brief closed questions to be efficient, then explain the plan to Karen", "D": "Direct clinical questions to Karen, as his quietness means he expects family to speak for him"}, "cluster_id": "CHS177", "cluster_text": "Brandon Simpson, 13, a Wiradjuri boy, attends the school sport clinic with his aunty, Karen, who is his primary carer. He plays football and basketball, trains five days a week, and has grown 6 cm this year. He reports 4 months of pain at the front of the left knee and pain in both heels, 5/10 after training and easing with rest. Findings: prominent, tender left tibial tuberosity; pain on resisted knee extension and single-leg hopping; no effusion; full knee range; tight quadriceps and hamstrings. Calcaneal squeeze test is painful bilaterally. Calf tightness is present, with ankle dorsiflexion 5 degrees short on both sides on lunge testing. No night pain, no systemic symptoms and no history of trauma. Brandon answers questions briefly and looks at the floor. He does not want to miss the school football carnival in three weeks.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Invite Karen to stay, yarn to build trust, explain plainly, and offer Aboriginal Health Worker support): Culturally safe practice involves relationship building, including family where the person wants it, plain language and an offer of cultural support such as an Aboriginal Health Worker. Looking down or giving short answers is not a reason to bypass him, as he is still the patient. Ignoring culture in the name of equal treatment misses the person's needs and preferences."}, {"id": 1669, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Dwayne has oligoarticular juvenile idiopathic arthritis. Alongside joint management, the physiotherapist wants to reinforce monitoring of the extra-articular complication most strongly associated with this subtype, which can be symptom-free in its early stages. Which complication should be monitored through regular specialist screening?", "options": {"A": "Renal impairment, detected through routine urine testing and blood pressure checks at each clinic visit", "B": "Restrictive lung disease, detected through annual spirometry and chest radiographs organised by the paediatrician", "C": "Pericarditis, detected through regular cardiac auscultation and electrocardiogram checks by the treating team", "D": "Chronic anterior uveitis, detected through regular slit-lamp eye examinations by an ophthalmologist"}, "cluster_id": "CHS178", "cluster_text": "Dwayne Brown, 14, lives in a regional town and attends community physiotherapy with his grandmother, who is his carer. He has oligoarticular juvenile idiopathic arthritis diagnosed two years ago, mainly affecting the left knee, managed by a paediatric rheumatology team in the city and the local Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service. Today the left knee is mildly swollen and warm, flexion 100 degrees compared with 140 on the right, extension lag 5 degrees, pain 3 out of 10. Morning stiffness lasts 20 minutes. He takes methotrexate prescribed by rheumatology. He has stopped playing football because of knee discomfort and wants to return. You have written a home and gym programme. Your manager suggests that Priya, an allied health assistant who has worked in the service for two years, runs the weekly group exercise session with Dwayne.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Chronic anterior uveitis, detected through regular slit-lamp eye examinations by an ophthalmologist): Oligoarticular juvenile idiopathic arthritis carries a recognised risk of chronic anterior uveitis, which can damage vision before the child notices symptoms, so scheduled eye screening matters. The other complications are possible in some systemic inflammatory diseases but are not the classic concern for this subtype."}, {"id": 1670, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Dwayne's left knee is mildly warm and swollen, flexion 100 degrees with an extension lag of 5 degrees, and pain 3 out of 10. He wants to return to football training. Which plan is most appropriate for his exercise programme at this stage?", "options": {"A": "High-load plyometric and agility training to rebuild muscle quickly before the next rheumatology review appointment", "B": "Low-impact strengthening and range work, with staged sport return guided by swelling and rheumatology advice", "C": "Full rest from sport and exercise until swelling resolves, then resume football training at full intensity", "D": "Passive stretching into end range flexion and extension daily, avoiding active strengthening until swelling has settled"}, "cluster_id": "CHS178", "cluster_text": "Dwayne Brown, 14, lives in a regional town and attends community physiotherapy with his grandmother, who is his carer. He has oligoarticular juvenile idiopathic arthritis diagnosed two years ago, mainly affecting the left knee, managed by a paediatric rheumatology team in the city and the local Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service. Today the left knee is mildly swollen and warm, flexion 100 degrees compared with 140 on the right, extension lag 5 degrees, pain 3 out of 10. Morning stiffness lasts 20 minutes. He takes methotrexate prescribed by rheumatology. He has stopped playing football because of knee discomfort and wants to return. You have written a home and gym programme. Your manager suggests that Priya, an allied health assistant who has worked in the service for two years, runs the weekly group exercise session with Dwayne.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Low-impact strengthening and range work, with staged sport return guided by swelling and rheumatology advice): Children with juvenile arthritis benefit from regular physical activity, so exercise should continue at a level the joint tolerates, with sport reintroduced stepwise using swelling and specialist advice as guides. Complete rest causes weakness and a sudden return to full intensity risks a flare. High-impact loading and forceful stretching of an inflamed joint are premature."}, {"id": 1671, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Your manager asks Priya, who has worked in the service for two years, to run the weekly group session independently, including assessing the knee and progressing the exercises, while Dwayne's arthritis is still active and rheumatology input is ongoing. Which arrangement for delegation is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Delegate delivery of the set programme with stopping criteria, and keep assessment and progression yourself", "B": "Allow Priya to progress exercises herself if she documents changes, given her two years of experience", "C": "Let Priya run the session alone after first aid training, with supervisor review every three months", "D": "Decline delegation because allied health assistants cannot work with children who have an active inflammatory condition"}, "cluster_id": "CHS178", "cluster_text": "Dwayne Brown, 14, lives in a regional town and attends community physiotherapy with his grandmother, who is his carer. He has oligoarticular juvenile idiopathic arthritis diagnosed two years ago, mainly affecting the left knee, managed by a paediatric rheumatology team in the city and the local Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service. Today the left knee is mildly swollen and warm, flexion 100 degrees compared with 140 on the right, extension lag 5 degrees, pain 3 out of 10. Morning stiffness lasts 20 minutes. He takes methotrexate prescribed by rheumatology. He has stopped playing football because of knee discomfort and wants to return. You have written a home and gym programme. Your manager suggests that Priya, an allied health assistant who has worked in the service for two years, runs the weekly group exercise session with Dwayne.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Delegate delivery of the set programme with stopping criteria, and keep assessment and progression yourself): Delivering a prescribed programme can be delegated to a trained assistant when the physiotherapist keeps responsibility for assessment, clinical reasoning and progression, and gives clear stopping and escalation criteria. Experience does not move clinical decisions to an assistant. Refusing outright is unnecessary, and infrequent review leaves an active joint without adequate supervision."}, {"id": 1672, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "During the group session Priya stops activity and tells you Dwayne's left knee is hotter and more swollen than last week, his pain is 6 out of 10, he is limping and he feels feverish. He takes methotrexate. What is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Arrange urgent same-day medical review, because infection must be excluded in a child on immunosuppression", "B": "Reduce session intensity, add gentle range work, and reassess the knee at the next group session", "C": "Apply a heat pack to the knee and continue the session at reduced load for relief", "D": "Manage as an arthritis flare with ice and rest, and message the rheumatology team next week"}, "cluster_id": "CHS178", "cluster_text": "Dwayne Brown, 14, lives in a regional town and attends community physiotherapy with his grandmother, who is his carer. He has oligoarticular juvenile idiopathic arthritis diagnosed two years ago, mainly affecting the left knee, managed by a paediatric rheumatology team in the city and the local Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service. Today the left knee is mildly swollen and warm, flexion 100 degrees compared with 140 on the right, extension lag 5 degrees, pain 3 out of 10. Morning stiffness lasts 20 minutes. He takes methotrexate prescribed by rheumatology. He has stopped playing football because of knee discomfort and wants to return. You have written a home and gym programme. Your manager suggests that Priya, an allied health assistant who has worked in the service for two years, runs the weekly group exercise session with Dwayne.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Arrange urgent same-day medical review, because infection must be excluded in a child on immunosuppression): A hot, rapidly worsening joint with fever in a child taking immunosuppressive medication could be septic arthritis, which threatens the joint and needs urgent medical assessment the same day. Treating it as an ordinary flare or continuing exercise delays diagnosis. Heat would also be inappropriate over an acutely inflamed or infected joint."}, {"id": 1673, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Ava's harness straps hold her hips in deep flexion, her left knee is not extending actively, and the left leg lies limp during stimulation, while the right leg moves normally. Linh says Ava seems comfortable. Which concern is most important to act on today?", "options": {"A": "Early quadriceps tightness from prolonged flexion, managed with gentle passive stretching during nappy changes", "B": "Femoral nerve compromise from over-flexion in the harness, needing prompt contact with the orthopaedic team", "C": "Avascular necrosis of the femoral head from excessive abduction, to be confirmed on the next ultrasound", "D": "Normal fatigue from restricted movement in the harness, to be rechecked at the next scheduled visit"}, "cluster_id": "CHS179", "cluster_text": "Ava Nguyen, 10 weeks old, first baby, born at term after a breech presentation. Her paediatrician referred her to the clinic. Left hip: ultrasound reported a dislocatable hip and the orthopaedic team fitted a Pavlik harness two weeks ago with written wear instructions. Right foot: idiopathic talipes equinovarus (rigid, forefoot adducted, hindfoot in equinus and varus), with weekly Ponseti casting by the orthopaedic team started three weeks ago. Today the harness straps hold her hips in deep flexion. Her left knee is not extending actively and the leg lies limp when Ava is stimulated, while the right leg kicks normally. Skin behind both knees is intact. Her mother, Linh, attends with Ava. The clinic employs an allied health assistant (AHA), Sione, who has worked there for two years and holds a Certificate IV.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Femoral nerve compromise from over-flexion in the harness, needing prompt contact with the orthopaedic team): Hip flexion that is too deep in a Pavlik harness can compress the femoral nerve and produce quadriceps inactivity, which is what the limp leg and absent knee extension suggest. The harness settings belong to the orthopaedic team, so prompt contact is needed rather than waiting. Avascular necrosis relates to forced abduction and would not explain the nerve picture, and passive stretching is inappropriate."}, {"id": 1674, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Three months on, Ava's foot is plantigrade after tenotomy and casting, and a foot abduction brace is prescribed. Linh says Ava cries in the brace and suggests using it only when Ava sleeps. How should the physiotherapist respond to this suggestion?", "options": {"A": "Explain that wear as prescribed prevents relapse, and troubleshoot fit and skin with the team", "B": "Advise stopping the brace until the next review, since the foot is already plantigrade and corrected", "C": "Agree to sleep-only use for now, as comfort supports adherence, and review foot position later", "D": "Teach daily passive stretching of the foot as a substitute, since stretching also holds the correction"}, "cluster_id": "CHS179", "cluster_text": "Ava Nguyen, 10 weeks old, first baby, born at term after a breech presentation. Her paediatrician referred her to the clinic. Left hip: ultrasound reported a dislocatable hip and the orthopaedic team fitted a Pavlik harness two weeks ago with written wear instructions. Right foot: idiopathic talipes equinovarus (rigid, forefoot adducted, hindfoot in equinus and varus), with weekly Ponseti casting by the orthopaedic team started three weeks ago. Today the harness straps hold her hips in deep flexion. Her left knee is not extending actively and the leg lies limp when Ava is stimulated, while the right leg kicks normally. Skin behind both knees is intact. Her mother, Linh, attends with Ava. The clinic employs an allied health assistant (AHA), Sione, who has worked there for two years and holds a Certificate IV.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Explain that wear as prescribed prevents relapse, and troubleshoot fit and skin with the team): After Ponseti correction, relapse is common and bracing as prescribed is the main protection, so adherence matters even when the foot looks corrected. Crying often reflects fit or skin problems that can be solved with the orthopaedic team. Stretching does not replace the brace, and reducing wear without medical direction risks losing the correction."}, {"id": 1675, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Linh asks about play and development while Ava remains in the harness. The orthopaedic team has instructed that it stays on full time, and Ava is otherwise well. She worries that movement might loosen the hip. Which advice about motor development is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Limit play to supported sitting and cuddles, which keep the hips flexed and stable", "B": "Offer supervised tummy time and floor play in the harness to support development", "C": "Delay tummy time until the harness is off, so pushing does not stress the hip joint", "D": "Remove the harness for daily tummy time, then replace it, so the hips can move freely"}, "cluster_id": "CHS179", "cluster_text": "Ava Nguyen, 10 weeks old, first baby, born at term after a breech presentation. Her paediatrician referred her to the clinic. Left hip: ultrasound reported a dislocatable hip and the orthopaedic team fitted a Pavlik harness two weeks ago with written wear instructions. Right foot: idiopathic talipes equinovarus (rigid, forefoot adducted, hindfoot in equinus and varus), with weekly Ponseti casting by the orthopaedic team started three weeks ago. Today the harness straps hold her hips in deep flexion. Her left knee is not extending actively and the leg lies limp when Ava is stimulated, while the right leg kicks normally. Skin behind both knees is intact. Her mother, Linh, attends with Ava. The clinic employs an allied health assistant (AHA), Sione, who has worked there for two years and holds a Certificate IV.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Offer supervised tummy time and floor play in the harness to support development): Infants in a Pavlik harness can safely lie on their tummy and play on the floor, and doing so supports normal motor development. The harness is designed to allow kicking within a safe range while holding the hip reduced. Removing it against the orthopaedic instructions risks redislocation, and restricting positions needlessly delays milestones."}, {"id": 1676, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "The clinic manager asks Sione to adjust Ava's harness straps and to teach Linh cast care, because the physiotherapist is fully booked this week. Ava's presentation is changing and the orthopaedic team set the harness instructions. What should the physiotherapist decide about this request?", "options": {"A": "Delegate both after demonstrating each once, since Linh can phone the clinic if any concerns arise", "B": "Delegate strap adjustment only, as it is a repeated mechanical task, and teach cast care personally", "C": "Delegate both tasks as Sione is experienced, then review his notes at week's end", "D": "Retain harness checks and adjustment, delegating only supervised tasks like reinforcing written cast advice"}, "cluster_id": "CHS179", "cluster_text": "Ava Nguyen, 10 weeks old, first baby, born at term after a breech presentation. Her paediatrician referred her to the clinic. Left hip: ultrasound reported a dislocatable hip and the orthopaedic team fitted a Pavlik harness two weeks ago with written wear instructions. Right foot: idiopathic talipes equinovarus (rigid, forefoot adducted, hindfoot in equinus and varus), with weekly Ponseti casting by the orthopaedic team started three weeks ago. Today the harness straps hold her hips in deep flexion. Her left knee is not extending actively and the leg lies limp when Ava is stimulated, while the right leg kicks normally. Skin behind both knees is intact. Her mother, Linh, attends with Ava. The clinic employs an allied health assistant (AHA), Sione, who has worked there for two years and holds a Certificate IV.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Retain harness checks and adjustment, delegating only supervised tasks like reinforcing written cast advice): Delegation depends on the patient's complexity and stability, and an infant with a changing neurological picture needs clinician judgement for harness checks and adjustment. Tasks with clear written instructions and low risk, such as reinforcing cast advice, can be delegated with supervision. Experience and qualifications of the assistant do not remove the physiotherapist's accountability for what is delegated."}, {"id": 1677, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "During the ulnar neurodynamic test, Mr Okafor's tingling eases when cervical side flexion is released and increases when it is added, without any change in arm position. What does this response most strongly suggest about the source of his symptoms?", "options": {"A": "Forearm flexor muscle tightness, as sustained keyboard and headset use loads those tissues", "B": "Cervical facet joint irritation, as side flexion loads the joint and refers tingling distally", "C": "Reduced blood flow to the hand, as arm and neck position alter vascular supply", "D": "Mechanosensitive neural tissue, as movement at a remote joint alters his symptoms"}, "cluster_id": "CHS180", "cluster_text": "David Okafor, 42, a call centre team leader, attends a community health centre with 3 months of tingling along the medial forearm and the ring and little fingers of his left hand. It is worse with prolonged headset and keyboard use and when reaching overhead. He has mild neck ache, no trauma and no reported weakness. Pain is 4 out of 10 and symptoms settle within about 15 minutes of rest. Examination: grip strength symmetrical, radial pulses equal, no skin colour change, cervical rotation full, Spurling test negative. The ulnar nerve neurodynamic test on the left reproduces tingling; ipsilateral cervical side flexion eases it and contralateral side flexion increases it. Tinel at the elbow is negative. The centre is introducing routine outcome measures for upper limb patients.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Mechanosensitive neural tissue, as movement at a remote joint alters his symptoms): Changing the position of a remote joint while the arm stays still alters tension in the nerve without changing local muscle or joint structures, so a symptom change points to mechanosensitive neural tissue. Forearm muscle tightness would not respond to cervical position. A facet joint would not typically produce this ring and little finger pattern, and normal pulses and skin colour make a vascular cause unlikely."}, {"id": 1678, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Okafor's symptoms are mild and settle within about 15 minutes, with no neurological deficit and a normal vascular screen. He wants to keep working at his desk and headset. Which management plan is the most appropriate to begin with?", "options": {"A": "Strengthening of the upper trapezius with loaded shrugs to lift the clavicle off the plexus", "B": "Education, regular position breaks, and gentle neural sliders kept symptom-free", "C": "Deep friction massage to the scalene muscles daily to release the entrapped nerve trunks", "D": "Neural tensioner stretches held at end range daily to increase the length of the nerve"}, "cluster_id": "CHS180", "cluster_text": "David Okafor, 42, a call centre team leader, attends a community health centre with 3 months of tingling along the medial forearm and the ring and little fingers of his left hand. It is worse with prolonged headset and keyboard use and when reaching overhead. He has mild neck ache, no trauma and no reported weakness. Pain is 4 out of 10 and symptoms settle within about 15 minutes of rest. Examination: grip strength symmetrical, radial pulses equal, no skin colour change, cervical rotation full, Spurling test negative. The ulnar nerve neurodynamic test on the left reproduces tingling; ipsilateral cervical side flexion eases it and contralateral side flexion increases it. Tinel at the elbow is negative. The centre is introducing routine outcome measures for upper limb patients.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Education, regular position breaks, and gentle neural sliders kept symptom-free): Mild, easily settling symptoms are managed conservatively by reducing sustained provocative postures and using gentle sliders that move the nerve without stretching it. End-range tensioners raise nerve strain and may aggravate it. Deep friction and loaded shrugs are not supported as a way to decompress the nerve and may add irritation."}, {"id": 1679, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "After his first session of neural sliders, Mr Okafor reports tingling that lingered for two hours and was worse that evening than before treatment. He is keen and willing to continue the programme. How should the physiotherapist adjust the neural mobilisation?", "options": {"A": "Reduce range and repetitions, staying short of symptom onset, as lingering aggravation shows irritability", "B": "Continue the same programme, as a lingering response shows the nerve is adapting to load", "C": "Stop neural mobilisation and change to passive cervical manipulation to settle the nerve irritation", "D": "Switch from sliders to end-range tensioners to produce a stronger therapeutic stretch on the nerve"}, "cluster_id": "CHS180", "cluster_text": "David Okafor, 42, a call centre team leader, attends a community health centre with 3 months of tingling along the medial forearm and the ring and little fingers of his left hand. It is worse with prolonged headset and keyboard use and when reaching overhead. He has mild neck ache, no trauma and no reported weakness. Pain is 4 out of 10 and symptoms settle within about 15 minutes of rest. Examination: grip strength symmetrical, radial pulses equal, no skin colour change, cervical rotation full, Spurling test negative. The ulnar nerve neurodynamic test on the left reproduces tingling; ipsilateral cervical side flexion eases it and contralateral side flexion increases it. Tinel at the elbow is negative. The centre is introducing routine outcome measures for upper limb patients.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reduce range and repetitions, staying short of symptom onset, as lingering aggravation shows irritability): Symptoms that last for hours indicate an irritable nerve, so the dose is reduced and kept below symptom provocation. Continuing unchanged or increasing tension would further aggravate it. Passive manipulation does not address the neural mechanosensitivity and abandons a suitable treatment before dosage is corrected."}, {"id": 1680, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "The community centre wants to audit upper limb outcomes. The physiotherapist proposes using the QuickDASH, a patient-reported upper limb measure, with Mr Okafor and similar patients. Which data collection approach best allows the service to judge whether treatment helps patients?", "options": {"A": "Collect scores from patients who finish treatment so groups remain comparable across the service", "B": "Record scores at discharge only, because baseline data burden patients and add little to the audit", "C": "Report only the number of attendances per patient, as it is easier to collect reliably", "D": "Record scores at baseline and discharge, comparing change with measurement error"}, "cluster_id": "CHS180", "cluster_text": "David Okafor, 42, a call centre team leader, attends a community health centre with 3 months of tingling along the medial forearm and the ring and little fingers of his left hand. It is worse with prolonged headset and keyboard use and when reaching overhead. He has mild neck ache, no trauma and no reported weakness. Pain is 4 out of 10 and symptoms settle within about 15 minutes of rest. Examination: grip strength symmetrical, radial pulses equal, no skin colour change, cervical rotation full, Spurling test negative. The ulnar nerve neurodynamic test on the left reproduces tingling; ipsilateral cervical side flexion eases it and contralateral side flexion increases it. Tinel at the elbow is negative. The centre is introducing routine outcome measures for upper limb patients.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Record scores at baseline and discharge, comparing change with measurement error): Change can only be judged by comparing a baseline score with later scores, and interpreting it against the measure's error shows whether the change is real. A discharge score alone has no reference point. Attendance counts are not an outcome, and excluding patients who drop out biases the audit toward success."}, {"id": 1681, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Maureen's right hip passive flexion and internal rotation are full and pain free, while single leg stance reproduces her lateral pain and the trochanter is tender. Which interpretation of these findings is most appropriate for guiding the first treatment phase?", "options": {"A": "Iliotibial band tightness, so prioritise foam rolling and stretching across the lateral thigh each day", "B": "Gluteal tendinopathy, so first reduce compressive load on the tendon before progressing strengthening", "C": "Hip joint osteoarthritis, so start with joint mobilisation and stretching into internal rotation and flexion", "D": "Lumbar referred pain, so begin with lumbar mobilisation and a graded extension exercise programme first"}, "cluster_id": "CHS181", "cluster_text": "Maureen Kowalski, 71, retired school cleaner, is referred by her GP to a community health centre for right lateral hip pain of four months. Pain is 6/10, worst when lying on the right side at night and climbing stairs. History: mild knee osteoarthritis, paracetamol as needed, no falls, no weight loss, no pain when lying on her back. Examination: single leg stance on the right reproduces lateral hip pain within 20 seconds with a contralateral pelvic drop. The greater trochanter is markedly tender to palpation. Resisted hip abduction is 4/5 and painful. Passive hip flexion and internal rotation are full and pain free. Lumbar screen is clear. She sits in low lounge chairs with her legs crossed. A district nurse visits twice weekly for leg ulcer dressings, and the GP has asked for an update in two weeks.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Gluteal tendinopathy, so first reduce compressive load on the tendon before progressing strengthening): Full, pain free passive hip range with tenderness over the trochanter and pain on single leg stance points to the gluteal tendons rather than the joint. These tendons are irritated by compression, so early management limits load across the lateral hip before building capacity. Joint mobilisation would target a tissue that is not the source here."}, {"id": 1682, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "The district nurse mentions that Maureen's pain wakes her and that she is anxious about falling. The GP has requested an update in two weeks. What is the most effective way for the physiotherapist to share relevant findings and the treatment plan with the team?", "options": {"A": "With consent, send the team a short note on diagnosis, load advice and falls", "B": "Send the GP a detailed letter covering all assessment findings, timed for the two week review", "C": "Update the clinic record in full and phone the GP only if something changes", "D": "Give Maureen a printed summary of the plan to show her nurse and GP herself"}, "cluster_id": "CHS181", "cluster_text": "Maureen Kowalski, 71, retired school cleaner, is referred by her GP to a community health centre for right lateral hip pain of four months. Pain is 6/10, worst when lying on the right side at night and climbing stairs. History: mild knee osteoarthritis, paracetamol as needed, no falls, no weight loss, no pain when lying on her back. Examination: single leg stance on the right reproduces lateral hip pain within 20 seconds with a contralateral pelvic drop. The greater trochanter is markedly tender to palpation. Resisted hip abduction is 4/5 and painful. Passive hip flexion and internal rotation are full and pain free. Lumbar screen is clear. She sits in low lounge chairs with her legs crossed. A district nurse visits twice weekly for leg ulcer dressings, and the GP has asked for an update in two weeks.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (With consent, send the team a short note on diagnosis, load advice and falls): A short, consented note sent now reaches both the nurse and the GP with the diagnosis, the load advice others can reinforce and the falls concern. A detailed letter timed for the review leaves the nurse uninformed in the meantime. Relying on the clinic record or on Maureen to carry a summary leaves communication to chance and delays action on her night pain and anxiety about falling."}, {"id": 1683, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Maureen wants to start exercising for her hip and asks what to do in the first weeks. Her pain is 6 out of 10 at night and single leg stance provokes symptoms. Which graded loading programme is most appropriate to begin with?", "options": {"A": "Side lying hip abduction through full range with ankle weights, adding repetitions every week at home", "B": "Deep bodyweight squats with a band around the knees, performed to full depth in daily sets", "C": "Submaximal isometric abduction in neutral pelvis position, progressing slowly to weight-bearing work", "D": "Static gluteal stretching with the knee pulled across the body, repeated several times through each day"}, "cluster_id": "CHS181", "cluster_text": "Maureen Kowalski, 71, retired school cleaner, is referred by her GP to a community health centre for right lateral hip pain of four months. Pain is 6/10, worst when lying on the right side at night and climbing stairs. History: mild knee osteoarthritis, paracetamol as needed, no falls, no weight loss, no pain when lying on her back. Examination: single leg stance on the right reproduces lateral hip pain within 20 seconds with a contralateral pelvic drop. The greater trochanter is markedly tender to palpation. Resisted hip abduction is 4/5 and painful. Passive hip flexion and internal rotation are full and pain free. Lumbar screen is clear. She sits in low lounge chairs with her legs crossed. A district nurse visits twice weekly for leg ulcer dressings, and the GP has asked for an update in two weeks.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Submaximal isometric abduction in neutral pelvis position, progressing slowly to weight-bearing work): An irritable tendon tolerates low-level isometric contraction in a position that avoids compression, and load is then raised in steps. Stretching across the body and deep hip flexion increase compression of the tendon, and early weighted range work is too demanding for an irritable presentation."}, {"id": 1684, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Maureen sleeps on her right side and sits with her legs crossed in low lounge chairs. Night pain is her main complaint. She asks which everyday changes could reduce her lateral hip pain. Which advice is most consistent with managing compression of the gluteal tendons?", "options": {"A": "Lie on the left with a pillow between the knees, and avoid crossing the legs", "B": "Lie on the left with the top knee unsupported, and avoid crossing the legs when sitting", "C": "Lie on the left with a pillow between the knees, and sit in low soft chairs", "D": "Sleep on the sore side with a towel under the waist, and avoid crossing the legs"}, "cluster_id": "CHS181", "cluster_text": "Maureen Kowalski, 71, retired school cleaner, is referred by her GP to a community health centre for right lateral hip pain of four months. Pain is 6/10, worst when lying on the right side at night and climbing stairs. History: mild knee osteoarthritis, paracetamol as needed, no falls, no weight loss, no pain when lying on her back. Examination: single leg stance on the right reproduces lateral hip pain within 20 seconds with a contralateral pelvic drop. The greater trochanter is markedly tender to palpation. Resisted hip abduction is 4/5 and painful. Passive hip flexion and internal rotation are full and pain free. Lumbar screen is clear. She sits in low lounge chairs with her legs crossed. A district nurse visits twice weekly for leg ulcer dressings, and the GP has asked for an update in two weeks.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Lie on the left with a pillow between the knees, and avoid crossing the legs): Gluteal tendon pain is aggravated by compression against the trochanter, which happens when the top leg drops into adduction or the legs are crossed. Lying on the left with a pillow between the knees and not crossing the legs reduces that compression. Leaving the top knee unsupported lets the leg fall across the body, a low soft chair keeps the hip flexed and adducted, and lying on the sore side keeps the tendon loaded."}, {"id": 1685, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Jarrah has preserved sacral sensation and voluntary anal contraction, with a T10 neurological level. Fewer than half of the key muscles below the level reach grade 3. Motor function is preserved below the level. Which ASIA impairment scale grade does this best fit?", "options": {"A": "Grade B, sensory incomplete, with sacral sensation but no voluntary motor function below the level", "B": "Grade C, a motor incomplete injury with most key muscles below the level under grade 3", "C": "Grade A, a complete injury, because the T10 level remains identified by the pattern of loss", "D": "Grade D, with half or more key muscles below the level at grade 3 or above"}, "cluster_id": "CHS182", "cluster_text": "Jarrah Mitchell, 24, Wiradjuri man and apprentice electrician, is 10 weeks after a motorbike crash causing a T10 spinal cord injury, stabilised surgically, and is in a spinal rehabilitation unit. Sacral sensation and voluntary anal contraction are present. Strength: hip flexors 3/5 left and 2/5 right, knee extensors 2/5 left and 1/5 right, ankle muscles 0 to 1/5. Sitting balance is good and he transfers with a sliding board. In the parallel bars he walks with bilateral knee-ankle-foot orthoses and moderate assistance. BP 110/70 with no orthostatic symptoms. His mother Denise visits daily. His apprenticeship supervisor has phoned the ward asking for details of his progress and return date. Jarrah is an adult with capacity and has given no instruction about sharing information with his employer.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Grade C, a motor incomplete injury with most key muscles below the level under grade 3): Sacral sparing means the injury is incomplete, and voluntary motor function below the level with most key muscles under grade 3 defines grade C. Grade D needs at least half of those muscles at grade 3 or better, and grade B has no motor function below the level."}, {"id": 1686, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "The apprenticeship supervisor phones the ward asking for details of Jarrah's diagnosis, prognosis and return date. Jarrah, who is an adult with capacity, has given no instruction about sharing information with his employer. How should the physiotherapist handle the supervisor's request for information?", "options": {"A": "Confirm his diagnosis but not the prognosis, and refer the caller to his mother", "B": "Share no health information, take the caller's details and pass the request to Jarrah", "C": "Give only general information about his progress, since employers have a legitimate interest in his return", "D": "Transfer the call to the rehabilitation physician, who may release details if workplace safety is involved"}, "cluster_id": "CHS182", "cluster_text": "Jarrah Mitchell, 24, Wiradjuri man and apprentice electrician, is 10 weeks after a motorbike crash causing a T10 spinal cord injury, stabilised surgically, and is in a spinal rehabilitation unit. Sacral sensation and voluntary anal contraction are present. Strength: hip flexors 3/5 left and 2/5 right, knee extensors 2/5 left and 1/5 right, ankle muscles 0 to 1/5. Sitting balance is good and he transfers with a sliding board. In the parallel bars he walks with bilateral knee-ankle-foot orthoses and moderate assistance. BP 110/70 with no orthostatic symptoms. His mother Denise visits daily. His apprenticeship supervisor has phoned the ward asking for details of his progress and return date. Jarrah is an adult with capacity and has given no instruction about sharing information with his employer.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Share no health information, take the caller's details and pass the request to Jarrah): Health information belongs to the patient, and without his consent or a legal requirement it is not released to an employer, even in general terms. Passing on the request lets Jarrah decide. His mother has no automatic right to disclose for him, and a workplace interest does not override his privacy."}, {"id": 1687, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "In the parallel bars Jarrah walks with bilateral knee-ankle-foot orthoses and needs moderate assistance. His right hip flexors are 2/5 and his trunk control is good. Which gait training approach is most appropriate to progress his walking safely at this stage?", "options": {"A": "Repeated stepping practice, such as body weight supported treadmill walking, with graded assistance", "B": "Passive standing in a frame for long periods to build tolerance, before attempting any stepping", "C": "Teach swing-through gait on elbow crutches with locked orthoses now, so he reaches community mobility sooner", "D": "Strengthen the right hip flexors in isolation and delay gait practice until they reach grade 3"}, "cluster_id": "CHS182", "cluster_text": "Jarrah Mitchell, 24, Wiradjuri man and apprentice electrician, is 10 weeks after a motorbike crash causing a T10 spinal cord injury, stabilised surgically, and is in a spinal rehabilitation unit. Sacral sensation and voluntary anal contraction are present. Strength: hip flexors 3/5 left and 2/5 right, knee extensors 2/5 left and 1/5 right, ankle muscles 0 to 1/5. Sitting balance is good and he transfers with a sliding board. In the parallel bars he walks with bilateral knee-ankle-foot orthoses and moderate assistance. BP 110/70 with no orthostatic symptoms. His mother Denise visits daily. His apprenticeship supervisor has phoned the ward asking for details of his progress and return date. Jarrah is an adult with capacity and has given no instruction about sharing information with his employer.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Repeated stepping practice, such as body weight supported treadmill walking, with graded assistance): Intensive task-specific stepping practice with partial support drives recovery in motor incomplete injury and can start before strength is fully restored. Standing alone does not train stepping, crutch gait is premature at moderate assistance, and delaying gait practice wastes the window of neural recovery."}, {"id": 1688, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "After a long session in his wheelchair, Jarrah has a red patch over the right ischial area that stays red when pressed. The skin is intact and warm. His buttock sensation is reduced. What is the most appropriate action for the physiotherapist now?", "options": {"A": "Apply a protective dressing over the area and continue the session on a pressure cushion", "B": "Reposition him on a pressure cushion, record the finding, and reassess at the next skin check", "C": "Elevate his legs and tilt the wheelchair back to redistribute pressure, then continue the planned session", "D": "Remove pressure from the area, inform the nursing team, and recheck the skin after rest"}, "cluster_id": "CHS182", "cluster_text": "Jarrah Mitchell, 24, Wiradjuri man and apprentice electrician, is 10 weeks after a motorbike crash causing a T10 spinal cord injury, stabilised surgically, and is in a spinal rehabilitation unit. Sacral sensation and voluntary anal contraction are present. Strength: hip flexors 3/5 left and 2/5 right, knee extensors 2/5 left and 1/5 right, ankle muscles 0 to 1/5. Sitting balance is good and he transfers with a sliding board. In the parallel bars he walks with bilateral knee-ankle-foot orthoses and moderate assistance. BP 110/70 with no orthostatic symptoms. His mother Denise visits daily. His apprenticeship supervisor has phoned the ward asking for details of his progress and return date. Jarrah is an adult with capacity and has given no instruction about sharing information with his employer.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Remove pressure from the area, inform the nursing team, and recheck the skin after rest): Non blanching redness over an area with reduced sensation is an early pressure injury, so the pressure must be taken off straight away and the nursing team informed so skin care and positioning can be organised. Dressing the area while he stays seated keeps loading it. A cushion and a delayed recheck, or tilting briefly before carrying on, do not give the tissue the sustained relief it needs."}, {"id": 1689, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "The ward is piloting a quality improvement project to track function in patients with functional disorders. You must choose measures for Mei-Ling that will show meaningful change across her admission. Which choice of measures is most appropriate for tracking both her progress and the project?", "options": {"A": "Record a single Barthel Index at discharge, since that score summarises her overall functional recovery", "B": "Use a daily diary of symptom severity, as patient reported change best reflects functional disorders", "C": "Test leg strength and reflexes each week, as these show impairment change over the admission", "D": "Repeat validated functional measures, such as walking distance and Barthel Index, at set intervals"}, "cluster_id": "CHS183", "cluster_text": "Mei-Ling Tran, 32, a primary school teacher, was admitted three days ago with sudden left leg weakness and a coarse tremor of the right hand that began after a viral illness and a period of high work stress. Brain and spine MRI were normal. The neurologist has diagnosed functional neurological disorder (functional limb weakness) based on positive clinical signs, including a positive Hoover sign and a tremor that reduces when she is distracted. She needs assistance from one person to walk 10 metres, with a dragging left foot. Sensation, reflexes and tone are normal. She feels anxious and fears a serious undiagnosed disease, and she asks, 'So is it all in my head?' The ward is piloting a quality improvement project that tracks function in patients with functional disorders. Baseline: she cannot complete the Timed Up and Go without assistance, and her Barthel Index is 80 out of 100.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Repeat validated functional measures, such as walking distance and Barthel Index, at set intervals): Both her care and a quality improvement project need valid, reliable functional measures repeated at agreed intervals, so progress can be compared over time. A single discharge score gives no change data. A symptom diary reflects perception rather than function, and strength and reflexes are already normal, so they cannot show the improvement in walking and independence that matters here."}, {"id": 1690, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "At the bedside, the neurologist documented a positive Hoover sign in Mei-Ling's left leg. A physiotherapy student on placement asks what this sign demonstrates and why it supports a functional diagnosis rather than structural weakness. What is the correct explanation of the finding?", "options": {"A": "Weak hip flexion that worsens during resisted extension of the opposite hip, suggesting a root lesion", "B": "Weakness in ankle dorsiflexion that varies between testing positions, showing inconsistent effort rather than disease", "C": "Voluntary hip extension is weak, but involuntary extension appears during resisted flexion of the opposite hip", "D": "A tremor that changes to match a rhythm tapped by the examiner, confirming a functional disorder"}, "cluster_id": "CHS183", "cluster_text": "Mei-Ling Tran, 32, a primary school teacher, was admitted three days ago with sudden left leg weakness and a coarse tremor of the right hand that began after a viral illness and a period of high work stress. Brain and spine MRI were normal. The neurologist has diagnosed functional neurological disorder (functional limb weakness) based on positive clinical signs, including a positive Hoover sign and a tremor that reduces when she is distracted. She needs assistance from one person to walk 10 metres, with a dragging left foot. Sensation, reflexes and tone are normal. She feels anxious and fears a serious undiagnosed disease, and she asks, 'So is it all in my head?' The ward is piloting a quality improvement project that tracks function in patients with functional disorders. Baseline: she cannot complete the Timed Up and Go without assistance, and her Barthel Index is 80 out of 100.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Voluntary hip extension is weak, but involuntary extension appears during resisted flexion of the opposite hip): Hoover sign is a positive clinical sign: voluntary hip extension of the affected leg is weak, but extension strength returns automatically when the opposite hip is flexed against resistance. This shows the motor pathway is intact and the weakness is functional, not due to structural damage. Interpreting inconsistency as poor effort is wrong, because functional disorders are genuine and not deliberately produced."}, {"id": 1691, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mei-Ling asks whether to rest her leg until strength returns, saying she is afraid of making it worse. She can stand with support for one minute. What is the most appropriate approach to her physiotherapy treatment at this stage of her admission?", "options": {"A": "Isolated strengthening of the left leg with resistance, progressed weekly until normal muscle power returns", "B": "Graded, task-focused practice of walking with attention on the goal rather than the leg itself", "C": "Wheelchair mobility training and a gait aid prescription, deferring walking until her anxiety has settled", "D": "Bed rest with passive movement to protect the leg until the neurologist reviews her"}, "cluster_id": "CHS183", "cluster_text": "Mei-Ling Tran, 32, a primary school teacher, was admitted three days ago with sudden left leg weakness and a coarse tremor of the right hand that began after a viral illness and a period of high work stress. Brain and spine MRI were normal. The neurologist has diagnosed functional neurological disorder (functional limb weakness) based on positive clinical signs, including a positive Hoover sign and a tremor that reduces when she is distracted. She needs assistance from one person to walk 10 metres, with a dragging left foot. Sensation, reflexes and tone are normal. She feels anxious and fears a serious undiagnosed disease, and she asks, 'So is it all in my head?' The ward is piloting a quality improvement project that tracks function in patients with functional disorders. Baseline: she cannot complete the Timed Up and Go without assistance, and her Barthel Index is 80 out of 100.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Graded, task-focused practice of walking with attention on the goal rather than the leg itself): Current practice for functional movement disorders uses graded, goal-directed movement practice with attention diverted from the affected limb, because automatic movement is more intact than consciously controlled movement. Isolated strengthening encourages self-focus on the weak leg, while rest and wheelchair-only plans reinforce immobility and disability. Education and repeated success build confidence."}, {"id": 1692, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mei-Ling asks you directly, 'Is this all in my head? Are you saying I am making it up?' She appears distressed and anxious. Which response best supports her understanding of the diagnosis and her engagement with therapy at this point in the conversation?", "options": {"A": "Your scans are normal, so nothing is damaged and the symptoms should settle on their own", "B": "Your symptoms are real but their cause is unknown, so no treatment can be promised yet", "C": "Stress and anxiety are the cause, so a psychologist should address that before physiotherapy can help", "D": "The symptoms are real and not made up; the brain's signalling is disrupted and can retrain"}, "cluster_id": "CHS183", "cluster_text": "Mei-Ling Tran, 32, a primary school teacher, was admitted three days ago with sudden left leg weakness and a coarse tremor of the right hand that began after a viral illness and a period of high work stress. Brain and spine MRI were normal. The neurologist has diagnosed functional neurological disorder (functional limb weakness) based on positive clinical signs, including a positive Hoover sign and a tremor that reduces when she is distracted. She needs assistance from one person to walk 10 metres, with a dragging left foot. Sensation, reflexes and tone are normal. She feels anxious and fears a serious undiagnosed disease, and she asks, 'So is it all in my head?' The ward is piloting a quality improvement project that tracks function in patients with functional disorders. Baseline: she cannot complete the Timed Up and Go without assistance, and her Barthel Index is 80 out of 100.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The symptoms are real and not made up; the brain's signalling is disrupted and can retrain): A clear, positive explanation that the symptoms are real, involuntary and due to a problem with how the nervous system functions, and that they can improve, supports acceptance and engagement. Telling her nothing is wrong or that stress is the cause can feel dismissive and imply blame, and an unclear message leaves her fearful of serious disease. Physiotherapists reinforce the diagnosis given by the medical team."}, {"id": 1693, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Terry has copious thick secretions and right lower lobe consolidation, but his intracranial pressure readings are borderline. The physiotherapist plans positioning to assist drainage of the affected lobe during treatment, with nursing staff present. Which approach is most appropriate for this session?", "options": {"A": "Left side lying with head elevated, monitoring intracranial pressure and stopping if readings rise", "B": "Head down tilt with right side uppermost to maximise gravity assisted drainage of the lower lobe", "C": "Prone positioning for several hours to recruit dependent lung regions, with head turned to the right", "D": "Supine with the head flat and manual hyperinflation first to loosen secretions before other treatment"}, "cluster_id": "CHS184", "cluster_text": "Terry Walsh, 54, scaffolder, fell from a ladder at work eight days ago with a traumatic brain injury and rib fractures. He is intubated and ventilated in the ICU on SIMV, FiO2 0.45, PEEP 8 cmH2O. Sedation has been lightened, and he opens his eyes to voice but does not follow commands. Intracranial pressure monitoring is in place with readings currently borderline. Temperature 38.4, HR 104, BP 128/74, SpO2 93 percent. Vomiting was witnessed on day six. Chest X-ray shows right lower lobe consolidation. Auscultation: coarse crackles and bronchial breathing at the right base, with copious thick yellow secretions on suction. The diagnosis is aspiration pneumonia. A workers compensation claim has been lodged. His wife, Lisa, is at the bedside and asks about future rehabilitation costs.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Left side lying with head elevated, monitoring intracranial pressure and stopping if readings rise): Draining the right lower lobe means placing the right side uppermost, which is left side lying, but head down tilt is not suitable when intracranial pressure is borderline, so the head stays elevated and pressure is monitored throughout. Flat positioning and prolonged prone positioning both risk raising intracranial pressure and do not target the affected lobe safely."}, {"id": 1694, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "The team reviews ventilator associated and aspiration pneumonia prevention for Terry in the ICU. His wife asks how the risk of a repeat aspiration event can be reduced while he is intubated. Which measure is best supported as part of the prevention bundle?", "options": {"A": "Semi-recumbent positioning where not contraindicated, regular oral care, and monitoring of endotracheal cuff pressure", "B": "Head of bed elevation, with routine cuff deflation each hour to rest the airway mucosa", "C": "Prophylactic antibiotics given routinely, with regular oral care and head of bed elevation", "D": "Saline instillation before suction, closed suction systems, and turning every four hours to loosen secretions"}, "cluster_id": "CHS184", "cluster_text": "Terry Walsh, 54, scaffolder, fell from a ladder at work eight days ago with a traumatic brain injury and rib fractures. He is intubated and ventilated in the ICU on SIMV, FiO2 0.45, PEEP 8 cmH2O. Sedation has been lightened, and he opens his eyes to voice but does not follow commands. Intracranial pressure monitoring is in place with readings currently borderline. Temperature 38.4, HR 104, BP 128/74, SpO2 93 percent. Vomiting was witnessed on day six. Chest X-ray shows right lower lobe consolidation. Auscultation: coarse crackles and bronchial breathing at the right base, with copious thick yellow secretions on suction. The diagnosis is aspiration pneumonia. A workers compensation claim has been lodged. His wife, Lisa, is at the bedside and asks about future rehabilitation costs.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Semi-recumbent positioning where not contraindicated, regular oral care, and monitoring of endotracheal cuff pressure): A supported prevention bundle combines head of bed elevation where safe, regular oral care and monitoring of cuff pressure so secretions do not leak past the cuff. Deflating the cuff each hour allows secretions to enter the lungs. Routine prophylactic antibiotics are not part of the bundle, and saline instillation is not supported even though closed suction is sensible."}, {"id": 1695, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Terry needs endotracheal suction through a closed circuit for secretions causing desaturation to 90 percent. His intracranial pressure is borderline and coughing raises it. The physiotherapist has checked the equipment and the circuit is secure. Which suction technique is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Instil saline before each pass, advance with suction applied, and continue until secretions clear", "B": "Use a large bore catheter to remove thick secretions quickly, applying suction during insertion and withdrawal", "C": "Disconnect the closed circuit to suction with a fresh catheter, taking longer passes for thick secretions", "D": "Pre-oxygenate, use a catheter less than half the tube diameter, and apply brief suction on withdrawal"}, "cluster_id": "CHS184", "cluster_text": "Terry Walsh, 54, scaffolder, fell from a ladder at work eight days ago with a traumatic brain injury and rib fractures. He is intubated and ventilated in the ICU on SIMV, FiO2 0.45, PEEP 8 cmH2O. Sedation has been lightened, and he opens his eyes to voice but does not follow commands. Intracranial pressure monitoring is in place with readings currently borderline. Temperature 38.4, HR 104, BP 128/74, SpO2 93 percent. Vomiting was witnessed on day six. Chest X-ray shows right lower lobe consolidation. Auscultation: coarse crackles and bronchial breathing at the right base, with copious thick yellow secretions on suction. The diagnosis is aspiration pneumonia. A workers compensation claim has been lodged. His wife, Lisa, is at the bedside and asks about future rehabilitation costs.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Pre-oxygenate, use a catheter less than half the tube diameter, and apply brief suction on withdrawal): Pre-oxygenation, a catheter small enough to avoid occluding the airway, and short passes with suction applied only on withdrawal limit hypoxia, mucosal trauma and the rise in intracranial pressure from coughing. Routine saline and suction during insertion are discouraged. A large catheter or a disconnected circuit with longer passes worsens desaturation and loss of PEEP."}, {"id": 1696, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Lisa says a claims manager told her all recommended rehabilitation will be covered automatically and asks whether Medicare or the NDIS would fund care if the insurer declines. Terry has not yet recovered enough for eligibility to be assessed. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Suggest applying to the NDIS now, since a brain injury usually leads to eligibility", "B": "Explain the insurer decides funding for a compensable injury, and offer clinical reports supporting requested services", "C": "Confirm rehabilitation is covered, since an accepted claim means every recommended service is funded automatically", "D": "Advise that Medicare would fund his rehabilitation if the insurer declines, so funding is secure"}, "cluster_id": "CHS184", "cluster_text": "Terry Walsh, 54, scaffolder, fell from a ladder at work eight days ago with a traumatic brain injury and rib fractures. He is intubated and ventilated in the ICU on SIMV, FiO2 0.45, PEEP 8 cmH2O. Sedation has been lightened, and he opens his eyes to voice but does not follow commands. Intracranial pressure monitoring is in place with readings currently borderline. Temperature 38.4, HR 104, BP 128/74, SpO2 93 percent. Vomiting was witnessed on day six. Chest X-ray shows right lower lobe consolidation. Auscultation: coarse crackles and bronchial breathing at the right base, with copious thick yellow secretions on suction. The diagnosis is aspiration pneumonia. A workers compensation claim has been lodged. His wife, Lisa, is at the bedside and asks about future rehabilitation costs.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Explain the insurer decides funding for a compensable injury, and offer clinical reports supporting requested services): Funding for a work injury is determined through the compensation scheme, so the physiotherapist should be honest about that process and support it with clear clinical reports rather than promising outcomes. Services are not approved simply because they are recommended. Medicare generally does not fund treatment for compensable injuries, and NDIS eligibility depends on permanent disability that cannot yet be judged."}, {"id": 1697, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Demetriou, who has motor neurone disease, has morning headaches, daytime sleepiness, breathlessness lying flat and paradoxical abdominal movement when supine, yet his SpO2 is 95 percent on room air. His chest sounds clear. What do these findings most strongly suggest?", "options": {"A": "Heart failure with pulmonary congestion, which commonly produces breathlessness lying flat and morning tiredness", "B": "Obstructive sleep apnoea from upper airway collapse, which also causes morning headaches and daytime sleepiness", "C": "Bulbar weakness with silent aspiration, which can produce breathlessness before changes on auscultation appear", "D": "Diaphragm weakness causing nocturnal hypoventilation, which normal daytime oxygen saturation can easily mask"}, "cluster_id": "CHS185", "cluster_text": "Christos Demetriou, 69, retired taxi driver, has had limb-onset motor neurone disease for 20 months. He lives with his daughter Sofia in a regional town and is under the community palliative care team. Over six weeks he has become breathless lying flat, wakes with morning headaches and dozes through the day. His speech is soft and his cough is weak. Observations: RR 22, HR 92, SpO2 95 percent on room air, chest clear to auscultation. Lower chest expansion is reduced, and his abdomen moves inwards on inspiration when he lies supine. He is alert, cooperative and follows commands. His advance care directive declines intubation. Non-invasive ventilation has been discussed but not started. The palliative physician can be reached by phone through the service, while the nearest respiratory physician is 200 km away.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Diaphragm weakness causing nocturnal hypoventilation, which normal daytime oxygen saturation can easily mask): Inward abdominal movement on inspiration when supine, breathlessness lying flat and morning headaches indicate diaphragm weakness with carbon dioxide retention overnight, which is common in motor neurone disease, and daytime saturation can stay normal. Heart failure and sleep apnoea explain some symptoms but not the paradoxical abdominal movement. Bulbar weakness could cause aspiration, but a clear chest and the supine abdominal sign fit diaphragm weakness better."}, {"id": 1698, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Demetriou has a weak, ineffective cough and cannot clear secretions during a chest infection. He is alert, cooperative and has no rib fractures or recent abdominal surgery. His daughter Sofia is willing to help. Which airway clearance technique should the physiotherapist teach her first?", "options": {"A": "Teach unassisted huffing at low lung volumes, because deep breaths risk fatiguing his weak inspiratory muscles", "B": "Manually assisted cough, with an abdominal thrust timed to his expiratory effort after a deep breath", "C": "Percussion and vibration over the lower chest with head down tipping, for ten minutes each session", "D": "Active cycle of breathing technique with forced expiration, repeated until secretions reach the upper airway"}, "cluster_id": "CHS185", "cluster_text": "Christos Demetriou, 69, retired taxi driver, has had limb-onset motor neurone disease for 20 months. He lives with his daughter Sofia in a regional town and is under the community palliative care team. Over six weeks he has become breathless lying flat, wakes with morning headaches and dozes through the day. His speech is soft and his cough is weak. Observations: RR 22, HR 92, SpO2 95 percent on room air, chest clear to auscultation. Lower chest expansion is reduced, and his abdomen moves inwards on inspiration when he lies supine. He is alert, cooperative and follows commands. His advance care directive declines intubation. Non-invasive ventilation has been discussed but not started. The palliative physician can be reached by phone through the service, while the nearest respiratory physician is 200 km away.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Manually assisted cough, with an abdominal thrust timed to his expiratory effort after a deep breath): A manually assisted cough supplements his weak expiratory muscles and is a practical technique a carer can learn. Active cycle of breathing relies on his own muscle strength, and head down tipping would worsen his orthopnoea and breathlessness."}, {"id": 1699, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Sofia says her father is more breathless and asks the physiotherapist to increase his morphine dose. She also mentions he choked on porridge yesterday. Sofia is distressed and wants action today. The physiotherapist does not prescribe medication. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Book an urgent outpatient appointment with the distant respiratory physician for a ventilation assessment", "B": "Leave the dose to the prescriber, contact the palliative physician today, and request speech pathology review", "C": "Ask Sofia to offer thickened fluids and soft foods herself until his GP appointment next week", "D": "Teach Sofia positioning and breathing control for breathlessness and review swallowing later."}, "cluster_id": "CHS185", "cluster_text": "Christos Demetriou, 69, retired taxi driver, has had limb-onset motor neurone disease for 20 months. He lives with his daughter Sofia in a regional town and is under the community palliative care team. Over six weeks he has become breathless lying flat, wakes with morning headaches and dozes through the day. His speech is soft and his cough is weak. Observations: RR 22, HR 92, SpO2 95 percent on room air, chest clear to auscultation. Lower chest expansion is reduced, and his abdomen moves inwards on inspiration when he lies supine. He is alert, cooperative and follows commands. His advance care directive declines intubation. Non-invasive ventilation has been discussed but not started. The palliative physician can be reached by phone through the service, while the nearest respiratory physician is 200 km away.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Leave the dose to the prescriber, contact the palliative physician today, and request speech pathology review): Changing medication is outside physiotherapy scope, and the choking episode is a new safety concern that needs timely swallowing assessment. Same day phone contact with the palliative physician is the fastest route. Waiting for the next visit delays care, and setting food textures without assessment oversteps scope."}, {"id": 1700, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Demetriou sleeps poorly because lying flat worsens his breathlessness, and he slides down the bed when propped on several pillows. His neck extensors are weak and his chin drops onto his chest. Which positioning plan at night is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Lie him on his side with a pillow between the knees to protect his hips", "B": "Use an upright recliner with the head tilted back to open the airway and aid swallowing", "C": "Position him in prone lying over pillows to let the diaphragm descend and ease his breathing", "D": "Raise the bed head, bend the knees slightly and support the head in neutral"}, "cluster_id": "CHS185", "cluster_text": "Christos Demetriou, 69, retired taxi driver, has had limb-onset motor neurone disease for 20 months. He lives with his daughter Sofia in a regional town and is under the community palliative care team. Over six weeks he has become breathless lying flat, wakes with morning headaches and dozes through the day. His speech is soft and his cough is weak. Observations: RR 22, HR 92, SpO2 95 percent on room air, chest clear to auscultation. Lower chest expansion is reduced, and his abdomen moves inwards on inspiration when he lies supine. He is alert, cooperative and follows commands. His advance care directive declines intubation. Non-invasive ventilation has been discussed but not started. The palliative physician can be reached by phone through the service, while the nearest respiratory physician is 200 km away.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Raise the bed head, bend the knees slightly and support the head in neutral): An elevated bed head lets the abdominal contents drop away from a weak diaphragm, slightly bent knees stop sliding, and a neutral head position keeps the airway open. Tilting the head back raises aspiration risk, prone lying restricts weak chest movement, and side lying alone does not relieve orthopnoea."}, {"id": 1701, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Papadopoulos will use a chair at home that is low and soft after discharge. The physiotherapist teaches him how to sit and stand safely while following the surgeon's posterior precautions. Which setup and technique is most appropriate for him on returning home?", "options": {"A": "Raise the seat with firm cushions, use armrests and extend the operated leg", "B": "Use armrests and firm cushions to raise the seat, keeping both feet tucked back beneath it", "C": "Keep the chair, add a lumbar roll, and use the walker to push up", "D": "Raise the seat with a cushion, slide forward and lean over his knees to rise"}, "cluster_id": "CHS186", "cluster_text": "Daniel Papadopoulos, 71, a retired bus driver, had an elective right total hip replacement (posterior approach, cemented femoral stem) two days ago for severe osteoarthritis. Surgeon's orders: weight bear as tolerated, use posterior hip precautions for six weeks, and discharge when safe. BMI 33. Pain is 4/10 on movement. Active assisted hip flexion is 70 degrees, straight leg raise has a 10 degree lag, the wound is clean and dry, the calves are soft and non-tender, and he is afebrile. He walks 15 metres with a wheeled walking frame and standby assistance. He lives with his wife in a home with two entry steps and a handrail on one side. While preparing for the session, the physiotherapist reads a recent systematic review suggesting restrictive precautions may not reduce dislocation after the posterior approach, though the included studies were small and varied.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Raise the seat with firm cushions, use armrests and extend the operated leg): Posterior precautions limit deep hip flexion and internal rotation, so a higher firm seat, armrests and the operated leg extended keep the hip within a safe range. Tucking the feet back increases flexion at the hip, and a low soft chair with only a lumbar roll still forces deep flexion. Leaning over the knees to rise flexes the hip past the permitted angle."}, {"id": 1702, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "That evening, after rolling onto his side in bed, Mr Papadopoulos hears a pop and has sudden severe hip pain. He cannot lift the leg. Which observation would most strongly indicate prosthetic dislocation after a posterior approach and require urgent medical review?", "options": {"A": "A shortened leg held in adduction and internal rotation, with inability to bear weight", "B": "A lengthened leg held in external rotation, with tenderness over the front of the groin", "C": "Marked wound swelling with redness and discharge, plus a low grade fever on observation", "D": "A warm, swollen calf with tenderness on palpation and a mildly raised resting heart rate"}, "cluster_id": "CHS186", "cluster_text": "Daniel Papadopoulos, 71, a retired bus driver, had an elective right total hip replacement (posterior approach, cemented femoral stem) two days ago for severe osteoarthritis. Surgeon's orders: weight bear as tolerated, use posterior hip precautions for six weeks, and discharge when safe. BMI 33. Pain is 4/10 on movement. Active assisted hip flexion is 70 degrees, straight leg raise has a 10 degree lag, the wound is clean and dry, the calves are soft and non-tender, and he is afebrile. He walks 15 metres with a wheeled walking frame and standby assistance. He lives with his wife in a home with two entry steps and a handrail on one side. While preparing for the session, the physiotherapist reads a recent systematic review suggesting restrictive precautions may not reduce dislocation after the posterior approach, though the included studies were small and varied.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A shortened leg held in adduction and internal rotation, with inability to bear weight): Posterior dislocation typically produces a shortened, adducted and internally rotated limb with sudden pain and loss of function, and it needs urgent medical review and reduction. A tender, swollen calf points to deep vein thrombosis and a red, discharging wound with fever suggests infection, which have a different time course. A lengthened externally rotated limb does not fit a posterior dislocation."}, {"id": 1703, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "The surgeon's orders require six weeks of posterior precautions. The physiotherapist has read a recent systematic review of small, varied studies suggesting restrictions may not reduce dislocation. Mr Papadopoulos asks whether he really needs to follow them. How should the physiotherapist respond?", "options": {"A": "Tell him the precautions are optional and let him decide which ones suit his daily routine", "B": "Keep only the precautions about hip flexion and drop the others, based on the review", "C": "Teach the current precautions and take the review to the surgical team for discussion", "D": "Stop teaching the precautions now, because the review shows restrictions add no benefit to hip stability"}, "cluster_id": "CHS186", "cluster_text": "Daniel Papadopoulos, 71, a retired bus driver, had an elective right total hip replacement (posterior approach, cemented femoral stem) two days ago for severe osteoarthritis. Surgeon's orders: weight bear as tolerated, use posterior hip precautions for six weeks, and discharge when safe. BMI 33. Pain is 4/10 on movement. Active assisted hip flexion is 70 degrees, straight leg raise has a 10 degree lag, the wound is clean and dry, the calves are soft and non-tender, and he is afebrile. He walks 15 metres with a wheeled walking frame and standby assistance. He lives with his wife in a home with two entry steps and a handrail on one side. While preparing for the session, the physiotherapist reads a recent systematic review suggesting restrictive precautions may not reduce dislocation after the posterior approach, though the included studies were small and varied.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Teach the current precautions and take the review to the surgical team for discussion): A single review of small, varied studies is not strong enough to override the treating surgeon's instructions, and changing them alone could expose the patient to harm. The sound approach is to follow current orders and raise the evidence with the surgical team and department so that the protocol can be reviewed properly. Making precautions optional or selecting some of them has no support from the evidence."}, {"id": 1704, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Papadopoulos is ready to practise the entry steps at home with a handrail on one side and a walking stick in the other hand. The right hip is the operated side. Which sequence is safest for going up and then coming down?", "options": {"A": "Up leading with the stick then the non-operated leg; down leading with the non-operated leg", "B": "Up leading with the non-operated leg; down leading with the stick and operated leg", "C": "Up with both feet on each step before the stick; down leading with the operated leg", "D": "Up leading with the operated leg and stick; down leading with the non-operated leg"}, "cluster_id": "CHS186", "cluster_text": "Daniel Papadopoulos, 71, a retired bus driver, had an elective right total hip replacement (posterior approach, cemented femoral stem) two days ago for severe osteoarthritis. Surgeon's orders: weight bear as tolerated, use posterior hip precautions for six weeks, and discharge when safe. BMI 33. Pain is 4/10 on movement. Active assisted hip flexion is 70 degrees, straight leg raise has a 10 degree lag, the wound is clean and dry, the calves are soft and non-tender, and he is afebrile. He walks 15 metres with a wheeled walking frame and standby assistance. He lives with his wife in a home with two entry steps and a handrail on one side. While preparing for the session, the physiotherapist reads a recent systematic review suggesting restrictive precautions may not reduce dislocation after the posterior approach, though the included studies were small and varied.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Up leading with the non-operated leg; down leading with the stick and operated leg): Going up, the stronger non-operated leg lifts the body first and the operated leg and stick follow. Going down, the stick and operated leg lead so the stronger leg can control the lowering. Leading up with the operated leg overloads a weak hip, and leading down with the non-operated leg forces the operated hip to bear the load while descending."}, {"id": 1705, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mai wants to start a strengthening programme for her weak ankles and asks how hard she should train. She also reports aching in her calves the morning after harder sessions, and is otherwise well. Which exercise approach is most appropriate for her condition?", "options": {"A": "High load resistance training for the weak dorsiflexors, progressing weights weekly without scheduled rest days", "B": "Stretching and balance work only, because strengthening speeds overwork weakness in muscles already affected by neuropathy", "C": "Daily intensive strengthening to fatigue, since regular overload helps stimulate regrowth of the damaged peripheral nerves", "D": "Moderate strengthening and aerobic work with rest days, monitoring for soreness lasting beyond a day"}, "cluster_id": "CHS187", "cluster_text": "Mai Ly, 15 years, has genetically confirmed Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease type 1A and plays school netball. Her mother speaks little English and prefers Vietnamese. At earlier visits her 12 year old brother has interpreted. Mai reports repeated lateral ankle sprains and tripping on her toes, and aching calves the morning after harder training sessions. Findings: high arched feet with hammer toes, bilateral foot drop with ankle dorsiflexion 3/5 and eversion 3/5, plantarflexion 4/5, absent ankle reflexes, reduced vibration sense at the toes. Romberg is mildly unsteady with eyes closed. She walks with a steppage gait. She wants to stay in the netball team. Today the physiotherapist will explain the plan and seek consent for an orthotist referral.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Moderate strengthening and aerobic work with rest days, monitoring for soreness lasting beyond a day): In Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease, moderate strengthening and aerobic exercise is beneficial, but excessive loading risks overwork weakness, so rest days and monitoring of next day soreness are needed. Avoiding strengthening entirely is outdated and removes a proven benefit. Heavy daily loading to fatigue does not regrow nerves and may harm weak muscle."}, {"id": 1706, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mai has steppage gait, repeated lateral ankle sprains, 3/5 dorsiflexion and eversion, and pes cavus. She is otherwise well and wants to keep playing. She and her mother want something that reduces tripping and sprains. Which first line option is most appropriate to arrange?", "options": {"A": "Referral to an orthotist for a lightweight ankle foot orthosis, with advice on supportive footwear", "B": "Referral for orthopaedic surgery first, since bracing accelerates weakness in the affected muscles and delays correction", "C": "Cushioned insoles in standard shoes, to absorb impact and correct the high arch before any bracing", "D": "Taping the ankle in dorsiflexion for daily activities, replaced before each session to prevent sprains"}, "cluster_id": "CHS187", "cluster_text": "Mai Ly, 15 years, has genetically confirmed Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease type 1A and plays school netball. Her mother speaks little English and prefers Vietnamese. At earlier visits her 12 year old brother has interpreted. Mai reports repeated lateral ankle sprains and tripping on her toes, and aching calves the morning after harder training sessions. Findings: high arched feet with hammer toes, bilateral foot drop with ankle dorsiflexion 3/5 and eversion 3/5, plantarflexion 4/5, absent ankle reflexes, reduced vibration sense at the toes. Romberg is mildly unsteady with eyes closed. She walks with a steppage gait. She wants to stay in the netball team. Today the physiotherapist will explain the plan and seek consent for an orthotist referral.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Referral to an orthotist for a lightweight ankle foot orthosis, with advice on supportive footwear): Foot drop and instability from dorsiflexor and evertor weakness are managed with a lightweight ankle foot orthosis and appropriate footwear, which improve clearance and stability. Insoles alone do not control the drop. Taping is impractical as a daily solution, and bracing does not worsen weakness, so surgery is not a first step."}, {"id": 1707, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mai asks whether she can keep playing netball this season, which includes jumping and quick changes of direction. Her ankle strength is 3/5 and her balance with eyes closed is mildly impaired. She is keen and well supported by her coach. Which advice is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Limit play to this season and review after her next neurologist appointment before deciding further", "B": "Support continued play with ankle protection, footwear and conditioning, and agree signs for reducing load", "C": "Advise stopping jumping and cutting sports now, to protect her joints and nerves long term", "D": "Allow training to continue as before because exercise is beneficial, reviewing only if an injury occurs"}, "cluster_id": "CHS187", "cluster_text": "Mai Ly, 15 years, has genetically confirmed Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease type 1A and plays school netball. Her mother speaks little English and prefers Vietnamese. At earlier visits her 12 year old brother has interpreted. Mai reports repeated lateral ankle sprains and tripping on her toes, and aching calves the morning after harder training sessions. Findings: high arched feet with hammer toes, bilateral foot drop with ankle dorsiflexion 3/5 and eversion 3/5, plantarflexion 4/5, absent ankle reflexes, reduced vibration sense at the toes. Romberg is mildly unsteady with eyes closed. She walks with a steppage gait. She wants to stay in the netball team. Today the physiotherapist will explain the plan and seek consent for an orthotist referral.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Support continued play with ankle protection, footwear and conditioning, and agree signs for reducing load): Sport participation supports fitness and wellbeing, so the aim is to enable it safely with orthotic or ankle protection, conditioning and agreed signs for backing off. Advising withdrawal removes the benefits without strong evidence of protection. Continuing unchanged ignores her sprain risk and balance deficit."}, {"id": 1708, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Today the physiotherapist must explain the orthotist referral plan and obtain consent, which must be informed and documented. Mai's mother speaks little English and her 12 year old brother is available to interpret as before. What is the most appropriate way to communicate?", "options": {"A": "Give her mother an information sheet translated by a free online tool to read at home", "B": "Use her brother as before, since he knows the family, and confirm understanding by signature", "C": "Book a professional Vietnamese interpreter, in person or by phone, and use teach back", "D": "Ask the bilingual clinic receptionist to interpret, saving time and the cost of an external service"}, "cluster_id": "CHS187", "cluster_text": "Mai Ly, 15 years, has genetically confirmed Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease type 1A and plays school netball. Her mother speaks little English and prefers Vietnamese. At earlier visits her 12 year old brother has interpreted. Mai reports repeated lateral ankle sprains and tripping on her toes, and aching calves the morning after harder training sessions. Findings: high arched feet with hammer toes, bilateral foot drop with ankle dorsiflexion 3/5 and eversion 3/5, plantarflexion 4/5, absent ankle reflexes, reduced vibration sense at the toes. Romberg is mildly unsteady with eyes closed. She walks with a steppage gait. She wants to stay in the netball team. Today the physiotherapist will explain the plan and seek consent for an orthotist referral.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Book a professional Vietnamese interpreter, in person or by phone, and use teach back): Valid consent needs accurate, impartial communication, which a professional interpreter provides, and teach back checks that the mother understood. A child is not an appropriate interpreter for clinical information. Staff without interpreter training and unchecked machine translation both risk errors and unclear consent."}, {"id": 1709, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Nguyen has some active wrist extension, can grasp large objects and is ten weeks post stroke. The physiotherapist is now planning his upper limb program. Which principle best supports recovery of arm function according to current Australian stroke guidance?", "options": {"A": "Strengthening his unaffected arm to compensate, as affected hand recovery is unlikely after ten weeks", "B": "Passive range of motion and sling positioning to prevent contracture while spontaneous recovery occurs", "C": "Therapist handling that facilitates normal movement patterns, with active practice delayed until muscle tone normalises", "D": "Intensive repetitive practice of meaningful tasks, progressed in difficulty as his control improves"}, "cluster_id": "CHS188", "cluster_text": "Mr Hung Nguyen, 71, is a retired mechanic who migrated from Vietnam 30 years ago. He speaks limited English and his daughter has been interpreting. He had a right middle cerebral artery ischaemic stroke ten weeks ago and attends outpatient rehabilitation. History: hypertension and type 2 diabetes. Left upper limb: active shoulder flexion to about 70 degrees, elbow extension incomplete, wrist extension 3/5, finger extension minimal. Modified Ashworth Scale 1+ in the elbow flexors. He can grasp a large cup but cannot reliably release it. Sensation is mildly reduced in the hand. Today he reports a pinching shoulder pain during overhead reaching, and the physiotherapist feels a palpable gap below the acromion with the arm hanging. Pain at rest is 1/10. He wants to use the arm to eat and hold a newspaper again.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Intensive repetitive practice of meaningful tasks, progressed in difficulty as his control improves): Current stroke guidelines support high dose, task specific practice of meaningful activities with progressive challenge, because motor recovery depends on repetition and active use. Passive stretching and slings do not drive motor learning, and prolonged immobilisation risks learned non use. Compensating with the other arm abandons recovery potential he still has at ten weeks."}, {"id": 1710, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Nguyen reaches forward to table height and grasps a large cup but cannot release it reliably because finger extension is minimal. His elbow flexor tone is mildly increased. Which task is most appropriate to practise next in this session?", "options": {"A": "Squeezing therapy putty with high resistance for many repetitions to build grip strength before practising release", "B": "Reaching to grasp and release large light objects onto a target, then reducing object size gradually", "C": "Isolated shoulder shrugs against resistance bands to build proximal strength before any hand tasks are attempted", "D": "Repeated passive finger extension stretches by the physiotherapist, with the arm resting on a supported pillow"}, "cluster_id": "CHS188", "cluster_text": "Mr Hung Nguyen, 71, is a retired mechanic who migrated from Vietnam 30 years ago. He speaks limited English and his daughter has been interpreting. He had a right middle cerebral artery ischaemic stroke ten weeks ago and attends outpatient rehabilitation. History: hypertension and type 2 diabetes. Left upper limb: active shoulder flexion to about 70 degrees, elbow extension incomplete, wrist extension 3/5, finger extension minimal. Modified Ashworth Scale 1+ in the elbow flexors. He can grasp a large cup but cannot reliably release it. Sensation is mildly reduced in the hand. Today he reports a pinching shoulder pain during overhead reaching, and the physiotherapist feels a palpable gap below the acromion with the arm hanging. Pain at rest is 1/10. He wants to use the arm to eat and hold a newspaper again.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reaching to grasp and release large light objects onto a target, then reducing object size gradually): Practising reach, grasp and release of graded objects trains the exact deficit, release, within a functional task, and starting with large light items sets a challenge he can succeed at. Strong gripping tends to increase flexor tone and does not train release, and passive stretching or isolated proximal strengthening transfers poorly to hand function."}, {"id": 1711, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "During overhead reaching Mr Nguyen reports a pinching shoulder pain, and the physiotherapist finds a palpable gap below the acromion with the arm hanging by his side. His shoulder girdle muscle activity is weak. What is the most appropriate way to manage the shoulder during therapy?", "options": {"A": "Pause upper limb practice until the shoulder pain has resolved, then restart overhead reaching", "B": "Support the arm during tasks, work within pain free range, and avoid pulling on the arm", "C": "Continue overhead pulley exercises to maintain shoulder range, as some pinching discomfort is expected during rehabilitation", "D": "Apply firm passive end range stretching to the shoulder to prevent a tight capsule developing"}, "cluster_id": "CHS188", "cluster_text": "Mr Hung Nguyen, 71, is a retired mechanic who migrated from Vietnam 30 years ago. He speaks limited English and his daughter has been interpreting. He had a right middle cerebral artery ischaemic stroke ten weeks ago and attends outpatient rehabilitation. History: hypertension and type 2 diabetes. Left upper limb: active shoulder flexion to about 70 degrees, elbow extension incomplete, wrist extension 3/5, finger extension minimal. Modified Ashworth Scale 1+ in the elbow flexors. He can grasp a large cup but cannot reliably release it. Sensation is mildly reduced in the hand. Today he reports a pinching shoulder pain during overhead reaching, and the physiotherapist feels a palpable gap below the acromion with the arm hanging. Pain at rest is 1/10. He wants to use the arm to eat and hold a newspaper again.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Support the arm during tasks, work within pain free range, and avoid pulling on the arm): A weak shoulder with inferior subluxation is vulnerable to traction and to loading in elevated positions, so the arm is supported and tasks are kept in pain free range while muscle activity returns. Overhead pulleys and forceful stretching can damage the unprotected joint. Stopping practice altogether removes the activity that drives recovery."}, {"id": 1712, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "The physiotherapist must explain the revised upper limb program, the shoulder precautions and obtain Mr Nguyen's agreement. His daughter, who is present and keen to help, has been interpreting at previous sessions. What is the most appropriate way to communicate?", "options": {"A": "Use his daughter to interpret, since she knows his history and he is comfortable with her", "B": "Give a translated written handout of the program and ask him to sign if he understands", "C": "Use short simple English with gestures and demonstration, and repeat points until he nods in agreement", "D": "Use a professional interpreter, speak to him directly, and check understanding with teach back"}, "cluster_id": "CHS188", "cluster_text": "Mr Hung Nguyen, 71, is a retired mechanic who migrated from Vietnam 30 years ago. He speaks limited English and his daughter has been interpreting. He had a right middle cerebral artery ischaemic stroke ten weeks ago and attends outpatient rehabilitation. History: hypertension and type 2 diabetes. Left upper limb: active shoulder flexion to about 70 degrees, elbow extension incomplete, wrist extension 3/5, finger extension minimal. Modified Ashworth Scale 1+ in the elbow flexors. He can grasp a large cup but cannot reliably release it. Sensation is mildly reduced in the hand. Today he reports a pinching shoulder pain during overhead reaching, and the physiotherapist feels a palpable gap below the acromion with the arm hanging. Pain at rest is 1/10. He wants to use the arm to eat and hold a newspaper again.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Use a professional interpreter, speak to him directly, and check understanding with teach back): Professional interpreters are trained to convey information accurately and impartially, and using one protects consent and safety, especially for precautions. Family members may filter, shorten or add information and may alter what the patient says. Teach back confirms real understanding, whereas a signature or a nod does not."}, {"id": 1713, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Meera walks about 400 metres before leg heaviness stops her, fatigue builds through the afternoon, and symptoms worsen in the heat. Strength is 4+/5 at hips and ankles. She asks for an exercise programme she can maintain alongside four days of work. What is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Prescribe continuous high-intensity aerobic training to fatigue, since multiple sclerosis fatigue mainly reflects deconditioning", "B": "Advise rest on high-fatigue days and avoiding afternoon activity, to conserve energy and prevent a relapse", "C": "Prescribe moderate aerobic and resistance exercise in short bouts, in a cool setting, monitoring next-day fatigue", "D": "Prescribe daily warm water pool sessions to ease spasticity before progressing to strengthening exercises on land"}, "cluster_id": "CHS189", "cluster_text": "Meera Menon, 41, a primary school teacher, has had relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis for six years, stable on disease-modifying therapy with no relapse in 18 months. Her EDSS score is 3.0. She walks about 400 metres before her legs feel heavy and she has to stop. Fatigue builds through the afternoon, and her symptoms worsen after a hot shower or in hot weather. Strength is 4+/5 at both hip flexors and ankle dorsiflexors, with mild plantarflexor spasticity (modified Ashworth 1). HR 72, BP 118/74 mmHg. She works four days a week and wants to keep her job. She has used all the Medicare-subsidised allied health visits available under her GP management plan this calendar year, is not an NDIS participant, is under 65, and asks about ongoing costs.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Prescribe moderate aerobic and resistance exercise in short bouts, in a cool setting, monitoring next-day fatigue): Moderate exercise in short bouts with rest, in a cool environment, builds fitness without provoking heat-related symptoms, and tracking next-day fatigue guides progression. Pushing to exhaustion risks a prolonged setback. Avoiding activity worsens deconditioning and does not prevent relapses, and warm water can bring on heat-sensitive symptoms."}, {"id": 1714, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "After a hot shower Meera has blurred vision and heavier legs, which settle within about an hour of cooling down. She is frightened that it is a relapse and asks the physiotherapist whether she should stop exercising. What best explains these temporary symptoms?", "options": {"A": "Increased spasticity from skin stimulation by hot water, which resolves once skin temperature returns to baseline", "B": "A new relapse from fresh inflammatory lesions, so she needs same-day neurological review", "C": "Postural hypotension from heat-related vasodilation, which causes symptoms mainly on standing and settles when she sits", "D": "Heat-related conduction block in demyelinated nerves, which is temporary and not a new relapse"}, "cluster_id": "CHS189", "cluster_text": "Meera Menon, 41, a primary school teacher, has had relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis for six years, stable on disease-modifying therapy with no relapse in 18 months. Her EDSS score is 3.0. She walks about 400 metres before her legs feel heavy and she has to stop. Fatigue builds through the afternoon, and her symptoms worsen after a hot shower or in hot weather. Strength is 4+/5 at both hip flexors and ankle dorsiflexors, with mild plantarflexor spasticity (modified Ashworth 1). HR 72, BP 118/74 mmHg. She works four days a week and wants to keep her job. She has used all the Medicare-subsidised allied health visits available under her GP management plan this calendar year, is not an NDIS participant, is under 65, and asks about ongoing costs.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Heat-related conduction block in demyelinated nerves, which is temporary and not a new relapse): A small rise in core temperature slows conduction in demyelinated axons, so old symptoms briefly reappear and then fade as she cools, a pattern known as Uhthoff phenomenon. A true relapse lasts longer than a day and does not settle with cooling. Postural hypotension would not explain the visual change, and spasticity does not cause blurred vision."}, {"id": 1715, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Meera teaches all day on Mondays and Tuesdays and then 'crashes' by evening, cancelling plans and sleeping poorly. She keeps no record of her activity and wants to keep working four days a week. Which fatigue management strategy should the physiotherapist introduce first?", "options": {"A": "Start a graded aerobic programme in a warm pool, progressing weekly to build her fatigue tolerance", "B": "Prescribe an hour of bed rest after work each day, and reduce evening activity", "C": "Teach pacing: plan activity and rest breaks across the day and review patterns in a diary", "D": "Arrange a workplace assessment to adjust her duties and rosters before teaching self management strategies"}, "cluster_id": "CHS189", "cluster_text": "Meera Menon, 41, a primary school teacher, has had relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis for six years, stable on disease-modifying therapy with no relapse in 18 months. Her EDSS score is 3.0. She walks about 400 metres before her legs feel heavy and she has to stop. Fatigue builds through the afternoon, and her symptoms worsen after a hot shower or in hot weather. Strength is 4+/5 at both hip flexors and ankle dorsiflexors, with mild plantarflexor spasticity (modified Ashworth 1). HR 72, BP 118/74 mmHg. She works four days a week and wants to keep her job. She has used all the Medicare-subsidised allied health visits available under her GP management plan this calendar year, is not an NDIS participant, is under 65, and asks about ongoing costs.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Teach pacing: plan activity and rest breaks across the day and review patterns in a diary): Her pattern of crashing after heavy days and her lack of any record call for pacing with planned rest breaks and a diary, which is a core first step in managing fatigue in multiple sclerosis. Exercise has a place later, but warm water worsens her heat sensitive symptoms. A long daily rest does not address activity patterns, and workplace changes can follow once she has data from her diary."}, {"id": 1716, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Meera has used all the Medicare-subsidised allied health visits available under her GP management plan this year, is under 65 and is not an NDIS participant. She worries about the cost of ongoing physiotherapy. What is the most appropriate advice from the physiotherapist?", "options": {"A": "Advise applying to My Aged Care for ongoing funding, as multiple sclerosis is a chronic condition", "B": "Advise stopping physiotherapy until the new calendar year, when her subsidised visits renew under the plan", "C": "Explain her subsidy limit, outline how NDIS access works, and agree a self-managed programme meanwhile", "D": "Continue weekly sessions and claim them under NDIS item numbers so she is not charged anything"}, "cluster_id": "CHS189", "cluster_text": "Meera Menon, 41, a primary school teacher, has had relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis for six years, stable on disease-modifying therapy with no relapse in 18 months. Her EDSS score is 3.0. She walks about 400 metres before her legs feel heavy and she has to stop. Fatigue builds through the afternoon, and her symptoms worsen after a hot shower or in hot weather. Strength is 4+/5 at both hip flexors and ankle dorsiflexors, with mild plantarflexor spasticity (modified Ashworth 1). HR 72, BP 118/74 mmHg. She works four days a week and wants to keep her job. She has used all the Medicare-subsidised allied health visits available under her GP management plan this calendar year, is not an NDIS participant, is under 65, and asks about ongoing costs.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Explain her subsidy limit, outline how NDIS access works, and agree a self-managed programme meanwhile): She needs honest information about the subsidy cap, the NDIS eligibility process (usually supported by medical evidence), and a home programme so progress continues. Billing the NDIS for a non-participant is improper. My Aged Care serves older people, so it does not suit a woman of 41, and stopping care for months ignores options that keep her active."}, {"id": 1717, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "While assessing, you feel a click over the sternum when Mr Bui coughs, and he reports pain at the same spot. The wound looks clean and dry, he is afebrile and his observations are stable. What is the most appropriate interpretation and action?", "options": {"A": "Musculoskeletal chest wall pain from coughing, so treat with soft tissue release and thoracic mobility exercises", "B": "Possible sternal instability: tell the surgical team and reinforce pillow splinting during coughing", "C": "Early deep wound infection, so stop therapy and request urgent wound swabs from the nurse", "D": "Expected healing noise at this stage, so continue current precautions and recheck the sternum next week"}, "cluster_id": "CHS190", "cluster_text": "Duc Bui, 78, a retired bus driver, is on day 7 after surgical aortic valve replacement through a median sternotomy and has moved to inpatient rehabilitation. History: atrial fibrillation, type 2 diabetes and hypertension. Medications include a beta blocker, warfarin and metformin. Three days ago he had a brief postoperative delirium, now documented as resolved. Today: HR 78 irregular, BP 118/70, RR 18, SpO2 95 percent on room air, afebrile. The sternal wound is dry and clean. Pain is 4/10 with coughing, and a click is palpable over the sternum during a cough. He walks 60 metres supervised with a frame. He is alert and oriented but tired, and declines the group exercise class, saying he wants to rest. His wife says he must attend because the surgeon wants him moving.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Possible sternal instability: tell the surgical team and reinforce pillow splinting during coughing): A palpable click with pain over the sternum when coughing suggests the bone edges are moving, which is sternal instability and needs surgical review before it worsens or becomes infected. Pillow splinting reduces shearing forces in the meantime. A clean, dry wound without fever makes infection less likely, so swabs would not address the finding, and manual treatment could add stress to the sternum."}, {"id": 1718, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Bui needs to rise from his bed and chair independently before discharge. He tends to push up hard through both arms, hold his breath and grunt with effort. Which instruction best protects his healing sternum from excessive stress during these transfers?", "options": {"A": "Push through the armrests with elbows held wide so the load spreads across the chest wall", "B": "Pull up on a bed rail with both hands so the legs and trunk move less", "C": "Rock forward and back to build momentum, then stand quickly while holding the breath", "D": "Keep arms close to the body, use leg drive, and breathe out while standing"}, "cluster_id": "CHS190", "cluster_text": "Duc Bui, 78, a retired bus driver, is on day 7 after surgical aortic valve replacement through a median sternotomy and has moved to inpatient rehabilitation. History: atrial fibrillation, type 2 diabetes and hypertension. Medications include a beta blocker, warfarin and metformin. Three days ago he had a brief postoperative delirium, now documented as resolved. Today: HR 78 irregular, BP 118/70, RR 18, SpO2 95 percent on room air, afebrile. The sternal wound is dry and clean. Pain is 4/10 with coughing, and a click is palpable over the sternum during a cough. He walks 60 metres supervised with a frame. He is alert and oriented but tired, and declines the group exercise class, saying he wants to rest. His wife says he must attend because the surgeon wants him moving.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Keep arms close to the body, use leg drive, and breathe out while standing): Keeping the arms close, using the legs and exhaling while rising avoids a Valsalva manoeuvre and limits the pull of the chest muscles on the healing sternum. Pulling hard on a rail or pushing with elbows wide increases lateral traction across the bone. Momentum with a held breath raises chest pressure and shear."}, {"id": 1719, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Bui now walks 60 metres comfortably. His heart rhythm is irregular and he takes a beta blocker. He tires by the end of the walk, and telemetry is not available on the ward. How should the physiotherapist monitor and progress his walking intensity safely?", "options": {"A": "Set a heart rate range by formula and progress speed while the pulse stays inside it", "B": "Hold intensity at the current level until discharge since further increases risk arrhythmia in atrial fibrillation", "C": "Use fingertip oximetry as the main guide and progress when saturation stays above ninety percent", "D": "Use perceived exertion with observed recovery, and progress distance before speed in small steps"}, "cluster_id": "CHS190", "cluster_text": "Duc Bui, 78, a retired bus driver, is on day 7 after surgical aortic valve replacement through a median sternotomy and has moved to inpatient rehabilitation. History: atrial fibrillation, type 2 diabetes and hypertension. Medications include a beta blocker, warfarin and metformin. Three days ago he had a brief postoperative delirium, now documented as resolved. Today: HR 78 irregular, BP 118/70, RR 18, SpO2 95 percent on room air, afebrile. The sternal wound is dry and clean. Pain is 4/10 with coughing, and a click is palpable over the sternum during a cough. He walks 60 metres supervised with a frame. He is alert and oriented but tired, and declines the group exercise class, saying he wants to rest. His wife says he must attend because the surgeon wants him moving.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Use perceived exertion with observed recovery, and progress distance before speed in small steps): Heart rate is unreliable for prescribing intensity in atrial fibrillation with a beta blocker, so perceived exertion, symptoms and recovery are the safer guides. Progressing one variable at a time, distance before speed, lets fatigue and sternal comfort be monitored. Holding intensity constant delays recovery without good reason, and oximetry does not reflect cardiac workload."}, {"id": 1720, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Bui declines the group class, saying he wants to rest. He was confused three days ago but is now alert and oriented, and understands what the class involves. His wife says he must attend. What should the physiotherapist do next?", "options": {"A": "Persuade him by stressing the risks of immobility until he agrees, then record his agreement", "B": "Accept his wife's consent on his behalf and enrol him in the class as planned", "C": "Respect his decision, explore his reasons, offer a shorter individual session, and document it", "D": "Request a formal capacity assessment from the medical team before any further offer of exercise sessions"}, "cluster_id": "CHS190", "cluster_text": "Duc Bui, 78, a retired bus driver, is on day 7 after surgical aortic valve replacement through a median sternotomy and has moved to inpatient rehabilitation. History: atrial fibrillation, type 2 diabetes and hypertension. Medications include a beta blocker, warfarin and metformin. Three days ago he had a brief postoperative delirium, now documented as resolved. Today: HR 78 irregular, BP 118/70, RR 18, SpO2 95 percent on room air, afebrile. The sternal wound is dry and clean. Pain is 4/10 with coughing, and a click is palpable over the sternum during a cough. He walks 60 metres supervised with a frame. He is alert and oriented but tired, and declines the group exercise class, saying he wants to rest. His wife says he must attend because the surgeon wants him moving.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Respect his decision, explore his reasons, offer a shorter individual session, and document it): Adults are presumed to have capacity, and his delirium has resolved with understanding shown, so his refusal stands and his wife cannot consent for him. Exploring his reasons may reveal fatigue or worries that an adapted session can address. A formal assessment is not needed without a reason to doubt capacity, and pressuring him undermines voluntary consent."}, {"id": 1721, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Kai propels with short rapid push strokes and tires after 200 metres, with right shoulder pain during propulsion. The physiotherapist wants to reduce load on the shoulders during everyday wheelchair use. Which propulsion instruction is most appropriate to teach Kai at this visit?", "options": {"A": "Grip the rim high with elbows raised to keep a quick rhythm of short pushes", "B": "Keep the trunk upright and push mainly with the wrists, so the larger shoulder muscles rest", "C": "Use long strokes but return the hands up and over the rims in a high arc", "D": "Use long, smooth strokes at a low frequency, letting the hands drift down after each push"}, "cluster_id": "CHS191", "cluster_text": "Kai Williams, 24, uses they/them pronouns and identifies as non-binary. Kai has a T5 complete spinal cord injury (ASIA Impairment Scale A) from a motorbike crash eight months ago and lives with a flatmate. Community physiotherapy review is for right shoulder pain with wheelchair propulsion, 6 out of 10 on a numeric scale. Findings: positive impingement signs on the right, reduced scapular control, short rapid push strokes, fatigue after 200 metres. Resting seated blood pressure is usually around 100/60 mmHg. Skin is intact; Kai uses a pressure cushion but rarely does weight shifts. Previous clinics recorded Kai as female and the referral letter uses a different name. Kai asks you to use their chosen name. Kai has also found an online article promoting a new shoulder support device, based on a small manufacturer-funded study of 12 healthy adults, and asks whether to buy it.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Use long, smooth strokes at a low frequency, letting the hands drift down after each push): Long, smooth strokes at a low frequency reduce the number of pushes and peak shoulder load, and a semicircular recovery pattern is preferred over an arc. Short, fast strokes raise stroke frequency and cumulative load, and a high arc takes the shoulder into elevated positions near the impingement range. Shifting the load to the wrists risks overuse injury there."}, {"id": 1722, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "During the session Kai develops a pounding headache, flushing and sweating above the injury level and blurred vision. Seated blood pressure is 165/105 mmHg, far above their usual reading. Kai has been sitting in the wheelchair for the past hour. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Offer fluids and a rest period, then repeat the blood pressure measurement after about thirty minutes", "B": "Lay Kai flat with the legs raised to improve venous return and lower the blood pressure", "C": "Continue the session at lower intensity, rechecking blood pressure every five minutes before sending Kai home", "D": "Keep Kai upright, loosen tight clothing, and check for triggers such as a full bladder"}, "cluster_id": "CHS191", "cluster_text": "Kai Williams, 24, uses they/them pronouns and identifies as non-binary. Kai has a T5 complete spinal cord injury (ASIA Impairment Scale A) from a motorbike crash eight months ago and lives with a flatmate. Community physiotherapy review is for right shoulder pain with wheelchair propulsion, 6 out of 10 on a numeric scale. Findings: positive impingement signs on the right, reduced scapular control, short rapid push strokes, fatigue after 200 metres. Resting seated blood pressure is usually around 100/60 mmHg. Skin is intact; Kai uses a pressure cushion but rarely does weight shifts. Previous clinics recorded Kai as female and the referral letter uses a different name. Kai asks you to use their chosen name. Kai has also found an online article promoting a new shoulder support device, based on a small manufacturer-funded study of 12 healthy adults, and asks whether to buy it.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Keep Kai upright, loosen tight clothing, and check for triggers such as a full bladder): These signs indicate autonomic dysreflexia, a medical emergency in injuries at T6 and above. Sitting upright lets gravity lower the pressure, and removing the trigger, often bladder or bowel, is the priority, with emergency help called if it does not settle. Lying flat raises the pressure, and waiting or continuing exercise leaves the trigger in place."}, {"id": 1723, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Kai asks to be called by their chosen name and pronouns. Earlier records list Kai as female, and the referral letter uses a different name. A shoulder and trunk examination is needed, and Kai seems uneasy about being touched. Which approach is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Record the chosen name and pronouns with Kai's consent, then agree on clothing and chaperone preferences", "B": "Use the chosen name aloud, but keep the legal name and female pronouns in notes", "C": "Ask Kai to explain their gender history first, so that the treatment plan reflects relevant anatomy", "D": "Use gender-neutral language in conversation and copy the referral's wording in the records for consistency"}, "cluster_id": "CHS191", "cluster_text": "Kai Williams, 24, uses they/them pronouns and identifies as non-binary. Kai has a T5 complete spinal cord injury (ASIA Impairment Scale A) from a motorbike crash eight months ago and lives with a flatmate. Community physiotherapy review is for right shoulder pain with wheelchair propulsion, 6 out of 10 on a numeric scale. Findings: positive impingement signs on the right, reduced scapular control, short rapid push strokes, fatigue after 200 metres. Resting seated blood pressure is usually around 100/60 mmHg. Skin is intact; Kai uses a pressure cushion but rarely does weight shifts. Previous clinics recorded Kai as female and the referral letter uses a different name. Kai asks you to use their chosen name. Kai has also found an online article promoting a new shoulder support device, based on a small manufacturer-funded study of 12 healthy adults, and asks whether to buy it.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Record the chosen name and pronouns with Kai's consent, then agree on clothing and chaperone preferences): Respectful practice uses the name and pronouns the person asks for, records them with consent, and asks only for information that is relevant to care. Examination preferences such as clothing and a chaperone should be agreed before touching. Asking for gender history that is not needed, or keeping outdated details for consistency, undermines trust."}, {"id": 1724, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Kai asks whether to buy the shoulder device from an online article. The article reports one small, manufacturer-funded study of 12 healthy adults. Kai has a complete spinal cord injury and shoulder pain. How should the physiotherapist respond when appraising this evidence?", "options": {"A": "Advise against it because all commercial devices lack evidence, and recommend relying on clinical experience alone", "B": "Ask the manufacturer for its full data, and recommend the device if the results appear positive", "C": "Support buying it, as the measured benefits in healthy adults should transfer to wheelchair users", "D": "Explain the evidence is weak for people with spinal cord injury, and check current guidelines"}, "cluster_id": "CHS191", "cluster_text": "Kai Williams, 24, uses they/them pronouns and identifies as non-binary. Kai has a T5 complete spinal cord injury (ASIA Impairment Scale A) from a motorbike crash eight months ago and lives with a flatmate. Community physiotherapy review is for right shoulder pain with wheelchair propulsion, 6 out of 10 on a numeric scale. Findings: positive impingement signs on the right, reduced scapular control, short rapid push strokes, fatigue after 200 metres. Resting seated blood pressure is usually around 100/60 mmHg. Skin is intact; Kai uses a pressure cushion but rarely does weight shifts. Previous clinics recorded Kai as female and the referral letter uses a different name. Kai asks you to use their chosen name. Kai has also found an online article promoting a new shoulder support device, based on a small manufacturer-funded study of 12 healthy adults, and asks whether to buy it.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain the evidence is weak for people with spinal cord injury, and check current guidelines): A small, manufacturer-funded study in healthy adults carries a high risk of bias and poor applicability to a person with a complete spinal cord injury. The physiotherapist should weigh it against high-quality guidelines and reviews on upper limb care and against Kai's own goals. Dismissing all evidence in favour of experience is as unsound as accepting company data."}, {"id": 1725, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Dwayne is eager to walk and has no pain, with stable vital signs today. Hip flexion is grade 3 on the right and 2 on the left, with ankle dorsiflexion at grade 1. Which gait training approach is most appropriate to begin with?", "options": {"A": "Parallel bar or supported stepping with orthoses, graded to his strength while monitoring blood pressure", "B": "Wheelchair skills training only, because walking is unlikely to be functional with his strength", "C": "Unsupported walking over ground with a four wheeled frame to encourage maximal voluntary effort", "D": "A standing frame programme alone until hip flexion reaches grade 4, deferring stepping practice until then"}, "cluster_id": "CHS192", "cluster_text": "Dwayne Mason, 16, an Aboriginal teenager from a remote community, fell from a motorbike four weeks ago and sustained a T10 spinal cord injury, AIS grade C, with surgical fixation completed. He is on a spinal injuries unit. Strength (out of 5): hip flexion right 3, left 2; knee extension right 3, left 2; ankle dorsiflexion 1 bilaterally. Sitting trunk control is fair and sensation below T10 is patchy. He transfers with a sliding board and is independent in a manual wheelchair. Resting HR 76, BP 110/70. He is keen to walk and uses the parallel bars in long leg braces. His mother, who is staying nearby and is anxious, asks the physiotherapist whether Dwayne will father children and when his bladder will recover. An Aboriginal liaison officer is available on the unit.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Parallel bar or supported stepping with orthoses, graded to his strength while monitoring blood pressure): Incomplete injury with preserved motor function gives real walking potential, so task specific stepping with support matched to his strength is appropriate and safe. Unsupported walking exceeds his control, deferring stepping loses a window for activity based recovery, and abandoning walking is premature at four weeks."}, {"id": 1726, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "During parallel bar standing, Dwayne feels light headed and looks pale. His blood pressure reads 85/55 compared with 110/70 at rest, and his heart rate has risen. The physiotherapist is working alone with him. What is the most appropriate immediate action?", "options": {"A": "Sit him upright and encourage a large drink before any further standing is attempted today", "B": "Continue standing with an abdominal binder applied and recheck his blood pressure after a minute", "C": "Sit him back down or recline the chair with legs raised, then recheck his blood pressure", "D": "Ask him to breathe slowly and deeply while standing for two minutes as pressure recovers"}, "cluster_id": "CHS192", "cluster_text": "Dwayne Mason, 16, an Aboriginal teenager from a remote community, fell from a motorbike four weeks ago and sustained a T10 spinal cord injury, AIS grade C, with surgical fixation completed. He is on a spinal injuries unit. Strength (out of 5): hip flexion right 3, left 2; knee extension right 3, left 2; ankle dorsiflexion 1 bilaterally. Sitting trunk control is fair and sensation below T10 is patchy. He transfers with a sliding board and is independent in a manual wheelchair. Resting HR 76, BP 110/70. He is keen to walk and uses the parallel bars in long leg braces. His mother, who is staying nearby and is anxious, asks the physiotherapist whether Dwayne will father children and when his bladder will recover. An Aboriginal liaison officer is available on the unit.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Sit him back down or recline the chair with legs raised, then recheck his blood pressure): Symptomatic hypotension on standing after spinal cord injury is managed by returning to a position that restores venous return, such as reclining with legs elevated, and then rechecking. Persisting with standing risks fainting, and sitting upright with a drink does not quickly restore blood flow to the brain."}, {"id": 1727, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Four weeks after injury, Dwayne's right calf is swollen, warm and red compared with the left, and he feels no pain in it. He is otherwise well and keen to continue today. Which condition must be considered before any further gait training takes place?", "options": {"A": "Deep vein thrombosis, because venous clots are common early after spinal cord injury and may embolise", "B": "Cellulitis from a pressure area, because skin infection often presents with redness and warmth over limbs", "C": "Muscle strain from stepping practice, because overuse of weak muscles commonly causes localised swelling", "D": "Heterotopic ossification, because new bone growth around joints commonly produces local warmth and swelling"}, "cluster_id": "CHS192", "cluster_text": "Dwayne Mason, 16, an Aboriginal teenager from a remote community, fell from a motorbike four weeks ago and sustained a T10 spinal cord injury, AIS grade C, with surgical fixation completed. He is on a spinal injuries unit. Strength (out of 5): hip flexion right 3, left 2; knee extension right 3, left 2; ankle dorsiflexion 1 bilaterally. Sitting trunk control is fair and sensation below T10 is patchy. He transfers with a sliding board and is independent in a manual wheelchair. Resting HR 76, BP 110/70. He is keen to walk and uses the parallel bars in long leg braces. His mother, who is staying nearby and is anxious, asks the physiotherapist whether Dwayne will father children and when his bladder will recover. An Aboriginal liaison officer is available on the unit.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Deep vein thrombosis, because venous clots are common early after spinal cord injury and may embolise): A unilateral swollen, warm calf in an immobile patient with spinal cord injury is deep vein thrombosis until excluded, and the lack of pain is expected with impaired sensation. Exercising could dislodge a clot, so training stops and urgent medical assessment is arranged."}, {"id": 1728, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Dwayne's mother asks, in front of him, whether he will father children and when his bladder will recover. Dwayne is listening and looks worried. These topics are beyond the physiotherapist's role on the unit. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Reassure her that recovery is likely because his injury is incomplete and classed AIS grade C", "B": "Acknowledge her worry, explain the spinal team will cover these topics, and arrange a joint meeting", "C": "Decline to discuss these topics and suggest she raises them with the general practitioner after discharge", "D": "Explain that bladder and sexual function recover alongside walking, so gait progress predicts both"}, "cluster_id": "CHS192", "cluster_text": "Dwayne Mason, 16, an Aboriginal teenager from a remote community, fell from a motorbike four weeks ago and sustained a T10 spinal cord injury, AIS grade C, with surgical fixation completed. He is on a spinal injuries unit. Strength (out of 5): hip flexion right 3, left 2; knee extension right 3, left 2; ankle dorsiflexion 1 bilaterally. Sitting trunk control is fair and sensation below T10 is patchy. He transfers with a sliding board and is independent in a manual wheelchair. Resting HR 76, BP 110/70. He is keen to walk and uses the parallel bars in long leg braces. His mother, who is staying nearby and is anxious, asks the physiotherapist whether Dwayne will father children and when his bladder will recover. An Aboriginal liaison officer is available on the unit.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Acknowledge her worry, explain the spinal team will cover these topics, and arrange a joint meeting): The physiotherapist should respond with empathy, stay within scope, and link the family to the clinicians who can give accurate advice, including Dwayne himself and cultural support. Offering predictions, or linking function recovery to gait, overreaches, while delaying until discharge leaves the family without answers."}, {"id": 1729, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Nam fell three metres onto concrete and now has midline lower cervical tenderness, tingling in both hands, and reduced grip and elbow extension strength, with the CT scan still unreported. Which interpretation of these findings is most appropriate for the physiotherapist at this point?", "options": {"A": "Likely soft tissue strain, because his GCS is 15 and he moves his neck voluntarily", "B": "Nerve root irritation from the fall, so he can sit up once the collar is refitted", "C": "Possible cervical injury with neurological signs, so precautions continue until imaging and medical clearance", "D": "Brachial plexus traction injury from the fall, so upper limb neurological monitoring is sufficient for now"}, "cluster_id": "CHS193", "cluster_text": "Nam Do, 34, scaffolder, arrived by ambulance at the emergency department after falling about three metres from a ladder onto concrete, landing on his upper back and head. No loss of consciousness, GCS 15. He is on a trolley wearing a cervical collar, and the spinal board has been removed. He reports neck pain 7/10 and intermittent tingling in both hands. Examination: midline tenderness over the lower cervical spinous processes, tenderness over right ribs 8 to 10 with shallow breathing, SpO2 95% on room air, RR 20, HR 96, BP 128/76. Grip and elbow extension are 4/5 bilaterally, lower limb strength is 5/5, and light touch is intact apart from patchy tingling in both thumbs and index fingers. CT of the cervical spine and chest has been ordered but not yet reported. The emergency department physiotherapist has been asked to help with chest clearance and sitting him up.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Possible cervical injury with neurological signs, so precautions continue until imaging and medical clearance): A fall from three metres, midline bony tenderness and bilateral hand symptoms with reduced strength mean cervical injury with neurological involvement must be assumed until imaging and medical clearance. A normal GCS does not exclude spinal injury. Treating this as nerve root irritation or a plexus injury would relax precautions too early when the CT is still unreported."}, {"id": 1730, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Nam's right rib pain limits his breathing, and the team wants chest physiotherapy while cervical precautions remain in place and the CT is still pending. His SpO2 is 95% and secretions are minimal. Which approach is most appropriate for supporting his lung expansion?", "options": {"A": "Sit him upright in a chair, collar loosened, to improve basal expansion on the injured side", "B": "Position in side-lying with a head pillow and use manual percussion to clear any secretions", "C": "Coached deep breathing with spirometry lying supine or head-up tilted, keeping spinal alignment and collar on", "D": "Active cycle of breathing in high sitting with slight neck flexion to reduce rib pain"}, "cluster_id": "CHS193", "cluster_text": "Nam Do, 34, scaffolder, arrived by ambulance at the emergency department after falling about three metres from a ladder onto concrete, landing on his upper back and head. No loss of consciousness, GCS 15. He is on a trolley wearing a cervical collar, and the spinal board has been removed. He reports neck pain 7/10 and intermittent tingling in both hands. Examination: midline tenderness over the lower cervical spinous processes, tenderness over right ribs 8 to 10 with shallow breathing, SpO2 95% on room air, RR 20, HR 96, BP 128/76. Grip and elbow extension are 4/5 bilaterally, lower limb strength is 5/5, and light touch is intact apart from patchy tingling in both thumbs and index fingers. CT of the cervical spine and chest has been ordered but not yet reported. The emergency department physiotherapist has been asked to help with chest clearance and sitting him up.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Coached deep breathing with spirometry lying supine or head-up tilted, keeping spinal alignment and collar on): Breathing exercises can be delivered in bed with alignment maintained, using a head-up tilt if the medical team agrees, so the spine is not moved. Sitting up, loosening the collar or percussion each risk spinal movement or rib pain before any clearance has been given."}, {"id": 1731, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Nam is practising breathing exercises when he says his legs feel heavy and his feet feel strange, and the tingling in his hands is stronger than before. His heart rate and oxygen saturation are unchanged. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Stop the activity, keep him still and aligned, and alert the treating medical team immediately", "B": "Pause the exercises, sit him upright to relieve strain, and ask the nurse to review him", "C": "Check lower limb power and sensation fully, then report your findings to the doctor in detail", "D": "Stop the exercises, tighten the collar for stability and reassess his neurology in ten minutes"}, "cluster_id": "CHS193", "cluster_text": "Nam Do, 34, scaffolder, arrived by ambulance at the emergency department after falling about three metres from a ladder onto concrete, landing on his upper back and head. No loss of consciousness, GCS 15. He is on a trolley wearing a cervical collar, and the spinal board has been removed. He reports neck pain 7/10 and intermittent tingling in both hands. Examination: midline tenderness over the lower cervical spinous processes, tenderness over right ribs 8 to 10 with shallow breathing, SpO2 95% on room air, RR 20, HR 96, BP 128/76. Grip and elbow extension are 4/5 bilaterally, lower limb strength is 5/5, and light touch is intact apart from patchy tingling in both thumbs and index fingers. CT of the cervical spine and chest has been ordered but not yet reported. The emergency department physiotherapist has been asked to help with chest clearance and sitting him up.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stop the activity, keep him still and aligned, and alert the treating medical team immediately): New leg symptoms and stronger hand tingling suggest evolving cord compromise, so the priority is to stop, avoid any movement and alert the medical team immediately. Sitting him up moves a possibly unstable spine. Detailed testing or waiting ten minutes to reassess delays medical review, even though the information may be useful later."}, {"id": 1732, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "A nurse asks the physiotherapist to take off the collar so Nam can sit up for the chest clearance, and to write that the neck has been cleared because the physiotherapist has examined it. The CT report is still not finalised. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Decline, explaining that spinal clearance is a medical decision, and document the request", "B": "Remove the collar after repeating a neurological screen, as physiotherapists are trained to examine necks", "C": "Remove the collar once the radiographer says preliminary images look normal, and note the conversation", "D": "Remove the collar only for the transfer to sitting, then refit it once settled"}, "cluster_id": "CHS193", "cluster_text": "Nam Do, 34, scaffolder, arrived by ambulance at the emergency department after falling about three metres from a ladder onto concrete, landing on his upper back and head. No loss of consciousness, GCS 15. He is on a trolley wearing a cervical collar, and the spinal board has been removed. He reports neck pain 7/10 and intermittent tingling in both hands. Examination: midline tenderness over the lower cervical spinous processes, tenderness over right ribs 8 to 10 with shallow breathing, SpO2 95% on room air, RR 20, HR 96, BP 128/76. Grip and elbow extension are 4/5 bilaterally, lower limb strength is 5/5, and light touch is intact apart from patchy tingling in both thumbs and index fingers. CT of the cervical spine and chest has been ordered but not yet reported. The emergency department physiotherapist has been asked to help with chest clearance and sitting him up.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Decline, explaining that spinal clearance is a medical decision, and document the request): Cervical spine clearance rests with the treating medical team using imaging and clinical criteria. A physiotherapist examination or an informal comment from another professional does not replace it. Briefly removing the collar still exposes him to the same risk, so the correct step is to decline and record why."}, {"id": 1733, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "The obstetric registrar asks why Mele's pain is attributed to pelvic girdle pain rather than a lumbar disc problem. Consider both her symptoms and her examination. Which finding best supports pregnancy related pelvic girdle pain as the primary diagnosis in this presentation?", "options": {"A": "Normal blood pressure and normal fetal movements, which show the pain is not of lumbar origin", "B": "Waddling gait and pain on stairs, as these are specific to pelvic girdle pain in pregnancy", "C": "Tenderness over the pubic symphysis, which excludes any contribution from the lumbar spine to her pain", "D": "Positive pelvic provocation tests eased by compression, with normal neurology and painless lumbar movement"}, "cluster_id": "CHS194", "cluster_text": "Mele Taufa, 31, primary school teacher, is 28 weeks pregnant with her second baby and attends the antenatal clinic physiotherapy service. For two weeks she has had worsening pain over both sacroiliac joints and the pubic symphysis, 7 out of 10, worse with walking, stairs, turning in bed and standing on one leg. There is no fever, no bladder or bowel change, no saddle numbness, no leg weakness. Obstetric history is normal; BP 112/70 and fetal movements are normal. Examination: waddling gait, tenderness over the pubic symphysis, positive posterior pelvic pain provocation test on both sides, and a painful active straight leg raise that improves with manual compression of the pelvis. Lumbar movements are full and pain free, and neurological examination is normal. She tells you she knows you from her children's school and would like you to treat her at her home after hours for cash. The antenatal clinic does not offer home visits.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Positive pelvic provocation tests eased by compression, with normal neurology and painless lumbar movement): Pain reproduced by pelvic provocation tests and relieved by compression, together with a normal lumbar and neurological examination, points to the pelvic joints as the source. Gait change and stairs pain occur with many conditions. Obstetric observations are unrelated to the spinal source, and symphyseal tenderness does not rule out other contributors."}, {"id": 1734, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mele's pain is 7 out of 10 and limits her walking to about ten minutes. She asks what she can do to reduce the load on her pelvis during daily activities and sleep at home. Which advice is most appropriate to give first?", "options": {"A": "Reduce walking to essential trips, rest with pillows between the legs, and stay within pain limits", "B": "Strengthen the gluteals and deep abdominals with supervised bridging exercises, progressing weekly to stabilise the pelvis", "C": "Apply heat and gentle adductor stretches daily, and walk a little further each day", "D": "Take smaller steps, avoid single leg loading, roll with knees together and consider a pelvic belt"}, "cluster_id": "CHS194", "cluster_text": "Mele Taufa, 31, primary school teacher, is 28 weeks pregnant with her second baby and attends the antenatal clinic physiotherapy service. For two weeks she has had worsening pain over both sacroiliac joints and the pubic symphysis, 7 out of 10, worse with walking, stairs, turning in bed and standing on one leg. There is no fever, no bladder or bowel change, no saddle numbness, no leg weakness. Obstetric history is normal; BP 112/70 and fetal movements are normal. Examination: waddling gait, tenderness over the pubic symphysis, positive posterior pelvic pain provocation test on both sides, and a painful active straight leg raise that improves with manual compression of the pelvis. Lumbar movements are full and pain free, and neurological examination is normal. She tells you she knows you from her children's school and would like you to treat her at her home after hours for cash. The antenatal clinic does not offer home visits.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Take smaller steps, avoid single leg loading, roll with knees together and consider a pelvic belt): First advice for pregnancy related pelvic girdle pain is to reduce asymmetric and single leg loading, take smaller steps, turn with the knees together and trial a support belt. Reducing activity and resting helps pain briefly but lacks load advice and encourages deconditioning. Strengthening may suit later once pain settles, and stretching the adductors with extra walking aggravates painful pelvic joints."}, {"id": 1735, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "At 31 weeks Mele phones the clinic. Her pelvic pain is unchanged but since yesterday her left calf is swollen, warm and tender, and she feels slightly breathless when climbing stairs. She is at home alone. What should the physiotherapist advise?", "options": {"A": "Call her GP for an urgent appointment today, and avoid massage while waiting for review", "B": "Attend the emergency department urgently, with a driver or ambulance, and avoid exercise and massage", "C": "Ring her midwife now for advice, and rest with the leg elevated until they respond", "D": "Attend the clinic today for a calf examination, driving herself and avoiding massage on the way"}, "cluster_id": "CHS194", "cluster_text": "Mele Taufa, 31, primary school teacher, is 28 weeks pregnant with her second baby and attends the antenatal clinic physiotherapy service. For two weeks she has had worsening pain over both sacroiliac joints and the pubic symphysis, 7 out of 10, worse with walking, stairs, turning in bed and standing on one leg. There is no fever, no bladder or bowel change, no saddle numbness, no leg weakness. Obstetric history is normal; BP 112/70 and fetal movements are normal. Examination: waddling gait, tenderness over the pubic symphysis, positive posterior pelvic pain provocation test on both sides, and a painful active straight leg raise that improves with manual compression of the pelvis. Lumbar movements are full and pain free, and neurological examination is normal. She tells you she knows you from her children's school and would like you to treat her at her home after hours for cash. The antenatal clinic does not offer home visits.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Attend the emergency department urgently, with a driver or ambulance, and avoid exercise and massage): A swollen, warm calf with new breathlessness in pregnancy raises concern for deep vein thrombosis with pulmonary embolism, which needs emergency assessment and transport without exertion. A GP appointment or midwife call adds delay when imaging and treatment are needed urgently. Driving herself to a clinic risks collapse and puts her somewhere that cannot manage a pulmonary embolism."}, {"id": 1736, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mele repeats her request for private after hours home visits for cash and says she would feel more comfortable with you than any colleague. Consider your duty of care and the clinic's referral pathway. What is the most appropriate response to her?", "options": {"A": "Decline private visits politely, explain the pathway, and continue her care through the clinic", "B": "Transfer her care to a colleague and end your involvement because friendship prevents objective treatment", "C": "Agree to occasional home visits because her pain limits travel, and document them afterwards", "D": "Offer home visits within clinic hours at the standard fee and record them in the system"}, "cluster_id": "CHS194", "cluster_text": "Mele Taufa, 31, primary school teacher, is 28 weeks pregnant with her second baby and attends the antenatal clinic physiotherapy service. For two weeks she has had worsening pain over both sacroiliac joints and the pubic symphysis, 7 out of 10, worse with walking, stairs, turning in bed and standing on one leg. There is no fever, no bladder or bowel change, no saddle numbness, no leg weakness. Obstetric history is normal; BP 112/70 and fetal movements are normal. Examination: waddling gait, tenderness over the pubic symphysis, positive posterior pelvic pain provocation test on both sides, and a painful active straight leg raise that improves with manual compression of the pelvis. Lumbar movements are full and pain free, and neurological examination is normal. She tells you she knows you from her children's school and would like you to treat her at her home after hours for cash. The antenatal clinic does not offer home visits.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Decline private visits politely, explain the pathway, and continue her care through the clinic): A knowing relationship does not by itself prevent care, but private cash arrangements outside the service blur the professional boundary and bypass clinical governance and records. Declining courteously and keeping her care within the clinic protects her and you. Ending care abruptly abandons duty of care, and an unapproved home visit model sits outside what the service offers."}, {"id": 1737, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Nair freezes at doorways and falls while turning in narrow spaces. The physiotherapist wants a baseline measure that is quick in clinic and reflects the mobility demands that cause his falls. Which single measure is most appropriate to add?", "options": {"A": "Berg Balance Scale repeated monthly, because its items directly sample turning in narrow spaces", "B": "Grip strength and five times sit to stand, because limb power drives freezing episodes", "C": "Timed Up and Go including a turn, repeated with a concurrent cognitive task", "D": "Ten metre walk test at comfortable speed, because gait velocity captures the turning and doorway demands"}, "cluster_id": "CHS195", "cluster_text": "Arvind Nair, 71, retired civil engineer, lives with his wife in a two storey home. Parkinson disease diagnosed six years ago, Hoehn and Yahr stage 3. He takes levodopa four times daily and notices wearing off by late afternoon. He reports two falls in three months, both while turning in narrow spaces, and freezing at doorways. Lying blood pressure 130/80 mmHg, standing at three minutes 106/68 mmHg with mild light headedness. Berg Balance Scale 47/56. Comfortable 10 metre walk speed 0.9 m/s with shuffling steps and reduced arm swing. Pull test: two steps backward before recovery. He is keen to exercise and has brought a printout of a small trial promoting a smartphone rhythmic cueing app, asking whether clinical guidelines support it. Seen in the outpatient clinic about an hour after his morning medication.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Timed Up and Go including a turn, repeated with a concurrent cognitive task): A turn built into the Timed Up and Go, with a dual task added, loads the situations in which people with Parkinson disease freeze and fall. His Berg score is already high and does not sample freezing, and straight line walking speed misses the turning problem. Strength tests do not explain freezing, which is a gait and attentional problem."}, {"id": 1738, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "On standing, Mr Nair's blood pressure falls from 130/80 to 106/68 mmHg at three minutes with mild light headedness, and he has had falls. He is due to start a balance circuit this session. What is the most appropriate physiotherapy response?", "options": {"A": "Use staged position changes, begin supported, and notify the GP of the symptomatic drop", "B": "Proceed with the circuit, ask him to stop if unsteady, and recheck pressure next visit", "C": "Cancel exercise today and send him to the emergency department for urgent cardiac assessment", "D": "Teach him to drink more fluid before sessions and defer reporting until symptoms occur at home"}, "cluster_id": "CHS195", "cluster_text": "Arvind Nair, 71, retired civil engineer, lives with his wife in a two storey home. Parkinson disease diagnosed six years ago, Hoehn and Yahr stage 3. He takes levodopa four times daily and notices wearing off by late afternoon. He reports two falls in three months, both while turning in narrow spaces, and freezing at doorways. Lying blood pressure 130/80 mmHg, standing at three minutes 106/68 mmHg with mild light headedness. Berg Balance Scale 47/56. Comfortable 10 metre walk speed 0.9 m/s with shuffling steps and reduced arm swing. Pull test: two steps backward before recovery. He is keen to exercise and has brought a printout of a small trial promoting a smartphone rhythmic cueing app, asking whether clinical guidelines support it. Seen in the outpatient clinic about an hour after his morning medication.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use staged position changes, begin supported, and notify the GP of the symptomatic drop): A symptomatic postural fall in pressure raises falls risk and needs medical review, since medication and autonomic features are medical matters. Meanwhile exercise can start in a supported way with slow transitions. Sending him to the emergency department is excessive for a stable, mildly symptomatic patient, and proceeding unchanged ignores a finding that may explain his falls."}, {"id": 1739, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "During assessment Mr Nair stops at the clinic doorway with his feet seeming stuck to the floor, and nearly falls forward when he tries to push through. Which strategy should the physiotherapist teach him for restarting walking during a freezing episode?", "options": {"A": "Shift weight side to side, then step over a floor line or follow a count", "B": "Lean forward and push through quickly, using body momentum to overcome the stuck feet", "C": "Stand still with feet together and wait for the freeze to settle before moving", "D": "Pivot on the spot with small shuffling steps to turn away from the doorway"}, "cluster_id": "CHS195", "cluster_text": "Arvind Nair, 71, retired civil engineer, lives with his wife in a two storey home. Parkinson disease diagnosed six years ago, Hoehn and Yahr stage 3. He takes levodopa four times daily and notices wearing off by late afternoon. He reports two falls in three months, both while turning in narrow spaces, and freezing at doorways. Lying blood pressure 130/80 mmHg, standing at three minutes 106/68 mmHg with mild light headedness. Berg Balance Scale 47/56. Comfortable 10 metre walk speed 0.9 m/s with shuffling steps and reduced arm swing. Pull test: two steps backward before recovery. He is keen to exercise and has brought a printout of a small trial promoting a smartphone rhythmic cueing app, asking whether clinical guidelines support it. Seen in the outpatient clinic about an hour after his morning medication.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Shift weight side to side, then step over a floor line or follow a count): Weight shift followed by an external visual or auditory cue is a standard way to restart stepping in freezing. Pushing forward with momentum increases the risk of a forward fall, and tight pivoting on the spot tends to provoke further freezing. Waiting with the feet together prolongs the episode and offers no trigger to restart."}, {"id": 1740, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Nair brings a small trial of a cueing app showing improved walking speed over four weeks. The authors are app developers and there was no control group. How should the physiotherapist best use this study when advising him about the app today?", "options": {"A": "Apply the findings only after obtaining the full dataset from the authors for independent reanalysis", "B": "Explain its limits, consult guideline recommendations on cueing, and trial the app with monitoring", "C": "Dismiss the study and advise against cueing, since evidence from industry funded trials cannot guide practice", "D": "Recommend the app confidently because any improvement in walking speed in a trial supports clinical use"}, "cluster_id": "CHS195", "cluster_text": "Arvind Nair, 71, retired civil engineer, lives with his wife in a two storey home. Parkinson disease diagnosed six years ago, Hoehn and Yahr stage 3. He takes levodopa four times daily and notices wearing off by late afternoon. He reports two falls in three months, both while turning in narrow spaces, and freezing at doorways. Lying blood pressure 130/80 mmHg, standing at three minutes 106/68 mmHg with mild light headedness. Berg Balance Scale 47/56. Comfortable 10 metre walk speed 0.9 m/s with shuffling steps and reduced arm swing. Pull test: two steps backward before recovery. He is keen to exercise and has brought a printout of a small trial promoting a smartphone rhythmic cueing app, asking whether clinical guidelines support it. Seen in the outpatient clinic about an hour after his morning medication.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Explain its limits, consult guideline recommendations on cueing, and trial the app with monitoring): A small uncontrolled study by the developers is weak evidence, so it should be weighed against clinical guidelines and higher quality trials on cueing, which already support external cues in Parkinson disease. A monitored trial respects his preferences and keeps him safe. Overstating the trial or rejecting it outright are both poor appraisal, and asking for raw data is not a realistic step for a clinician."}, {"id": 1741, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Mbeki scores 8 out of 12 on the Westmead PTA Scale and cannot retain new information from one session to the next. Which statement correctly describes how this should shape the way the physiotherapist structures his therapy during this phase?", "options": {"A": "Introduce new compensatory strategies each session, with written reminders for him to check", "B": "Limit therapy to bedside rest and positioning until PTA resolves, as agitation limits participation", "C": "Use short, familiar, repeated routines with errorless practice and few new instructions", "D": "Use frequent orientation prompts during tasks, and gently correct wrong answers about date and place"}, "cluster_id": "CHS196", "cluster_text": "Mr Craig Mbeki, 41, cabinet maker, fell from a ladder 5 weeks ago (diffuse axonal injury, right frontal contusion). Now in subacute rehabilitation. Westmead PTA Scale 8 out of 12 today: disoriented to time and place and unable to retain new information beyond minutes. Restless and agitated, mostly late afternoon, calls out and has swung his arm at staff when startled. Walks with supervision of one but is impulsive and has no awareness of risks. Fatigues quickly and sleeps poorly. Strength is good (4+/5 all limbs) with mild left ataxia on finger to nose. During sessions he has begun calling you 'darling', tried to hold your hand twice, and today leaned to kiss your cheek. His wife visits each evening.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use short, familiar, repeated routines with errorless practice and few new instructions): During post traumatic amnesia new learning is very limited, so therapy works best with short, familiar, repeated routines, errorless practice and few new instructions. Introducing new strategies or reminders depends on memory he does not have. Restricting him to bedside rest removes useful activity, and correcting wrong answers about orientation tends to raise distress without improving recall."}, {"id": 1742, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Mbeki becomes restless and agitated at 4 pm during a corridor session, shouting and trying to push past you. There is no immediate danger. The corridor is noisy and busy with visitors and he slept poorly last night. Which response by the physiotherapist is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Physically restrain his arms until he calms, then reorientate him to date and place", "B": "Reduce stimulation, move him to a quiet space, and redirect to a familiar task", "C": "End the session and leave him resting alone in his room until the agitation has settled", "D": "Firmly tell him to stop, explain the ward rules, and continue with a second staff member"}, "cluster_id": "CHS196", "cluster_text": "Mr Craig Mbeki, 41, cabinet maker, fell from a ladder 5 weeks ago (diffuse axonal injury, right frontal contusion). Now in subacute rehabilitation. Westmead PTA Scale 8 out of 12 today: disoriented to time and place and unable to retain new information beyond minutes. Restless and agitated, mostly late afternoon, calls out and has swung his arm at staff when startled. Walks with supervision of one but is impulsive and has no awareness of risks. Fatigues quickly and sleeps poorly. Strength is good (4+/5 all limbs) with mild left ataxia on finger to nose. During sessions he has begun calling you 'darling', tried to hold your hand twice, and today leaned to kiss your cheek. His wife visits each evening.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reduce stimulation, move him to a quiet space, and redirect to a familiar task): Agitation in PTA is usually driven by confusion and overstimulation, so lowering noise and demands and offering a familiar activity is the first line approach. Arguing or enforcing rules relies on reasoning he cannot use. Physical restraint is a last resort for danger, and leaving an impulsive patient alone creates a safety risk."}, {"id": 1743, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "With supervision of one, Mr Mbeki is asked to walk to the gym. He is impulsive, shows mild left ataxia and cannot retain safety instructions between walks. He insists he can walk alone. What is the most appropriate way to progress his mobility practice?", "options": {"A": "Allow independent walking in the corridor, as his strength is good and the route is short", "B": "Stop walking practice and use seated balance exercises until PTA resolves because of falls risk", "C": "Keep supervised walking on a consistent route, increasing difficulty as safety and memory improve", "D": "Add a dual task such as counting backwards while walking to train attention and balance together"}, "cluster_id": "CHS196", "cluster_text": "Mr Craig Mbeki, 41, cabinet maker, fell from a ladder 5 weeks ago (diffuse axonal injury, right frontal contusion). Now in subacute rehabilitation. Westmead PTA Scale 8 out of 12 today: disoriented to time and place and unable to retain new information beyond minutes. Restless and agitated, mostly late afternoon, calls out and has swung his arm at staff when startled. Walks with supervision of one but is impulsive and has no awareness of risks. Fatigues quickly and sleeps poorly. Strength is good (4+/5 all limbs) with mild left ataxia on finger to nose. During sessions he has begun calling you 'darling', tried to hold your hand twice, and today leaned to kiss your cheek. His wife visits each evening.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Keep supervised walking on a consistent route, increasing difficulty as safety and memory improve): Strength is not the limit here: impulsivity and absent safety awareness mean supervision must continue, and a consistent route supports learning through repetition. Independent walking is premature, and adding a dual task overloads an injured attention system. Withdrawing walking practice removes beneficial activity that can be done safely with supervision."}, {"id": 1744, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Mbeki has again called you 'darling', held your hand and tried to kiss your cheek today, saying you are his girlfriend. He is in PTA and disinhibited. Therapy sessions for him are still clinically indicated. What is the most appropriate professional response?", "options": {"A": "Ignore the behaviour as an expected effect of PTA, say nothing, and continue the session", "B": "Tell his wife at her evening visit and ask her to discuss the behaviour with him", "C": "Stop treating him until PTA resolves and ask the manager to reassign him to another therapist", "D": "Calmly state the limit, continue treating, document the behaviour, and inform the team"}, "cluster_id": "CHS196", "cluster_text": "Mr Craig Mbeki, 41, cabinet maker, fell from a ladder 5 weeks ago (diffuse axonal injury, right frontal contusion). Now in subacute rehabilitation. Westmead PTA Scale 8 out of 12 today: disoriented to time and place and unable to retain new information beyond minutes. Restless and agitated, mostly late afternoon, calls out and has swung his arm at staff when startled. Walks with supervision of one but is impulsive and has no awareness of risks. Fatigues quickly and sleeps poorly. Strength is good (4+/5 all limbs) with mild left ataxia on finger to nose. During sessions he has begun calling you 'darling', tried to hold your hand twice, and today leaned to kiss your cheek. His wife visits each evening.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Calmly state the limit, continue treating, document the behaviour, and inform the team): Disinhibition is part of the injury, so the response is to set a clear, kind boundary, keep providing care, and record and share the behaviour so the whole team responds consistently. Ignoring it fails to protect the therapist or teach appropriate limits, and withdrawing care breaches duty of care. Discussing it with his wife raises privacy concerns and does not manage the behaviour in sessions."}, {"id": 1745, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Bernadette has multiple rib fractures, a weak cough and a history of COPD. The physiotherapist explains to the ward team why chest physiotherapy is a priority. Which complication are older patients with rib fractures most at risk of, because pain restricts their breathing and coughing?", "options": {"A": "Atelectasis and pneumonia, from shallow breathing and poor secretion clearance after fracture", "B": "Pulmonary embolism from reduced mobility, which is the leading respiratory risk after rib fracture", "C": "Delayed pneumothorax from a rib end moving during breathing, which is the main concern", "D": "Chest wall stiffness and restricted expansion, from prolonged splinting of the injured side"}, "cluster_id": "CHS197", "cluster_text": "Bernadette Walker, 79, fell down three steps at home yesterday and has fractures of left ribs 5 to 8 on chest X-ray, with no flail segment and no pneumothorax. She is an ex-smoker with mild COPD (inhaled bronchodilator) and takes regular paracetamol and an opioid. No regional analgesia is in place. Pain is 4/10 at rest and 7/10 on deep breathing or coughing. She is sitting semi-reclined, taking shallow breaths and splinting her left side. RR 22, SpO2 93 percent on 2 L/min nasal prongs, HR 96, BP 142/80 mmHg. Auscultation shows reduced air entry at the left base and fine crackles at the right base. Her cough is weak and non-productive. She lives alone and was independent before the fall. The physiotherapist is working on the ward alongside the nurse in charge.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Atelectasis and pneumonia, from shallow breathing and poor secretion clearance after fracture): Pain causes shallow breathing and a weak cough, so the lung bases collapse and secretions are retained, and atelectasis with pneumonia follows. Pulmonary embolism is a risk of immobility but not the result of painful breathing. Pneumothorax relates to the fracture itself rather than restricted coughing, and chest wall stiffness is a later issue rather than the main early complication."}, {"id": 1746, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Bernadette received her scheduled analgesia 30 minutes ago and rates pain at 4/10 at rest. She is sitting upright in a chair with the ward nurse present. The physiotherapist begins a treatment session. Which technique is most appropriate to improve her lung expansion and cough?", "options": {"A": "Start slow, prolonged maximal inspiration with long breath holds until the collapsed lung base reinflates", "B": "Coach deep breaths with a short hold, then a huff while hugging a pillow", "C": "Use manual percussion and vibration over the fractured ribs to loosen secretions before she coughs", "D": "Apply firm elastic strapping around the chest wall to splint the fractures and make coughing easier"}, "cluster_id": "CHS197", "cluster_text": "Bernadette Walker, 79, fell down three steps at home yesterday and has fractures of left ribs 5 to 8 on chest X-ray, with no flail segment and no pneumothorax. She is an ex-smoker with mild COPD (inhaled bronchodilator) and takes regular paracetamol and an opioid. No regional analgesia is in place. Pain is 4/10 at rest and 7/10 on deep breathing or coughing. She is sitting semi-reclined, taking shallow breaths and splinting her left side. RR 22, SpO2 93 percent on 2 L/min nasal prongs, HR 96, BP 142/80 mmHg. Auscultation shows reduced air entry at the left base and fine crackles at the right base. Her cough is weak and non-productive. She lives alone and was independent before the fall. The physiotherapist is working on the ward alongside the nurse in charge.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Coach deep breaths with a short hold, then a huff while hugging a pillow): Timing treatment at peak analgesia, upright positioning and deep breaths with a supported huff expand the lung bases and clear secretions while controlling pain. Strapping restricts expansion and is outdated. Percussion and vibration over fractured ribs is painful and unsafe, and very long breath holds increase pain and fatigue without a clear benefit."}, {"id": 1747, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Four hours later Bernadette's pain on deep breathing is 7/10, and she avoids coughing during treatment. Her sputum is minimal but breath sounds are unchanged. Physiotherapy goals are limited by pain. What is the most appropriate action within the physiotherapist's scope of practice?", "options": {"A": "Ask the nurse to give an extra opioid dose from ward stock before the next treatment", "B": "Report pain limiting her breathing and request analgesia review by the medical or pain team", "C": "Defer chest physiotherapy until tomorrow, when pain is usually lower, and reassess her chest then", "D": "Substitute TENS over the fracture sites for medication review and continue the planned session"}, "cluster_id": "CHS197", "cluster_text": "Bernadette Walker, 79, fell down three steps at home yesterday and has fractures of left ribs 5 to 8 on chest X-ray, with no flail segment and no pneumothorax. She is an ex-smoker with mild COPD (inhaled bronchodilator) and takes regular paracetamol and an opioid. No regional analgesia is in place. Pain is 4/10 at rest and 7/10 on deep breathing or coughing. She is sitting semi-reclined, taking shallow breaths and splinting her left side. RR 22, SpO2 93 percent on 2 L/min nasal prongs, HR 96, BP 142/80 mmHg. Auscultation shows reduced air entry at the left base and fine crackles at the right base. Her cough is weak and non-productive. She lives alone and was independent before the fall. The physiotherapist is working on the ward alongside the nurse in charge.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report pain limiting her breathing and request analgesia review by the medical or pain team): Uncontrolled pain is the barrier to effective breathing and coughing, and changing analgesia is a medical or pain service decision, so the physiotherapist reports and requests review. Asking for an unprescribed dose is outside scope and bypasses the prescriber. Deferring treatment leaves her at risk of chest complications overnight, and substituting TENS for review does not address the underlying inadequate analgesia."}, {"id": 1748, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Later that afternoon Bernadette is confused and restless. RR is 30, SpO2 is 88 percent on 4 L/min, HR is 118 and she uses accessory muscles. A planned physiotherapy session is due. The nurse in charge is on another ward. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Stop treatment, keep her upright and activate the ward clinical deterioration escalation process", "B": "Sit her upright, increase oxygen per ward protocol and reassess in ten minutes before escalating", "C": "Stop treatment, keep her upright and leave a message for the nurse in charge to review", "D": "Start airway clearance with suction and positioning, then escalate if her saturation stays low"}, "cluster_id": "CHS197", "cluster_text": "Bernadette Walker, 79, fell down three steps at home yesterday and has fractures of left ribs 5 to 8 on chest X-ray, with no flail segment and no pneumothorax. She is an ex-smoker with mild COPD (inhaled bronchodilator) and takes regular paracetamol and an opioid. No regional analgesia is in place. Pain is 4/10 at rest and 7/10 on deep breathing or coughing. She is sitting semi-reclined, taking shallow breaths and splinting her left side. RR 22, SpO2 93 percent on 2 L/min nasal prongs, HR 96, BP 142/80 mmHg. Auscultation shows reduced air entry at the left base and fine crackles at the right base. Her cough is weak and non-productive. She lives alone and was independent before the fall. The physiotherapist is working on the ward alongside the nurse in charge.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stop treatment, keep her upright and activate the ward clinical deterioration escalation process): New confusion, a respiratory rate of 30, falling saturation despite more oxygen and accessory muscle use show acute deterioration, so treatment stops and the ward escalation process is activated at once. Increasing oxygen and waiting ten minutes delays medical review. Leaving a message for a nurse who is elsewhere does not summon help, and starting treatment first exposes a unwell patient to further risk."}, {"id": 1749, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "On finger to nose testing, Maria's hand overshoots the target and the tremor increases as she nears it, while her strength and sensation are intact and her rapid alternating hand movements are slow. Which interpretation of this finding is correct?", "options": {"A": "Resting tremor with rigidity, consistent with basal ganglia dysfunction affecting the amplitude of repeated movements", "B": "Intention tremor with dysmetria, consistent with cerebellar dysfunction affecting the coordination of voluntary limb movement", "C": "Postural tremor that settles with relaxation, consistent with essential tremor affecting posture and fine hand tasks", "D": "Sensory ataxia from dorsal column loss, consistent with reduced joint position sense in the hand"}, "cluster_id": "CHS198", "cluster_text": "Maria Papas, 74, has slowly progressive cerebellar ataxia diagnosed two years ago, and lives with her husband in a two storey house with the only bathroom upstairs. She speaks Greek and limited English. Her son usually attends and answers for her, but previous clinic notes record that he sometimes shortens her answers. She has fallen twice in three months, both times on the stairs at night. Gait is wide based and unsteady, finger to nose testing shows overshoot with tremor increasing near the target, and rapid alternating hand movements are slow and irregular. Strength is 4+/5 throughout and sensation is intact. The Berg Balance Scale score is 38 out of 56. Her goal is to walk to the local shops.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Intention tremor with dysmetria, consistent with cerebellar dysfunction affecting the coordination of voluntary limb movement): Overshoot of the target is dysmetria, and a tremor that grows as the target is approached is an intention tremor, both typical of cerebellar disease. A resting tremor with rigidity points to the basal ganglia, and sensory ataxia would come with impaired joint position sense, which is intact here."}, {"id": 1750, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Maria has a wide based gait, a Berg score of 38 out of 56, and two recent falls on stairs. The physiotherapist is planning her community exercise programme over the coming months to improve her walking safely. Which approach is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Home based strengthening of the legs with resistance bands, progressed as her strength improves", "B": "Supervised balance and gait training on functional tasks, progressed as her control improves", "C": "Standing balance drills on foam with eyes closed, progressed as her control improves", "D": "Prescribe a rollator and fit a second stair rail, with advice to limit walking to essentials"}, "cluster_id": "CHS198", "cluster_text": "Maria Papas, 74, has slowly progressive cerebellar ataxia diagnosed two years ago, and lives with her husband in a two storey house with the only bathroom upstairs. She speaks Greek and limited English. Her son usually attends and answers for her, but previous clinic notes record that he sometimes shortens her answers. She has fallen twice in three months, both times on the stairs at night. Gait is wide based and unsteady, finger to nose testing shows overshoot with tremor increasing near the target, and rapid alternating hand movements are slow and irregular. Strength is 4+/5 throughout and sensation is intact. The Berg Balance Scale score is 38 out of 56. Her goal is to walk to the local shops.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Supervised balance and gait training on functional tasks, progressed as her control improves): Supervised, task specific balance and gait training that is graded as control improves is supported for cerebellar ataxia and addresses her goal of walking to the shops safely. Her strength is already near normal, so strengthening alone does not train coordination. Eyes closed foam work removes vision and adds fall risk, and equipment with reduced walking does not build the control she needs."}, {"id": 1751, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Maria's two falls happened on the stairs at night, the only bathroom is upstairs, and she walks slowly with poor lighting on the landing and a stair rail on one side only. Which action should be the physiotherapist's priority to reduce her fall risk at home?", "options": {"A": "Teach her to walk slowly and concentrate harder on the stairs, since attention reduces falls", "B": "Refer for hip protectors and a personal alert pendant as the main strategy to limit injury", "C": "Advise moving the bed downstairs and restricting stair use before any further assessment is done", "D": "Arrange an occupational therapy home assessment, and trial rails, lighting and a commode downstairs"}, "cluster_id": "CHS198", "cluster_text": "Maria Papas, 74, has slowly progressive cerebellar ataxia diagnosed two years ago, and lives with her husband in a two storey house with the only bathroom upstairs. She speaks Greek and limited English. Her son usually attends and answers for her, but previous clinic notes record that he sometimes shortens her answers. She has fallen twice in three months, both times on the stairs at night. Gait is wide based and unsteady, finger to nose testing shows overshoot with tremor increasing near the target, and rapid alternating hand movements are slow and irregular. Strength is 4+/5 throughout and sensation is intact. The Berg Balance Scale score is 38 out of 56. Her goal is to walk to the local shops.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Arrange an occupational therapy home assessment, and trial rails, lighting and a commode downstairs): Falls here are linked to a specific environmental hazard, so a home assessment with practical changes such as rails, lighting and a downstairs toilet option addresses the cause. Restricting her without assessment limits independence, attention advice relies on a skill reduced by ataxia, and protection devices do not prevent falls."}, {"id": 1752, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "The physiotherapist needs Maria's informed agreement to a new balance programme at this appointment and wants to set goals with her. Her son offers to interpret again, though earlier notes show he sometimes shortens her answers. What should the physiotherapist do?", "options": {"A": "Provide written Greek handouts and go through them with a bilingual allied health assistant", "B": "Book a professional interpreter, explain their role, and address Maria directly, with her son welcome", "C": "Continue with her son interpreting because he knows her history, and document the family's preference", "D": "Use a phone translation app so the session starts promptly, as it is similar in accuracy"}, "cluster_id": "CHS198", "cluster_text": "Maria Papas, 74, has slowly progressive cerebellar ataxia diagnosed two years ago, and lives with her husband in a two storey house with the only bathroom upstairs. She speaks Greek and limited English. Her son usually attends and answers for her, but previous clinic notes record that he sometimes shortens her answers. She has fallen twice in three months, both times on the stairs at night. Gait is wide based and unsteady, finger to nose testing shows overshoot with tremor increasing near the target, and rapid alternating hand movements are slow and irregular. Strength is 4+/5 throughout and sensation is intact. The Berg Balance Scale score is 38 out of 56. Her goal is to walk to the local shops.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Book a professional interpreter, explain their role, and address Maria directly, with her son welcome): Consent and goal setting need accurate, complete communication, which a trained interpreter provides while keeping the conversation with Maria. Family members may filter or shorten information, apps are unreliable for clinical content, and an assistant is not a qualified interpreter. Her son can still attend to support her."}, {"id": 1753, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "On examination the left calf is mildly swollen and tender while the thigh and knee swelling is moderate, and her resting observations are stable. Which interpretation of these findings is the most appropriate for the physiotherapist to act on at this visit?", "options": {"A": "Early wound infection, so redress the incision and encourage fluids while monitoring temperature daily at home", "B": "Muscle strain from walking practice, so apply gentle calf stretching and heat before the next walk", "C": "Expected post-operative swelling, so continue exercises and advise elevation and ice.", "D": "Possible deep vein thrombosis, so stop calf loading and arrange urgent medical assessment today"}, "cluster_id": "CHS199", "cluster_text": "Margaret Hendry, 72, a retired bookkeeper, is day 9 after a left total knee replacement for osteoarthritis. She lives alone in a two-storey home in regional Victoria and receives home-based rehabilitation after discharge from a private hospital. History: type 2 diabetes, hypertension, BMI 33. Her surgeon prescribed an anticoagulant for clot prevention. Today: active knee flexion 75 degrees, extension lag 8 degrees, pain 4 out of 10 walking and 2 out of 10 at rest. The incision is closed with staples, with mild redness and no discharge. The left calf is mildly swollen and tender, and the thigh and knee swelling is moderate. Temperature 37.4 C, HR 88, BP 138/82, SpO2 96 percent on room air. She walks on level ground with a four-wheeled frame. Her bedroom is upstairs, and the staircase has one handrail. She bruises easily, and asks whether you could also adjust her blood-thinning tablets.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Possible deep vein thrombosis, so stop calf loading and arrange urgent medical assessment today): Calf swelling and tenderness, even with stable observations, in a person early after knee replacement who has diabetes and a high BMI raises the possibility of deep vein thrombosis and needs same-day medical assessment. Loading or massaging the calf could dislodge a clot. Attributing the findings to normal swelling is tempting because the thigh and knee are swollen, but the calf signs are the discriminating feature."}, {"id": 1754, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Medical review has excluded thrombosis and she may bear weight as tolerated. Margaret must use a staircase with a single handrail to reach her bedroom, and she is steady on level ground with her frame. Which technique should the physiotherapist teach and practise first for going up and down?", "options": {"A": "Use a reciprocal pattern, one foot per step, to restore normal gait mechanics early in recovery", "B": "Sit and shuffle up and down the stairs on her buttocks to avoid loading the knee", "C": "Operated leg leads going up and non-operated leg leads going down, holding the rail", "D": "Non-operated leg leads going up, operated leg leads going down, using the rail"}, "cluster_id": "CHS199", "cluster_text": "Margaret Hendry, 72, a retired bookkeeper, is day 9 after a left total knee replacement for osteoarthritis. She lives alone in a two-storey home in regional Victoria and receives home-based rehabilitation after discharge from a private hospital. History: type 2 diabetes, hypertension, BMI 33. Her surgeon prescribed an anticoagulant for clot prevention. Today: active knee flexion 75 degrees, extension lag 8 degrees, pain 4 out of 10 walking and 2 out of 10 at rest. The incision is closed with staples, with mild redness and no discharge. The left calf is mildly swollen and tender, and the thigh and knee swelling is moderate. Temperature 37.4 C, HR 88, BP 138/82, SpO2 96 percent on room air. She walks on level ground with a four-wheeled frame. Her bedroom is upstairs, and the staircase has one handrail. She bruises easily, and asks whether you could also adjust her blood-thinning tablets.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Non-operated leg leads going up, operated leg leads going down, using the rail): The stronger leg does the lifting on the way up and controls the descent with the operated leg lowering first, which is the safest pattern when quadriceps control is limited. A reciprocal pattern needs more strength and knee flexion than she has at this stage. Reversing the leading leg would demand the weak quadriceps to lift her body weight."}, {"id": 1755, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Margaret says she bruises easily and asks whether you could reduce her blood-thinning tablets, which the surgeon prescribed for clot prevention. The bruising is confined to the thigh and she has no bleeding elsewhere. What is the most appropriate response within the scope of physiotherapy practice?", "options": {"A": "Advise skipping alternate doses until bruising settles and monitor the thigh at each visit", "B": "Reduce exercise intensity and apply compression bandaging to limit bruising, managing it within physiotherapy care alone", "C": "Say medication changes are outside physiotherapy scope, document bruising, and report it to the prescriber", "D": "Tell her to ask her pharmacist for a lower dose and continue exercising while she waits"}, "cluster_id": "CHS199", "cluster_text": "Margaret Hendry, 72, a retired bookkeeper, is day 9 after a left total knee replacement for osteoarthritis. She lives alone in a two-storey home in regional Victoria and receives home-based rehabilitation after discharge from a private hospital. History: type 2 diabetes, hypertension, BMI 33. Her surgeon prescribed an anticoagulant for clot prevention. Today: active knee flexion 75 degrees, extension lag 8 degrees, pain 4 out of 10 walking and 2 out of 10 at rest. The incision is closed with staples, with mild redness and no discharge. The left calf is mildly swollen and tender, and the thigh and knee swelling is moderate. Temperature 37.4 C, HR 88, BP 138/82, SpO2 96 percent on room air. She walks on level ground with a four-wheeled frame. Her bedroom is upstairs, and the staircase has one handrail. She bruises easily, and asks whether you could also adjust her blood-thinning tablets.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Say medication changes are outside physiotherapy scope, document bruising, and report it to the prescriber): Altering anticoagulant doses is prescribing, which sits outside physiotherapy scope, so the physiotherapist should acknowledge the concern, record the findings and pass them to the surgeon or GP who prescribed it. Telling her to skip doses or seek a pharmacist dose change leaves the clot risk unmanaged. Compression applied without medical input could be unsafe given the earlier calf concern."}, {"id": 1756, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Margaret's active knee flexion is 75 degrees, the extension lag is 8 degrees and pain is controlled. The physiotherapist wants an exercise that specifically improves active terminal extension control at this stage of recovery. Which exercise is the most appropriate to prescribe in her home programme?", "options": {"A": "Heel slides on a smooth surface with overpressure at end range to gain flexion first", "B": "Stationary cycling with the seat set high to encourage knee extension on each revolution", "C": "Open-chain knee extension with a heavy ankle weight through full range to build quadriceps strength rapidly", "D": "Quadriceps setting with a towel under the heel, progressing to supported straight leg raises"}, "cluster_id": "CHS199", "cluster_text": "Margaret Hendry, 72, a retired bookkeeper, is day 9 after a left total knee replacement for osteoarthritis. She lives alone in a two-storey home in regional Victoria and receives home-based rehabilitation after discharge from a private hospital. History: type 2 diabetes, hypertension, BMI 33. Her surgeon prescribed an anticoagulant for clot prevention. Today: active knee flexion 75 degrees, extension lag 8 degrees, pain 4 out of 10 walking and 2 out of 10 at rest. The incision is closed with staples, with mild redness and no discharge. The left calf is mildly swollen and tender, and the thigh and knee swelling is moderate. Temperature 37.4 C, HR 88, BP 138/82, SpO2 96 percent on room air. She walks on level ground with a four-wheeled frame. Her bedroom is upstairs, and the staircase has one handrail. She bruises easily, and asks whether you could also adjust her blood-thinning tablets.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Quadriceps setting with a towel under the heel, progressing to supported straight leg raises): An extension lag reflects poor active quadriceps activation, so quadriceps setting and straight leg raises target it directly and safely at day 9. Heel slides address flexion, which is not the problem being treated. Cycling needs more knee flexion than 75 degrees, and heavily loaded open-chain work is premature this early after surgery."}, {"id": 1757, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Dimitri asks what the best-supported first-line treatments are for his knee osteoarthritis, given his age, body weight, and the findings on examination. Based on current clinical practice guidelines, which combination should the physiotherapist recommend as the core of his management plan?", "options": {"A": "Rest from loading activity, a knee brace, and glucosamine supplements, with arthroscopy considered if symptoms persist", "B": "Hyaluronic acid injection arranged through his GP, with activity pacing and avoidance of weight-bearing exercise", "C": "Education, exercise therapy and weight management, with pain medication options reviewed by his GP", "D": "Passive modalities such as ultrasound and electrotherapy, plus manual therapy, with exercise added after pain settles"}, "cluster_id": "CHS200", "cluster_text": "Dimitri Papadopoulos, 58, is a delivery driver attending a private practice with 18 months of bilateral knee pain, worse on the right. Pain is 6 out of 10 after a shift and 3 out of 10 at rest, with morning stiffness of about 15 minutes. BMI 31, no diabetes, no recent injury, no night pain, no systemic symptoms. Weight-bearing X-rays arranged by his GP show medial joint space narrowing on both sides. Examination of the right knee: flexion 120 degrees, extension 3 degrees short, mild effusion, crepitus, medial joint line tenderness, quadriceps strength 4 out of 5, ligaments stable. He walks 20 minutes before pain limits him and completes 9 repetitions in the 30 second sit-to-stand test. He has read online that rest protects cartilage and that glucosamine and arthroscopic clean-outs work, and he asks what the evidence says. He has limited time and wants short supervised sessions.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Education, exercise therapy and weight management, with pain medication options reviewed by his GP): Guidelines for knee osteoarthritis place education, exercise and weight management at the centre of care, with medicines discussed by the prescribing doctor. Rest, supplements, passive modalities and arthroscopy have weak or no support as primary treatments. Injections are not first-line, and avoiding weight-bearing exercise works against the benefits of a loading programme."}, {"id": 1758, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Dimitri starts a supervised exercise programme. After the first week he reports knee pain rising to 4 out of 10 during exercise that settles within an hour, with no increase in swelling or stiffness the next morning. How should the physiotherapist respond to this pattern?", "options": {"A": "Switch to non-weight-bearing exercise only, since pain during weight-bearing indicates ongoing joint damage needing protection", "B": "Continue the programme, explaining that mild temporary pain during exercise is acceptable, and monitor response", "C": "Increase the load and repetitions to push through the pain, which builds tolerance to joint loading", "D": "Stop exercise until the pain resolves, then restart at half the load so cartilage recovers"}, "cluster_id": "CHS200", "cluster_text": "Dimitri Papadopoulos, 58, is a delivery driver attending a private practice with 18 months of bilateral knee pain, worse on the right. Pain is 6 out of 10 after a shift and 3 out of 10 at rest, with morning stiffness of about 15 minutes. BMI 31, no diabetes, no recent injury, no night pain, no systemic symptoms. Weight-bearing X-rays arranged by his GP show medial joint space narrowing on both sides. Examination of the right knee: flexion 120 degrees, extension 3 degrees short, mild effusion, crepitus, medial joint line tenderness, quadriceps strength 4 out of 5, ligaments stable. He walks 20 minutes before pain limits him and completes 9 repetitions in the 30 second sit-to-stand test. He has read online that rest protects cartilage and that glucosamine and arthroscopic clean-outs work, and he asks what the evidence says. He has limited time and wants short supervised sessions.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Continue the programme, explaining that mild temporary pain during exercise is acceptable, and monitor response): A modest, short-lived rise in pain during exercise with no lasting flare is an acceptable response in knee osteoarthritis and does not signal harm. Stopping or avoiding weight-bearing reinforces fear and loses the benefit of exercise. Pushing into more load ignores the principle of monitoring the response over the following 24 hours."}, {"id": 1759, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "At the next session Dimitri reports a flare after a long shift: pain 7 out of 10 and a mild increase in effusion. There is no warmth, fever, trauma or locking, and he can still walk. What is the best adjustment to his management at this point?", "options": {"A": "Temporarily lower exercise dose, keep him moving with gentle activity, and plan an early review", "B": "Cease all exercise for two weeks and rest the knee in elevation to settle the effusion", "C": "Send him back to his GP that day for aspiration as increased effusion needs drainage", "D": "Continue the programme unchanged, since flares reflect deconditioning and further loading will strengthen the joint"}, "cluster_id": "CHS200", "cluster_text": "Dimitri Papadopoulos, 58, is a delivery driver attending a private practice with 18 months of bilateral knee pain, worse on the right. Pain is 6 out of 10 after a shift and 3 out of 10 at rest, with morning stiffness of about 15 minutes. BMI 31, no diabetes, no recent injury, no night pain, no systemic symptoms. Weight-bearing X-rays arranged by his GP show medial joint space narrowing on both sides. Examination of the right knee: flexion 120 degrees, extension 3 degrees short, mild effusion, crepitus, medial joint line tenderness, quadriceps strength 4 out of 5, ligaments stable. He walks 20 minutes before pain limits him and completes 9 repetitions in the 30 second sit-to-stand test. He has read online that rest protects cartilage and that glucosamine and arthroscopic clean-outs work, and he asks what the evidence says. He has limited time and wants short supervised sessions.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Temporarily lower exercise dose, keep him moving with gentle activity, and plan an early review): A flare without red flag features is managed by temporarily reducing load while staying active, then returning to the programme as symptoms allow. Prolonged rest deconditions the muscles that support the joint. Ignoring the flare risks aggravation, and urgent aspiration is reserved for a hot, acutely swollen joint or signs of infection, which are absent here."}, {"id": 1760, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Dimitri shows you an online article claiming a supplement reduced knee pain in a trial of 24 participants, with no control group, funded by the manufacturer. How should the physiotherapist best use this article when advising him about his treatment?", "options": {"A": "Dismiss the article since manufacturer funding makes the findings invalid, and advise him to stop searching", "B": "Accept the result because improved pain scores in a published trial justify recommending the supplement", "C": "Ask him to trial the supplement for a month and use his pain diary as proof", "D": "Explain that a small uncontrolled, industry-funded study is weak evidence, and refer to guideline advice"}, "cluster_id": "CHS200", "cluster_text": "Dimitri Papadopoulos, 58, is a delivery driver attending a private practice with 18 months of bilateral knee pain, worse on the right. Pain is 6 out of 10 after a shift and 3 out of 10 at rest, with morning stiffness of about 15 minutes. BMI 31, no diabetes, no recent injury, no night pain, no systemic symptoms. Weight-bearing X-rays arranged by his GP show medial joint space narrowing on both sides. Examination of the right knee: flexion 120 degrees, extension 3 degrees short, mild effusion, crepitus, medial joint line tenderness, quadriceps strength 4 out of 5, ligaments stable. He walks 20 minutes before pain limits him and completes 9 repetitions in the 30 second sit-to-stand test. He has read online that rest protects cartilage and that glucosamine and arthroscopic clean-outs work, and he asks what the evidence says. He has limited time and wants short supervised sessions.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain that a small uncontrolled, industry-funded study is weak evidence, and refer to guideline advice): A small trial without a control group cannot separate the supplement's effect from placebo or natural fluctuation, and industry funding adds risk of bias, so it is weak evidence to set beside guidelines built on systematic reviews. Declaring it invalid by funding alone is too blunt and discourages his engagement. A personal pain diary cannot show cause either."}, {"id": 1761, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Kouris has altered sensation across the ulnar side of his hand. The physiotherapist is deciding whether the nerve is compressed at the elbow or at the wrist, because the two sites differ in clinical features. Which finding most strongly supports compression at the elbow rather than the wrist?", "options": {"A": "Reduced sensation on the back of the hand, from a branch arising above the wrist", "B": "Tingling provoked by sustained wrist extension and pressure over the palm near the hook of hamate", "C": "Weakness of thumb opposition with wasting of the muscles at the base of the thumb", "D": "Clawing of the ring and little fingers with intact sensation over the back of the hand"}, "cluster_id": "CHS201", "cluster_text": "Mr Daniel Kouris, 44, cabinet maker, presents with eight weeks of tingling in the ring and little fingers of his right hand, worse at night and when holding his phone to his ear. No neck pain. Past history: nil. Medications: nil. Baseline outcome measures: QuickDASH 36 out of 100, pain 4/10 at worst. Observation: slight flattening of the first dorsal interosseous. The elbow flexion test reproduces paraesthesia within 40 seconds. Tinel's sign at the medial elbow is positive. Light touch sensation is reduced over the little finger, the ulnar half of the ring finger and the dorsal ulnar border of the hand. Finger abduction strength is 4/5 and grip strength is 20 percent lower than the left. Cervical screen is clear. The ulnar nerve neurodynamic test reproduces tingling that eases when the neck is side flexed toward the arm. Reflexes are normal. The clinic manager has asked all physiotherapists to improve how outcomes are recorded across the upper limb service.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reduced sensation on the back of the hand, from a branch arising above the wrist): The dorsal cutaneous branch of the ulnar nerve leaves above the wrist, so sensory change on the back of the hand points to a lesion proximal to the wrist, such as the cubital tunnel. A lesion in the wrist canal spares that area, which is why intact dorsal sensation fits the wrist site better. Thumb muscle wasting suggests the median nerve."}, {"id": 1762, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Kouris reports the tingling comes on within forty seconds of elbow flexion and settles when he straightens his arm. Pain is 4/10 at worst. He wants to begin neural mobilisation exercises. Which approach is most appropriate to start with at this stage?", "options": {"A": "Tensioner techniques held at end range until tingling is fully reproduced, to stretch the nerve bed", "B": "Rest in a rigid splint with the elbow flexed, avoiding movement until symptoms resolve", "C": "Sustained ulnar nerve tensioning in elbow flexion with wrist extension and shoulder abduction for several minutes", "D": "Gentle slider techniques within a symptom-free range, with advice to avoid sustained elbow flexion"}, "cluster_id": "CHS201", "cluster_text": "Mr Daniel Kouris, 44, cabinet maker, presents with eight weeks of tingling in the ring and little fingers of his right hand, worse at night and when holding his phone to his ear. No neck pain. Past history: nil. Medications: nil. Baseline outcome measures: QuickDASH 36 out of 100, pain 4/10 at worst. Observation: slight flattening of the first dorsal interosseous. The elbow flexion test reproduces paraesthesia within 40 seconds. Tinel's sign at the medial elbow is positive. Light touch sensation is reduced over the little finger, the ulnar half of the ring finger and the dorsal ulnar border of the hand. Finger abduction strength is 4/5 and grip strength is 20 percent lower than the left. Cervical screen is clear. The ulnar nerve neurodynamic test reproduces tingling that eases when the neck is side flexed toward the arm. Reflexes are normal. The clinic manager has asked all physiotherapists to improve how outcomes are recorded across the upper limb service.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Gentle slider techniques within a symptom-free range, with advice to avoid sustained elbow flexion): Sliders move the nerve through its bed with little increase in strain, which suits a reactive nerve that already produces symptoms within seconds of elbow flexion. Reducing time spent in the provoking position removes ongoing compression. Tensioners and sustained end-range loading raise strain on an irritable nerve, and a flexed splint holds the elbow in the aggravating posture."}, {"id": 1763, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "At review two weeks later Mr Kouris has dropped objects at work. Finger abduction is now 3/5, the ring finger rests slightly clawed, and the first dorsal interosseous is visibly wasted. Sensory symptoms are otherwise unchanged. What is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Continue the slider programme and add grip strengthening, then retest finger strength after four more weeks", "B": "Contact the GP promptly to request nerve conduction studies and a surgical opinion", "C": "Fit an elbow night splint and review his workstation ergonomics, then reassess.", "D": "Progress to tensioner techniques to increase nerve glide, as the motor loss suggests inadequate neural mobility"}, "cluster_id": "CHS201", "cluster_text": "Mr Daniel Kouris, 44, cabinet maker, presents with eight weeks of tingling in the ring and little fingers of his right hand, worse at night and when holding his phone to his ear. No neck pain. Past history: nil. Medications: nil. Baseline outcome measures: QuickDASH 36 out of 100, pain 4/10 at worst. Observation: slight flattening of the first dorsal interosseous. The elbow flexion test reproduces paraesthesia within 40 seconds. Tinel's sign at the medial elbow is positive. Light touch sensation is reduced over the little finger, the ulnar half of the ring finger and the dorsal ulnar border of the hand. Finger abduction strength is 4/5 and grip strength is 20 percent lower than the left. Cervical screen is clear. The ulnar nerve neurodynamic test reproduces tingling that eases when the neck is side flexed toward the arm. Reflexes are normal. The clinic manager has asked all physiotherapists to improve how outcomes are recorded across the upper limb service.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Contact the GP promptly to request nerve conduction studies and a surgical opinion): Progressive motor loss with wasting and clawing signals significant axonal compromise, and conservative care alone is no longer appropriate. Timely investigation and a surgical opinion protect nerve recovery. Adding load, progressing neurodynamics or waiting for another review delays escalation and risks permanent weakness."}, {"id": 1764, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "The clinic manager wants to audit whether upper limb patients like Mr Kouris improve with treatment. His baseline QuickDASH was 36 out of 100. Which approach to measurement would best support a meaningful audit of patient outcomes across the whole service?", "options": {"A": "Use grip dynamometry at each visit, because objective strength is preferable to patient reported scores", "B": "Record a measure only at discharge for each patient, then compare average scores between different clinicians", "C": "Record patient satisfaction ratings at discharge only, since satisfaction reflects the overall quality of care", "D": "Repeat the same validated questionnaire at set intervals, comparing change with a meaningful threshold"}, "cluster_id": "CHS201", "cluster_text": "Mr Daniel Kouris, 44, cabinet maker, presents with eight weeks of tingling in the ring and little fingers of his right hand, worse at night and when holding his phone to his ear. No neck pain. Past history: nil. Medications: nil. Baseline outcome measures: QuickDASH 36 out of 100, pain 4/10 at worst. Observation: slight flattening of the first dorsal interosseous. The elbow flexion test reproduces paraesthesia within 40 seconds. Tinel's sign at the medial elbow is positive. Light touch sensation is reduced over the little finger, the ulnar half of the ring finger and the dorsal ulnar border of the hand. Finger abduction strength is 4/5 and grip strength is 20 percent lower than the left. Cervical screen is clear. The ulnar nerve neurodynamic test reproduces tingling that eases when the neck is side flexed toward the arm. Reflexes are normal. The clinic manager has asked all physiotherapists to improve how outcomes are recorded across the upper limb service.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Repeat the same validated questionnaire at set intervals, comparing change with a meaningful threshold): An audit of improvement needs a baseline and repeat scores on the same validated tool at agreed time points, judged against a change large enough to matter to patients. Satisfaction does not show functional change, and a discharge-only score has no baseline to compare. Grip strength is useful but captures only one aspect of upper limb disability."}, {"id": 1765, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "During the six minute walk test Mr Santoro walked 180 m with two rests, rated breathlessness 5 on the Borg scale and desaturated to 85 percent, and he uses ventilation only three hours nightly. Which interpretation best guides your physiotherapy planning?", "options": {"A": "Hypoventilation and exertional hypoxaemia limit him, so ventilator use and oxygen needs warrant medical review", "B": "Fluid retention in his ankles is the main limiter, so restriction advice is the first priority", "C": "Desaturation to 85 percent on exertion is expected with obesity, so exercise prescription can proceed", "D": "Low fitness alone explains the desaturation, so progressive walking will correct it without further medical input"}, "cluster_id": "CHS202", "cluster_text": "Mr Marco Santoro, 71, a retired baker, has a BMI of 46 and lives with his wife in a two storey home. A sleep study led to a diagnosis of obesity hypoventilation syndrome, and he started overnight bilevel ventilation six weeks ago but uses it about three hours a night. He reports morning headaches, daytime sleepiness and breathlessness walking to the letterbox. His GP referred him to community physiotherapy. Findings: resting SpO2 91 percent on room air, HR 86, RR 20, BP 146/90, bilateral ankle pitting oedema. Six minute walk test: 180 m with two rests, Borg breathlessness 5, SpO2 fell to 85 percent. He takes a diuretic and an ACE inhibitor. No home oxygen is prescribed. The clinic has an allied health assistant who can run walking sessions twice weekly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hypoventilation and exertional hypoxaemia limit him, so ventilator use and oxygen needs warrant medical review): A significant fall in saturation on exertion in someone with obesity hypoventilation and poor ventilator adherence suggests ventilation and oxygenation problems that the treating medical team needs to know about. Treating it as simple deconditioning or as normal for obesity misses the safety issue. Oedema is present but does not explain the desaturation."}, {"id": 1766, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Santoro has a resting saturation of 91 percent, no home oxygen, and reaches Borg 5 within two minutes of continuous walking. He wants to walk to the local shops. Which initial walking prescription is most appropriate and safe for him at present?", "options": {"A": "Short interval walking with rests, monitoring saturation and breathlessness against limits set with his doctor", "B": "Continuous moderate walking for thirty minutes daily to promote weight loss, guided by perceived exertion only", "C": "High intensity interval cycling to build ventilatory capacity quickly, with saturation checked after each session ends", "D": "Upper limb resistance exercises only, because walking is unsafe until home oxygen has been prescribed"}, "cluster_id": "CHS202", "cluster_text": "Mr Marco Santoro, 71, a retired baker, has a BMI of 46 and lives with his wife in a two storey home. A sleep study led to a diagnosis of obesity hypoventilation syndrome, and he started overnight bilevel ventilation six weeks ago but uses it about three hours a night. He reports morning headaches, daytime sleepiness and breathlessness walking to the letterbox. His GP referred him to community physiotherapy. Findings: resting SpO2 91 percent on room air, HR 86, RR 20, BP 146/90, bilateral ankle pitting oedema. Six minute walk test: 180 m with two rests, Borg breathlessness 5, SpO2 fell to 85 percent. He takes a diuretic and an ACE inhibitor. No home oxygen is prescribed. The clinic has an allied health assistant who can run walking sessions twice weekly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Short interval walking with rests, monitoring saturation and breathlessness against limits set with his doctor): Because he becomes breathless quickly and may desaturate, short bouts with rests and objective monitoring against agreed limits allow safe progression. Long continuous walking or high intensity work exceeds his current tolerance. Withholding all walking is unnecessary when it can be monitored and limits agreed with the doctor."}, {"id": 1767, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "At the next visit Mr Santoro has gained 3 kg in a week, has more ankle swelling, needs three pillows to sleep and has a resting saturation of 88 percent with a heart rate of 104. What should you do about today's session?", "options": {"A": "Reduce to seated limb exercises with saturation monitoring and report the weight gain at review", "B": "Withhold exercise today and contact his GP the same day, reporting weight, oedema and saturation", "C": "Check his bilevel ventilator use history and encourage longer nightly use, then continue the planned session", "D": "Give a short supervised walking session with oxygen from the clinic, and notify his GP afterwards"}, "cluster_id": "CHS202", "cluster_text": "Mr Marco Santoro, 71, a retired baker, has a BMI of 46 and lives with his wife in a two storey home. A sleep study led to a diagnosis of obesity hypoventilation syndrome, and he started overnight bilevel ventilation six weeks ago but uses it about three hours a night. He reports morning headaches, daytime sleepiness and breathlessness walking to the letterbox. His GP referred him to community physiotherapy. Findings: resting SpO2 91 percent on room air, HR 86, RR 20, BP 146/90, bilateral ankle pitting oedema. Six minute walk test: 180 m with two rests, Borg breathlessness 5, SpO2 fell to 85 percent. He takes a diuretic and an ACE inhibitor. No home oxygen is prescribed. The clinic has an allied health assistant who can run walking sessions twice weekly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Withhold exercise today and contact his GP the same day, reporting weight, oedema and saturation): Rapid weight gain, more oedema, orthopnoea and a resting saturation of 88 percent with a faster heart rate point to deterioration, possibly fluid overload or cardiac decompensation, which needs same day medical review. Seated exercise is gentler but still treats a possible acute problem as routine and delays the report. Discussing ventilator adherence is useful at some point but does not address today's findings. Supervised walking, even with oxygen and a later phone call, loads a patient who may be decompensating and oxygen has not been prescribed."}, {"id": 1768, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "The clinic's allied health assistant, who has not used oximetry here, offers to supervise Mr Santoro's walking sessions twice weekly. You have written the program. Given his exertional desaturation, which approach to delegation is most appropriate for this client at present?", "options": {"A": "Give a verbal handover only, because responsibility transfers to the assistant once the tasks are accepted", "B": "Delegate the whole program including progression, since the assistant has experience with other respiratory clients", "C": "Provide written instructions with stop limits, confirm oximetry competence, and keep reviewing him yourself regularly", "D": "Delegate sessions only after the assistant repeats his six minute walk test to set new targets"}, "cluster_id": "CHS202", "cluster_text": "Mr Marco Santoro, 71, a retired baker, has a BMI of 46 and lives with his wife in a two storey home. A sleep study led to a diagnosis of obesity hypoventilation syndrome, and he started overnight bilevel ventilation six weeks ago but uses it about three hours a night. He reports morning headaches, daytime sleepiness and breathlessness walking to the letterbox. His GP referred him to community physiotherapy. Findings: resting SpO2 91 percent on room air, HR 86, RR 20, BP 146/90, bilateral ankle pitting oedema. Six minute walk test: 180 m with two rests, Borg breathlessness 5, SpO2 fell to 85 percent. He takes a diuretic and an ACE inhibitor. No home oxygen is prescribed. The clinic has an allied health assistant who can run walking sessions twice weekly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Provide written instructions with stop limits, confirm oximetry competence, and keep reviewing him yourself regularly): The physiotherapist remains accountable for delegated care, so clear individual instructions, confirmed competence for monitoring and ongoing review are needed, especially with a patient who may desaturate. Progression and reassessment need clinical reasoning that stays with the physiotherapist. Responsibility does not transfer with the task."}, {"id": 1769, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Dimitra agrees to sit out and walk a few steps after her analgesia. The drain system must travel with her, and a nurse is available to help carry it. Which approach to managing the drain system during the transfer and walk is correct?", "options": {"A": "Keep the bottle upright below chest level, leave tubing unclamped, and keep all connections secure", "B": "Hold the bottle at chest level to improve drainage and reduce tension on the tubing", "C": "Clamp the tubing near the chest wall while she stands, then release it once she sits", "D": "Disconnect the tubing from the bottle during the transfer to reduce weight, reconnecting once seated"}, "cluster_id": "CHS203", "cluster_text": "Dimitra Papadakis, 79, retired seamstress, is on day one after video assisted right upper lobectomy for lung cancer. She has a single intercostal chest drain to an underwater seal drainage system, on low wall suction. History: mild hypertension, independent and living alone before admission, no cognitive concerns. Observations: RR 20, HR 88, BP 130/76, SpO2 94 percent on 2 L/min. Pain is 6 out of 10 on coughing, managed with regular oral analgesia. Drain: fluid in the tubing swings with breathing, 120 mL serous drainage overnight, and a small intermittent bubble appears in the water seal chamber on coughing. Dimitra says she is exhausted and does not want to get out of bed today. Her daughter, Anna, insists the physiotherapist should just get her up.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Keep the bottle upright below chest level, leave tubing unclamped, and keep all connections secure): The drainage bottle stays upright and below the level of the chest so fluid and air drain out and cannot flow back, tubing is left open so air can escape, and the closed system is kept intact. Clamping can cause a tension pneumothorax if air is still leaking. Raising the bottle risks reflux, and disconnecting breaks the sealed system."}, {"id": 1770, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "While walking, Dimitra becomes breathless and SpO2 falls to 88 percent. The water seal chamber shows continuous bubbling, and the physiotherapist finds the tubing has pulled away from the drainage bottle. A nurse is in the corridor. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Lower the bottle to the floor and coach deep breathing until bubbling and desaturation settle", "B": "Clamp the tubing near the chest wall to stop the air leak, then recheck saturation", "C": "Walk her slowly to a chair, as bubbling is expected after lobectomy and saturation will recover", "D": "Sit her down, call urgently, and reconnect the tubing or submerge its end in sterile water"}, "cluster_id": "CHS203", "cluster_text": "Dimitra Papadakis, 79, retired seamstress, is on day one after video assisted right upper lobectomy for lung cancer. She has a single intercostal chest drain to an underwater seal drainage system, on low wall suction. History: mild hypertension, independent and living alone before admission, no cognitive concerns. Observations: RR 20, HR 88, BP 130/76, SpO2 94 percent on 2 L/min. Pain is 6 out of 10 on coughing, managed with regular oral analgesia. Drain: fluid in the tubing swings with breathing, 120 mL serous drainage overnight, and a small intermittent bubble appears in the water seal chamber on coughing. Dimitra says she is exhausted and does not want to get out of bed today. Her daughter, Anna, insists the physiotherapist should just get her up.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Sit her down, call urgently, and reconnect the tubing or submerge its end in sterile water): A disconnected drain with breathlessness and desaturation is an emergency in which air can enter the pleural space, so she is seated, help is called, and the system is restored or the tube end is placed in sterile water to keep a seal. Clamping can trap air under pressure. Waiting or relying on breathing exercises ignores the breach in the system."}, {"id": 1771, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Dimitra's cough is weak because of incisional pain, and secretions are accumulating. Her analgesia was last given three hours ago and is due now. She is willing to try airway clearance, and the drain site is on the right side. Which approach is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Use manual percussion over the operated side to loosen secretions, then ask her to cough forcefully", "B": "Time treatment after analgesia and support the wound and drain site with a towel while huffing", "C": "Begin with deep inspiratory holds and forceful coughing before analgesia takes effect, to clear secretions early", "D": "Avoid coughing and use gentle breathing exercises only, since coughing risks increasing the air leak"}, "cluster_id": "CHS203", "cluster_text": "Dimitra Papadakis, 79, retired seamstress, is on day one after video assisted right upper lobectomy for lung cancer. She has a single intercostal chest drain to an underwater seal drainage system, on low wall suction. History: mild hypertension, independent and living alone before admission, no cognitive concerns. Observations: RR 20, HR 88, BP 130/76, SpO2 94 percent on 2 L/min. Pain is 6 out of 10 on coughing, managed with regular oral analgesia. Drain: fluid in the tubing swings with breathing, 120 mL serous drainage overnight, and a small intermittent bubble appears in the water seal chamber on coughing. Dimitra says she is exhausted and does not want to get out of bed today. Her daughter, Anna, insists the physiotherapist should just get her up.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Time treatment after analgesia and support the wound and drain site with a towel while huffing): Pain limits effective clearance, so treatment follows analgesia and the incision and drain site are supported to make huffing and coughing tolerable and effective. Percussion over a fresh surgical site and drain is inappropriate. Avoiding coughing leaves secretions in place, and forceful efforts before analgesia work are painful and ineffective."}, {"id": 1772, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Dimitra clearly states she is too tired to get up and wants to rest today. She can explain in her own words that staying in bed may increase her risk of chest complications. Anna says her mother is confused and insists the physiotherapist proceed. What is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Record the refusal and cease physiotherapy until the surgical team has confirmed her capacity in writing", "B": "Proceed with mobilisation as Anna requests, because the daughter is the closest relative and can consent", "C": "Respect her informed refusal, offer bedside exercises as an alternative, document it, and review later", "D": "Request a formal capacity assessment by a geriatrician before any further physiotherapy decisions are made"}, "cluster_id": "CHS203", "cluster_text": "Dimitra Papadakis, 79, retired seamstress, is on day one after video assisted right upper lobectomy for lung cancer. She has a single intercostal chest drain to an underwater seal drainage system, on low wall suction. History: mild hypertension, independent and living alone before admission, no cognitive concerns. Observations: RR 20, HR 88, BP 130/76, SpO2 94 percent on 2 L/min. Pain is 6 out of 10 on coughing, managed with regular oral analgesia. Drain: fluid in the tubing swings with breathing, 120 mL serous drainage overnight, and a small intermittent bubble appears in the water seal chamber on coughing. Dimitra says she is exhausted and does not want to get out of bed today. Her daughter, Anna, insists the physiotherapist should just get her up.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Respect her informed refusal, offer bedside exercises as an alternative, document it, and review later): Adults are presumed to have capacity, and she shows understanding of the risks and a clear choice, so her informed refusal stands even though a relative disagrees. Offering an alternative and reviewing later keeps her care going. A family member cannot consent on behalf of a capable adult, and a formal assessment or stopping treatment is not warranted without genuine doubt about capacity."}, {"id": 1773, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Viliami has diffuse wheeze and has been prescribed hypertonic saline to inhale before his airway clearance session. Hypertonic saline can provoke bronchospasm in people with cystic fibrosis. What is the most appropriate preparation and monitoring around this nebuliser in the clinic?", "options": {"A": "Complete a full huffing session first so the saline can reach cleared airways more evenly", "B": "Use a prescribed bronchodilator beforehand, and watch for chest tightness, wheeze or cough during the nebuliser", "C": "Give the saline after airway clearance instead of before, to limit irritation of already loosened airways", "D": "Monitor saturation and heart rate throughout, and give the nebuliser unchanged since wheeze is his baseline"}, "cluster_id": "CHS204", "cluster_text": "Viliami Tuipulotu, 27, is a warehouse supervisor with cystic fibrosis (pancreatic insufficient, chronically colonised with Pseudomonas aeruginosa). He attends the outpatient CF clinic after a week of increased cough and thick green sputum. The CF physician has started antibiotics. He performs 20 minutes of the active cycle of breathing technique each morning and reports tiring, wanting something he can do at work. No positive expiratory pressure (PEP) device is used. Observations: SpO2 94 percent on room air, RR 18, HR 84, afebrile. Chest: coarse upper zone crackles and diffuse wheeze. FEV1 is 55 percent of predicted. Hypertonic saline nebulisation has been prescribed before airway clearance. The clinic manager proposes that the allied health assistant (AHA), who has two years in the clinic, teach and review any new device at weekly visits.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use a prescribed bronchodilator beforehand, and watch for chest tightness, wheeze or cough during the nebuliser): Hypertonic saline draws water into the airway and can trigger bronchospasm, so a prescribed bronchodilator beforehand and close observation of symptoms during the dose protect him, particularly with wheeze already present. Clearing the airways first removes the point of the saline, which is to loosen secretions before clearance. Giving it afterwards reverses the prescribed sequence and loses that benefit. Monitoring saturation alone does not prevent or detect bronchospasm early, and existing wheeze raises rather than lowers the risk."}, {"id": 1774, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Viliami tires during twenty minutes of active cycle of breathing and wants a technique he can do alone at work. He has thick sputum, wheeze and is chronically colonised with Pseudomonas. Which airway clearance option is most suitable to trial first?", "options": {"A": "Modified postural drainage with huffing, which uses gravity and is less tiring than the breathing cycles", "B": "Autogenic drainage taught by the allied health assistant at weekly visits, since it needs no equipment", "C": "A high frequency chest wall oscillation vest, since it works without effort or technique", "D": "An oscillating PEP device with huffing, which he can use alone and adjust for comfort"}, "cluster_id": "CHS204", "cluster_text": "Viliami Tuipulotu, 27, is a warehouse supervisor with cystic fibrosis (pancreatic insufficient, chronically colonised with Pseudomonas aeruginosa). He attends the outpatient CF clinic after a week of increased cough and thick green sputum. The CF physician has started antibiotics. He performs 20 minutes of the active cycle of breathing technique each morning and reports tiring, wanting something he can do at work. No positive expiratory pressure (PEP) device is used. Observations: SpO2 94 percent on room air, RR 18, HR 84, afebrile. Chest: coarse upper zone crackles and diffuse wheeze. FEV1 is 55 percent of predicted. Hypertonic saline nebulisation has been prescribed before airway clearance. The clinic manager proposes that the allied health assistant (AHA), who has two years in the clinic, teach and review any new device at weekly visits.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (An oscillating PEP device with huffing, which he can use alone and adjust for comfort): An oscillating PEP device with huffing is portable, less tiring than a long session of breathing cycles, can be used independently at work and supports airway stability in wheezy airways. Drainage positions are not practical at a workplace, and head-down positions are no longer routine first line. Autogenic drainage is a skilled technique that the physiotherapist must assess and teach, not the assistant. A chest wall vest is bulky, needs power and is not a portable first trial for a young man at work."}, {"id": 1775, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "At the next visit Viliami reports streaks of blood in his sputum for two days. SpO2 is 95 percent, he has no chest pain, and his breathing is unchanged. How should the physiotherapist modify his airway clearance plan at this visit?", "options": {"A": "Advise rest and review at the next clinic visit, since the antibiotics should settle the bleeding", "B": "Stop airway clearance until the blood clears, because coughing and huffing may worsen the bleeding", "C": "Continue his usual routine including percussion, since small streaks are common and airways need clearing", "D": "Inform the medical team, avoid percussion and vibration, and continue gentle active techniques as tolerated"}, "cluster_id": "CHS204", "cluster_text": "Viliami Tuipulotu, 27, is a warehouse supervisor with cystic fibrosis (pancreatic insufficient, chronically colonised with Pseudomonas aeruginosa). He attends the outpatient CF clinic after a week of increased cough and thick green sputum. The CF physician has started antibiotics. He performs 20 minutes of the active cycle of breathing technique each morning and reports tiring, wanting something he can do at work. No positive expiratory pressure (PEP) device is used. Observations: SpO2 94 percent on room air, RR 18, HR 84, afebrile. Chest: coarse upper zone crackles and diffuse wheeze. FEV1 is 55 percent of predicted. Hypertonic saline nebulisation has been prescribed before airway clearance. The clinic manager proposes that the allied health assistant (AHA), who has two years in the clinic, teach and review any new device at weekly visits.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Inform the medical team, avoid percussion and vibration, and continue gentle active techniques as tolerated): Streaks of blood are common with an infective exacerbation in CF, but the team should be told and forceful hands-on techniques avoided. Gentle active techniques keep secretions moving, which also helps the infection settle. Stopping everything lets secretions accumulate, while carrying on with percussion or simply waiting both ignore the need for medical awareness."}, {"id": 1776, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "The clinic manager suggests the allied health assistant teach Viliami the new oscillating device and adjust his technique at weekly clinic visits, as the assistant has two years of clinic experience. Which response best reflects safe delegation by the treating physiotherapist?", "options": {"A": "Delegate teaching and adjustment to the assistant, since two years of experience shows competence", "B": "The physiotherapist teaches and adjusts the device, then delegates defined practice checks with instructions and supervision", "C": "Delegate the teaching to the assistant, who phones the physiotherapist only if Viliami reports a problem", "D": "Decline delegation, since patients with chronic infection should be seen only by the registered physiotherapist"}, "cluster_id": "CHS204", "cluster_text": "Viliami Tuipulotu, 27, is a warehouse supervisor with cystic fibrosis (pancreatic insufficient, chronically colonised with Pseudomonas aeruginosa). He attends the outpatient CF clinic after a week of increased cough and thick green sputum. The CF physician has started antibiotics. He performs 20 minutes of the active cycle of breathing technique each morning and reports tiring, wanting something he can do at work. No positive expiratory pressure (PEP) device is used. Observations: SpO2 94 percent on room air, RR 18, HR 84, afebrile. Chest: coarse upper zone crackles and diffuse wheeze. FEV1 is 55 percent of predicted. Hypertonic saline nebulisation has been prescribed before airway clearance. The clinic manager proposes that the allied health assistant (AHA), who has two years in the clinic, teach and review any new device at weekly visits.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The physiotherapist teaches and adjusts the device, then delegates defined practice checks with instructions and supervision): Assessment, clinical reasoning and the initial teaching and setting of a device remain the physiotherapist's responsibility. Routine, clearly defined reinforcement tasks can be delegated to a trained assistant with explicit instructions and ongoing supervision. Experience alone does not transfer clinical judgement, but a blanket refusal ignores that assistants can safely support well-defined tasks."}, {"id": 1777, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Franco freezes at the kitchen threshold most days, especially when his medication is wearing off, and he has fallen twice at doorways. He understands instructions well. Which strategy should the physiotherapist teach first to help him get through the doorway safely?", "options": {"A": "Place a coloured strip at the threshold and cue a big step over it", "B": "Have him stop, count aloud to ten while standing, then walk on at his usual pace", "C": "Provide a four-wheeled walker for indoor walking, removing the need for cues at thresholds", "D": "Teach him to push through a freeze by leaning forward and taking quick small steps"}, "cluster_id": "CHS205", "cluster_text": "Franco Marino, 76, a retired baker, lives with his wife Rosa in a single-storey home. He has had Parkinson disease for 9 years, Hoehn and Yahr stage 3, on levodopa with carbidopa four times daily. He has fallen twice in three months, both times at doorways while turning. Freezing is worse in the hour before his next dose and in narrow spaces. Blood pressure is 128/78 mmHg lying and 106/68 mmHg after one minute standing, with mild light-headedness. Gait is shuffling with reduced arm swing; he hesitates at the kitchen threshold and turns en bloc. Cognition is intact and he is keen to keep walking to the local bakery. At the third home visit Rosa offers the physiotherapist an envelope containing $200 and an invitation to the family's Sunday lunch to say thank you. The clinic has no written gift policy.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Place a coloured strip at the threshold and cue a big step over it): External visual cues such as a line on the floor are an established way to trigger a large step and break a freeze. Leaning forward and shuffling quickly encourages festination and falls. A four-wheeled walker can worsen freezing at narrow gaps, and waiting without a cue does little to restart stepping."}, {"id": 1778, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Franco's blood pressure is 128/78 mmHg lying and 106/68 mmHg after one minute of standing, with mild light-headedness on the second reading. He has fallen twice recently and asks whether it matters because he feels only slightly dizzy. How should the physiotherapist interpret this finding?", "options": {"A": "A medication timing effect, so advise him to adjust when he takes his levodopa doses", "B": "Orthostatic hypotension contributing to fall risk, so report to his GP and teach staged rising", "C": "An anxiety response to measurement, so repeat after sitting rest and act only if symptoms worsen", "D": "Normal postural variation at his age, so continue exercise and recheck pressure at a later visit"}, "cluster_id": "CHS205", "cluster_text": "Franco Marino, 76, a retired baker, lives with his wife Rosa in a single-storey home. He has had Parkinson disease for 9 years, Hoehn and Yahr stage 3, on levodopa with carbidopa four times daily. He has fallen twice in three months, both times at doorways while turning. Freezing is worse in the hour before his next dose and in narrow spaces. Blood pressure is 128/78 mmHg lying and 106/68 mmHg after one minute standing, with mild light-headedness. Gait is shuffling with reduced arm swing; he hesitates at the kitchen threshold and turns en bloc. Cognition is intact and he is keen to keep walking to the local bakery. At the third home visit Rosa offers the physiotherapist an envelope containing $200 and an invitation to the family's Sunday lunch to say thank you. The clinic has no written gift policy.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Orthostatic hypotension contributing to fall risk, so report to his GP and teach staged rising): A fall of more than 20 mmHg systolic on standing with symptoms meets the usual definition of orthostatic hypotension, which is common in Parkinson disease and raises fall risk. The GP should review it, and staged rising is a safe physiotherapy strategy. Changing medication timing is outside physiotherapy scope, and dismissing the reading ignores his falls."}, {"id": 1779, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Franco's second fall happened while turning at the end of the hallway using several shuffling pivot steps. He walks the hallway most days to reach the bakery, his cognition is intact and he is motivated. Which turning strategy should the physiotherapist practise with him?", "options": {"A": "Practise wide-arc turns using several small steps with a rhythmic cue, avoiding pivoting on one foot", "B": "Teach a quick pivot on his stronger leg to complete turns in fewer steps near doorways", "C": "Practise turns only towards his less affected side to limit freezing, increasing difficulty as confidence grows", "D": "Teach him to turn by twisting his trunk first and swinging his feet around to follow"}, "cluster_id": "CHS205", "cluster_text": "Franco Marino, 76, a retired baker, lives with his wife Rosa in a single-storey home. He has had Parkinson disease for 9 years, Hoehn and Yahr stage 3, on levodopa with carbidopa four times daily. He has fallen twice in three months, both times at doorways while turning. Freezing is worse in the hour before his next dose and in narrow spaces. Blood pressure is 128/78 mmHg lying and 106/68 mmHg after one minute standing, with mild light-headedness. Gait is shuffling with reduced arm swing; he hesitates at the kitchen threshold and turns en bloc. Cognition is intact and he is keen to keep walking to the local bakery. At the third home visit Rosa offers the physiotherapist an envelope containing $200 and an invitation to the family's Sunday lunch to say thank you. The clinic has no written gift policy.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Practise wide-arc turns using several small steps with a rhythmic cue, avoiding pivoting on one foot): Turning is a common trigger for freezing and falls, and a wide arc with cued small steps keeps the feet moving and avoids a single-leg pivot. Rotating the trunk before the feet reproduces the en bloc pattern he already has. A fast pivot loads one leg with little base of support, and practising in one direction leaves the other turn untrained."}, {"id": 1780, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "At the third home visit Rosa offers the physiotherapist an envelope containing cash and invites her to the family's Sunday lunch to say thank you. Franco nods encouragingly. The clinic has no written gift policy. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Decline the cash politely, explain professional boundaries kindly, and document the offer in the clinical record", "B": "Accept the cash only once his episode of care ends, so treatment decisions stay uninfluenced", "C": "Accept the cash as a clinic donation and attend lunch, since refusing could harm the relationship", "D": "Accept the lunch invitation but decline the cash, because social contact outside clinical time improves adherence"}, "cluster_id": "CHS205", "cluster_text": "Franco Marino, 76, a retired baker, lives with his wife Rosa in a single-storey home. He has had Parkinson disease for 9 years, Hoehn and Yahr stage 3, on levodopa with carbidopa four times daily. He has fallen twice in three months, both times at doorways while turning. Freezing is worse in the hour before his next dose and in narrow spaces. Blood pressure is 128/78 mmHg lying and 106/68 mmHg after one minute standing, with mild light-headedness. Gait is shuffling with reduced arm swing; he hesitates at the kitchen threshold and turns en bloc. Cognition is intact and he is keen to keep walking to the local bakery. At the third home visit Rosa offers the physiotherapist an envelope containing $200 and an invitation to the family's Sunday lunch to say thank you. The clinic has no written gift policy.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Decline the cash politely, explain professional boundaries kindly, and document the offer in the clinical record): The professional code expects practitioners to avoid gifts that could influence care or blur the therapeutic relationship, and a kind explanation preserves trust while recording the event protects everyone. Taking the money as a donation still accepts it, and a family social visit crosses a boundary. Waiting until care ends does not change the fact that the offer is tied to ongoing treatment."}, {"id": 1781, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Rowan fires the pressurised inhaler into their mouth and then breathes in quickly, holding their breath for only a second or two. The physiotherapist wants to improve delivery of both inhalers to the airways. Which change is most likely to improve medication delivery?", "options": {"A": "Breathe in hard and fast right after pressing, to carry the medication deeper into the lungs", "B": "Fire two puffs together into a spacer and breathe in quickly so both arrive together", "C": "Use a spacer, one puff at a time, then breathe in slowly and hold the breath", "D": "Press the inhaler first and then begin breathing in a second or two later, slowly"}, "cluster_id": "CHS206", "cluster_text": "Rowan Mehta, 27, uses they/them pronouns and identifies as non binary. They work as a barista and were referred by their GP after an emergency department visit for acute asthma two weeks ago. Asthma since childhood. They use salbutamol most days and take a preventer inhaler irregularly, both from pressurised metered dose inhalers without a spacer. They wake with cough or wheeze about twice a week and avoid running because of chest tightness. Triggers: cold air, dust and exercise. Observations: RR 16, SpO2 98 percent, scattered expiratory wheeze after a brisk walk that eases within ten minutes. Peak expiratory flow is 80 percent of personal best. On arrival the receptionist called them Ms Mehta, and the intake form offered only male and female options. Rowan says they have avoided health services after being misgendered. They have no written asthma action plan.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use a spacer, one puff at a time, then breathe in slowly and hold the breath): A spacer lets the aerosol be breathed slowly from one puff at a time, which reduces throat deposition and removes the need to coordinate pressing with breathing. A fast hard breath increases impaction in the throat, so less reaches the airways. Firing several puffs together overloads the spacer and reduces delivery of each dose. Pressing before breathing in wastes much of the dose in the mouth even when the breath is slow."}, {"id": 1782, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Rowan reports reliever use most days, waking about twice a week with cough or wheeze, and activity limitation from tightness, despite irregular preventer use. How should the physiotherapist interpret this pattern, and what does it suggest about their current asthma management?", "options": {"A": "Asthma is reasonably controlled because peak flow stays close to personal best and symptoms are intermittent", "B": "Symptoms mainly reflect exercise induced bronchoconstriction, so a longer warm up should settle the night waking", "C": "Poor inhaler technique explains the symptoms, so spacer training first and a medical review later", "D": "Asthma is poorly controlled, so a medical review of preventer therapy is needed"}, "cluster_id": "CHS206", "cluster_text": "Rowan Mehta, 27, uses they/them pronouns and identifies as non binary. They work as a barista and were referred by their GP after an emergency department visit for acute asthma two weeks ago. Asthma since childhood. They use salbutamol most days and take a preventer inhaler irregularly, both from pressurised metered dose inhalers without a spacer. They wake with cough or wheeze about twice a week and avoid running because of chest tightness. Triggers: cold air, dust and exercise. Observations: RR 16, SpO2 98 percent, scattered expiratory wheeze after a brisk walk that eases within ten minutes. Peak expiratory flow is 80 percent of personal best. On arrival the receptionist called them Ms Mehta, and the intake form offered only male and female options. Rowan says they have avoided health services after being misgendered. They have no written asthma action plan.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Asthma is poorly controlled, so a medical review of preventer therapy is needed): Night waking, near daily reliever use and activity limitation indicate poor control, and irregular preventer use with poor technique probably contributes, so the doctor needs to reassess therapy. A peak flow near personal best does not outweigh these symptoms. Exercise induced bronchoconstriction does not explain waking at night. Teaching spacer use is worthwhile but postponing medical review leaves a poorly controlled person with a recent emergency visit without a treatment reassessment."}, {"id": 1783, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "At the second visit the receptionist again uses the wrong title, and Rowan seems quiet and reluctant to discuss exercise. The physiotherapist wants to build trust and record their details accurately going forward. Which action is the most appropriate and respectful response?", "options": {"A": "Use the name and sex on their Medicare card for records and avoid raising it unprompted", "B": "Explain the software allows two options and ask Rowan to choose the closest one for now", "C": "Ask Rowan privately how they wish to be addressed, record this, and brief the front desk", "D": "Write to the GP about Rowan's identity so the referrer can advise the team"}, "cluster_id": "CHS206", "cluster_text": "Rowan Mehta, 27, uses they/them pronouns and identifies as non binary. They work as a barista and were referred by their GP after an emergency department visit for acute asthma two weeks ago. Asthma since childhood. They use salbutamol most days and take a preventer inhaler irregularly, both from pressurised metered dose inhalers without a spacer. They wake with cough or wheeze about twice a week and avoid running because of chest tightness. Triggers: cold air, dust and exercise. Observations: RR 16, SpO2 98 percent, scattered expiratory wheeze after a brisk walk that eases within ten minutes. Peak expiratory flow is 80 percent of personal best. On arrival the receptionist called them Ms Mehta, and the intake form offered only male and female options. Rowan says they have avoided health services after being misgendered. They have no written asthma action plan.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ask Rowan privately how they wish to be addressed, record this, and brief the front desk): Asking privately about preferred name and pronouns, recording them and briefing staff builds trust and keeps records accurate. Defaulting to the card or a forced choice repeats the harm Rowan described. Sharing their identity with the GP without Rowan's agreement breaches privacy."}, {"id": 1784, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Rowan wants to run again and asks what they can do to feel in control between appointments. The physiotherapist supports self management and health promotion. Which approach would best support Rowan's asthma self management while returning to running and daily life?", "options": {"A": "Encourage a written action plan with their GP, trigger management and a graded return to running", "B": "Give a printed asthma handout and advise avoiding cold air and vigorous exercise until symptoms resolve", "C": "Draft an action plan with preventer adjustment steps and send a copy to their GP", "D": "Teach daily peak flow charting and ask them to phone the physiotherapist when readings drop"}, "cluster_id": "CHS206", "cluster_text": "Rowan Mehta, 27, uses they/them pronouns and identifies as non binary. They work as a barista and were referred by their GP after an emergency department visit for acute asthma two weeks ago. Asthma since childhood. They use salbutamol most days and take a preventer inhaler irregularly, both from pressurised metered dose inhalers without a spacer. They wake with cough or wheeze about twice a week and avoid running because of chest tightness. Triggers: cold air, dust and exercise. Observations: RR 16, SpO2 98 percent, scattered expiratory wheeze after a brisk walk that eases within ten minutes. Peak expiratory flow is 80 percent of personal best. On arrival the receptionist called them Ms Mehta, and the intake form offered only male and female options. Rowan says they have avoided health services after being misgendered. They have no written asthma action plan.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Encourage a written action plan with their GP, trigger management and a graded return to running): A written action plan prepared with the GP gives clear steps for worsening symptoms, and trigger management with graded exercise builds the confidence to return to running. Avoiding exercise lowers fitness and leaves manageable exercise symptoms untreated, and a handout alone is not individualised. Writing medication adjustment steps is outside physiotherapy scope and belongs with the prescriber. Peak flow charting without an agreed plan is incomplete, and calling the physiotherapist is not the right escalation path."}, {"id": 1785, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Dalton asks for his Aunty to stay during treatment and for an Aboriginal Liaison Officer to attend. The unit usually limits visitors during procedures, and today's session involves suction and assisted coughing. How should the physiotherapist respond to his request?", "options": {"A": "Involve the liaison officer but ask family to wait outside during suction, then update them afterwards", "B": "Support family presence with Dalton's consent, involve the liaison officer, and explain each step to him", "C": "Allow family for education only, since airway clearance is a clinical procedure needing a controlled environment", "D": "Ask the Aunty to nominate one family spokesperson to attend, keeping the visitor policy applied consistently"}, "cluster_id": "CHS207", "cluster_text": "Dalton Dunn, 26, an Aboriginal man from a town about six hours away, is on day 6 after a motor vehicle crash causing a complete C5 spinal cord injury (ASIA impairment scale A). He is on the acute spinal unit, not ventilated, with a cervical collar in place. Observations: HR 58, BP 92/54 supine, RR 24, SpO2 93 percent on room air. He has a weak, ineffective cough and a paradoxical breathing pattern, with a vital capacity of 1.2 L. Chest auscultation reveals coarse crackles and reduced air entry at the right base, and the chest X-ray shows right lower lobe collapse. His Aunty and a cousin have travelled far to be at the bedside. Dalton says he is worried and would like family with him during treatment, and he asks whether an Aboriginal Liaison Officer can attend.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Support family presence with Dalton's consent, involve the liaison officer, and explain each step to him): Culturally safe practice lets the patient decide who supports him, and family and liaison staff are often central to trust and wellbeing. Consent and clear explanation allow Dalton to choose his level of involvement, and visitor limits can be applied flexibly with ward staff. Excluding family assumes clinical care and cultural support cannot happen together."}, {"id": 1786, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Dalton's abdomen rises while his upper chest draws inward during inspiration, and it is less marked when he is lying flat. The physiotherapist is explaining the pattern to a junior colleague. Which explanation best accounts for it after a complete C5 injury?", "options": {"A": "Phrenic nerve loss makes the diaphragm ascend on inspiration while accessory muscles take over the effort", "B": "Paralysed accessory muscles fail to lift the upper ribs, so the chest wall sinks on inspiration", "C": "Reduced compliance from the collapsed lobe stiffens the lungs, drawing the upper chest inwards", "D": "Paralysed intercostals and abdominals leave the diaphragm working alone, so the upper chest is drawn inwards"}, "cluster_id": "CHS207", "cluster_text": "Dalton Dunn, 26, an Aboriginal man from a town about six hours away, is on day 6 after a motor vehicle crash causing a complete C5 spinal cord injury (ASIA impairment scale A). He is on the acute spinal unit, not ventilated, with a cervical collar in place. Observations: HR 58, BP 92/54 supine, RR 24, SpO2 93 percent on room air. He has a weak, ineffective cough and a paradoxical breathing pattern, with a vital capacity of 1.2 L. Chest auscultation reveals coarse crackles and reduced air entry at the right base, and the chest X-ray shows right lower lobe collapse. His Aunty and a cousin have travelled far to be at the bedside. Dalton says he is worried and would like family with him during treatment, and he asks whether an Aboriginal Liaison Officer can attend.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Paralysed intercostals and abdominals leave the diaphragm working alone, so the upper chest is drawn inwards): After a complete C5 injury the phrenic roots lie mostly above the lesion, so the diaphragm still works while the intercostals and abdominals are paralysed. With nothing stabilising the rib cage, the upper chest is pulled in as the diaphragm descends, and this is less marked lying flat because abdominal contents support the diaphragm. Phrenic nerve loss would be expected with a higher injury. The accessory muscles are supplied above the lesion and still work. Lobe collapse can add to the effort but does not by itself create this paradoxical pattern."}, {"id": 1787, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Dalton has copious secretions at the right base and cannot cough effectively. The medical team has confirmed his spine is stable for manual techniques, and he last ate five hours ago. He is supine with his abdominal binder removed. How should the physiotherapist apply a manually assisted cough?", "options": {"A": "Compress the upper chest quickly with both hands as he coughs to raise intrathoracic pressure", "B": "Compress the lateral lower ribs during inspiration to expand the chest before he coughs", "C": "Hold a sustained abdominal push through his deep breath to lift the diaphragm before coughing", "D": "Time an inward and upward abdominal thrust with his attempted cough after a deep breath"}, "cluster_id": "CHS207", "cluster_text": "Dalton Dunn, 26, an Aboriginal man from a town about six hours away, is on day 6 after a motor vehicle crash causing a complete C5 spinal cord injury (ASIA impairment scale A). He is on the acute spinal unit, not ventilated, with a cervical collar in place. Observations: HR 58, BP 92/54 supine, RR 24, SpO2 93 percent on room air. He has a weak, ineffective cough and a paradoxical breathing pattern, with a vital capacity of 1.2 L. Chest auscultation reveals coarse crackles and reduced air entry at the right base, and the chest X-ray shows right lower lobe collapse. His Aunty and a cousin have travelled far to be at the bedside. Dalton says he is worried and would like family with him during treatment, and he asks whether an Aboriginal Liaison Officer can attend.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Time an inward and upward abdominal thrust with his attempted cough after a deep breath): A manually assisted cough replaces the missing abdominal contraction: after a deep breath, an inward and upward thrust below the ribs is timed with the attempted cough. Pressure during inspiration or a sustained push works against lung filling, and upper chest compression does not generate an effective expulsive force. Spinal stability and time since eating are checked first."}, {"id": 1788, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "During deep nasopharyngeal suction, seconds after the catheter passed, Dalton's heart rate falls from 58 to 38 beats per minute, and he becomes pale and complains of feeling faint. His blood pressure is 80/48. What should the physiotherapist do immediately?", "options": {"A": "Tilt the bed head up to raise his blood pressure and finish suction in that position", "B": "Pause suction and sit him over the bed edge to settle his heart rate and pressure", "C": "Stop suctioning, give oxygen, and call for urgent medical review of the vagally driven bradycardia", "D": "Complete the suction pass to clear the right base, then reassess once his heart rate recovers"}, "cluster_id": "CHS207", "cluster_text": "Dalton Dunn, 26, an Aboriginal man from a town about six hours away, is on day 6 after a motor vehicle crash causing a complete C5 spinal cord injury (ASIA impairment scale A). He is on the acute spinal unit, not ventilated, with a cervical collar in place. Observations: HR 58, BP 92/54 supine, RR 24, SpO2 93 percent on room air. He has a weak, ineffective cough and a paradoxical breathing pattern, with a vital capacity of 1.2 L. Chest auscultation reveals coarse crackles and reduced air entry at the right base, and the chest X-ray shows right lower lobe collapse. His Aunty and a cousin have travelled far to be at the bedside. Dalton says he is worried and would like family with him during treatment, and he asks whether an Aboriginal Liaison Officer can attend.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop suctioning, give oxygen, and call for urgent medical review of the vagally driven bradycardia): In high spinal cord injury unopposed vagal stimulation from suctioning can cause marked bradycardia and hypotension. Suction should stop, oxygen be given and urgent medical help called. Sitting him up lowers his blood pressure further because his vasomotor control is impaired, and finishing the pass delays treatment of a dangerous rhythm."}, {"id": 1789, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "His mother says the cough followed two days of a runny nose. Rahul has a barking cough, hoarse cry and inspiratory stridor when agitated, with a clear chest and good air entry. Which explanation best accounts for these findings in croup?", "options": {"A": "Retained bronchial secretions obstruct the lower airways, producing a harsh cough and noisy breathing on inspiration", "B": "Bronchospasm narrows the small airways, causing expiratory wheeze, prolonged expiration and a dry cough", "C": "Viral inflammation and oedema narrow the subglottic airway, causing turbulent flow, stridor and a barking cough", "D": "Bacterial epiglottitis swells the supraglottic tissues, producing stridor with drooling and a toxic appearance"}, "cluster_id": "CHS208", "cluster_text": "Rahul Chopra, 2 years old, was admitted overnight with a 2 day history of coryza followed by a barking cough and hoarse cry. No prematurity or previous airway problems. He received oral dexamethasone and nebulised adrenaline 90 minutes ago. Now: mild inspiratory stridor when agitated, none at rest, mild suprasternal recession, RR 38, HR 128, SpO2 97% on room air, alert and drinking. Chest clear on auscultation with good air entry. Temperature 37.8. His mother is at the bedside. The medical registrar has written in the notes: 'physio chest physiotherapy for cough'. The nurse in charge is managing a busy ward and has asked for an update before your afternoon list.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Viral inflammation and oedema narrow the subglottic airway, causing turbulent flow, stridor and a barking cough): Croup is a viral laryngotracheobronchitis with subglottic swelling, which produces a barking cough, hoarseness and inspiratory stridor while the lower chest stays clear. Retained secretions would give crackles or reduced air entry, which are absent here. Epiglottitis and bronchospasm show different features, such as drooling and expiratory wheeze."}, {"id": 1790, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "The registrar's note requests chest physiotherapy for cough. Rahul's chest is clear, he has mild stridor when agitated, and he is settled in his mother's arms. He drank well and no sputum has been seen. What is the most appropriate physiotherapy response?", "options": {"A": "Use head-down side lying postural drainage with gentle nasopharyngeal suction beforehand to clear secretions", "B": "Start nebulised saline and manual techniques to support his cough and reduce his work of breathing", "C": "Give brief gentle percussion and vibration while his mother holds him to keep him calm", "D": "Withhold airway clearance, limit handling, and explain to the registrar why it is not indicated"}, "cluster_id": "CHS208", "cluster_text": "Rahul Chopra, 2 years old, was admitted overnight with a 2 day history of coryza followed by a barking cough and hoarse cry. No prematurity or previous airway problems. He received oral dexamethasone and nebulised adrenaline 90 minutes ago. Now: mild inspiratory stridor when agitated, none at rest, mild suprasternal recession, RR 38, HR 128, SpO2 97% on room air, alert and drinking. Chest clear on auscultation with good air entry. Temperature 37.8. His mother is at the bedside. The medical registrar has written in the notes: 'physio chest physiotherapy for cough'. The nurse in charge is managing a busy ward and has asked for an update before your afternoon list.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Withhold airway clearance, limit handling, and explain to the registrar why it is not indicated): Croup is an upper airway problem, and airway clearance does not help a clear chest. Handling, percussion or suction can distress the child and increase airway turbulence and stridor. Explaining the reasoning to the registrar keeps the physiotherapist within safe practice and maintains a good working relationship."}, {"id": 1791, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Forty minutes later Rahul is crying, with stridor now present at rest, marked suprasternal and subcostal recession, RR 52, HR 160 and SpO2 92%. His mother says he is tired. The adrenaline was given over two hours ago. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Keep him calm in his mother's arms and activate the ward's paediatric rapid response call", "B": "Place him supine on the bed for a respiratory assessment, then ask the nurse to review", "C": "Stay with him, keep him calm and ask the nurse to arrange a medical review soon", "D": "Start gentle positioning and chest clearance techniques, then reassess his stridor and saturation afterwards"}, "cluster_id": "CHS208", "cluster_text": "Rahul Chopra, 2 years old, was admitted overnight with a 2 day history of coryza followed by a barking cough and hoarse cry. No prematurity or previous airway problems. He received oral dexamethasone and nebulised adrenaline 90 minutes ago. Now: mild inspiratory stridor when agitated, none at rest, mild suprasternal recession, RR 38, HR 128, SpO2 97% on room air, alert and drinking. Chest clear on auscultation with good air entry. Temperature 37.8. His mother is at the bedside. The medical registrar has written in the notes: 'physio chest physiotherapy for cough'. The nurse in charge is managing a busy ward and has asked for an update before your afternoon list.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Keep him calm in his mother's arms and activate the ward's paediatric rapid response call): Stridor at rest with marked recession, tachycardia and falling saturation indicates severe airway obstruction and tiring, which needs an immediate medical response through the ward's rapid response process. Keeping him calm and upright with his mother limits agitation, which worsens the obstruction. Lying him flat to assess him can worsen the obstruction. Asking for a review soon is too slow for this severity. Chest clearance does not treat upper airway swelling and agitating him makes it worse."}, {"id": 1792, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "The rapid response team has arrived and the registrar asks you for a handover about Rahul. You have limited time and several observations changed since the morning. You were the first clinician to see the change. Which approach to the handover best supports safe, effective interprofessional communication?", "options": {"A": "State that he looks worse, ask the nurse to relay the details and document your findings", "B": "Use ISBAR, stating the deterioration, the key observations and your recommendation for urgent review", "C": "Tell the registrar the observations and your plan, and leave the nurse to read the notes", "D": "Give a chronological account of everything done since admission so the team has the full story"}, "cluster_id": "CHS208", "cluster_text": "Rahul Chopra, 2 years old, was admitted overnight with a 2 day history of coryza followed by a barking cough and hoarse cry. No prematurity or previous airway problems. He received oral dexamethasone and nebulised adrenaline 90 minutes ago. Now: mild inspiratory stridor when agitated, none at rest, mild suprasternal recession, RR 38, HR 128, SpO2 97% on room air, alert and drinking. Chest clear on auscultation with good air entry. Temperature 37.8. His mother is at the bedside. The medical registrar has written in the notes: 'physio chest physiotherapy for cough'. The nurse in charge is managing a busy ward and has asked for an update before your afternoon list.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use ISBAR, stating the deterioration, the key observations and your recommendation for urgent review): A structured format such as ISBAR gives the team the identity, the problem, the context, your assessment and what you want done in a predictable order, which suits a time-pressured, deteriorating situation. A chronological history buries the key change, and passing information through a third person risks loss or delay. Documentation follows but does not replace a direct verbal handover."}, {"id": 1793, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Ten minutes into cycling, Ms Iyer reports tightness in her chest. The monitor shows new horizontal ST depression that was not present at rest, and her heart rate is steady at the programmed level. What is the best immediate action for the physiotherapist to take?", "options": {"A": "Encourage deep breathing and complete the planned session, then report the ECG change to the cardiologist", "B": "Reduce the workload by half and continue cycling while watching whether the chest tightness settles", "C": "Switch to a light load cool down for five minutes and reassess the tracing before stopping", "D": "Stop exercise, seat her, monitor ECG and symptoms, and activate the emergency protocol if they persist"}, "cluster_id": "CHS209", "cluster_text": "Deepa Iyer, 52, an accountant, is in week 3 of an outpatient cardiac rehabilitation programme after a myocardial infarction treated with a coronary stent four weeks ago. She takes aspirin, a second antiplatelet, a beta blocker, a statin and an ACE inhibitor. Resting HR 62, BP 124/78, and her left ventricular ejection fraction was recorded as mildly reduced. Exercise is on a cycle ergometer with continuous ECG telemetry at an intensity set from her earlier symptom-limited test. Previous sessions were uneventful and she has had no chest pain since the infarct. Her husband drives her to each session. She asks whether the high intensity interval training she read about in a small single-centre trial would speed her recovery.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stop exercise, seat her, monitor ECG and symptoms, and activate the emergency protocol if they persist): New horizontal ST depression with chest tightness during exertion suggests myocardial ischaemia, which meets the criteria for ending exercise. She should be stopped and seated so the ECG and symptoms can be observed, with the emergency protocol started if they do not settle. Reducing load, finishing the session or cooling down all keep the heart working while ischaemic."}, {"id": 1794, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "In a later session, ventricular beats appear at rest and stay unchanged during warm-up. At moderate intensity she has a run of three consecutive ventricular beats, feels light-headed, and her systolic pressure drops from 126 to 108 mmHg. What is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Continue at lower workload, as ventricular beats at rest are common, rechecking rhythm in two minutes", "B": "Give her a drink and rest for one minute, then continue session if the light-headedness resolves", "C": "Stop exercise, sit her down, monitor rhythm and pressure, and inform the supervising doctor", "D": "Ask her to cough forcefully to restore sinus rhythm, then resume the previous workload if comfortable"}, "cluster_id": "CHS209", "cluster_text": "Deepa Iyer, 52, an accountant, is in week 3 of an outpatient cardiac rehabilitation programme after a myocardial infarction treated with a coronary stent four weeks ago. She takes aspirin, a second antiplatelet, a beta blocker, a statin and an ACE inhibitor. Resting HR 62, BP 124/78, and her left ventricular ejection fraction was recorded as mildly reduced. Exercise is on a cycle ergometer with continuous ECG telemetry at an intensity set from her earlier symptom-limited test. Previous sessions were uneventful and she has had no chest pain since the infarct. Her husband drives her to each session. She asks whether the high intensity interval training she read about in a small single-centre trial would speed her recovery.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop exercise, sit her down, monitor rhythm and pressure, and inform the supervising doctor): Runs of consecutive ventricular beats with light-headedness and a falling blood pressure are a recognised reason to terminate exercise, because the rhythm is compromising cardiac output. She should be seated and monitored, and the doctor informed so the programme can be reviewed. Isolated beats at rest alone would not stop exercise, but the new run with symptoms changes the decision, and coughing or a short rest does not make it safe to continue."}, {"id": 1795, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Ms Iyer asks how to judge her effort at home between sessions, since she takes a beta blocker and plans brisk walks on rest days. Which statement about using heart rate for her exercise intensity is most accurate for the physiotherapist to explain?", "options": {"A": "Her usual formula based target heart rate remains accurate because the medicine only affects resting pulse", "B": "Her heart rate rises faster than predicted on the medicine, so a lower ceiling is needed", "C": "Resting pulse is affected, so she should use her morning pulse to set walking pace", "D": "The medicine blunts her heart rate response, so perceived exertion and talking ease guide intensity better"}, "cluster_id": "CHS209", "cluster_text": "Deepa Iyer, 52, an accountant, is in week 3 of an outpatient cardiac rehabilitation programme after a myocardial infarction treated with a coronary stent four weeks ago. She takes aspirin, a second antiplatelet, a beta blocker, a statin and an ACE inhibitor. Resting HR 62, BP 124/78, and her left ventricular ejection fraction was recorded as mildly reduced. Exercise is on a cycle ergometer with continuous ECG telemetry at an intensity set from her earlier symptom-limited test. Previous sessions were uneventful and she has had no chest pain since the infarct. Her husband drives her to each session. She asks whether the high intensity interval training she read about in a small single-centre trial would speed her recovery.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The medicine blunts her heart rate response, so perceived exertion and talking ease guide intensity better): Beta blockers reduce heart rate at rest and during exercise, so formula based targets overestimate the rate she can reach and are unreliable. Rating of perceived exertion and the ability to talk give an effort guide that still works. Using pulse alone would misjudge intensity and could push her harder than intended."}, {"id": 1796, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Ms Iyer asks whether high intensity interval training from a small single-centre trial would speed her recovery. The programme's guideline recommends moderate continuous training with supervised progression. She is four weeks after her infarct. How should the physiotherapist best respond using the evidence?", "options": {"A": "Start interval training now, since newer trial results replace older guideline advice, with close monitoring", "B": "Let her choose the training type, as patient preference outweighs both the guideline and published research", "C": "Set the trial aside because single-centre studies seldom matter in practice, and keep to the guideline", "D": "Explain that one small trial is weak evidence, compare it with guidelines, and involve her cardiologist"}, "cluster_id": "CHS209", "cluster_text": "Deepa Iyer, 52, an accountant, is in week 3 of an outpatient cardiac rehabilitation programme after a myocardial infarction treated with a coronary stent four weeks ago. She takes aspirin, a second antiplatelet, a beta blocker, a statin and an ACE inhibitor. Resting HR 62, BP 124/78, and her left ventricular ejection fraction was recorded as mildly reduced. Exercise is on a cycle ergometer with continuous ECG telemetry at an intensity set from her earlier symptom-limited test. Previous sessions were uneventful and she has had no chest pain since the infarct. Her husband drives her to each session. She asks whether the high intensity interval training she read about in a small single-centre trial would speed her recovery.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain that one small trial is weak evidence, compare it with guidelines, and involve her cardiologist): A single small trial can show promise but carries risks of bias and chance findings, so it should be weighed against guidelines and larger syntheses before care changes. The cardiology team shares responsibility for intensity decisions after a recent infarct. Swapping guideline advice for one trial, or dismissing the study outright, are both poor reasoning, and patient preference matters but does not replace safety evidence."}, {"id": 1797, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Ms Haddad sits leaning to the right, rarely looks to her left and has bumped her left wheelchair armrest twice. Her sensation to light touch is intact. What is the most appropriate way to structure her environment and approach during early rehabilitation?", "options": {"A": "Keep the environment uncluttered and wait for scanning to recover spontaneously without cueing", "B": "Place items and approach from her left, using cues to scan to that side", "C": "Place items and approach from her right to limit confusion, and fade cues later", "D": "Cover the right eye with a patch during sessions to force leftward looking"}, "cluster_id": "CHS210", "cluster_text": "Leila Haddad, 52, trans woman, Lebanese Australian, former bus driver. Right middle cerebral artery infarct three weeks ago; now on the inpatient rehabilitation unit. She uses she and her. The hospital's legacy system and her legal documents still show her former name and sex, though her chosen name is on her bed and in the notes at her request. She has not told her brother, who is listed as emergency contact only, about her gender history and has asked staff not to share it. The medical team is reviewing her hormone therapy. Findings: left shoulder flexion 2/5, left hip flexion 3/5, knee extension 3/5, ankle dorsiflexion 1/5. Light touch sensation intact. She sits leaning right, rarely looks left and has bumped her left armrest. Sitting balance fair; stands with one person assisting, left knee buckles. BP 138/84 mmHg, HR 76, SpO2 97 percent.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Place items and approach from her left, using cues to scan to that side): Inattention to the left after a right hemisphere stroke is addressed by drawing attention across the midline with cues and by presenting items on the neglected side. Presenting everything on the right reinforces the neglect. Eye patching is not a routine early approach, and waiting without cueing wastes the window for practising scanning."}, {"id": 1798, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Ms Haddad now has left knee extension at grade 3 and ankle dorsiflexion at grade 1, with fair sitting balance. She can stand with one person assisting but her left knee buckles in stance. What is the most appropriate lower limb training step to improve safe standing?", "options": {"A": "Repeated sit to stand with symmetrical foot loading and manual or orthotic knee control", "B": "Standing practice allowing the left knee to lock in hyperextension for stability during transfers", "C": "Gait practice with a rigid ankle foot orthosis only, avoiding standing practice until dorsiflexion returns", "D": "Progressive resistance leg press at high load on the left side to build quadriceps quickly"}, "cluster_id": "CHS210", "cluster_text": "Leila Haddad, 52, trans woman, Lebanese Australian, former bus driver. Right middle cerebral artery infarct three weeks ago; now on the inpatient rehabilitation unit. She uses she and her. The hospital's legacy system and her legal documents still show her former name and sex, though her chosen name is on her bed and in the notes at her request. She has not told her brother, who is listed as emergency contact only, about her gender history and has asked staff not to share it. The medical team is reviewing her hormone therapy. Findings: left shoulder flexion 2/5, left hip flexion 3/5, knee extension 3/5, ankle dorsiflexion 1/5. Light touch sensation intact. She sits leaning right, rarely looks left and has bumped her left armrest. Sitting balance fair; stands with one person assisting, left knee buckles. BP 138/84 mmHg, HR 76, SpO2 97 percent.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Repeated sit to stand with symmetrical foot loading and manual or orthotic knee control): Task specific, repeated sit to stand with even weight bearing and controlled knee position trains the movement she needs while protecting the joint. Locking into hyperextension teaches a harmful pattern, and heavy loading is premature with grade 3 strength and poor control. Withholding standing practice delays functional recovery."}, {"id": 1799, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Ms Haddad's brother visits and asks the physiotherapist why her records show a different name and what her history is. She has not named him as a contact for information about her care. What should the physiotherapist do in response to his questions?", "options": {"A": "Discuss the stroke only, since next of kin may receive current clinical information routinely", "B": "Politely decline to share details, inform her of the request, and document the conversation", "C": "Refer him to the treating doctor, who can disclose information to relatives when family members ask", "D": "Explain the name change only, since he is her emergency contact, and withhold other details"}, "cluster_id": "CHS210", "cluster_text": "Leila Haddad, 52, trans woman, Lebanese Australian, former bus driver. Right middle cerebral artery infarct three weeks ago; now on the inpatient rehabilitation unit. She uses she and her. The hospital's legacy system and her legal documents still show her former name and sex, though her chosen name is on her bed and in the notes at her request. She has not told her brother, who is listed as emergency contact only, about her gender history and has asked staff not to share it. The medical team is reviewing her hormone therapy. Findings: left shoulder flexion 2/5, left hip flexion 3/5, knee extension 3/5, ankle dorsiflexion 1/5. Light touch sensation intact. She sits leaning right, rarely looks left and has bumped her left armrest. Sitting balance fair; stands with one person assisting, left knee buckles. BP 138/84 mmHg, HR 76, SpO2 97 percent.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Politely decline to share details, inform her of the request, and document the conversation): Her health and gender history are private information, and an emergency contact is not entitled to it without her consent. The physiotherapist should respectfully decline, tell her so she can decide what to share, and record the exchange. Passing him to the doctor or sharing selected details still discloses information she has not authorised."}, {"id": 1800, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "At the next session Ms Haddad has new slurred speech, a facial droop that was not present yesterday and her left arm is weaker than at morning review. Her blood pressure is unchanged. What should the physiotherapist do first in response?", "options": {"A": "Rest her supine for ten minutes, then reassess and report if the signs have not settled", "B": "Continue with a gentler session focused on facial exercises and report changes at handover", "C": "Stop the session, keep her safe, and call the nurse in charge for urgent review", "D": "Complete a neurological screen in full first, documenting each finding before informing the team"}, "cluster_id": "CHS210", "cluster_text": "Leila Haddad, 52, trans woman, Lebanese Australian, former bus driver. Right middle cerebral artery infarct three weeks ago; now on the inpatient rehabilitation unit. She uses she and her. The hospital's legacy system and her legal documents still show her former name and sex, though her chosen name is on her bed and in the notes at her request. She has not told her brother, who is listed as emergency contact only, about her gender history and has asked staff not to share it. The medical team is reviewing her hormone therapy. Findings: left shoulder flexion 2/5, left hip flexion 3/5, knee extension 3/5, ankle dorsiflexion 1/5. Light touch sensation intact. She sits leaning right, rarely looks left and has bumped her left armrest. Sitting balance fair; stands with one person assisting, left knee buckles. BP 138/84 mmHg, HR 76, SpO2 97 percent.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop the session, keep her safe, and call the nurse in charge for urgent review): New focal deficits in a recent stroke patient suggest extension or a new event and need immediate medical review. Safety and escalation come before documentation or further testing. Waiting, resting or carrying on delays time critical treatment."}, {"id": 1801, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Ms Yeung has neck pain, reduced movement and paraspinal tenderness six days after the collision, with a normal neurological examination and no fracture. Using the Quebec Task Force classification of whiplash associated disorders, which grade best describes her presentation today?", "options": {"A": "Grade I: neck pain or stiffness with no objective physical signs found on examination", "B": "Grade IV: neck complaint with a fracture or dislocation that needs urgent surgical management", "C": "Grade III: neck complaint with neurological signs such as weakened reflexes or a sensory deficit", "D": "Grade II: neck complaint with musculoskeletal signs but no neurological signs"}, "cluster_id": "CHS211", "cluster_text": "Mei Yeung, 34, an accountant, presents to private practice 6 days after her stationary car was hit from behind at moderate speed. In the emergency department a cervical spine fracture was excluded clinically and she was discharged. Complaints: neck pain 6 out of 10, tension-type headache and poor sleep. She is afraid that moving her neck will cause damage, wears a soft collar and has stopped driving. Examination: cervical rotation 40 degrees to both sides, flexion and extension limited by pain, paraspinal tenderness, and normal upper limb reflexes, power and sensation. Neck Disability Index 38 percent. She has a compulsory third party insurance claim open and asks for a plan she can follow.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Grade II: neck complaint with musculoskeletal signs but no neurological signs): Grade II requires musculoskeletal signs such as reduced movement or point tenderness without neurological findings, which matches her examination. Grade I has no objective signs, so reduced rotation and tenderness exclude it. Grades III and IV require neurological deficit or fracture, neither of which is present."}, {"id": 1802, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Ms Yeung is wearing a soft collar and asks whether she should rest her neck until the pain settles. She is worried that exercise will cause damage. Which advice is most consistent with current evidence for the early management of her whiplash injury?", "options": {"A": "Keep the collar for two weeks so inflamed soft tissues can heal before any movement starts", "B": "Begin passive manual mobilisation of every cervical segment to restore range before active movement", "C": "Rest from work and driving until the pain scores fall below three out of ten", "D": "Reassure her, stop collar use, and encourage normal activity with gentle active neck movements"}, "cluster_id": "CHS211", "cluster_text": "Mei Yeung, 34, an accountant, presents to private practice 6 days after her stationary car was hit from behind at moderate speed. In the emergency department a cervical spine fracture was excluded clinically and she was discharged. Complaints: neck pain 6 out of 10, tension-type headache and poor sleep. She is afraid that moving her neck will cause damage, wears a soft collar and has stopped driving. Examination: cervical rotation 40 degrees to both sides, flexion and extension limited by pain, paraspinal tenderness, and normal upper limb reflexes, power and sensation. Neck Disability Index 38 percent. She has a compulsory third party insurance claim open and asks for a plan she can follow.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reassure her, stop collar use, and encourage normal activity with gentle active neck movements): Early reassurance, staying active and gentle active movement lead to better recovery than immobilisation, and collars prolong disability and fear of movement. Waiting for pain to fall before resuming activity reinforces avoidance. Passive mobilisation alone makes her dependent on treatment and does not address her beliefs."}, {"id": 1803, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Two weeks later Ms Yeung arrives reporting a sudden severe occipital headache unlike her usual one, new dizziness, double vision and numbness on one side of her face that began this morning. She also feels unsteady walking. What should the physiotherapist do?", "options": {"A": "Stop neck care and arrange urgent emergency transfer for suspected vascular compromise", "B": "Stop neck treatment and ask her GP for an urgent appointment within twenty four hours", "C": "Do a vascular screen with blood pressure and cranial nerve checks before deciding on transfer", "D": "Observe her in the clinic for thirty minutes and refer to her GP if stable"}, "cluster_id": "CHS211", "cluster_text": "Mei Yeung, 34, an accountant, presents to private practice 6 days after her stationary car was hit from behind at moderate speed. In the emergency department a cervical spine fracture was excluded clinically and she was discharged. Complaints: neck pain 6 out of 10, tension-type headache and poor sleep. She is afraid that moving her neck will cause damage, wears a soft collar and has stopped driving. Examination: cervical rotation 40 degrees to both sides, flexion and extension limited by pain, paraspinal tenderness, and normal upper limb reflexes, power and sensation. Neck Disability Index 38 percent. She has a compulsory third party insurance claim open and asks for a plan she can follow.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stop neck care and arrange urgent emergency transfer for suspected vascular compromise): A sudden unfamiliar headache with dizziness, double vision, facial numbness and unsteadiness after neck trauma suggests possible cervical artery dissection or posterior circulation stroke, which is an emergency needing ambulance transfer. A next day GP appointment is too slow for a time critical event. Further screening in the clinic delays transport when the red flags already justify it. Watching her for half an hour and referring only if she looks stable risks serious harm if she deteriorates."}, {"id": 1804, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Ms Yeung is improving but still worried about flare-ups and avoids exercise and driving. She wants a plan she can follow at home between appointments. Which approach best supports her self-management and general health over the coming weeks of recovery?", "options": {"A": "Give a fixed exercise sheet and delay review until her insurer approves further sessions", "B": "Agree graded activity, pacing, sleep and flare-up strategies with her and review progress", "C": "Explain in detail the ligament and joint damage seen in whiplash so she respects her pain", "D": "Book frequent passive treatment sessions so her neck is supervised until all symptoms resolve"}, "cluster_id": "CHS211", "cluster_text": "Mei Yeung, 34, an accountant, presents to private practice 6 days after her stationary car was hit from behind at moderate speed. In the emergency department a cervical spine fracture was excluded clinically and she was discharged. Complaints: neck pain 6 out of 10, tension-type headache and poor sleep. She is afraid that moving her neck will cause damage, wears a soft collar and has stopped driving. Examination: cervical rotation 40 degrees to both sides, flexion and extension limited by pain, paraspinal tenderness, and normal upper limb reflexes, power and sensation. Neck Disability Index 38 percent. She has a compulsory third party insurance claim open and asks for a plan she can follow.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Agree graded activity, pacing, sleep and flare-up strategies with her and review progress): A collaborative plan with graded activity, pacing, sleep advice and a flare-up strategy builds confidence and reduces fear avoidance. Detailed talk of structural damage can increase fear and disability. A fixed sheet without review ignores her progress, and passive care alone fosters dependence."}, {"id": 1805, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Today Mrs Greco has gained 2.5 kg in a week, needs an extra pillow at night, and has new bibasal crackles with worsening ankle oedema and resting breathlessness. She is booked for a mobility session. How should the physiotherapist manage the planned mobility session?", "options": {"A": "Reduce to seated exercises only and note the weight gain for her next scheduled review", "B": "Hold exercise and arrange same day medical review, passing on her weight, symptoms and observations", "C": "Teach her fluid restriction and daily weighing, and review the response at her next visit", "D": "Perform manual airway clearance techniques to reduce the crackles, then reassess saturation before finishing the visit"}, "cluster_id": "CHS212", "cluster_text": "Francesca Greco, 82, lives alone and receives a Home Care Package through a community aged care provider. Diagnoses: chronic heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, hypertension and osteoarthritis of both knees. Medications include a diuretic and a beta blocker. Today she reports needing an extra pillow at night and ankle swelling that has worsened over four days. Her weight is 2.5 kg above the usual weight the community nurse recorded last week. Observations: HR 96 regular, BP 108/66, RR 22, SpO2 92 percent on room air, bibasal crackles, pitting oedema to mid shin. She walks 15 metres with a frame before stopping for breathlessness (Borg 5). Her daughter, who is present, asks whether the package can fund twice weekly physiotherapy for exercise.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hold exercise and arrange same day medical review, passing on her weight, symptoms and observations): A rapid weight gain with orthopnoea, new crackles, more oedema and a lower saturation suggests decompensating heart failure, which needs prompt medical review and adjustment of treatment. Reporting objective findings lets the GP act the same day. Seated exercise still treats an acute change as routine and delays the report. Self management education is helpful once she is stable but does not replace urgent review. Airway clearance treats the sound rather than the cause, since crackles here come from fluid and not retained secretions."}, {"id": 1806, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Greco's daughter asks what her mother can check at home between physiotherapy visits to detect fluid build-up early. Heart failure education should emphasise one simple measure that reflects fluid retention reliably over days. Which self-monitoring measure should the physiotherapist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Log daily step counts with a pedometer and report any sustained drop in the weekly total", "B": "Measure ankle circumference weekly with a tape at a marked landmark, comparing with last week", "C": "Weigh each morning on the same scales after toileting and report steady gains", "D": "Record resting pulse oximetry readings three times daily and report any fall below her usual baseline"}, "cluster_id": "CHS212", "cluster_text": "Francesca Greco, 82, lives alone and receives a Home Care Package through a community aged care provider. Diagnoses: chronic heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, hypertension and osteoarthritis of both knees. Medications include a diuretic and a beta blocker. Today she reports needing an extra pillow at night and ankle swelling that has worsened over four days. Her weight is 2.5 kg above the usual weight the community nurse recorded last week. Observations: HR 96 regular, BP 108/66, RR 22, SpO2 92 percent on room air, bibasal crackles, pitting oedema to mid shin. She walks 15 metres with a frame before stopping for breathlessness (Borg 5). Her daughter, who is present, asks whether the package can fund twice weekly physiotherapy for exercise.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Weigh each morning on the same scales after toileting and report steady gains): Daily morning weight is the standard self-monitoring tool because fluid retention shows as weight gain before oedema or breathlessness become obvious. Using the same scales and conditions keeps readings comparable. Oximetry, ankle measurement and step counts can change with fluid but are less sensitive and less practical for early warning."}, {"id": 1807, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Two weeks later, after medical review and diuretic adjustment, her weight is stable and the crackles have cleared. She still stops after 15 metres with a frame because of breathlessness. How should the physiotherapist prescribe her initial walking exercise intensity and format?", "options": {"A": "One continuous walk, building by a few minutes each day, stopping when breathlessness reaches moderate", "B": "Walking at seventy percent of age predicted maximum heart rate, monitored with a finger sensor", "C": "Several short walks with seated rests, keeping breathlessness around moderate and conversation possible", "D": "Limb resistance training only, deferring aerobic work until she is free of breathlessness on exertion"}, "cluster_id": "CHS212", "cluster_text": "Francesca Greco, 82, lives alone and receives a Home Care Package through a community aged care provider. Diagnoses: chronic heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, hypertension and osteoarthritis of both knees. Medications include a diuretic and a beta blocker. Today she reports needing an extra pillow at night and ankle swelling that has worsened over four days. Her weight is 2.5 kg above the usual weight the community nurse recorded last week. Observations: HR 96 regular, BP 108/66, RR 22, SpO2 92 percent on room air, bibasal crackles, pitting oedema to mid shin. She walks 15 metres with a frame before stopping for breathlessness (Borg 5). Her daughter, who is present, asks whether the package can fund twice weekly physiotherapy for exercise.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Several short walks with seated rests, keeping breathlessness around moderate and conversation possible): Interval style walking with seated rests lets an older person with heart failure accumulate activity while keeping symptoms moderate, and perceived exertion is the guide. A single continuous walk is harder for someone who stops at 15 metres and risks pushing past her tolerance. Heart rate targets are unreliable on a beta blocker. Resistance work alone leaves out the aerobic training that benefits stable heart failure."}, {"id": 1808, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mrs Greco's daughter asks whether the Home Care Package can fund twice weekly physiotherapy for exercise. The care manager has told you most of the package budget already covers personal care and domestic help. How should the physiotherapist answer the daughter's question about funding?", "options": {"A": "Explain the package budget limits services and refer the family to the care manager", "B": "Explain physiotherapy is funded through the package only after a GP writes a referral", "C": "Agree to twice weekly sessions now and ask the care manager to adjust the budget later", "D": "Advise that Medicare will separately fund twice weekly sessions on top of the package budget"}, "cluster_id": "CHS212", "cluster_text": "Francesca Greco, 82, lives alone and receives a Home Care Package through a community aged care provider. Diagnoses: chronic heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, hypertension and osteoarthritis of both knees. Medications include a diuretic and a beta blocker. Today she reports needing an extra pillow at night and ankle swelling that has worsened over four days. Her weight is 2.5 kg above the usual weight the community nurse recorded last week. Observations: HR 96 regular, BP 108/66, RR 22, SpO2 92 percent on room air, bibasal crackles, pitting oedema to mid shin. She walks 15 metres with a frame before stopping for breathlessness (Borg 5). Her daughter, who is present, asks whether the package can fund twice weekly physiotherapy for exercise.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Explain the package budget limits services and refer the family to the care manager): Home Care Package funds are managed by the provider within an agreed budget and care plan, so the care manager and Mrs Greco weigh physiotherapy against other needs. Referring the family to the care manager gives an accurate and honest answer. Package funded physiotherapy does not hinge on a GP referral. Booking sessions before funding is agreed risks a billing dispute and bypasses the care plan. Any separate Medicare access would need checking and should not be promised."}, {"id": 1809, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "The GP referral lists both neurogenic and vascular causes for Ivan's leg symptoms. His symptoms ease with sitting and cycling, extension reproduces them, and pedal pulses are palpable. Which interpretation of these examination findings best favours neurogenic over vascular claudication?", "options": {"A": "Relief on flexion and provocation on extension point to canal narrowing, not arterial supply", "B": "Calf aching after a set distance suggests arterial disease, with cycling tolerated through different muscle demand", "C": "Hypertension and diabetes make spinal causes unlikely, so a vascular source is the most probable explanation", "D": "Easing with rest suggests ischaemic muscle pain, so vascular testing should precede any treatment"}, "cluster_id": "CHS213", "cluster_text": "Ivan Petrov, 74, retired bus driver, attends outpatient physiotherapy with a GP referral. He has bilateral buttock and calf aching and heaviness after walking about 200 metres, relieved by sitting or leaning on a shopping trolley. Symptoms ease when cycling. History: hypertension, type 2 diabetes and a BMI of 33; he takes metformin and an antihypertensive. Pedal pulses are palpable and equal. Lumbar extension reproduces buttock symptoms and flexion relieves them. Reflexes are symmetrical, myotomes 5/5, sensation intact, straight leg raise negative. The MRI report lists moderate central canal stenosis at L4/5. He has no bladder or bowel change. He wants to keep his daily walk with his dog but has been sitting more because walking hurts. Swimming is not accessible locally, though the clinic has a stationary bike.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Relief on flexion and provocation on extension point to canal narrowing, not arterial supply): In neurogenic claudication the symptoms depend on spinal posture: extension narrows the canal and provokes them, while flexion and cycling open it and relieve them. Vascular claudication follows a consistent walking distance and does not change with spinal position, and Ivan's palpable pulses further weaken that explanation."}, {"id": 1810, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Ivan wants to keep his daily dog walk but stops after about 200 metres and has been sitting more. Swimming is not accessible locally, but a stationary bike is available at the clinic. Which exercise advice best supports his walking capacity given his stenosis symptoms?", "options": {"A": "Walk through the symptoms each day and add distance gradually, as the discomfort is not harmful", "B": "Walk downhill on slopes to load the back extensors, which widens the canal and delays symptoms", "C": "Replace walking with cycling alone for now, then review whether short walks can be added later", "D": "Combine interval walking with planned rests and stationary cycling to build aerobic fitness"}, "cluster_id": "CHS213", "cluster_text": "Ivan Petrov, 74, retired bus driver, attends outpatient physiotherapy with a GP referral. He has bilateral buttock and calf aching and heaviness after walking about 200 metres, relieved by sitting or leaning on a shopping trolley. Symptoms ease when cycling. History: hypertension, type 2 diabetes and a BMI of 33; he takes metformin and an antihypertensive. Pedal pulses are palpable and equal. Lumbar extension reproduces buttock symptoms and flexion relieves them. Reflexes are symmetrical, myotomes 5/5, sensation intact, straight leg raise negative. The MRI report lists moderate central canal stenosis at L4/5. He has no bladder or bowel change. He wants to keep his daily walk with his dog but has been sitting more because walking hurts. Swimming is not accessible locally, though the clinic has a stationary bike.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Combine interval walking with planned rests and stationary cycling to build aerobic fitness): Short walking intervals with planned flexed or seated rests keep him practising the goal activity within tolerance, and cycling adds aerobic conditioning in a posture that is comfortable. Stopping walking loses the specific task he values, and downhill walking increases extension, which narrows the canal."}, {"id": 1811, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Ivan's symptoms ease in flexion and his neurological screen is normal. The physiotherapist plans a home programme for the first two weeks. He can spare about twenty minutes daily at home. Which exercise selection is most appropriate to prescribe initially?", "options": {"A": "Hip flexor lunge stretches with an arched lower back and standing back bends each morning", "B": "Passive modalities such as heat and ultrasound over the lumbar spine, with relative rest from walking", "C": "Prone press ups and sustained lumbar extension holds to centralise leg symptoms and widen the canal", "D": "Pelvic tilts, knee to chest stretches and gentle trunk stabilisation in positions of lumbar flexion"}, "cluster_id": "CHS213", "cluster_text": "Ivan Petrov, 74, retired bus driver, attends outpatient physiotherapy with a GP referral. He has bilateral buttock and calf aching and heaviness after walking about 200 metres, relieved by sitting or leaning on a shopping trolley. Symptoms ease when cycling. History: hypertension, type 2 diabetes and a BMI of 33; he takes metformin and an antihypertensive. Pedal pulses are palpable and equal. Lumbar extension reproduces buttock symptoms and flexion relieves them. Reflexes are symmetrical, myotomes 5/5, sensation intact, straight leg raise negative. The MRI report lists moderate central canal stenosis at L4/5. He has no bladder or bowel change. He wants to keep his daily walk with his dog but has been sitting more because walking hurts. Swimming is not accessible locally, though the clinic has a stationary bike.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Pelvic tilts, knee to chest stretches and gentle trunk stabilisation in positions of lumbar flexion): Flexion based positions increase canal space and are tolerated in this presentation, so they form a sensible base for early exercise. Extension exercises suit disc patterns that centralise with extension, not stenosis. Passive modalities alone do not build capacity or change function."}, {"id": 1812, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Ivan has a BMI of 33, type 2 diabetes and hypertension and is sitting more. He agrees weight and fitness matter. He says he forgets exercises and works best with simple routines. Which approach best supports his self-management beyond the clinic?", "options": {"A": "Set a small activity goal with him, link it to dog walks and review it", "B": "Hand him a printed sheet of twelve exercises to complete daily, and review it next visit", "C": "Provide a detailed weight loss meal plan from the clinic, and arrange review in three months", "D": "Refer him to a supervised gym class and drop the home programme to avoid overloading him"}, "cluster_id": "CHS213", "cluster_text": "Ivan Petrov, 74, retired bus driver, attends outpatient physiotherapy with a GP referral. He has bilateral buttock and calf aching and heaviness after walking about 200 metres, relieved by sitting or leaning on a shopping trolley. Symptoms ease when cycling. History: hypertension, type 2 diabetes and a BMI of 33; he takes metformin and an antihypertensive. Pedal pulses are palpable and equal. Lumbar extension reproduces buttock symptoms and flexion relieves them. Reflexes are symmetrical, myotomes 5/5, sensation intact, straight leg raise negative. The MRI report lists moderate central canal stenosis at L4/5. He has no bladder or bowel change. He wants to keep his daily walk with his dog but has been sitting more because walking hurts. Swimming is not accessible locally, though the clinic has a stationary bike.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Set a small activity goal with him, link it to dog walks and review it): Self-management is strongest when the person sets a small, specific goal tied to a routine he already values, with follow-up to adjust it. A long exercise sheet suits a motivated, organised person, a detailed meal plan is dietitian territory, and dropping home practice removes his daily activity."}, {"id": 1813, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Jack was injured while accelerating at full pace, with his right leg swinging forward before foot strike. When explaining the injury to him, which mechanism best accounts for hamstring strain during sprinting, and should therefore shape the rehabilitation plan you describe?", "options": {"A": "Concentric hamstring shortening against resistance in early stance while the planted foot pushes into the ground", "B": "Eccentric lengthening of the hamstrings in late swing as they decelerate the extending knee", "C": "Repeated compressive impact at the ischial tuberosity attachment during ground contact, overloading the proximal tendon", "D": "Sudden passive stretch of relaxed hamstrings while the quadriceps contract concentrically during mid stance loading"}, "cluster_id": "CHS214", "cluster_text": "Jack Nguyen, 22, is a semi-professional football midfielder who attends your private practice five days after feeling a sudden pain at the back of his right thigh while accelerating in a match. He limped off and bruising appeared on day two. He has no back pain, numbness or tingling and no previous hamstring injury. He walks with a shortened stride. Findings: tender over the proximal muscle belly of biceps femoris; prone knee flexion against resistance at 90 degrees reproduces pain at 5/10 with 4/5 strength; passive straight leg raise is 60 degrees on the right and 85 degrees on the left, limited by posterior thigh pain; slump test and lumbar screening do not reproduce his symptoms. Observations are normal. Jack has signed the clinic's standard consent form, which does not mention sharing information with third parties. The club's team manager has phoned the clinic about injured players before.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Eccentric lengthening of the hamstrings in late swing as they decelerate the extending knee): Sprinting strains typically occur when the hamstrings work eccentrically in late swing, braking the leg before foot contact. This is why rehabilitation later needs eccentric and high speed exposure. Early stance concentric work is a normal hamstring role but is not the usual injury phase, and a relaxed, passively stretched muscle is not the load pattern of sprinting."}, {"id": 1814, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "At day five Jack walks with a shortened stride, and resisted prone knee flexion reproduces pain at 5/10 with mild weakness. He wants to know what to do this week. Which management approach is most appropriate at this stage of his hamstring strain?", "options": {"A": "Static hamstring stretching to strong discomfort several times daily to restore length before strengthening", "B": "Therapeutic ultrasound and deep friction massage over the bruise to speed fibre repair before exercise", "C": "Pain-limited isometric hamstring work and walking, progressing load gradually as symptoms allow", "D": "Rest from all loading until pain settles, then restart with progressive sprint drills"}, "cluster_id": "CHS214", "cluster_text": "Jack Nguyen, 22, is a semi-professional football midfielder who attends your private practice five days after feeling a sudden pain at the back of his right thigh while accelerating in a match. He limped off and bruising appeared on day two. He has no back pain, numbness or tingling and no previous hamstring injury. He walks with a shortened stride. Findings: tender over the proximal muscle belly of biceps femoris; prone knee flexion against resistance at 90 degrees reproduces pain at 5/10 with 4/5 strength; passive straight leg raise is 60 degrees on the right and 85 degrees on the left, limited by posterior thigh pain; slump test and lumbar screening do not reproduce his symptoms. Observations are normal. Jack has signed the clinic's standard consent form, which does not mention sharing information with third parties. The club's team manager has phoned the clinic about injured players before.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Pain-limited isometric hamstring work and walking, progressing load gradually as symptoms allow): Early, pain-limited loading and walking promote tissue healing and maintain function, with progression guided by symptoms. Aggressive stretching risks aggravating the injured fibres, and prolonged rest followed by sprinting jumps over the graded loading the muscle needs. Passive modalities have little evidence of speeding healing compared with active loading."}, {"id": 1815, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Three weeks later Jack jogs without pain, has full pain-free stretch range and near equal strength on testing. He has not yet sprinted or changed direction at speed, but asks to rejoin full team training tomorrow. What is the most appropriate decision?", "options": {"A": "Progress through graded sprint and eccentric loading, confirming tolerance before full training", "B": "Clear him for full training because strength and range now match the uninjured leg", "C": "Allow full training with a compression garment and taping to protect the healing muscle", "D": "Continue jogging only for another month so the muscle remodels before any sprinting"}, "cluster_id": "CHS214", "cluster_text": "Jack Nguyen, 22, is a semi-professional football midfielder who attends your private practice five days after feeling a sudden pain at the back of his right thigh while accelerating in a match. He limped off and bruising appeared on day two. He has no back pain, numbness or tingling and no previous hamstring injury. He walks with a shortened stride. Findings: tender over the proximal muscle belly of biceps femoris; prone knee flexion against resistance at 90 degrees reproduces pain at 5/10 with 4/5 strength; passive straight leg raise is 60 degrees on the right and 85 degrees on the left, limited by posterior thigh pain; slump test and lumbar screening do not reproduce his symptoms. Observations are normal. Jack has signed the clinic's standard consent form, which does not mention sharing information with third parties. The club's team manager has phoned the clinic about injured players before.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Progress through graded sprint and eccentric loading, confirming tolerance before full training): Jogging and static strength tests do not show that the hamstring tolerates the high speed eccentric demand that caused the injury, so sprint exposure must be graded and tolerance confirmed first. Clearing him now ignores that gap. Prolonged jogging delays the specific loading he needs, and garments or tape do not substitute for it."}, {"id": 1816, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "The football club's team manager phones the clinic asking for Jack's diagnosis and expected return date. Jack is an adult, signed only the standard consent form, and has not mentioned any wish to share details. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Give a brief summary because the manager has a legitimate interest in squad availability and safety", "B": "Email the full notes to the club physiotherapist, as a fellow health professional caring for him", "C": "Confirm the diagnosis only, since a return date is a prediction and not personal health information", "D": "Decline to share details and ask Jack what information he agrees to release to the club"}, "cluster_id": "CHS214", "cluster_text": "Jack Nguyen, 22, is a semi-professional football midfielder who attends your private practice five days after feeling a sudden pain at the back of his right thigh while accelerating in a match. He limped off and bruising appeared on day two. He has no back pain, numbness or tingling and no previous hamstring injury. He walks with a shortened stride. Findings: tender over the proximal muscle belly of biceps femoris; prone knee flexion against resistance at 90 degrees reproduces pain at 5/10 with 4/5 strength; passive straight leg raise is 60 degrees on the right and 85 degrees on the left, limited by posterior thigh pain; slump test and lumbar screening do not reproduce his symptoms. Observations are normal. Jack has signed the clinic's standard consent form, which does not mention sharing information with third parties. The club's team manager has phoned the clinic about injured players before.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Decline to share details and ask Jack what information he agrees to release to the club): Health information belongs to the patient and is disclosed to a third party only with his informed consent or a legal requirement. The manager's interest does not create either. A diagnosis and a return estimate are both personal health information, and even a club clinician needs Jack's agreement before receiving his notes."}, {"id": 1817, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Gordon freezes at doorways and when turning, then almost falls. He can otherwise walk ten metres with a shuffling gait. The physiotherapist wants to teach a strategy that helps him restart stepping when his feet feel stuck. Which approach is most appropriate to teach first?", "options": {"A": "Lean forward and take quick, larger steps to build momentum before reaching the doorway", "B": "Step over a visual cue such as a floor line, or step to a steady beat", "C": "Stand still and wait for the stiffness to ease, then push forward with one large step", "D": "Count backwards aloud while walking so that attention is taken away from the stepping itself"}, "cluster_id": "CHS215", "cluster_text": "Uncle Gordon Mitchell, 76, attends the Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service in a remote community. He has had Parkinson disease for six years and takes levodopa three times daily. He says his legs lock at doorways and when turning. He has had two falls in three months, without injury. Findings: shuffling gait with stooped posture, freezing at turns, Timed Up and Go 18 seconds, and he needed catching by the examiner during a pull test. Blood pressure lying is 130/80 mmHg and after three minutes standing is 108/68 mmHg, with light-headedness. His walking is best about an hour after a dose. His first language is Pitjantjatjara and English is his second. His niece Maureen says the family wants a home walking program and that Gordon will agree to whatever is suggested. Gordon looks at the floor and has not said what he wants. A cognitive screen at the last review showed mild impairment.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Step over a visual cue such as a floor line, or step to a steady beat): External visual or rhythmic cues bypass the impaired automatic stepping of Parkinson disease and are the first strategy taught for freezing. Quick leaning steps increase festination and fall risk, and a divided-attention task such as counting usually makes freezing worse. Waiting and then pushing off with a large step gives the movement no cue to trigger it."}, {"id": 1818, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Gordon's blood pressure falls from 130/80 mmHg lying to 108/68 mmHg after three minutes standing, and he feels light-headed. He is keen to start walking practice at home today. What is the most appropriate physiotherapy action before starting the walking program?", "options": {"A": "Begin seated exercise only and recheck his standing blood pressure at the next monthly review", "B": "Start the walking program, as a drop like this is expected with levodopa and settles", "C": "Defer all exercise until a neurologist reviews him, and advise him to remain seated daily", "D": "Teach slow position changes with supervised practice, and report the readings to his doctor"}, "cluster_id": "CHS215", "cluster_text": "Uncle Gordon Mitchell, 76, attends the Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service in a remote community. He has had Parkinson disease for six years and takes levodopa three times daily. He says his legs lock at doorways and when turning. He has had two falls in three months, without injury. Findings: shuffling gait with stooped posture, freezing at turns, Timed Up and Go 18 seconds, and he needed catching by the examiner during a pull test. Blood pressure lying is 130/80 mmHg and after three minutes standing is 108/68 mmHg, with light-headedness. His walking is best about an hour after a dose. His first language is Pitjantjatjara and English is his second. His niece Maureen says the family wants a home walking program and that Gordon will agree to whatever is suggested. Gordon looks at the floor and has not said what he wants. A cognitive screen at the last review showed mild impairment.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Teach slow position changes with supervised practice, and report the readings to his doctor): A fall of about 20 mmHg systolic with symptoms suggests orthostatic hypotension, which raises fall risk in Parkinson disease and may relate to medication, so the doctor should know. Slow, supervised position changes reduce the risk during exercise. Waiting a month or stopping all exercise is unnecessary, and dismissing the drop as normal ignores a modifiable fall risk."}, {"id": 1819, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Maureen tells the physiotherapist that Gordon will agree to whatever is suggested. Gordon has not spoken about the program and had mild cognitive impairment on screening. The clinic is busy and relatives are waiting outside. Which action best supports valid informed consent?", "options": {"A": "Explain the program slowly in English, then treat his nod as consent and proceed", "B": "Presume capacity, explain the program to Gordon with an interpreter, and ask who he wants involved", "C": "Arrange a formal capacity assessment before any treatment, because the screening showed mild cognitive impairment", "D": "Accept Maureen's agreement for him, as family decision making is valued, and note consent"}, "cluster_id": "CHS215", "cluster_text": "Uncle Gordon Mitchell, 76, attends the Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service in a remote community. He has had Parkinson disease for six years and takes levodopa three times daily. He says his legs lock at doorways and when turning. He has had two falls in three months, without injury. Findings: shuffling gait with stooped posture, freezing at turns, Timed Up and Go 18 seconds, and he needed catching by the examiner during a pull test. Blood pressure lying is 130/80 mmHg and after three minutes standing is 108/68 mmHg, with light-headedness. His walking is best about an hour after a dose. His first language is Pitjantjatjara and English is his second. His niece Maureen says the family wants a home walking program and that Gordon will agree to whatever is suggested. Gordon looks at the floor and has not said what he wants. A cognitive screen at the last review showed mild impairment.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Presume capacity, explain the program to Gordon with an interpreter, and ask who he wants involved): Adults are presumed to have capacity and mild impairment does not remove it, so Gordon must receive information in a form he understands, here with an interpreter, and be asked who he wants involved. Family can support him but cannot consent for a person who has capacity. A formal assessment before any treatment is not needed unless there is real doubt about his decision making."}, {"id": 1820, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Gordon consents through the interpreter. He walks best about an hour after a levodopa dose and has had two recent falls. The physiotherapist is planning his first home walking session with Maureen, who lives next door. How should the session be arranged?", "options": {"A": "Schedule it in his best medication window, with Maureen beside him and clear space around", "B": "Schedule it in his best window and let Maureen set the walking goals on his behalf", "C": "Schedule it in his best window with Maureen beside him, using a long outdoor route", "D": "Schedule it in his best window, with him walking alone for independence while Maureen waits indoors"}, "cluster_id": "CHS215", "cluster_text": "Uncle Gordon Mitchell, 76, attends the Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service in a remote community. He has had Parkinson disease for six years and takes levodopa three times daily. He says his legs lock at doorways and when turning. He has had two falls in three months, without injury. Findings: shuffling gait with stooped posture, freezing at turns, Timed Up and Go 18 seconds, and he needed catching by the examiner during a pull test. Blood pressure lying is 130/80 mmHg and after three minutes standing is 108/68 mmHg, with light-headedness. His walking is best about an hour after a dose. His first language is Pitjantjatjara and English is his second. His niece Maureen says the family wants a home walking program and that Gordon will agree to whatever is suggested. Gordon looks at the floor and has not said what he wants. A cognitive screen at the last review showed mild impairment.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Schedule it in his best medication window, with Maureen beside him and clear space around): Practice during the on period, when levodopa is working best, gives the safest and most productive movement, and a nearby person limits the harm from a fall given his recent falls and positive pull test. Clear space reduces triggers for freezing. Letting a family member set goals overrides Gordon's own voice, which he has not yet been given a chance to express. The long outdoor route over uneven ground increases freezing and fall risk in a first session. Leaving him to walk alone removes the safety net."}, {"id": 1821, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Tran freezes most severely about 30 minutes before his next levodopa dose and walks more smoothly an hour after taking it. The physiotherapist is planning when to schedule gait and balance training. Which statement about freezing of gait is correct?", "options": {"A": "Freezing mostly occurs at peak medication effect, so training should be scheduled during wearing-off periods", "B": "Freezing is mostly caused by anxiety at doorways, so relaxation training alone will settle it", "C": "Freezing often worsens during medication wearing-off, so training is best timed in the on phase", "D": "Freezing results from muscle rigidity, so stretching before walking is the main way to settle it"}, "cluster_id": "CHS216", "cluster_text": "Mr Binh Truong Tran, 79, lives in a residential aged care home. He has had Parkinson disease for nine years and takes levodopa four times daily. He has had two falls in three months, both when he stopped suddenly at a doorway. Findings: freezing of gait at doorways and when turning, walks well in open corridors, shuffling steps with reduced arm swing, mild cognitive impairment, no orthostatic drop. Timed Up and Go 22 seconds with a four-wheeled walker. Freezing is worst about 30 minutes before his next levodopa dose. Staff report he is rushed at mealtimes. Weight 64 kg, BP 130/80, pulse 74. The facility manager wants to evaluate a new falls and freezing program for residents with Parkinson disease.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Freezing often worsens during medication wearing-off, so training is best timed in the on phase): Freezing tends to worsen when medication effect is low, so sessions are best timed in the on phase when he can move most freely and practise safely. Scheduling during wearing-off increases fall risk during practice and reverses the pattern he shows. Anxiety can aggravate freezing, but relaxation training does not treat it and does not guide timing. Freezing is not simply rigidity, so stretching alone does not resolve it."}, {"id": 1822, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Tran freezes when turning and in narrow doorways but walks well in open corridors. His falls occurred when he stopped abruptly at a doorway. He can follow simple instructions despite mild cognitive impairment. Which gait training strategy is most appropriate to teach him?", "options": {"A": "External cues such as floor lines or a beat, with wide turning arcs instead of pivots", "B": "Rapid small shuffling steps to push through each freeze, keeping the walker in front", "C": "Internal cues such as thinking big steps, with tight turns on the spot to practise pivoting", "D": "Dual-task walking while counting backwards, to automate stepping and reduce reliance on conscious attention"}, "cluster_id": "CHS216", "cluster_text": "Mr Binh Truong Tran, 79, lives in a residential aged care home. He has had Parkinson disease for nine years and takes levodopa four times daily. He has had two falls in three months, both when he stopped suddenly at a doorway. Findings: freezing of gait at doorways and when turning, walks well in open corridors, shuffling steps with reduced arm swing, mild cognitive impairment, no orthostatic drop. Timed Up and Go 22 seconds with a four-wheeled walker. Freezing is worst about 30 minutes before his next levodopa dose. Staff report he is rushed at mealtimes. Weight 64 kg, BP 130/80, pulse 74. The facility manager wants to evaluate a new falls and freezing program for residents with Parkinson disease.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (External cues such as floor lines or a beat, with wide turning arcs instead of pivots): External cues such as lines on the floor or a rhythmic beat bypass the impaired automatic stepping pathway and help him start and keep stepping, and wide arcs avoid the tight pivots that provoke freezing. Internal cueing is less effective with cognitive impairment, shuffling to push through a freeze raises fall risk, and dual-task practice tends to worsen freezing."}, {"id": 1823, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "A care worker, in a hurry, pulls Mr Tran forward by the arm when he freezes at the doorway, and he nearly falls. The physiotherapist is now teaching staff what to do during a freezing episode. Which instruction is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Guide him forward with firm traction on his arm so momentum carries him through the doorway", "B": "Wait quietly beside him until the freeze passes on its own, without prompting him", "C": "Encourage him to try harder and walk straight on, allowing several quick attempts before offering help", "D": "Pause, prompt a weight shift, then give an external cue such as a floor line"}, "cluster_id": "CHS216", "cluster_text": "Mr Binh Truong Tran, 79, lives in a residential aged care home. He has had Parkinson disease for nine years and takes levodopa four times daily. He has had two falls in three months, both when he stopped suddenly at a doorway. Findings: freezing of gait at doorways and when turning, walks well in open corridors, shuffling steps with reduced arm swing, mild cognitive impairment, no orthostatic drop. Timed Up and Go 22 seconds with a four-wheeled walker. Freezing is worst about 30 minutes before his next levodopa dose. Staff report he is rushed at mealtimes. Weight 64 kg, BP 130/80, pulse 74. The facility manager wants to evaluate a new falls and freezing program for residents with Parkinson disease.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Pause, prompt a weight shift, then give an external cue such as a floor line): Pulling or rushing a person who is frozen increases the risk of a fall because his feet stay fixed while his upper body moves forward. Pausing, shifting weight and using an external cue lets the movement restart safely. Waiting without a cue leaves the freeze to persist and the person to become more anxious. Urging him to try harder and repeat quick attempts prolongs the freeze and raises the chance of a stumble."}, {"id": 1824, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "The facility wants to evaluate a falls and freezing program for residents with Parkinson disease, and asks the physiotherapist to choose how to measure progress. Mr Tran's Timed Up and Go time is 22 seconds at baseline. Which approach is most appropriate for quality improvement?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the Timed Up and Go alone at set intervals, since it reflects freezing and falls", "B": "Use satisfaction surveys at set intervals, since performance tests burden residents with cognitive change", "C": "Count falls from the program start and compare the total with similar facilities", "D": "Repeat the same validated measures at set intervals, including falls and a freezing questionnaire"}, "cluster_id": "CHS216", "cluster_text": "Mr Binh Truong Tran, 79, lives in a residential aged care home. He has had Parkinson disease for nine years and takes levodopa four times daily. He has had two falls in three months, both when he stopped suddenly at a doorway. Findings: freezing of gait at doorways and when turning, walks well in open corridors, shuffling steps with reduced arm swing, mild cognitive impairment, no orthostatic drop. Timed Up and Go 22 seconds with a four-wheeled walker. Freezing is worst about 30 minutes before his next levodopa dose. Staff report he is rushed at mealtimes. Weight 64 kg, BP 130/80, pulse 74. The facility manager wants to evaluate a new falls and freezing program for residents with Parkinson disease.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Repeat the same validated measures at set intervals, including falls and a freezing questionnaire): Repeating the same valid, reliable measures at fixed intervals, with falls recorded, lets change be tracked against baseline and reported to the facility. The Timed Up and Go is a useful measure but alone it does not capture freezing or falls, which the program targets. Satisfaction surveys describe experience, not function, and validated tests can be used with mild cognitive change. Falls counted without a baseline and compared with other facilities do not show whether this program worked."}, {"id": 1825, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Ron has a documented history of carbon dioxide retention during a previous admission. The physiotherapist is setting an oxygen saturation target for his walking program at home. Which target range is most appropriate to work within during exercise for a person with this risk?", "options": {"A": "An SpO2 of 94 to 98 percent, which is the standard range used for healthy adults", "B": "An SpO2 of 88 to 92 percent, which avoids both hypoxaemia and excess oxygen", "C": "An SpO2 of 92 to 96 percent, which keeps a margin above desaturation", "D": "An SpO2 of 85 to 88 percent, which is acceptable once exercise begins"}, "cluster_id": "CHS217", "cluster_text": "Ron Hargreaves, 74, a retired plumber, lives with his wife in a single-storey home. He has severe COPD (FEV1 32 percent predicted), is an ex-smoker, and uses home oxygen at 2 L/min from a concentrator with 15 metres of tubing running through the hallway. A previous admission documented carbon dioxide retention. Community physiotherapy home visit for exercise prescription. At rest: SpO2 91 percent on 2 L/min, HR 88, RR 20. After walking 40 metres to the letterbox on 2 L/min: SpO2 84 percent, HR 112, RR 28, Borg dyspnoea 6 out of 10; SpO2 returned to 90 percent after four minutes of seated pursed lip breathing. His wife reports that he has twice turned the flow up to 4 L/min himself when breathless. During the visit Ron caught his foot on the tubing and stumbled against the wall. He was not hurt.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (An SpO2 of 88 to 92 percent, which avoids both hypoxaemia and excess oxygen): People who retain carbon dioxide can worsen their hypercapnia when given too much oxygen, so Australian practice uses a lower target of 88 to 92 percent. The ranges used for healthy adults risk hyperoxia in Ron. A target below 88 percent would leave him hypoxaemic during activity."}, {"id": 1826, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "During the walk Ron's SpO2 fell to 84 percent with a Borg dyspnoea score of 6, then recovered to 90 percent after seated pursed lip breathing. How should the physiotherapist plan his ongoing walking program in light of this response and his home oxygen?", "options": {"A": "Use walking intervals with rests and add pursed lip breathing, continuing at the current flow", "B": "Continue continuous walking to build tolerance, as exercise desaturation is common in severe COPD", "C": "Use walking intervals with rests and ask his doctor to review exertional oxygen flow", "D": "Raise the concentrator flow during walks until his saturation stays near resting levels"}, "cluster_id": "CHS217", "cluster_text": "Ron Hargreaves, 74, a retired plumber, lives with his wife in a single-storey home. He has severe COPD (FEV1 32 percent predicted), is an ex-smoker, and uses home oxygen at 2 L/min from a concentrator with 15 metres of tubing running through the hallway. A previous admission documented carbon dioxide retention. Community physiotherapy home visit for exercise prescription. At rest: SpO2 91 percent on 2 L/min, HR 88, RR 20. After walking 40 metres to the letterbox on 2 L/min: SpO2 84 percent, HR 112, RR 28, Borg dyspnoea 6 out of 10; SpO2 returned to 90 percent after four minutes of seated pursed lip breathing. His wife reports that he has twice turned the flow up to 4 L/min himself when breathless. During the visit Ron caught his foot on the tubing and stumbled against the wall. He was not hurt.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use walking intervals with rests and ask his doctor to review exertional oxygen flow): Ron desaturates on his current flow, so paced intervals with recovery periods limit the fall while the prescriber reviews whether his exertional oxygen needs to change. Oxygen flow is a prescribed treatment, and a person who retains carbon dioxide can be harmed by unprescribed increases. Intervals with breathing control alone leave a significant desaturation unreviewed. Pushing through desaturation with continuous walking is common but does not improve tolerance safely."}, {"id": 1827, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Ron's wife says he turns the concentrator to 4 L/min whenever he feels breathless, and he then becomes drowsy and headachy. The physiotherapist wants to explain the risk to the couple. Which explanation is most appropriate and within physiotherapy scope?", "options": {"A": "Extra oxygen can worsen carbon dioxide retention, so flow changes need a prescriber's advice", "B": "Higher flow is safe during breathless episodes if he reduces it again once he has rested", "C": "Symptoms suggest oxygen toxicity from the higher flow, so he should turn it back down", "D": "Drowsiness means his oxygen is too low, so the physiotherapist should advise a higher flow"}, "cluster_id": "CHS217", "cluster_text": "Ron Hargreaves, 74, a retired plumber, lives with his wife in a single-storey home. He has severe COPD (FEV1 32 percent predicted), is an ex-smoker, and uses home oxygen at 2 L/min from a concentrator with 15 metres of tubing running through the hallway. A previous admission documented carbon dioxide retention. Community physiotherapy home visit for exercise prescription. At rest: SpO2 91 percent on 2 L/min, HR 88, RR 20. After walking 40 metres to the letterbox on 2 L/min: SpO2 84 percent, HR 112, RR 28, Borg dyspnoea 6 out of 10; SpO2 returned to 90 percent after four minutes of seated pursed lip breathing. His wife reports that he has twice turned the flow up to 4 L/min himself when breathless. During the visit Ron caught his foot on the tubing and stumbled against the wall. He was not hurt.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Extra oxygen can worsen carbon dioxide retention, so flow changes need a prescriber's advice): Drowsiness and headache after raising the flow fit rising carbon dioxide in a person who retains it, rather than low oxygen. The physiotherapist can explain this and direct the couple back to the prescribed rate with medical review. Self adjusting up and down during episodes still risks carbon dioxide build up each time. Oxygen toxicity is the wrong mechanism at these flows, and turning it down without advice from the prescriber leaves the breathlessness unaddressed. Advising a higher flow worsens the hypercapnia."}, {"id": 1828, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "After Ron stumbled on the oxygen tubing he asks the physiotherapist not to write anything down because no one was hurt. The tubing crosses a hallway used daily by his wife and visiting carers. What is the most appropriate action regarding this near miss?", "options": {"A": "Tell the GP verbally at the next review and keep it out of the clinical record", "B": "Respect his wish and record nothing, since incident reports are only needed when harm occurs", "C": "Record it, make the tubing safer with Ron, and lodge an incident report", "D": "Tape the tubing along the wall and keep the matter within the clinical file"}, "cluster_id": "CHS217", "cluster_text": "Ron Hargreaves, 74, a retired plumber, lives with his wife in a single-storey home. He has severe COPD (FEV1 32 percent predicted), is an ex-smoker, and uses home oxygen at 2 L/min from a concentrator with 15 metres of tubing running through the hallway. A previous admission documented carbon dioxide retention. Community physiotherapy home visit for exercise prescription. At rest: SpO2 91 percent on 2 L/min, HR 88, RR 20. After walking 40 metres to the letterbox on 2 L/min: SpO2 84 percent, HR 112, RR 28, Borg dyspnoea 6 out of 10; SpO2 returned to 90 percent after four minutes of seated pursed lip breathing. His wife reports that he has twice turned the flow up to 4 L/min himself when breathless. During the visit Ron caught his foot on the tubing and stumbled against the wall. He was not hurt.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Record it, make the tubing safer with Ron, and lodge an incident report): A near miss with a known hazard is a reportable incident, because reporting lets the organisation manage the risk for other clients and staff. The physiotherapist should also document it and reduce the hazard with Ron. Fixing the tubing alone or telling the GP informally leaves the incident system unused."}, {"id": 1829, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Zayn was born at 32 weeks and is now 8 months old. A colleague says his motor skills after this bronchiolitis admission are well behind a typical 8 month old. Which age should the physiotherapist use for comparison, and why?", "options": {"A": "His gestational age at birth of 32 weeks, since this reflects his neurological maturity best", "B": "A midpoint of 7 months, because illness and prematurity both affect development to a similar extent", "C": "His corrected age of 6 months, because prematurity shifts expected milestones until about two years", "D": "His chronological age of 8 months, because milestone charts are standardised to time since birth"}, "cluster_id": "CHS218", "cluster_text": "Zayn Haddad, 8 months old, was born at 32 weeks and is now on the paediatric ward recovering from bronchiolitis. He is fed through a nasogastric tube while oral feeds are re-established. His parents say he is not rolling and feels floppy. Observation: head lag on pull to sit, hands brought to midline inconsistently, prefers lying on his back. In prone he lifts his head briefly but does not prop on his forearms. Trunk tone is low and limb reflexes are normal. Social smile and visual tracking are appropriate. HR 138, RR 40, SpO2 97% on room air. The nasogastric tube is taped to his cheek and is checked by nursing staff each shift.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (His corrected age of 6 months, because prematurity shifts expected milestones until about two years): For infants born preterm, milestones are judged against corrected age, calculated from the expected due date, until around two years. Using chronological age would exaggerate any delay and could trigger unnecessary concern. Averaging ages has no basis in developmental assessment."}, {"id": 1830, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "In prone Zayn lifts his head briefly but does not prop on his forearms, and he cries after a minute. Parents ask how to help during the rest of his admission and after discharge. Which advice best supports his motor development?", "options": {"A": "Use a baby bouncer for extended periods in upright to strengthen his trunk and leg muscles", "B": "Offer brief, frequent supervised tummy time, with a rolled towel under his chest for support", "C": "Avoid tummy time until he can lift his head well in supported sitting, to prevent distress", "D": "Keep him mostly supine with toys overhead, as he is not yet strong enough for prone"}, "cluster_id": "CHS218", "cluster_text": "Zayn Haddad, 8 months old, was born at 32 weeks and is now on the paediatric ward recovering from bronchiolitis. He is fed through a nasogastric tube while oral feeds are re-established. His parents say he is not rolling and feels floppy. Observation: head lag on pull to sit, hands brought to midline inconsistently, prefers lying on his back. In prone he lifts his head briefly but does not prop on his forearms. Trunk tone is low and limb reflexes are normal. Social smile and visual tracking are appropriate. HR 138, RR 40, SpO2 97% on room air. The nasogastric tube is taped to his cheek and is checked by nursing staff each shift.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Offer brief, frequent supervised tummy time, with a rolled towel under his chest for support): Short, frequent periods of supervised prone play with chest support build neck and trunk extensor strength and tolerance gradually. Prolonged upright equipment places him in a posture he cannot control and does not train extensors. Avoiding prone or relying on supine delays the very skills he is missing."}, {"id": 1831, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Zayn finished a nasogastric feed 20 minutes ago. You plan tummy time practice with him. He is settled and his observations are stable. The tube is taped securely to his cheek. What is the most appropriate way to proceed with the session?", "options": {"A": "Aspirate the tube first to empty his stomach, then proceed with prone play as planned", "B": "Cancel the session today because handling with a nasogastric tube in place risks displacing it", "C": "Defer prone play, or use semi-reclined or side-lying positions, after checking the tube is secure", "D": "Proceed with prone play now, since the tube is secured and his observations are stable"}, "cluster_id": "CHS218", "cluster_text": "Zayn Haddad, 8 months old, was born at 32 weeks and is now on the paediatric ward recovering from bronchiolitis. He is fed through a nasogastric tube while oral feeds are re-established. His parents say he is not rolling and feels floppy. Observation: head lag on pull to sit, hands brought to midline inconsistently, prefers lying on his back. In prone he lifts his head briefly but does not prop on his forearms. Trunk tone is low and limb reflexes are normal. Social smile and visual tracking are appropriate. HR 138, RR 40, SpO2 97% on room air. The nasogastric tube is taped to his cheek and is checked by nursing staff each shift.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Defer prone play, or use semi-reclined or side-lying positions, after checking the tube is secure): Prone positioning soon after a feed increases the risk of reflux and vomiting, so prone play is better delayed while less loaded positions are used. Aspirating a feeding tube is not part of the physiotherapist's role. Having a tube does not prevent handling provided it is secured and managed carefully."}, {"id": 1832, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "While you lift Zayn for the next session, his nasogastric tube catches on the cot rail and is pulled partly out. He coughs briefly then settles, with SpO2 96% and no distress. What is the most appropriate sequence of actions?", "options": {"A": "Stay with him, alert the nurse to check the tube, then complete an incident report", "B": "Gently reposition the tube to its mark and continue, then record the event in his notes", "C": "Tell the parents it was minor and record it in his notes, as no harm occurred", "D": "Continue the session as he is stable, and mention the event to the nurse at handover"}, "cluster_id": "CHS218", "cluster_text": "Zayn Haddad, 8 months old, was born at 32 weeks and is now on the paediatric ward recovering from bronchiolitis. He is fed through a nasogastric tube while oral feeds are re-established. His parents say he is not rolling and feels floppy. Observation: head lag on pull to sit, hands brought to midline inconsistently, prefers lying on his back. In prone he lifts his head briefly but does not prop on his forearms. Trunk tone is low and limb reflexes are normal. Social smile and visual tracking are appropriate. HR 138, RR 40, SpO2 97% on room air. The nasogastric tube is taped to his cheek and is checked by nursing staff each shift.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stay with him, alert the nurse to check the tube, then complete an incident report): A displaced feeding tube can cause aspiration if used, so a nurse must check its position, and the physiotherapist must not reposition it. The event is a reportable incident even without harm, so it should be entered in the incident system as well as the notes. Delaying notification or minimising it with the family undermines safety and transparency."}, {"id": 1833, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "The referral letter classifies Ms Tan's injury as ASIA impairment scale D, so the injury is neurologically incomplete. A student on placement asks what this grade specifically means for her motor function below the injury level. Which explanation is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Sensory function is preserved below the injury but there is no voluntary motor function", "B": "Sacral sensation and motor function are absent, although some strength is preserved above the injury", "C": "Motor function persists below the injury, with at least half of key muscles antigravity or stronger", "D": "Sensory and motor function have returned to normal on testing, with only residual spasticity remaining"}, "cluster_id": "CHS219", "cluster_text": "Jing Huang Tan, 34, graphic designer, attends an outpatient spinal rehabilitation clinic 5 months after a fall from a ladder caused a T10 incomplete spinal cord injury (ASIA impairment scale D). She walks with elbow crutches and a left ankle foot orthosis. Hip flexion and knee extension: right 4/5, left 3/5. Ankle dorsiflexion: right 3/5, left 2/5. Light touch is reduced below T10. Modified Ashworth score is 1+ in the left quadriceps. Walking speed over ten metres is slow, with left foot drag and reduced hip flexion in swing. Resting BP 118/72, HR 76. She has read online about robotic exoskeleton walking and brought a news article and a small uncontrolled study. She lives in a two-storey townhouse with her partner.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Motor function persists below the injury, with at least half of key muscles antigravity or stronger): Grade D means motor function is preserved below the neurological level and at least half of the key muscles there are antigravity strength or better. Absent sacral function describes a complete injury, and sensory-only preservation describes grade B. A normal examination would be grade E."}, {"id": 1834, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Ms Tan walks with left foot drag and reduced hip flexion in swing, and fatigues after 50 metres. She has left ankle dorsiflexion 2/5 and quadriceps strength 3/5. Which gait training approach is most appropriate to improve her walking at this stage?", "options": {"A": "Bed-based strengthening of the weak muscle groups until antigravity strength allows safe progression to standing activities", "B": "Task-specific walking practice overground and on a treadmill with an orthosis, graded for intensity", "C": "Robotic exoskeleton walking as the main gait training, since the study she read showed large gains", "D": "Treadmill walking with body weight support only, avoiding overground walking until her gait pattern is normal"}, "cluster_id": "CHS219", "cluster_text": "Jing Huang Tan, 34, graphic designer, attends an outpatient spinal rehabilitation clinic 5 months after a fall from a ladder caused a T10 incomplete spinal cord injury (ASIA impairment scale D). She walks with elbow crutches and a left ankle foot orthosis. Hip flexion and knee extension: right 4/5, left 3/5. Ankle dorsiflexion: right 3/5, left 2/5. Light touch is reduced below T10. Modified Ashworth score is 1+ in the left quadriceps. Walking speed over ten metres is slow, with left foot drag and reduced hip flexion in swing. Resting BP 118/72, HR 76. She has read online about robotic exoskeleton walking and brought a news article and a small uncontrolled study. She lives in a two-storey townhouse with her partner.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Task-specific walking practice overground and on a treadmill with an orthosis, graded for intensity): People with incomplete injury and walking potential improve most with repeated, task-specific, graded walking practice, using an orthosis for the foot drop. Waiting for strength to reach a threshold before standing delays the practice that drives recovery. An exoskeleton is not supported by the small uncontrolled study she brought and is not a replacement for task practice. Restricting her to supported treadmill work and withholding overground walking until the gait is normal prevents the varied practice she needs."}, {"id": 1835, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Ms Tan must climb the ten stairs at her townhouse each day. She uses one rail and one elbow crutch, and her right leg is stronger than her left. Which sequence should she be taught for ascending the stairs safely at home?", "options": {"A": "Take two steps at a time using the rail, to reduce fatigue over the full flight", "B": "Hop up each step on the stronger right leg alone, keeping the left leg lifted", "C": "Lead with the stronger right leg, then bring the left leg and crutch up together", "D": "Lead with the weaker left leg, then bring the right leg and crutch up together"}, "cluster_id": "CHS219", "cluster_text": "Jing Huang Tan, 34, graphic designer, attends an outpatient spinal rehabilitation clinic 5 months after a fall from a ladder caused a T10 incomplete spinal cord injury (ASIA impairment scale D). She walks with elbow crutches and a left ankle foot orthosis. Hip flexion and knee extension: right 4/5, left 3/5. Ankle dorsiflexion: right 3/5, left 2/5. Light touch is reduced below T10. Modified Ashworth score is 1+ in the left quadriceps. Walking speed over ten metres is slow, with left foot drag and reduced hip flexion in swing. Resting BP 118/72, HR 76. She has read online about robotic exoskeleton walking and brought a news article and a small uncontrolled study. She lives in a two-storey townhouse with her partner.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Lead with the stronger right leg, then bring the left leg and crutch up together): Going up, the stronger leg lifts the body weight to the next step first, and the weaker leg and crutch then follow together. Leading with the weaker leg would require it to lift her whole body weight and risks a fall. Skipping steps or hopping adds balance and fatigue demands she does not need."}, {"id": 1836, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Ms Tan asks about robotic exoskeleton walking after reading a news article and a small uncontrolled study. You search and find a clinical guideline and one randomised trial relevant to her. How should you best use this information in planning her programme?", "options": {"A": "Decline further discussion, since the uncontrolled study provides too little evidence to inform her programme", "B": "Appraise their quality and relevance, then weigh them with her goals, findings and preferences", "C": "Follow the guideline recommendations unchanged, as they are written to apply to people with her injury", "D": "Adopt the protocol from the randomised trial as written, because trials rank above guidelines"}, "cluster_id": "CHS219", "cluster_text": "Jing Huang Tan, 34, graphic designer, attends an outpatient spinal rehabilitation clinic 5 months after a fall from a ladder caused a T10 incomplete spinal cord injury (ASIA impairment scale D). She walks with elbow crutches and a left ankle foot orthosis. Hip flexion and knee extension: right 4/5, left 3/5. Ankle dorsiflexion: right 3/5, left 2/5. Light touch is reduced below T10. Modified Ashworth score is 1+ in the left quadriceps. Walking speed over ten metres is slow, with left foot drag and reduced hip flexion in swing. Resting BP 118/72, HR 76. She has read online about robotic exoskeleton walking and brought a news article and a small uncontrolled study. She lives in a two-storey townhouse with her partner.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Appraise their quality and relevance, then weigh them with her goals, findings and preferences): Evidence-based practice combines appraised research with clinical expertise and the person's values and circumstances. Copying a single trial protocol, or applying a guideline unchanged, ignores her individual findings, and a trial may have excluded people like her. Dismissing her question also fails to respect her involvement in decisions."}, {"id": 1837, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Conti has been sitting in the wheelchair most of the day with his residual limb supported on a pillow. Which positioning advice best prevents further flexion contractures that would otherwise delay fitting of his prosthesis and walking practice later in the programme?", "options": {"A": "Keep the limb raised on a pillow while sitting to control oedema and protect healing", "B": "Alternate side lying with sitting, and keep the knee supported straight on a board", "C": "Lie on the back with the limb flat, avoiding prone lying because it compresses the wound", "D": "Lie prone daily and keep the knee extended on a support when sitting"}, "cluster_id": "CHS220", "cluster_text": "Mr Dino Conti, 71, retired bricklayer, is in subacute rehabilitation three weeks after a left transtibial amputation for a diabetic foot infection with peripheral arterial disease. Type 2 diabetes for 20 years, HbA1c 8.4 percent, hypertension. Residual limb: incision healed except a small area of delayed healing at the lateral end, moderate oedema. Knee extension is full; the left hip has a 10 degree flexion contracture. Right foot: loss of protective sensation, dry skin and a callus under the first metatarsal head. Standing balance with a walking frame is fair and he relies on his arms. He lives alone in a two storey home, eats mostly takeaway meals and admits he sometimes skips his insulin. He spends most of the day in a wheelchair with the residual limb resting on a pillow. A temporary prosthesis fitting is planned.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Lie prone daily and keep the knee extended on a support when sitting): Time spent with the hip extended in prone lying and the knee held straight on a support counters the shortening that develops with prolonged sitting. Raising the limb on a pillow in a seated position keeps the hip and knee flexed and encourages exactly the contractures he already has. Side lying with a straight knee protects the knee but does not stretch the hip flexors. Lying supine and avoiding prone lying removes the main hip stretch available to him."}, {"id": 1838, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Conti's right foot has lost protective sensation and has a callus under the first metatarsal head. He will bear more load through this foot during gait training. What is the most appropriate action for the physiotherapist to take to protect the foot?", "options": {"A": "Arrange podiatry review of the callus and fit protective footwear before increasing standing load", "B": "Refer to podiatry for callus debridement, but continue full gait training in standard ward shoes meanwhile", "C": "Pad the callus with a soft sponge dressing and check the skin after each session", "D": "Fit a cushioned insole in his current shoes and teach him to inspect the foot weekly"}, "cluster_id": "CHS220", "cluster_text": "Mr Dino Conti, 71, retired bricklayer, is in subacute rehabilitation three weeks after a left transtibial amputation for a diabetic foot infection with peripheral arterial disease. Type 2 diabetes for 20 years, HbA1c 8.4 percent, hypertension. Residual limb: incision healed except a small area of delayed healing at the lateral end, moderate oedema. Knee extension is full; the left hip has a 10 degree flexion contracture. Right foot: loss of protective sensation, dry skin and a callus under the first metatarsal head. Standing balance with a walking frame is fair and he relies on his arms. He lives alone in a two storey home, eats mostly takeaway meals and admits he sometimes skips his insulin. He spends most of the day in a wheelchair with the residual limb resting on a pillow. A temporary prosthesis fitting is planned.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Arrange podiatry review of the callus and fit protective footwear before increasing standing load): A foot without protective sensation that is about to take more load needs expert callus care and protective footwear in place before the load rises, because pressure over a callus can ulcerate unnoticed. Podiatry review without suitable footwear still exposes the foot to shear in ordinary ward shoes. A sponge dressing does not remove the callus or offload it, and an insole with weekly checks is too infrequent and leaves the callus untreated."}, {"id": 1839, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "After 20 minutes in the parallel bars with his temporary prosthesis, the skin over the lateral end of Mr Conti's residual limb, near the small area of delayed healing, is red and tender. The redness persists after the socket has been off for some time. What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Add an extra sock ply to cushion the area and complete the remaining walking practice", "B": "Reduce wearing time to short sessions and recheck the skin at the end of each session", "C": "Stop prosthetic wear, document the skin changes and review socket fit with the prosthetist", "D": "Apply a hydrocolloid dressing over the red area and resume walking with shorter bouts and rests"}, "cluster_id": "CHS220", "cluster_text": "Mr Dino Conti, 71, retired bricklayer, is in subacute rehabilitation three weeks after a left transtibial amputation for a diabetic foot infection with peripheral arterial disease. Type 2 diabetes for 20 years, HbA1c 8.4 percent, hypertension. Residual limb: incision healed except a small area of delayed healing at the lateral end, moderate oedema. Knee extension is full; the left hip has a 10 degree flexion contracture. Right foot: loss of protective sensation, dry skin and a callus under the first metatarsal head. Standing balance with a walking frame is fair and he relies on his arms. He lives alone in a two storey home, eats mostly takeaway meals and admits he sometimes skips his insulin. He spends most of the day in a wheelchair with the residual limb resting on a pillow. A temporary prosthesis fitting is planned.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop prosthetic wear, document the skin changes and review socket fit with the prosthetist): Redness that does not fade after removal signals excess pressure over tissue already at risk, in a person with poor circulation and diabetes. Wear stops while the cause is found, and the prosthetist needs to assess the socket fit. Padding, dressing the area or simply shortening sessions keeps loading skin that has already told you the fit is wrong."}, {"id": 1840, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Conti admits he skips insulin on some days and eats mostly takeaway. He wants to walk well enough to live alone at home. Which approach to supporting his diabetes self-management is most appropriate within the physiotherapist's scope in the rehabilitation gym?", "options": {"A": "Advise him to change his insulin timing around therapy sessions to avoid low glucose levels", "B": "Keep sessions on gait training and leave diet and medication concerns to the nursing staff", "C": "Give him a written diet plan and set a weekly limit on takeaway meals", "D": "Use his walking goal to motivate foot care, then refer to the diabetes educator"}, "cluster_id": "CHS220", "cluster_text": "Mr Dino Conti, 71, retired bricklayer, is in subacute rehabilitation three weeks after a left transtibial amputation for a diabetic foot infection with peripheral arterial disease. Type 2 diabetes for 20 years, HbA1c 8.4 percent, hypertension. Residual limb: incision healed except a small area of delayed healing at the lateral end, moderate oedema. Knee extension is full; the left hip has a 10 degree flexion contracture. Right foot: loss of protective sensation, dry skin and a callus under the first metatarsal head. Standing balance with a walking frame is fair and he relies on his arms. He lives alone in a two storey home, eats mostly takeaway meals and admits he sometimes skips his insulin. He spends most of the day in a wheelchair with the residual limb resting on a pillow. A temporary prosthesis fitting is planned.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Use his walking goal to motivate foot care, then refer to the diabetes educator): Linking his own goal of independent walking to daily foot checks and glucose control uses motivational support within physiotherapy scope, and referral gets him expert diabetes education. Advising on insulin timing is outside scope and unsafe. Ignoring the issue misses a health promotion opportunity, and writing a diet plan belongs to a dietitian."}, {"id": 1841, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Kavita notes her leg heaviness and blurred vision appear after hot showers and warm exercise, then settle within an hour of cooling down, with no lasting change in her neurological examination. Which explanation best accounts for this pattern in multiple sclerosis?", "options": {"A": "Cardiovascular deconditioning, which lowers cardiac output in warm conditions and causes temporary leg heaviness during activity", "B": "A new relapse from an active inflammatory lesion, needing urgent medical review before further exercise sessions", "C": "Heat temporarily worsens conduction in demyelinated nerve fibres, so symptoms settle as her temperature falls", "D": "Delayed onset muscle soreness from overexertion, which usually peaks a day or two after unaccustomed exercise"}, "cluster_id": "CHS221", "cluster_text": "Kavita Reddy, 38, is a primary school teacher with relapsing remitting multiple sclerosis diagnosed six years ago. She attends a hospital outpatient clinic for a fitness and fatigue program. She reports overwhelming tiredness from about 2 pm most days, and her legs feel heavy and her vision blurs after hot showers or exercising in a warm room, settling within an hour of cooling. She has mild left foot drop with ankle dorsiflexion 4/5 on the left and 5/5 on the right, and no new symptoms in the past year. Sensation and reflexes are unchanged from her last neurologist review. Six minute walk test: 380 metres, Borg leg effort 15 at the end, with a 4 minute rest needed afterwards. She tends to do chores and marking on good days until she crashes for a day or two. The clinic manager asks whether an allied health assistant can run her gym sessions.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Heat temporarily worsens conduction in demyelinated nerve fibres, so symptoms settle as her temperature falls): A rise in core temperature slows or blocks conduction in demyelinated axons, and symptoms resolve once temperature returns to baseline without new damage. A relapse would produce symptoms lasting longer than a day and a changed examination, so this pattern does not need urgent review. Cooling strategies are therefore useful during exercise."}, {"id": 1842, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Kavita wants to rebuild her fitness but fatigues most after about 2 pm and recovers slowly from continuous effort. Her six minute walk test ended at Borg 15 after one long bout. Which exercise structure is most appropriate to begin her program?", "options": {"A": "Short moderate bouts in a cool morning setting, with planned rests before fatigue builds", "B": "Moderate continuous aerobic work in the cool morning, building duration steadily each week until fatigue appears", "C": "Short low intensity bouts in the late afternoon in a cool room, with rests between sets", "D": "Strength training with long rests, avoiding aerobic exercise because it raises body temperature and fatigue"}, "cluster_id": "CHS221", "cluster_text": "Kavita Reddy, 38, is a primary school teacher with relapsing remitting multiple sclerosis diagnosed six years ago. She attends a hospital outpatient clinic for a fitness and fatigue program. She reports overwhelming tiredness from about 2 pm most days, and her legs feel heavy and her vision blurs after hot showers or exercising in a warm room, settling within an hour of cooling. She has mild left foot drop with ankle dorsiflexion 4/5 on the left and 5/5 on the right, and no new symptoms in the past year. Sensation and reflexes are unchanged from her last neurologist review. Six minute walk test: 380 metres, Borg leg effort 15 at the end, with a 4 minute rest needed afterwards. She tends to do chores and marking on good days until she crashes for a day or two. The clinic manager asks whether an allied health assistant can run her gym sessions.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Short moderate bouts in a cool morning setting, with planned rests before fatigue builds): Short moderate bouts in cool conditions, timed away from her afternoon fatigue peak and with rests taken before fatigue builds, limit heat accumulation and avoid the crash that follows overexertion. Continuous work progressed until fatigue appears tends to provoke a prolonged flare. Training at 2 pm meets her worst period of tiredness. Strength training alone ignores her fitness goal, since aerobic work is suitable when it is paced and kept cool."}, {"id": 1843, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Kavita had a productive weekend completing chores and marking, then needed two days in bed. She asks how to manage her week. Using energy conservation principles, which strategy should the physiotherapist recommend to reduce these boom and bust cycles in her daily routine?", "options": {"A": "Cut activity to a minimum, since fatigue signals ongoing nerve damage and exertion could worsen disease", "B": "Plan the week in advance, spread demanding tasks across days, and schedule rests before fatigue builds", "C": "Keep an identical fixed activity quota every day, even when symptoms vary, to build a routine", "D": "Complete as much as possible on good days to bank energy, then rest once fatigue arrives"}, "cluster_id": "CHS221", "cluster_text": "Kavita Reddy, 38, is a primary school teacher with relapsing remitting multiple sclerosis diagnosed six years ago. She attends a hospital outpatient clinic for a fitness and fatigue program. She reports overwhelming tiredness from about 2 pm most days, and her legs feel heavy and her vision blurs after hot showers or exercising in a warm room, settling within an hour of cooling. She has mild left foot drop with ankle dorsiflexion 4/5 on the left and 5/5 on the right, and no new symptoms in the past year. Sensation and reflexes are unchanged from her last neurologist review. Six minute walk test: 380 metres, Borg leg effort 15 at the end, with a 4 minute rest needed afterwards. She tends to do chores and marking on good days until she crashes for a day or two. The clinic manager asks whether an allied health assistant can run her gym sessions.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Plan the week in advance, spread demanding tasks across days, and schedule rests before fatigue builds): Planning, prioritising and pacing demanding tasks, with rest taken before fatigue is severe, keeps energy more stable across the week. Doing everything on good days produces the boom and bust cycle, while cutting activity heavily causes deconditioning, which worsens fatigue. Fatigue in MS does not indicate ongoing nerve damage, and a rigid identical quota ignores day to day variation."}, {"id": 1844, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "The clinic manager asks that an allied health assistant, experienced but new to Kavita, run her gym sessions unsupervised from next week to free clinic time. The physiotherapist has completed the assessment and program. What is the most appropriate physiotherapist response?", "options": {"A": "Transfer the program and responsibility for progression, since an experienced assistant can judge readiness to advance", "B": "Delegate on the written program and let the assistant adjust intensity as her symptoms change", "C": "Decline delegation, as assistants cannot work with neurological conditions and the physiotherapist must deliver every session", "D": "Delegate with written instructions, stop criteria and review dates, keeping responsibility for reassessment and progression"}, "cluster_id": "CHS221", "cluster_text": "Kavita Reddy, 38, is a primary school teacher with relapsing remitting multiple sclerosis diagnosed six years ago. She attends a hospital outpatient clinic for a fitness and fatigue program. She reports overwhelming tiredness from about 2 pm most days, and her legs feel heavy and her vision blurs after hot showers or exercising in a warm room, settling within an hour of cooling. She has mild left foot drop with ankle dorsiflexion 4/5 on the left and 5/5 on the right, and no new symptoms in the past year. Sensation and reflexes are unchanged from her last neurologist review. Six minute walk test: 380 metres, Borg leg effort 15 at the end, with a 4 minute rest needed afterwards. She tends to do chores and marking on good days until she crashes for a day or two. The clinic manager asks whether an allied health assistant can run her gym sessions.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Delegate with written instructions, stop criteria and review dates, keeping responsibility for reassessment and progression): Under delegation the physiotherapist stays accountable for assessment, program design and progression, and gives the assistant clear instructions, limits and review points. Allowing the assistant to alter intensity alone moves clinical decisions outside the assistant's role. Refusing delegation outright is unnecessary, since assistants can safely deliver a defined exercise program with appropriate supervision."}, {"id": 1845, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Ngo has a swollen, tender right calf and new right-sided chest pain that worsens on deep breathing, with a heart rate of 106 and saturation of 94 percent. Which clinical interpretation is most appropriate before any further mobilisation is attempted today?", "options": {"A": "Cellulitis of the calf with a secondary chest infection, needing skin assessment and antibiotic review first", "B": "A ruptured popliteal cyst mimicking thrombosis, with separate chest wall pain, needing ultrasound review before activity", "C": "A probable pulmonary embolism from a calf thrombosis, needing urgent medical assessment before activity", "D": "A calf deep vein thrombosis alone, needing compression stockings and early supervised walking to prevent progression"}, "cluster_id": "CHS222", "cluster_text": "Mr Khoa Ngo, 58, a long haul bus driver, presents to the emergency department with right calf pain after a 14 hour drive three days ago. History: type 2 diabetes, BMI 33, smoker. Physiotherapy is asked to assess his mobility before discharge. Findings: right calf visibly swollen, 3 cm larger than the left 10 cm below the tibial tuberosity, warm and tender along the deep calf, pitting oedema at the ankle. HR 106 bpm, RR 22, BP 128/80, SpO2 94 percent on room air, temperature 37.4. He mentions mild sharp pain on the right side of his chest when he breathes deeply, which started this morning. Earlier today, a nursing student walked him to the toilet and back unsupervised because no one had told them to avoid it. Nothing was recorded.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A probable pulmonary embolism from a calf thrombosis, needing urgent medical assessment before activity): Unilateral calf swelling, warmth and deep tenderness with several risk factors, together with pleuritic chest pain, tachycardia and a lowered saturation, point to a thrombosis with probable embolism to the lung, which needs urgent medical assessment before any activity. Cellulitis would show skin changes and a raised temperature. A cyst would not explain the chest pain and vital signs. Treating the calf alone with walking ignores the signs of lung involvement and risks further embolisation."}, {"id": 1846, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Ngo appears comfortable on the trolley but you suspect a pulmonary embolism. He asks if you will still check how he walks so he can go home. What should you do first given your concern about his safety right now?", "options": {"A": "Keep him resting, check his observations again in an hour, and report if they worsen", "B": "Stop mobilisation, keep him resting and report your findings to the nurse in charge at handover", "C": "Perform a calf squeeze and Homans sign first, then report your findings to the emergency doctor", "D": "Stop mobilisation, keep him resting and report your findings to the emergency doctor immediately"}, "cluster_id": "CHS222", "cluster_text": "Mr Khoa Ngo, 58, a long haul bus driver, presents to the emergency department with right calf pain after a 14 hour drive three days ago. History: type 2 diabetes, BMI 33, smoker. Physiotherapy is asked to assess his mobility before discharge. Findings: right calf visibly swollen, 3 cm larger than the left 10 cm below the tibial tuberosity, warm and tender along the deep calf, pitting oedema at the ankle. HR 106 bpm, RR 22, BP 128/80, SpO2 94 percent on room air, temperature 37.4. He mentions mild sharp pain on the right side of his chest when he breathes deeply, which started this morning. Earlier today, a nursing student walked him to the toilet and back unsupervised because no one had told them to avoid it. Nothing was recorded.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stop mobilisation, keep him resting and report your findings to the emergency doctor immediately): The suspected pulmonary embolism is a medical emergency, so the safe first step is to stop mobilising, rest him and tell the emergency doctor now. Waiting an hour or until handover delays treatment for a deteriorating patient. Homans sign is neither sensitive nor specific, and provoking the calf could dislodge clot, so it should not come before escalation."}, {"id": 1847, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Anticoagulation has started and imaging confirmed a proximal right leg thrombosis with a small pulmonary embolism. He is now haemodynamically stable on the ward, with resting saturation 96 percent and no ongoing chest pain. He asks about activity. When is walking most appropriate to begin?", "options": {"A": "Delay walking until a repeat scan confirms the clot has dissolved and the lung has cleared", "B": "Keep him on bed rest with ankle pumps only until the calf swelling has fully settled", "C": "After medical clearance, begin symptom limited walking with monitoring and stop if breathlessness increases", "D": "Begin walking with vigorous calf pumping and stair climbing to prevent post thrombotic changes developing later"}, "cluster_id": "CHS222", "cluster_text": "Mr Khoa Ngo, 58, a long haul bus driver, presents to the emergency department with right calf pain after a 14 hour drive three days ago. History: type 2 diabetes, BMI 33, smoker. Physiotherapy is asked to assess his mobility before discharge. Findings: right calf visibly swollen, 3 cm larger than the left 10 cm below the tibial tuberosity, warm and tender along the deep calf, pitting oedema at the ankle. HR 106 bpm, RR 22, BP 128/80, SpO2 94 percent on room air, temperature 37.4. He mentions mild sharp pain on the right side of his chest when he breathes deeply, which started this morning. Earlier today, a nursing student walked him to the toilet and back unsupervised because no one had told them to avoid it. Nothing was recorded.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (After medical clearance, begin symptom limited walking with monitoring and stop if breathlessness increases): Current practice favours early, symptom limited mobilisation once the person is stable and anticoagulated and the medical team agrees. Prolonged bed rest has no benefit and adds deconditioning risk. Vigorous activity is unnecessary and may provoke symptoms, and waiting for scan resolution would delay recovery for no clear gain."}, {"id": 1848, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Earlier today, before your assessment, you learn the nursing student walked Mr Ngo to the toilet in the emergency department unsupervised, without instruction, and nobody recorded it. No harm is apparent so far. What is the most appropriate action regarding this event?", "options": {"A": "Submit a factual incident report promptly and inform the nurse in charge of the event", "B": "Speak privately with the student about expectations and avoid a formal report because no harm occurred", "C": "Describe the student's error in the clinical notes so the doctors know who was responsible", "D": "Wait to see whether his condition worsens before deciding if the event warrants a report"}, "cluster_id": "CHS222", "cluster_text": "Mr Khoa Ngo, 58, a long haul bus driver, presents to the emergency department with right calf pain after a 14 hour drive three days ago. History: type 2 diabetes, BMI 33, smoker. Physiotherapy is asked to assess his mobility before discharge. Findings: right calf visibly swollen, 3 cm larger than the left 10 cm below the tibial tuberosity, warm and tender along the deep calf, pitting oedema at the ankle. HR 106 bpm, RR 22, BP 128/80, SpO2 94 percent on room air, temperature 37.4. He mentions mild sharp pain on the right side of his chest when he breathes deeply, which started this morning. Earlier today, a nursing student walked him to the toilet and back unsupervised because no one had told them to avoid it. Nothing was recorded.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Submit a factual incident report promptly and inform the nurse in charge of the event): Incidents and near misses should be reported promptly through the organisation's incident system in factual, non judgemental terms, so that the system can learn from them, and the person in charge should be told. Clinical notes record patient care, not blame. Reporting does not depend on harm having occurred or on later outcomes."}, {"id": 1849, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "The physiotherapist reviews Tony's chart to find which single finding most strongly indicates diaphragm weakness with nocturnal hypoventilation, rather than limb or bulbar involvement, so that urgent respiratory review can be requested. His chart lists several abnormal findings from today's clinic. Which finding is most indicative?", "options": {"A": "Proximal limb strength of 3/5, progressing over the fourteen months since his diagnosis", "B": "Dysarthric speech requiring a communication board and tablet application for most daily conversations", "C": "Weak voluntary cough with two chest infections during one winter and difficulty clearing secretions", "D": "Vital capacity lower lying than sitting, with orthopnoea and morning headaches"}, "cluster_id": "CHS223", "cluster_text": "Tony Nguyen, 58, a former electrician, was diagnosed with motor neurone disease 14 months ago. He attends the outpatient respiratory and neurology clinic with his wife, Linh. Vital capacity has fallen to 48 percent of predicted and is lower lying than sitting. He reports poor sleep, morning headaches and breathlessness lying flat. His cough is weak, peak cough flow is low, and he has had two chest infections this winter. SpO2 94 percent on room air, RR 20, HR 88. Speech is dysarthric; he uses a communication board and a tablet app, answers questions quickly and appropriately, and manages his own finances. Proximal limb strength is 3/5. Linh wants the team to start a cough-assist machine immediately. When it is explained, Tony shakes his head and writes 'not now'.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Vital capacity lower lying than sitting, with orthopnoea and morning headaches): A fall in vital capacity on lying flat reflects diaphragm weakness, and orthopnoea with morning headaches points to nocturnal hypoventilation that may need ventilatory support. A weak cough reflects expiratory and bulbar muscle weakness, limb strength reflects spinal involvement, and dysarthria is a bulbar sign."}, {"id": 1850, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Tony's peak cough flow is low and he has had two chest infections this winter. He cannot tolerate lying flat, and Linh is willing to learn techniques at home. Which airway clearance approach should the physiotherapist teach first, given his weak inspiratory and expiratory muscles?", "options": {"A": "High-intensity inspiratory muscle training with a threshold device, to strengthen his diaphragm and improve cough strength", "B": "Lung volume recruitment with breath stacking followed by a manually assisted cough, taught to Linh", "C": "Percussion and postural drainage in head-down tilt, taught to Linh, to mobilise secretions from lung bases", "D": "Active cycle of breathing technique with forced expirations, using his expiratory muscles for high flow"}, "cluster_id": "CHS223", "cluster_text": "Tony Nguyen, 58, a former electrician, was diagnosed with motor neurone disease 14 months ago. He attends the outpatient respiratory and neurology clinic with his wife, Linh. Vital capacity has fallen to 48 percent of predicted and is lower lying than sitting. He reports poor sleep, morning headaches and breathlessness lying flat. His cough is weak, peak cough flow is low, and he has had two chest infections this winter. SpO2 94 percent on room air, RR 20, HR 88. Speech is dysarthric; he uses a communication board and a tablet app, answers questions quickly and appropriately, and manages his own finances. Proximal limb strength is 3/5. Linh wants the team to start a cough-assist machine immediately. When it is explained, Tony shakes his head and writes 'not now'.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Lung volume recruitment with breath stacking followed by a manually assisted cough, taught to Linh): Breath stacking raises lung volume so the next cough is stronger, and a manual assist adds the expiratory force his muscles cannot supply; a carer can learn both. Head-down tilt will worsen breathlessness with diaphragm weakness. Forced expirations depend on muscle power he lacks, and high-intensity muscle training can fatigue weak muscles without proven benefit in this disease."}, {"id": 1851, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "During a positioning assessment lying flat, Tony becomes breathless, with SpO2 falling to 91 percent and respiratory rate rising to 26. He is alert and signals distress, and Linh is beside him. How should the physiotherapist act immediately in this situation?", "options": {"A": "Start high-flow oxygen to correct the saturation quickly, then continue the assessment while monitoring breathing rate", "B": "Sit him upright with pillow support, coach slow breathing, and inform the clinic doctor promptly", "C": "Keep him lying flat and coach deep breathing to recruit the lung bases, completing the assessment", "D": "Give vigorous chest percussion to clear secretions that may be causing the desaturation, then recheck saturation"}, "cluster_id": "CHS223", "cluster_text": "Tony Nguyen, 58, a former electrician, was diagnosed with motor neurone disease 14 months ago. He attends the outpatient respiratory and neurology clinic with his wife, Linh. Vital capacity has fallen to 48 percent of predicted and is lower lying than sitting. He reports poor sleep, morning headaches and breathlessness lying flat. His cough is weak, peak cough flow is low, and he has had two chest infections this winter. SpO2 94 percent on room air, RR 20, HR 88. Speech is dysarthric; he uses a communication board and a tablet app, answers questions quickly and appropriately, and manages his own finances. Proximal limb strength is 3/5. Linh wants the team to start a cough-assist machine immediately. When it is explained, Tony shakes his head and writes 'not now'.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Sit him upright with pillow support, coach slow breathing, and inform the clinic doctor promptly): Lying flat loads a weak diaphragm, so sitting him up gives immediate relief and the medical team must know about the acute change. High-flow oxygen alone can mask or worsen hypoventilation and carbon dioxide retention. Staying flat prolongs the distress, and percussion treats a secretion problem that has not been shown to exist."}, {"id": 1852, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "When the cough-assist machine is explained, Tony shakes his head and writes 'not now'. Linh says he does not grasp how serious his chest is and urges the physiotherapist to proceed. Tony answers questions quickly and appropriately. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Refer the family disagreement to the state guardianship tribunal before any further discussion with Tony", "B": "Proceed with a trial, since Linh as his wife can consent for him with impaired speech", "C": "Respect his decision after checking understanding with his communication aids, and document the discussion", "D": "Arrange a formal capacity assessment before accepting his refusal, given his dysarthria and progressive disease"}, "cluster_id": "CHS223", "cluster_text": "Tony Nguyen, 58, a former electrician, was diagnosed with motor neurone disease 14 months ago. He attends the outpatient respiratory and neurology clinic with his wife, Linh. Vital capacity has fallen to 48 percent of predicted and is lower lying than sitting. He reports poor sleep, morning headaches and breathlessness lying flat. His cough is weak, peak cough flow is low, and he has had two chest infections this winter. SpO2 94 percent on room air, RR 20, HR 88. Speech is dysarthric; he uses a communication board and a tablet app, answers questions quickly and appropriately, and manages his own finances. Proximal limb strength is 3/5. Linh wants the team to start a cough-assist machine immediately. When it is explained, Tony shakes his head and writes 'not now'.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Respect his decision after checking understanding with his communication aids, and document the discussion): Adults are presumed to have capacity, and difficulty speaking does not remove it, so the task is to support communication, confirm he understands the benefits and risks, and honour his choice, leaving the door open to revisit. A spouse cannot consent for a person who can decide. Formal assessment or tribunal referral is unwarranted when there is no real doubt about his capacity."}, {"id": 1853, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Kapoor asks why his bladder behaves differently from before the injury, and what to expect. He reports no voluntary control of emptying. The physiotherapist explains the pattern of bladder dysfunction expected after a complete T6 injury. Which description is accurate?", "options": {"A": "Underactive detrusor with absent fullness sensation, managed safely by abdominal straining and manual expression", "B": "Flaccid bladder from lower motor neurone injury, with low pressure overflow incontinence and reduced bladder tone", "C": "Overactive bladder from loss of cortical inhibition, which usually responds to pelvic floor strengthening alone", "D": "Reflex bladder from upper motor neurone injury, with risk of high pressures and incomplete emptying"}, "cluster_id": "CHS224", "cluster_text": "Mr Suresh Kapoor, 29, former scaffolder, has a complete T6 spinal cord injury (ASIA impairment scale A) from a workplace fall eight months ago. He lives with his partner in a rented house and uses a manual wheelchair. Bladder: intermittent self-catheterisation four times daily. Bowel: scheduled routine every second morning using suppositories and digital stimulation. Resting blood pressure is usually about 100/60 mmHg. He sits in the wheelchair about ten hours daily on a foam cushion that is now compressed. Skin check: a 2 cm area of non-blanching redness over the right ischial tuberosity, skin intact. His workers compensation claim for the injury has been accepted, with a case manager at the insurer. He has asked about applying for the NDIS and whether Medicare covers a new cushion and a shower commode chair. Setting: community physiotherapy home visit.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reflex bladder from upper motor neurone injury, with risk of high pressures and incomplete emptying): An injury above the sacral segments leaves the sacral reflex arc intact, so the detrusor contracts reflexly and often against a closed sphincter, causing high pressures and poor emptying. The flaccid pattern belongs to sacral or cauda equina injury. Straining, manual expression and pelvic floor work do not control this pattern or protect the kidneys."}, {"id": 1854, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "During the visit Mr Kapoor develops a pounding headache, sweating and flushing above the waist. His blood pressure is 168/100 mmHg and heart rate 52 beats per minute. He is sitting in his wheelchair with his legs resting on the footplates. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Lie him flat with legs raised to improve cerebral perfusion, then recheck blood pressure soon after", "B": "Transfer him to the plinth for gentle passive leg movements to settle the sympathetic response", "C": "Keep him upright with legs down, loosen tight clothing, and check bladder fullness", "D": "Stop the session and let him rest quietly in the chair, then recheck blood pressure"}, "cluster_id": "CHS224", "cluster_text": "Mr Suresh Kapoor, 29, former scaffolder, has a complete T6 spinal cord injury (ASIA impairment scale A) from a workplace fall eight months ago. He lives with his partner in a rented house and uses a manual wheelchair. Bladder: intermittent self-catheterisation four times daily. Bowel: scheduled routine every second morning using suppositories and digital stimulation. Resting blood pressure is usually about 100/60 mmHg. He sits in the wheelchair about ten hours daily on a foam cushion that is now compressed. Skin check: a 2 cm area of non-blanching redness over the right ischial tuberosity, skin intact. His workers compensation claim for the injury has been accepted, with a case manager at the insurer. He has asked about applying for the NDIS and whether Medicare covers a new cushion and a shower commode chair. Setting: community physiotherapy home visit.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Keep him upright with legs down, loosen tight clothing, and check bladder fullness): These signs indicate autonomic dysreflexia, a medical emergency in injuries at T6 or above. Staying upright with legs dependent uses gravity to lower blood pressure, and removing the trigger such as a full bladder is the priority while blood pressure is monitored. Lying flat raises pressure further, and waiting or moving his legs leaves the cause untreated."}, {"id": 1855, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "The non-blanching redness over the right ischial tuberosity is unchanged one week later and the skin remains intact. Mr Kapoor still sits about ten hours daily and says he cannot afford time off the chair. What is the most appropriate management?", "options": {"A": "Offload the area, review his cushion, and teach pressure relief with regular skin checks", "B": "Apply a barrier dressing and continue usual sitting hours, because the skin is still intact", "C": "Massage the reddened area firmly each day to improve local circulation and encourage tissue recovery", "D": "Increase sitting time gradually on the current cushion so the tissue adapts and tolerance improves"}, "cluster_id": "CHS224", "cluster_text": "Mr Suresh Kapoor, 29, former scaffolder, has a complete T6 spinal cord injury (ASIA impairment scale A) from a workplace fall eight months ago. He lives with his partner in a rented house and uses a manual wheelchair. Bladder: intermittent self-catheterisation four times daily. Bowel: scheduled routine every second morning using suppositories and digital stimulation. Resting blood pressure is usually about 100/60 mmHg. He sits in the wheelchair about ten hours daily on a foam cushion that is now compressed. Skin check: a 2 cm area of non-blanching redness over the right ischial tuberosity, skin intact. His workers compensation claim for the injury has been accepted, with a case manager at the insurer. He has asked about applying for the NDIS and whether Medicare covers a new cushion and a shower commode chair. Setting: community physiotherapy home visit.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Offload the area, review his cushion, and teach pressure relief with regular skin checks): Non-blanching redness is early pressure injury, and the cause is sustained pressure on a failing cushion, so removing load and fixing the seating are essential. Pressure relief and daily checks catch deterioration early. Massage damages compromised tissue, and continuing the same sitting pattern on a compressed cushion lets the injury progress."}, {"id": 1856, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Kapoor asks how to get a pressure-relieving cushion and a shower commode chair funded. His injury-related needs fall under an accepted workers compensation claim with a case manager. He is 29 years old. What is the most appropriate first step for the physiotherapist?", "options": {"A": "Refer him to My Aged Care, which coordinates equipment funding for adults with spinal injury", "B": "Prepare a Medicare chronic disease management plan so the cushion and commode can be claimed", "C": "Help him lodge an NDIS access request first, as he is under 65 with permanent disability", "D": "Write a justified equipment request with functional assessment findings for the insurer's case manager"}, "cluster_id": "CHS224", "cluster_text": "Mr Suresh Kapoor, 29, former scaffolder, has a complete T6 spinal cord injury (ASIA impairment scale A) from a workplace fall eight months ago. He lives with his partner in a rented house and uses a manual wheelchair. Bladder: intermittent self-catheterisation four times daily. Bowel: scheduled routine every second morning using suppositories and digital stimulation. Resting blood pressure is usually about 100/60 mmHg. He sits in the wheelchair about ten hours daily on a foam cushion that is now compressed. Skin check: a 2 cm area of non-blanching redness over the right ischial tuberosity, skin intact. His workers compensation claim for the injury has been accepted, with a case manager at the insurer. He has asked about applying for the NDIS and whether Medicare covers a new cushion and a shower commode chair. Setting: community physiotherapy home visit.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Write a justified equipment request with functional assessment findings for the insurer's case manager): Needs arising from a compensable injury are the responsibility of the accepted claim, so the insurer should receive an assessment-based request that justifies the equipment. The NDIS is not the first funder for supports another scheme covers. My Aged Care serves mainly older people, and Medicare plans fund limited allied health visits rather than equipment."}, {"id": 1857, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Ms Kapoor has a mechanical pattern with intact neurology, but she avoids lifting light items and fears re-injury. Her recovery has slowed and her employer is asking about return to work. What is the most appropriate next step in her assessment?", "options": {"A": "Measure maximal lifting capacity on a dynamometer to set a safe limit for resident handling", "B": "Screen for psychosocial yellow flags with a validated tool and explore her beliefs about lifting", "C": "Request repeat lumbar imaging through the GP to confirm a structural cause before graded activity", "D": "Fit a lumbar brace to build her confidence on shift and reduce her fear of lifting"}, "cluster_id": "CHS225", "cluster_text": "Ms Divya Kapoor, 41, is a registered nurse in an aged care facility who injured her lower back five weeks ago while repositioning a resident. She has an accepted workers compensation claim, and her GP certificate allows suitable duties. She attends your private practice. She reports right-sided low back and buttock pain, 4 out of 10 at rest and 6 out of 10 with bending. Lumbar flexion reaches mid-shin, extension is painful at end range, straight leg raise is negative and lower limb neurology is intact. Her Oswestry Disability Index score is 30 percent. She worries she will hurt her back again and never nurse again, and she avoids lifting even light items. Her recovery has slowed over the past two weeks. The employer can offer light duties with no resident handling. She is warm and talkative, and often brings the clinic small gifts.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Screen for psychosocial yellow flags with a validated tool and explore her beliefs about lifting): Fear of movement and slowed recovery despite a favourable mechanical pattern suggest psychosocial barriers, which a validated screening tool and a discussion of beliefs can identify and address. Further imaging is not indicated without red flags, and a brace or capacity test does not address the fear."}, {"id": 1858, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "The GP, employer and Ms Kapoor agree to a graded return to light duties. The physiotherapist is asked to advise how hours and tasks should progress over the coming weeks. Which principle should guide the progression of her return to work?", "options": {"A": "Time-based steps in hours and duties agreed with the employer, not waiting for pain to resolve", "B": "Full duties after a short strengthening course, adjusting tasks if her pain flares during a shift", "C": "Reduced hours with all manual handling excluded until her treatment has finished and she is discharged", "D": "Pain-contingent steps, increasing duties only after her pain has stayed at zero for a week"}, "cluster_id": "CHS225", "cluster_text": "Ms Divya Kapoor, 41, is a registered nurse in an aged care facility who injured her lower back five weeks ago while repositioning a resident. She has an accepted workers compensation claim, and her GP certificate allows suitable duties. She attends your private practice. She reports right-sided low back and buttock pain, 4 out of 10 at rest and 6 out of 10 with bending. Lumbar flexion reaches mid-shin, extension is painful at end range, straight leg raise is negative and lower limb neurology is intact. Her Oswestry Disability Index score is 30 percent. She worries she will hurt her back again and never nurse again, and she avoids lifting even light items. Her recovery has slowed over the past two weeks. The employer can offer light duties with no resident handling. She is warm and talkative, and often brings the clinic small gifts.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Time-based steps in hours and duties agreed with the employer, not waiting for pain to resolve): Planned, time-based increases in hours and duties reduce fear and avoidance and keep her connected to work while she recovers, with some pain accepted as part of progress. Waiting for pain to resolve, jumping to full duties or excluding handling until discharge each lack a structured, shared plan."}, {"id": 1859, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "At week two, Ms Kapoor's pain is 3 out of 10, but she reports completing a double shift including resident transfers, as the unit was short-staffed. The employer phones for an update on her capacity. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Cut treatment to one session, since her pain has improved, and discharge her to home exercise", "B": "Advise a week of rest to settle a flare before her GP reviews the plan", "C": "Explore what happened with her, then with her consent update the employer and GP on duties", "D": "Tell the employer she breached her restrictions so the supervisor can enforce the plan"}, "cluster_id": "CHS225", "cluster_text": "Ms Divya Kapoor, 41, is a registered nurse in an aged care facility who injured her lower back five weeks ago while repositioning a resident. She has an accepted workers compensation claim, and her GP certificate allows suitable duties. She attends your private practice. She reports right-sided low back and buttock pain, 4 out of 10 at rest and 6 out of 10 with bending. Lumbar flexion reaches mid-shin, extension is painful at end range, straight leg raise is negative and lower limb neurology is intact. Her Oswestry Disability Index score is 30 percent. She worries she will hurt her back again and never nurse again, and she avoids lifting even light items. Her recovery has slowed over the past two weeks. The employer can offer light duties with no resident handling. She is warm and talkative, and often brings the clinic small gifts.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Explore what happened with her, then with her consent update the employer and GP on duties): She remains the person the physiotherapist works with, so the first step is to understand the event and agree a response, then share only what she consents to with the employer and GP. Reporting a breach, enforcing rest or discharging her ignores both her consent and the need to review the plan."}, {"id": 1860, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Ms Kapoor sends a social media connection request and a friendly message asking the physiotherapist to look at her brother's sore knee informally, saying it would be quicker than booking an appointment. How should the physiotherapist respond to maintain professional boundaries?", "options": {"A": "Decline the connection kindly, explain boundaries, and suggest her brother book his own assessment", "B": "Ignore the request, but answer the knee question by phone since a short call is harmless", "C": "Accept the request to build rapport, and keep messages limited to her own injury and treatment", "D": "Accept with limited profile visibility, and answer the knee question briefly in a private message"}, "cluster_id": "CHS225", "cluster_text": "Ms Divya Kapoor, 41, is a registered nurse in an aged care facility who injured her lower back five weeks ago while repositioning a resident. She has an accepted workers compensation claim, and her GP certificate allows suitable duties. She attends your private practice. She reports right-sided low back and buttock pain, 4 out of 10 at rest and 6 out of 10 with bending. Lumbar flexion reaches mid-shin, extension is painful at end range, straight leg raise is negative and lower limb neurology is intact. Her Oswestry Disability Index score is 30 percent. She worries she will hurt her back again and never nurse again, and she avoids lifting even light items. Her recovery has slowed over the past two weeks. The employer can offer light duties with no resident handling. She is warm and talkative, and often brings the clinic small gifts.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Decline the connection kindly, explain boundaries, and suggest her brother book his own assessment): Social media connections and informal advice blur the therapeutic relationship and leave no assessment, consent or record for the brother. A kind explanation and an offer of a proper appointment protects both parties, whereas accepting with restrictions or advising informally keeps the boundary problem."}, {"id": 1861, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Jake sometimes uses his oscillating PEP device before his nebulised treatments, which reduces their benefit. He has a bronchodilator, hypertonic saline and an inhaled antibiotic prescribed. Which sequence is most appropriate for his airway clearance session at home before school?", "options": {"A": "Bronchodilator, then hypertonic saline, then airway clearance with the device, then inhaled antibiotic", "B": "Hypertonic saline first, then inhaled antibiotic, then the device, and bronchodilator at the end", "C": "Airway clearance with the device first, then bronchodilator, hypertonic saline and inhaled antibiotic", "D": "Inhaled antibiotic first, then bronchodilator, hypertonic saline and finally airway clearance with the device"}, "cluster_id": "CHS226", "cluster_text": "Jake Nguyen, 15, Year 10 student, has cystic fibrosis and is seen in a community physiotherapy clinic between clinic visits at the specialist CF centre. Chronic Pseudomonas aeruginosa colonisation, FEV1 74% predicted at his last review, and pancreatic insufficiency on enzymes. His airway clearance routine is nebulised hypertonic saline, an oscillating positive expiratory pressure device, then inhaled antibiotic. Today he has a mild morning cough with sputum, afebrile, SpO2 96%, RR 16, chest clear except for scattered crackles at the right upper zone. Jake admits he skips sessions on school days and sometimes does the device before his nebuliser. The physiotherapist plans to ask an allied health assistant (AHA), who runs the clinic exercise group, to supervise Jake's weekly exercise session while he is absent.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Bronchodilator, then hypertonic saline, then airway clearance with the device, then inhaled antibiotic): The bronchodilator opens airways and reduces bronchospasm from hypertonic saline, the saline then hydrates secretions, airway clearance mobilises them, and the antibiotic goes last so it reaches cleared airways. Doing clearance before the nebulisers misses the benefit of loosened secretions."}, {"id": 1862, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Jake is colonised with Pseudomonas aeruginosa and attends the same community physiotherapy clinic as other children and adolescents with cystic fibrosis, who share the waiting area and exercise group. The clinic wants to reduce cross-infection risk. Which arrangement is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Include him in the group class with masks worn, and clean shared equipment between sessions", "B": "Treat him in a private room but seat him in the shared waiting area before sessions", "C": "Allow him to join other colonised patients in a cohort group, with equipment cleaned between patients", "D": "Schedule him apart from other CF patients, use a private room, and clean equipment between patients"}, "cluster_id": "CHS226", "cluster_text": "Jake Nguyen, 15, Year 10 student, has cystic fibrosis and is seen in a community physiotherapy clinic between clinic visits at the specialist CF centre. Chronic Pseudomonas aeruginosa colonisation, FEV1 74% predicted at his last review, and pancreatic insufficiency on enzymes. His airway clearance routine is nebulised hypertonic saline, an oscillating positive expiratory pressure device, then inhaled antibiotic. Today he has a mild morning cough with sputum, afebrile, SpO2 96%, RR 16, chest clear except for scattered crackles at the right upper zone. Jake admits he skips sessions on school days and sometimes does the device before his nebuliser. The physiotherapist plans to ask an allied health assistant (AHA), who runs the clinic exercise group, to supervise Jake's weekly exercise session while he is absent.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Schedule him apart from other CF patients, use a private room, and clean equipment between patients): People with CF can pass resistant organisms to each other, so each patient is kept apart in time and space, treated in a private room, and equipment is cleaned between patients. Masks and cleaning do not remove the risk of a shared exercise space. A private room is undone if he waits with others. Grouping colonised patients together can still exchange different strains, so separation is preferred."}, {"id": 1863, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Jake says he skips airway clearance on school days because it makes him late and he feels embarrassed in front of friends. He agrees it matters, and he has a good understanding of the routine. How should the physiotherapist best address his reasons for skipping treatment?", "options": {"A": "Tell him the sessions can be dropped on school days because his FEV1 is currently stable", "B": "Move the whole routine to bedtime so it is done once daily on every day", "C": "Explain the risks of missed sessions again and ask his parents to supervise every treatment", "D": "Plan with him a realistic routine that fits school mornings, and agree on goals he chooses"}, "cluster_id": "CHS226", "cluster_text": "Jake Nguyen, 15, Year 10 student, has cystic fibrosis and is seen in a community physiotherapy clinic between clinic visits at the specialist CF centre. Chronic Pseudomonas aeruginosa colonisation, FEV1 74% predicted at his last review, and pancreatic insufficiency on enzymes. His airway clearance routine is nebulised hypertonic saline, an oscillating positive expiratory pressure device, then inhaled antibiotic. Today he has a mild morning cough with sputum, afebrile, SpO2 96%, RR 16, chest clear except for scattered crackles at the right upper zone. Jake admits he skips sessions on school days and sometimes does the device before his nebuliser. The physiotherapist plans to ask an allied health assistant (AHA), who runs the clinic exercise group, to supervise Jake's weekly exercise session while he is absent.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Plan with him a realistic routine that fits school mornings, and agree on goals he chooses): At 15, adherence is best supported by working with the young person: identifying barriers, agreeing flexible timing and setting goals he owns, with the CF team involved for any change. Repeating warnings or relying on parental supervision tends to increase resistance. Dropping sessions without advice from the CF team risks his lung function."}, {"id": 1864, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "The physiotherapist will be away next week and wants the AHA, who runs the clinic exercise group, to supervise Jake's weekly exercise session. The AHA is experienced, has completed local training, and Jake is currently clinically stable. Which delegation arrangement is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Delegate the session verbally without a written plan since the AHA has run the group before", "B": "Delegate the full session including reassessing his chest and adjusting airway clearance if he coughs more", "C": "Delegate exercise supervision using a written plan with stop criteria, and keep reassessment yourself", "D": "Delegate it if the AHA will also progress his intensity whenever he tolerates it well"}, "cluster_id": "CHS226", "cluster_text": "Jake Nguyen, 15, Year 10 student, has cystic fibrosis and is seen in a community physiotherapy clinic between clinic visits at the specialist CF centre. Chronic Pseudomonas aeruginosa colonisation, FEV1 74% predicted at his last review, and pancreatic insufficiency on enzymes. His airway clearance routine is nebulised hypertonic saline, an oscillating positive expiratory pressure device, then inhaled antibiotic. Today he has a mild morning cough with sputum, afebrile, SpO2 96%, RR 16, chest clear except for scattered crackles at the right upper zone. Jake admits he skips sessions on school days and sometimes does the device before his nebuliser. The physiotherapist plans to ask an allied health assistant (AHA), who runs the clinic exercise group, to supervise Jake's weekly exercise session while he is absent.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Delegate exercise supervision using a written plan with stop criteria, and keep reassessment yourself): A physiotherapist may delegate a defined task such as supervising an established exercise program to a competent AHA, with clear written instructions, stop and report criteria and access to supervision. Assessment, clinical reasoning and changes to airway clearance or exercise progression remain the physiotherapist's responsibility."}, {"id": 1865, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Daniel has a thickened tender tendon about 5 cm above the calcaneal insertion, a painful arc sign, and pain that eases when warm but returns after running. His calf squeeze test is normal and dorsiflexion is equal on both sides. Which diagnosis best fits these findings?", "options": {"A": "Posterior ankle impingement with pain at end range plantarflexion and deep tenderness behind the joint", "B": "Partial Achilles tendon tear with a palpable gap and loss of tendon continuity on examination", "C": "Midportion Achilles tendinopathy, related to tendon load having exceeded its recent capacity to adapt", "D": "Insertional Achilles tendinopathy with compression of the retrocalcaneal bursa during ankle dorsiflexion"}, "cluster_id": "CHS227", "cluster_text": "Daniel Walker, 38, a Wiradjuri man who works as a land management ranger, presents to your private practice with four months of right Achilles pain. He is a recreational runner who recently increased his weekly distance in preparation for a half marathon. Pain is worst with the first steps in the morning and settles when warm, then returns after runs. Findings: thickened, tender tendon about 5 cm above the calcaneal insertion; painful arc sign present, with the swelling moving as the ankle moves; single leg heel raises 12 repetitions on the right with pain against 25 on the left; hop test provokes pain; calf squeeze test normal; ankle dorsiflexion equal on both sides. Daniel is quiet during the history. He mentions that his cousin lives nearby and often comes with him to appointments, and that he usually attends the local Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service for his general health care.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Midportion Achilles tendinopathy, related to tendon load having exceeded its recent capacity to adapt): Tenderness and thickening in the midportion, a painful arc that moves with the tendon, and warm-up behaviour after a recent load increase are typical of midportion tendinopathy. Insertional problems cause pain at the heel and are aggravated by dorsiflexion. A tear would show a gap or a positive squeeze test, and impingement gives deep end range pain rather than a swollen tendon."}, {"id": 1866, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Daniel wants to keep training for his half marathon in eight weeks. Pain is 5/10 after runs and morning stiffness is present. He has been running four times weekly and has no other injuries. Which management approach is most appropriate for his midportion Achilles tendinopathy?", "options": {"A": "Eccentric calf stretching on a step to full dorsiflexion range, with strengthening deferred until symptoms ease", "B": "Therapeutic ultrasound and a heel lift to unload the tendon, delaying calf strengthening until symptoms stop", "C": "Rest from all running and calf loading until the tendon is pain free for four weeks", "D": "Progressive calf loading such as slow heavy raises, tolerating moderate pain, with reduced running volume"}, "cluster_id": "CHS227", "cluster_text": "Daniel Walker, 38, a Wiradjuri man who works as a land management ranger, presents to your private practice with four months of right Achilles pain. He is a recreational runner who recently increased his weekly distance in preparation for a half marathon. Pain is worst with the first steps in the morning and settles when warm, then returns after runs. Findings: thickened, tender tendon about 5 cm above the calcaneal insertion; painful arc sign present, with the swelling moving as the ankle moves; single leg heel raises 12 repetitions on the right with pain against 25 on the left; hop test provokes pain; calf squeeze test normal; ankle dorsiflexion equal on both sides. Daniel is quiet during the history. He mentions that his cousin lives nearby and often comes with him to appointments, and that he usually attends the local Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service for his general health care.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Progressive calf loading such as slow heavy raises, tolerating moderate pain, with reduced running volume): Progressive tendon loading, with pain kept at a tolerable level and running adjusted, builds capacity and is the mainstay of treatment. Complete rest deconditions the tendon, and stretching into dorsiflexion compresses it. Passive modalities and delaying strengthening do not address the underlying load tolerance problem."}, {"id": 1867, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Three weeks later Daniel reports hearing a pop and feeling kicked in the back of the calf while sprinting, and now has difficulty pushing off. He is able to walk but limps. Which test result would most strongly suggest a complete Achilles tendon rupture?", "options": {"A": "Inability to complete a single leg heel raise on the affected side, with pain", "B": "Marked bruising and swelling behind the ankle, with plantarflexion still produced on calf squeeze", "C": "Weak painful resisted plantarflexion with a normal tendon outline on palpation and light touch", "D": "Absent plantarflexion of the foot when the calf is squeezed with the knee flexed"}, "cluster_id": "CHS227", "cluster_text": "Daniel Walker, 38, a Wiradjuri man who works as a land management ranger, presents to your private practice with four months of right Achilles pain. He is a recreational runner who recently increased his weekly distance in preparation for a half marathon. Pain is worst with the first steps in the morning and settles when warm, then returns after runs. Findings: thickened, tender tendon about 5 cm above the calcaneal insertion; painful arc sign present, with the swelling moving as the ankle moves; single leg heel raises 12 repetitions on the right with pain against 25 on the left; hop test provokes pain; calf squeeze test normal; ankle dorsiflexion equal on both sides. Daniel is quiet during the history. He mentions that his cousin lives nearby and often comes with him to appointments, and that he usually attends the local Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service for his general health care.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Absent plantarflexion of the foot when the calf is squeezed with the knee flexed): When the calf is squeezed, an intact Achilles tendon produces passive plantarflexion, so absence of this response is the most specific sign that tendon continuity is lost. Difficulty with a single leg heel raise occurs with painful tendinopathy and partial tears as well, so it does not separate them. Bruising with a preserved response suggests the tendon is still in continuity, and weak painful loading with a normal outline fits tendon pain without a rupture."}, {"id": 1868, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Daniel asks whether his cousin can join the session and says he prefers to talk things through. He attends the local Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service for his general care and has been quiet during assessment. How should the physiotherapist best provide culturally safe care at this appointment?", "options": {"A": "Follow the usual process and ask his cousin to wait outside to protect Daniel's privacy", "B": "Welcome his cousin, ask how he wants information shared, and use plain language", "C": "Speak mainly to his cousin to show respect and let him relay advice to Daniel", "D": "Assume he wants an Aboriginal health worker and arrange one for his visit."}, "cluster_id": "CHS227", "cluster_text": "Daniel Walker, 38, a Wiradjuri man who works as a land management ranger, presents to your private practice with four months of right Achilles pain. He is a recreational runner who recently increased his weekly distance in preparation for a half marathon. Pain is worst with the first steps in the morning and settles when warm, then returns after runs. Findings: thickened, tender tendon about 5 cm above the calcaneal insertion; painful arc sign present, with the swelling moving as the ankle moves; single leg heel raises 12 repetitions on the right with pain against 25 on the left; hop test provokes pain; calf squeeze test normal; ankle dorsiflexion equal on both sides. Daniel is quiet during the history. He mentions that his cousin lives nearby and often comes with him to appointments, and that he usually attends the local Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service for his general health care.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Welcome his cousin, ask how he wants information shared, and use plain language): Culturally safe practice lets the person decide who is involved and how information is shared, so inviting family and asking his preferences puts Daniel in control. Excluding family imposes a clinic norm, and arranging a worker without asking replaces his choice with an assumption. Directing advice at the cousin sidelines Daniel as the decision maker."}, {"id": 1869, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Marco wants to resume regular exercise while he awaits his cardiac rehabilitation start date. His resting observations are stable, he walks 15 minutes at a comfortable pace and his knee pain is mild. Which home exercise plan is the most appropriate for the physiotherapist to begin?", "options": {"A": "Heavy isometric holds and straining exercises to rebuild leg strength before beginning any walking progression", "B": "Gentle stretching and range-of-motion work only, with aerobic exercise held until his cardiologist review", "C": "Walking at moderate perceived effort, increasing duration gradually, and light leg strengthening", "D": "Interval walking at high effort to maximise early gains, as the stent has restored blood flow"}, "cluster_id": "CHS228", "cluster_text": "Marco Esposito, 74, a retired mechanic and widower, lives alone in a suburban home. He is three weeks after a drug-eluting stent to his left anterior descending artery following an NSTEMI. He takes dual antiplatelet therapy, a beta blocker and a statin, and has knee osteoarthritis. His GP referred him to community physiotherapy for a home exercise programme while he waits for cardiac rehabilitation. Resting HR 62, BP 134/78, SpO2 97 percent. He walks about 15 minutes at a comfortable pace with mild knee pain. He enjoys the physiotherapist's visits, talks at length about his family and says he feels lonely since his wife died. He has hinted that he would like to repay your kindness.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Walking at moderate perceived effort, increasing duration gradually, and light leg strengthening): Three weeks after a stent with stable observations, graded moderate aerobic exercise and light strengthening are safe and recommended, and also suit his knee. Withholding aerobic work is unnecessarily conservative. High effort intervals and straining isometrics raise cardiac load without being appropriate at this early stage."}, {"id": 1870, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "At the next visit Marco mentions that while digging in his garden he felt tight chest pressure that eased within minutes of resting. He has told no one and asks the physiotherapist to keep it confidential. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Telephone his daughter to explain the symptom, as family support is the quickest route to review", "B": "Pause progression, explain why prompt medical review matters, and help him contact his GP or cardiologist", "C": "Document the symptom and end physiotherapy, leaving his GP to notice it at the next review", "D": "Respect his confidentiality request, continue exercise at lower intensity, and reassess his symptoms next visit"}, "cluster_id": "CHS228", "cluster_text": "Marco Esposito, 74, a retired mechanic and widower, lives alone in a suburban home. He is three weeks after a drug-eluting stent to his left anterior descending artery following an NSTEMI. He takes dual antiplatelet therapy, a beta blocker and a statin, and has knee osteoarthritis. His GP referred him to community physiotherapy for a home exercise programme while he waits for cardiac rehabilitation. Resting HR 62, BP 134/78, SpO2 97 percent. He walks about 15 minutes at a comfortable pace with mild knee pain. He enjoys the physiotherapist's visits, talks at length about his family and says he feels lonely since his wife died. He has hinted that he would like to repay your kindness.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Pause progression, explain why prompt medical review matters, and help him contact his GP or cardiologist): Exertional chest pressure after a stent may signal ischaemia and needs prompt medical review, so exercise progression stops while he is supported to seek it himself, respecting his autonomy. Continuing at lower intensity delays review of a possibly serious symptom. Contacting his daughter without consent breaches confidentiality, and ending care abandons him."}, {"id": 1871, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "As the visit ends, Marco offers a gift voucher of considerable value and invites the physiotherapist to a family dinner, explaining that he has felt lonely since his wife died. How should the physiotherapist respond to maintain professional boundaries while meeting their duty of care?", "options": {"A": "Decline politely, then close his episode of care early to avoid further boundary problems", "B": "Accept the voucher only, since refusing may offend him, and declare it to the practice manager", "C": "Decline both politely, acknowledge his loneliness, and connect him with rehabilitation and community supports", "D": "Accept the dinner invitation once to build rapport, since social connection aids recovery, and record it"}, "cluster_id": "CHS228", "cluster_text": "Marco Esposito, 74, a retired mechanic and widower, lives alone in a suburban home. He is three weeks after a drug-eluting stent to his left anterior descending artery following an NSTEMI. He takes dual antiplatelet therapy, a beta blocker and a statin, and has knee osteoarthritis. His GP referred him to community physiotherapy for a home exercise programme while he waits for cardiac rehabilitation. Resting HR 62, BP 134/78, SpO2 97 percent. He walks about 15 minutes at a comfortable pace with mild knee pain. He enjoys the physiotherapist's visits, talks at length about his family and says he feels lonely since his wife died. He has hinted that he would like to repay your kindness.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Decline both politely, acknowledge his loneliness, and connect him with rehabilitation and community supports): A valuable gift and social invitation blur the therapeutic relationship, so both are declined kindly while his underlying need for connection is addressed through appropriate supports. Accepting either, even with disclosure, compromises objectivity. Ending care to remove the risk would abandon a vulnerable patient still needing treatment."}, {"id": 1872, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Marco takes dual antiplatelet therapy after his stent and enjoys pruning high branches from a ladder. The physiotherapist is finalising his home programme and activity advice. Which precaution is most relevant to this medication and his recent stent when planning activities?", "options": {"A": "Bruising risk with muscle loading, so resistance training is replaced by walking while on therapy", "B": "Increased bleeding risk after trauma, so avoid unsupported height work and activities with high fall risk", "C": "Beta blocker blunts heart rate, so intensity should be set by perceived exertion rather than pulse", "D": "Stent site healing needs protection, so avoid walking and upper limb loading until cardiac rehabilitation begins"}, "cluster_id": "CHS228", "cluster_text": "Marco Esposito, 74, a retired mechanic and widower, lives alone in a suburban home. He is three weeks after a drug-eluting stent to his left anterior descending artery following an NSTEMI. He takes dual antiplatelet therapy, a beta blocker and a statin, and has knee osteoarthritis. His GP referred him to community physiotherapy for a home exercise programme while he waits for cardiac rehabilitation. Resting HR 62, BP 134/78, SpO2 97 percent. He walks about 15 minutes at a comfortable pace with mild knee pain. He enjoys the physiotherapist's visits, talks at length about his family and says he feels lonely since his wife died. He has hinted that he would like to repay your kindness.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Increased bleeding risk after trauma, so avoid unsupported height work and activities with high fall risk): Dual antiplatelet therapy impairs clotting, so a fall or knock can cause significant bleeding, which makes unsupported height work and fall prone activity the relevant precaution. Setting intensity by perceived exertion is sound but arises from his beta blocker, not the antiplatelets or stent. Muscle loading is not the main bleeding concern, and healing of the stent site does not require stopping walking, which is encouraged."}, {"id": 1873, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Bruno has a painful arc between 70 and 110 degrees, pain with resisted abduction and external rotation, strength of 4/5, full passive range, a negative drop arm sign and a normal upper limb neurological screen. Which clinical impression best fits these findings?", "options": {"A": "Rotator cuff related shoulder pain without full thickness tear, where tendon loading provokes symptoms", "B": "Full thickness rotator cuff tear, with pain on loading and weakness that needs urgent surgical referral", "C": "Bicipital tendinopathy, with anterior shoulder pain and tenderness along the long head of biceps", "D": "Acromioclavicular joint osteoarthritis, with local tenderness and pain on cross body adduction of the arm"}, "cluster_id": "CHS229", "cluster_text": "Bruno Ferrari, 72, retired mechanic, right-hand dominant, was referred by his GP to a community health physiotherapy clinic with four months of right shoulder pain, worse with overhead lifting and lying on the right side at night. History: type 2 diabetes managed by diet, hypertension on medication, no earlier shoulder injury. Findings: painful arc between 70 and 110 degrees of active abduction, full passive range with pain at end of elevation, resisted external rotation and abduction painful with strength 4/5, empty can test painful, drop arm sign negative, upper limb neurological screen normal, cervical spine clear. Pain is 6/10 at worst. He wants to keep gardening. The plinth step stool in the clinic has a loose rubber foot, which a colleague flagged last month, and it remains in use.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Rotator cuff related shoulder pain without full thickness tear, where tendon loading provokes symptoms): A painful arc, pain on resisted cuff testing, preserved passive movement and a negative drop arm sign fit rotator cuff related pain without a major tear. Mild weakness of 4/5 with a negative drop arm sign does not suggest a full thickness tear needing urgent surgery. Biceps tendon pain would centre anteriorly, and acromioclavicular pain would be localised with painful cross body adduction, neither of which is described."}, {"id": 1874, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Bruno rates his pain 6/10 at worst, aggravated by overhead lifting, and he wants to keep gardening. He has no red flags and takes no anticoagulants. The physiotherapist has explained the diagnosis and his goals are clear. Which initial treatment approach is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Pendular exercises alone, avoiding resisted work until the painful arc has gone and range returns", "B": "Therapeutic ultrasound and deep friction massage to the tendon, thrice weekly, before active exercise", "C": "Total rest of the shoulder for six weeks, then gradual stretching of the posterior capsule", "D": "Load management advice, then graded isometric and progressive cuff and scapular exercise within tolerable pain"}, "cluster_id": "CHS229", "cluster_text": "Bruno Ferrari, 72, retired mechanic, right-hand dominant, was referred by his GP to a community health physiotherapy clinic with four months of right shoulder pain, worse with overhead lifting and lying on the right side at night. History: type 2 diabetes managed by diet, hypertension on medication, no earlier shoulder injury. Findings: painful arc between 70 and 110 degrees of active abduction, full passive range with pain at end of elevation, resisted external rotation and abduction painful with strength 4/5, empty can test painful, drop arm sign negative, upper limb neurological screen normal, cervical spine clear. Pain is 6/10 at worst. He wants to keep gardening. The plinth step stool in the clinic has a loose rubber foot, which a colleague flagged last month, and it remains in use.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Load management advice, then graded isometric and progressive cuff and scapular exercise within tolerable pain): Education on load and graded strengthening of the cuff and scapular muscles is the best supported first line for tendinopathy. Total rest causes deconditioning, passive modalities have limited benefit alone, and waiting for the arc to disappear delays the loading that drives recovery."}, {"id": 1875, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "After four weeks Bruno's pain is 3/10, yet he still cannot lift a kettle overhead without weakness, and he wants to return to full gardening within a month. He is tolerating current exercises well with no next-day flare. How should the physiotherapist progress his programme?", "options": {"A": "Switch to passive stretching of the shoulder into end range to restore full overhead movement first", "B": "Hold current loads until pain is zero at rest and in every position, before progressing", "C": "Progress cuff and scapular loading gradually and add garden-specific overhead tasks, checking next-day symptoms", "D": "Add heavy overhead lifting immediately now pain has dropped, because tendons need maximal load to adapt"}, "cluster_id": "CHS229", "cluster_text": "Bruno Ferrari, 72, retired mechanic, right-hand dominant, was referred by his GP to a community health physiotherapy clinic with four months of right shoulder pain, worse with overhead lifting and lying on the right side at night. History: type 2 diabetes managed by diet, hypertension on medication, no earlier shoulder injury. Findings: painful arc between 70 and 110 degrees of active abduction, full passive range with pain at end of elevation, resisted external rotation and abduction painful with strength 4/5, empty can test painful, drop arm sign negative, upper limb neurological screen normal, cervical spine clear. Pain is 6/10 at worst. He wants to keep gardening. The plinth step stool in the clinic has a loose rubber foot, which a colleague flagged last month, and it remains in use.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Progress cuff and scapular loading gradually and add garden-specific overhead tasks, checking next-day symptoms): Pain has settled enough to progress load in a staged way towards his real task demands, while checking next-day response. Waiting for zero pain stalls recovery, jumping to heavy lifting risks a flare, and stretching does not address the strength deficit."}, {"id": 1876, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Bruno steps off the plinth using the step stool, which tips, and he falls onto his left side with a forearm graze and a sore hip. He is alert and has been assessed by a doctor. What should the physiotherapist do next?", "options": {"A": "Ask the colleague who noticed the fault to lodge the report, as they know its history", "B": "Tape the loose foot temporarily, keep using the stool, and renew the repair request", "C": "Remove the stool from use, lodge an incident report, and openly discuss the event with Bruno", "D": "Record the fall in his clinical notes only, because the incident system is for staff injuries"}, "cluster_id": "CHS229", "cluster_text": "Bruno Ferrari, 72, retired mechanic, right-hand dominant, was referred by his GP to a community health physiotherapy clinic with four months of right shoulder pain, worse with overhead lifting and lying on the right side at night. History: type 2 diabetes managed by diet, hypertension on medication, no earlier shoulder injury. Findings: painful arc between 70 and 110 degrees of active abduction, full passive range with pain at end of elevation, resisted external rotation and abduction painful with strength 4/5, empty can test painful, drop arm sign negative, upper limb neurological screen normal, cervical spine clear. Pain is 6/10 at worst. He wants to keep gardening. The plinth step stool in the clinic has a loose rubber foot, which a colleague flagged last month, and it remains in use.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Remove the stool from use, lodge an incident report, and openly discuss the event with Bruno): A client harmed by faulty equipment requires immediate hazard control, a formal incident report so the organisation can act and learn, and open disclosure to the patient. Notes alone or a temporary repair leave the risk unmanaged, and the person involved should report rather than delegate."}, {"id": 1877, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Meera's blurred vision after hot showers settles within an hour, and her legs weaken during longer walks in warm weather. No new neurological deficits are found on examination, and symptoms ease fully with cooling and rest. How should the physiotherapist best interpret this pattern?", "options": {"A": "A temporary, heat-related worsening of existing symptoms, not a relapse, so manage heat exposure", "B": "An early relapse that needs same day neurologist review and a pause to all exercise", "C": "Progressive spasticity of the plantarflexors, which should be treated with sustained stretching and night splinting first", "D": "Deconditioning from inactivity, which a gradual increase in aerobic training load should resolve"}, "cluster_id": "CHS230", "cluster_text": "Meera Desai, 38, primary school teacher, has had relapsing remitting multiple sclerosis for six years with no relapse in 18 months. Her neurologist referred her for fatigue and mild right foot drop. She describes heavy fatigue from early afternoon, worse in hot weather, and blurred vision after hot showers that settles within an hour. She sleeps about seven hours. Examination: right ankle dorsiflexion 4/5, right plantarflexor tone mildly increased, sensation intact, steady Romberg, HR 80, BP 116/72, temperature normal. She walks 10 metres comfortably but says her legs turn to lead by the end of a longer walk. She attends with her husband Anil, who answers most questions for her and says she should simply agree to whatever the physiotherapist suggests. Meera has no cognitive impairment on screening and seems hesitant about exercise because she fears it will cause a relapse.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A temporary, heat-related worsening of existing symptoms, not a relapse, so manage heat exposure): A transient worsening of known symptoms with heat that resolves on cooling is a pseudo-exacerbation (Uhthoff phenomenon), not new disease activity. A relapse would involve new or worsening deficits lasting beyond a day, and the pattern does not suggest deconditioning or spasticity."}, {"id": 1878, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Anil says Meera should simply agree to whatever is proposed and offers to sign the exercise plan for her. Meera has no cognitive impairment and is hesitant about starting. What should the physiotherapist do to obtain valid consent for her programme?", "options": {"A": "Accept Anil's signature as her nominated representative, then summarise the plan to Meera afterwards", "B": "Start the programme on Anil's agreement and ask Meera for her consent once benefits are seen", "C": "Explain the plan and risks to Meera directly, address her concerns, and record her own consent", "D": "Ask Meera's neurologist to provide consent for the programme because the diagnosis may affect her decisions"}, "cluster_id": "CHS230", "cluster_text": "Meera Desai, 38, primary school teacher, has had relapsing remitting multiple sclerosis for six years with no relapse in 18 months. Her neurologist referred her for fatigue and mild right foot drop. She describes heavy fatigue from early afternoon, worse in hot weather, and blurred vision after hot showers that settles within an hour. She sleeps about seven hours. Examination: right ankle dorsiflexion 4/5, right plantarflexor tone mildly increased, sensation intact, steady Romberg, HR 80, BP 116/72, temperature normal. She walks 10 metres comfortably but says her legs turn to lead by the end of a longer walk. She attends with her husband Anil, who answers most questions for her and says she should simply agree to whatever the physiotherapist suggests. Meera has no cognitive impairment on screening and seems hesitant about exercise because she fears it will cause a relapse.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Explain the plan and risks to Meera directly, address her concerns, and record her own consent): An adult with decision-making capacity gives her own consent after receiving information about the plan, its risks and alternatives. A spouse cannot consent for her, and a diagnosis alone does not remove capacity or shift consent to the neurologist."}, {"id": 1879, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Meera wants to start exercising but is worried about worsening fatigue. Her fatigue peaks mid afternoon and she cannot cook dinner. She teaches until 3 pm and then drives home in warm weather. Which exercise approach is most appropriate to begin with?", "options": {"A": "One longer session after work each day to build tolerance and push through fatigue", "B": "Brief moderate sessions in the cooler morning with rest breaks, progressed gradually and monitored", "C": "Rest from structured exercise until fatigue improves, because exertion may provoke relapses", "D": "High-intensity interval training in a heated pool to maximise gains while her symptoms are currently stable"}, "cluster_id": "CHS230", "cluster_text": "Meera Desai, 38, primary school teacher, has had relapsing remitting multiple sclerosis for six years with no relapse in 18 months. Her neurologist referred her for fatigue and mild right foot drop. She describes heavy fatigue from early afternoon, worse in hot weather, and blurred vision after hot showers that settles within an hour. She sleeps about seven hours. Examination: right ankle dorsiflexion 4/5, right plantarflexor tone mildly increased, sensation intact, steady Romberg, HR 80, BP 116/72, temperature normal. She walks 10 metres comfortably but says her legs turn to lead by the end of a longer walk. She attends with her husband Anil, who answers most questions for her and says she should simply agree to whatever the physiotherapist suggests. Meera has no cognitive impairment on screening and seems hesitant about exercise because she fears it will cause a relapse.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Brief moderate sessions in the cooler morning with rest breaks, progressed gradually and monitored): Exercise is safe and beneficial in multiple sclerosis when it is paced, kept cool and scheduled away from her fatigue peak. Resting from exercise leads to deconditioning and does not prevent relapse, and heat or high intensity risks provoking symptoms."}, {"id": 1880, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Meera's right foot catches on the ground late in the school day and dorsiflexion strength is 4/5 with mildly increased plantarflexor tone. She has had no falls but is anxious about tripping. Which intervention best addresses her foot clearance while preserving walking endurance?", "options": {"A": "Give daily sustained plantarflexor stretching with night splinting, and limit walking to conserve her energy", "B": "Teach a high stepping gait with exaggerated hip flexion, practised on the treadmill at speed", "C": "Trial an ankle foot orthosis or electrical stimulation with dorsiflexor strengthening and gait practice", "D": "Use a rigid ankle foot orthosis fixed in neutral for all walking, including indoor household distances"}, "cluster_id": "CHS230", "cluster_text": "Meera Desai, 38, primary school teacher, has had relapsing remitting multiple sclerosis for six years with no relapse in 18 months. Her neurologist referred her for fatigue and mild right foot drop. She describes heavy fatigue from early afternoon, worse in hot weather, and blurred vision after hot showers that settles within an hour. She sleeps about seven hours. Examination: right ankle dorsiflexion 4/5, right plantarflexor tone mildly increased, sensation intact, steady Romberg, HR 80, BP 116/72, temperature normal. She walks 10 metres comfortably but says her legs turn to lead by the end of a longer walk. She attends with her husband Anil, who answers most questions for her and says she should simply agree to whatever the physiotherapist suggests. Meera has no cognitive impairment on screening and seems hesitant about exercise because she fears it will cause a relapse.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Trial an ankle foot orthosis or electrical stimulation with dorsiflexor strengthening and gait practice): A lightweight orthosis or functional electrical stimulation improves clearance with little energy cost, and strengthening and gait practice address the underlying weakness. High stepping is energy expensive and fatiguing, and a rigid orthosis for all walking reduces push-off and increases effort."}, {"id": 1881, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "On forward bend testing, Aisha shows a right thoracic prominence that remains visible when she bends and the scoliometer registers trunk rotation. The physiotherapist needs to explain what this sign indicates to the school nurse. Which interpretation is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Vertebral rotation consistent with a structural curve, needing radiographic confirmation of curve size", "B": "Postural asymmetry that usually corrects on forward bend, so monitoring and posture advice are sufficient", "C": "Vertebral rotation consistent with a structural curve, so bracing should begin immediately without radiographs", "D": "A structural curve of small size, so repeat scoliometer screening in school next year is sufficient"}, "cluster_id": "CHS231", "cluster_text": "Aisha Khalil, 13, is seen at a school screening day run by your community physiotherapy service, with the school nurse present. Her parents have given written consent for screening but are not at school. Aisha is premenarchal, 148 cm tall, and has noticed her shirt hangs unevenly. On forward bend testing there is a right thoracic prominence and a scoliometer reading of 9 degrees of trunk rotation at T7. Standing, her right shoulder is slightly higher and her waist creases are asymmetrical. She reports no back pain, numbness or weakness. Neurological screening and gait are normal. A relative on her father's side has scoliosis. During the day a student trips over a bag and bruises her forearm against a table. She is comfortable, but the school has no protocol for events run by outside services, and your service policy requires an incident report for any harm at community events.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Vertebral rotation consistent with a structural curve, needing radiographic confirmation of curve size): A rib prominence that persists on forward bending with measurable trunk rotation reflects vertebral rotation, the hallmark of a structural curve, and its size must be measured on a radiograph with specialist review. Postural curves tend to correct on bending. Bracing decisions need imaging and specialist input first. Waiting a year for repeat screening risks progression in a growing adolescent with a family history."}, {"id": 1882, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Aisha has no pain, a normal neurological screen, is premenarchal, and has a right thoracic curve with measurable rotation. Her parents are not present at school today. What is the most appropriate next step for the physiotherapist after the screening and before she leaves?", "options": {"A": "Start a Schroth-based exercise programme at school and review rotation again in six months before referral", "B": "Advise no action is needed because she has no pain and a normal neurological screen", "C": "Give parents a written result and advise GP review for radiographs and specialist referral", "D": "Fit a rigid brace immediately and arrange follow-up with the school nurse for wear monitoring"}, "cluster_id": "CHS231", "cluster_text": "Aisha Khalil, 13, is seen at a school screening day run by your community physiotherapy service, with the school nurse present. Her parents have given written consent for screening but are not at school. Aisha is premenarchal, 148 cm tall, and has noticed her shirt hangs unevenly. On forward bend testing there is a right thoracic prominence and a scoliometer reading of 9 degrees of trunk rotation at T7. Standing, her right shoulder is slightly higher and her waist creases are asymmetrical. She reports no back pain, numbness or weakness. Neurological screening and gait are normal. A relative on her father's side has scoliosis. During the day a student trips over a bag and bruises her forearm against a table. She is comfortable, but the school has no protocol for events run by outside services, and your service policy requires an incident report for any harm at community events.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Give parents a written result and advise GP review for radiographs and specialist referral): A positive screen in a skeletally immature adolescent needs radiographic confirmation and specialist opinion, because curves can progress during growth. Parents should receive a written result so the GP can arrange imaging. Exercise alone while waiting delays referral, and bracing decisions belong to the specialist after imaging. Absence of pain does not exclude a progressive curve."}, {"id": 1883, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Later, a paediatric orthopaedic specialist prescribes a rigid brace for Aisha after radiographs confirm a curve, and she has considerable growth remaining. She asks how long she should wear it and why, and her mother wants to understand the purpose. Which explanation reflects current evidence-informed practice?", "options": {"A": "Bracing slows progression, so wearing can be reduced once the curve looks smaller in clothes", "B": "Bracing holds the curve while she grows, but she can choose the hours to wear it", "C": "Bracing is worn until radiographs show the curve has fully corrected, then it can be stopped", "D": "Prescribed daily brace wear lowers the risk of progression, so adherence matters until skeletal maturity"}, "cluster_id": "CHS231", "cluster_text": "Aisha Khalil, 13, is seen at a school screening day run by your community physiotherapy service, with the school nurse present. Her parents have given written consent for screening but are not at school. Aisha is premenarchal, 148 cm tall, and has noticed her shirt hangs unevenly. On forward bend testing there is a right thoracic prominence and a scoliometer reading of 9 degrees of trunk rotation at T7. Standing, her right shoulder is slightly higher and her waist creases are asymmetrical. She reports no back pain, numbness or weakness. Neurological screening and gait are normal. A relative on her father's side has scoliosis. During the day a student trips over a bag and bruises her forearm against a table. She is comfortable, but the school has no protocol for events run by outside services, and your service policy requires an incident report for any harm at community events.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Prescribed daily brace wear lowers the risk of progression, so adherence matters until skeletal maturity): Bracing aims to stop progression during growth, and better outcomes are linked to more hours of wear as prescribed, so adherence until skeletal maturity is the key message. Appearance in clothes does not show that the curve is safe. Wear time is set by the specialist, not left to preference. Bracing seldom corrects a curve fully, and it is stopped at maturity rather than at correction."}, {"id": 1884, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "During the screening a student trips over a bag and bruises her forearm against a table. She is comfortable and declines further review, and the school has no protocol for events run by outside services. What should the physiotherapist do next?", "options": {"A": "Wait until the school creates a protocol, then report the event using its form retrospectively", "B": "Email the school principal only, since the school is responsible for student safety on its grounds", "C": "Take no further action since the injury is minor, but mention it to the school nurse", "D": "Assess and record the injury, inform parents and school, and complete an incident report"}, "cluster_id": "CHS231", "cluster_text": "Aisha Khalil, 13, is seen at a school screening day run by your community physiotherapy service, with the school nurse present. Her parents have given written consent for screening but are not at school. Aisha is premenarchal, 148 cm tall, and has noticed her shirt hangs unevenly. On forward bend testing there is a right thoracic prominence and a scoliometer reading of 9 degrees of trunk rotation at T7. Standing, her right shoulder is slightly higher and her waist creases are asymmetrical. She reports no back pain, numbness or weakness. Neurological screening and gait are normal. A relative on her father's side has scoliosis. During the day a student trips over a bag and bruises her forearm against a table. She is comfortable, but the school has no protocol for events run by outside services, and your service policy requires an incident report for any harm at community events.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Assess and record the injury, inform parents and school, and complete an incident report): Harm at an event the service runs triggers the service's own incident reporting, together with documentation of the assessment and notification of the parents and school. A minor injury still needs reporting so hazards can be reviewed. Waiting for a school protocol or leaving reporting to the school ignores the physiotherapist's own organisational duty."}, {"id": 1885, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Hai is still considered potentially infectious while public health review is pending. He will attend the clinic for assessment next week. Which precaution should the physiotherapist use when in close contact with him in an enclosed room and examining his chest?", "options": {"A": "A surgical mask and eye protection for the physiotherapist, with Hai also wearing a surgical mask", "B": "Contact precautions only: gloves, a gown, and hand hygiene before and after the assessment", "C": "A fitted P2 respirator for the physiotherapist and a surgical mask for Hai", "D": "A fitted P2 respirator on both people so that exhaled and inhaled air are each filtered"}, "cluster_id": "CHS232", "cluster_text": "Hai Tran, 54, arrived from Vietnam four years ago and speaks very little English. He lives with his wife and 16-year-old son. Pulmonary tuberculosis was confirmed 10 days ago and he started four-drug therapy. The public health unit has not yet advised that he is non-infectious. He is referred to community physiotherapy for deconditioning and a weak, productive cough. Observations: HR 88, RR 22, SpO2 94% on room air, afebrile. Weight 52 kg, down from 61 kg. Coarse crackles in the right upper zone. He walks 150 m with one rest, Borg 4/10. His son has attended every appointment and has translated so far. The community health centre has a shared gym and a professional interpreter booking service. The first appointment is planned for next Thursday.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A fitted P2 respirator for the physiotherapist and a surgical mask for Hai): Pulmonary TB spreads by airborne particles, so the clinician needs a fitted P2 respirator while the patient wears a surgical mask to reduce spread from his own coughing. Surgical masks do not protect the wearer from airborne particles, and a respirator on the patient is not the usual approach. Contact precautions alone do not address airborne spread."}, {"id": 1886, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Hai's son offers to translate again, saying he missed school to help. The physiotherapist needs to explain infection control, take a history of weight loss and discuss exercise limits. Hai seems reluctant to discuss his weight in front of his son. Which approach to communication is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Use a translation app on a phone for the key points and give a Vietnamese leaflet", "B": "Book a professional Vietnamese interpreter, brief them, and speak directly to Hai during assessment", "C": "Let his son interpret, speaking slowly, then use teach-back by asking Hai to repeat key points", "D": "Ask a Vietnamese-speaking staff member from the gym team to interpret informally during the appointment"}, "cluster_id": "CHS232", "cluster_text": "Hai Tran, 54, arrived from Vietnam four years ago and speaks very little English. He lives with his wife and 16-year-old son. Pulmonary tuberculosis was confirmed 10 days ago and he started four-drug therapy. The public health unit has not yet advised that he is non-infectious. He is referred to community physiotherapy for deconditioning and a weak, productive cough. Observations: HR 88, RR 22, SpO2 94% on room air, afebrile. Weight 52 kg, down from 61 kg. Coarse crackles in the right upper zone. He walks 150 m with one rest, Borg 4/10. His son has attended every appointment and has translated so far. The community health centre has a shared gym and a professional interpreter booking service. The first appointment is planned for next Thursday.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Book a professional Vietnamese interpreter, brief them, and speak directly to Hai during assessment): Family members, especially a child, should not interpret clinical content such as weight loss or infection risk because accuracy, privacy and the patient's willingness to speak freely are all compromised. A professionally trained interpreter, briefed beforehand, with the clinician speaking directly to the patient, supports informed participation. Apps and untrained staff lack the accuracy and accountability required."}, {"id": 1887, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Hai's walking tolerance and weakness suggest he would benefit from a graded exercise programme. Public health has not yet cleared him. The clinic's shared gym is used by other clients on Thursdays. Where and how should the first sessions be delivered?", "options": {"A": "A home programme with telehealth review, or a single well-ventilated room, until public health advises clearance", "B": "The shared gym with Hai kept two metres from other clients and wearing a surgical mask", "C": "Outdoor group walking with other rehabilitation clients, since fresh air removes infectious particles, wearing surgical masks", "D": "The shared gym after hours when empty, with both Hai and the physiotherapist wearing surgical masks"}, "cluster_id": "CHS232", "cluster_text": "Hai Tran, 54, arrived from Vietnam four years ago and speaks very little English. He lives with his wife and 16-year-old son. Pulmonary tuberculosis was confirmed 10 days ago and he started four-drug therapy. The public health unit has not yet advised that he is non-infectious. He is referred to community physiotherapy for deconditioning and a weak, productive cough. Observations: HR 88, RR 22, SpO2 94% on room air, afebrile. Weight 52 kg, down from 61 kg. Coarse crackles in the right upper zone. He walks 150 m with one rest, Borg 4/10. His son has attended every appointment and has translated so far. The community health centre has a shared gym and a professional interpreter booking service. The first appointment is planned for next Thursday.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A home programme with telehealth review, or a single well-ventilated room, until public health advises clearance): While infectiousness is unconfirmed he should avoid shared spaces, so a home programme with remote review or a single well-ventilated room protects others and still starts rehabilitation. Surgical masks and spacing do not reliably prevent airborne spread in a gym. Group outdoor sessions still bring him close to vulnerable clients."}, {"id": 1888, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "During the first session Hai coughs up a small clot of bright red blood after walking 100 m. His SpO2 is 93%, HR 104 and he is anxious. The room is well ventilated and he is wearing a surgical mask. What is the most appropriate immediate action?", "options": {"A": "Stop exercise, sit him upright, reassure him, monitor observations and contact the treating doctor today", "B": "Continue walking more slowly while monitoring saturation, and report the blood at his next medical appointment", "C": "Lie him flat with legs raised to support blood pressure and call an ambulance", "D": "Teach active cycle of breathing technique to clear the blood from his airways, then resume exercise"}, "cluster_id": "CHS232", "cluster_text": "Hai Tran, 54, arrived from Vietnam four years ago and speaks very little English. He lives with his wife and 16-year-old son. Pulmonary tuberculosis was confirmed 10 days ago and he started four-drug therapy. The public health unit has not yet advised that he is non-infectious. He is referred to community physiotherapy for deconditioning and a weak, productive cough. Observations: HR 88, RR 22, SpO2 94% on room air, afebrile. Weight 52 kg, down from 61 kg. Coarse crackles in the right upper zone. He walks 150 m with one rest, Borg 4/10. His son has attended every appointment and has translated so far. The community health centre has a shared gym and a professional interpreter booking service. The first appointment is planned for next Thursday.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stop exercise, sit him upright, reassure him, monitor observations and contact the treating doctor today): A small bloodstained clot in active TB needs the treating team to know the same day, and stopping exercise with upright positioning protects the airway while observations are monitored. Airway clearance techniques or continued exercise provoke more coughing, and lying flat risks aspiration and worse breathlessness. Emergency help would be needed if bleeding became heavy."}, {"id": 1889, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "At today's review Daniel cannot recall yesterday's physiotherapy session, is disoriented to date and place, but follows simple one-step commands. Mei asks whether repeating explanations is worth the effort. Which approach to planning his treatment this week is most appropriate for the physiotherapist?", "options": {"A": "Quiz him on orientation each session to speed recovery of recall and chart daily progress", "B": "Give detailed written exercise instructions so he can practise alone and retain the programme overnight", "C": "Withhold active mobilisation until orientation returns, because movement during amnesia risks agitation and falls", "D": "Use short sessions with consistent routines and repeated cues, expecting limited new learning until amnesia resolves"}, "cluster_id": "CHS233", "cluster_text": "Daniel Kowalczyk, 34, a courier and recreational cyclist, was hit by a car 9 days ago. CT showed bifrontal contusions and a small right subdural haematoma managed conservatively. He is on a neurosurgical ward. Glasgow Coma Scale today is 12. He is disoriented to date and place and cannot recall yesterday's physiotherapy session, consistent with ongoing post-traumatic amnesia. Left upper limb strength is MRC grade 3, left lower limb grade 3 to 4, right side grade 5. He sits at the bed edge with close supervision but is impulsive and stands without warning. HR 76 beats per minute, BP 124/78 mmHg, SpO2 97 percent on room air, headache 2/10. He fatigues after about 10 minutes of activity. He smokes 15 cigarettes daily and drinks heavily on weekends. His partner, Mei, visits every evening and asks how she can help.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Use short sessions with consistent routines and repeated cues, expecting limited new learning until amnesia resolves): During post-traumatic amnesia new information is poorly stored, so predictable routines, brief sessions and repeated cues support safe participation while learning stays limited. Written instructions fail because he cannot retain them. Withholding mobilisation delays recovery and deconditions him, and repeated orientation quizzing tends to frustrate rather than speed recovery."}, {"id": 1890, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Daniel stood from the bed edge without warning during today's session and his left knee buckled briefly. Left lower limb strength is grade 3 to 4, and he remains impulsive and amnesic. Which plan for his next mobility session is the safest and most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Rely on a bed alarm and low bed, leaving out-of-bed activity to nursing staff until transfer", "B": "Provide supervised standing and transfers with two staff, giving one simple cue before each movement", "C": "Progress to walking with a gait belt and one therapist, promoting active motor learning and confidence", "D": "Limit him to bed exercises until impulsivity settles, then restart standing practice with one therapist"}, "cluster_id": "CHS233", "cluster_text": "Daniel Kowalczyk, 34, a courier and recreational cyclist, was hit by a car 9 days ago. CT showed bifrontal contusions and a small right subdural haematoma managed conservatively. He is on a neurosurgical ward. Glasgow Coma Scale today is 12. He is disoriented to date and place and cannot recall yesterday's physiotherapy session, consistent with ongoing post-traumatic amnesia. Left upper limb strength is MRC grade 3, left lower limb grade 3 to 4, right side grade 5. He sits at the bed edge with close supervision but is impulsive and stands without warning. HR 76 beats per minute, BP 124/78 mmHg, SpO2 97 percent on room air, headache 2/10. He fatigues after about 10 minutes of activity. He smokes 15 cigarettes daily and drinks heavily on weekends. His partner, Mei, visits every evening and asks how she can help.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Provide supervised standing and transfers with two staff, giving one simple cue before each movement): A buckling knee combined with impulsivity calls for enough staff to control a sudden rise, plus one clear cue at a time that suits his impaired memory. Prolonged bed-based work would deprive him of the graded upright practice he needs. Walking with a single therapist is too ambitious given the buckle, and handing all activity to nursing abandons the physiotherapy role."}, {"id": 1891, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "On day 12 Daniel develops a worsening headache and vomits once during a bed exercise session. He is drowsier than this morning, with eye opening only to voice, heart rate 58 beats per minute and blood pressure 156/90 mmHg. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Continue at reduced intensity, then recheck heart rate, blood pressure and alertness after a short rest", "B": "Sit him out of bed to ease the headache and reassess alertness after thirty minutes upright", "C": "Lay him flat to improve cerebral perfusion, finish the session and report the headache at handover", "D": "Stop the session, position him safely with head elevated, and urgently alert the nurse in charge"}, "cluster_id": "CHS233", "cluster_text": "Daniel Kowalczyk, 34, a courier and recreational cyclist, was hit by a car 9 days ago. CT showed bifrontal contusions and a small right subdural haematoma managed conservatively. He is on a neurosurgical ward. Glasgow Coma Scale today is 12. He is disoriented to date and place and cannot recall yesterday's physiotherapy session, consistent with ongoing post-traumatic amnesia. Left upper limb strength is MRC grade 3, left lower limb grade 3 to 4, right side grade 5. He sits at the bed edge with close supervision but is impulsive and stands without warning. HR 76 beats per minute, BP 124/78 mmHg, SpO2 97 percent on room air, headache 2/10. He fatigues after about 10 minutes of activity. He smokes 15 cigarettes daily and drinks heavily on weekends. His partner, Mei, visits every evening and asks how she can help.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stop the session, position him safely with head elevated, and urgently alert the nurse in charge): Worsening headache, vomiting, falling alertness, a slow pulse and rising blood pressure suggest raised intracranial pressure, which is a medical emergency needing immediate escalation. Treatment must stop, and a head-up position with a protected airway is safest while help is called. Waiting, continuing or deferring the report to handover loses critical time."}, {"id": 1892, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Daniel smokes daily and drinks heavily on weekends, and Mei asks how she can help his long-term health. His insight is limited while amnesia persists. What is the most appropriate health promotion and self-management approach for the physiotherapist to take now?", "options": {"A": "Give brief repeated advice to both, involve Mei in goals, and link him with cessation support", "B": "Provide a detailed written plan to reduce alcohol and tobacco, then review with Daniel next week", "C": "Ask Mei to supervise his abstinence from smoking and alcohol and report lapses to the team", "D": "Defer lifestyle discussion until discharge, since advice cannot be retained during amnesia and would be wasted"}, "cluster_id": "CHS233", "cluster_text": "Daniel Kowalczyk, 34, a courier and recreational cyclist, was hit by a car 9 days ago. CT showed bifrontal contusions and a small right subdural haematoma managed conservatively. He is on a neurosurgical ward. Glasgow Coma Scale today is 12. He is disoriented to date and place and cannot recall yesterday's physiotherapy session, consistent with ongoing post-traumatic amnesia. Left upper limb strength is MRC grade 3, left lower limb grade 3 to 4, right side grade 5. He sits at the bed edge with close supervision but is impulsive and stands without warning. HR 76 beats per minute, BP 124/78 mmHg, SpO2 97 percent on room air, headache 2/10. He fatigues after about 10 minutes of activity. He smokes 15 cigarettes daily and drinks heavily on weekends. His partner, Mei, visits every evening and asks how she can help.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Give brief repeated advice to both, involve Mei in goals, and link him with cessation support): Brief, repeated messages delivered with his partner build supportive habits now and prepare for self-management as memory returns, while cessation support addresses a recognised risk after brain injury. Complete deferral wastes a chance to involve family. A detailed written plan exceeds his current capacity, and making Mei an enforcer undermines his autonomy and their relationship."}, {"id": 1893, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Elena's Spurling's test is negative, cervical rotation is full, and ulnar neurodynamic testing reproduces her tingling with symptoms changing when the wrist is moved. Symptoms also change with shoulder girdle position. What does this most strongly suggest about the source of her symptoms?", "options": {"A": "Carpal tunnel syndrome at the wrist, because wrist movement changed her symptoms during neurodynamic testing", "B": "Arterial thoracic outlet syndrome, because overhead positioning reproduces heaviness and grip strength is reduced", "C": "Cervical radiculopathy at C8, because ring and little finger tingling follows that dermatome", "D": "Peripheral ulnar nerve tissue sensitivity near the thoracic outlet rather than a cervical root problem"}, "cluster_id": "CHS234", "cluster_text": "Elena Rossi, 29, hairdresser, attends a private practice with six months of right arm aching, tingling in the ring and little fingers, and heaviness with overhead work, worse by the end of the day. She works standing for hours with her arms raised cutting hair and cannot take time off. Holding her arms overhead for about a minute reproduces her symptoms. She has a forward head posture and rounded shoulders. Supraclavicular palpation on the right is tender and reproduces tingling. Elevated arm stress test is positive on the right, Spurling's test is negative and cervical rotation is full and pain free. Ulnar neurodynamic testing reproduces tingling, and symptoms change when the wrist is moved. Radial pulses are present and equal, with no colour or temperature change. Grip strength is mildly reduced. Elena is friendly and has started following the physiotherapist's personal social media accounts.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Peripheral ulnar nerve tissue sensitivity near the thoracic outlet rather than a cervical root problem): A negative Spurling's test with full, pain free neck movement makes a cervical root source less likely, while symptoms that change with wrist and shoulder girdle position implicate the nerve tissue along its path. Carpal tunnel affects median nerve territory, and her equal pulses and normal colour argue against an arterial cause."}, {"id": 1894, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Elena's overhead positioning reproduces aching and tingling. The physiotherapist considers which examination findings would indicate vascular compromise needing medical review before any treatment. Her radial pulses are currently equal and she has no colour change. Which finding would most strongly indicate this?", "options": {"A": "Radial pulse fading and hand colour changing only during overhead arm positioning, normal on lowering", "B": "Tingling and aching reproduced by sustained overhead elevation, relieved by lowering the arm", "C": "Tenderness over the scalenes with reproduction of arm tingling and a mildly reduced grip strength", "D": "A cool pale hand with a diminished radial pulse and a supraclavicular bruit"}, "cluster_id": "CHS234", "cluster_text": "Elena Rossi, 29, hairdresser, attends a private practice with six months of right arm aching, tingling in the ring and little fingers, and heaviness with overhead work, worse by the end of the day. She works standing for hours with her arms raised cutting hair and cannot take time off. Holding her arms overhead for about a minute reproduces her symptoms. She has a forward head posture and rounded shoulders. Supraclavicular palpation on the right is tender and reproduces tingling. Elevated arm stress test is positive on the right, Spurling's test is negative and cervical rotation is full and pain free. Ulnar neurodynamic testing reproduces tingling, and symptoms change when the wrist is moved. Radial pulses are present and equal, with no colour or temperature change. Grip strength is mildly reduced. Elena is friendly and has started following the physiotherapist's personal social media accounts.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A cool pale hand with a diminished radial pulse and a supraclavicular bruit): Pallor, coolness, a diminished resting pulse and a bruit suggest compression of the subclavian artery, which needs medical review before treatment. Pulse or colour change that appears only with the arms overhead and settles on lowering is common and does not alone show vascular compromise. Positional tingling, scalene tenderness and mild weakness fit a neurogenic presentation that can be managed conservatively."}, {"id": 1895, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Elena's symptoms are irritable with overhead work and the elevated arm stress test. She asks what to do first. She works standing for hours with her arms raised and cannot take time off. Which initial treatment approach is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Sustained ulnar nerve tensioning to end range daily, with firm stretching of the scalene muscles", "B": "Modify work postures, then add gentle neural gliding and scapular control exercises within comfort", "C": "Rest the arm in a sling for several weeks, beginning exercises once symptoms settle", "D": "Heavy resisted overhead press and row training three times weekly to strengthen the shoulder girdle"}, "cluster_id": "CHS234", "cluster_text": "Elena Rossi, 29, hairdresser, attends a private practice with six months of right arm aching, tingling in the ring and little fingers, and heaviness with overhead work, worse by the end of the day. She works standing for hours with her arms raised cutting hair and cannot take time off. Holding her arms overhead for about a minute reproduces her symptoms. She has a forward head posture and rounded shoulders. Supraclavicular palpation on the right is tender and reproduces tingling. Elevated arm stress test is positive on the right, Spurling's test is negative and cervical rotation is full and pain free. Ulnar neurodynamic testing reproduces tingling, and symptoms change when the wrist is moved. Radial pulses are present and equal, with no colour or temperature change. Grip strength is mildly reduced. Elena is friendly and has started following the physiotherapist's personal social media accounts.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Modify work postures, then add gentle neural gliding and scapular control exercises within comfort): An irritable nerve disorder responds best to reducing provoking positions and loading it gently with sliding rather than tensioning movements, alongside improving scapular control. Aggressive tensioning or heavy overhead loading flares symptoms, and prolonged sling rest is impractical for her work and promotes stiffness."}, {"id": 1896, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "At her third visit Elena invites the physiotherapist to a private party, suggests after hours sessions at her home, and sends a social media friend request, saying she would feel more relaxed. She is progressing well with treatment. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Decline kindly, explain the professional boundary, and continue her care in the clinic setting", "B": "Discharge Elena with a referral elsewhere and explain that social contact was the reason", "C": "Attend the party once, keep sessions in the clinic, and write a note in her record", "D": "Agree to after hours clinic sessions, decline the party, and accept the social media friend request"}, "cluster_id": "CHS234", "cluster_text": "Elena Rossi, 29, hairdresser, attends a private practice with six months of right arm aching, tingling in the ring and little fingers, and heaviness with overhead work, worse by the end of the day. She works standing for hours with her arms raised cutting hair and cannot take time off. Holding her arms overhead for about a minute reproduces her symptoms. She has a forward head posture and rounded shoulders. Supraclavicular palpation on the right is tender and reproduces tingling. Elevated arm stress test is positive on the right, Spurling's test is negative and cervical rotation is full and pain free. Ulnar neurodynamic testing reproduces tingling, and symptoms change when the wrist is moved. Radial pulses are present and equal, with no colour or temperature change. Grip strength is mildly reduced. Elena is friendly and has started following the physiotherapist's personal social media accounts.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Decline kindly, explain the professional boundary, and continue her care in the clinic setting): Maintaining a clear therapeutic relationship protects the patient and the physiotherapist, and a respectful explanation lets treatment continue without damage to trust. Socialising or personal online contact blurs the boundary, and discharge is disproportionate because the boundary can be restored by discussion."}, {"id": 1897, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "You prescribe aerobic exercise intensity for Mr Fontana's walking programme. He takes metoprolol, so his heart rate response to exercise is blunted. Which method is most appropriate for guiding and progressing his exercise intensity across the eight weeks of the programme?", "options": {"A": "Calculate heart rate reserve from age-predicted maximum and prescribe fifty to seventy percent of that range", "B": "Progress by adding a set walking distance each week, calculated from his baseline walk test result", "C": "Use a perceived exertion target in the moderate range, checking symptoms and blood pressure", "D": "Hold heart rate at seventy percent of age-predicted maximum, checked each session with a wrist monitor"}, "cluster_id": "CHS235", "cluster_text": "Mr Salvatore Fontana, 64, a retired baker, attends phase two cardiac rehabilitation five weeks after coronary artery bypass grafting (three grafts) for triple vessel disease. The sternotomy has healed well with no sternal clicking. Ejection fraction 45 percent. Medications: metoprolol, aspirin, atorvastatin, metformin. Resting HR 62, BP 118/72 mmHg, SpO2 97 percent. Baseline 6 minute walk test: 380 m, peak HR 98, Borg rating of 13 at the end, no symptoms. He has type 2 diabetes, walks his dog daily and is anxious about overdoing it. The programme runs twice weekly for eight weeks as a group circuit plus walking. The unit manager wants to show that the programme improves outcomes and has asked every physiotherapist to help choose measures to collect for all participants.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use a perceived exertion target in the moderate range, checking symptoms and blood pressure): Beta-blockers blunt the heart rate response, so targets built on age-predicted maximum heart rate underestimate his true effort. A perceived exertion range with symptom and blood pressure checks tracks his actual intensity and can be progressed as fitness improves. Distance-only progression ignores how he responds on the day."}, {"id": 1898, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "At session four, Mr Fontana mentions a clicking sensation in his sternum when pushing up from a chair, and the lower wound looks red with slight discharge. His temperature is 37.9 degrees Celsius. What should you do first in this session?", "options": {"A": "Fit a sternal support and continue walking at usual intensity, recording the symptoms in his notes", "B": "Reassure him that clicking is common and continue the programme with a pillow hugged for support", "C": "Advise him to avoid arm exercise until review and mention it to his GP this week", "D": "Stop upper limb loading, document the findings, and contact the cardiothoracic team the same day"}, "cluster_id": "CHS235", "cluster_text": "Mr Salvatore Fontana, 64, a retired baker, attends phase two cardiac rehabilitation five weeks after coronary artery bypass grafting (three grafts) for triple vessel disease. The sternotomy has healed well with no sternal clicking. Ejection fraction 45 percent. Medications: metoprolol, aspirin, atorvastatin, metformin. Resting HR 62, BP 118/72 mmHg, SpO2 97 percent. Baseline 6 minute walk test: 380 m, peak HR 98, Borg rating of 13 at the end, no symptoms. He has type 2 diabetes, walks his dog daily and is anxious about overdoing it. The programme runs twice weekly for eight weeks as a group circuit plus walking. The unit manager wants to show that the programme improves outcomes and has asked every physiotherapist to help choose measures to collect for all participants.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stop upper limb loading, document the findings, and contact the cardiothoracic team the same day): A sternal click with wound redness, discharge and a raised temperature raises concern for sternal instability and deep wound infection, which need prompt surgical assessment. Loading through the arms is stopped while this is reviewed. Reassurance, a binder or waiting for routine follow-up delays treatment of a serious complication."}, {"id": 1899, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "The unit manager wants an audit of whether the eight week programme improves participants' function and confidence, using data the team can collect routinely. Which set of measures best allows a fair before and after comparison for every participant in the programme?", "options": {"A": "Compare 6 minute walk distance and validated quality of life scores at entry and exit", "B": "Report attendance numbers, session completion and patient satisfaction ratings collected at the end of the programme", "C": "Count readmissions and deaths among attendees over the following year compared with last year's published rates", "D": "Record resting heart rate and blood pressure at each session and graph the group average"}, "cluster_id": "CHS235", "cluster_text": "Mr Salvatore Fontana, 64, a retired baker, attends phase two cardiac rehabilitation five weeks after coronary artery bypass grafting (three grafts) for triple vessel disease. The sternotomy has healed well with no sternal clicking. Ejection fraction 45 percent. Medications: metoprolol, aspirin, atorvastatin, metformin. Resting HR 62, BP 118/72 mmHg, SpO2 97 percent. Baseline 6 minute walk test: 380 m, peak HR 98, Borg rating of 13 at the end, no symptoms. He has type 2 diabetes, walks his dog daily and is anxious about overdoing it. The programme runs twice weekly for eight weeks as a group circuit plus walking. The unit manager wants to show that the programme improves outcomes and has asked every physiotherapist to help choose measures to collect for all participants.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Compare 6 minute walk distance and validated quality of life scores at entry and exit): A fair evaluation uses the same validated outcome measures at entry and exit for each person, and a walking test with a quality of life tool captures the function and confidence the manager asked about. Attendance and satisfaction describe the service rather than change in patients. Vital signs vary from day to day, and population outcomes cannot be attributed to the programme without a comparison group."}, {"id": 1900, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Fontana is walking on a treadmill during a supervised cardiac rehabilitation group session, with his heart rate, blood pressure and symptoms being monitored. Which new development during the walk is a recognised reason to stop exercise and reassess him immediately?", "options": {"A": "Systolic pressure falling below his resting level as workload increases, with new light-headedness", "B": "Mild leg muscle fatigue reported as 13 on the Borg scale after several minutes of walking", "C": "Slightly faster breathing with systolic pressure rising about twenty mmHg above his resting value", "D": "Heart rate rising steadily with speed to about thirty beats above his resting rate"}, "cluster_id": "CHS235", "cluster_text": "Mr Salvatore Fontana, 64, a retired baker, attends phase two cardiac rehabilitation five weeks after coronary artery bypass grafting (three grafts) for triple vessel disease. The sternotomy has healed well with no sternal clicking. Ejection fraction 45 percent. Medications: metoprolol, aspirin, atorvastatin, metformin. Resting HR 62, BP 118/72 mmHg, SpO2 97 percent. Baseline 6 minute walk test: 380 m, peak HR 98, Borg rating of 13 at the end, no symptoms. He has type 2 diabetes, walks his dog daily and is anxious about overdoing it. The programme runs twice weekly for eight weeks as a group circuit plus walking. The unit manager wants to show that the programme improves outcomes and has asked every physiotherapist to help choose measures to collect for all participants.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Systolic pressure falling below his resting level as workload increases, with new light-headedness): A fall in systolic pressure as workload rises suggests the heart cannot raise its output to meet demand and is a standard criterion to stop exercise. A moderate heart rate rise, mild muscle fatigue at a moderate exertion rating and a rise in systolic pressure with effort are expected responses. Beta-blockade does not make a falling pressure normal."}, {"id": 1901, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Jayden is still wheezy, with a heart rate of 108 and respiratory rate of 24, and is now speaking in sentences with SpO2 95% on oxygen. Which new finding during physiotherapy would most strongly suggest deterioration towards life-threatening asthma and require immediate medical help?", "options": {"A": "Louder expiratory wheeze with a longer expiratory phase and a slightly higher heart rate than before", "B": "A cough that becomes productive of thick sputum while wheeze persists and his breathing eases slightly", "C": "Soreness of the lower ribs and abdomen from repeated forceful coughing, with unchanged wheeze and saturations", "D": "A silent chest with markedly reduced air entry, new drowsiness and weakening respiratory effort"}, "cluster_id": "CHS236", "cluster_text": "Jayden Walker, 15, Year 10 student and keen footballer, attended the emergency department with his mother. Six hours of worsening wheeze, cough and chest tightness after a cold. Asthma since age six; he has a preventer inhaler but admits missing doses lately, and a reliever at school. On arrival: RR 30, HR 120, SpO2 92% on room air, wheeze in both lungs, accessory muscle use, speaking in short phrases, alert. The team gave repeated salbutamol through a spacer, an oral corticosteroid and oxygen. Now RR 24, HR 108, SpO2 95% on 2 L/min, still wheezy, speaking in sentences, dry cough. He is to move to the short stay unit and the physiotherapist is asked to provide breathing education. His mother read about a breathing retraining programme online and asks whether it should replace his preventer.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A silent chest with markedly reduced air entry, new drowsiness and weakening respiratory effort): A quiet chest with poor air entry, drowsiness and tiring means airflow is so limited that wheeze has stopped, which is a pre-arrest warning. Loud wheeze indicates airflow is still present. Sputum and muscle soreness are common features of an exacerbation and are not signs of life-threatening deterioration."}, {"id": 1902, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Jayden remains wheezy and breathless with a dry cough and no sputum. Medical staff have cleared him for physiotherapy. He leans forward on his elbows, looks anxious and is gripping the bed rail. Which intervention is most appropriate to help his breathing right now?", "options": {"A": "Support forward-lean sitting and coach relaxed breathing control with reassurance while medication works", "B": "Perform percussion and vibrations in postural drainage positions to mobilise secretions and relieve wheeze", "C": "Teach forced expiratory huffing for ten minutes to clear the airways and reduce the wheeze", "D": "Lie him flat to recruit the diaphragm and slow his breathing while the bronchodilator works"}, "cluster_id": "CHS236", "cluster_text": "Jayden Walker, 15, Year 10 student and keen footballer, attended the emergency department with his mother. Six hours of worsening wheeze, cough and chest tightness after a cold. Asthma since age six; he has a preventer inhaler but admits missing doses lately, and a reliever at school. On arrival: RR 30, HR 120, SpO2 92% on room air, wheeze in both lungs, accessory muscle use, speaking in short phrases, alert. The team gave repeated salbutamol through a spacer, an oral corticosteroid and oxygen. Now RR 24, HR 108, SpO2 95% on 2 L/min, still wheezy, speaking in sentences, dry cough. He is to move to the short stay unit and the physiotherapist is asked to provide breathing education. His mother read about a breathing retraining programme online and asks whether it should replace his preventer.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Support forward-lean sitting and coach relaxed breathing control with reassurance while medication works): In acute asthma the problem is bronchospasm, not retained secretions, so relaxed positioning and breathing control reduce work of breathing while drugs act. Percussion and forced huffing can provoke more bronchospasm and fatigue. Lying flat worsens breathlessness in a patient who is using accessory muscles."}, {"id": 1903, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Before moving to the short stay unit, Jayden demonstrates his reliever technique: he fires four puffs into the spacer at once and then takes one deep breath. His mother says this is what he does at school. How should the physiotherapist correct this technique?", "options": {"A": "Fire one puff at a time into the spacer, then take several breaths before the next", "B": "Use the inhaler directly without the spacer, because a spacer reduces delivery during a flare", "C": "Keep four puffs together, but add a ten second breath hold after inhaling from the spacer", "D": "Use the spacer for the first puff only, then inhale directly once his breathing settles"}, "cluster_id": "CHS236", "cluster_text": "Jayden Walker, 15, Year 10 student and keen footballer, attended the emergency department with his mother. Six hours of worsening wheeze, cough and chest tightness after a cold. Asthma since age six; he has a preventer inhaler but admits missing doses lately, and a reliever at school. On arrival: RR 30, HR 120, SpO2 92% on room air, wheeze in both lungs, accessory muscle use, speaking in short phrases, alert. The team gave repeated salbutamol through a spacer, an oral corticosteroid and oxygen. Now RR 24, HR 108, SpO2 95% on 2 L/min, still wheezy, speaking in sentences, dry cough. He is to move to the short stay unit and the physiotherapist is asked to provide breathing education. His mother read about a breathing retraining programme online and asks whether it should replace his preventer.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Fire one puff at a time into the spacer, then take several breaths before the next): Puffs fired together into a spacer stick to the walls and collide, so less drug reaches the lungs. Delivering one puff at a time, followed by several tidal breaths, gives the best delivery. A breath hold does not fix the problem with multiple puffs, and a spacer improves delivery during a flare."}, {"id": 1904, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Jayden's mother shows a social media post about a breathing retraining programme supported by one small unblinded trial, and asks whether it can replace his daily preventer inhaler. How should the physiotherapist respond, drawing on guidelines and appraising the evidence?", "options": {"A": "Discourage the programme as unproven, and advise that only the GP can discuss breathing techniques", "B": "Explain the trial's limits, cite asthma guidelines, and offer retraining only alongside his medication", "C": "Support trialling the programme in place of the preventer, since published trial results show benefit", "D": "Defer to the ED team and decline to discuss evidence, as appraisal falls outside physiotherapy practice"}, "cluster_id": "CHS236", "cluster_text": "Jayden Walker, 15, Year 10 student and keen footballer, attended the emergency department with his mother. Six hours of worsening wheeze, cough and chest tightness after a cold. Asthma since age six; he has a preventer inhaler but admits missing doses lately, and a reliever at school. On arrival: RR 30, HR 120, SpO2 92% on room air, wheeze in both lungs, accessory muscle use, speaking in short phrases, alert. The team gave repeated salbutamol through a spacer, an oral corticosteroid and oxygen. Now RR 24, HR 108, SpO2 95% on 2 L/min, still wheezy, speaking in sentences, dry cough. He is to move to the short stay unit and the physiotherapist is asked to provide breathing education. His mother read about a breathing retraining programme online and asks whether it should replace his preventer.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Explain the trial's limits, cite asthma guidelines, and offer retraining only alongside his medication): A small unblinded trial carries a high risk of bias and cannot justify stopping preventer therapy, which guidelines recommend for persistent asthma. Physiotherapists are expected to appraise evidence and explain it, and breathing retraining may help as an adjunct. Refusing to discuss it, or dismissing it outright, does not meet that expectation."}, {"id": 1905, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Samuel says he plans to stop candesartan on training days to avoid light-headedness after exercise. He asks the physiotherapist to confirm this is fine. His next GP appointment is in a month. What is the most appropriate response within the physiotherapist's scope of practice?", "options": {"A": "Suggest he takes it after training rather than before, since timing changes are within exercise advice", "B": "Agree if he checks his home blood pressure before each session and stops exercise when low", "C": "Tell him candesartan blunts training gains in hypertension, so skipping it is reasonable for conditioning phases", "D": "Advise against changing it, record the symptoms, and ask permission to inform his GP"}, "cluster_id": "CHS237", "cluster_text": "Samuel Adeyemi, 63, truck driver, has a 4.6 cm infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm found incidentally and is under ultrasound surveillance with his GP. He attends outpatient physiotherapy for a general conditioning program before a knee arthroscopy. Hypertension treated with amlodipine and candesartan. Resting BP 148/88 mmHg, HR 76 bpm, SpO2 97 percent. Exercise tolerance test: BP rose to 196/98 mmHg at moderate effort with no symptoms. No abdominal or back pain. Pulses palpable at both feet. He is anxious about the aneurysm. He asks if he can do heavy leg press and says he feels light-headed for an hour after sessions, so he plans to stop taking candesartan on training days.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Advise against changing it, record the symptoms, and ask permission to inform his GP): Starting, stopping or adjusting medication is a medical decision outside physiotherapy scope, so the safest response is to advise against self-adjusting and to communicate the post-exercise light-headedness to the GP with his permission. Suggesting timing changes or conditional rules still amounts to medication advice. Blood pressure control matters even more given his aneurysm."}, {"id": 1906, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Samuel's aneurysm is small and asymptomatic, and his blood pressure rises steeply with effort. The physiotherapist wants to limit aortic wall stress during sessions. He enjoys heavy lifting and wants to continue it. Which principle should guide exercise selection and breathing technique?", "options": {"A": "Avoid resistance and aerobic exercise until the aneurysm is surgically repaired, protecting the vessel wall", "B": "Choose isometric holds at high loads, because static contractions place less stress on the aortic wall", "C": "Favour moderate aerobic and light resistance work, with relaxed breathing and no straining", "D": "Use short maximal lifts with a brief breath hold, which keep exposure to high pressure low"}, "cluster_id": "CHS237", "cluster_text": "Samuel Adeyemi, 63, truck driver, has a 4.6 cm infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm found incidentally and is under ultrasound surveillance with his GP. He attends outpatient physiotherapy for a general conditioning program before a knee arthroscopy. Hypertension treated with amlodipine and candesartan. Resting BP 148/88 mmHg, HR 76 bpm, SpO2 97 percent. Exercise tolerance test: BP rose to 196/98 mmHg at moderate effort with no symptoms. No abdominal or back pain. Pulses palpable at both feet. He is anxious about the aneurysm. He asks if he can do heavy leg press and says he feels light-headed for an hour after sessions, so he plans to stop taking candesartan on training days.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Favour moderate aerobic and light resistance work, with relaxed breathing and no straining): Straining and breath holding cause abrupt rises in blood pressure, and heavy isometric or maximal efforts do the same, so they raise wall stress. Moderate aerobic work and light to moderate resistance with continuous breathing keep pressure rises modest. Avoiding all exercise is not required for a small asymptomatic aneurysm and has its own cardiovascular costs."}, {"id": 1907, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Samuel's vascular specialist has set an upper systolic limit for exercise. Mid-set today his reading reaches that limit, although he feels well and wants to finish the last few repetitions. The cuff fits correctly and the device is validated. What should the physiotherapist do?", "options": {"A": "Stop the set, rest, and resume lighter once pressure is below the limit", "B": "Continue at the same load but have him exhale on effort to keep pressure down throughout", "C": "Stop the session and send him to emergency, because reaching the limit signals imminent rupture", "D": "Let him finish the last repetitions, then rest and recheck, as he has no symptoms"}, "cluster_id": "CHS237", "cluster_text": "Samuel Adeyemi, 63, truck driver, has a 4.6 cm infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm found incidentally and is under ultrasound surveillance with his GP. He attends outpatient physiotherapy for a general conditioning program before a knee arthroscopy. Hypertension treated with amlodipine and candesartan. Resting BP 148/88 mmHg, HR 76 bpm, SpO2 97 percent. Exercise tolerance test: BP rose to 196/98 mmHg at moderate effort with no symptoms. No abdominal or back pain. Pulses palpable at both feet. He is anxious about the aneurysm. He asks if he can do heavy leg press and says he feels light-headed for an hour after sessions, so he plans to stop taking candesartan on training days.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stop the set, rest, and resume lighter once pressure is below the limit): A set limit is a ceiling to be respected even when the patient feels well, because the pressure rise toward it is the hazard, not the symptoms. Stopping, resting and resuming at a reduced load brings pressure back under the limit without overreacting. Finishing the set or relying on breathing technique alone accepts exposure above the limit, while an emergency transfer is not warranted for an expected exercise response."}, {"id": 1908, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Two weeks later Samuel arrives reporting sudden deep abdominal and back pain since this morning, radiating to his groin. He looks pale and sweaty. HR 112 bpm, BP 96/58 mmHg. He asks to continue his program. What should the physiotherapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Examine his abdomen for a pulsatile mass using deep palpation, then decide whether referral is needed", "B": "Stop activity, keep him lying down, call 000, and monitor until paramedics arrive", "C": "Move him to a quiet room, give fluids, and reassess pressure and pain after rest", "D": "Phone his GP for an urgent appointment today and arrange for family to drive him there"}, "cluster_id": "CHS237", "cluster_text": "Samuel Adeyemi, 63, truck driver, has a 4.6 cm infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm found incidentally and is under ultrasound surveillance with his GP. He attends outpatient physiotherapy for a general conditioning program before a knee arthroscopy. Hypertension treated with amlodipine and candesartan. Resting BP 148/88 mmHg, HR 76 bpm, SpO2 97 percent. Exercise tolerance test: BP rose to 196/98 mmHg at moderate effort with no symptoms. No abdominal or back pain. Pulses palpable at both feet. He is anxious about the aneurysm. He asks if he can do heavy leg press and says he feels light-headed for an hour after sessions, so he plans to stop taking candesartan on training days.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stop activity, keep him lying down, call 000, and monitor until paramedics arrive): Sudden severe abdominal or back pain with tachycardia, low blood pressure and pallor in a person with a known aneurysm suggests rupture, a time-critical emergency needing an ambulance. Deep palpation could worsen bleeding, and resting, giving fluids or private transport delays definitive care. Keeping him lying down while paramedics are called is the correct first action."}, {"id": 1909, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Marco becomes frightened and breathless in bed at night, and Carla asks about a low cost option that needs no prescription. The physiotherapist suggests a handheld fan. Which explanation of how a fan helps breathlessness should the physiotherapist give them?", "options": {"A": "The fan works mainly as a distraction, so any benefit is placebo and fades with familiarity", "B": "The fan raises blood oxygen by moving fresh air towards the mouth and easing breathing effort", "C": "Airflow across the face stimulates receptors and may ease the sensation of breathlessness", "D": "The fan cools and humidifies inspired air, which loosens secretions and lessens breathlessness within minutes"}, "cluster_id": "CHS238", "cluster_text": "Marco Bruno, 72, has metastatic non small cell lung cancer and long standing COPD. He lives with his wife Carla in a single storey home and receives community palliative care, including a nurse visit twice weekly. The palliative team recently started a low dose opioid for breathlessness. He is referred to physiotherapy for breathlessness and function. Observations at rest: RR 24, HR 96, SpO2 93 percent on room air, Borg breathlessness 3 out of 10 at rest and 7 out of 10 after walking 8 metres to the bathroom. He uses accessory muscles when anxious and leans forward on the kitchen bench to recover. Chest is hyperinflated with reduced air entry bilaterally. His goals are to shower without help and sit in the garden. Carla is exhausted and fears he will stop breathing. He has declined hospital admission.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Airflow across the face stimulates receptors and may ease the sensation of breathlessness): Airflow across the cheek and nose stimulates facial receptors that can reduce the sensation of breathlessness, and it is cheap and under the person's own control. It does not meaningfully change blood oxygen or secretions. The benefit goes beyond simple distraction."}, {"id": 1910, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "After showering, Marco becomes panicked and breathes rapidly with raised shoulders, and his SpO2 reads 92 percent on room air. He is seated on a stool in the bathroom. What is the most appropriate immediate physiotherapy response to reduce his distress?", "options": {"A": "Help him lie flat to rest breathing muscles, rechecking saturation each minute until it settles", "B": "Commence high flow oxygen immediately because the saturation is below target, and arrange a nurse review", "C": "Stay with him, support a forward lean with relaxed shoulders, and coach slow breathing control", "D": "Encourage big breaths in and forceful breaths out to clear air trapped in his hyperinflated chest"}, "cluster_id": "CHS238", "cluster_text": "Marco Bruno, 72, has metastatic non small cell lung cancer and long standing COPD. He lives with his wife Carla in a single storey home and receives community palliative care, including a nurse visit twice weekly. The palliative team recently started a low dose opioid for breathlessness. He is referred to physiotherapy for breathlessness and function. Observations at rest: RR 24, HR 96, SpO2 93 percent on room air, Borg breathlessness 3 out of 10 at rest and 7 out of 10 after walking 8 metres to the bathroom. He uses accessory muscles when anxious and leans forward on the kitchen bench to recover. Chest is hyperinflated with reduced air entry bilaterally. His goals are to shower without help and sit in the garden. Carla is exhausted and fears he will stop breathing. He has declined hospital admission.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stay with him, support a forward lean with relaxed shoulders, and coach slow breathing control): A supported forward lean lets the diaphragm work better and relaxes accessory muscles, and calm coaching of slow breathing control reduces panic. Lying flat increases the effort of breathing in a hyperinflated chest. Forceful breathing and high flow oxygen do not address the cause and may worsen anxiety or carbon dioxide retention."}, {"id": 1911, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Marco's top goal is showering without help, but the task leaves him at Borg 8 and he now avoids it. He has a shower stool. Which approach best supports this goal within his palliative care plan and his wish to stay at home?", "options": {"A": "Build capacity first with a walking program, delaying showering until his effort rating falls below four", "B": "Break the task into stages, exhale on effort, rest between steps, and keep items within reach", "C": "Arrange a carer to shower him, saving his limited energy for time in the garden", "D": "Advise a quick hot shower to loosen secretions, finishing before the effort builds up"}, "cluster_id": "CHS238", "cluster_text": "Marco Bruno, 72, has metastatic non small cell lung cancer and long standing COPD. He lives with his wife Carla in a single storey home and receives community palliative care, including a nurse visit twice weekly. The palliative team recently started a low dose opioid for breathlessness. He is referred to physiotherapy for breathlessness and function. Observations at rest: RR 24, HR 96, SpO2 93 percent on room air, Borg breathlessness 3 out of 10 at rest and 7 out of 10 after walking 8 metres to the bathroom. He uses accessory muscles when anxious and leans forward on the kitchen bench to recover. Chest is hyperinflated with reduced air entry bilaterally. His goals are to shower without help and sit in the garden. Carla is exhausted and fears he will stop breathing. He has declined hospital admission.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Break the task into stages, exhale on effort, rest between steps, and keep items within reach): Pacing, exhaling on effort, resting and arranging the environment let him keep a valued activity with less breathlessness, which suits palliative goals. A long reconditioning program is unrealistic with advanced cancer, and handing the task over removes his independence. Hot steam can worsen breathlessness for some people."}, {"id": 1912, "domain": "cardiorespiratory_physiotherapy", "question": "Two days after the palliative nurse increased his opioid dose, Carla reports Marco is unusually drowsy. At the visit his RR is 9, SpO2 91 percent, and he rouses to voice but falls asleep mid sentence. What is the most appropriate physiotherapy action?", "options": {"A": "Phone the palliative team urgently with current observations, baseline status and the recent dose change", "B": "Document the findings and raise the drowsiness with the nurse at the next scheduled visit", "C": "Advise Carla to withhold the next opioid dose until the nurse reviews him, and document this", "D": "Keep him awake with seated exercise and breathing control, then reassess within the hour before reporting"}, "cluster_id": "CHS238", "cluster_text": "Marco Bruno, 72, has metastatic non small cell lung cancer and long standing COPD. He lives with his wife Carla in a single storey home and receives community palliative care, including a nurse visit twice weekly. The palliative team recently started a low dose opioid for breathlessness. He is referred to physiotherapy for breathlessness and function. Observations at rest: RR 24, HR 96, SpO2 93 percent on room air, Borg breathlessness 3 out of 10 at rest and 7 out of 10 after walking 8 metres to the bathroom. He uses accessory muscles when anxious and leans forward on the kitchen bench to recover. Chest is hyperinflated with reduced air entry bilaterally. His goals are to shower without help and sit in the garden. Carla is exhausted and fears he will stop breathing. He has declined hospital admission.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Phone the palliative team urgently with current observations, baseline status and the recent dose change): A slow respiratory rate with drowsiness after a dose change may signal opioid toxicity, so the palliative team must know promptly, with clear observations and baseline for comparison. Physiotherapists do not adjust medication, and waiting delays review. Keeping him awake with exercise treats the sign rather than reporting its likely cause. He has declined admission, so urgent palliative review is the appropriate pathway."}, {"id": 1913, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Redness over the tibial crest of Mr Mavros's residual limb persists throughout the visit after prosthesis removal, though the skin is intact. He wears the prosthesis most of the day and increased his walking recently. How should the physiotherapist interpret this finding?", "options": {"A": "Reactive hyperaemia from limb volume loss, resolved by adding more prosthetic socks before walking", "B": "A superficial liner friction reaction, managed by washing and drying the limb carefully after each use", "C": "Normal skin response over a pressure-tolerant area that will adapt with graded continued wearing", "D": "Excess pressure over a bony prominence, suggesting a socket fit problem needing prosthetist review"}, "cluster_id": "CHS239", "cluster_text": "Mr Kostas Mavros, 68, had a right transtibial amputation five months ago for peripheral arterial disease and type 2 diabetes. He is a retired baker living with his wife in a single-storey home. A prosthetist fitted a new socket with a silicone liner three weeks ago, and you are visiting him at home. He wears the prosthesis most of the day and has recently walked further to the letterbox and around the garden with a single-point stick. The skin is intact, but redness over the tibial crest of the residual limb has not faded 45 minutes after he removed the prosthesis. Left foot: dry skin, thick callus under the first metatarsal head and reduced protective sensation to monofilament. Timed Up and Go is 18 seconds with the stick. No calf pain, fever or wound discharge. You have 40 minutes before travelling to your next visit, and your phone shows a missed call from a client's carer.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Excess pressure over a bony prominence, suggesting a socket fit problem needing prosthetist review): Redness over a bony prominence such as the tibial crest that persists well after unloading indicates pressure the socket is not distributing well, so the prosthetist should review the fit. Redness over tolerant tissue fades quickly, and adding socks without checking the socket can increase pressure further."}, {"id": 1914, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "Mr Mavros has thick callus and reduced protective sensation in his remaining left foot, which now takes extra load during walking. His wife asks what they should do each day to protect his skin. Which advice about foot and skin care is most important to give?", "options": {"A": "Inspect the limb and both feet daily, moisturise freely, and see the podiatrist if pain develops", "B": "Inspect both feet and the limb daily, and file the callus gently at home", "C": "Inspect both feet and the limb daily, wear suitable footwear, and report skin damage early", "D": "Increase prosthesis wearing time to toughen the skin, and inspect both feet weekly with a mirror"}, "cluster_id": "CHS239", "cluster_text": "Mr Kostas Mavros, 68, had a right transtibial amputation five months ago for peripheral arterial disease and type 2 diabetes. He is a retired baker living with his wife in a single-storey home. A prosthetist fitted a new socket with a silicone liner three weeks ago, and you are visiting him at home. He wears the prosthesis most of the day and has recently walked further to the letterbox and around the garden with a single-point stick. The skin is intact, but redness over the tibial crest of the residual limb has not faded 45 minutes after he removed the prosthesis. Left foot: dry skin, thick callus under the first metatarsal head and reduced protective sensation to monofilament. Timed Up and Go is 18 seconds with the stick. No calf pain, fever or wound discharge. You have 40 minutes before travelling to your next visit, and your phone shows a missed call from a client's carer.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Inspect both feet and the limb daily, wear suitable footwear, and report skin damage early): With diabetes and reduced protective sensation, ulcers arise unnoticed under load, so daily inspection of both feet and the residual limb, suitable footwear and early reporting of any damage is the priority. Waiting for pain fails because he cannot feel the injury. Filing callus at home can break insensate skin. Longer wearing does not toughen skin safely, and weekly checks are too infrequent."}, {"id": 1915, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "The prosthetist has adjusted the socket and the redness over the tibial crest has settled with the skin intact. Mr Mavros is keen to return to long walks in the garden. How should the physiotherapist progress his prosthetic wearing and walking programme?", "options": {"A": "Extend walking distance first on uneven garden ground so the limb adapts, then add wearing time", "B": "Resume full daily wearing now and check the skin each evening, since the socket is corrected", "C": "Limit walking to indoors for several weeks, then reassess the skin and the timed walk", "D": "Increase wearing time in graded blocks with skin checks after each, before lengthening walking distance"}, "cluster_id": "CHS239", "cluster_text": "Mr Kostas Mavros, 68, had a right transtibial amputation five months ago for peripheral arterial disease and type 2 diabetes. He is a retired baker living with his wife in a single-storey home. A prosthetist fitted a new socket with a silicone liner three weeks ago, and you are visiting him at home. He wears the prosthesis most of the day and has recently walked further to the letterbox and around the garden with a single-point stick. The skin is intact, but redness over the tibial crest of the residual limb has not faded 45 minutes after he removed the prosthesis. Left foot: dry skin, thick callus under the first metatarsal head and reduced protective sensation to monofilament. Timed Up and Go is 18 seconds with the stick. No calf pain, fever or wound discharge. You have 40 minutes before travelling to your next visit, and your phone shows a missed call from a client's carer.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Increase wearing time in graded blocks with skin checks after each, before lengthening walking distance): After a pressure problem, tolerance should be rebuilt gradually with skin checks showing that each step is safe before the next load is added. Full wearing straight away risks repeated breakdown, and uneven ground or prolonged restriction does not suit a limb that has just had the socket changed."}, {"id": 1916, "domain": "musculoskeletal_physiotherapy", "question": "In the car with forty minutes before the next visit, the physiotherapist has today's notes to finish and a programme review due for a stable client. The carer of Mrs Hilda Brandt, discharged last week after hip fracture surgery, left a message about a fall and new pain in the operated leg. What should be done first?", "options": {"A": "Finish today's notes while details are fresh, then return phone messages in the order received", "B": "Email the carer general falls advice and book the earliest appointment next week for review", "C": "Review the stable client's programme first, and ask the carer to ring back tomorrow", "D": "Return the carer's call, assess the fall and leg pain, and advise urgent review if needed"}, "cluster_id": "CHS239", "cluster_text": "Mr Kostas Mavros, 68, had a right transtibial amputation five months ago for peripheral arterial disease and type 2 diabetes. He is a retired baker living with his wife in a single-storey home. A prosthetist fitted a new socket with a silicone liner three weeks ago, and you are visiting him at home. He wears the prosthesis most of the day and has recently walked further to the letterbox and around the garden with a single-point stick. The skin is intact, but redness over the tibial crest of the residual limb has not faded 45 minutes after he removed the prosthesis. Left foot: dry skin, thick callus under the first metatarsal head and reduced protective sensation to monofilament. Timed Up and Go is 18 seconds with the stick. No calf pain, fever or wound discharge. You have 40 minutes before travelling to your next visit, and your phone shows a missed call from a client's carer.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Return the carer's call, assess the fall and leg pain, and advise urgent review if needed): A recent fall with new pain in a limb after hip fracture surgery may signal a fracture, dislocation or clot, so the safety call takes priority over documentation and routine reviews. Notes can be completed after the call, and general advice by email does not assess the risk."}, {"id": 1917, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Liam has bilateral basal crackles, SpO2 of 96 percent on FiO2 0.35 and an ICP of 14 mmHg within the unit's target. He is sedated and ventilated. Before planning chest physiotherapy techniques, which factor needs the most careful monitoring during the treatment?", "options": {"A": "Respiratory rate and tidal volume on the ventilator, since the lung bases are the treatment target", "B": "Oxygen saturation, with treatment stopped if it falls even a few percent from baseline", "C": "Intracranial pressure and cerebral perfusion pressure responses to positioning, suction and handling", "D": "Left femoral fracture pain responses, because the nail fixation is the main limit to handling"}, "cluster_id": "CHS240", "cluster_text": "Liam O'Connor, 22, apprentice plumber, motorbike crash 6 days ago. Severe traumatic brain injury: Glasgow Coma Scale 8 on admission, now E3 V1T M5 with sedation lightened. Intubated and ventilated, head of bed 30 degrees, ICP monitor in situ reading 14 mmHg (within the unit's target), mean arterial pressure 85 mmHg. Left femoral shaft fracture fixed with an intramedullary nail; orthopaedics allow weight bearing as tolerated. Mild increase in tone in both legs. Passive range is full except left hip flexion, limited by pain. Chest: bilateral basal crackles, SpO2 96 percent on FiO2 0.35. His family are at the bedside. The ICU is introducing a standard mobility outcome measure and has asked physiotherapists to record it at every session.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Intracranial pressure and cerebral perfusion pressure responses to positioning, suction and handling): In severe traumatic brain injury, handling, suction and position changes can raise intracranial pressure and lower cerebral perfusion, so these are watched continuously and treatment is modified or paused if they leave target. Oxygenation and ventilator values matter but are secondary to protecting the injured brain. The nailed fracture permits weight bearing and is not the main limit."}, {"id": 1918, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Day 6: Liam's ICP is 14 mmHg, MAP 85 mmHg, he is E3 V1T M5, on low dose sedation, and orthopaedics allow weight bearing as tolerated. The ICU team asks whether early mobilisation with sitting out of bed can begin. What is the best decision now?", "options": {"A": "Start sitting him out of bed now, using the standard ICU protocol and routine observations", "B": "Delay mobilisation until he follows commands and sedation is ceased, because movement can raise ICP", "C": "Start graded mobilisation with continuous monitoring and stop criteria agreed with the intensivist", "D": "Limit treatment to passive range of motion and positioning until the ICP monitor is removed"}, "cluster_id": "CHS240", "cluster_text": "Liam O'Connor, 22, apprentice plumber, motorbike crash 6 days ago. Severe traumatic brain injury: Glasgow Coma Scale 8 on admission, now E3 V1T M5 with sedation lightened. Intubated and ventilated, head of bed 30 degrees, ICP monitor in situ reading 14 mmHg (within the unit's target), mean arterial pressure 85 mmHg. Left femoral shaft fracture fixed with an intramedullary nail; orthopaedics allow weight bearing as tolerated. Mild increase in tone in both legs. Passive range is full except left hip flexion, limited by pain. Chest: bilateral basal crackles, SpO2 96 percent on FiO2 0.35. His family are at the bedside. The ICU is introducing a standard mobility outcome measure and has asked physiotherapists to record it at every session.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Start graded mobilisation with continuous monitoring and stop criteria agreed with the intensivist): Early mobilisation is feasible when ICP and haemodynamics are stable, provided there are agreed physiological limits and close monitoring. Waiting for him to follow commands or for the monitor to come out would delay benefit and increase deconditioning. Applying the standard protocol without brain injury specific limits is unsafe."}, {"id": 1919, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "Liam's passive left hip flexion is limited by pain and mild bilateral lower limb tone is present. Positioning in bed for the next shift needs planning to protect his brain injury and the femoral fixation. Which positioning plan is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Head of bed at 30 degrees, head midline, regular turns, and the left leg supported", "B": "Head of bed at 30 degrees, head midline, turns only once a shift to protect fixation", "C": "Head of bed at 30 degrees, regular turns, with hips and knees held flexed on pillows", "D": "Head of bed flat overnight to ease hip pain, head midline, regular turns, left leg supported"}, "cluster_id": "CHS240", "cluster_text": "Liam O'Connor, 22, apprentice plumber, motorbike crash 6 days ago. Severe traumatic brain injury: Glasgow Coma Scale 8 on admission, now E3 V1T M5 with sedation lightened. Intubated and ventilated, head of bed 30 degrees, ICP monitor in situ reading 14 mmHg (within the unit's target), mean arterial pressure 85 mmHg. Left femoral shaft fracture fixed with an intramedullary nail; orthopaedics allow weight bearing as tolerated. Mild increase in tone in both legs. Passive range is full except left hip flexion, limited by pain. Chest: bilateral basal crackles, SpO2 96 percent on FiO2 0.35. His family are at the bedside. The ICU is introducing a standard mobility outcome measure and has asked physiotherapists to record it at every session.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Head of bed at 30 degrees, head midline, regular turns, and the left leg supported): The raised head of bed with the head midline supports venous drainage, regular turns protect skin and lungs, and support under the injured leg protects the fixation and eases pain. Turning only once a shift risks pressure injury and chest complications. Holding the hips and knees flexed on pillows invites contracture and is not needed for mild tone. Lying flat can raise intracranial pressure."}, {"id": 1920, "domain": "neurological_physiotherapy", "question": "The ICU asks physiotherapists to record a standard mobility score at every session. Liam's score will be compared with unit data each month. What is the main benefit of using one consistent outcome measure for all patients in this quality improvement project?", "options": {"A": "It gives an early prediction of each patient's final recovery, so families can be counselled", "B": "It allows change over time and unit performance to be compared reliably", "C": "It standardises treatment so that all patients with similar scores receive the same mobilisation plan", "D": "It shows which physiotherapist achieves the best results, so staffing can be allocated accordingly"}, "cluster_id": "CHS240", "cluster_text": "Liam O'Connor, 22, apprentice plumber, motorbike crash 6 days ago. Severe traumatic brain injury: Glasgow Coma Scale 8 on admission, now E3 V1T M5 with sedation lightened. Intubated and ventilated, head of bed 30 degrees, ICP monitor in situ reading 14 mmHg (within the unit's target), mean arterial pressure 85 mmHg. Left femoral shaft fracture fixed with an intramedullary nail; orthopaedics allow weight bearing as tolerated. Mild increase in tone in both legs. Passive range is full except left hip flexion, limited by pain. Chest: bilateral basal crackles, SpO2 96 percent on FiO2 0.35. His family are at the bedside. The ICU is introducing a standard mobility outcome measure and has asked physiotherapists to record it at every session.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It allows change over time and unit performance to be compared reliably): A consistent, valid and reliable measure makes it possible to track change over time and to audit unit practice against targets. Early scores do not predict final recovery with enough accuracy to counsel families. Scores support clinical reasoning and do not replace it, so treatment is still individualised. The aim is to compare unit performance and test practice changes, not to rank individual clinicians."}];
